window.CERT_DATA = window.CERT_DATA || {}; window.CERT_DATA["gcdl"] = {"domains":[{"n":1,"title":"Digital Transformation with Google Cloud","short":"Digital Transformation","weight":"~17%","desc":"Why cloud, cloud models and economics (CapEx vs OpEx, TCO), Google Cloud infrastructure, and leading cloud adoption.","lessons":[{"id":"d1l1","num":1,"title":"What digital transformation means","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Digital transformation</strong> = using modern digital technology (cloud, data, analytics, automation, AI) to <strong>redesign how the business works and delivers value</strong>: workflows, collaboration, customer experience and speed of response to demand <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q39\">Exam 2 · Q39</a>.</li>\n<li>It is <strong>not just a change of hosting location</strong>. Moving to the cloud is a chance to <strong>redesign current business processes and services</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q33\">Exam 4 · Q33</a>.</li>\n<li>It is <strong>not only for IT</strong> and <strong>not only for large companies</strong>. It changes the business model, not just the cost line <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q33\">Exam 4 · Q33</a>.</li>\n<li>The value over time comes from teams spending less effort on running infrastructure and more on <strong>building solutions</strong> that differentiate the business <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q29\">Exam 1 · Q29</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q31\">Exam 4 · Q31</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Customer expectations drive it.</strong> The exam's recurring user expectations:</li>\n<li>Information <strong>simple to access from any device</strong> (hence mobile apps) <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q7\">Exam 2 · Q7</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Always-on services</strong>: help and service outside business hours <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q16\">Exam 4 · Q16</a>.</li>\n<li>Cloud gives developers <strong>new tools to innovate and optimize resource usage</strong>: fast provisioning, easy scaling, room to experiment <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q31\">Exam 4 · Q31</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Narrower labels the exam uses as distractors</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q39\">Exam 2 · Q39</a>:</li>\n<li><strong>Data center migration</strong>: moving workloads and data to another environment. It can be one step of a transformation, but it doesn't redesign processes by itself.</li>\n<li><strong>Operations optimization</strong>: making existing operations more efficient, reliable or cheaper. It supports transformation but is only part of it.</li>\n<li><strong>User personalization</strong>: tailoring content to each user. It is one possible outcome, not the overall approach.</li>\n<li>Moving the same systems to the cloud without improving processes or services (\"lift and shift only\") gets the cost benefits at best and <strong>misses most of the value</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q33\">Exam 4 · Q33</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>The cloud as a transformation platform</strong> <em>(not in the question bank)</em>: Google frames cloud as an enabler of new business models, data-driven decisions, and faster experimentation. Older IT was a cost center that kept the lights on; modern cloud is a driver of innovation.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Phrases that map to \"digital transformation\"</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Scenario wording</th>\n<th>Answer</th>\n<th>Not this</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Cloud + analytics + automation to redesign workflows and customer experience</td>\n<td>✅ Digital transformation <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q39\">Exam 2 · Q39</a></td>\n<td>❌ Data center migration, operations optimization, user personalization</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>What does adopting cloud mainly represent?</td>\n<td>✅ Chance to redesign processes and services <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q33\">Exam 4 · Q33</a></td>\n<td>❌ \"Only IT\", \"only large orgs\", \"same business model, lower cost\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Why launch a mobile app?</td>\n<td>✅ Customers expect access from any device <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q7\">Exam 2 · Q7</a></td>\n<td>❌ \"Only through mobile\", \"mobile is the only way to secure data\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Why extend support to evenings and weekends?</td>\n<td>✅ Users expect always-on services <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q16\">Exam 4 · Q16</a></td>\n<td>❌ Personalization, regional access, professional expertise</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>If the scenario combines several technologies to change <strong>how the business operates</strong>, the answer is <strong>digital transformation</strong>, not a narrower label <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q39\">Exam 2 · Q39</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Gain value over time\" from cloud → <strong>focus on solution development / innovation</strong>, not on maintaining infrastructure <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q29\">Exam 1 · Q29</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Why are customers asking for X?\" → pick the <strong>broad modern expectation</strong> (any device, always on), not a narrow technical reason <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q7\">Exam 2 · Q7</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q16\">Exam 4 · Q16</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Words like \"only\", \"exclusively\", \"never\" in options are usually wrong at this level <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q7\">Exam 2 · Q7</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q33\">Exam 4 · Q33</a>.</li>\n<li>A mobile app is not more secure by itself. Security comes from identity and access controls, encryption and secure design across the whole system <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q7\">Exam 2 · Q7</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Always-on\" is about <strong>when</strong> a service is available (time of day). \"Regional access\" is about <strong>where</strong> (geography, language, local markets). \"Personalization\" is about tailoring to each user <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q16\">Exam 4 · Q16</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Expand internal hosting infrastructure\" or \"relax on-prem security\" are never the cloud benefit <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q29\">Exam 1 · Q29</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Avoid the limitations of serverless\" or \"optimize on-prem maintenance\" don't describe moving to cloud <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q31\">Exam 4 · Q31</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"Digital transformation means using cloud, data, automation and AI to redesign how a business operates and serves customers. This lesson covers what the term means on the exam, which customer expectations drive it, and why it is more than a hosting change.","objectives":["Define digital transformation and tell it apart from narrower labels like data center migration","Explain why cloud adoption is a chance to redesign processes and services, not just cut costs","Recognize the modern customer expectations behind transformation: any-device access and always-on service"],"takeaways":["Cloud + analytics + automation to redesign workflows and customer experience = digital transformation.","Data center migration, operations optimization and personalization are narrower parts or outcomes of digital transformation, not the whole approach.","Cloud adoption is a business change, not an IT-only change, and not only for large companies.","The long-term value of cloud is that teams focus on building solutions instead of running infrastructure.","Customers expect information on any device and services that are always on.","Options with 'only', 'exclusively' or 'never' are usually wrong at this level."],"questions":["e1q29","e2q7","e2q39","e4q16","e4q31","e4q33"]},{"id":"d1l2","num":2,"title":"Limits of on-premises infrastructure and why cloud helps","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>On-prem capacity is <strong>fixed</strong>. Adding compute means <strong>buying, installing, configuring and maintaining hardware</strong>, which is <strong>time-consuming and costly</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q35\">Exam 6 · Q35</a>.</li>\n<li>To survive traffic peaks on-prem, you must <strong>provision hardware for peak usage</strong>, which is expensive and sits idle the rest of the time <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q3\">Exam 2 · Q3</a>.</li>\n<li>When peak traffic exceeds what you bought, the app goes down: <strong>not enough servers to handle the traffic</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q15\">Exam 6 · Q15</a>.</li>\n<li>Both directions hurt: you <strong>over-buy</strong> capacity (waste) or <strong>under-buy</strong> (outages) <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q29\">Exam 3 · Q29</a>.</li>\n<li>Cloud fixes this with <strong>elastic, on-demand capacity</strong>: scale up for the spike, scale down after, and <strong>pay only for what you use</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q29\">Exam 3 · Q29</a>.</li>\n<li>Cloud lets you reach data and compute <strong>without being tied to one physical location</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q8\">Exam 1 · Q8</a>.</li>\n<li>Public cloud is <strong>cost-effective for storing and serving data at large scale</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q21\">Exam 4 · Q21</a>.</li>\n<li>Slow provisioning and limited experimentation on fixed hardware are solved by cloud's <strong>self-service tools</strong>: on-demand environments, managed databases, automated deployments, monitoring and autoscaling <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q31\">Exam 4 · Q31</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>\"Agile infrastructure\"</strong> on this exam means you can <strong>provision, scale and release resources on demand</strong> instead of buying hardware for the highest expected peak. Combined with consumption pricing, you only pay for what you need, when you need it <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q29\">Exam 3 · Q29</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Cloud availability is high but never 100%.</strong> Providers publish an <strong>SLA (service level agreement)</strong> per service, with targets such as 99.9% or 99.99%. Real uptime still depends on your design: multiple zones, load balancing, backups and disaster recovery <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q8\">Exam 1 · Q8</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q29\">Exam 3 · Q29</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q31\">Exam 4 · Q31</a>.</li>\n<li>Cloud also offers managed <strong>backup, replication and disaster recovery</strong> capabilities, so teams build less of this themselves <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q29\">Exam 1 · Q29</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Serverless</strong> services (Cloud Run, Cloud Run functions) scale automatically with demand. That is why \"a serverless function can't scale\" is never the cause in an on-prem scenario <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q3\">Exam 2 · Q3</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q31\">Exam 4 · Q31</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q15\">Exam 6 · Q15</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Scenario = \"own data center + traffic spikes + struggle/outage\" → the cause is <strong>fixed hardware capacity / cost of provisioning for peak</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q3\">Exam 2 · Q3</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q15\">Exam 6 · Q15</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q35\">Exam 6 · Q35</a>.</li>\n<li>Scenario = \"over-buy or under-buy for holidays\" → benefit is <strong>pay only for what you need, when you need it</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q29\">Exam 3 · Q29</a>.</li>\n<li>Public cloud benefit for a distributed company → <strong>access not limited to one physical location</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q8\">Exam 1 · Q8</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Serverless and multicloud distractors</strong>: if the company runs on its own servers, options blaming \"serverless functions that can't scale\" or \"multi-cloud complexity\" don't fit the scenario <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q3\">Exam 2 · Q3</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q15\">Exam 6 · Q15</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q35\">Exam 6 · Q35</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Overreliance on PaaS\", \"perimeter security makes data inaccessible\", \"inconsistent customer data\", \"compliance blocks scaling\" are all off-topic <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q3\">Exam 2 · Q3</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q15\">Exam 6 · Q15</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q35\">Exam 6 · Q35</a>.</li>\n<li>PaaS is adopted to <strong>reduce</strong> operational burden, so it can't be the cause of on-prem scaling pain <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q3\">Exam 2 · Q3</a>.</li>\n<li>Bad data quality causes wrong reports and logic errors, <strong>not outages at peak traffic</strong>. Failures that happen only at peak point to a <strong>capacity</strong> limit <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q15\">Exam 6 · Q15</a>.</li>\n<li>Adding capacity doesn't automatically breach compliance. Compliance depends on how systems are designed, secured and governed <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q35\">Exam 6 · Q35</a>.</li>\n<li>Cloud does <strong>not</strong> guarantee 100% availability, does not remove the need for security, and doesn't mean \"unlimited storage = no downtime\" <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q8\">Exam 1 · Q8</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q29\">Exam 3 · Q29</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q31\">Exam 4 · Q31</a>.</li>\n<li>Moving to cloud is never a reason to <strong>relax security protocols</strong>. Under shared responsibility the customer still secures identities, access, data and configuration <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q29\">Exam 1 · Q29</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q8\">Exam 1 · Q8</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Schedule physical server repairs after hours\" is your problem on-prem, not a cloud benefit <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q8\">Exam 1 · Q8</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"On-premises capacity is fixed, so traffic spikes force a choice between paying for idle peak hardware or suffering outages. This lesson explains the scaling pain the exam describes, how elastic cloud capacity removes it, and what cloud does not promise: 100% availability or freedom from security work.","objectives":["Identify why on-prem infrastructure struggles to scale: buying, installing and maintaining hardware is slow and costly","Explain how cloud elasticity and pay-per-use solve over- and under-provisioning","Reject serverless, multicloud and compliance distractors in on-prem scenarios","Explain what a cloud SLA promises and why availability still depends on your own design"],"takeaways":["On-prem traffic spike problems come down to fixed capacity and the cost of provisioning for peak.","'Agile infrastructure' means provisioning, scaling and releasing resources on demand, so you pay only for what you need, when you need it.","Public cloud gives access to data and compute not tied to one physical location.","Public cloud is cost-effective for storing and serving data at large scale.","Cloud SLAs set targets such as 99.9% or 99.99%, never 100%; uptime still depends on multi-zone design, load balancing and disaster recovery.","Serverless scales automatically, data quality issues don't cause peak-time outages, and adding capacity doesn't breach compliance by itself."],"questions":["e1q8","e1q29","e2q3","e3q29","e4q21","e4q31","e6q15","e6q35"]},{"id":"d1l3","num":3,"title":"Cloud economics: CapEx, OpEx, TCO and pay-as-you-go","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>CapEx (capital expenditure)</strong>: large upfront purchases of long-lived assets, such as servers and data centers. Paid before you know if you need them.</li>\n<li><strong>OpEx (operational expenditure)</strong>: ongoing spending for services as you consume them, such as a monthly cloud bill.</li>\n<li><strong>Moving to cloud shifts spending from CapEx to OpEx.</strong> This is the single most repeated fact in this domain <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q4\">Exam 2 · Q4</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q29\">Exam 4 · Q29</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q37\">Exam 4 · Q37</a>.</li>\n<li>On-prem hardware purchases are capital assets, <strong>depreciated over several years</strong>. Cloud services are paid as they are consumed, so they are booked as operating costs <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q4\">Exam 2 · Q4</a>.</li>\n<li>After migration, the customer must <strong>adjust to a consumption-based, pay-as-you-go spending model</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q32\">Exam 3 · Q32</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Cost management itself changes.</strong> Spending becomes usage-based and variable, so teams must <strong>monitor billing, set budgets and alerts, label resources, right-size, and remove idle resources</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q4\">Exam 2 · Q4</a>. Billing tools are covered in Domain 6.</li>\n<li>TCO is <strong>often but not always lower</strong> in the cloud. It depends on architecture, usage patterns, licensing and how well the environment is optimized. A poorly managed cloud can be expensive <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q4\">Exam 2 · Q4</a>.</li>\n<li>Cloud <strong>reduces on-prem infrastructure management costs</strong> (hardware upkeep, staff time on racking and patching servers) <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q27\">Exam 4 · Q27</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>TCO (total cost of ownership)</strong> = all costs over time: hardware, software licenses, power, cooling, space, staff, maintenance, downtime, and for the cloud option also the <strong>migration effort</strong>. Review TCO before migrating, and compare it with the expected business benefits, to <strong>evaluate return on investment (ROI)</strong> of cloud vs staying on-prem <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q49\">Exam 4 · Q49</a>.</li>\n<li>Large-scale storage and serving is cheaper in public cloud thanks to economies of scale <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q21\">Exam 4 · Q21</a>.</li>\n<li>Google Cloud also offers <strong>discounts for steady usage</strong> (sustained use and committed use discounts) <em>(not in the question bank; billing and cost control are in Domain 6)</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>CapEx vs OpEx</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>CapEx</th>\n<th>OpEx</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Typical example</td>\n<td>Buy servers, build a data center</td>\n<td>Pay a monthly cloud bill</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Timing</td>\n<td>Upfront</td>\n<td>As you go</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Capacity</td>\n<td>Fixed, sized for peak</td>\n<td>Elastic, sized for actual use</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Risk</td>\n<td>Over- or under-buying</td>\n<td>Bill grows with usage, needs monitoring</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cloud migration direction</td>\n<td>❌ Away from</td>\n<td>✅ Toward <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q4\">Exam 2 · Q4</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q29\">Exam 4 · Q29</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q37\">Exam 4 · Q37</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Any \"how does spending change when moving to cloud?\" question → <strong>CapEx → OpEx</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q4\">Exam 2 · Q4</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q29\">Exam 4 · Q29</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q37\">Exam 4 · Q37</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Why look at TCO before migration?\" → <strong>to evaluate ROI</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q49\">Exam 4 · Q49</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Effect on operations and personnel costs\" → <strong>reduced on-prem infrastructure management costs</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q27\">Exam 4 · Q27</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>OpEx → CapEx</strong> is always backwards <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q4\">Exam 2 · Q4</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q29\">Exam 4 · Q29</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q28\">Exam 5 · Q28</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Cost management stays the same, only TCO changes\" is wrong. The spending model itself changes <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q4\">Exam 2 · Q4</a>.</li>\n<li>Error budget, SLA availability and compute sizing are not why you review TCO <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q49\">Exam 4 · Q49</a>:</li>\n<li><strong>Error budget</strong>: an SRE idea. With an SLO (service level objective), it is how much unreliability is acceptable before teams must prioritize reliability work.</li>\n<li><strong>Service level availability</strong>: a reliability target set in SLAs and SLOs, not a financial comparison.</li>\n<li><strong>Compute sizing</strong>: technical capacity planning. It feeds cost estimates, but TCO is much broader.</li>\n<li>\"Increased hardware maintenance\" or \"increased cloud hardware management costs\" for the customer is wrong. The provider manages the hardware <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q27\">Exam 4 · Q27</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q37\">Exam 4 · Q37</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Reduced cloud software licensing costs\" sounds plausible but isn't the point. Licensing may change with the vendor agreement and architecture, but it doesn't automatically go down; the clear saving is on-prem management <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q27\">Exam 4 · Q27</a>.</li>\n<li>\"A shift toward structured data\" has nothing to do with migration economics. Cloud supports structured, semi-structured (JSON) and unstructured data (images, video, logs) alike <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q37\">Exam 4 · Q37</a>.</li>\n<li>\"No change to existing responsibilities\" is wrong. Infrastructure duties move to the provider; the customer keeps application design, access control, data protection and configuration <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q37\">Exam 4 · Q37</a>.</li>\n<li>OpEx does not make TCO automatically \"more predictable\". Usage-based bills can vary <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q29\">Exam 3 · Q29</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"Moving to cloud changes how IT is paid for and managed: from large upfront capital purchases to ongoing, usage-based operational spending. This lesson covers CapEx vs OpEx, how cost management changes, TCO, ROI and the pay-as-you-go model.","objectives":["Define CapEx and OpEx and state the direction of the shift when moving to cloud","Explain what total cost of ownership includes and why it is reviewed before migration","Describe how cloud affects infrastructure management and personnel costs","Explain why cost management changes after migration and which practices replace upfront capacity planning"],"takeaways":["Moving to cloud shifts spending from CapEx to OpEx. OpEx to CapEx is always the wrong direction.","After migration, the business adapts to a consumption-based, pay-as-you-go model.","Cost management changes too: monitor billing, set budgets and alerts, label, right-size and remove idle resources.","Review TCO, including migration effort, before migrating to evaluate return on investment. Cloud TCO is often lower but not guaranteed.","Cloud reduces on-prem infrastructure management costs; the provider maintains the hardware. Licensing costs don't automatically drop.","Usage-based bills are not automatically more predictable; they need monitoring."],"questions":["e2q4","e3q29","e3q32","e4q21","e4q27","e4q29","e4q37","e4q49","e5q28"]},{"id":"d1l4","num":4,"title":"Cloud deployment models: public, private, hybrid, multicloud","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Public cloud</strong>: shared infrastructure owned and run by a provider (Google Cloud), rented on demand. Benefits: global reach, elasticity, scale economics <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q8\">Exam 1 · Q8</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q21\">Exam 4 · Q21</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Private cloud</strong> <em>(not in the question bank)</em>: cloud-style infrastructure dedicated to one organization, usually in its own data center.</li>\n<li><strong>Hybrid cloud</strong> = <strong>private (on-prem) infrastructure + public cloud services</strong> used together. Typical reason: keep sensitive or regulated systems on-prem while using public cloud for analytics, scaling and new services <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q22\">Exam 3 · Q22</a>.</li>\n<li>The private side of a hybrid cloud can be an <strong>on-prem data center, a private cloud or an edge environment</strong>. The public side adds scalability, analytics, modernization, storage or disaster recovery <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q22\">Exam 3 · Q22</a>.</li>\n<li>Hybrid and multicloud are <strong>not exclusive</strong>: one organization can be both at once <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q22\">Exam 3 · Q22</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Multicloud</strong>: using <strong>more than one public cloud provider</strong>. Reasons: avoid lock-in, use the best service from each, meet regional or regulatory needs. Hybrid and multicloud products (GKE Enterprise, formerly Anthos) are in Domain 4.</li>\n<li><strong>On-premises</strong>: everything in your own data center, all managed by you.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Deployment models compared</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Model</th>\n<th>What it combines</th>\n<th>Exam signal</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>On-premises</td>\n<td>Own hardware, own data center</td>\n<td>\"aging data centers\", \"fixed capacity\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Public cloud</td>\n<td>Provider's shared infrastructure</td>\n<td>\"global access\", \"pay for what you use\" <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q8\">Exam 1 · Q8</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Private cloud</td>\n<td>Dedicated cloud-style infrastructure</td>\n<td><em>(not in the question bank)</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hybrid cloud</td>\n<td>✅ Private/on-prem + public cloud <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q22\">Exam 3 · Q22</a></td>\n<td>\"keep sensitive systems on-prem, use cloud for the rest\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Multicloud</td>\n<td>Two or more public providers</td>\n<td>\"multiple public cloud providers\" (the distractor in <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q22\">Exam 3 · Q22</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Own data center + Google Cloud together\" → <strong>hybrid</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q22\">Exam 3 · Q22</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Several public cloud providers\" → <strong>multicloud</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"A combination of multiple public cloud providers\" is <strong>multicloud</strong>, not hybrid <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q22\">Exam 3 · Q22</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Serverless + public cloud\" and \"different virtualized servers\" are not deployment models <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q22\">Exam 3 · Q22</a>. Serverless runs on the provider's public cloud, so that option has no private side. Virtual machines can run anywhere, so virtualization alone doesn't make an architecture hybrid.</li>\n<li>\"Hybrid cloud\" also shows up as a distractor in service-model questions. It is a deployment model, not IaaS/PaaS/SaaS <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q11\">Exam 6 · Q11</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"Deployment models describe where workloads run: on-premises, public cloud, private cloud, hybrid or multicloud. The exam mostly tests the definition of hybrid cloud and how it differs from multicloud.","objectives":["Define public, private, hybrid and multicloud deployment models","Distinguish hybrid (private + public) from multicloud (several public providers)","Explain why organizations keep some systems on-prem while using public cloud"],"takeaways":["Hybrid cloud = private or on-prem infrastructure + public cloud services used together.","Multicloud = two or more public cloud providers.","Hybrid lets sensitive systems stay on-prem while analytics and new services use the cloud.","An organization can be hybrid and multicloud at the same time; the private side of hybrid can be on-prem, a private cloud or the edge.","Deployment models are not service models: 'hybrid cloud' is a distractor in IaaS/PaaS/SaaS questions.","Hybrid and multicloud products such as GKE Enterprise are covered in Domain 4."],"questions":["e1q8","e3q22","e4q21","e6q11"]},{"id":"d1l5","num":5,"title":"Service models: IaaS, PaaS, SaaS and shared responsibility","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>IaaS (Infrastructure as a service)</strong>: rent <strong>VMs, storage and networking</strong>; you build and configure the servers, OS and everything above. Example: Compute Engine <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q11\">Exam 6 · Q11</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>PaaS (Platform as a service)</strong>: you deploy <strong>your own code</strong>; the provider runs servers, OS and scaling. Examples: App Engine, Cloud Run <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q13\">Exam 6 · Q13</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>SaaS (Software as a service)</strong>: a <strong>finished application</strong> delivered over the internet; you only use it and manage your data/content. Examples: Google Workspace, an online payroll app <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q1\">Exam 2 · Q1</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Shared responsibility</strong>: the more managed the model, the less you manage. In SaaS, you are left responsible mainly for <strong>your content, data and users</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q22\">Exam 2 · Q22</a>.</li>\n<li>Whatever the model, after migrating you never manage Google's network, physical storage or data centers. You do adapt to <strong>pay-as-you-go</strong> spending <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q32\">Exam 3 · Q32</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>SaaS provider duties</strong>: the provider manages the application, platform, infrastructure, updates and availability. Users reach it through a browser, mobile app or client <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q1\">Exam 2 · Q1</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Serverless / FaaS (functions as a service)</strong>: you write small pieces of <strong>event-driven code</strong> and define the events that trigger them; the provider runs and scales them. Example: Cloud Run functions (formerly Cloud Functions). You still write code, so FaaS is never the answer for \"use a finished app\" <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q1\">Exam 2 · Q1</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>App Engine</strong> is Google's classic PaaS: a fully managed, serverless platform where Google handles provisioning and scaling <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q13\">Exam 6 · Q13</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>NaaS (network as a service)</strong>: managed networking such as connectivity, routing and network security functions. It doesn't host applications <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q13\">Exam 6 · Q13</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Security of vs in the cloud</strong>: the provider secures <strong>the cloud</strong> (data centers, hardware, its network). The customer secures what it puts <strong>in the cloud</strong>: identities, access, data, configurations and workloads <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q29\">Exam 1 · Q29</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q8\">Exam 1 · Q8</a>. Google extends this idea as <strong>shared fate</strong>: it also provides secure defaults, blueprints and guidance to help customers do their part <em>(not in the question bank; security is Domain 5)</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Who manages what</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Layer</th>\n<th>On-prem</th>\n<th>IaaS</th>\n<th>PaaS</th>\n<th>SaaS</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Physical data center, network, hardware</td>\n<td>You</td>\n<td>Google</td>\n<td>Google</td>\n<td>Google</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>OS, runtime, scaling</td>\n<td>You</td>\n<td>You</td>\n<td>Google</td>\n<td>Google</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Application code</td>\n<td>You</td>\n<td>You</td>\n<td>You</td>\n<td>Provider</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Data, content, user access</td>\n<td>You</td>\n<td>You</td>\n<td>You</td>\n<td>✅ You <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q22\">Exam 2 · Q22</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Rent VMs, storage, networking; build custom servers; pay per use\" → <strong>IaaS</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q11\">Exam 6 · Q11</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Deploy our own app, it should scale, we don't want to manage servers or OS\" → <strong>PaaS</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q13\">Exam 6 · Q13</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Use a vendor's ready-made app; no servers, no platform, no code\" → <strong>SaaS</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q1\">Exam 2 · Q1</a>.</li>\n<li>\"What is the SaaS customer exclusively responsible for?\" → <strong>customer-facing content</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q22\">Exam 2 · Q22</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Network as a service\" and \"Functions as a service\" are distractors <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q13\">Exam 6 · Q13</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q1\">Exam 2 · Q1</a>.</li>\n<li>In SaaS, keeping the platform running and monitoring servers and networks are the <strong>provider's</strong> job <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q22\">Exam 2 · Q22</a>. The customer may still handle its own local network connectivity, allowed settings and who gets access <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q22\">Exam 2 · Q22</a>.</li>\n<li>Shared responsibility is about <strong>who secures and operates what</strong>. It doesn't give you \"visibility during traffic spikes\"; that comes from monitoring and logging tools <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q29\">Exam 3 · Q29</a>.</li>\n<li>The customer does not get paid compensation for outages as a \"responsibility\", and does not manage Google's network or storage hardware <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q32\">Exam 3 · Q32</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"Service models describe how much the provider manages for you. This lesson covers IaaS, PaaS and SaaS, the distractor models FaaS and NaaS, the shared responsibility split between customer and provider, and the scenario wording that points to each model.","objectives":["Match a scenario to IaaS, PaaS or SaaS based on what the customer wants to manage","Describe who manages each layer under each service model","Identify what a SaaS customer remains responsible for","Explain why functions as a service and network as a service don't fit 'use a finished app' or 'host my custom app' scenarios"],"takeaways":["IaaS: rent VMs, storage and networking and configure them yourself.","PaaS: deploy your own code; the provider runs servers, OS and scaling.","SaaS: use a ready-made application; you manage only your content, data and users.","The provider always manages the physical data center, network and hardware.","FaaS still means writing event-driven code; NaaS is managed networking. Both are distractors in these questions.","The provider secures the cloud; the customer secures what it puts in the cloud: identities, access, data and configuration."],"questions":["e1q8","e1q29","e2q1","e2q22","e3q29","e3q32","e6q11","e6q13"]},{"id":"d1l6","num":6,"title":"Google Cloud infrastructure: regions, zones, latency and custom hardware","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Region</strong> = an independent geographic area (for example <code>europe-west3</code>, Frankfurt). <strong>Zone</strong> = an isolated deployment area inside a region. Multiple zones protect against a single-zone failure; multiple regions put the app <strong>closer to users</strong> and survive a regional outage.</li>\n<li><strong>Network latency</strong> = the delay for data to travel between user and server; it grows with <strong>physical distance</strong>. <strong>Bandwidth</strong> = how much data can move per second. Users far from the region can have <strong>high bandwidth but high latency</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q32\">Exam 4 · Q32</a>.</li>\n<li>To cut latency for users on another continent, <strong>deploy a replica of the app in a region near them</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q41\">Exam 4 · Q41</a>. A <strong>global load balancer</strong> then sends each user to the nearest healthy backend, and <strong>Cloud CDN</strong> caches content at the edge <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q32\">Exam 4 · Q32</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q41\">Exam 4 · Q41</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Zones</strong> protect against a <strong>zonal failure</strong> (availability). They don't change the distance to users, so they don't fix latency for far-away users <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q41\">Exam 4 · Q41</a>.</li>\n<li>Capacity or configuration problems hit <strong>all users</strong>. A slowdown that hits only distant users points to latency <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q32\">Exam 4 · Q32</a>.</li>\n<li>Google designs <strong>purpose-built (custom) servers</strong> optimized for specific workloads, which makes them <strong>more efficient</strong> than off-the-shelf hardware. Google tunes hardware, networking, storage, power and cooling together for its scale <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q44\">Exam 4 · Q44</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Edge network and points of presence</strong> <em>(not in the question bank)</em>: Google's private global fiber network plus edge locations and Cloud CDN cache content close to users, lowering latency further.</li>\n<li><strong>Google's global network</strong> <em>(not in the question bank)</em>: traffic can enter Google's network close to the user and travel on Google's backbone rather than the public internet.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Latency vs bandwidth</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Symptom</th>\n<th>Cause</th>\n<th>Fix</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Far-away users see slow pages, but their speed tests are fast</td>\n<td>✅ Network latency <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q32\">Exam 4 · Q32</a></td>\n<td>Deploy closer: a region near them <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q41\">Exam 4 · Q41</a>, CDN</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Large transfers are slow for everyone</td>\n<td>Insufficient bandwidth</td>\n<td>More bandwidth / better connectivity</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Slow everywhere under load</td>\n<td>Too few or misconfigured servers</td>\n<td>Scale out / fix config</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Users in another continent + slow + good internet speed\" → <strong>latency</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q32\">Exam 4 · Q32</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Improve experience for users in country X\" → <strong>deploy in a region in country X</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q41\">Exam 4 · Q41</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Why purpose-built servers?\" → <strong>optimized for specific tasks, more efficient</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q44\">Exam 4 · Q44</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>More <strong>zones in the same region</strong> or more regions <strong>on the same continent</strong> don't help users far away <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q41\">Exam 4 · Q41</a>.</li>\n<li>A separate billing account changes nothing about performance. Billing accounts handle payment, invoicing and cost tracking, not where the app runs <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q41\">Exam 4 · Q41</a>.</li>\n<li>Custom servers don't run \"software that can't run elsewhere\". The same software runs elsewhere, just less efficiently at Google's scale <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q44\">Exam 4 · Q44</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Cheaper to build\", \"backward compatible with legacy disks\", \"run software that can't run elsewhere\" are wrong reasons for custom servers <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q44\">Exam 4 · Q44</a>.</li>\n<li>Don't pick \"insufficient bandwidth\" when the scenario says speed tests are fine <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q32\">Exam 4 · Q32</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"Google Cloud runs on a global network of regions and zones built with custom hardware. This lesson covers regions vs zones, the difference between latency and bandwidth, how to serve distant users, and why Google builds purpose-built servers.","objectives":["Explain regions and zones and what each protects against","Distinguish network latency from bandwidth from a user symptom","Choose where to deploy to reduce latency for distant users","State the benefit of Google's purpose-built servers"],"takeaways":["Far-away users with fast speed tests but slow pages have a latency problem, not a bandwidth problem.","Capacity or configuration problems hit all users; a slowdown only for distant users points to latency.","To serve users on another continent, deploy a replica in a region near them, with global load balancing sending users to the nearest backend.","Zones protect against zonal failures; more zones in the same region, or more regions on the same continent, don't help distant users.","Google's purpose-built servers are optimized for specific tasks, which makes them more efficient.","Edge locations and Cloud CDN bring content even closer to users."],"questions":["e4q32","e4q41","e4q44"]},{"id":"d1l7","num":7,"title":"Leading cloud adoption: culture, skills and sponsorship","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Moving from a few cost-saving migrations to a <strong>company-wide transformation</strong> needs <strong>visible top-down executive sponsorship</strong> and a <strong>blameless culture</strong> where teams can experiment and learn from failure <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q28\">Exam 5 · Q28</a>.</li>\n<li>Building cloud skills: create <strong>a culture of continuous peer-to-peer learning using official training materials</strong>. Learning is shared across teams, not done in isolation <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q2\">Exam 3 · Q2</a>.</li>\n<li>The right mix is to <strong>upskill existing staff</strong> while hiring where needed. Relying on contractors with elevated access is a security and knowledge risk <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q2\">Exam 3 · Q2</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Google Cloud Adoption Framework (CAF)</strong>: measures cloud maturity on four themes — <strong>Learn</strong> (upskilling staff, plus help from experienced partners), <strong>Lead</strong> (top-down sponsorship plus bottom-up, cross-functional teams), <strong>Scale</strong> (cloud-native, managed services and automation), <strong>Secure</strong> (identity-centric, multilayered security) — across three phases. The bank doesn't name the CAF, but its wrong options in <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q2\">Exam 3 · Q2</a> and <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q28\">Exam 5 · Q28</a> are the CAF's <strong>tactical</strong> behaviors and the keys are its <strong>transformational</strong> ones.</li>\n<li><strong>Tactical</strong>: individual workloads, no overall plan. Goal: cut costs fast with little disruption (short term).</li>\n<li><strong>Strategic</strong>: a broader vision guides workloads; people and processes start to change (mid term).</li>\n<li><strong>Transformational</strong>: IT becomes an <strong>engine of innovation and a partner to the business</strong> (long term).</li>\n<li><strong>Cross-functional teams and change management</strong> <em>(not in the question bank)</em>: transformation fails when it is treated as an IT-only project. Business, finance and security must be involved from the start (see also <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q33\">Exam 4 · Q33</a>).</li>\n<li>Sustainability and cost governance are often part of the business case (carbon-free energy goals, FinOps) <em>(not in the question bank; covered in Domain 6)</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Cloud Adoption Framework: Learn and Lead by phase</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Phase</th>\n<th>Learn (skills)</th>\n<th>Lead (sponsorship and teams)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Tactical</td>\n<td>Best-effort, self-motivated learning. Contractors do essential work with <strong>wide privileged access</strong>. No hiring of cloud-experienced staff <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q2\">Exam 3 · Q2</a></td>\n<td>Sponsorship from one line of business. Adoption <strong>driven by individual contributors</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q28\">Exam 5 · Q28</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Strategic</td>\n<td>Managed training program, certifications. Partners fill narrow gaps. <strong>Hire</strong> cloud-experienced people</td>\n<td>Sponsorship up to the C-level. A dedicated cross-functional team (Cloud Center of Excellence) drives adoption</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Transformational</td>\n<td>✅ <strong>Continuous, collaborative learning</strong>: formal training plus hackathons and tech talks. Contractors have no privileged access <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q2\">Exam 3 · Q2</a></td>\n<td>✅ Sponsorship across the <strong>whole C-level</strong>, a <strong>culture of blameless postmortems</strong>, autonomous cross-functional teams <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q28\">Exam 5 · Q28</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Make adoption company-wide / support innovation\" → <strong>top-down sponsorship + blameless culture</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q28\">Exam 5 · Q28</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Build long-term cloud skills, share knowledge\" → <strong>continuous peer-to-peer learning with official training</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q2\">Exam 3 · Q2</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Adoption driven primarily by individual contributors\" is the CAF's <strong>tactical</strong> phase: it doesn't scale across the company <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q28\">Exam 5 · Q28</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Move funding to CapEx\" and \"invest in on-prem infrastructure\" go backwards <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q28\">Exam 5 · Q28</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Self-directed, separate learning\" misses the <strong>sharing</strong> part: knowledge stays scattered. \"Train existing staff instead of hiring\" is the CAF's tactical stance; mature organizations upskill <strong>and</strong> hire. \"Give contractors elevated access\" builds dependency, not internal skills, and breaks least privilege <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q2\">Exam 3 · Q2</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"Technology alone does not transform a company. This lesson covers the leadership and culture side of cloud adoption: executive sponsorship, blameless culture, upskilling through shared learning, and how Google's Cloud Adoption Framework separates tactical from transformational behavior.","objectives":["Describe the leadership behaviors that turn small migrations into company-wide transformation","Choose the right approach to building cloud skills across teams","Outline the themes and phases of the Google Cloud Adoption Framework and place a behavior in the tactical, strategic or transformational phase"],"takeaways":["Company-wide adoption needs visible top-down sponsorship and a blameless culture.","Build skills through continuous peer-to-peer learning with official training materials.","Isolated self-study, contractors with privileged access, 'train instead of hiring' and adoption driven by individual contributors are tactical-phase behaviors.","The Cloud Adoption Framework themes are Learn, Lead, Scale and Secure, across tactical, strategic and transformational phases.","At the transformational phase, IT becomes an engine of innovation and a partner to the business."],"questions":["e3q2","e4q33","e5q28"]}],"ref":{"cheat":"<ul>\n<li>Cloud + analytics + automation to redesign the business = <strong>digital transformation</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q39\">Exam 2 · Q39</a>.</li>\n<li>Cloud adoption = <strong>chance to redesign processes and services</strong>, not only IT, not only cost <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q33\">Exam 4 · Q33</a>.</li>\n<li>Value over time = <strong>focus on solution development</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q29\">Exam 1 · Q29</a>. Developers get <strong>tools to innovate</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q31\">Exam 4 · Q31</a>.</li>\n<li>Customers expect <strong>any-device access</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q7\">Exam 2 · Q7</a> and <strong>always-on services</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q16\">Exam 4 · Q16</a>.</li>\n<li>On-prem scaling pain = <strong>cost of provisioning for peak</strong>, <strong>not enough servers</strong>, <strong>slow and costly to add capacity</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q3\">Exam 2 · Q3</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q15\">Exam 6 · Q15</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q35\">Exam 6 · Q35</a>.</li>\n<li>Holiday spikes → cloud lets you <strong>pay only for what you need, when you need it</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q29\">Exam 3 · Q29</a>.</li>\n<li>Public cloud = access <strong>not tied to one location</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q8\">Exam 1 · Q8</a>, <strong>cost-effective at scale</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q21\">Exam 4 · Q21</a>.</li>\n<li>Spending moves <strong>CapEx → OpEx</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q4\">Exam 2 · Q4</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q29\">Exam 4 · Q29</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q37\">Exam 4 · Q37</a>. Adapt to <strong>pay-as-you-go</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q32\">Exam 3 · Q32</a>.</li>\n<li>Cloud <strong>reduces on-prem infrastructure management costs</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q27\">Exam 4 · Q27</a>.</li>\n<li>Review TCO to <strong>evaluate ROI</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q49\">Exam 4 · Q49</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Hybrid</strong> = private/on-prem + public cloud. <strong>Multicloud</strong> = several public clouds <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q22\">Exam 3 · Q22</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>IaaS</strong> = rent VMs/storage/network <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q11\">Exam 6 · Q11</a>. <strong>PaaS</strong> = deploy your code, no servers <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q13\">Exam 6 · Q13</a>. <strong>SaaS</strong> = ready-made app <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q1\">Exam 2 · Q1</a>.</li>\n<li>SaaS customer owns <strong>content and data</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q22\">Exam 2 · Q22</a>.</li>\n<li>Far users slow, fast speed test → <strong>latency</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q32\">Exam 4 · Q32</a> → <strong>deploy in a region near them</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q41\">Exam 4 · Q41</a>.</li>\n<li>Google's <strong>purpose-built servers</strong> are <strong>optimized for specific tasks</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q44\">Exam 4 · Q44</a>.</li>\n<li>Company-wide adoption → <strong>top-down sponsorship + blameless culture</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q28\">Exam 5 · Q28</a>.</li>\n<li>Skills → <strong>continuous peer-to-peer learning with official training</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q2\">Exam 3 · Q2</a>.</li>\n<li>CAF: individual contributors, isolated self-study, contractors with privileged access = <strong>tactical</strong>. Blameless culture, C-level sponsorship, shared learning = <strong>transformational</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q2\">Exam 3 · Q2</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q28\">Exam 5 · Q28</a>.</li>\n<li>Cloud SLAs are 99.9%–99.99%, <strong>never 100%</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q8\">Exam 1 · Q8</a>. Cost management <strong>changes</strong> after migration: budgets, alerts, right-sizing <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q4\">Exam 2 · Q4</a>.</li>\n<li>Zones = availability, not latency. Global load balancing + Cloud CDN + a nearby region = lower latency <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q41\">Exam 4 · Q41</a>.</li>\n</ul>","rules":[{"lesson":"d1l1","title":"What digital transformation means","html":"<ul>\n<li>If the scenario combines several technologies to change <strong>how the business operates</strong>, the answer is <strong>digital transformation</strong>, not a narrower label <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q39\">Exam 2 · Q39</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Gain value over time\" from cloud → <strong>focus on solution development / innovation</strong>, not on maintaining infrastructure <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q29\">Exam 1 · Q29</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Why are customers asking for X?\" → pick the <strong>broad modern expectation</strong> (any device, always on), not a narrow technical reason <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q7\">Exam 2 · Q7</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q16\">Exam 4 · Q16</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d1l2","title":"Limits of on-premises infrastructure and why cloud helps","html":"<ul>\n<li>Scenario = \"own data center + traffic spikes + struggle/outage\" → the cause is <strong>fixed hardware capacity / cost of provisioning for peak</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q3\">Exam 2 · Q3</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q15\">Exam 6 · Q15</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q35\">Exam 6 · Q35</a>.</li>\n<li>Scenario = \"over-buy or under-buy for holidays\" → benefit is <strong>pay only for what you need, when you need it</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q29\">Exam 3 · Q29</a>.</li>\n<li>Public cloud benefit for a distributed company → <strong>access not limited to one physical location</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q8\">Exam 1 · Q8</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d1l3","title":"Cloud economics: CapEx, OpEx, TCO and pay-as-you-go","html":"<ul>\n<li>Any \"how does spending change when moving to cloud?\" question → <strong>CapEx → OpEx</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q4\">Exam 2 · Q4</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q29\">Exam 4 · Q29</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q37\">Exam 4 · Q37</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Why look at TCO before migration?\" → <strong>to evaluate ROI</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q49\">Exam 4 · Q49</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Effect on operations and personnel costs\" → <strong>reduced on-prem infrastructure management costs</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q27\">Exam 4 · Q27</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d1l4","title":"Cloud deployment models: public, private, hybrid, multicloud","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"Own data center + Google Cloud together\" → <strong>hybrid</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q22\">Exam 3 · Q22</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Several public cloud providers\" → <strong>multicloud</strong>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d1l5","title":"Service models: IaaS, PaaS, SaaS and shared responsibility","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"Rent VMs, storage, networking; build custom servers; pay per use\" → <strong>IaaS</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q11\">Exam 6 · Q11</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Deploy our own app, it should scale, we don't want to manage servers or OS\" → <strong>PaaS</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q13\">Exam 6 · Q13</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Use a vendor's ready-made app; no servers, no platform, no code\" → <strong>SaaS</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q1\">Exam 2 · Q1</a>.</li>\n<li>\"What is the SaaS customer exclusively responsible for?\" → <strong>customer-facing content</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q22\">Exam 2 · Q22</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d1l6","title":"Google Cloud infrastructure: regions, zones, latency and custom hardware","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"Users in another continent + slow + good internet speed\" → <strong>latency</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q32\">Exam 4 · Q32</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Improve experience for users in country X\" → <strong>deploy in a region in country X</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q41\">Exam 4 · Q41</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Why purpose-built servers?\" → <strong>optimized for specific tasks, more efficient</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q44\">Exam 4 · Q44</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d1l7","title":"Leading cloud adoption: culture, skills and sponsorship","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"Make adoption company-wide / support innovation\" → <strong>top-down sponsorship + blameless culture</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q28\">Exam 5 · Q28</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Build long-term cloud skills, share knowledge\" → <strong>continuous peer-to-peer learning with official training</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q2\">Exam 3 · Q2</a>.</li>\n</ul>"}],"traps":[{"lesson":"d1l1","title":"What digital transformation means","html":"<ul>\n<li>Words like \"only\", \"exclusively\", \"never\" in options are usually wrong at this level <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q7\">Exam 2 · Q7</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q33\">Exam 4 · Q33</a>.</li>\n<li>A mobile app is not more secure by itself. Security comes from identity and access controls, encryption and secure design across the whole system <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q7\">Exam 2 · Q7</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Always-on\" is about <strong>when</strong> a service is available (time of day). \"Regional access\" is about <strong>where</strong> (geography, language, local markets). \"Personalization\" is about tailoring to each user <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q16\">Exam 4 · Q16</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Expand internal hosting infrastructure\" or \"relax on-prem security\" are never the cloud benefit <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q29\">Exam 1 · Q29</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Avoid the limitations of serverless\" or \"optimize on-prem maintenance\" don't describe moving to cloud <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q31\">Exam 4 · Q31</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d1l2","title":"Limits of on-premises infrastructure and why cloud helps","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Serverless and multicloud distractors</strong>: if the company runs on its own servers, options blaming \"serverless functions that can't scale\" or \"multi-cloud complexity\" don't fit the scenario <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q3\">Exam 2 · Q3</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q15\">Exam 6 · Q15</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q35\">Exam 6 · Q35</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Overreliance on PaaS\", \"perimeter security makes data inaccessible\", \"inconsistent customer data\", \"compliance blocks scaling\" are all off-topic <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q3\">Exam 2 · Q3</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q15\">Exam 6 · Q15</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q35\">Exam 6 · Q35</a>.</li>\n<li>PaaS is adopted to <strong>reduce</strong> operational burden, so it can't be the cause of on-prem scaling pain <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q3\">Exam 2 · Q3</a>.</li>\n<li>Bad data quality causes wrong reports and logic errors, <strong>not outages at peak traffic</strong>. Failures that happen only at peak point to a <strong>capacity</strong> limit <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q15\">Exam 6 · Q15</a>.</li>\n<li>Adding capacity doesn't automatically breach compliance. Compliance depends on how systems are designed, secured and governed <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q35\">Exam 6 · Q35</a>.</li>\n<li>Cloud does <strong>not</strong> guarantee 100% availability, does not remove the need for security, and doesn't mean \"unlimited storage = no downtime\" <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q8\">Exam 1 · Q8</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q29\">Exam 3 · Q29</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q31\">Exam 4 · Q31</a>.</li>\n<li>Moving to cloud is never a reason to <strong>relax security protocols</strong>. Under shared responsibility the customer still secures identities, access, data and configuration <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q29\">Exam 1 · Q29</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q8\">Exam 1 · Q8</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Schedule physical server repairs after hours\" is your problem on-prem, not a cloud benefit <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q8\">Exam 1 · Q8</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d1l3","title":"Cloud economics: CapEx, OpEx, TCO and pay-as-you-go","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>OpEx → CapEx</strong> is always backwards <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q4\">Exam 2 · Q4</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q29\">Exam 4 · Q29</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q28\">Exam 5 · Q28</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Cost management stays the same, only TCO changes\" is wrong. The spending model itself changes <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q4\">Exam 2 · Q4</a>.</li>\n<li>Error budget, SLA availability and compute sizing are not why you review TCO <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q49\">Exam 4 · Q49</a>:</li>\n<li><strong>Error budget</strong>: an SRE idea. With an SLO (service level objective), it is how much unreliability is acceptable before teams must prioritize reliability work.</li>\n<li><strong>Service level availability</strong>: a reliability target set in SLAs and SLOs, not a financial comparison.</li>\n<li><strong>Compute sizing</strong>: technical capacity planning. It feeds cost estimates, but TCO is much broader.</li>\n<li>\"Increased hardware maintenance\" or \"increased cloud hardware management costs\" for the customer is wrong. The provider manages the hardware <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q27\">Exam 4 · Q27</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q37\">Exam 4 · Q37</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Reduced cloud software licensing costs\" sounds plausible but isn't the point. Licensing may change with the vendor agreement and architecture, but it doesn't automatically go down; the clear saving is on-prem management <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q27\">Exam 4 · Q27</a>.</li>\n<li>\"A shift toward structured data\" has nothing to do with migration economics. Cloud supports structured, semi-structured (JSON) and unstructured data (images, video, logs) alike <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q37\">Exam 4 · Q37</a>.</li>\n<li>\"No change to existing responsibilities\" is wrong. Infrastructure duties move to the provider; the customer keeps application design, access control, data protection and configuration <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q37\">Exam 4 · Q37</a>.</li>\n<li>OpEx does not make TCO automatically \"more predictable\". Usage-based bills can vary <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q29\">Exam 3 · Q29</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d1l4","title":"Cloud deployment models: public, private, hybrid, multicloud","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"A combination of multiple public cloud providers\" is <strong>multicloud</strong>, not hybrid <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q22\">Exam 3 · Q22</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Serverless + public cloud\" and \"different virtualized servers\" are not deployment models <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q22\">Exam 3 · Q22</a>. Serverless runs on the provider's public cloud, so that option has no private side. Virtual machines can run anywhere, so virtualization alone doesn't make an architecture hybrid.</li>\n<li>\"Hybrid cloud\" also shows up as a distractor in service-model questions. It is a deployment model, not IaaS/PaaS/SaaS <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q11\">Exam 6 · Q11</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d1l5","title":"Service models: IaaS, PaaS, SaaS and shared responsibility","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"Network as a service\" and \"Functions as a service\" are distractors <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q13\">Exam 6 · Q13</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q1\">Exam 2 · Q1</a>.</li>\n<li>In SaaS, keeping the platform running and monitoring servers and networks are the <strong>provider's</strong> job <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q22\">Exam 2 · Q22</a>. The customer may still handle its own local network connectivity, allowed settings and who gets access <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q22\">Exam 2 · Q22</a>.</li>\n<li>Shared responsibility is about <strong>who secures and operates what</strong>. It doesn't give you \"visibility during traffic spikes\"; that comes from monitoring and logging tools <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q29\">Exam 3 · Q29</a>.</li>\n<li>The customer does not get paid compensation for outages as a \"responsibility\", and does not manage Google's network or storage hardware <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q32\">Exam 3 · Q32</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d1l6","title":"Google Cloud infrastructure: regions, zones, latency and custom hardware","html":"<ul>\n<li>More <strong>zones in the same region</strong> or more regions <strong>on the same continent</strong> don't help users far away <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q41\">Exam 4 · Q41</a>.</li>\n<li>A separate billing account changes nothing about performance. Billing accounts handle payment, invoicing and cost tracking, not where the app runs <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q41\">Exam 4 · Q41</a>.</li>\n<li>Custom servers don't run \"software that can't run elsewhere\". The same software runs elsewhere, just less efficiently at Google's scale <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q44\">Exam 4 · Q44</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Cheaper to build\", \"backward compatible with legacy disks\", \"run software that can't run elsewhere\" are wrong reasons for custom servers <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q44\">Exam 4 · Q44</a>.</li>\n<li>Don't pick \"insufficient bandwidth\" when the scenario says speed tests are fine <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q32\">Exam 4 · Q32</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d1l7","title":"Leading cloud adoption: culture, skills and sponsorship","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"Adoption driven primarily by individual contributors\" is the CAF's <strong>tactical</strong> phase: it doesn't scale across the company <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q28\">Exam 5 · Q28</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Move funding to CapEx\" and \"invest in on-prem infrastructure\" go backwards <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q28\">Exam 5 · Q28</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Self-directed, separate learning\" misses the <strong>sharing</strong> part: knowledge stays scattered. \"Train existing staff instead of hiring\" is the CAF's tactical stance; mature organizations upskill <strong>and</strong> hire. \"Give contractors elevated access\" builds dependency, not internal skills, and breaks least privilege <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q2\">Exam 3 · Q2</a>.</li>\n</ul>"}],"questionable":"<p>No open items. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q29\">Exam 3 · Q29</a> was listed here for its loose \"agile infrastructure\" wording. The dump's explanation reads \"agile\" as provisioning, scaling and releasing resources on demand, which makes the keyed answer sound; see lesson 2.</p>"},"glossary":[{"term":"Agile infrastructure","definition":"Infrastructure you can provision, scale and release on demand instead of buying it in advance for peak.","exam_context":"With consumption pricing, it answers holiday-spike scenarios: pay only for what you need, when you need it.","lesson":"d1l2","domain":1},{"term":"Always-on services","definition":"Services and support available whenever customers need them, not only during business hours.","exam_context":"Why a company extends support into evenings and weekends. It is about time, not region or personalization.","lesson":"d1l1","domain":1},{"term":"App Engine","definition":"Google Cloud's fully managed, serverless application platform where Google handles provisioning and scaling.","exam_context":"The classic PaaS example: deploy a custom app without managing servers or the OS.","lesson":"d1l5","domain":1},{"term":"Bandwidth","definition":"The amount of data a network connection can carry per second.","exam_context":"Users with fast speed tests still see slow pages from a distant region. That is latency, not bandwidth.","lesson":"d1l6","domain":1},{"term":"Blameless culture","definition":"A culture where incidents and failures are reviewed to learn and improve, not to assign personal blame.","exam_context":"Paired with top-down sponsorship as the way to make cloud adoption company-wide.","lesson":"d1l7","domain":1},{"term":"Capital expenditure (CapEx)","definition":"Upfront spending on long-lived physical assets such as servers and data centers.","exam_context":"Cloud migration moves spending away from CapEx. Any option moving toward CapEx is wrong.","lesson":"d1l3","domain":1},{"term":"Cloud Adoption Framework","definition":"Google's model for measuring cloud maturity on four themes (Learn, Lead, Scale, Secure) across three phases (tactical, strategic, transformational).","exam_context":"Not named in the bank, but its tactical behaviors are the wrong options and its transformational behaviors the keys in skills and leadership questions.","lesson":"d1l7","domain":1},{"term":"Cloud CDN","definition":"Google Cloud's content delivery network, which caches content at edge locations close to users.","exam_context":"Together with global load balancing and nearby regions, it lowers latency for distant users.","lesson":"d1l6","domain":1},{"term":"Cloud Center of Excellence (CCoE)","definition":"A dedicated cross-functional team that drives cloud adoption across project boundaries.","exam_context":"A strategic-phase Lead practice in the Cloud Adoption Framework.","lesson":"d1l7","domain":1},{"term":"Cloud maturity phases","definition":"The Cloud Adoption Framework's three stages: tactical (quick cost wins, no overall plan), strategic (broader vision, people and processes change) and transformational (IT drives innovation).","exam_context":"Adoption driven by individual contributors is tactical; company-wide sponsorship and blameless culture are transformational.","lesson":"d1l7","domain":1},{"term":"Consumption-based pricing","definition":"Paying for cloud resources according to what you actually use, also called pay-as-you-go.","exam_context":"After migrating, the customer adapts to this spending model; it solves over- and under-buying for peaks.","lesson":"d1l3","domain":1},{"term":"Data center migration","definition":"Moving workloads, applications and data from an existing data center to another environment such as Google Cloud.","exam_context":"Can be one step of digital transformation, but it is narrower and doesn't redesign processes by itself.","lesson":"d1l1","domain":1},{"term":"Digital transformation","definition":"Using cloud, data, analytics, automation and AI to redesign business processes, collaboration and customer experiences.","exam_context":"The label for a scenario that combines several technologies to change how the business works.","lesson":"d1l1","domain":1},{"term":"Elasticity","definition":"The ability to add and remove capacity automatically as demand changes.","exam_context":"Answers holiday-spike scenarios where on-prem companies over-buy or run out of servers.","lesson":"d1l2","domain":1},{"term":"Error budget","definition":"The amount of unreliability a service may have under its service level objective (SLO) before teams must prioritize reliability work.","exam_context":"An SRE reliability idea, used as a distractor for why you review TCO.","lesson":"d1l3","domain":1},{"term":"Executive sponsorship","definition":"Visible, top-down support for a transformation from senior leadership.","exam_context":"Needed to move from a few cost-saving migrations to company-wide transformation.","lesson":"d1l7","domain":1},{"term":"Functions as a service (FaaS)","definition":"A serverless model where you write small event-driven functions and define the triggers; the provider runs and scales them.","exam_context":"You still write code, so FaaS is wrong when the customer wants a ready-made app.","lesson":"d1l5","domain":1},{"term":"Global load balancing","definition":"Load balancing that gives one global entry point and sends each user to the nearest healthy backend across regions.","exam_context":"Pairs with deploying replicas in regions near users to cut latency.","lesson":"d1l6","domain":1},{"term":"Hybrid cloud","definition":"An architecture that combines private infrastructure (on-premises data center, private cloud or edge) with public cloud services.","exam_context":"Keep sensitive systems on-prem and use Google Cloud for analytics and scaling. Not the same as multicloud.","lesson":"d1l4","domain":1},{"term":"Infrastructure as a service (IaaS)","definition":"A service model where you rent virtual machines, storage and networking and manage the OS and software yourself.","exam_context":"Rent VMs and build customized servers, paying per use. Example: Compute Engine.","lesson":"d1l5","domain":1},{"term":"Multicloud","definition":"Using services from two or more public cloud providers.","exam_context":"The 'multiple public cloud providers' option is multicloud, the distractor in hybrid questions.","lesson":"d1l4","domain":1},{"term":"Network as a service (NaaS)","definition":"Managed networking capabilities such as connectivity, routing and network security functions, delivered as a service.","exam_context":"A distractor in service-model questions; it doesn't host your application.","lesson":"d1l5","domain":1},{"term":"Network latency","definition":"The delay for data to travel between a user and a server, mostly driven by distance.","exam_context":"Distant users with high-speed internet but slow loading pages. Fix by deploying closer.","lesson":"d1l6","domain":1},{"term":"On-premises","definition":"Infrastructure owned and operated by the company in its own data center.","exam_context":"Fixed capacity makes scaling slow and costly and forces provisioning for peak.","lesson":"d1l2","domain":1},{"term":"Operational expenditure (OpEx)","definition":"Ongoing spending on services as they are consumed, such as a monthly cloud bill.","exam_context":"Moving to cloud shifts spending toward OpEx.","lesson":"d1l3","domain":1},{"term":"Platform as a service (PaaS)","definition":"A service model where you deploy your own code and the provider manages servers, OS and scaling.","exam_context":"Custom app that must scale, with no servers or OS to manage. Examples: App Engine, Cloud Run.","lesson":"d1l5","domain":1},{"term":"Private cloud","definition":"Cloud-style infrastructure dedicated to a single organization, usually in its own data center.","exam_context":"Combined with public cloud it forms a hybrid cloud.","lesson":"d1l4","domain":1},{"term":"Public cloud","definition":"Shared computing infrastructure owned and run by a provider and rented on demand over the internet.","exam_context":"Benefits: access not tied to one location, cost-effective at scale, pay for what you use.","lesson":"d1l4","domain":1},{"term":"Purpose-built servers","definition":"Custom server hardware designed by Google for specific workloads in its data centers.","exam_context":"Their benefit is efficiency from being optimized for specific tasks, not lower build cost.","lesson":"d1l6","domain":1},{"term":"Region","definition":"An independent geographic area where Google Cloud resources are hosted, made up of several zones.","exam_context":"Deploy a replica in a region near distant users to reduce latency.","lesson":"d1l6","domain":1},{"term":"Return on investment (ROI)","definition":"The value gained from an investment compared with its cost.","exam_context":"Reviewing TCO before migration lets leadership evaluate the ROI of moving to cloud.","lesson":"d1l3","domain":1},{"term":"Serverless computing","definition":"A cloud model where the provider runs and scales the infrastructure automatically and you deploy only code or containers. Examples: Cloud Run, Cloud Run functions.","exam_context":"Serverless scales on demand, so 'a serverless function can't scale' is never the cause in on-prem scenarios.","lesson":"d1l2","domain":1},{"term":"Service level agreement (SLA)","definition":"A provider's published commitment to a service's availability, such as 99.9% or 99.99%, with credits if it is missed.","exam_context":"No cloud service promises 100% availability; uptime also depends on your own architecture.","lesson":"d1l2","domain":1},{"term":"Shared fate","definition":"Google's extension of shared responsibility, where it also provides secure defaults, blueprints and guidance to help customers secure their part.","exam_context":"Context for shared responsibility; covered in more depth in the security domain.","lesson":"d1l5","domain":1},{"term":"Shared responsibility model","definition":"The division of security and operational duties between the cloud provider and the customer, which depends on the service model.","exam_context":"In SaaS, the customer is responsible for its own content and data; the provider runs the platform and infrastructure.","lesson":"d1l5","domain":1},{"term":"Software as a service (SaaS)","definition":"A service model where the provider delivers a complete application over the internet.","exam_context":"Ready-made payroll or content apps with no servers, platform or code to manage. Example: Google Workspace.","lesson":"d1l5","domain":1},{"term":"Total cost of ownership (TCO)","definition":"All costs of a solution over its life: hardware, software licenses, facilities, power, staff, maintenance, downtime and, for a move, migration effort.","exam_context":"Compare on-prem vs cloud TCO before migration to evaluate ROI.","lesson":"d1l3","domain":1},{"term":"Zone","definition":"An isolated deployment area within a region. Spreading an app across zones protects against a zonal failure.","exam_context":"More zones in the same region improve availability, not latency for users on another continent.","lesson":"d1l6","domain":1}]},{"n":2,"title":"Exploring Data Transformation with Google Cloud","short":"Data Transformation","weight":"~16%","desc":"The data value chain, structured vs unstructured data, databases and storage options, BigQuery, Looker and data governance.","lessons":[{"id":"d2l1","num":1,"title":"Why data matters: the data value chain and cloud data benefits","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The data value chain (also called the data supply chain)</strong> is the full journey of data: <strong>collection/ingestion → processing → storage → analysis/use</strong> (reporting, analytics, AI). When a question asks for \"the end-to-end journey from creation to business decisions\" or \"how data is collected, transformed and used\", the answer is <strong>data supply chain</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q35\">Exam 1 · Q35</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q33\">Exam 3 · Q33</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Processing</strong> is the step that turns raw data into a <strong>clean, structured, usable format</strong>: cleaning, transforming, aggregating, filtering and validating. Collection gathers it, storage keeps it, analysis finds trends in it <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q1\">Exam 1 · Q1</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Collection</strong> is best when it is <strong>automatic and real time</strong>. Wearable <strong>IoT devices</strong> that upload readings continuously beat manual entry, nightly uploads or clinic visits <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q21\">Exam 2 · Q21</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Digitizing a process</strong> creates a record of every event. Moving from cash to a cloud-connected payment system lets a company <strong>view data history and see each transaction</strong>, which is how it finds mismatches (<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q3\">Exam 1 · Q3</a>, see ⚠️).</li>\n<li><strong>Breaking down silos</strong> is the main benefit of a unified cloud data platform. Data that sits in one place can be combined and used for innovation. It does not make data automatically cheaper, threat-proof or impossible to lose <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q9\">Exam 5 · Q9</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Transactional data in the cloud</strong> supports <strong>accurate business transactions at scale</strong>, including sudden spikes such as holiday bookings, without anyone touching physical servers <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q39\">Exam 6 · Q39</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Value chain steps</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Step</th>\n<th>What happens</th>\n<th>Exam phrasing</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Collection / ingestion</td>\n<td>Data is captured from apps, devices, partners</td>\n<td>\"IoT devices upload in real time\" <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q21\">Exam 2 · Q21</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Processing</td>\n<td>Raw data is cleaned, standardized, structured</td>\n<td>\"converting raw information into a cleaner, structured format\" <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q1\">Exam 1 · Q1</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Storage</td>\n<td>Data is kept in a database, lake or warehouse</td>\n<td>see lessons 5–7</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Analysis / use</td>\n<td>Reports, dashboards, ML models, AI</td>\n<td>\"managers discover business trends\"</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"End-to-end lifecycle of data, creation to decisions\" → <strong>data supply chain</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q35\">Exam 1 · Q35</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q33\">Exam 3 · Q33</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Making raw data clean and structured\" → <strong>processing</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q1\">Exam 1 · Q1</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Collect automatically, no manual entry\" → <strong>IoT, real-time upload</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q21\">Exam 2 · Q21</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Benefit of a unified data solution\" → <strong>data is no longer in silos, so it can drive innovation</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q9\">Exam 5 · Q9</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Absolute promises (\"always cheaper\", \"cannot be lost\", \"protected from all threats\") are always wrong <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q9\">Exam 5 · Q9</a>.</li>\n<li>Distractors from other domains (error budget, perimeter security, resource hierarchy, IaaS, multicloud networking) show up as filler. They don't describe data flow <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q3\">Exam 1 · Q3</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q35\">Exam 1 · Q35</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q33\">Exam 3 · Q33</a>.</li>\n<li>Options that mention on-premises hardware or physical server access are never the cloud benefit <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q39\">Exam 6 · Q39</a>.</li>\n<li>Unifying data does not remove the customer's security work. Security is shared responsibility: you still configure access, governance and backups <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q9\">Exam 5 · Q9</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"Data creates value as it moves through a chain: collection, processing, storage and analysis. This lesson covers that data value (supply) chain and the basic business benefits of putting data in the cloud: digital records, real-time collection, no silos and transactions at scale.","objectives":["Name the steps of the data value chain and identify which step a scenario describes","Recognize 'data supply chain' as the term for the end-to-end data lifecycle","Explain why unified cloud data beats siloed data","Spot absolute or off-topic distractors in cloud-data benefit questions"],"takeaways":["End-to-end journey from creation to decisions is the data supply chain.","Turning raw data into a clean, structured format is processing.","Automatic, real-time collection points to IoT devices uploading data.","Unified cloud data supports innovation because it is no longer in silos.","Cloud transactional data supports accurate transactions at scale, even during spikes.","Options promising 'always cheaper' or 'cannot be lost' are always wrong."],"questions":["e1q1","e1q3","e1q35","e2q21","e3q33","e5q9","e6q39"]},{"id":"d2l2","num":2,"title":"AI-ready data, governance and openness","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>AI-ready data</strong> is data that is <strong>unified, clean, trusted, governed and discoverable</strong>, so it can feed AI and ML reliably. Fragmented, inconsistent, duplicated, poorly governed data is the opposite <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q34\">Exam 1 · Q34</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q38\">Exam 3 · Q38</a>.</li>\n<li>Building that state is described as an <strong>AI-ready data foundation</strong> or <strong>AI-ready data platform</strong>. It is the foundational step before generative AI or AI agents <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q38\">Exam 3 · Q38</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q49\">Exam 5 · Q49</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>AI-ready data cloud</strong> is how the exam names the Google Cloud differentiator for combining first-party, partner and external data in one place so data scientists can train models <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q48\">Exam 5 · Q48</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Data governance</strong> is a program of policies, roles and controls for data quality, access, lifecycle and compliance. It is the answer when a company wants <strong>more value, controlled costs and regulatory compliance</strong> together <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q8\">Exam 5 · Q8</a>.</li>\n<li>What it covers: ownership, classification, access control, retention rules, quality standards, lineage, auditing and privacy. Value comes from trusted, findable data. Cost control comes from finding unused or badly stored data. Compliance comes from retention, access and audit rules <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q8\">Exam 5 · Q8</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Openness and interoperability</strong> is a Google Cloud differentiator: open standards, open source and multicloud tools (BigQuery Omni, Looker, AlloyDB Omni) so data platforms work together and you avoid <strong>vendor lock-in</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q37\">Exam 5 · Q37</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Data is fragmented, inconsistent or ungoverned, and the goal is AI → <strong>AI-ready data / data foundation / data platform</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q34\">Exam 1 · Q34</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q38\">Exam 3 · Q38</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q49\">Exam 5 · Q49</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Combine own, partner and market data to train models\" → <strong>AI-ready data cloud</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q48\">Exam 5 · Q48</a>.</li>\n<li>Value + cost + compliance in one sentence → <strong>data governance program</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q8\">Exam 5 · Q8</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Work across clouds, avoid lock-in\" → <strong>openness and interoperability</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q37\">Exam 5 · Q37</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>One-off actions (delete old data, quarterly access reports, move everything to NoSQL) are narrower than a governance program. A blanket \"delete after 18 months\" rule can even break financial retention laws, and reports only show what already happened <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q8\">Exam 5 · Q8</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Shift OpEx to CapEx\" is backwards. Cloud moves spend <strong>from CapEx to OpEx</strong>, so that option is always wrong <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q37\">Exam 5 · Q37</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q49\">Exam 5 · Q49</a>.</li>\n<li>Coldline, ephemeral or on-premises data are not quality states <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q34\">Exam 1 · Q34</a>. Restricting data to on-premises works against AI readiness <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q49\">Exam 5 · Q49</a>.</li>\n<li>Other differentiators are filler in data questions: <strong>AI Hypercomputer</strong> (GPU/TPU infrastructure for AI), <strong>Bare Metal Solution</strong> (dedicated physical servers), the <strong>global edge network</strong> (fast delivery to users) and <strong>2-Step Verification</strong> (sign-in security) <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q37\">Exam 5 · Q37</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q48\">Exam 5 · Q48</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Knowledge Catalog</strong> (formerly Dataplex Universal Catalog, renamed in April 2026) is Google Cloud's data catalog for governance: discovery, metadata, lineage, data quality checks and business glossaries across lakes and warehouses. It describes data; it doesn't store files <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q12\">Exam 2 · Q12</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"AI is only as good as its data. This lesson covers AI-ready data (unified, clean, trusted, governed, discoverable), what a data governance program includes, Knowledge Catalog for governing data, and Google Cloud's openness and interoperability differentiator.","objectives":["Define AI-ready data and recognize it as the foundation for generative AI and agents","Explain what a data governance program achieves for value, cost and compliance","List what a governance program covers: ownership, classification, access, retention, quality, lineage and auditing","Connect openness and interoperability with avoiding vendor lock-in","Recognize Knowledge Catalog (formerly Dataplex Universal Catalog) as the governance catalog, not a storage service"],"takeaways":["Fragmented, inconsistent, ungoverned data plus an AI goal means building AI-ready data.","Combining own, partner and external data to train models is the AI-ready data cloud.","Value, cost control and compliance together point to a data governance program.","One-off actions such as blanket deletion or quarterly reports are only pieces of governance.","Avoiding lock-in across clouds is openness and interoperability.","Cloud shifts spend from CapEx to OpEx, never the reverse.","Knowledge Catalog describes, governs and checks data quality; it does not store files."],"questions":["e1q34","e2q12","e3q38","e5q8","e5q37","e5q48","e5q49"]},{"id":"d2l3","num":3,"title":"Types of data: structure and source","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Structured data</strong> fits fixed fields, rows and columns. It is easy to search, filter and analyze in tables. Examples: blood pressure history, stock levels, orders, revenue, GPS coordinates, star ratings <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q40\">Exam 6 · Q40</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q30\">Exam 4 · Q30</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Semi-structured data</strong> has no fixed relational schema but carries <strong>tags, keys, nested fields or hierarchy</strong> that give it organization. Examples: <strong>JSON, XML</strong>, HTML <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q19\">Exam 1 · Q19</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Unstructured data</strong> has no predefined model. Examples: <strong>free-text feedback, call transcripts, audio, video, images, scans, handwritten notes</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q22\">Exam 1 · Q22</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q6\">Exam 3 · Q6</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q8\">Exam 6 · Q8</a>.</li>\n<li>Only structured raw data goes into a <strong>relational database without extra processing</strong>. Images, videos and call recordings do not <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q30\">Exam 4 · Q30</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Metadata about a media file is structured; the content is not.</strong> The usual design keeps the file in Cloud Storage and its metadata (product ID, duration, file path) in a database <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q6\">Exam 3 · Q6</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q30\">Exam 4 · Q30</a>.</li>\n<li>Unstructured data still has value. Natural language processing, sentiment analysis, OCR and image analysis turn text, handwriting and images into usable insight. A transcribed call can become structured fields <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q22\">Exam 1 · Q22</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q40\">Exam 6 · Q40</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q30\">Exam 4 · Q30</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>First-party data</strong>: collected <strong>directly</strong> by the company from its own customers (website clicks, orders, loyalty forms) <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q38\">Exam 1 · Q38</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Second-party data</strong>: another company's first-party data, <strong>shared directly</strong> with you by a partner <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q5\">Exam 3 · Q5</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Third-party data</strong>: bought from an <strong>outside aggregator</strong> that has no direct relationship with your customers <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q39\">Exam 1 · Q39</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Structure</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Shape</th>\n<th>Examples in the bank</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Structured</td>\n<td>Rows and columns, fixed schema</td>\n<td>Blood pressure history, stock levels</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q40\">Exam 6 · Q40</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q30\">Exam 4 · Q30</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Semi-structured</td>\n<td>Tags, keys, nesting</td>\n<td>JSON and XML logs and config</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q19\">Exam 1 · Q19</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Unstructured</td>\n<td>No schema</td>\n<td>Written feedback, call transcripts, audio, video</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q22\">Exam 1 · Q22</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q6\">Exam 3 · Q6</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q8\">Exam 6 · Q8</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Source</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Who collected it</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>First-party</td>\n<td>You, from your customers</td>\n<td>Clicks, orders, loyalty sign-ups</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q38\">Exam 1 · Q38</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Second-party</td>\n<td>A partner, shared directly</td>\n<td>Senior-living network shares resident data</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q5\">Exam 3 · Q5</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Third-party</td>\n<td>An aggregator with no link to your customers</td>\n<td>Purchased audience segments</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q39\">Exam 1 · Q39</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Free text, speech, images or video → <strong>unstructured</strong>, even if the text is a \"transcript\" <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q8\">Exam 6 · Q8</a>.</li>\n<li>JSON / XML / \"tags and attributes\" → <strong>semi-structured</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q19\">Exam 1 · Q19</a>.</li>\n<li>A number or field you could put in a table column → <strong>structured</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q40\">Exam 6 · Q40</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Directly from our own customers\" → first-party. \"Partner shares its data with us\" → second-party. \"Bought from a provider\" → third-party <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q38\">Exam 1 · Q38</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q5\">Exam 3 · Q5</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q39\">Exam 1 · Q39</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>In an \"unstructured\" question, numeric options like revenue trends, stock counts, GPS coordinates or star ratings are all structured <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q22\">Exam 1 · Q22</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q8\">Exam 6 · Q8</a>.</li>\n<li>Audio and video stored <strong>next to</strong> HTML and JSON are still unstructured. The JSON is the semi-structured part <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q6\">Exam 3 · Q6</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Relational data\" and \"open standard data\" or \"public domain data\" are not categories the exam uses for these questions <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q19\">Exam 1 · Q19</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q38\">Exam 1 · Q38</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q5\">Exam 3 · Q5</a>.</li>\n<li>Purchase orders stored as JSON are still predictable records (structured or semi-structured), never the \"unstructured\" answer <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q8\">Exam 6 · Q8</a>.</li>\n<li>Structure and source are separate axes. \"Unstructured data\" is never the answer to a first/second/third-party question <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q39\">Exam 1 · Q39</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"The exam classifies data two ways: by structure (structured, semi-structured, unstructured) and by source (first-, second- and third-party). This lesson shows how to place any example in the right bucket, and how media files and their metadata are stored and analyzed.","objectives":["Classify examples as structured, semi-structured or unstructured","Recognize JSON and XML as semi-structured","Distinguish first-, second- and third-party data by who collected it","Separate a media file (unstructured) from its metadata (structured) and know where each is stored"],"takeaways":["Table fields and numbers are structured; JSON and XML are semi-structured.","Free text, transcripts, audio, video and images are unstructured.","Only structured raw data fits a relational database without processing.","Metadata about a video is structured; the video itself is unstructured and belongs in Cloud Storage.","NLP, sentiment analysis and OCR turn unstructured text and images into insight.","First-party is collected by you, second-party is shared by a partner, third-party is bought from an aggregator."],"questions":["e1q19","e1q22","e1q38","e1q39","e3q5","e3q6","e4q30","e6q8","e6q40"]},{"id":"d2l4","num":4,"title":"Data quality dimensions","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Poor data quality leads to weak ML models and bad reports. The exam describes a symptom and asks which <strong>dimension</strong> is broken.</li>\n<li><strong>Completeness</strong>: required fields or attributes are <strong>missing</strong> (no date of birth, no age group or location) <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q16\">Exam 3 · Q16</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q46\">Exam 3 · Q46</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Accuracy</strong>: the stored value <strong>matches the real-world truth</strong> (order total equals the invoice) <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q16\">Exam 6 · Q16</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Validity</strong>: the value <strong>follows the expected format, rule, range or list of allowed values</strong> (an email must contain \"@\"; an age of 250 or a negative price is invalid) <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q28\">Exam 6 · Q28</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q46\">Exam 3 · Q46</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q42\">Exam 4 · Q42</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Uniqueness</strong>: each real thing is recorded <strong>once</strong>. Duplicates such as one scooter under two asset codes break it <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q29\">Exam 6 · Q29</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Timeliness</strong> (also called <strong>freshness</strong>): data is <strong>current enough</strong> and available when needed. A fashion model trained on three-year-old data fails <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q42\">Exam 4 · Q42</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Consistency</strong>: the same fact agrees across systems (one system says a jacket is in stock, another says discontinued). It appears only as a distractor in the bank <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q42\">Exam 4 · Q42</a>.</li>\n<li>For ML, quality gaps hurt the model directly: missing fields weaken or bias it, duplicates make it over-count, wrong values teach it false patterns <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q46\">Exam 3 · Q46</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q29\">Exam 6 · Q29</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q16\">Exam 6 · Q16</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Symptom → dimension</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Symptom</th>\n<th>Dimension</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Missing attributes or empty required fields</td>\n<td>Completeness</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q16\">Exam 3 · Q16</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q46\">Exam 3 · Q46</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Value differs from the source of truth</td>\n<td>Accuracy</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q16\">Exam 6 · Q16</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Value breaks a format rule</td>\n<td>Validity</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q28\">Exam 6 · Q28</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Same entity stored more than once</td>\n<td>Uniqueness</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q29\">Exam 6 · Q29</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Data is old for a fast-changing domain</td>\n<td>Timeliness</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q42\">Exam 4 · Q42</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Two systems disagree on the same fact</td>\n<td>Consistency</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q42\">Exam 4 · Q42</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Ask \"what is wrong with the value?\" Missing → completeness. Wrong → accuracy. Wrong shape → validity. Doubled → uniqueness. Stale → timeliness.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Validity vs accuracy: an email without \"@\" is <strong>invalid</strong> regardless of whether it belongs to the person. A well-formed total that doesn't match the invoice is <strong>inaccurate</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q28\">Exam 6 · Q28</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q16\">Exam 6 · Q16</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Explainability\" is a responsible-AI idea, not a data quality dimension <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q29\">Exam 6 · Q29</a>.</li>\n<li>Duplicates can each look valid. Two well-formed asset codes for one scooter is a <strong>uniqueness</strong> problem, not validity <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q29\">Exam 6 · Q29</a>.</li>\n<li>Old data can be accurate, valid and unique and still fail <strong>timeliness</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q42\">Exam 4 · Q42</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"Weak models and bad reports often come from poor data quality. This lesson maps common symptoms to the data quality dimensions the exam tests: completeness, accuracy, validity, uniqueness, timeliness and consistency, and shows how each one hurts an ML model.","objectives":["Match a data-quality symptom to its dimension","Tell validity apart from accuracy and uniqueness","Recognize stale data as a timeliness (freshness) problem","Explain how quality gaps weaken ML models"],"takeaways":["Missing attributes break completeness.","A value that differs from the source of truth breaks accuracy.","A value that fails a format, range or allowed-value rule breaks validity.","Duplicate records break uniqueness, even when each record looks valid.","Old data in a fast-changing domain breaks timeliness, also called freshness.","Two systems disagreeing on the same fact breaks consistency."],"questions":["e3q16","e4q42","e6q16","e6q28","e6q29"]},{"id":"d2l5","num":5,"title":"Databases, data lakes and data warehouses","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Relational database</strong>: predictable structure, <strong>related tables</strong>, cross-references (joins), strict schema. It stores <strong>transactional data</strong> that applications read and write <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q46\">Exam 4 · Q46</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q14\">Exam 5 · Q14</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-relational (NoSQL) database</strong>: defined by a <strong>flexible data model</strong>. Good when the structure will change, such as new device metrics <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q7\">Exam 6 · Q7</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Data lake</strong>: holds <strong>large volumes of raw data in its original format</strong> (structured, semi-structured, unstructured) <strong>before you decide how to process it</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q33\">Exam 2 · Q33</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q22\">Exam 5 · Q22</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Data warehouse</strong>: holds <strong>processed</strong> structured and semi-structured data for <strong>analysis</strong>: flexible queries, ad-hoc analysis, custom reports, dashboards <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q46\">Exam 6 · Q46</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q20\">Exam 6 · Q20</a>.</li>\n<li>A modern cloud warehouse like <strong>BigQuery</strong> can analyze structured and unstructured data at scale <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q27\">Exam 2 · Q27</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>OLTP vs OLAP.</strong> Operational databases do <strong>OLTP</strong> (online transaction processing): many small reads and writes of single records. Warehouses do <strong>OLAP</strong> (online analytical processing): big scans and aggregations over historical data <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q4\">Exam 1 · Q4</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q15\">Exam 3 · Q15</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q41\">Exam 3 · Q41</a>.</li>\n<li>Lake and warehouse work together. Raw data lands in the lake (on Google Cloud, usually Cloud Storage). Once cleaned and modeled, it moves into the warehouse for reporting <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q33\">Exam 2 · Q33</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q22\">Exam 5 · Q22</a>.</li>\n<li>NoSQL comes in several models: <strong>document</strong> (Firestore), <strong>key-value</strong>, <strong>wide-column</strong> (Bigtable) and <strong>graph</strong>. They avoid multi-table joins and keep related data together for fast access <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q7\">Exam 6 · Q7</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Comparison</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Store</th>\n<th>Data state</th>\n<th>Main use</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Relational database</td>\n<td>Structured, schema first</td>\n<td>Day-to-day transactions</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q46\">Exam 4 · Q46</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q14\">Exam 5 · Q14</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Non-relational database</td>\n<td>Flexible schema</td>\n<td>Changing app data at scale</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q7\">Exam 6 · Q7</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Data lake</td>\n<td>Raw, any format</td>\n<td>Keep everything now, decide later</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q33\">Exam 2 · Q33</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q22\">Exam 5 · Q22</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Data warehouse</td>\n<td>Cleaned, modeled</td>\n<td>Analytics, reporting, BI</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q46\">Exam 6 · Q46</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q20\">Exam 6 · Q20</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Keep in original format\" or \"before deciding how to process it\" → <strong>data lake</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q33\">Exam 2 · Q33</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q22\">Exam 5 · Q22</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Analysts query for reports, dashboards, ad-hoc analysis\" → <strong>data warehouse</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q46\">Exam 6 · Q46</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q20\">Exam 6 · Q20</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Related tables, cross-references, predictable structure\" → <strong>relational database</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q46\">Exam 4 · Q46</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Structure will change over time\" → <strong>non-relational, flexible model</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q7\">Exam 6 · Q7</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>\"Data field\"</strong> is a made-up distractor. It is never a storage solution <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q33\">Exam 2 · Q33</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q22\">Exam 5 · Q22</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q46\">Exam 6 · Q46</a>.</li>\n<li>Strict schemas and multi-table joins describe <strong>relational</strong> databases, not NoSQL <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q7\">Exam 6 · Q7</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Fast analysis of huge multi-dimensional data\" describes a <strong>warehouse</strong>. \"Raw data in the original format\" describes a <strong>lake</strong>. Neither is what a relational database provides <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q14\">Exam 5 · Q14</a>.</li>\n<li>In an analytics question, \"structured tables for transactional queries\" is the database answer dressed up as the warehouse one <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q20\">Exam 6 · Q20</a>.</li>\n<li>A \"time-series database\" is too narrow for general business reporting <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q20\">Exam 6 · Q20</a>. \"Copy everything into one object store\" describes a lake, not a warehouse <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q20\">Exam 6 · Q20</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"Different stores serve different jobs. This lesson compares relational and non-relational databases, data lakes and data warehouses, the OLTP vs OLAP split behind them, and the phrases the exam uses for each.","objectives":["Describe what relational and non-relational databases are designed for","Distinguish a data lake from a data warehouse","Explain OLTP vs OLAP and match each to databases or warehouses","Pick the right store from scenario wording"],"takeaways":["Related tables and transactions mean a relational database.","A flexible data model is the defining feature of NoSQL.","NoSQL models include document, key-value, wide-column and graph.","Raw data kept in its original format for later processing goes in a data lake.","Processed data for ad-hoc queries, reports and dashboards goes in a data warehouse.","Databases do OLTP; warehouses do OLAP.","'Data field' is a fake distractor."],"questions":["e1q4","e2q27","e2q33","e3q15","e3q41","e4q46","e5q14","e5q22","e6q7","e6q20","e6q46"]},{"id":"d2l6","num":6,"title":"Google Cloud databases: Cloud SQL, AlloyDB, Spanner, Firestore","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cloud SQL</strong>: fully managed <strong>MySQL, PostgreSQL and SQL Server</strong>. The <strong>cost-effective</strong> relational choice for a <strong>single region</strong>, small and medium apps, no server management <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q15\">Exam 3 · Q15</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q23\">Exam 4 · Q23</a>.</li>\n<li>Like any operational database, it <strong>integrates with BI and analytics platforms</strong> to produce insights. It doesn't build dashboards or run ML on its own <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q30\">Exam 2 · Q30</a>.</li>\n<li>Cloud SQL's managed operations: Google handles <strong>backups, patching, replication and high availability</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q23\">Exam 4 · Q23</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q14\">Exam 2 · Q14</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>AlloyDB for PostgreSQL</strong>: fully managed, <strong>PostgreSQL-compatible</strong>, higher performance and availability than standard PostgreSQL. It includes a <strong>columnar engine</strong> that speeds up analytical queries (scans, joins, aggregates) on the same operational data. Running transactions and analytics on one database is called <strong>HTAP</strong> (hybrid transactional and analytical processing) <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q4\">Exam 1 · Q4</a>.</li>\n<li>Pick it to <strong>move an existing PostgreSQL app</strong> that is slowing down, with <strong>minimal code changes</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q13\">Exam 1 · Q13</a>.</li>\n<li>Pick it for high performance while <strong>staying aligned with open-source PostgreSQL</strong> to reduce lock-in (<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q39\">Exam 3 · Q39</a>, see ⚠️).</li>\n<li><strong>AlloyDB Omni</strong>: the downloadable, <strong>self-managed</strong> edition of AlloyDB that runs <strong>anywhere</strong>: your <strong>own data center</strong>, the edge or other clouds <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q40\">Exam 2 · Q40</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q14\">Exam 2 · Q14</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Spanner</strong> (Cloud Spanner): relational, strongly consistent (<strong>ACID</strong> transactions), <strong>horizontally scalable</strong> and able to <strong>replicate across regions</strong> in real time. It comes in regional, dual-region and multi-region configurations. Pick it for <strong>global, multi-region transactional</strong> apps <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q25\">Exam 2 · Q25</a>.</li>\n<li>Spanner is now multi-model: besides relational tables it supports key-value, graph and search. It is still an operational database, not a file store <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q4\">Exam 3 · Q4</a>.</li>\n<li>Spanner has a <strong>PostgreSQL interface</strong>, but that is not full PostgreSQL compatibility. Moving an existing PostgreSQL app to it usually needs schema, query or behavior changes <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q13\">Exam 1 · Q13</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q39\">Exam 3 · Q39</a>.</li>\n<li>It is more powerful and <strong>more expensive</strong> than a single-region app needs <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q15\">Exam 3 · Q15</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Firestore</strong>: serverless <strong>NoSQL document database</strong> for <strong>web and mobile apps</strong> (profiles, posts, comments, settings). It adds <strong>real-time sync, offline support</strong> and Firebase integration. <em>Firestore in Datastore mode</em> is the same database for server-side apps <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q45\">Exam 4 · Q45</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q39\">Exam 3 · Q39</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Bigtable</strong> <em>(only a distractor in the bank)</em>: wide-column NoSQL for very large, low-latency, high-throughput workloads such as IoT time series, financial data and personalization. Not relational, no joins, not for media files <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q13\">Exam 1 · Q13</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q4\">Exam 3 · Q4</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q23\">Exam 2 · Q23</a>.</li>\n<li>Operational databases feed insight; they don't create it. Dashboards come from <strong>Looker</strong> or <strong>Data Studio</strong>, predictions from <strong>BigQuery ML</strong> or <strong>Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform</strong> (formerly Vertex AI) <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q30\">Exam 2 · Q30</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Picking a database</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Need</th>\n<th>Pick</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Relational, one region, budget-friendly</td>\n<td>Cloud SQL</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q15\">Exam 3 · Q15</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q23\">Exam 4 · Q23</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>PostgreSQL-compatible, faster, analytics on live data</td>\n<td>AlloyDB for PostgreSQL</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q4\">Exam 1 · Q4</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q13\">Exam 1 · Q13</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q39\">Exam 3 · Q39</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>AlloyDB on-premises or in another cloud</td>\n<td>AlloyDB Omni</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q40\">Exam 2 · Q40</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Global, multi-region, consistent transactions</td>\n<td>Spanner</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q25\">Exam 2 · Q25</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Flexible NoSQL for web and mobile</td>\n<td>Firestore</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q45\">Exam 4 · Q45</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Huge-scale wide-column NoSQL</td>\n<td>Bigtable</td>\n<td><em>(not in the question bank)</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Only one region\" + \"cost-effective\" + transactions → <strong>Cloud SQL</strong>, not Spanner <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q15\">Exam 3 · Q15</a>.</li>\n<li>\"PostgreSQL\" + \"performance\" or \"columnar engine\" → <strong>AlloyDB</strong>. Add \"on-premises data center\" → <strong>AlloyDB Omni</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q4\">Exam 1 · Q4</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q40\">Exam 2 · Q40</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Several continents, consistent, highly available\" → <strong>Spanner</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q25\">Exam 2 · Q25</a>.</li>\n<li>\"NoSQL\" + \"mobile/web app\" → <strong>Firestore</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q45\">Exam 4 · Q45</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Spanner is not cold storage, not for unstructured ingestion and not a visualization tool <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q25\">Exam 2 · Q25</a>.</li>\n<li>BigQuery is for analytics, not an app's transactional database <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q15\">Exam 3 · Q15</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q23\">Exam 4 · Q23</a>.</li>\n<li>Bigtable and Firestore are NoSQL, so they are wrong when the question says PostgreSQL <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q13\">Exam 1 · Q13</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q39\">Exam 3 · Q39</a>.</li>\n<li>Cloud SQL doesn't \"convert unstructured files into tables\" or \"build predictions directly\" <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q30\">Exam 2 · Q30</a>. Parsing files into tables is the job of a processing or ETL service.</li>\n<li>Storing large images or videos inside a relational database is possible but costly and slow. Files go in Cloud Storage <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q4\">Exam 3 · Q4</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q30\">Exam 4 · Q30</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"Google Cloud offers several managed operational databases. This lesson covers when to pick Cloud SQL, AlloyDB for PostgreSQL (HTAP with a columnar engine), AlloyDB Omni, Spanner or Firestore, with Bigtable as a recurring distractor, and how databases feed BI and ML tools.","objectives":["Choose between Cloud SQL, AlloyDB and Spanner for relational workloads","Identify AlloyDB's columnar engine and PostgreSQL compatibility","Know where AlloyDB Omni runs","Explain why Spanner's PostgreSQL interface is not the same as PostgreSQL compatibility","Pick Firestore for NoSQL web and mobile apps"],"takeaways":["Single region and cost-effective relational means Cloud SQL.","PostgreSQL plus performance or a columnar engine means AlloyDB; transactions plus analytics on one database is HTAP.","AlloyDB in your own data center means AlloyDB Omni, which is self-managed.","Global, multi-region consistent transactions mean Spanner; its PostgreSQL interface still needs app changes.","NoSQL for web and mobile apps with real-time sync and offline support means Firestore.","Operational databases feed Looker, Data Studio and BigQuery ML; they don't make dashboards or predictions themselves."],"questions":["e1q4","e1q13","e2q14","e2q23","e2q25","e2q30","e2q40","e3q4","e3q15","e3q39","e4q23","e4q30","e4q45"]},{"id":"d2l7","num":7,"title":"Cloud Storage: classes, locations and Autoclass","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cloud Storage</strong> is object storage for <strong>unstructured files</strong> at any scale: images, videos, audio, backups, exports <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q4\">Exam 3 · Q4</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Storage classes</strong> trade storage price for access cost. Standard (frequent) → <strong>Nearline</strong> (about once a month) → <strong>Coldline</strong> (about once a quarter) → <strong>Archive</strong> (less than once a year). Use them to cut the cost of rarely read files <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q12\">Exam 2 · Q12</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Archive</strong> is cheapest for data read <strong>about once a year</strong>, such as disaster-recovery exports <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q13\">Exam 4 · Q13</a>.</li>\n<li>Colder classes trade a lower storage price for <strong>higher retrieval costs</strong> and a <strong>minimum storage duration</strong>: Nearline 30 days, Coldline 90 days, Archive 365 days <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q13\">Exam 4 · Q13</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Autoclass</strong> moves each object between classes <strong>automatically, based on its actual access</strong>. Unused objects go colder; an object that is read again goes back to Standard. No rules to write, no access patterns to predict <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q28\">Exam 1 · Q28</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q32\">Exam 1 · Q32</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Object Lifecycle Management</strong> applies rules <strong>you write</strong> on conditions such as age, creation date, storage class or number of newer versions. Actions are change class or delete. Use it when you know the pattern <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q28\">Exam 1 · Q28</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q32\">Exam 1 · Q32</a>.</li>\n<li>A bucket's <strong>location type</strong> is your choice: <strong>region</strong>, <strong>dual-region</strong> or <strong>multi-region</strong>. Data placement follows that choice, never chance <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q9\">Exam 3 · Q9</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Multi-region</strong> (and dual-region) buckets keep <strong>geo-redundant copies</strong>, giving broad geographic access and higher availability <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q6\">Exam 1 · Q6</a>, (<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q9\">Exam 3 · Q9</a>, see ⚠️).</li>\n<li>Encryption at rest is <strong>on by default and free</strong>. It is never a paid add-on. For more control you can use <strong>customer-managed encryption keys (CMEK)</strong> in Cloud KMS <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q9\">Exam 3 · Q9</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q6\">Exam 1 · Q6</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Storage classes</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Class</th>\n<th>Typical access</th>\n<th>Bank example</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Standard</td>\n<td>Frequent, hot data</td>\n<td>Latest files</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q12\">Exam 2 · Q12</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Nearline</td>\n<td>About once a month</td>\n<td>Less-used files</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q12\">Exam 2 · Q12</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Coldline</td>\n<td>About once a quarter</td>\n<td>Older files</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q12\">Exam 2 · Q12</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Archive</td>\n<td>Less than once a year</td>\n<td>DR exports read yearly</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q13\">Exam 4 · Q13</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Can't predict access\" + \"don't want rules\" → <strong>Autoclass</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q28\">Exam 1 · Q28</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q32\">Exam 1 · Q32</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Move after X days\" → <strong>Lifecycle rules</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q28\">Exam 1 · Q28</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Read once a year, minimize cost\" → <strong>Archive</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q13\">Exam 4 · Q13</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Users in many countries\" + \"high availability\" → <strong>multi-region bucket</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q6\">Exam 1 · Q6</a>.</li>\n<li>Large volumes of media files → <strong>Cloud Storage</strong>, not a database <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q4\">Exam 3 · Q4</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Storage Transfer Service moves data in; Cloud CDN caches content near users. Neither changes storage class <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q28\">Exam 1 · Q28</a>.</li>\n<li>Object Versioning keeps old copies for recovery and can <strong>increase</strong> storage use. Requester Pays makes the downloader pay access costs. Neither saves on cold data <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q32\">Exam 1 · Q32</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q6\">Exam 1 · Q6</a>.</li>\n<li>Object metadata (content type, cache settings, custom labels) describes objects. It doesn't decide where data lives or how available it is <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q6\">Exam 1 · Q6</a>.</li>\n<li>Filestore is file (NFS) storage, and Knowledge Catalog is a catalog, not where you store objects <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q12\">Exam 2 · Q12</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Randomly places data\" and \"small minimum storage quantity\" are invented benefits <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q9\">Exam 3 · Q9</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"Cloud Storage holds unstructured objects at any scale. This lesson covers storage classes and their minimum storage durations, bucket location types, encryption options, Autoclass and lifecycle rules for keeping costs low.","objectives":["Match access frequency to Standard, Nearline, Coldline or Archive","Know the minimum storage durations: Nearline 30, Coldline 90, Archive 365 days","Choose Autoclass over lifecycle rules when access is unpredictable","Explain the availability benefit of multi-region buckets","Choose a bucket location type: region, dual-region or multi-region"],"takeaways":["Media and backup files belong in Cloud Storage.","Data read about once a year belongs in Archive (365-day minimum).","Colder classes cost less to store but more to read.","Unpredictable access with no rules to write means Autoclass; accessed objects move back to Standard.","Known patterns such as 'after 45 days' mean lifecycle rules, which can change class or delete.","Multi-region buckets give broad access and geo-redundant copies.","Encryption at rest is default and free; CMEK adds customer control."],"questions":["e1q6","e1q28","e1q32","e2q12","e3q4","e3q9","e4q13"]},{"id":"d2l8","num":8,"title":"BigQuery and Looker: analytics and business intelligence","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>BigQuery</strong> is Google Cloud's <strong>serverless data warehouse</strong>: stores large datasets, runs fast SQL at <strong>petabyte</strong> scale, no servers to manage, with built-in ML and AI features <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q48\">Exam 1 · Q48</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q41\">Exam 3 · Q41</a>.</li>\n<li>It <strong>separates storage and compute</strong> and scales automatically <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q41\">Exam 3 · Q41</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>BigQuery ML</strong> lets analysts build and run ML models with SQL inside BigQuery <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q48\">Exam 1 · Q48</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q30\">Exam 2 · Q30</a>.</li>\n<li>A common pattern keeps raw files in Cloud Storage and analyzes them with BigQuery. Cloud Storage alone is not a query engine <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q41\">Exam 3 · Q41</a>.</li>\n<li>BigQuery can analyze <strong>structured and unstructured</strong> data at scale, such as AI assistant transcripts and call metadata <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q27\">Exam 2 · Q27</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>BigQuery Omni</strong> runs BigQuery SQL on data that <strong>stays in AWS or Azure</strong> storage (Amazon S3, Azure Blob Storage), through <strong>BigLake</strong> tables, so you don't copy it into Google Cloud first. That avoids the cost and delay of moving data <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q14\">Exam 2 · Q14</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Looker</strong> is Google Cloud's enterprise <strong>business intelligence</strong> platform. It gives business users <strong>self-service</strong> access to trusted, modeled data to explore, build dashboards and reports. It also supports <strong>embedded analytics</strong> (dashboards inside your own apps) <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q20\">Exam 1 · Q20</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q8\">Exam 2 · Q8</a>.</li>\n<li>Looker's <strong>governed semantic model</strong> (LookML) defines each metric once, so every team sees the same numbers <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q20\">Exam 1 · Q20</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q5\">Exam 2 · Q5</a>.</li>\n<li>Looker is <strong>multicloud</strong>: it connects to many databases and warehouses (BigQuery, Snowflake, Redshift, MySQL, Oracle and more) on Google Cloud, AWS and Azure <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q5\">Exam 2 · Q5</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Data Studio</strong>: the free, lighter dashboarding tool, with a paid <strong>Data Studio Pro</strong> edition. It was called Looker Studio from 2022 until Google restored the Data Studio name in April 2026. Looker adds the governed semantic model for the whole enterprise <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q30\">Exam 2 · Q30</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Who does what</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Product</th>\n<th>Role</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>BigQuery</td>\n<td>Store and query data with SQL at scale</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q48\">Exam 1 · Q48</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q41\">Exam 3 · Q41</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>BigQuery Omni</td>\n<td>Query data in other clouds in place</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q14\">Exam 2 · Q14</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Looker</td>\n<td>BI: explore, model, visualize, share insights</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q20\">Exam 1 · Q20</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q5\">Exam 2 · Q5</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q8\">Exam 2 · Q8</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Serverless data warehouse\", \"petabytes\", \"SQL analysis\" → <strong>BigQuery</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q48\">Exam 1 · Q48</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q41\">Exam 3 · Q41</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Analyze data in another cloud without copying it\" → <strong>BigQuery Omni</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q14\">Exam 2 · Q14</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Business users explore data and build reports without engineers\" → <strong>Looker</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q20\">Exam 1 · Q20</a>.</li>\n<li>\"What's Looker's benefit?\" → <strong>advanced business intelligence and analytics</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q8\">Exam 2 · Q8</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>BigQuery stores and queries. Looker visualizes and governs the business view. If the question is about <strong>people exploring and reporting</strong>, pick Looker over BigQuery <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q20\">Exam 1 · Q20</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Leading serverless warehousing technology\" describes BigQuery, not Looker <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q8\">Exam 2 · Q8</a>.</li>\n<li>AlloyDB Omni runs a database elsewhere. It doesn't query data across clouds <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q14\">Exam 2 · Q14</a>.</li>\n<li>Cloud SQL, Firestore and Cloud Storage don't do petabyte-scale analytics <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q41\">Exam 3 · Q41</a>.</li>\n<li>BigQuery doesn't encrypt network links or convert records into audio <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q27\">Exam 2 · Q27</a>.</li>\n<li>Looker has its own permissions, but access management for Google Cloud resources is <strong>IAM</strong>, and rollback or recovery belongs to databases and storage. Neither is Looker's benefit <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q8\">Exam 2 · Q8</a>.</li>\n<li>Compute Engine and Spot VMs are infrastructure. You could install BI software on them, but they are not a BI product <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q5\">Exam 2 · Q5</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q48\">Exam 1 · Q48</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"BigQuery is the serverless warehouse that stores and queries data at scale, with BigQuery ML built in, and Looker is the governed BI layer that lets business users explore it. This lesson also covers BigQuery Omni for data in other clouds and Data Studio (formerly Looker Studio) for lighter dashboards.","objectives":["Recognize BigQuery from 'serverless data warehouse' and petabyte-scale SQL","Use BigQuery Omni to query data in other clouds in place","Explain what Looker adds: self-service, governed BI across clouds","Tell Looker (governed enterprise BI) from Data Studio (free, lighter dashboards)"],"takeaways":["Serverless warehouse with fast SQL at petabyte scale is BigQuery; it separates storage and compute.","BigQuery ML builds ML models with SQL inside BigQuery.","Querying AWS or Azure data without copying it is BigQuery Omni.","Business users exploring data and building reports means Looker.","Looker's governed semantic model keeps metric definitions consistent.","Looker works across Google Cloud, AWS and Azure data sources.","Data Studio is the free dashboard tool, renamed back from Looker Studio in 2026."],"questions":["e1q20","e1q48","e2q5","e2q8","e2q14","e2q27","e2q30","e3q41"]},{"id":"d2l9","num":9,"title":"Processing data: batch vs streaming, Dataflow and serverless Spark","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Batch processing</strong> handles data in scheduled chunks (nightly, quarterly). <strong>Streaming</strong> processes records <strong>continuously as they arrive</strong>, giving near real-time insight <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q26\">Exam 3 · Q26</a>.</li>\n<li>To react to demand <strong>as it happens</strong>, switch <strong>from batch to stream processing</strong>. Running batches more often only shortens the delay <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q4\">Exam 6 · Q4</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Dataflow</strong> is a fully managed, <strong>serverless</strong> service for batch and streaming pipelines (built on Apache Beam). It reads, transforms and loads data, for example into BigQuery <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q23\">Exam 2 · Q23</a>.</li>\n<li>The main benefit of a serverless pipeline service is that it <strong>manages and scales the infrastructure automatically</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q39\">Exam 4 · Q39</a>.</li>\n<li>Batch still has its place: daily reports, monthly billing, historical summaries and <strong>backfills</strong> (reloading a past period) are batch jobs <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q26\">Exam 3 · Q26</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Managed Service for Apache Spark</strong> (formerly Dataproc) runs Spark and Hadoop. Its <strong>serverless</strong> option lets you <strong>submit Spark jobs (batch workloads or interactive sessions) without creating, sizing or tuning clusters</strong>. The <strong>clusters</strong> option is for teams that want control over cluster configuration <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q44\">Exam 1 · Q44</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q34\">Exam 3 · Q34</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Pub/Sub</strong>: the managed messaging service that ingests event streams and decouples systems. It moves messages but doesn't transform them <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q23\">Exam 2 · Q23</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q4\">Exam 6 · Q4</a>.</li>\n<li>The standard streaming pipeline is <strong>Pub/Sub (ingest) → Dataflow (process) → BigQuery (analyze)</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q26\">Exam 3 · Q26</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q4\">Exam 6 · Q4</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Cloud Data Fusion</strong>: a fully managed, <strong>visual</strong> (graphical, low-code) tool for building ETL and ELT pipelines between many sources and destinations <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q44\">Exam 1 · Q44</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>ETL</strong> transforms data before loading it into the warehouse. <strong>ELT</strong> loads raw data first and transforms it inside the warehouse <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q44\">Exam 1 · Q44</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q23\">Exam 2 · Q23</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Processing options</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Need</th>\n<th>Pick</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Continuous, real-time insight</td>\n<td>Stream processing</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q26\">Exam 3 · Q26</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q4\">Exam 6 · Q4</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Managed, auto-scaling ETL into a warehouse</td>\n<td>Dataflow</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q23\">Exam 2 · Q23</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q39\">Exam 4 · Q39</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Existing Spark code, no cluster work</td>\n<td>Managed Service for Apache Spark serverless</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q44\">Exam 1 · Q44</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q34\">Exam 3 · Q34</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Spark with control over clusters</td>\n<td>Managed Service for Apache Spark clusters</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q44\">Exam 1 · Q44</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ingest event streams</td>\n<td>Pub/Sub</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q23\">Exam 2 · Q23</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q4\">Exam 6 · Q4</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Visual, low-code ETL/ELT</td>\n<td>Cloud Data Fusion</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q44\">Exam 1 · Q44</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"As it happens\" or \"as events arrive\" → <strong>streaming</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q26\">Exam 3 · Q26</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q4\">Exam 6 · Q4</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Transform before loading into a warehouse\" + \"scale automatically\" → <strong>Dataflow</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q23\">Exam 2 · Q23</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Spark\" + \"don't want to manage clusters\" → <strong>Managed Service for Apache Spark serverless</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q44\">Exam 1 · Q44</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q34\">Exam 3 · Q34</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Serverless does <strong>not</strong> mean full control of compute, no need for analysis, or error-free data <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q39\">Exam 4 · Q39</a>.</li>\n<li>Pub/Sub moves messages. Bigtable and Cloud Storage store data. None of them is the transform pipeline <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q23\">Exam 2 · Q23</a>.</li>\n<li>The <strong>clusters</strong> option of the Spark service still needs cluster management. Cloud Data Fusion is a visual integration tool, not serverless Spark. Installing Spark on Compute Engine VMs is the most work of all <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q44\">Exam 1 · Q44</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q34\">Exam 3 · Q34</a>.</li>\n<li>BigQuery is serverless too, but it runs SQL, not existing Spark code <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q44\">Exam 1 · Q44</a>.</li>\n<li>Switching to NoSQL or filtering data doesn't make insights real time <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q4\">Exam 6 · Q4</a>.</li>\n<li>Moving data into Google Cloud uses <strong>Storage Transfer Service</strong> (online copies from Amazon S3, Azure Blob Storage, on-premises storage or other buckets) <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q28\">Exam 1 · Q28</a>. Transfer Appliance and Database Migration Service <em>(not in the question bank)</em> cover offline bulk transfer and database moves.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"Data must be processed before it is useful. This lesson contrasts batch and streaming processing and covers the managed services for pipelines: Pub/Sub for ingesting events, Dataflow, Managed Service for Apache Spark (formerly Dataproc) serverless, Cloud Data Fusion, and Storage Transfer Service for moving data in.","objectives":["Explain the difference between batch and stream processing","Identify Dataflow as the serverless, auto-scaling pipeline service","Place Pub/Sub, Dataflow and BigQuery in a streaming pipeline","Choose serverless Spark when a team wants to run Spark without managing clusters","Recognize Cloud Data Fusion as visual ETL/ELT"],"takeaways":["Streaming processes records continuously; it is how you react as things happen.","Running batches more often is not the same as streaming; backfills are batch.","Pub/Sub ingests events, Dataflow transforms them, BigQuery analyzes them.","Dataflow manages and scales pipeline infrastructure automatically.","Spark without cluster work means Managed Service for Apache Spark serverless.","Cloud Data Fusion builds ETL/ELT pipelines visually; it is not a Spark runtime.","Serverless never means full control or error-free data."],"questions":["e1q28","e1q44","e2q23","e3q26","e4q39","e6q4"]}],"ref":{"cheat":"<ul>\n<li>End-to-end data journey → <strong>data supply chain</strong>. Raw → clean and structured → <strong>processing</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q35\">Exam 1 · Q35</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q33\">Exam 3 · Q33</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q1\">Exam 1 · Q1</a>.</li>\n<li>Automatic real-time collection → <strong>IoT devices</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q21\">Exam 2 · Q21</a>. Digital payments → <strong>transaction history</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q3\">Exam 1 · Q3</a>.</li>\n<li>Unified data → <strong>no silos, enables innovation</strong>. Cloud transactional data → <strong>accurate transactions at scale</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q9\">Exam 5 · Q9</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q39\">Exam 6 · Q39</a>.</li>\n<li>Unified, clean, governed data for AI → <strong>AI-ready data / foundation / platform / data cloud</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q34\">Exam 1 · Q34</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q38\">Exam 3 · Q38</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q49\">Exam 5 · Q49</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q48\">Exam 5 · Q48</a>.</li>\n<li>Value + cost + compliance → <strong>data governance program</strong>. Avoid lock-in → <strong>openness and interoperability</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q8\">Exam 5 · Q8</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q37\">Exam 5 · Q37</a>.</li>\n<li>JSON/XML → <strong>semi-structured</strong>. Text, audio, video, transcripts → <strong>unstructured</strong>. Table fields → <strong>structured</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q19\">Exam 1 · Q19</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q22\">Exam 1 · Q22</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q6\">Exam 3 · Q6</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q8\">Exam 6 · Q8</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q40\">Exam 6 · Q40</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q30\">Exam 4 · Q30</a>.</li>\n<li>Own customers → <strong>first-party</strong>. Partner shares → <strong>second-party</strong>. Bought → <strong>third-party</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q38\">Exam 1 · Q38</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q5\">Exam 3 · Q5</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q39\">Exam 1 · Q39</a>.</li>\n<li>Missing → <strong>completeness</strong>. Matches source → <strong>accuracy</strong>. Format → <strong>validity</strong>. Duplicates → <strong>uniqueness</strong>. Stale → <strong>timeliness</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q16\">Exam 3 · Q16</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q46\">Exam 3 · Q46</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q16\">Exam 6 · Q16</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q28\">Exam 6 · Q28</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q29\">Exam 6 · Q29</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q42\">Exam 4 · Q42</a>.</li>\n<li>Raw, original format → <strong>data lake</strong>. Analyst reporting → <strong>data warehouse</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q33\">Exam 2 · Q33</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q22\">Exam 5 · Q22</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q46\">Exam 6 · Q46</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q20\">Exam 6 · Q20</a>.</li>\n<li>Related tables, transactions → <strong>relational</strong>. Changing structure → <strong>NoSQL, flexible model</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q46\">Exam 4 · Q46</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q14\">Exam 5 · Q14</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q7\">Exam 6 · Q7</a>.</li>\n<li>One region, cheap relational → <strong>Cloud SQL</strong> (integrates with BI) <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q15\">Exam 3 · Q15</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q23\">Exam 4 · Q23</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q30\">Exam 2 · Q30</a>.</li>\n<li>PostgreSQL + performance + columnar engine → <strong>AlloyDB</strong>. On-prem → <strong>AlloyDB Omni</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q4\">Exam 1 · Q4</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q13\">Exam 1 · Q13</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q39\">Exam 3 · Q39</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q40\">Exam 2 · Q40</a>.</li>\n<li>Multi-region consistent transactions → <strong>Spanner</strong>. Mobile/web NoSQL → <strong>Firestore</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q25\">Exam 2 · Q25</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q45\">Exam 4 · Q45</a>.</li>\n<li>Media files → <strong>Cloud Storage</strong>. Rare access → <strong>Nearline/Coldline/Archive</strong>. Yearly → <strong>Archive</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q4\">Exam 3 · Q4</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q12\">Exam 2 · Q12</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q13\">Exam 4 · Q13</a>.</li>\n<li>Unpredictable access, no rules → <strong>Autoclass</strong>. Global users → <strong>multi-region bucket</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q28\">Exam 1 · Q28</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q32\">Exam 1 · Q32</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q6\">Exam 1 · Q6</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q9\">Exam 3 · Q9</a>.</li>\n<li>Serverless warehouse, petabytes, SQL → <strong>BigQuery</strong>. Data in other clouds → <strong>BigQuery Omni</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q48\">Exam 1 · Q48</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q41\">Exam 3 · Q41</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q27\">Exam 2 · Q27</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q14\">Exam 2 · Q14</a>.</li>\n<li>Self-service BI, multicloud → <strong>Looker</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q20\">Exam 1 · Q20</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q5\">Exam 2 · Q5</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q8\">Exam 2 · Q8</a>.</li>\n<li>Transactions → <strong>OLTP database</strong>. Large-scale analysis → <strong>OLAP warehouse</strong>. Both on one PostgreSQL database → <strong>AlloyDB (HTAP)</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q15\">Exam 3 · Q15</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q41\">Exam 3 · Q41</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q4\">Exam 1 · Q4</a>.</li>\n<li>Archive minimum storage 365 days; Coldline 90; Nearline 30 <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q13\">Exam 4 · Q13</a>.</li>\n<li>React as it happens → <strong>streaming</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q26\">Exam 3 · Q26</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q4\">Exam 6 · Q4</a>.</li>\n<li>Managed auto-scaling pipeline → <strong>Dataflow</strong>. Spark without clusters → <strong>Managed Service for Apache Spark serverless</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q23\">Exam 2 · Q23</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q39\">Exam 4 · Q39</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q44\">Exam 1 · Q44</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q34\">Exam 3 · Q34</a>.</li>\n<li>Ingest events → <strong>Pub/Sub</strong>. Visual ETL → <strong>Cloud Data Fusion</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q23\">Exam 2 · Q23</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q4\">Exam 6 · Q4</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q44\">Exam 1 · Q44</a>.</li>\n</ul>","rules":[{"lesson":"d2l1","title":"Why data matters: the data value chain and cloud data benefits","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"End-to-end lifecycle of data, creation to decisions\" → <strong>data supply chain</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q35\">Exam 1 · Q35</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q33\">Exam 3 · Q33</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Making raw data clean and structured\" → <strong>processing</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q1\">Exam 1 · Q1</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Collect automatically, no manual entry\" → <strong>IoT, real-time upload</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q21\">Exam 2 · Q21</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Benefit of a unified data solution\" → <strong>data is no longer in silos, so it can drive innovation</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q9\">Exam 5 · Q9</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d2l2","title":"AI-ready data, governance and openness","html":"<ul>\n<li>Data is fragmented, inconsistent or ungoverned, and the goal is AI → <strong>AI-ready data / data foundation / data platform</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q34\">Exam 1 · Q34</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q38\">Exam 3 · Q38</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q49\">Exam 5 · Q49</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Combine own, partner and market data to train models\" → <strong>AI-ready data cloud</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q48\">Exam 5 · Q48</a>.</li>\n<li>Value + cost + compliance in one sentence → <strong>data governance program</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q8\">Exam 5 · Q8</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Work across clouds, avoid lock-in\" → <strong>openness and interoperability</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q37\">Exam 5 · Q37</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d2l3","title":"Types of data: structure and source","html":"<ul>\n<li>Free text, speech, images or video → <strong>unstructured</strong>, even if the text is a \"transcript\" <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q8\">Exam 6 · Q8</a>.</li>\n<li>JSON / XML / \"tags and attributes\" → <strong>semi-structured</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q19\">Exam 1 · Q19</a>.</li>\n<li>A number or field you could put in a table column → <strong>structured</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q40\">Exam 6 · Q40</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Directly from our own customers\" → first-party. \"Partner shares its data with us\" → second-party. \"Bought from a provider\" → third-party <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q38\">Exam 1 · Q38</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q5\">Exam 3 · Q5</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q39\">Exam 1 · Q39</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d2l4","title":"Data quality dimensions","html":"<ul>\n<li>Ask \"what is wrong with the value?\" Missing → completeness. Wrong → accuracy. Wrong shape → validity. Doubled → uniqueness. Stale → timeliness.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d2l5","title":"Databases, data lakes and data warehouses","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"Keep in original format\" or \"before deciding how to process it\" → <strong>data lake</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q33\">Exam 2 · Q33</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q22\">Exam 5 · Q22</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Analysts query for reports, dashboards, ad-hoc analysis\" → <strong>data warehouse</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q46\">Exam 6 · Q46</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q20\">Exam 6 · Q20</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Related tables, cross-references, predictable structure\" → <strong>relational database</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q46\">Exam 4 · Q46</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Structure will change over time\" → <strong>non-relational, flexible model</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q7\">Exam 6 · Q7</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d2l6","title":"Google Cloud databases: Cloud SQL, AlloyDB, Spanner, Firestore","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"Only one region\" + \"cost-effective\" + transactions → <strong>Cloud SQL</strong>, not Spanner <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q15\">Exam 3 · Q15</a>.</li>\n<li>\"PostgreSQL\" + \"performance\" or \"columnar engine\" → <strong>AlloyDB</strong>. Add \"on-premises data center\" → <strong>AlloyDB Omni</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q4\">Exam 1 · Q4</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q40\">Exam 2 · Q40</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Several continents, consistent, highly available\" → <strong>Spanner</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q25\">Exam 2 · Q25</a>.</li>\n<li>\"NoSQL\" + \"mobile/web app\" → <strong>Firestore</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q45\">Exam 4 · Q45</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d2l7","title":"Cloud Storage: classes, locations and Autoclass","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"Can't predict access\" + \"don't want rules\" → <strong>Autoclass</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q28\">Exam 1 · Q28</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q32\">Exam 1 · Q32</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Move after X days\" → <strong>Lifecycle rules</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q28\">Exam 1 · Q28</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Read once a year, minimize cost\" → <strong>Archive</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q13\">Exam 4 · Q13</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Users in many countries\" + \"high availability\" → <strong>multi-region bucket</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q6\">Exam 1 · Q6</a>.</li>\n<li>Large volumes of media files → <strong>Cloud Storage</strong>, not a database <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q4\">Exam 3 · Q4</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d2l8","title":"BigQuery and Looker: analytics and business intelligence","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"Serverless data warehouse\", \"petabytes\", \"SQL analysis\" → <strong>BigQuery</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q48\">Exam 1 · Q48</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q41\">Exam 3 · Q41</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Analyze data in another cloud without copying it\" → <strong>BigQuery Omni</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q14\">Exam 2 · Q14</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Business users explore data and build reports without engineers\" → <strong>Looker</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q20\">Exam 1 · Q20</a>.</li>\n<li>\"What's Looker's benefit?\" → <strong>advanced business intelligence and analytics</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q8\">Exam 2 · Q8</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d2l9","title":"Processing data: batch vs streaming, Dataflow and serverless Spark","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"As it happens\" or \"as events arrive\" → <strong>streaming</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q26\">Exam 3 · Q26</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q4\">Exam 6 · Q4</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Transform before loading into a warehouse\" + \"scale automatically\" → <strong>Dataflow</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q23\">Exam 2 · Q23</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Spark\" + \"don't want to manage clusters\" → <strong>Managed Service for Apache Spark serverless</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q44\">Exam 1 · Q44</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q34\">Exam 3 · Q34</a>.</li>\n</ul>"}],"traps":[{"lesson":"d2l1","title":"Why data matters: the data value chain and cloud data benefits","html":"<ul>\n<li>Absolute promises (\"always cheaper\", \"cannot be lost\", \"protected from all threats\") are always wrong <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q9\">Exam 5 · Q9</a>.</li>\n<li>Distractors from other domains (error budget, perimeter security, resource hierarchy, IaaS, multicloud networking) show up as filler. They don't describe data flow <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q3\">Exam 1 · Q3</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q35\">Exam 1 · Q35</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q33\">Exam 3 · Q33</a>.</li>\n<li>Options that mention on-premises hardware or physical server access are never the cloud benefit <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q39\">Exam 6 · Q39</a>.</li>\n<li>Unifying data does not remove the customer's security work. Security is shared responsibility: you still configure access, governance and backups <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q9\">Exam 5 · Q9</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d2l2","title":"AI-ready data, governance and openness","html":"<ul>\n<li>One-off actions (delete old data, quarterly access reports, move everything to NoSQL) are narrower than a governance program. A blanket \"delete after 18 months\" rule can even break financial retention laws, and reports only show what already happened <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q8\">Exam 5 · Q8</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Shift OpEx to CapEx\" is backwards. Cloud moves spend <strong>from CapEx to OpEx</strong>, so that option is always wrong <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q37\">Exam 5 · Q37</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q49\">Exam 5 · Q49</a>.</li>\n<li>Coldline, ephemeral or on-premises data are not quality states <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q34\">Exam 1 · Q34</a>. Restricting data to on-premises works against AI readiness <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q49\">Exam 5 · Q49</a>.</li>\n<li>Other differentiators are filler in data questions: <strong>AI Hypercomputer</strong> (GPU/TPU infrastructure for AI), <strong>Bare Metal Solution</strong> (dedicated physical servers), the <strong>global edge network</strong> (fast delivery to users) and <strong>2-Step Verification</strong> (sign-in security) <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q37\">Exam 5 · Q37</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q48\">Exam 5 · Q48</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Knowledge Catalog</strong> (formerly Dataplex Universal Catalog, renamed in April 2026) is Google Cloud's data catalog for governance: discovery, metadata, lineage, data quality checks and business glossaries across lakes and warehouses. It describes data; it doesn't store files <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q12\">Exam 2 · Q12</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d2l3","title":"Types of data: structure and source","html":"<ul>\n<li>In an \"unstructured\" question, numeric options like revenue trends, stock counts, GPS coordinates or star ratings are all structured <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q22\">Exam 1 · Q22</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q8\">Exam 6 · Q8</a>.</li>\n<li>Audio and video stored <strong>next to</strong> HTML and JSON are still unstructured. The JSON is the semi-structured part <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q6\">Exam 3 · Q6</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Relational data\" and \"open standard data\" or \"public domain data\" are not categories the exam uses for these questions <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q19\">Exam 1 · Q19</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q38\">Exam 1 · Q38</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q5\">Exam 3 · Q5</a>.</li>\n<li>Purchase orders stored as JSON are still predictable records (structured or semi-structured), never the \"unstructured\" answer <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q8\">Exam 6 · Q8</a>.</li>\n<li>Structure and source are separate axes. \"Unstructured data\" is never the answer to a first/second/third-party question <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q39\">Exam 1 · Q39</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d2l4","title":"Data quality dimensions","html":"<ul>\n<li>Validity vs accuracy: an email without \"@\" is <strong>invalid</strong> regardless of whether it belongs to the person. A well-formed total that doesn't match the invoice is <strong>inaccurate</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q28\">Exam 6 · Q28</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q16\">Exam 6 · Q16</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Explainability\" is a responsible-AI idea, not a data quality dimension <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q29\">Exam 6 · Q29</a>.</li>\n<li>Duplicates can each look valid. Two well-formed asset codes for one scooter is a <strong>uniqueness</strong> problem, not validity <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q29\">Exam 6 · Q29</a>.</li>\n<li>Old data can be accurate, valid and unique and still fail <strong>timeliness</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q42\">Exam 4 · Q42</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d2l5","title":"Databases, data lakes and data warehouses","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>\"Data field\"</strong> is a made-up distractor. It is never a storage solution <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q33\">Exam 2 · Q33</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q22\">Exam 5 · Q22</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q46\">Exam 6 · Q46</a>.</li>\n<li>Strict schemas and multi-table joins describe <strong>relational</strong> databases, not NoSQL <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q7\">Exam 6 · Q7</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Fast analysis of huge multi-dimensional data\" describes a <strong>warehouse</strong>. \"Raw data in the original format\" describes a <strong>lake</strong>. Neither is what a relational database provides <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q14\">Exam 5 · Q14</a>.</li>\n<li>In an analytics question, \"structured tables for transactional queries\" is the database answer dressed up as the warehouse one <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q20\">Exam 6 · Q20</a>.</li>\n<li>A \"time-series database\" is too narrow for general business reporting <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q20\">Exam 6 · Q20</a>. \"Copy everything into one object store\" describes a lake, not a warehouse <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q20\">Exam 6 · Q20</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d2l6","title":"Google Cloud databases: Cloud SQL, AlloyDB, Spanner, Firestore","html":"<ul>\n<li>Spanner is not cold storage, not for unstructured ingestion and not a visualization tool <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q25\">Exam 2 · Q25</a>.</li>\n<li>BigQuery is for analytics, not an app's transactional database <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q15\">Exam 3 · Q15</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q23\">Exam 4 · Q23</a>.</li>\n<li>Bigtable and Firestore are NoSQL, so they are wrong when the question says PostgreSQL <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q13\">Exam 1 · Q13</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q39\">Exam 3 · Q39</a>.</li>\n<li>Cloud SQL doesn't \"convert unstructured files into tables\" or \"build predictions directly\" <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q30\">Exam 2 · Q30</a>. Parsing files into tables is the job of a processing or ETL service.</li>\n<li>Storing large images or videos inside a relational database is possible but costly and slow. Files go in Cloud Storage <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q4\">Exam 3 · Q4</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q30\">Exam 4 · Q30</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d2l7","title":"Cloud Storage: classes, locations and Autoclass","html":"<ul>\n<li>Storage Transfer Service moves data in; Cloud CDN caches content near users. Neither changes storage class <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q28\">Exam 1 · Q28</a>.</li>\n<li>Object Versioning keeps old copies for recovery and can <strong>increase</strong> storage use. Requester Pays makes the downloader pay access costs. Neither saves on cold data <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q32\">Exam 1 · Q32</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q6\">Exam 1 · Q6</a>.</li>\n<li>Object metadata (content type, cache settings, custom labels) describes objects. It doesn't decide where data lives or how available it is <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q6\">Exam 1 · Q6</a>.</li>\n<li>Filestore is file (NFS) storage, and Knowledge Catalog is a catalog, not where you store objects <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q12\">Exam 2 · Q12</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Randomly places data\" and \"small minimum storage quantity\" are invented benefits <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q9\">Exam 3 · Q9</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d2l8","title":"BigQuery and Looker: analytics and business intelligence","html":"<ul>\n<li>BigQuery stores and queries. Looker visualizes and governs the business view. If the question is about <strong>people exploring and reporting</strong>, pick Looker over BigQuery <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q20\">Exam 1 · Q20</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Leading serverless warehousing technology\" describes BigQuery, not Looker <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q8\">Exam 2 · Q8</a>.</li>\n<li>AlloyDB Omni runs a database elsewhere. It doesn't query data across clouds <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q14\">Exam 2 · Q14</a>.</li>\n<li>Cloud SQL, Firestore and Cloud Storage don't do petabyte-scale analytics <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q41\">Exam 3 · Q41</a>.</li>\n<li>BigQuery doesn't encrypt network links or convert records into audio <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q27\">Exam 2 · Q27</a>.</li>\n<li>Looker has its own permissions, but access management for Google Cloud resources is <strong>IAM</strong>, and rollback or recovery belongs to databases and storage. Neither is Looker's benefit <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q8\">Exam 2 · Q8</a>.</li>\n<li>Compute Engine and Spot VMs are infrastructure. You could install BI software on them, but they are not a BI product <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q5\">Exam 2 · Q5</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q48\">Exam 1 · Q48</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d2l9","title":"Processing data: batch vs streaming, Dataflow and serverless Spark","html":"<ul>\n<li>Serverless does <strong>not</strong> mean full control of compute, no need for analysis, or error-free data <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q39\">Exam 4 · Q39</a>.</li>\n<li>Pub/Sub moves messages. Bigtable and Cloud Storage store data. None of them is the transform pipeline <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q23\">Exam 2 · Q23</a>.</li>\n<li>The <strong>clusters</strong> option of the Spark service still needs cluster management. Cloud Data Fusion is a visual integration tool, not serverless Spark. Installing Spark on Compute Engine VMs is the most work of all <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q44\">Exam 1 · Q44</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q34\">Exam 3 · Q34</a>.</li>\n<li>BigQuery is serverless too, but it runs SQL, not existing Spark code <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q44\">Exam 1 · Q44</a>.</li>\n<li>Switching to NoSQL or filtering data doesn't make insights real time <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q4\">Exam 6 · Q4</a>.</li>\n<li>Moving data into Google Cloud uses <strong>Storage Transfer Service</strong> (online copies from Amazon S3, Azure Blob Storage, on-premises storage or other buckets) <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q28\">Exam 1 · Q28</a>. Transfer Appliance and Database Migration Service <em>(not in the question bank)</em> cover offline bulk transfer and database moves.</li>\n</ul>"}],"questionable":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Item</th>\n<th>Issue</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q9\">Exam 3 · Q9</a></strong></td>\n<td>\"Stores geographically distributed copies\" is only true for <strong>multi-region and dual-region</strong> buckets. A regional bucket keeps redundant copies across zones in one region. Pick it on the exam, but know the condition.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q39\">Exam 3 · Q39</a></strong></td>\n<td>AlloyDB is <strong>PostgreSQL-compatible</strong>, but AlloyDB itself is not open source. The \"open-source engine\" framing means compatibility with open-source PostgreSQL. Spanner also has a PostgreSQL interface, so the question leans on AlloyDB's closer compatibility.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q3\">Exam 1 · Q3</a></strong></td>\n<td>\"View data history to see transactions\" is vague wording for the real benefit: a digital record of every purchase that can be reconciled. The other options are clearly wrong, so the key stands.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q1\">Exam 1 · Q1</a></strong>, <strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q35\">Exam 1 · Q35</a></strong></td>\n<td>The bank uses both \"data value chain\" and \"data supply chain\" for the same idea. Treat them as synonyms.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"},"glossary":[{"term":"Accuracy","definition":"Data quality dimension: stored values match the real-world source of truth.","exam_context":"An order total that must match the original invoice tests accuracy.","lesson":"d2l4","domain":2},{"term":"AI-ready data","definition":"Data that is unified, clean, trusted and governed so it can reliably feed AI and ML workloads.","exam_context":"Fragmented, ungoverned data plus an AI goal points to AI-ready data, an AI-ready data foundation or platform.","lesson":"d2l2","domain":2},{"term":"AlloyDB for PostgreSQL","definition":"Fully managed, PostgreSQL-compatible database with higher performance than standard PostgreSQL and a built-in columnar engine.","exam_context":"Pick it to move a slowing PostgreSQL app with minimal code changes or to run analytics on live operational data.","lesson":"d2l6","domain":2},{"term":"AlloyDB Omni","definition":"Downloadable, self-managed edition of AlloyDB that runs in your own data center, at the edge or in other clouds.","exam_context":"PostgreSQL-compatible Google database that must stay on-premises for compliance.","lesson":"d2l6","domain":2},{"term":"Archive storage","definition":"Coldest, cheapest Cloud Storage class for data read less than once a year; 365-day minimum storage duration and higher retrieval costs.","exam_context":"Disaster-recovery exports read once a year during testing.","lesson":"d2l7","domain":2},{"term":"Autoclass","definition":"Cloud Storage feature that moves each object between storage classes automatically based on its actual access.","exam_context":"Unpredictable access and no wish to write rules means Autoclass, not lifecycle management.","lesson":"d2l7","domain":2},{"term":"Batch processing","definition":"Processing data in groups on a schedule or after enough has accumulated, such as daily reports or backfills.","exam_context":"Quarterly or fortnightly batch jobs can't let managers react as demand happens; switch to streaming.","lesson":"d2l9","domain":2},{"term":"BigQuery","definition":"Google Cloud's serverless data warehouse for fast SQL analysis at petabyte scale; separates storage and compute and includes BigQuery ML.","exam_context":"Serverless warehouse, large datasets, SQL analysis without managing servers.","lesson":"d2l8","domain":2},{"term":"BigQuery ML","definition":"BigQuery feature for creating and running machine learning models with SQL, inside the warehouse.","exam_context":"Predictions from operational data come from tools like BigQuery ML, not from Cloud SQL itself.","lesson":"d2l8","domain":2},{"term":"BigQuery Omni","definition":"Runs BigQuery SQL on data that stays in AWS or Azure storage (Amazon S3, Azure Blob Storage), using BigLake tables.","exam_context":"Analyze data in another public cloud without copying it into Google Cloud first.","lesson":"d2l8","domain":2},{"term":"Bigtable","definition":"Fully managed wide-column NoSQL database for very large, low-latency, high-throughput workloads such as IoT time series and financial data.","exam_context":"A recurring distractor: not relational, not PostgreSQL-compatible, not for media files or pipelines.","lesson":"d2l6","domain":2},{"term":"Cloud Data Fusion","definition":"Fully managed, visual (low-code) service for building ETL and ELT data integration pipelines.","exam_context":"A distractor for serverless Spark: it builds pipelines graphically but isn't a Spark runtime.","lesson":"d2l9","domain":2},{"term":"Cloud SQL","definition":"Fully managed MySQL, PostgreSQL and SQL Server service.","exam_context":"Cost-effective relational database for a single-region app; it integrates with BI tools for insights.","lesson":"d2l6","domain":2},{"term":"Cloud Storage","definition":"Object storage for unstructured files such as images, video, audio and backups.","exam_context":"Large archives of media files, with Nearline, Coldline and Archive classes to cut cost.","lesson":"d2l7","domain":2},{"term":"Columnar engine","definition":"AlloyDB feature that keeps data in a column format in memory to speed up analytical scans, joins and aggregates on live transactional data.","exam_context":"PostgreSQL-compatible database with a columnar engine for analytics without moving data means AlloyDB.","lesson":"d2l6","domain":2},{"term":"Completeness","definition":"Data quality dimension: all required records and attributes are present.","exam_context":"Missing age, location, date of birth or diagnosis codes means a completeness problem.","lesson":"d2l4","domain":2},{"term":"Consistency","definition":"Data quality dimension: the same fact agrees across systems and records.","exam_context":"A distractor in timeliness questions; it applies when two systems disagree, not when data is old.","lesson":"d2l4","domain":2},{"term":"Customer-managed encryption keys (CMEK)","definition":"Encryption keys you create and control in Cloud KMS, used instead of Google's default keys.","exam_context":"Encryption at rest is on by default and free; CMEK only adds control, it isn't needed to turn encryption on.","lesson":"d2l7","domain":2},{"term":"Data governance","definition":"Program of policies, roles and controls for data quality, access, lifecycle and compliance.","exam_context":"The answer when a company wants more value, controlled costs and regulatory compliance from its data.","lesson":"d2l2","domain":2},{"term":"Data lake","definition":"Repository that stores large volumes of raw data in its original format, of any structure.","exam_context":"Keep everything now and decide how to process it later.","lesson":"d2l5","domain":2},{"term":"Data Studio","definition":"Google's free, self-service dashboarding and reporting tool, with a paid Data Studio Pro edition; called Looker Studio from 2022 until April 2026.","exam_context":"Dashboards on Cloud SQL or BigQuery data come from Data Studio or Looker, not from the database.","lesson":"d2l8","domain":2},{"term":"Data supply chain","definition":"The end-to-end journey of data from creation and collection through processing and storage to analysis and use; also called the data value chain.","exam_context":"Questions describing the full lifecycle from creation to AI-driven decisions.","lesson":"d2l1","domain":2},{"term":"Data warehouse","definition":"Store of processed, modeled data optimized for analytical queries and reporting.","exam_context":"Analysts running ad-hoc queries, custom reports and dashboards.","lesson":"d2l5","domain":2},{"term":"Dataflow","definition":"Fully managed, serverless service for batch and streaming data pipelines, built on Apache Beam.","exam_context":"Transform data before loading it into a warehouse, scaling automatically with minimal operations.","lesson":"d2l9","domain":2},{"term":"ETL and ELT","definition":"Extract, transform, load (transform before loading into the warehouse) versus extract, load, transform (load raw data first, transform inside the warehouse).","exam_context":"Transforming data before loading it into a warehouse is a Dataflow-style ETL pipeline.","lesson":"d2l9","domain":2},{"term":"Firestore","definition":"Serverless NoSQL document database for web and mobile apps, with real-time sync, offline support and Firebase integration.","exam_context":"Flexible, scalable NoSQL for user profiles, posts, comments and app settings.","lesson":"d2l6","domain":2},{"term":"First-party data","definition":"Data a company collects directly from its own customers.","exam_context":"Website clicks, orders and loyalty sign-ups collected by the company itself.","lesson":"d2l3","domain":2},{"term":"HTAP","definition":"Hybrid transactional and analytical processing: running OLTP transactions and OLAP queries on the same database.","exam_context":"AlloyDB's columnar engine gives fast analytics on live PostgreSQL transactional data.","lesson":"d2l6","domain":2},{"term":"Knowledge Catalog","definition":"Google Cloud's data catalog for governance (formerly Dataplex Universal Catalog, renamed April 2026): discovery, metadata, lineage, data quality and business glossaries.","exam_context":"A distractor in storage questions: it describes and governs data but doesn't store objects.","lesson":"d2l2","domain":2},{"term":"Looker","definition":"Google Cloud's enterprise business intelligence platform for modeling, exploring and visualizing data through a governed semantic model; also supports embedded analytics.","exam_context":"Self-service BI for business users, including across Google Cloud, AWS and Azure.","lesson":"d2l8","domain":2},{"term":"Managed Service for Apache Spark","definition":"Google Cloud's managed Spark and Hadoop service (formerly Dataproc), with cluster and serverless options.","exam_context":"Spark jobs without provisioning or tuning clusters means its serverless option.","lesson":"d2l9","domain":2},{"term":"Multi-region bucket","definition":"Cloud Storage location type that stores data redundantly across a large area such as the US, EU or Asia.","exam_context":"Users in many countries plus high availability means a multi-region (or dual-region) bucket.","lesson":"d2l7","domain":2},{"term":"Non-relational database","definition":"Database (NoSQL) with a flexible data model rather than fixed related tables.","exam_context":"Its defining feature is a flexible data model for changing structures.","lesson":"d2l5","domain":2},{"term":"Object Lifecycle Management","definition":"Cloud Storage rules you define, on conditions such as age, creation date or number of newer versions, that change an object's class or delete it.","exam_context":"Known patterns ('after 45 days') mean lifecycle rules; unpredictable access with no rules means Autoclass.","lesson":"d2l7","domain":2},{"term":"OLAP","definition":"Online analytical processing: large scans and aggregations over historical data for reporting and analysis.","exam_context":"Petabyte-scale analysis and ad-hoc reporting point to a warehouse such as BigQuery.","lesson":"d2l5","domain":2},{"term":"OLTP","definition":"Online transaction processing: many small, fast reads and writes of individual records such as orders and payments.","exam_context":"App transactions point to an operational database such as Cloud SQL, AlloyDB or Spanner, not BigQuery.","lesson":"d2l5","domain":2},{"term":"Openness and interoperability","definition":"Google Cloud's commitment to open standards, open source and multicloud tools.","exam_context":"Data platforms working across clouds without vendor lock-in.","lesson":"d2l2","domain":2},{"term":"Pub/Sub","definition":"Managed messaging service that ingests event streams and decouples the systems that send and receive them.","exam_context":"It moves messages but doesn't transform them; the usual streaming pipeline is Pub/Sub, then Dataflow, then BigQuery.","lesson":"d2l9","domain":2},{"term":"Second-party data","definition":"Another organization's first-party data, shared directly with you by that partner.","exam_context":"A partner securely shares its customer data with you.","lesson":"d2l3","domain":2},{"term":"Semi-structured data","definition":"Data without a fixed relational schema that still carries tags, keys or nesting, such as JSON and XML.","exam_context":"Logs and config files in JSON or XML.","lesson":"d2l3","domain":2},{"term":"Spanner","definition":"Relational, strongly consistent (ACID), horizontally scalable database that replicates across regions; also supports key-value, graph and search, and offers a PostgreSQL interface.","exam_context":"Global transactional apps needing consistency and availability across continents. Too costly for a single-region app, and its PostgreSQL interface still needs app changes.","lesson":"d2l6","domain":2},{"term":"Storage Transfer Service","definition":"Service that moves or syncs data into Cloud Storage from Amazon S3, Azure Blob Storage, on-premises storage or other buckets.","exam_context":"A distractor in Autoclass questions: it transfers data but doesn't change storage classes.","lesson":"d2l9","domain":2},{"term":"Streaming analytics","definition":"Processing data records continuously as they arrive to give near real-time insight.","exam_context":"Reacting to demand as it happens means switching from batch to streaming.","lesson":"d2l9","domain":2},{"term":"Structured data","definition":"Data organized into fixed fields, rows and columns with a predefined schema.","exam_context":"Blood pressure history, stock levels and star ratings are structured; they fit a relational table without processing.","lesson":"d2l3","domain":2},{"term":"Third-party data","definition":"Data bought from an outside provider that has no direct relationship with your customers.","exam_context":"Purchased, aggregated audience segments.","lesson":"d2l3","domain":2},{"term":"Timeliness","definition":"Data quality dimension: data is current enough and available when needed; also called freshness.","exam_context":"A fashion model trained on three-year-old data suffers from poor timeliness.","lesson":"d2l4","domain":2},{"term":"Uniqueness","definition":"Data quality dimension: each real-world entity is recorded once.","exam_context":"The same scooter stored twice under different asset codes.","lesson":"d2l4","domain":2},{"term":"Unstructured data","definition":"Data with no predefined model, such as free text, transcripts, audio, video and images.","exam_context":"Customer written feedback and support call transcripts are unstructured.","lesson":"d2l3","domain":2},{"term":"Validity","definition":"Data quality dimension: values conform to the expected format, type, range or list of allowed values.","exam_context":"Email values missing the '@' character fail validity.","lesson":"d2l4","domain":2}]},{"n":3,"title":"Innovating with Google Cloud Artificial Intelligence","short":"AI & ML","weight":"~16%","desc":"AI/ML fundamentals, pre-trained APIs, AutoML, Vertex AI, generative AI and agents, and responsible AI.","lessons":[{"id":"d3l1","num":1,"title":"AI and ML fundamentals and where they add business value","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Artificial intelligence (AI)</strong> is any system that can perform tasks that normally require <strong>human cognition</strong>: seeing, understanding language, reasoning, recommending <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q34\">Exam 6 · Q34</a>.</li>\n<li>AI is the broad field. <strong>Machine learning</strong> is one part of it, <strong>deep learning</strong> is a part of ML, and <strong>generative AI</strong> is one area inside that. \"Uses analytics only to make predictions\" is too narrow to define AI <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q34\">Exam 6 · Q34</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q24\">Exam 3 · Q24</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Machine learning (ML)</strong> is the subset of AI where a system <strong>learns patterns from data</strong> instead of following hand-written rules. Classifying symptom photos into condition categories is an ML capability, not a logging or hosting product (<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q5\">Exam 5 · Q5</a>, see ⚠️).</li>\n<li>ML <strong>improves as more data arrives</strong>. Personalized suggestions that should get more accurate over time point to ML, not to static trend analysis or one-off regression <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q18\">Exam 6 · Q18</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q29\">Exam 5 · Q29</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>ML vs business intelligence (BI):</strong> BI looks at <strong>historical data</strong> through reports and dashboards (what happened). ML <strong>learns from data</strong> to make <strong>automated predictions or decisions</strong> (what will happen, what to do) <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q14\">Exam 6 · Q14</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Kinds of analytics:</strong> <strong>descriptive analytics</strong> summarizes what already happened (reports, dashboards, BI) <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q3\">Exam 3 · Q3</a>. <strong>Predictive analytics</strong> uses historical and current data to forecast outcomes, such as churn <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q40\">Exam 3 · Q40</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q29\">Exam 2 · Q29</a>. Neither one creates content (that's gen AI) or takes actions (that's agentic AI).</li>\n<li>ML unlocks value from <strong>unstructured data</strong> such as audio, images, video and free text. Detecting caller sentiment from support-call recordings is the classic example. Spreadsheet records, purchase histories and payment logs are structured <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q37\">Exam 1 · Q37</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Predicting churn</strong> = train a model on the demographics and behavior of customers who already left, then score current customers. Dashboards, surveys and last year's feedback report only describe the past <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q29\">Exam 2 · Q29</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Combine data sources</strong> before predicting. Clicks, installs, web visits and social data analyzed together give better predictions than separate silos or archived data <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q21\">Exam 5 · Q21</a>.</li>\n<li>Google Cloud offers <strong>AI tools for every skill level</strong>: ready-made APIs for developers, SQL-based ML for analysts, AutoML for people without ML coding skills, and full custom training for data scientists <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q2\">Exam 1 · Q2</a>.</li>\n<li>Common ML problem types: <strong>classification</strong> (which category?), <strong>regression/forecasting</strong> (what number?), <strong>clustering</strong> (which items are similar?), <strong>recommendation</strong> and <strong>anomaly detection</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q24\">Exam 3 · Q24</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Supervised learning</strong> learns from <strong>labeled examples</strong>, such as product photos tagged \"shoes\" or \"bags\" <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q28\">Exam 4 · Q28</a>. <strong>Unsupervised learning</strong> finds groupings in unlabeled data, but even it needs real patterns in the data to find <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q29\">Exam 5 · Q29</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Good and bad uses of ML</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Scenario</th>\n<th>ML fit?</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Personalized product recommendations from browsing, purchases, ratings</td>\n<td>✅</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q29\">Exam 5 · Q29</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q18\">Exam 6 · Q18</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Predict which active customers will leave</td>\n<td>✅</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q29\">Exam 2 · Q29</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sentiment from call-center audio</td>\n<td>✅ (unstructured)</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q37\">Exam 1 · Q37</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Grouping data with no useful past examples or patterns</td>\n<td>❌ Nothing to learn from</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q29\">Exam 5 · Q29</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tasks that mainly depend on human judgment, life experience, intuition</td>\n<td>❌ ML can support, humans decide</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q29\">Exam 5 · Q29</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Final decisions on complex ethical dilemmas</td>\n<td>❌ Keep humans accountable</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q29\">Exam 5 · Q29</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Learns from data\", \"predicts\", \"gets better with more data\", \"automated decisions\" → <strong>machine learning</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q14\">Exam 6 · Q14</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q18\">Exam 6 · Q18</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Dashboards\", \"reports\", \"compare past performance\" → <strong>BI</strong> / descriptive analytics <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q14\">Exam 6 · Q14</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q3\">Exam 3 · Q3</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Images, audio, video, free text\" → <strong>unstructured data</strong>, a strong ML use case <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q37\">Exam 1 · Q37</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Swapped definitions: \"BI predicts, ML only visualizes\" or \"ML only works on structured tables\" are both reversed. The BI vs ML difference is not about hardware (TPUs vs CPUs) either <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q14\">Exam 6 · Q14</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Any system that receives real-time data\" or \"organizes data into a structured format\" describes pipelines, not AI <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q34\">Exam 6 · Q34</a>.</li>\n<li>Recommendations and fraud detection are valid ML uses, but they run on <strong>structured</strong> data. Don't pick them when the question asks about <strong>unstructured</strong> data <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q37\">Exam 1 · Q37</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Trends analysis</strong> describes past patterns and <strong>data automation</strong> moves data. Neither learns each user's preferences <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q18\">Exam 6 · Q18</a>.</li>\n<li>A <strong>survey</strong> can feed a churn model as one input, but on its own it depends on who answers and doesn't predict who will leave <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q29\">Exam 2 · Q29</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"AI is any system that performs tasks that normally need human cognition, and ML is the part of AI that learns patterns from data. This lesson separates ML from business intelligence and shows which business problems ML suits, especially those involving unstructured data.","objectives":["Define AI and machine learning in business terms","Distinguish ML (learn and predict) from BI (report on the past)","Recognize good ML use cases such as recommendations, churn and sentiment from audio","Spot tasks ML is a poor fit for","Tell descriptive analytics, predictive analytics, supervised and unsupervised learning apart"],"takeaways":["AI = tasks that need human cognition. ML = learns from data to make predictions or decisions.","BI uses dashboards and reports on historical data. ML predicts and automates.","Descriptive analytics reports what happened. Predictive analytics forecasts. AI is the broad field, with ML, deep learning and gen AI inside it.","Unstructured data such as audio, images and free text is where ML adds unique value.","Predict churn by training a model on customers who already left.","Supervised learning uses labeled examples. Unsupervised learning finds groupings in unlabeled data.","ML needs past examples to learn from and should not make final ethical decisions.","Google Cloud offers AI tools for every skill level, from APIs to custom training."],"questions":["e1q2","e1q37","e2q29","e3q3","e3q24","e3q40","e4q28","e5q5","e5q21","e5q29","e6q14","e6q18","e6q34"]},{"id":"d3l2","num":2,"title":"Data for ML: quality, quantity and preparation","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>A model is only as good as its data. <strong>More (good) training data</strong> is the main lever for better prediction accuracy, because the model sees more examples and variations and <strong>generalizes</strong> better to new data. More storage, bandwidth or faster CPUs change speed or cost, not accuracy <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q26\">Exam 6 · Q26</a>.</li>\n<li>The extra data must be <strong>relevant, high quality and correctly labeled</strong>. Adding noisy or irrelevant data may not help <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q26\">Exam 6 · Q26</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Prepare data before training.</strong> When sources use different column names, date formats and file structures, <strong>transform the data into a consistent format</strong> (a common schema). Don't throw data away, split it into one model per format, or hope a model handles every format by itself <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q17\">Exam 2 · Q17</a>.</li>\n<li>Typical preparation steps: standardize date formats, rename fields, convert data types, handle missing values, remove duplicates, and normalize category values so each feature means the same thing everywhere <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q17\">Exam 2 · Q17</a>.</li>\n<li>Keep the <strong>full history</strong>. Older data shows seasonality, holidays and long-term trends that a forecast needs <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q17\">Exam 2 · Q17</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q21\">Exam 5 · Q21</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Breadth matters.</strong> Combine related datasets so the model sees the full picture. BigQuery and BigQuery ML can combine large datasets and predict where the data is stored <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q21\">Exam 5 · Q21</a>.</li>\n<li>Training data must be <strong>representative</strong>: it should cover every condition the model will meet. A storm model that predicts across several regions needs <strong>all weather data, evenly from all regions</strong>, normal days as well as extreme events <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q35\">Exam 4 · Q35</a>.</li>\n<li><em>(not in the question bank)</em> Data quality dimensions: <strong>accuracy, completeness, consistency, timeliness, relevance and representativeness</strong>. Biased or unrepresentative data produces biased predictions (see lesson 10).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Improve accuracy\" → <strong>more and better training data</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q26\">Exam 6 · Q26</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Different formats, column names, date styles\" → <strong>transform to a consistent format</strong> first <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q17\">Exam 2 · Q17</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Several related sources\" → <strong>combine them</strong>, then predict <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q21\">Exam 5 · Q21</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Infrastructure answers (storage, bandwidth, CPU) to a model-accuracy question. Hardware cuts training time, not prediction quality <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q26\">Exam 6 · Q26</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Use only the most recent 10 months\" throws away history that a long-range forecast needs <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q17\">Exam 2 · Q17</a>.</li>\n<li>\"One model per file format\" splits the data into smaller, less representative sets and leaves more models to build and maintain. File format isn't the pattern you want to predict <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q17\">Exam 2 · Q17</a>.</li>\n<li>Storing or archiving data in a warehouse or data lake isn't analysis. Archiving older data also removes history the model needs <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q21\">Exam 5 · Q21</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Only severe weather data\" leaves the model no normal cases to compare against <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q35\">Exam 4 · Q35</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"Model quality depends on data quality and quantity. This lesson covers why more training data improves accuracy, why inconsistent sources must be transformed first, and why combined, representative data beats narrow slices.","objectives":["Explain why more and better training data improves prediction accuracy","Prepare inconsistent data by transforming it into a consistent format","Describe why training data should be combined and representative","List the common data preparation steps and why full history matters"],"takeaways":["To improve accuracy, add more relevant, good-quality training data. Storage, bandwidth and CPU speed don't help.","Different column names and date formats? Transform to one consistent format before training.","Preparation means standardizing dates, field names, types and categories, handling missing values and removing duplicates. Keep the full history for seasonality.","Combine related datasets before predicting.","Training data should cover all conditions the model will see, not only extreme cases."],"questions":["e2q17","e4q35","e5q21","e6q26"]},{"id":"d3l3","num":3,"title":"Choosing an approach: buy, adapt or build","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Google Cloud offers a spectrum, from least to most effort: <strong>pre-trained APIs</strong> and pre-built agents → <strong>BigQuery ML</strong> (SQL) → <strong>AutoML</strong> (your labeled data, no code) → <strong>custom training</strong> (your code, full control) <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q2\">Exam 1 · Q2</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Pre-built / pre-trained</strong> (APIs, agents, templates): <strong>fastest to implement</strong>, little ML expertise needed, but <strong>less business differentiation</strong>, because competitors can use the same thing <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q2\">Exam 6 · Q2</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q23\">Exam 3 · Q23</a>. You still need people to configure, integrate, secure, test and govern the solution, just not a large data-science team <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q2\">Exam 6 · Q2</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Custom models</strong>: <strong>more effort and expertise</strong> (collect and prepare data, train, evaluate, tune, deploy, monitor, maintain), but <strong>higher differentiation</strong>, because they encode your proprietary data and needs <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q1\">Exam 4 · Q1</a>.</li>\n<li>Pre-trained APIs cover <strong>common tasks</strong> (vision, speech, translation, text, documents). Predicting from <strong>your own business data</strong>, such as rental demand from booking history, needs a model trained on that data <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q36\">Exam 6 · Q36</a>.</li>\n<li>Neither approach changes <strong>CapEx vs OpEx</strong> (both are cloud pay-as-you-go), and nothing guarantees 100% accuracy. Accuracy depends on data quality, model design and monitoring <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q1\">Exam 4 · Q1</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q2\">Exam 6 · Q2</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Approach comparison</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Approach</th>\n<th>Who</th>\n<th>Data needed</th>\n<th>Speed</th>\n<th>Differentiation</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Pre-trained APIs</td>\n<td>App developers</td>\n<td>None to train</td>\n<td>Fastest</td>\n<td>Low</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q23\">Exam 3 · Q23</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q46\">Exam 5 · Q46</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pre-built agents/templates</td>\n<td>Business + developers</td>\n<td>Your business content</td>\n<td>Fast</td>\n<td>Lower</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q2\">Exam 6 · Q2</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>BigQuery ML</td>\n<td>SQL analysts, DBAs</td>\n<td>Tables in BigQuery</td>\n<td>Fast</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q44\">Exam 2 · Q44</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q36\">Exam 6 · Q36</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>AutoML</td>\n<td>Teams with labeled data, no ML coders</td>\n<td>Labeled examples</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q33\">Exam 6 · Q33</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q28\">Exam 4 · Q28</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Custom training</td>\n<td>Data scientists, ML engineers</td>\n<td>Your data + your code</td>\n<td>Slowest</td>\n<td>Highest</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q41\">Exam 5 · Q41</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q1\">Exam 4 · Q1</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"As quickly as possible\", \"without building or training a model\" → <strong>pre-trained API</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q23\">Exam 3 · Q23</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Data in BigQuery\" + \"knows SQL\" + \"no data scientists\" → <strong>BigQuery ML</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q36\">Exam 6 · Q36</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Labeled images/text\" + \"no ML experience / can't write training code\" → <strong>AutoML</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q33\">Exam 6 · Q33</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Data scientists writing their own training code\" → <strong>custom training</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q41\">Exam 5 · Q41</a>.</li>\n<li>Tradeoff questions: pre-built = <strong>speed vs differentiation</strong>; custom = <strong>effort/expertise vs differentiation</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q2\">Exam 6 · Q2</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q1\">Exam 4 · Q1</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Answers that put pre-built solutions on the slow, expert-heavy side (or custom models on the easy side) are reversed <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q2\">Exam 6 · Q2</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q1\">Exam 4 · Q1</a>.</li>\n<li>Any option mentioning a shift to <strong>CapEx</strong> is a distractor in this domain <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q1\">Exam 4 · Q1</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q2\">Exam 6 · Q2</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Models that always require custom training\" contradicts the whole point of Google's tiered offering <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q2\">Exam 1 · Q2</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"Google Cloud offers a spectrum from pre-trained APIs and pre-built agents to BigQuery ML, AutoML and fully custom training. This lesson teaches how to pick a point on that spectrum and the speed-versus-differentiation tradeoff behind it.","objectives":["Order the AI options from least to most effort","Match team skills and data to the right approach","Explain the tradeoff between pre-built and custom solutions"],"takeaways":["Pre-built APIs and agents are fastest but give less business differentiation.","Custom models need more effort and expertise but give the most differentiation.","Pre-trained APIs cover common tasks. Predicting from your own business data needs a model trained on it.","SQL plus BigQuery means BigQuery ML. Labeled data with no ML coders means AutoML.","Neither approach shifts spending to CapEx or guarantees 100% accuracy."],"questions":["e1q2","e2q44","e3q23","e4q1","e4q28","e5q41","e5q46","e6q2","e6q33","e6q36"]},{"id":"d3l4","num":4,"title":"Pre-trained APIs: Vision, Speech, Translation, Natural Language","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Pre-trained APIs</strong> are models Google has already trained. You send data, you get results. No training data, no ML team, and the fastest route to value <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q23\">Exam 3 · Q23</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q46\">Exam 5 · Q46</a>.</li>\n<li>They make AI easy to adopt because they <strong>don't require ML specialists</strong>. Developers call them like any other API <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q46\">Exam 5 · Q46</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Vision API</strong>: <strong>label detection</strong>, <strong>object localization</strong>, <strong>landmark detection</strong>, logos, faces, <strong>text in images (OCR)</strong>, explicit-content detection. Use it to auto-tag photo libraries and read text in photos <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q1\">Exam 5 · Q1</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q24\">Exam 6 · Q24</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q44\">Exam 2 · Q44</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Sensitive Data Protection</strong> (formerly Cloud DLP) finds and redacts sensitive data such as <strong>payment card numbers</strong> and other personal information. With Vision OCR it can flag ID or payment cards caught in uploaded photos, with no model training <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q23\">Exam 3 · Q23</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Speech-to-Text API</strong>: turns recorded or live audio into text, for example call recordings before sentiment analysis <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q14\">Exam 4 · Q14</a>. <strong>Text-to-Speech</strong> does the reverse: natural-sounding audio from text <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q13\">Exam 2 · Q13</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q5\">Exam 6 · Q5</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Cloud Translation API</strong>: translates text between languages, such as product pages into 18 languages. It can also <strong>detect the language</strong> of a text, and it can translate transcripts after Speech-to-Text <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q37\">Exam 6 · Q37</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q47\">Exam 6 · Q47</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q14\">Exam 4 · Q14</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Natural Language API</strong>: sentiment, entities, <strong>content classification</strong>, syntax analysis and topic discovery in text such as chat transcripts <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q47\">Exam 6 · Q47</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q36\">Exam 1 · Q36</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Scanned forms</strong>: APIs (Vision OCR, or <strong>Document AI</strong> for structured extraction) turn scans into <strong>searchable digital text</strong>. Document AI also pulls out <strong>key-value pairs, entities, tables and fields</strong> such as names and dates from forms, invoices, receipts, contracts and ID documents <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q17\">Exam 6 · Q17</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q36\">Exam 1 · Q36</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Video Intelligence API</strong> labels <strong>video</strong> content: objects, shots, explicit content. It is not for still photos <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q41\">Exam 1 · Q41</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Which API?</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Input</th>\n<th>Need</th>\n<th>API</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Photos</td>\n<td>Labels, objects, places, landmarks, text</td>\n<td>Vision API</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q1\">Exam 5 · Q1</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q24\">Exam 6 · Q24</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Photos</td>\n<td>Detect ID or payment cards, fast, no model</td>\n<td>Pre-trained APIs (Vision OCR + Sensitive Data Protection)</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q23\">Exam 3 · Q23</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Audio recordings</td>\n<td>Transcribe to text</td>\n<td>Speech-to-Text API</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q14\">Exam 4 · Q14</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Text</td>\n<td>Spoken audio output</td>\n<td>Text-to-Speech</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q5\">Exam 6 · Q5</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Text</td>\n<td>Translate into other languages</td>\n<td>Cloud Translation API</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q37\">Exam 6 · Q37</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Text</td>\n<td>Topics, sentiment, entities</td>\n<td>Natural Language API</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q47\">Exam 6 · Q47</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Scanned forms</td>\n<td>Searchable text and key fields</td>\n<td>Vision OCR / Document AI</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q17\">Exam 6 · Q17</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Video</td>\n<td>Labels, shots, objects</td>\n<td>Video Intelligence API</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q41\">Exam 1 · Q41</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Generic categories (objects, places, labels, common items) and \"without training\" → <strong>Vision API</strong>, not AutoML Vision <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q1\">Exam 5 · Q1</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q24\">Exam 6 · Q24</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Your own</strong> categories (beaches vs mountains for your app, shoes vs bags in your catalog, faulty vs acceptable parts from your factory) → <strong>AutoML</strong>, see lesson 5. A generic model doesn't know your company-specific patterns <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q41\">Exam 1 · Q41</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q28\">Exam 4 · Q28</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q33\">Exam 6 · Q33</a>.</li>\n<li>Audio → Speech-to-Text first, then Natural Language for sentiment <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q14\">Exam 4 · Q14</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q37\">Exam 1 · Q37</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Pre-trained APIs still need <strong>input data</strong> and permissions. They don't skip data validation or need fewer security permissions <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q46\">Exam 5 · Q46</a>.</li>\n<li>Pre-trained APIs can't forecast from your own tabular data, such as holiday demand for your listings <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q36\">Exam 6 · Q36</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Convert documents into unstructured data\" is backwards. Scans are already unstructured, and the API makes them searchable and structured <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q17\">Exam 6 · Q17</a>.</li>\n<li>Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform and BigQuery ML can do image work, but they are overkill when a simple pre-trained API fits <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q24\">Exam 6 · Q24</a>.</li>\n<li>Translation and Speech-to-Text don't find topics in text that is already written <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q47\">Exam 6 · Q47</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"Pre-trained APIs give ready-made ML through a simple API call with no training. This lesson maps common inputs (photos, audio, text, video, scanned forms) to the Vision, Speech-to-Text, Text-to-Speech, Translation, Natural Language, Video Intelligence and Document AI services, plus Sensitive Data Protection for card numbers and personal data.","objectives":["Choose the correct pre-trained API for images, audio, translation and text analysis","Explain why pre-trained APIs speed up AI adoption","Tell generic pre-trained labeling apart from custom AutoML categories"],"takeaways":["Generic labels, objects, landmarks and text in photos mean Vision API.","Card numbers or ID data in photos: Vision OCR plus Sensitive Data Protection, no training needed.","Audio to text means Speech-to-Text. Text into other languages means Translation API.","Topics and sentiment in written text mean Natural Language API.","Document AI extracts key-value pairs, entities and tables from forms, invoices and receipts. Video Intelligence handles video, not photos.","Scanned forms become searchable text with extracted fields through APIs such as Document AI.","Pre-trained APIs remove the need for ML specialists, not for input data or permissions."],"questions":["e1q36","e1q37","e1q41","e2q13","e2q44","e3q23","e4q14","e4q28","e5q1","e5q46","e6q17","e6q24","e6q33","e6q36","e6q37","e6q47"]},{"id":"d3l5","num":5,"title":"BigQuery ML, AutoML and custom training","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>BigQuery ML</strong> lets you <strong>create, train, evaluate and run ML models with SQL</strong> (GoogleSQL statements such as <code>CREATE MODEL</code>, or the console) inside BigQuery. The data stays where it is, so there's no export and no Python or Java <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q44\">Exam 2 · Q44</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q36\">Exam 6 · Q36</a>.</li>\n<li>It works on structured or semi-structured data in BigQuery: classification, regression, <strong>forecasting</strong>, clustering, recommendation and anomaly detection <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q1\">Exam 5 · Q1</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q23\">Exam 3 · Q23</a>.</li>\n<li>Typical uses: demand forecasting, churn, <strong>predictive maintenance</strong> (a bike-share predicting when docks need repair, cutting needless part swaps, which also helps sustainability) <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q15\">Exam 2 · Q15</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q36\">Exam 6 · Q36</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>AutoML</strong> (now <strong>AutoML on Agent Platform</strong>; older names <strong>AutoML Vision</strong>, <strong>AutoML Natural Language</strong>) trains a <strong>custom model on your labeled data</strong> without you writing training code <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q28\">Exam 4 · Q28</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q33\">Exam 6 · Q33</a>.</li>\n<li>Images in <strong>your own categories</strong>: travel photos into beaches, mountains, landmarks, forests <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q41\">Exam 1 · Q41</a>, catalog photos into shoes, bags, watches <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q28\">Exam 4 · Q28</a>, faulty vs acceptable parts on an assembly line <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q33\">Exam 6 · Q33</a>.</li>\n<li>Text in <strong>your own categories</strong>: support messages into billing, feedback, technical issue <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q6\">Exam 2 · Q6</a>.</li>\n<li>It still means preparing and labeling data, training, evaluating and deploying, so it is slower than calling a pre-trained API <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q23\">Exam 3 · Q23</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Custom training</strong> in Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform: data scientists write their <strong>own training code</strong> (for example in Agent Platform notebooks) for full control. It runs as <strong>managed training jobs</strong> with your choice of framework, custom containers, machine types and accelerators (GPUs, TPUs) <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q41\">Exam 5 · Q41</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>TensorFlow</strong> is Google's <strong>open-source, end-to-end ML platform</strong> for building, training and deploying models, with a large ecosystem of tools and community resources. It is the answer for \"open-source ML framework\" <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q9\">Exam 6 · Q9</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q38\">Exam 2 · Q38</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>BigQuery ML vs AutoML vs custom training</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Signal in the question</th>\n<th>Answer</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Data in BigQuery, team knows SQL, no exporting, no Python</td>\n<td>BigQuery ML</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q44\">Exam 2 · Q44</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q36\">Exam 6 · Q36</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q15\">Exam 2 · Q15</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Labeled images/text, custom categories, no ML coding skills</td>\n<td>AutoML</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q41\">Exam 1 · Q41</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q6\">Exam 2 · Q6</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q28\">Exam 4 · Q28</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q33\">Exam 6 · Q33</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Data scientists writing training code</td>\n<td>Custom training</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q41\">Exam 5 · Q41</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Open-source ML library</td>\n<td>TensorFlow</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q9\">Exam 6 · Q9</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"SQL\" + \"BigQuery\" in the same question → <strong>BigQuery ML</strong>, nearly always. Even when AutoML is offered, a SQL team with data in BigQuery points to BigQuery ML, because AutoML means a separate platform workflow instead of SQL <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q36\">Exam 6 · Q36</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Already labeled\" + \"no ML experience\" → <strong>AutoML</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q33\">Exam 6 · Q33</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Custom training code\" → <strong>custom training</strong>, not AutoML and not raw Compute Engine <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q41\">Exam 5 · Q41</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Apache Beam</strong> is an open-source programming model for data pipelines, and <strong>Dataflow</strong> is the managed service that runs Beam pipelines. Both process data. Neither is an ML library. <strong>Cloud Run functions</strong> run small event-driven code, they don't train models <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q9\">Exam 6 · Q9</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Compute Engine</strong> can train a model on VMs, but then you manage provisioning, environments and scaling yourself <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q41\">Exam 5 · Q41</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Dataproc</strong> (now Managed Service for Apache Spark), <strong>Coldline</strong> and <strong>Interconnect</strong> are not ML services <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q28\">Exam 4 · Q28</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Video Intelligence API</strong> is for video, and <strong>Looker</strong> is BI. Neither trains an image classifier <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q41\">Exam 1 · Q41</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Agent Studio</strong> builds agents and <strong>Spanner</strong> is a transactional database. Neither trains models on BigQuery tables with SQL <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q44\">Exam 2 · Q44</a>.</li>\n<li>Security (IAM), APIs (Apigee) or cheaper storage don't create business insight from data. A model does <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q15\">Exam 2 · Q15</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Recommendations from Agent Search\" recommends products, <strong>Pub/Sub</strong> moves messages and <strong>Google Distributed Cloud</strong> runs infrastructure at the edge. None classifies support messages <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q6\">Exam 2 · Q6</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Discovery AI for Retail</strong> improves product search and recommendations. It doesn't detect factory defects <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q33\">Exam 6 · Q33</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"When pre-trained APIs are not enough, Google Cloud offers three ways to build your own model. BigQuery ML uses SQL, AutoML trains on labeled data without code, and custom training runs data scientists' own code, often with TensorFlow.","objectives":["Identify when BigQuery ML is the right choice","Identify when AutoML is the right choice for custom image or text categories","Recognize custom training and TensorFlow scenarios","Reject data-processing tools offered as ML answers"],"takeaways":["Data in BigQuery plus SQL skills means BigQuery ML (CREATE MODEL), with no data export, even when AutoML is also offered.","Labeled examples in your own categories plus no ML coding skills means AutoML.","Data scientists writing training code means custom training: managed jobs with their own framework, containers and accelerators.","TensorFlow is Google's open-source, end-to-end ML platform. Apache Beam is a pipeline model and Dataflow runs it.","Predictive maintenance in BigQuery ML is a typical business-value example."],"questions":["e1q41","e2q6","e2q15","e2q38","e2q44","e3q23","e4q28","e5q1","e5q41","e6q9","e6q33","e6q36"]},{"id":"d3l6","num":6,"title":"Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform (formerly Vertex AI)","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform</strong> is Google Cloud's <strong>unified, managed AI platform</strong> (the dumps' name for what was Vertex AI). It covers the <strong>whole ML lifecycle</strong>: prepare data, train, evaluate, deploy, <strong>monitor</strong> models after deployment, track experiments and manage models <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q36\">Exam 1 · Q36</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q41\">Exam 6 · Q41</a>.</li>\n<li>It is also the platform for <strong>generative AI</strong>: developers <strong>explore foundation models, adapt them with company data, and build, deploy, govern and optimize production-ready agents</strong> without managing infrastructure <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q17\">Exam 4 · Q17</a>.</li>\n<li>It offers <strong>model choice</strong>: Google models (Gemini), <strong>open models</strong>, and <strong>third-party partner models</strong>, so you aren't tied to one provider. Teams compare them on cost, capability, performance and governance <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q3\">Exam 6 · Q3</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Model Garden</strong> is the catalog inside the platform where you discover and use Google, open and partner models <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q3\">Exam 6 · Q3</a>.</li>\n<li>It includes AutoML and custom training (lesson 5) <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q28\">Exam 4 · Q28</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q41\">Exam 5 · Q41</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Agent Studio</strong>: the <strong>no-code / low-code</strong> visual designer in the Google Cloud console for designing, configuring, testing and publishing agents, aimed at analysts without coding skills <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q45\">Exam 3 · Q45</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q44\">Exam 2 · Q44</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Agent Platform API</strong>: manage and call a finished model or agent <strong>from your own Android, iOS and web apps</strong> in code <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q30\">Exam 6 · Q30</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>AI Hub</strong>: a <strong>repository of ready-to-use AI/ML resources</strong> (models, notebooks, pipelines) to help you start without building from scratch. It doesn't serve predictions, write all your code or clean your data (<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q16\">Exam 5 · Q16</a>, see ⚠️).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Managed platform\" + \"prepare, train, deploy, monitor\" → <strong>Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q36\">Exam 1 · Q36</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q41\">Exam 6 · Q41</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Foundation models\" + \"adapt with company data\" + \"deploy agents\" → <strong>Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q17\">Exam 4 · Q17</a>.</li>\n<li>\"No-code/low-code agent builder for analysts\" → <strong>Agent Studio</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q45\">Exam 3 · Q45</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Call the agent from our mobile/web app\" → <strong>Agent Platform API</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q30\">Exam 6 · Q30</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>GKE</strong> and <strong>Compute Engine</strong> can run ML, but they aren't managed ML platforms. You'd build the lifecycle yourself <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q36\">Exam 1 · Q36</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q17\">Exam 4 · Q17</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q41\">Exam 6 · Q41</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Cloud Load Balancing</strong> spreads traffic. It isn't an AI platform <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q17\">Exam 4 · Q17</a>. <strong>Dataproc</strong> processes data with Spark and Hadoop, but it isn't a full ML lifecycle platform <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q41\">Exam 6 · Q41</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Document AI</strong> and <strong>Natural Language API</strong> are single-purpose pre-trained services, not a platform for custom models <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q36\">Exam 1 · Q36</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Recommendations AI</strong> is a packaged retail solution, not a general ML platform <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q41\">Exam 6 · Q41</a>.</li>\n<li>Model choice: \"only Google models\", \"only third-party\" and \"only open models\" are all wrong <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q3\">Exam 6 · Q3</a>.</li>\n<li>Billing Export API, Cloud Storage API and IAM don't invoke agents. IAM controls who may call the agent, but it isn't the interface <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q30\">Exam 6 · Q30</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform (formerly Vertex AI) is Google Cloud's unified, managed AI platform for the full ML lifecycle and for generative AI and agents. This lesson covers its scope, model choice through Model Garden, Agent Studio, the Agent Platform API and AI Hub.","objectives":["Describe the ML lifecycle stages the platform manages","Explain the platform's model choice across Google, open and partner models","Match Agent Studio and the Agent Platform API to their use cases","Explain what AI Hub provides"],"takeaways":["Prepare, train, deploy and monitor in one managed service means Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform.","It offers Google, open and third-party partner models through Model Garden.","Agent Studio is the no-code or low-code agent builder.","Call a finished agent from mobile or web apps through the Agent Platform API.","IAM controls who can call an agent, but the Agent Platform API is the interface.","GKE and Compute Engine can run ML but are not managed ML platforms."],"questions":["e1q36","e2q44","e3q45","e4q17","e4q28","e5q16","e5q41","e6q3","e6q30"]},{"id":"d3l7","num":7,"title":"Generative AI, foundation models and Gemini","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Generative AI</strong> is a <strong>specific field of AI that creates new content</strong>: text, images, audio, video, code, summaries, from prompts <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q24\">Exam 3 · Q24</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q40\">Exam 3 · Q40</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Foundation models</strong> are large models <strong>pre-trained on broad data</strong>. They give a <strong>powerful starting point</strong> that you adapt for your business, which <strong>cuts development time</strong> compared with building a model from scratch <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q45\">Exam 6 · Q45</a>.</li>\n<li>Ways to adapt a foundation model: <strong>prompting</strong> (instructions and examples), <strong>grounding</strong> in trusted sources, often through <strong>retrieval-augmented generation (RAG)</strong> over your own documents, and <strong>fine-tuning</strong> on your data <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q45\">Exam 6 · Q45</a>.</li>\n<li>Gen AI can produce incomplete, outdated or false output (<strong>hallucinations</strong>), especially when it isn't grounded. Grounding, evaluation and human review reduce the risk <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q45\">Exam 6 · Q45</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Gemini</strong> is Google's <strong>multimodal</strong> foundation model family. It understands and combines <strong>text, code, audio, images and video</strong> in one model <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q24\">Exam 4 · Q24</a>.</li>\n<li>Through Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform you can choose Gemini, open or partner models <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q3\">Exam 6 · Q3</a>.</li>\n<li><em>(not in the question bank)</em> Gemini also appears inside products, such as Gemini for Google Workspace and Gemini Code Assist.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Create new content from prompts\" → <strong>generative AI</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q40\">Exam 3 · Q40</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Multimodal\" + \"text, code, audio, images, video\" → <strong>Gemini</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q24\">Exam 4 · Q24</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Start from a broadly trained model and adapt it\" → <strong>foundation model</strong>, valued for <strong>time saved</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q45\">Exam 6 · Q45</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Gen AI is not \"only translation\", \"only image labeling\", or \"every ML algorithm\". It is a specific field focused on creation. Image labeling is recognition (classification), not generation <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q24\">Exam 3 · Q24</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Predictive analytics</strong> forecasts, it doesn't create content. Data warehouses and relational databases aren't AI branches <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q40\">Exam 3 · Q40</a>.</li>\n<li>Foundation models <strong>don't</strong> remove the need for governance and security, <strong>don't</strong> make compute free (inference, tuning and hosting still cost money), and <strong>don't guarantee factual accuracy</strong>. Human review still matters <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q45\">Exam 6 · Q45</a>.</li>\n<li>BigQuery, Cloud Storage and Cloud SQL are data services, not models <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q24\">Exam 4 · Q24</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"Generative AI creates new content from prompts, and foundation models are the large pre-trained models that make it possible. This lesson covers Gemini as Google's multimodal model and the real advantages and limits of foundation models.","objectives":["Define generative AI and tell it apart from predictive analytics","Explain the strategic value of foundation models","Identify Gemini as Google's multimodal foundation model","Name the ways to adapt a foundation model and the risk of hallucinations"],"takeaways":["Generative AI is a specific field of AI that creates content such as text, images, audio and code.","Foundation models give a pre-trained starting point that you can fine-tune, which cuts development time.","Adapt foundation models by prompting, grounding (RAG) or fine-tuning. Ungrounded output can hallucinate.","Gemini understands text, code, audio, images and video together.","Foundation models don't remove governance, don't make compute free and don't guarantee facts."],"questions":["e3q24","e3q40","e4q24","e6q3","e6q45"]},{"id":"d3l8","num":8,"title":"Agentic AI, AI agents and Contact Center AI","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Agentic AI</strong> = AI that <strong>understands a goal, plans and reasons, decides next steps, calls tools, takes actions</strong> (check account status, update records, trigger refunds, escalate only when needed) and <strong>completes multi-step workflows</strong> with little human involvement <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q3\">Exam 3 · Q3</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q32\">Exam 5 · Q32</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q36\">Exam 5 · Q36</a>.</li>\n<li>Generative AI <strong>creates content</strong>. Agentic AI <strong>acts</strong>. An agent usually uses a generative model as its \"brain\" to understand intent, plus tools to do things <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q3\">Exam 3 · Q3</a>.</li>\n<li>Building agents on Google Cloud: <strong>Agent Studio</strong> for no-code/low-code <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q45\">Exam 3 · Q45</a>, <strong>Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform</strong> for developers <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q17\">Exam 4 · Q17</a>, <strong>Agent Platform API</strong> to call them from apps <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q30\">Exam 6 · Q30</a>. Pre-built agents and templates are faster but less differentiated <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q2\">Exam 6 · Q2</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Gemini Enterprise</strong> (the app, formerly Google Agentspace) is where employees chat with Gemini models and use ready-made and custom agents over company data. Don't confuse it with Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform, the developer platform <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q11\">Exam 5 · Q11</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Contact Center AI (CCAI)</strong> / <strong>Contact Center AI Platform</strong> is Google's <strong>purpose-built customer-service solution</strong>: virtual agents that automate common requests, <strong>intelligent routing</strong>, agent assist that helps human agents resolve issues faster, and conversation insights across chat and phone, while keeping conversations personalized <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q13\">Exam 2 · Q13</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q5\">Exam 6 · Q5</a>.</li>\n<li>Other packaged AI solutions show up as distractors. Know what each one does:</li>\n<li><strong>Document AI</strong>: process and extract data from invoices, forms, receipts, contracts, ID documents <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q36\">Exam 1 · Q36</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q5\">Exam 6 · Q5</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Recommendations AI</strong> and <strong>Recommendations from Agent Search</strong>: personalized product or content recommendations <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q13\">Exam 2 · Q13</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q6\">Exam 2 · Q6</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q41\">Exam 6 · Q41</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Discovery AI for Retail</strong>: product search, recommendations and personalization for retailers <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q33\">Exam 6 · Q33</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Cloud Talent Solution</strong>: job search and candidate matching for recruiting sites <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q5\">Exam 6 · Q5</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Which concept or solution?</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Scenario</th>\n<th>Answer</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Understand goal, call tools, update records, finish the workflow</td>\n<td>Agentic AI</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q3\">Exam 3 · Q3</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q32\">Exam 5 · Q32</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Plan, reason and carry out multi-step tasks with minimal direction</td>\n<td>Agentic AI</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q36\">Exam 5 · Q36</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Produce emails, banners, code from prompts</td>\n<td>Generative AI</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q40\">Exam 3 · Q40</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Dashboards about past results</td>\n<td>Business intelligence / descriptive analytics</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q3\">Exam 3 · Q3</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q14\">Exam 6 · Q14</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Handle many chat/phone requests, help support agents, stay personal</td>\n<td>Contact Center AI (Platform)</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q13\">Exam 2 · Q13</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q5\">Exam 6 · Q5</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Verbs like <strong>decide, act, trigger, call tools, complete, plan</strong> → <strong>agentic AI</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q32\">Exam 5 · Q32</a>.</li>\n<li>Verbs like <strong>generate, draft, create, summarize</strong> → <strong>generative AI</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q24\">Exam 3 · Q24</a>.</li>\n<li>Customer support at scale with phone and chat → <strong>Contact Center AI</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q5\">Exam 6 · Q5</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Agentic AI is not IaaS, VMs, hybrid cloud, DNS or open-source software. Those options are filler <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q32\">Exam 5 · Q32</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q36\">Exam 5 · Q36</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Generative AI\" is the tempting wrong answer for agent scenarios. If the system <strong>takes actions</strong>, choose agentic <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q3\">Exam 3 · Q3</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Descriptive analytics</strong> and BI report on the past. They don't act <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q3\">Exam 3 · Q3</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Text-to-Speech</strong>, <strong>Cloud Talent Solution</strong> and <strong>Document AI</strong> each cover one narrow task, not a full support operation <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q5\">Exam 6 · Q5</a>. <strong>Recommendations AI</strong>, <strong>Cloud Identity</strong> and <strong>Cloud Text-to-Speech</strong> don't run a contact center <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q13\">Exam 2 · Q13</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"Agentic AI goes beyond generating content. It plans, decides, calls tools and completes multi-step workflows. This lesson also covers Contact Center AI, Google's purpose-built solution for customer support, the Gemini Enterprise app, and the packaged AI solutions that appear as distractors.","objectives":["Define agentic AI and distinguish it from generative AI and BI","Identify Google Cloud tools for building and calling agents","Choose Contact Center AI for customer-support scenarios","Recognize Document AI, Recommendations AI, Discovery AI for Retail and Cloud Talent Solution"],"takeaways":["Agentic AI understands goals, plans, calls tools and completes workflows with little human input.","Generative AI creates content, and agentic AI takes actions.","Agent Studio, Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform and the Agent Platform API build and expose agents.","High-volume chat and phone support with routing, virtual agents, agent assist and personalization means Contact Center AI.","Talent Solution is for jobs, Discovery AI and Recommendations AI are for retail search and recommendations, Document AI is for documents."],"questions":["e1q36","e2q6","e2q13","e3q3","e3q24","e3q40","e3q45","e4q17","e5q11","e5q32","e5q36","e6q2","e6q14","e6q30","e6q33"]},{"id":"d3l9","num":9,"title":"AI infrastructure: TPUs, GPUs and AI Hypercomputer","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cloud TPUs (Tensor Processing Units)</strong> are Google's <strong>custom-built ASICs (application-specific chips) designed specifically for ML</strong>. They are fast at the tensor and matrix math in neural networks, <strong>speed up training</strong> of large models such as image recognition, and work well with TensorFlow <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q38\">Exam 2 · Q38</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q31\">Exam 5 · Q31</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q26\">Exam 4 · Q26</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>GPUs</strong> are highly parallel, general-purpose accelerators that are also widely used for deep learning training and inference. For \"train very large foundation models quickly\", the answer is <strong>GPUs and TPUs</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q26\">Exam 4 · Q26</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>AI Hypercomputer</strong> is Google Cloud's <strong>integrated supercomputing architecture for AI</strong>: performance-optimized hardware (TPUs, GPUs), high-performance networking and storage, <strong>open software and popular ML frameworks</strong>, workload orchestration and <strong>flexible consumption models</strong>, for <strong>training, tuning and serving</strong> at scale <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q48\">Exam 4 · Q48</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q34\">Exam 5 · Q34</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q39\">Exam 5 · Q39</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>World-leading AI</strong> is one of Google Cloud's top differentiators: ready-to-use agents, Google's first-party models (Gemini), the Gemini Enterprise app, a unified AI development platform (Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform) and AI infrastructure <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q11\">Exam 5 · Q11</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Hardware specifically designed for ML\", \"domain-specific hardware\" → <strong>TPUs</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q38\">Exam 2 · Q38</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q31\">Exam 5 · Q31</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Hardware for very large models\" with GPUs and TPUs offered together → <strong>GPUs and TPUs</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q26\">Exam 4 · Q26</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Hardware + open software + ML frameworks + flexible consumption\" → <strong>AI Hypercomputer</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q48\">Exam 4 · Q48</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q34\">Exam 5 · Q34</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q39\">Exam 5 · Q39</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Agents + first-party models + unified AI platform\" as a differentiator → <strong>world-leading AI</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q11\">Exam 5 · Q11</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>CPUs are general-purpose, and high-memory VMs add RAM, not ML acceleration. Neither is the ML-specific answer <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q38\">Exam 2 · Q38</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Bare Metal Solution</strong>, <strong>Spot VMs</strong> and <strong>containers</strong> are ways to run or price compute, not ML-specific hardware. Spot VMs are cheaper spare capacity that can be preempted. Containers package code; the speed comes from the hardware underneath <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q31\">Exam 5 · Q31</a>.</li>\n<li>HDDs, archival storage, VPC routers, DNS, edge locations, SaaS, Coldline, VPC and Workspace are filler <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q26\">Exam 4 · Q26</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q34\">Exam 5 · Q34</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q39\">Exam 5 · Q39</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Multi-cloud architecture\", \"serverless data pipeline\" and \"DDoS protection\" don't describe an AI compute stack <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q48\">Exam 4 · Q48</a>. \"Global network\", \"CapEx reduction\" and \"open standards\" are other categories, not the AI differentiator <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q11\">Exam 5 · Q11</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"Training large models quickly needs specialized hardware. This lesson covers TPUs, GPUs, the AI Hypercomputer architecture and the world-leading AI differentiator.","objectives":["Explain what TPUs are and when they are the answer","Describe AI Hypercomputer's components","Identify world-leading AI as a Google Cloud differentiator"],"takeaways":["TPUs are Google's custom ASICs designed specifically for ML training, fast at tensor and matrix math.","For very large foundation models, use GPUs and TPUs.","AI Hypercomputer combines AI-optimized hardware, networking and storage, open software, ML frameworks and flexible consumption for training, tuning and serving.","Ready-to-use agents, Gemini models, the Gemini Enterprise app and a unified AI platform make up the world-leading AI differentiator."],"questions":["e2q38","e4q26","e4q48","e5q11","e5q31","e5q39"]},{"id":"d3l10","num":10,"title":"Responsible AI and explainable AI","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Responsible AI</strong> means designing, building, deploying and operating AI that is <strong>transparent, fair, safe, privacy-preserving and accountable</strong>, and <strong>reduces harmful bias</strong> and harm to users. Defining such principles to keep user trust is responsible AI practice. It matters even more with gen AI, whose output can affect users and decisions <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q43\">Exam 4 · Q43</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Explainable AI</strong> shows <strong>why a model made a specific prediction</strong>: which factors or features drove it, for example a mortgage rejection. Regulated industries need it so auditors and compliance teams can review decisions. It also helps <strong>detect bias</strong> and check that the model behaves as intended <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q50\">Exam 4 · Q50</a>.</li>\n<li>Humans stay accountable: ML shouldn't make final calls on complex ethical dilemmas <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q29\">Exam 5 · Q29</a>, and gen AI output still needs review because it isn't guaranteed correct. Governance, access control and data protection remain the organization's job <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q45\">Exam 6 · Q45</a>.</li>\n<li><em>(not in the question bank)</em> <strong>Google's AI Principles</strong> guide its own AI work (be socially beneficial, avoid unfair bias, be safe, be accountable, protect privacy, uphold scientific excellence). Bias usually comes from <strong>unrepresentative training data</strong>, so responsible AI starts with data (lesson 2). Your data used with Google Cloud AI services stays under your control and isn't used to train Google's general models without permission.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Transparent, fair, safe, private, reduce bias\" → <strong>responsible AI</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q43\">Exam 4 · Q43</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Explain why the model decided X for this applicant\", \"auditors\", \"regulated\" → <strong>explainable AI</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q50\">Exam 4 · Q50</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Data virtualization, edge computing and legacy modernization have nothing to do with AI ethics. Edge computing can keep some data local, but it isn't the practice of setting AI principles <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q43\">Exam 4 · Q43</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Generative AI</strong> creates content. It doesn't explain decisions. <strong>Unstructured data processing</strong> and <strong>multi-cloud networking</strong> don't either <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q50\">Exam 4 · Q50</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"Responsible AI keeps AI systems fair, transparent, safe and private, and explainable AI shows why a model made a specific decision. Both are essential for trust and for regulated industries.","objectives":["Define responsible AI and its core principles","Explain when explainable AI is required","Recognize where human oversight remains necessary"],"takeaways":["Transparent, fair, safe, privacy-preserving and low-bias AI is responsible AI.","Explaining an individual decision to auditors calls for explainable AI, which also helps detect bias.","Bias often starts with unrepresentative training data.","Humans stay accountable for ethical decisions and for reviewing gen AI output."],"questions":["e4q43","e4q50","e5q29","e6q45"]}],"ref":{"cheat":"<ul>\n<li><strong>AI</strong> = tasks that need human cognition. <strong>ML</strong> = learns from data to predict and decide. <strong>BI</strong> = dashboards about the past <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q34\">Exam 6 · Q34</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q14\">Exam 6 · Q14</a>.</li>\n<li>AI ⊃ ML ⊃ deep learning; gen AI is one area inside. Descriptive = what happened. Predictive = what will happen <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q34\">Exam 6 · Q34</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q3\">Exam 3 · Q3</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q40\">Exam 3 · Q40</a>.</li>\n<li>ML shines on <strong>unstructured data</strong> (audio sentiment) and on <strong>recommendations</strong> and <strong>churn</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q37\">Exam 1 · Q37</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q29\">Exam 5 · Q29</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q29\">Exam 2 · Q29</a>.</li>\n<li>Supervised = labeled examples. Unsupervised = groupings in unlabeled data <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q28\">Exam 4 · Q28</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q29\">Exam 5 · Q29</a>.</li>\n<li>Accuracy ← <strong>more good training data</strong>, not hardware. Messy sources → <strong>consistent format</strong>, keep full history. Related sources → <strong>combine</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q26\">Exam 6 · Q26</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q17\">Exam 2 · Q17</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q21\">Exam 5 · Q21</a>.</li>\n<li>Pre-built = <strong>fast, less differentiation</strong>. Custom = <strong>more effort, more differentiation</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q2\">Exam 6 · Q2</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q1\">Exam 4 · Q1</a>.</li>\n<li>Generic photo labels, landmarks, text → <strong>Vision API</strong>. Card numbers in photos → Vision OCR + <strong>Sensitive Data Protection</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q1\">Exam 5 · Q1</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q24\">Exam 6 · Q24</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q23\">Exam 3 · Q23</a>.</li>\n<li>Audio → <strong>Speech-to-Text</strong>. Text → audio = <strong>Text-to-Speech</strong>. Languages → <strong>Translation</strong>. Text topics/sentiment → <strong>Natural Language</strong>. Video → <strong>Video Intelligence</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q14\">Exam 4 · Q14</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q37\">Exam 6 · Q37</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q47\">Exam 6 · Q47</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q41\">Exam 1 · Q41</a>.</li>\n<li>Scanned forms → APIs make them <strong>searchable</strong> and extract fields; Document AI pulls key-value pairs and tables <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q17\">Exam 6 · Q17</a>.</li>\n<li>SQL analysts + BigQuery → <strong>BigQuery ML</strong> (<code>CREATE MODEL</code>), even when AutoML is offered <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q44\">Exam 2 · Q44</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q36\">Exam 6 · Q36</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q15\">Exam 2 · Q15</a>.</li>\n<li>Labeled data + your own categories + no ML coders → <strong>AutoML</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q41\">Exam 1 · Q41</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q6\">Exam 2 · Q6</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q28\">Exam 4 · Q28</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q33\">Exam 6 · Q33</a>.</li>\n<li>Data scientists' own code → <strong>custom training</strong> (managed jobs, your framework and accelerators). Open-source ML library → <strong>TensorFlow</strong>. Beam/Dataflow = data pipelines <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q41\">Exam 5 · Q41</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q9\">Exam 6 · Q9</a>.</li>\n<li>Full managed ML lifecycle, foundation models, agents → <strong>Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q36\">Exam 1 · Q36</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q41\">Exam 6 · Q41</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q17\">Exam 4 · Q17</a>.</li>\n<li>Model choice = Google + open + partner models in <strong>Model Garden</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q3\">Exam 6 · Q3</a>. No-code agents → <strong>Agent Studio</strong>. Call agents from apps → <strong>Agent Platform API</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q45\">Exam 3 · Q45</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q30\">Exam 6 · Q30</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>AI Hub</strong> = repository of ready-to-use AI resources <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q16\">Exam 5 · Q16</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Gen AI</strong> creates content. <strong>Gemini</strong> is multimodal. <strong>Foundation models</strong> = pre-trained start; adapt by prompting, grounding/RAG or fine-tuning; save time; still hallucinate and need review <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q24\">Exam 3 · Q24</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q40\">Exam 3 · Q40</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q24\">Exam 4 · Q24</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q45\">Exam 6 · Q45</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Agentic AI</strong> plans, calls tools and completes workflows <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q3\">Exam 3 · Q3</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q32\">Exam 5 · Q32</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q36\">Exam 5 · Q36</a>.</li>\n<li>Support at scale → <strong>Contact Center AI</strong>. Talent Solution = jobs. Discovery AI / Recommendations AI = retail search and recs <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q13\">Exam 2 · Q13</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q5\">Exam 6 · Q5</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q33\">Exam 6 · Q33</a>.</li>\n<li>ML-specific chips → <strong>TPUs</strong> (ASICs). Big models → <strong>GPUs and TPUs</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q38\">Exam 2 · Q38</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q31\">Exam 5 · Q31</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q26\">Exam 4 · Q26</a>.</li>\n<li>Hardware + open software + frameworks + flexible consumption → <strong>AI Hypercomputer</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q48\">Exam 4 · Q48</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q34\">Exam 5 · Q34</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q39\">Exam 5 · Q39</a>.</li>\n<li>Agents + Gemini + Gemini Enterprise + unified platform → <strong>world-leading AI</strong> differentiator <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q11\">Exam 5 · Q11</a>.</li>\n<li>Fair, transparent, safe, private → <strong>responsible AI</strong>. \"Why this decision?\" → <strong>explainable AI</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q43\">Exam 4 · Q43</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q50\">Exam 4 · Q50</a>.</li>\n<li>Tools for every skill level is Google's AI pitch <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q2\">Exam 1 · Q2</a>. Image symptom triage = <strong>ML</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q5\">Exam 5 · Q5</a>. Learns as data grows = <strong>ML</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q18\">Exam 6 · Q18</a>. Representative data from every region, normal and extreme <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q35\">Exam 4 · Q35</a>.</li>\n</ul>","rules":[{"lesson":"d3l1","title":"AI and ML fundamentals and where they add business value","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"Learns from data\", \"predicts\", \"gets better with more data\", \"automated decisions\" → <strong>machine learning</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q14\">Exam 6 · Q14</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q18\">Exam 6 · Q18</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Dashboards\", \"reports\", \"compare past performance\" → <strong>BI</strong> / descriptive analytics <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q14\">Exam 6 · Q14</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q3\">Exam 3 · Q3</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Images, audio, video, free text\" → <strong>unstructured data</strong>, a strong ML use case <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q37\">Exam 1 · Q37</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d3l2","title":"Data for ML: quality, quantity and preparation","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"Improve accuracy\" → <strong>more and better training data</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q26\">Exam 6 · Q26</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Different formats, column names, date styles\" → <strong>transform to a consistent format</strong> first <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q17\">Exam 2 · Q17</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Several related sources\" → <strong>combine them</strong>, then predict <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q21\">Exam 5 · Q21</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d3l3","title":"Choosing an approach: buy, adapt or build","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"As quickly as possible\", \"without building or training a model\" → <strong>pre-trained API</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q23\">Exam 3 · Q23</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Data in BigQuery\" + \"knows SQL\" + \"no data scientists\" → <strong>BigQuery ML</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q36\">Exam 6 · Q36</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Labeled images/text\" + \"no ML experience / can't write training code\" → <strong>AutoML</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q33\">Exam 6 · Q33</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Data scientists writing their own training code\" → <strong>custom training</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q41\">Exam 5 · Q41</a>.</li>\n<li>Tradeoff questions: pre-built = <strong>speed vs differentiation</strong>; custom = <strong>effort/expertise vs differentiation</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q2\">Exam 6 · Q2</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q1\">Exam 4 · Q1</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d3l4","title":"Pre-trained APIs: Vision, Speech, Translation, Natural Language","html":"<ul>\n<li>Generic categories (objects, places, labels, common items) and \"without training\" → <strong>Vision API</strong>, not AutoML Vision <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q1\">Exam 5 · Q1</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q24\">Exam 6 · Q24</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Your own</strong> categories (beaches vs mountains for your app, shoes vs bags in your catalog, faulty vs acceptable parts from your factory) → <strong>AutoML</strong>, see lesson 5. A generic model doesn't know your company-specific patterns <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q41\">Exam 1 · Q41</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q28\">Exam 4 · Q28</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q33\">Exam 6 · Q33</a>.</li>\n<li>Audio → Speech-to-Text first, then Natural Language for sentiment <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q14\">Exam 4 · Q14</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q37\">Exam 1 · Q37</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d3l5","title":"BigQuery ML, AutoML and custom training","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"SQL\" + \"BigQuery\" in the same question → <strong>BigQuery ML</strong>, nearly always. Even when AutoML is offered, a SQL team with data in BigQuery points to BigQuery ML, because AutoML means a separate platform workflow instead of SQL <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q36\">Exam 6 · Q36</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Already labeled\" + \"no ML experience\" → <strong>AutoML</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q33\">Exam 6 · Q33</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Custom training code\" → <strong>custom training</strong>, not AutoML and not raw Compute Engine <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q41\">Exam 5 · Q41</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d3l6","title":"Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform (formerly Vertex AI)","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"Managed platform\" + \"prepare, train, deploy, monitor\" → <strong>Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q36\">Exam 1 · Q36</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q41\">Exam 6 · Q41</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Foundation models\" + \"adapt with company data\" + \"deploy agents\" → <strong>Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q17\">Exam 4 · Q17</a>.</li>\n<li>\"No-code/low-code agent builder for analysts\" → <strong>Agent Studio</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q45\">Exam 3 · Q45</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Call the agent from our mobile/web app\" → <strong>Agent Platform API</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q30\">Exam 6 · Q30</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d3l7","title":"Generative AI, foundation models and Gemini","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"Create new content from prompts\" → <strong>generative AI</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q40\">Exam 3 · Q40</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Multimodal\" + \"text, code, audio, images, video\" → <strong>Gemini</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q24\">Exam 4 · Q24</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Start from a broadly trained model and adapt it\" → <strong>foundation model</strong>, valued for <strong>time saved</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q45\">Exam 6 · Q45</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d3l8","title":"Agentic AI, AI agents and Contact Center AI","html":"<ul>\n<li>Verbs like <strong>decide, act, trigger, call tools, complete, plan</strong> → <strong>agentic AI</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q32\">Exam 5 · Q32</a>.</li>\n<li>Verbs like <strong>generate, draft, create, summarize</strong> → <strong>generative AI</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q24\">Exam 3 · Q24</a>.</li>\n<li>Customer support at scale with phone and chat → <strong>Contact Center AI</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q5\">Exam 6 · Q5</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d3l9","title":"AI infrastructure: TPUs, GPUs and AI Hypercomputer","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"Hardware specifically designed for ML\", \"domain-specific hardware\" → <strong>TPUs</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q38\">Exam 2 · Q38</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q31\">Exam 5 · Q31</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Hardware for very large models\" with GPUs and TPUs offered together → <strong>GPUs and TPUs</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q26\">Exam 4 · Q26</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Hardware + open software + ML frameworks + flexible consumption\" → <strong>AI Hypercomputer</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q48\">Exam 4 · Q48</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q34\">Exam 5 · Q34</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q39\">Exam 5 · Q39</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Agents + first-party models + unified AI platform\" as a differentiator → <strong>world-leading AI</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q11\">Exam 5 · Q11</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d3l10","title":"Responsible AI and explainable AI","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"Transparent, fair, safe, private, reduce bias\" → <strong>responsible AI</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q43\">Exam 4 · Q43</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Explain why the model decided X for this applicant\", \"auditors\", \"regulated\" → <strong>explainable AI</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q50\">Exam 4 · Q50</a>.</li>\n</ul>"}],"traps":[{"lesson":"d3l1","title":"AI and ML fundamentals and where they add business value","html":"<ul>\n<li>Swapped definitions: \"BI predicts, ML only visualizes\" or \"ML only works on structured tables\" are both reversed. The BI vs ML difference is not about hardware (TPUs vs CPUs) either <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q14\">Exam 6 · Q14</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Any system that receives real-time data\" or \"organizes data into a structured format\" describes pipelines, not AI <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q34\">Exam 6 · Q34</a>.</li>\n<li>Recommendations and fraud detection are valid ML uses, but they run on <strong>structured</strong> data. Don't pick them when the question asks about <strong>unstructured</strong> data <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q37\">Exam 1 · Q37</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Trends analysis</strong> describes past patterns and <strong>data automation</strong> moves data. Neither learns each user's preferences <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q18\">Exam 6 · Q18</a>.</li>\n<li>A <strong>survey</strong> can feed a churn model as one input, but on its own it depends on who answers and doesn't predict who will leave <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q29\">Exam 2 · Q29</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d3l2","title":"Data for ML: quality, quantity and preparation","html":"<ul>\n<li>Infrastructure answers (storage, bandwidth, CPU) to a model-accuracy question. Hardware cuts training time, not prediction quality <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q26\">Exam 6 · Q26</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Use only the most recent 10 months\" throws away history that a long-range forecast needs <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q17\">Exam 2 · Q17</a>.</li>\n<li>\"One model per file format\" splits the data into smaller, less representative sets and leaves more models to build and maintain. File format isn't the pattern you want to predict <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q17\">Exam 2 · Q17</a>.</li>\n<li>Storing or archiving data in a warehouse or data lake isn't analysis. Archiving older data also removes history the model needs <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q21\">Exam 5 · Q21</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Only severe weather data\" leaves the model no normal cases to compare against <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q35\">Exam 4 · Q35</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d3l3","title":"Choosing an approach: buy, adapt or build","html":"<ul>\n<li>Answers that put pre-built solutions on the slow, expert-heavy side (or custom models on the easy side) are reversed <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q2\">Exam 6 · Q2</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q1\">Exam 4 · Q1</a>.</li>\n<li>Any option mentioning a shift to <strong>CapEx</strong> is a distractor in this domain <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q1\">Exam 4 · Q1</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q2\">Exam 6 · Q2</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Models that always require custom training\" contradicts the whole point of Google's tiered offering <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q2\">Exam 1 · Q2</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d3l4","title":"Pre-trained APIs: Vision, Speech, Translation, Natural Language","html":"<ul>\n<li>Pre-trained APIs still need <strong>input data</strong> and permissions. They don't skip data validation or need fewer security permissions <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q46\">Exam 5 · Q46</a>.</li>\n<li>Pre-trained APIs can't forecast from your own tabular data, such as holiday demand for your listings <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q36\">Exam 6 · Q36</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Convert documents into unstructured data\" is backwards. Scans are already unstructured, and the API makes them searchable and structured <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q17\">Exam 6 · Q17</a>.</li>\n<li>Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform and BigQuery ML can do image work, but they are overkill when a simple pre-trained API fits <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q24\">Exam 6 · Q24</a>.</li>\n<li>Translation and Speech-to-Text don't find topics in text that is already written <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q47\">Exam 6 · Q47</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d3l5","title":"BigQuery ML, AutoML and custom training","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Apache Beam</strong> is an open-source programming model for data pipelines, and <strong>Dataflow</strong> is the managed service that runs Beam pipelines. Both process data. Neither is an ML library. <strong>Cloud Run functions</strong> run small event-driven code, they don't train models <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q9\">Exam 6 · Q9</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Compute Engine</strong> can train a model on VMs, but then you manage provisioning, environments and scaling yourself <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q41\">Exam 5 · Q41</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Dataproc</strong> (now Managed Service for Apache Spark), <strong>Coldline</strong> and <strong>Interconnect</strong> are not ML services <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q28\">Exam 4 · Q28</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Video Intelligence API</strong> is for video, and <strong>Looker</strong> is BI. Neither trains an image classifier <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q41\">Exam 1 · Q41</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Agent Studio</strong> builds agents and <strong>Spanner</strong> is a transactional database. Neither trains models on BigQuery tables with SQL <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q44\">Exam 2 · Q44</a>.</li>\n<li>Security (IAM), APIs (Apigee) or cheaper storage don't create business insight from data. A model does <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q15\">Exam 2 · Q15</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Recommendations from Agent Search\" recommends products, <strong>Pub/Sub</strong> moves messages and <strong>Google Distributed Cloud</strong> runs infrastructure at the edge. None classifies support messages <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q6\">Exam 2 · Q6</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Discovery AI for Retail</strong> improves product search and recommendations. It doesn't detect factory defects <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q33\">Exam 6 · Q33</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d3l6","title":"Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform (formerly Vertex AI)","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>GKE</strong> and <strong>Compute Engine</strong> can run ML, but they aren't managed ML platforms. You'd build the lifecycle yourself <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q36\">Exam 1 · Q36</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q17\">Exam 4 · Q17</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q41\">Exam 6 · Q41</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Cloud Load Balancing</strong> spreads traffic. It isn't an AI platform <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q17\">Exam 4 · Q17</a>. <strong>Dataproc</strong> processes data with Spark and Hadoop, but it isn't a full ML lifecycle platform <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q41\">Exam 6 · Q41</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Document AI</strong> and <strong>Natural Language API</strong> are single-purpose pre-trained services, not a platform for custom models <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q36\">Exam 1 · Q36</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Recommendations AI</strong> is a packaged retail solution, not a general ML platform <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q41\">Exam 6 · Q41</a>.</li>\n<li>Model choice: \"only Google models\", \"only third-party\" and \"only open models\" are all wrong <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q3\">Exam 6 · Q3</a>.</li>\n<li>Billing Export API, Cloud Storage API and IAM don't invoke agents. IAM controls who may call the agent, but it isn't the interface <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q30\">Exam 6 · Q30</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d3l7","title":"Generative AI, foundation models and Gemini","html":"<ul>\n<li>Gen AI is not \"only translation\", \"only image labeling\", or \"every ML algorithm\". It is a specific field focused on creation. Image labeling is recognition (classification), not generation <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q24\">Exam 3 · Q24</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Predictive analytics</strong> forecasts, it doesn't create content. Data warehouses and relational databases aren't AI branches <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q40\">Exam 3 · Q40</a>.</li>\n<li>Foundation models <strong>don't</strong> remove the need for governance and security, <strong>don't</strong> make compute free (inference, tuning and hosting still cost money), and <strong>don't guarantee factual accuracy</strong>. Human review still matters <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q45\">Exam 6 · Q45</a>.</li>\n<li>BigQuery, Cloud Storage and Cloud SQL are data services, not models <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q24\">Exam 4 · Q24</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d3l8","title":"Agentic AI, AI agents and Contact Center AI","html":"<ul>\n<li>Agentic AI is not IaaS, VMs, hybrid cloud, DNS or open-source software. Those options are filler <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q32\">Exam 5 · Q32</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q36\">Exam 5 · Q36</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Generative AI\" is the tempting wrong answer for agent scenarios. If the system <strong>takes actions</strong>, choose agentic <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q3\">Exam 3 · Q3</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Descriptive analytics</strong> and BI report on the past. They don't act <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q3\">Exam 3 · Q3</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Text-to-Speech</strong>, <strong>Cloud Talent Solution</strong> and <strong>Document AI</strong> each cover one narrow task, not a full support operation <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q5\">Exam 6 · Q5</a>. <strong>Recommendations AI</strong>, <strong>Cloud Identity</strong> and <strong>Cloud Text-to-Speech</strong> don't run a contact center <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q13\">Exam 2 · Q13</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d3l9","title":"AI infrastructure: TPUs, GPUs and AI Hypercomputer","html":"<ul>\n<li>CPUs are general-purpose, and high-memory VMs add RAM, not ML acceleration. Neither is the ML-specific answer <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q38\">Exam 2 · Q38</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Bare Metal Solution</strong>, <strong>Spot VMs</strong> and <strong>containers</strong> are ways to run or price compute, not ML-specific hardware. Spot VMs are cheaper spare capacity that can be preempted. Containers package code; the speed comes from the hardware underneath <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q31\">Exam 5 · Q31</a>.</li>\n<li>HDDs, archival storage, VPC routers, DNS, edge locations, SaaS, Coldline, VPC and Workspace are filler <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q26\">Exam 4 · Q26</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q34\">Exam 5 · Q34</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q39\">Exam 5 · Q39</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Multi-cloud architecture\", \"serverless data pipeline\" and \"DDoS protection\" don't describe an AI compute stack <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q48\">Exam 4 · Q48</a>. \"Global network\", \"CapEx reduction\" and \"open standards\" are other categories, not the AI differentiator <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q11\">Exam 5 · Q11</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d3l10","title":"Responsible AI and explainable AI","html":"<ul>\n<li>Data virtualization, edge computing and legacy modernization have nothing to do with AI ethics. Edge computing can keep some data local, but it isn't the practice of setting AI principles <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q43\">Exam 4 · Q43</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Generative AI</strong> creates content. It doesn't explain decisions. <strong>Unstructured data processing</strong> and <strong>multi-cloud networking</strong> don't either <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q50\">Exam 4 · Q50</a>.</li>\n</ul>"}],"questionable":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Item</th>\n<th>Issue</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q16\">Exam 5 · Q16</a></strong></td>\n<td><strong>AI Hub</strong> is an older Google Cloud product. Its role (a catalog of reusable models and assets) is now mostly filled by <strong>Model Garden</strong> in the AI platform. Know the definition, but it's dated.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q41\">Exam 1 · Q41</a></strong>, <strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q6\">Exam 2 · Q6</a></strong></td>\n<td>\"AutoML Vision\" and \"AutoML Natural Language\" are the old standalone product names. Today AutoML lives inside the AI platform (\"AutoML on Agent Platform\" in <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q28\">Exam 4 · Q28</a>), and Google steers new text-classification work toward tuning Gemini models. The concept (custom categories + labeled data + no code = AutoML) is still right.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q18\">Exam 6 · Q18</a></strong></td>\n<td>Multiple regression is itself a statistical/ML technique, so D isn't strictly wrong. \"Machine learning\" is keyed because it's the broader approach and the system must keep improving as new data arrives.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"},"glossary":[{"term":"Agent Platform API","definition":"Programmatic interface to Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform for managing and calling deployed models and agents from code.","exam_context":"Answer when a finished agent must be called from Android, iOS or web apps. IAM secures the call but isn't the interface. Billing and Storage APIs are distractors.","lesson":"d3l6","domain":3},{"term":"Agent Studio","definition":"No-code / low-code visual designer in the Google Cloud console for designing, configuring, testing and publishing AI agents.","exam_context":"Answer when analysts without coding or ML skills must build agents. It doesn't train models on BigQuery tables.","lesson":"d3l6","domain":3},{"term":"Agentic AI","definition":"AI that understands a goal, plans, decides next steps, calls tools and completes multi-step workflows with minimal human involvement.","exam_context":"Look for verbs like decide, trigger, update records, complete. Generative AI is the tempting wrong answer.","lesson":"d3l8","domain":3},{"term":"AI Hub","definition":"Google Cloud repository of ready-to-use AI and ML resources such as models, notebooks and pipelines.","exam_context":"It provides reusable resources. It doesn't serve predictions, write all your code or clean data. Dated product name.","lesson":"d3l6","domain":3},{"term":"AI Hypercomputer","definition":"Google Cloud's integrated AI supercomputing architecture: performance-optimized hardware (TPUs, GPUs), networking and storage, open software, ML frameworks and flexible consumption models.","exam_context":"Answer when a question lists hardware + open software + frameworks + flexible consumption for training, tuning and serving large models.","lesson":"d3l9","domain":3},{"term":"Apache Beam / Dataflow","definition":"Apache Beam is an open-source programming model for batch and streaming data pipelines. Dataflow is Google Cloud's managed service that runs Beam pipelines.","exam_context":"Data processing, not an ML library. The open-source ML library answer is TensorFlow.","lesson":"d3l5","domain":3},{"term":"Artificial intelligence (AI)","definition":"Any system that can perform tasks that normally require human cognition.","exam_context":"Not \"any real-time system\" or \"any system that structures data\".","lesson":"d3l1","domain":3},{"term":"AutoML","definition":"Trains a custom model on your labeled data without writing training code. Now AutoML on Agent Platform, formerly AutoML Vision and AutoML Natural Language.","exam_context":"Labeled data + your own categories + no ML expertise. Generic labels go to the Vision API instead.","lesson":"d3l5","domain":3},{"term":"BigQuery ML","definition":"Feature of BigQuery that creates and runs ML models with SQL where the data already lives.","exam_context":"SQL-skilled analysts or DBAs, data in BigQuery, no data scientists, no export.","lesson":"d3l5","domain":3},{"term":"Business intelligence (BI)","definition":"Analysis of historical data through reports and dashboards.","exam_context":"Describes the past. ML learns from data to predict and automate.","lesson":"d3l1","domain":3},{"term":"Cloud Talent Solution","definition":"Google Cloud AI service that improves job search and candidate matching for recruiting sites.","exam_context":"Distractor in customer-support questions. It is about hiring, not support.","lesson":"d3l8","domain":3},{"term":"Cloud Translation API","definition":"Pre-trained API that translates text between languages and can detect a text's language.","exam_context":"Translate product pages into many languages. It doesn't transcribe audio or find topics.","lesson":"d3l4","domain":3},{"term":"Contact Center AI (CCAI)","definition":"Google Cloud's purpose-built AI solution for customer service: virtual agents, intelligent routing, agent assist and conversation insights across chat and phone.","exam_context":"High-volume chat/phone support that must stay personalized. Text-to-Speech or Recommendations AI alone don't fit.","lesson":"d3l8","domain":3},{"term":"Custom training","definition":"Training a model with your own code as managed jobs in Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform, with your choice of framework, containers and accelerators.","exam_context":"Data scientists writing training code. Most effort, highest differentiation. Compute Engine would leave the infrastructure to you.","lesson":"d3l5","domain":3},{"term":"Descriptive analytics","definition":"Analysis that summarizes what already happened, through reports and dashboards.","exam_context":"Doesn't predict or act. Distractor for agentic AI and ML questions.","lesson":"d3l1","domain":3},{"term":"Discovery AI for Retail","definition":"Google Cloud AI for retail product search, recommendations and personalization.","exam_context":"Distractor for defect detection or custom image classification.","lesson":"d3l8","domain":3},{"term":"Document AI","definition":"Pre-trained service that uses OCR and extracts structured data (key-value pairs, entities, tables, fields) from documents such as forms, invoices, receipts and contracts.","exam_context":"Scanned forms become searchable data. Not a general ML platform or a contact-center solution.","lesson":"d3l4","domain":3},{"term":"Explainable AI","definition":"Techniques that show why a model made a specific prediction.","exam_context":"Regulated decisions (mortgages, credit) where auditors need the reason for each outcome.","lesson":"d3l10","domain":3},{"term":"Fine-tuning","definition":"Further training a pre-trained foundation model on your own examples so it fits a specific task.","exam_context":"One way to adapt a foundation model, alongside prompting and grounding.","lesson":"d3l7","domain":3},{"term":"Foundation model","definition":"Large model pre-trained on broad data that can be prompted, grounded or fine-tuned for many tasks.","exam_context":"Main advantage: a pre-trained starting point that cuts development time. It doesn't guarantee factual answers.","lesson":"d3l7","domain":3},{"term":"Gemini","definition":"Google's multimodal foundation model family that understands text, code, audio, images and video.","exam_context":"Answer for \"multimodal model\" across many input types.","lesson":"d3l7","domain":3},{"term":"Gemini Enterprise","definition":"Google's enterprise AI app (formerly Google Agentspace) where employees use Gemini models and ready-made or custom agents over company data.","exam_context":"Part of the world-leading AI differentiator. Not the same as Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform, the developer platform.","lesson":"d3l8","domain":3},{"term":"Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform","definition":"Google Cloud's unified, managed AI platform (formerly Vertex AI) for the full ML lifecycle, foundation models and agents.","exam_context":"Prepare, train, deploy, monitor. Explore and adapt foundation models. Offers Google, open and partner models.","lesson":"d3l6","domain":3},{"term":"Generative AI","definition":"Specific field of AI that creates new content such as text, images, audio, code and summaries from prompts.","exam_context":"Creates content. Predictive analytics forecasts, and agentic AI acts.","lesson":"d3l7","domain":3},{"term":"GPU","definition":"Graphics processing unit, a general-purpose accelerator widely used for ML training and inference.","exam_context":"Paired with TPUs for training very large foundation models.","lesson":"d3l9","domain":3},{"term":"Grounding / RAG","definition":"Connecting a model's answers to trusted sources, such as search or your own documents. Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) fetches relevant content and adds it to the prompt.","exam_context":"Reduces hallucinations and adapts foundation models to company data without retraining.","lesson":"d3l7","domain":3},{"term":"Hallucination","definition":"Generative AI output that sounds confident but is false, incomplete or outdated.","exam_context":"Why foundation models don't guarantee accuracy and still need grounding and human review.","lesson":"d3l7","domain":3},{"term":"Machine learning (ML)","definition":"Subset of AI in which systems learn patterns from data to make predictions or decisions, improving with more data. Deep learning and generative AI sit inside it.","exam_context":"Recommendations, churn prediction, image classification. Not suited to tasks with no past patterns or ethical final decisions.","lesson":"d3l1","domain":3},{"term":"Model Garden","definition":"Catalog in Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform for discovering and using Google, open and third-party partner models.","exam_context":"Answer for model choice across providers. It has largely taken over AI Hub's role.","lesson":"d3l6","domain":3},{"term":"Natural Language API","definition":"Pre-trained API for sentiment, entities, syntax and content classification in text.","exam_context":"Find main topics in chat transcripts. Speech must be transcribed first.","lesson":"d3l4","domain":3},{"term":"Pre-trained APIs","definition":"Ready-made Google ML models called through an API, with no training needed.","exam_context":"Fastest option, no ML specialists needed, less differentiation.","lesson":"d3l3","domain":3},{"term":"Predictive analytics","definition":"Using historical and current data to forecast likely outcomes, such as churn or demand.","exam_context":"Forecasts, but doesn't create content. Distractor for generative AI questions.","lesson":"d3l1","domain":3},{"term":"Recommendations AI","definition":"Packaged Google Cloud solution for personalized product and content recommendations.","exam_context":"Not a contact center, not a general ML platform, not a text classifier.","lesson":"d3l8","domain":3},{"term":"Responsible AI","definition":"Practice of building AI that is transparent, fair, safe, privacy-preserving and accountable and that reduces harmful bias.","exam_context":"Answer when a company defines principles to keep trust in its AI.","lesson":"d3l10","domain":3},{"term":"Sensitive Data Protection","definition":"Google Cloud service (formerly Cloud DLP) that discovers, classifies and redacts sensitive data such as payment card numbers and personal information.","exam_context":"With Vision OCR, flags ID or payment cards in uploaded photos without training a model.","lesson":"d3l4","domain":3},{"term":"Speech-to-Text API","definition":"Pre-trained API that transcribes audio into text.","exam_context":"Transcribe call recordings before sentiment analysis.","lesson":"d3l4","domain":3},{"term":"Supervised learning","definition":"Training a model on labeled examples so it learns to predict the label for new data.","exam_context":"AutoML image classification on labeled photos is supervised learning.","lesson":"d3l1","domain":3},{"term":"TensorFlow","definition":"Google's open-source machine learning library with a large ecosystem of tools and community.","exam_context":"Answer for \"open-source ML library\". Apache Beam and Dataflow are data processing.","lesson":"d3l5","domain":3},{"term":"Text-to-Speech","definition":"Pre-trained API that turns text into natural-sounding spoken audio.","exam_context":"The reverse of Speech-to-Text. One building block, not a full contact center.","lesson":"d3l4","domain":3},{"term":"TPU (Tensor Processing Unit)","definition":"Google's custom ASIC designed specifically to accelerate machine learning workloads, especially tensor and matrix math.","exam_context":"\"Hardware designed for ML\" or \"domain-specific hardware\" to cut training time.","lesson":"d3l9","domain":3},{"term":"Training data","definition":"The examples a model learns from. Its quantity, quality and representativeness drive accuracy.","exam_context":"More relevant, good-quality training data improves accuracy. Transform inconsistent sources first and keep the full history.","lesson":"d3l2","domain":3},{"term":"Unstructured data","definition":"Data that doesn't fit rows and columns, such as audio, images, video and free text.","exam_context":"Sentiment from call audio is the classic ML-on-unstructured-data example.","lesson":"d3l1","domain":3},{"term":"Unsupervised learning","definition":"Finding structure, such as groupings, in data without labels.","exam_context":"Still needs real patterns in the data. With no useful patterns, ML is a poor fit.","lesson":"d3l1","domain":3},{"term":"Video Intelligence API","definition":"Pre-trained API that detects labels, objects, shots and explicit content in video.","exam_context":"For video only. Still photos go to Vision API or AutoML.","lesson":"d3l4","domain":3},{"term":"Vision API","definition":"Pre-trained API that detects labels, objects, landmarks, logos, faces and text in images.","exam_context":"Generic image tagging without training. Custom categories go to AutoML.","lesson":"d3l4","domain":3},{"term":"World-leading AI","definition":"Google Cloud differentiator covering ready-to-use agents, first-party models like Gemini and a unified AI platform.","exam_context":"Not global network, open standards or CapEx reduction.","lesson":"d3l9","domain":3}]},{"n":4,"title":"Modernize Infrastructure and Applications with Google Cloud","short":"Infrastructure & Apps","weight":"~17%","desc":"Compute options, containers and serverless, migration paths, hybrid and multicloud, APIs and application modernization.","lessons":[{"id":"d4l1","num":1,"title":"Why modernize: agility, faster releases and managed services","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The business driver is speed.</strong> Legacy data-center apps that take <strong>weeks to update and release</strong> lose customers who expect frequent features and quick fixes. That pain is what pushes a company toward cloud-based architecture <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q45\">Exam 5 · Q45</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Modernizing on Google Cloud lets you release updates more quickly</strong>, including bug fixes, which is how it helps meet user expectations <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q23\">Exam 1 · Q23</a>.</li>\n<li>The speed comes from what modernization makes possible: <strong>CI/CD pipelines, automated testing, managed services and automated deployment</strong>. Legacy apps are often tightly coupled and deployed by hand, so every release is slow <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q23\">Exam 1 · Q23</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q45\">Exam 5 · Q45</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Cloud-native apps</strong> let developers <strong>release new features in an agile way</strong>. That is the reason to build cloud-native rather than only move an old app when requirements change often, for example per-user, per-season personalization <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q36\">Exam 4 · Q36</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Managed services</strong> mean Google Cloud takes over routine administration: OS patching, component upgrades, setting up database servers. Developers spend that time on features instead. <strong>Cloud SQL</strong> is the classic example: Google handles database setup, patching, backups and maintenance <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q24\">Exam 2 · Q24</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q34\">Exam 2 · Q34</a>.</li>\n<li>The task a managed platform typically handles <strong>automatically</strong> is <strong>patching and upgrades</strong>. Managing user access, deciding what to archive and optimizing cost remain your job <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q31\">Exam 2 · Q31</a>.</li>\n<li>Google gives you <strong>tools</strong> for those customer tasks (IAM roles, Cloud Storage lifecycle rules and archive classes, cost recommendations and committed use discounts), but the <strong>decisions</strong> are yours: who gets access, what to keep and for how long, what to right-size. You may still pick maintenance windows and test your app after upgrades <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q31\">Exam 2 · Q31</a>.</li>\n<li>Cloud shifts spending from <strong>CapEx to OpEx (pay-as-you-go)</strong>, not the other way round <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q45\">Exam 5 · Q45</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q36\">Exam 4 · Q36</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Takes weeks to release\", \"can't keep up with feature requests\": the motive is <strong>agility / faster release cycles</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q45\">Exam 5 · Q45</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q23\">Exam 1 · Q23</a>.</li>\n<li>\"IT team spends its week patching and setting up servers\": the answer is <strong>adopting managed services</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q24\">Exam 2 · Q24</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q31\">Exam 2 · Q31</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Why build cloud-native?\": <strong>agile feature releases</strong>, not \"the provider writes code for you\" <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q36\">Exam 4 · Q36</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Toil automation helps perform automatic updates\" and \"DevOps requires market trends to be measured\" sound like SRE/DevOps vocabulary but don't answer the question. <strong>Toil</strong> is repetitive manual operations work; automating it frees time but doesn't make app updates happen by themselves. <strong>DevOps</strong> is culture and practices for faster, more reliable delivery, not market tracking <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q23\">Exam 1 · Q23</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Personalized content from customer data\" is an AI/data benefit, not a modernization benefit <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q23\">Exam 1 · Q23</a>.</li>\n<li>Options that say code \"changes by itself\", the provider \"writes all source code\" or that you can \"speed up capital expenditure planning\" are always wrong <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q45\">Exam 5 · Q45</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q36\">Exam 4 · Q36</a>.</li>\n<li>Managed services do <strong>not</strong> take over <strong>user access management</strong>. Under shared responsibility, IAM decisions stay with the customer <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q31\">Exam 2 · Q31</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Edge computing</strong> runs workloads close to users or devices to cut latency. <strong>Bare metal provisioning</strong> leaves even more admin with you. Neither hands patching to Google <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q24\">Exam 2 · Q24</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"Companies modernize because legacy apps are slow to change. This lesson covers the business case: faster releases, agile cloud-native development, and managed services that take routine administration off the IT team.","objectives":["Explain why slow release cycles drive application modernization","Describe what building cloud-native adds over only moving an existing app","Identify which tasks a managed service takes over and which decisions stay with the customer"],"takeaways":["Apps that take weeks to update are the classic trigger for modernization.","Modernizing on Google Cloud lets teams release fixes and features faster.","Faster releases come from CI/CD, automated testing and managed services, not from apps updating themselves.","Cloud-native apps support agile feature releases.","Managed services handle patching and upgrades. Access, archiving and cost decisions stay with you, even though Google provides tools for them.","Cloud moves spending toward OpEx, never back to CapEx."],"questions":["e1q23","e2q24","e2q31","e2q34","e4q36","e5q45"]},{"id":"d4l2","num":2,"title":"Virtualization, virtual machines and Compute Engine","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>hypervisor</strong> is the software layer that lets <strong>several operating systems</strong> (for example Linux and Windows) run on <strong>one physical machine</strong>, each in its own virtual machine <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q15\">Exam 4 · Q15</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Virtual machines</strong> each have their <strong>own dedicated OS</strong>. If apps can't share an OS environment, they need VMs, not containers <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q6\">Exam 6 · Q6</a>.</li>\n<li>A VM looks like a traditional server (guest OS, virtual CPU, memory, disk, network). That is why legacy apps move onto Compute Engine with little change <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q6\">Exam 6 · Q6</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Compute Engine</strong> is Google Cloud's IaaS VM service. Choose it when you need <strong>detailed control</strong> over the VM, OS configuration, networking and machine types. It still supports <strong>autoscaling and automatic rolling updates</strong> through <strong>managed instance groups (MIGs)</strong>: a MIG adds or removes identical VMs as load changes and rolls configuration updates out across them <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q37\">Exam 3 · Q37</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Spot VMs</strong> (successor to <strong>Preemptible VMs</strong>) use spare capacity at a deep discount. Google can <strong>stop them when it needs the capacity back</strong>. They fit batch jobs that <strong>checkpoint, can restart and aren't urgent</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q45\">Exam 2 · Q45</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q34\">Exam 4 · Q34</a>. Typical uses: batch processing, rendering, testing and other fault-tolerant jobs. Spot is a <strong>pricing and provisioning option</strong>, not an architecture pattern <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q26\">Exam 2 · Q26</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q18\">Exam 2 · Q18</a>.</li>\n<li>Pricing options on Compute Engine beyond on-demand: <strong>sustained-use discounts</strong> (automatic for long-running VMs) and <strong>committed-use discounts</strong> (1- or 3-year commitment) <em>(not in the question bank)</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Compute Engine options compared</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Need</th>\n<th>Choice</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Full control of OS, network, machine type</td>\n<td>✅ Compute Engine (standard or custom VM)</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q37\">Exam 3 · Q37</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Each app needs its own OS</td>\n<td>✅ Virtual machines</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q6\">Exam 6 · Q6</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cheapest for fault-tolerant, restartable, non-urgent batch</td>\n<td>✅ Spot VMs / Preemptible VMs</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q45\">Exam 2 · Q45</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q34\">Exam 4 · Q34</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Same batch job on standard or custom pay-as-you-go VM</td>\n<td>❌ Works but costs more</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q34\">Exam 4 · Q34</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Same batch job on Cloud Run functions</td>\n<td>❌ Meant for short event-driven code, not long nightly jobs</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q45\">Exam 2 · Q45</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q34\">Exam 4 · Q34</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Run multiple operating systems on one physical server\": <strong>hypervisor</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q15\">Exam 4 · Q15</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Dedicated OS per application\" or \"cannot share an OS\": <strong>virtual machines</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q6\">Exam 6 · Q6</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Detailed control over VMs, OS and machine types\": <strong>Compute Engine</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q37\">Exam 3 · Q37</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Can be interrupted\", \"restarts from checkpoints\", \"cheapest\": <strong>Spot VMs</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q45\">Exam 2 · Q45</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q34\">Exam 4 · Q34</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Containers share the host OS kernel</strong>, so they don't give each app its own OS <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q6\">Exam 6 · Q6</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q15\">Exam 4 · Q15</a>.</li>\n<li>Cloud Build is a CI/CD build service, not somewhere to run an app. App Engine and Cloud Run hide the VM, so they fail a \"detailed VM control\" requirement <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q37\">Exam 3 · Q37</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Premium tier virtual machines\" isn't a real cost-saving option. In Google Cloud, <strong>Premium Tier</strong> is a <strong>Network Service Tier</strong> (traffic rides Google's global network). It's a networking choice, not a discounted VM <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q45\">Exam 2 · Q45</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Managed services\" is a general approach, not a compute option that trades interruptions for a lower price <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q45\">Exam 2 · Q45</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Hardware virtualization</strong> abstracts physical servers into VMs. It's an infrastructure layer, not a way to split an app into services <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q26\">Exam 2 · Q26</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"Virtualization is the base of cloud compute. This lesson covers hypervisors, virtual machines, Compute Engine and its managed instance groups for workloads that need full control, and Spot VMs for cheap, interruptible batch jobs.","objectives":["Explain what a hypervisor does","Choose virtual machines when each app needs its own operating system","Pick Compute Engine when a workload needs control over OS, networking and machine types","Identify workloads that suit Spot VMs"],"takeaways":["A hypervisor runs several operating systems on one physical machine.","VMs give each app a dedicated OS. Containers share the host kernel.","Compute Engine = maximum control. Managed instance groups still give autoscaling and rolling updates.","Spot VMs (successor to Preemptible VMs) are the cheapest option for restartable, non-urgent batch work.","\"Premium Tier\" is a Network Service Tier, not a cheaper kind of VM."],"questions":["e2q18","e2q26","e2q45","e3q37","e4q15","e4q34","e6q6"]},{"id":"d4l3","num":3,"title":"Containers, microservices and Google Kubernetes Engine","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Containerization</strong> bundles application code <strong>with its dependencies</strong> (runtime libraries, package versions), so it behaves the same on a laptop, in test and in production. It fixes \"works on my machine\". The packaged unit is a <strong>container image</strong> (code, runtime, system tools, libraries, config); the same image runs on a laptop, in test and on Cloud Run or GKE <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q40\">Exam 5 · Q40</a>.</li>\n<li>Containers are lighter than VMs because they <strong>share the host OS kernel</strong>. They start fast and pack densely.</li>\n<li><strong>Microservices</strong> split a large app into <strong>small independent services</strong>, each owning one business function, each built, deployed and scaled separately. The opposite is a <strong>monolith</strong>: one tightly coupled unit (UI, business logic, data access deployed together), so changing or scaling one part means handling the whole app <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q26\">Exam 2 · Q26</a>.</li>\n<li>Breaking a monolith into microservices (refactoring) makes the app <strong>more efficient and scalable</strong>. Each service (catalog, checkout, payments) scales with its own demand instead of the whole app scaling together. It needs code changes and is not the quickest route to the cloud <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q37\">Exam 2 · Q37</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE)</strong> is Google's managed <strong>Kubernetes</strong> service for <strong>orchestrating containers</strong>: deploying, scaling and operating many of them, up to thousands <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q43\">Exam 5 · Q43</a>.</li>\n<li>The exam's stock phrasing of GKE's benefit: it <strong>enables rapid application deployment and testing</strong> and <strong>shortens the time to test and refine fixes</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q15\">Exam 1 · Q15</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q12\">Exam 3 · Q12</a>.</li>\n<li>Why: you push a new image and Kubernetes <strong>rolls it out</strong>; a bad release <strong>rolls back</strong>. GKE also gives service discovery, load balancing and autoscaling, and plugs into CI/CD pipelines <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q12\">Exam 3 · Q12</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q15\">Exam 1 · Q15</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>GKE Autopilot</strong> goes further: Google manages <strong>nodes, scaling, security settings and cluster operations</strong>, so the team focuses on apps while still using Kubernetes. <strong>GKE Standard</strong> leaves node configuration to you <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q43\">Exam 2 · Q43</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>GKE modes compared</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>GKE Standard</th>\n<th>GKE Autopilot</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Who manages nodes</td>\n<td>You</td>\n<td>Google</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Billing</td>\n<td>Per node (VM)</td>\n<td>Per Pod resource request</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pick when</td>\n<td>You need node-level control</td>\n<td>\"Reduce time spent configuring nodes and cluster operations\" <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q43\">Exam 2 · Q43</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Same code breaks in production because of different libraries\": <strong>containerization</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q40\">Exam 5 · Q40</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Split into independent services, deployed and scaled separately\": <strong>microservices</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q26\">Exam 2 · Q26</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Run and coordinate thousands of containers\", \"orchestrate containers\": <strong>GKE</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q43\">Exam 5 · Q43</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q15\">Exam 1 · Q15</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Kubernetes, but less cluster admin\": <strong>GKE Autopilot</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q43\">Exam 2 · Q43</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Why GKE?\": <strong>rapid deployment and testing / faster test-and-refine cycles</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q15\">Exam 1 · Q15</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q12\">Exam 3 · Q12</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>GKE does <strong>not</strong> remove the need to package apps in containers, and it doesn't suggest code redesigns or copy source code between servers. Source-code collaboration belongs in version control <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q12\">Exam 3 · Q12</a>.</li>\n<li>Installing Kubernetes yourself on Compute Engine VMs works, but you then manage VMs, nodes, upgrades and scaling. That fails \"less cluster admin\" <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q43\">Exam 2 · Q43</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q25\">Exam 5 · Q25</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Apigee</strong> can sit in front of microservices as an API layer, but it doesn't schedule or scale containers <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q43\">Exam 5 · Q43</a>.</li>\n<li>GKE doesn't \"provide pre-written source code\", \"optimize VM performance\" or \"connect legacy systems\" (that's an API job) <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q15\">Exam 1 · Q15</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Cloud Run</strong> also runs containers and is less work, but it's not Kubernetes. When the stem says \"Kubernetes\" or \"thousands of containers to orchestrate\", stay with GKE <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q43\">Exam 2 · Q43</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q43\">Exam 5 · Q43</a>.</li>\n<li>Spot VMs and load balancing don't solve dependency drift <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q40\">Exam 5 · Q40</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"Containers package code with its dependencies, microservices split apps into independent pieces, and GKE orchestrates the containers. This lesson covers each and when GKE Autopilot fits better than Standard.","objectives":["Explain how containerization fixes environment drift between development and production","Describe microservices and how they differ from a monolith","Explain when to use GKE and the exam's stock description of its benefit","Distinguish GKE Autopilot from GKE Standard"],"takeaways":["Containers bundle code and dependencies so apps behave the same everywhere.","Microservices are built, deployed and scaled independently, each owning one business function. A monolith is one tightly coupled unit.","GKE orchestrates containers at scale, up to thousands.","GKE's exam-phrased benefit is rapid deployment and testing, thanks to rollouts, rollbacks and CI/CD.","Autopilot lets Google manage nodes and cluster operations."],"questions":["e1q15","e2q26","e2q37","e2q43","e3q12","e5q25","e5q40","e5q43"]},{"id":"d4l4","num":4,"title":"Serverless: Cloud Run, Cloud Run functions and App Engine","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Serverless computing</strong> means you write code and Google Cloud handles <strong>server provisioning, scaling, patching and runtime infrastructure</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q9\">Exam 2 · Q9</a>.</li>\n<li>The headline serverless benefit is <strong>scalability</strong>: it scales from a few users to tens of thousands and <strong>down to zero</strong>, so you don't pay for idle capacity <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q16\">Exam 2 · Q16</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Cloud Run</strong> runs <strong>container images</strong> serverlessly: autoscaling, <strong>scale to zero</strong>, no clusters or servers to manage, any language or tool you can put in a container <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q47\">Exam 1 · Q47</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q12\">Exam 5 · Q12</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q35\">Exam 5 · Q35</a>.</li>\n<li>Cloud Run suits <strong>stateless</strong> containers that answer web requests or events. It now hosts <strong>services, jobs and functions</strong> under one platform <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q12\">Exam 5 · Q12</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q35\">Exam 5 · Q35</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Cloud Run functions</strong> (formerly Cloud Functions) run <strong>small pieces of code in response to events</strong>: a form submission, a file upload to Cloud Storage, a price crossing a threshold, an event from another service <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q16\">Exam 1 · Q16</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q18\">Exam 2 · Q18</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q31\">Exam 6 · Q31</a>.</li>\n<li>Functions are <strong>single-purpose</strong> and can be triggered by HTTP requests, Pub/Sub messages or Cloud Storage changes. Events from Google Cloud services reach the function through <strong>Eventarc</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q18\">Exam 2 · Q18</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q47\">Exam 5 · Q47</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>App Engine</strong> is Google's original PaaS for <strong>web apps</strong>. You deploy code, it handles the infrastructure and <strong>scales automatically</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q50\">Exam 2 · Q50</a>. It has two environments: <strong>standard</strong> (supported language runtimes) and <strong>flexible</strong> (runs your app in containers on Compute Engine VMs) <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q2\">Exam 2 · Q2</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q47\">Exam 1 · Q47</a>.</li>\n<li>App Engine <strong>versions and traffic splitting</strong> let you run <strong>different versions for different users</strong>, for example to A/B test a redesigned home page before full release. The same mechanism supports <strong>canary and phased rollouts</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q32\">Exam 2 · Q32</a>.</li>\n<li>Serverless also cuts development and operations effort, but on the exam that is a secondary benefit. Unknown traffic plus \"no idle cost\" is answered by <strong>scalability</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q16\">Exam 2 · Q16</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Serverless options compared</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Clue in the stem</th>\n<th>Choice</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>\"Packaged as a container image\", \"scale to zero\", \"no clusters\"</td>\n<td>✅ Cloud Run</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q47\">Exam 1 · Q47</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q12\">Exam 5 · Q12</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"Preferred languages and tools\" + \"less server management\"</td>\n<td>✅ Cloud Run</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q35\">Exam 5 · Q35</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"Small piece of code\" + \"whenever X happens\" (upload, form, event)</td>\n<td>✅ Cloud Run functions</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q16\">Exam 1 · Q16</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q18\">Exam 2 · Q18</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q31\">Exam 6 · Q31</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"Write the function code, Google handles servers\" (generic)</td>\n<td>✅ Serverless computing</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q9\">Exam 2 · Q9</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Web app, auto-scaling, focus on code</td>\n<td>✅ App Engine</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q50\">Exam 2 · Q50</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Different app versions for different users</td>\n<td>✅ App Engine traffic splitting</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q32\">Exam 2 · Q32</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Need VM/OS control</td>\n<td>❌ Serverless. Use Compute Engine</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q37\">Exam 3 · Q37</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Event-driven + small code + no servers: <strong>Cloud Run functions</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q16\">Exam 1 · Q16</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q18\">Exam 2 · Q18</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q31\">Exam 6 · Q31</a>.</li>\n<li>One container + serverless + autoscaling: <strong>Cloud Run</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q47\">Exam 1 · Q47</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q12\">Exam 5 · Q12</a>.</li>\n<li>Unpredictable launch traffic + don't pay when idle: <strong>scalability</strong> benefit of serverless <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q16\">Exam 2 · Q16</a>.</li>\n<li>A/B or canary of an app version for a subset of users: <strong>App Engine</strong> versions <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q32\">Exam 2 · Q32</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>App Engine is a valid distractor for Cloud Run</strong> questions. App Engine flexible can run containers, but when the stem says \"packaged as one container image\" and \"simplest serverless\", pick Cloud Run <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q47\">Exam 1 · Q47</a>.</li>\n<li>App Engine traffic splitting serves <strong>different versions to different users</strong>, not different versions to the same user <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q32\">Exam 2 · Q32</a>.</li>\n<li>Artifact Registry stores container images and language packages (it replaced Container Registry). It doesn't run them <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q9\">Exam 2 · Q9</a>.</li>\n<li>Managed Service for Apache Spark (formerly Dataproc) runs data processing jobs. <strong>Pub/Sub</strong> is asynchronous messaging that decouples services; an app on Cloud Run can use it, but it doesn't host apps <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q35\">Exam 5 · Q35</a>.</li>\n<li>AlloyDB Omni is a database. Bare Metal Solution is the opposite of serverless <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q12\">Exam 5 · Q12</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q16\">Exam 1 · Q16</a>.</li>\n<li>Disaster recovery and built-in security are real cloud benefits, but they don't answer \"unknown traffic, no idle cost\" <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q16\">Exam 2 · Q16</a>.</li>\n<li>Traffic splitting keeps each user on one version. Switching the <strong>same</strong> user between versions would spoil the test <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q32\">Exam 2 · Q32</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"Serverless means Google runs the servers and the app scales automatically. This lesson separates Cloud Run (stateless containers), Cloud Run functions (event-driven snippets, triggered through Eventarc) and App Engine (standard and flexible environments, with versions and traffic splitting).","objectives":["Define serverless computing and its main benefit","Choose Cloud Run for a containerized app that should scale to zero","Choose Cloud Run functions for small event-driven code","Explain how App Engine versions support testing with different users"],"takeaways":["Serverless: you write code, Google handles provisioning, scaling and patching.","Scalability, including scale to zero, is the headline serverless benefit.","Container image + serverless = Cloud Run.","Small code that runs when something happens = Cloud Run functions.","App Engine runs different versions for different users.","App Engine flexible can run containers, but \"one container image, simplest serverless\" still means Cloud Run.","Pub/Sub carries messages between services and Artifact Registry stores images. Neither hosts apps."],"questions":["e1q16","e1q47","e2q2","e2q9","e2q16","e2q32","e2q50","e3q37","e5q12","e5q35","e5q47"]},{"id":"d4l5","num":5,"title":"Migration paths: rehost, replatform, refactor, reimagine","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Rehost (lift and shift):</strong> move the app as-is, <strong>no code changes, no redesign</strong>. Same OS images, settings and configs, only the minimum changes to run on Compute Engine. Fastest path, typical when a data-center contract is ending <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q5\">Exam 1 · Q5</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q13\">Exam 5 · Q13</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Replatform (move and improve):</strong> keep the app logic mostly unchanged but swap a component for a managed one, for example a self-managed database for <strong>Cloud SQL</strong>. A newer runtime platform or limited configuration changes also count <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q34\">Exam 2 · Q34</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q13\">Exam 5 · Q13</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Refactor:</strong> change the code, often splitting a monolith into microservices. Result: <strong>more efficient and scalable</strong>, but slower and more work <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q37\">Exam 2 · Q37</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Reimagine / rebuild:</strong> rethink how the business process or app should work and build it new with cloud-native capabilities. The most change of all <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q5\">Exam 1 · Q5</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q13\">Exam 5 · Q13</a>.</li>\n<li>Google also frames modernization as <strong>\"invent in greenfield\"</strong> and <strong>\"invent in brownfield\"</strong>:</li>\n<li><strong>Greenfield</strong>: no existing app. You build everything new on Google Cloud <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q44\">Exam 3 · Q44</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Brownfield</strong>: you build a <strong>new cloud-native app that will replace an existing legacy system</strong>. The old system keeps running until cutover, so you plan around its users, data and integrations <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q30\">Exam 3 · Q30</a>.</li>\n<li>While building cloud-native apps, companies also <strong>move selected legacy workloads to the cloud</strong>. Modernization runs in parallel with migration, not instead of it <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q33\">Exam 5 · Q33</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Retain</strong> = keep the workload where it is for now (not ready, complex dependencies, or compliance). <strong>Retire</strong> = decommission a workload nobody needs. Neither moves anything <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q34\">Exam 2 · Q34</a>.</li>\n<li>Google's migration tooling: <strong>Migration Center</strong> for discovery and TCO assessment, <strong>Migrate to Virtual Machines</strong> for moving VMs into Compute Engine, <strong>Migrate to Containers</strong> for turning VMs into containers <em>(not in the question bank)</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Migration strategies compared</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Strategy</th>\n<th>Code changes</th>\n<th>Speed</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Rehost (lift and shift)</td>\n<td>None</td>\n<td>Fastest</td>\n<td>Copy VMs to Compute Engine unchanged</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q5\">Exam 1 · Q5</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q13\">Exam 5 · Q13</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Replatform (move and improve)</td>\n<td>Small</td>\n<td>Fast</td>\n<td>Self-managed DB → Cloud SQL</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q34\">Exam 2 · Q34</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Refactor</td>\n<td>Significant</td>\n<td>Slower</td>\n<td>Monolith → microservices</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q37\">Exam 2 · Q37</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reimagine</td>\n<td>New design</td>\n<td>Slowest</td>\n<td>Rethink the process with cloud-native services</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q5\">Exam 1 · Q5</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q13\">Exam 5 · Q13</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Retain</td>\n<td>None (stays put)</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Keep on-premises for now</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q34\">Exam 2 · Q34</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Retire</td>\n<td>None (switched off)</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Decommission an unneeded app</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q34\">Exam 2 · Q34</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Invent in greenfield</td>\n<td>New app</td>\n<td>Depends</td>\n<td>No existing app to move</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q44\">Exam 3 · Q44</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Invent in brownfield</td>\n<td>New app, replaces old</td>\n<td>Depends</td>\n<td>Cloud-native replacement for on-prem claims system</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q30\">Exam 3 · Q30</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"No changes to code or architecture\" / \"same images and configs\": <strong>rehost</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q5\">Exam 1 · Q5</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q13\">Exam 5 · Q13</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Replace one self-managed layer with a managed service, logic unchanged\": <strong>replatform</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q34\">Exam 2 · Q34</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Benefit of refactoring into microservices\": <strong>more efficient and scalable</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q37\">Exam 2 · Q37</a>.</li>\n<li>\"No existing application\": <strong>greenfield</strong>. \"New app that will replace an existing one\": <strong>brownfield</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q44\">Exam 3 · Q44</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q30\">Exam 3 · Q30</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Refactoring is <strong>not</strong> the quickest path and does <strong>not</strong> avoid code changes. No migration makes an app \"automatically PCI-DSS compliant\" <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q37\">Exam 2 · Q37</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Move then change\" and \"change then move\" describe rehost-then-modernize and modernize-then-rehost. They aren't the answer when the stem stresses building a <strong>new</strong> app <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q30\">Exam 3 · Q30</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q44\">Exam 3 · Q44</a>.</li>\n<li>Rehost vs replatform: if <strong>anything</strong> is swapped for a managed service, it's no longer pure lift and shift <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q34\">Exam 2 · Q34</a>.</li>\n<li>Retain and retire are distractors whenever the stem says the app is <strong>actively moving</strong> to Google Cloud <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q34\">Exam 2 · Q34</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"Migration strategies differ in how much the app changes. This lesson covers rehost, replatform, refactor and reimagine, the retain and retire options, and Google's greenfield and brownfield framing.","objectives":["Match rehost, replatform, refactor, reimagine, retain and retire to their scenarios","Explain the trade-off between migration speed and modernization benefit","Distinguish greenfield from brownfield invention"],"takeaways":["Rehost (lift and shift) changes nothing and is the fastest.","Replatform swaps in a managed component, such as Cloud SQL, with logic mostly unchanged.","Refactor into microservices for efficiency and scale, at the cost of time and code changes.","Retain keeps a workload where it is for now. Retire switches it off.","Greenfield = no existing app. Brownfield = new app that replaces an old one.","Building cloud-native goes alongside moving selected legacy workloads."],"questions":["e1q5","e2q34","e2q37","e3q30","e3q44","e5q13","e5q33"]},{"id":"d4l6","num":6,"title":"Specialized migration targets: VMware, bare metal and databases","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Google Cloud VMware Engine</strong> runs your <strong>VMware stack natively</strong> on Google Cloud. Admins keep using <strong>familiar VMware tools and procedures</strong>. It's the low-change path for a large VMware estate <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q47\">Exam 2 · Q47</a>. The managed environment includes <strong>vSphere, vCenter, vSAN, NSX and HCX</strong>, so there's no immediate refactoring or retraining <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q47\">Exam 2 · Q47</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Bare Metal Solution</strong> gives dedicated physical servers in Google-run facilities, close to Google Cloud. Use it for workloads with <strong>strict third-party licensing</strong>, <strong>low-level server access</strong> or architectures that <strong>can't be virtualized</strong> (classic case: Oracle databases). Workloads run <strong>as-is</strong> and reach Google Cloud services over <strong>low-latency networking</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q42\">Exam 1 · Q42</a>. A <strong>Bare Metal Solution for SAP</strong> variant targets SAP workloads with certification needs <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q16\">Exam 1 · Q16</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Database Migration Service</strong> moves databases (including <strong>SQL Server</strong>, MySQL and PostgreSQL) into <strong>Cloud SQL</strong> with minimal downtime. It's the fast way to get a <strong>managed</strong> database <strong>without changing database engine</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q38\">Exam 4 · Q38</a>. Cloud SQL supports <strong>SQL Server, MySQL and PostgreSQL</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q38\">Exam 4 · Q38</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"VMware estate, keep VMware tools\": <strong>Google Cloud VMware Engine</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q47\">Exam 2 · Q47</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Licensing terms, low-level/hardware access, can't run on VMs\": <strong>Bare Metal Solution</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q42\">Exam 1 · Q42</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Move quickly to a managed service, keep SQL Server\": <strong>Database Migration Service → Cloud SQL</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q38\">Exam 4 · Q38</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>VMware vSphere</strong> is VMware's own product, not a Google Cloud service. Compute Engine would mean converting the VMs <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q47\">Exam 2 · Q47</a>.</li>\n<li>Compute Engine and serverless options fail the \"low-level server access + licensing\" requirement <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q42\">Exam 1 · Q42</a>.</li>\n<li>Moving SQL Server data to <strong>Firestore</strong> (redesign), <strong>BigQuery</strong> (analytics warehouse) or <strong>Cloud Storage</strong> exports doesn't keep the SQL Server approach <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q38\">Exam 4 · Q38</a>. Firestore is a <strong>NoSQL document</strong> database, so switching means rewriting queries, schemas and app logic. Cloud Storage is object storage, fine for backups and archives but not a running database <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q38\">Exam 4 · Q38</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>AlloyDB Omni</strong> is downloadable PostgreSQL-compatible database software you run yourself. It isn't a place to host apps <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q12\">Exam 5 · Q12</a>.</li>\n<li>Compute Engine now offers some bare metal machine types too, but \"strict licensing + run as-is + low-level access\" still points to <strong>Bare Metal Solution</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q42\">Exam 1 · Q42</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"Some workloads need a specialized landing zone. This lesson covers Google Cloud VMware Engine, Bare Metal Solution and Database Migration Service into Cloud SQL.","objectives":["Choose VMware Engine for VMware estates that must keep their tools (vSphere, vCenter, vSAN, NSX, HCX)","Choose Bare Metal Solution for licensing-bound or hardware-dependent workloads","Use Database Migration Service to move SQL Server and other engines into Cloud SQL"],"takeaways":["VMware tools and procedures unchanged = Google Cloud VMware Engine.","Strict licensing or low-level server access = Bare Metal Solution.","Keep the database engine but make it managed = Database Migration Service to Cloud SQL.","Cloud SQL runs SQL Server, MySQL and PostgreSQL. Firestore is NoSQL, so moving to it means a redesign.","vSphere, Firestore and BigQuery are distractors in these scenarios."],"questions":["e1q16","e1q42","e2q47","e4q38","e5q12"]},{"id":"d4l7","num":7,"title":"Hybrid cloud, multicloud, GKE Enterprise and open standards","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hybrid cloud</strong> = on-premises (or private cloud) <strong>plus</strong> a public cloud. Typical reasons: some data must stay in your data center by regulation or choice, while you still use Google Cloud services such as BigQuery or ML <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q12\">Exam 6 · Q12</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q44\">Exam 6 · Q44</a>. Other drivers: data residency, latency, legacy systems, or moving gradually instead of all at once <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q44\">Exam 6 · Q44</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q12\">Exam 6 · Q12</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Multicloud</strong> = <strong>more than one public cloud provider</strong>, for example running Kubernetes apps across three clouds for more locations <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q5\">Exam 4 · Q5</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>GKE Enterprise</strong> (formerly Anthos) is Google's platform for managing <strong>Kubernetes clusters consistently across Google Cloud, on-premises and other public clouds</strong>: one control point, consistent security policies, modernizing old apps and building new container services <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q24\">Exam 1 · Q24</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q20\">Exam 2 · Q20</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q31\">Exam 3 · Q31</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q25\">Exam 5 · Q25</a>.</li>\n<li>Its building blocks: <strong>fleets</strong> (groups of clusters managed as one), centralized <strong>policy and configuration</strong>, service management and security controls. Other clouds supported include AWS and Azure <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q24\">Exam 1 · Q24</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q20\">Exam 2 · Q20</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q31\">Exam 3 · Q31</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Config Sync</strong> (successor to Anthos Config Management) applies Kubernetes configuration and policies from one source of truth, such as a Git repo, to every registered cluster <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q38\">Exam 5 · Q38</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Openness and interoperability</strong> guide strategy when leaders fear <strong>vendor lock-in</strong> and want freedom to run across public, private and on-prem environments <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q18\">Exam 5 · Q18</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Open standards</strong> reduce the risk that one provider makes future migration hard <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q21\">Exam 6 · Q21</a>. Examples: open APIs, open data formats, containers and Kubernetes. They <strong>reduce</strong> migration effort; they don't remove it <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q21\">Exam 6 · Q21</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q18\">Exam 5 · Q18</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Open source</strong> = source code is public, so anyone can study, change and contribute to it. Kubernetes is Google's best-known open-source contribution <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q44\">Exam 5 · Q44</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>BigQuery Omni</strong> lets BigQuery query data stored in other clouds (AWS, Azure) without moving it. It is analytics, not workload management <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q20\">Exam 2 · Q20</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Google Distributed Cloud</strong> extends Google Cloud infrastructure to edge locations and customer data centers. GKE Enterprise uses it to run clusters on-premises <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q5\">Exam 4 · Q5</a>. Air-gapped options exist for strict sovereignty needs.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Environment types compared</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Environment</th>\n<th>What it is</th>\n<th>Exam clue</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Hybrid cloud</td>\n<td>On-prem + public cloud</td>\n<td>\"Keep part of data in our data center\", \"regulation requires on-prem\"</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q12\">Exam 6 · Q12</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q44\">Exam 6 · Q44</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Multicloud</td>\n<td>2+ public clouds</td>\n<td>\"Three public cloud providers\"</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q5\">Exam 4 · Q5</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Private cloud</td>\n<td>Cloud-style infra dedicated to one org</td>\n<td>Rarely the answer when Google services must be used</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q44\">Exam 6 · Q44</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Public cloud only</td>\n<td>Everything in one provider</td>\n<td>Fails \"data must stay on-prem\"</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q44\">Exam 6 · Q44</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Kubernetes across <strong>Google Cloud + on-prem and/or another cloud</strong>, one management plane: <strong>GKE Enterprise</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q24\">Exam 1 · Q24</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q20\">Exam 2 · Q20</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q31\">Exam 3 · Q31</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q25\">Exam 5 · Q25</a>.</li>\n<li>Use Google Cloud services but <strong>keep data on-prem</strong>: <strong>hybrid</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q12\">Exam 6 · Q12</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q44\">Exam 6 · Q44</a>.</li>\n<li>Several <strong>public</strong> clouds: <strong>multicloud</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q5\">Exam 4 · Q5</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Worried about lock-in\": <strong>openness, interoperability, open standards</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q18\">Exam 5 · Q18</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q21\">Exam 6 · Q21</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Source code public, anyone can contribute\": <strong>open source</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q44\">Exam 5 · Q44</a>.</li>\n<li>Data spread across Google Cloud and other public cloud providers, brought together for analysis: <strong>multicloud</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q48\">Exam 6 · Q48</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>A stem can mention <strong>GKE Enterprise</strong> and still ask for the deployment type. Three public clouds = <strong>multicloud</strong>, not hybrid <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q5\">Exam 4 · Q5</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Apigee hybrid</strong> manages APIs across environments, not Kubernetes workloads. It runs the API runtime in your own Kubernetes while Google manages the control plane <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q24\">Exam 1 · Q24</a>.</li>\n<li>Cloud Run, Cloud Run functions, App Engine and Compute Engine run only in Google Cloud, so none is a cross-environment management answer <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q31\">Exam 3 · Q31</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q25\">Exam 5 · Q25</a>.</li>\n<li>Proprietary databases, single private cloud or \"SaaS only\" increase dependence rather than reduce it <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q18\">Exam 5 · Q18</a>. SLAs and scalable infrastructure don't address portability. \"Database services\" alone don't either; a highly specialized managed database can <strong>increase</strong> lock-in <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q21\">Exam 6 · Q21</a>.</li>\n<li>PaaS and \"closed standard\" are distractors for open source. <strong>Proprietary software</strong> = the vendor controls the code. <strong>Closed standard</strong> = a spec you need permission or a license to implement. PaaS is a service model, not a licensing model <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q44\">Exam 5 · Q44</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Config Sync</strong> keeps cluster configuration consistent. It doesn't share content with partners <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q38\">Exam 5 · Q38</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"Many companies run across on-premises and several clouds. This lesson defines hybrid and multicloud, covers GKE Enterprise (fleets, central policy, Config Sync) for consistent Kubernetes management everywhere, and explains openness as a hedge against lock-in.","objectives":["Distinguish hybrid cloud from multicloud from a scenario","Explain when GKE Enterprise is the answer","Connect openness, open standards and open source to avoiding vendor lock-in"],"takeaways":["Hybrid = on-premises plus public cloud. Multicloud = more than one public cloud.","Data must stay on-premises but you want Google services = hybrid.","Kubernetes managed consistently across Google Cloud, on-prem and other clouds = GKE Enterprise (formerly Anthos), organized into fleets.","Openness, interoperability and open standards reduce lock-in.","Open source = public code anyone can study, change and contribute to.","Config Sync keeps cluster configuration consistent. Apigee hybrid manages APIs, not workloads."],"questions":["e1q24","e2q20","e3q31","e4q5","e5q18","e5q38","e5q44","e6q12","e6q21","e6q44","e6q48"]},{"id":"d4l8","num":8,"title":"APIs and Apigee: unlocking legacy systems and partner value","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>An <strong>API (application programming interface)</strong> is a <strong>defined set of rules and methods that lets software applications communicate</strong> with each other through requests and responses <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q42\">Exam 2 · Q42</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Unlock legacy systems without replacing them.</strong> An API in front of an old system exposes selected data to modern apps and partners, so you <strong>don't have to rewrite the legacy platform</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q18\">Exam 1 · Q18</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q8\">Exam 4 · Q8</a>.</li>\n<li>The API is an <strong>abstraction layer</strong>: consumers only need to know how to call it, not how the backend works. That loose coupling lets you modernize the backend later, piece by piece <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q21\">Exam 1 · Q21</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q8\">Exam 4 · Q8</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q42\">Exam 2 · Q42</a>.</li>\n<li>APIs give <strong>control</strong>: authentication, authorization, quotas, rate limits and monitoring. Partners never touch the backend directly <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q18\">Exam 1 · Q18</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q21\">Exam 1 · Q21</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q2\">Exam 5 · Q2</a>.</li>\n<li>APIs make existing capabilities <strong>reusable</strong> across mobile apps, portals and cloud services, which gives <strong>long-term operational flexibility</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q21\">Exam 1 · Q21</a>.</li>\n<li>APIs <strong>connect systems programmatically</strong>, so data stays current: live store inventory on a website, live product availability in a partner app, loyalty points across partner locations <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q11\">Exam 4 · Q11</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q9\">Exam 1 · Q9</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q3\">Exam 5 · Q3</a>.</li>\n<li>APIs <strong>create new business value and revenue</strong>: a <strong>paid API</strong> to monetize data, content shared with partner sites, a booking system connected to a catering partner's order form <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q2\">Exam 5 · Q2</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q38\">Exam 5 · Q38</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q42\">Exam 5 · Q42</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Apigee</strong> is Google Cloud's <strong>API management</strong> platform. It helps <strong>expose, secure, manage and reuse</strong> APIs, and adds <strong>traffic analytics, access control, security policies and monetization (pricing plans)</strong> for external developers <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q2\">Exam 2 · Q2</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q47\">Exam 5 · Q47</a>.</li>\n<li>How Apigee works: an <strong>API proxy</strong> sits in front of backend services (which may run on GKE, Cloud Run or on-premises). It enforces API keys or OAuth, quotas and rate limits, collects usage analytics, and packages APIs as products that developers sign up and pay for <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q47\">Exam 5 · Q47</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q2\">Exam 2 · Q2</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>API benefit patterns</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Scenario</th>\n<th>API value</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Legacy core system, no budget to modernize, partner needs data</td>\n<td>Expose selected data securely</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q18\">Exam 1 · Q18</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Old reservation platform, new mobile app</td>\n<td>No rewrite of the legacy platform</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q8\">Exam 4 · Q8</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Website shows stale inventory</td>\n<td>Programmatic, live connection</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q11\">Exam 4 · Q11</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Real-time availability in, orders out to partner stores</td>\n<td>Two-way exchange between systems</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q9\">Exam 1 · Q9</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Loyalty points across partners</td>\n<td>Keep details current across systems</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q3\">Exam 5 · Q3</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Valuable data, new income stream</td>\n<td>Paid API</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q2\">Exam 5 · Q2</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Share content with partner sites</td>\n<td>API</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q38\">Exam 5 · Q38</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Booking + catering partner</td>\n<td>Connect the two systems</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q42\">Exam 5 · Q42</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reuse old capabilities everywhere</td>\n<td>Long-term operational flexibility</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q21\">Exam 1 · Q21</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Track traffic, secure, charge developers</td>\n<td>Apigee API Management</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q47\">Exam 5 · Q47</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q2\">Exam 2 · Q2</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Systems need to exchange data\", \"partners\", \"legacy system + modern app\": <strong>API</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q9\">Exam 1 · Q9</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q18\">Exam 1 · Q18</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q38\">Exam 5 · Q38</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Manage, secure, analyze, monetize APIs\": <strong>Apigee</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q2\">Exam 2 · Q2</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q47\">Exam 5 · Q47</a>.</li>\n<li>When asked <em>how</em> an API creates value, pick the option that <strong>connects specific systems</strong> over vague \"discover opportunities\" or ML options <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q42\">Exam 5 · Q42</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q3\">Exam 5 · Q3</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>APIs expose <strong>selected</strong> capabilities under control. \"Reveal every backend record so partners can change it directly\" or \"add partner users directly to the database\" is wrong <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q21\">Exam 1 · Q21</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q2\">Exam 5 · Q2</a>. Direct database access needs credentials and schema knowledge, risks bad changes, and breaks partner apps whenever the schema changes <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q2\">Exam 5 · Q2</a>.</li>\n<li>Paid <strong>file downloads</strong> of exported data are stale and hard to integrate. Moving data to a different database type doesn't create a chargeable access model <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q2\">Exam 5 · Q2</a>.</li>\n<li>Compute Engine, GKE and GKE Enterprise all mean infrastructure or modernization work. They don't, by themselves, expose legacy data to a partner <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q18\">Exam 1 · Q18</a>.</li>\n<li>An API doesn't fix the legacy system's stability, retire it immediately, convert it to cloud-native or build the mobile app for you <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q8\">Exam 4 · Q8</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q11\">Exam 4 · Q11</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Manually connecting each source one at a time\" is the opposite of an API <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q11\">Exam 4 · Q11</a>.</li>\n<li>Personalized offers, charts and ML demand forecasts are AI/analytics answers <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q3\">Exam 5 · Q3</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q42\">Exam 5 · Q42</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q11\">Exam 4 · Q11</a>.</li>\n<li>Pay-as-you-go is a cloud benefit, not an API benefit <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q21\">Exam 1 · Q21</a>.</li>\n<li>AppSheet is no-code app building, and \"App Engine flexible\" is hosting. Neither manages APIs <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q2\">Exam 2 · Q2</a>. Config Sync and NoSQL databases don't share content with partners <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q38\">Exam 5 · Q38</a>.</li>\n<li>SRE, custom ML and multi-regional databases don't connect apps to stores <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q9\">Exam 1 · Q9</a>.</li>\n<li>What an API is <strong>not</strong>: an IDE plugin, a link between cloud networks (that's VPN or Interconnect), or container scheduling (that's Kubernetes/GKE) <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q42\">Exam 2 · Q42</a>.</li>\n<li>GKE can host the API backend, but Apigee does the management and monetization <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q47\">Exam 5 · Q47</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"APIs let software systems talk to each other. This lesson covers how APIs unlock legacy systems without a rewrite, keep partner data current, create new revenue, and how Apigee manages, secures and monetizes them.","objectives":["Define an API","Explain how an API exposes legacy data without replacing the system","Identify scenarios where APIs create new business value with partners","Choose Apigee for API management, analytics and monetization"],"takeaways":["An API is a defined set of rules for applications to communicate.","APIs expose selected legacy capabilities so you don't have to rewrite the system.","APIs control access with authentication, quotas and rate limits. Partners never get direct database access.","APIs connect systems programmatically so data stays current.","A paid API turns valuable data into a new revenue stream.","Apigee puts an API proxy in front of backends to secure, track and monetize APIs."],"questions":["e1q9","e1q18","e1q21","e2q2","e2q42","e4q8","e4q11","e5q2","e5q3","e5q38","e5q42","e5q47"]}],"ref":{"cheat":"<ul>\n<li>Legacy apps take weeks to release → modernize for <strong>agility and faster updates</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q45\">Exam 5 · Q45</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q23\">Exam 1 · Q23</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q36\">Exam 4 · Q36</a>.</li>\n<li>Managed services take over <strong>patching and upgrades</strong>, not user access <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q24\">Exam 2 · Q24</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q31\">Exam 2 · Q31</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Hypervisor</strong> = many OSes on one server. <strong>VMs</strong> = dedicated OS per app <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q15\">Exam 4 · Q15</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q6\">Exam 6 · Q6</a>.</li>\n<li>Need full VM/OS/network control → <strong>Compute Engine</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q37\">Exam 3 · Q37</a>.</li>\n<li>Restartable, non-urgent, cheapest → <strong>Spot VMs</strong>. \"Premium Tier\" is networking, not a VM type <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q45\">Exam 2 · Q45</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q34\">Exam 4 · Q34</a>.</li>\n<li>VM control + autoscaling + rolling updates → <strong>Compute Engine managed instance groups</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q37\">Exam 3 · Q37</a>.</li>\n<li>Code + dependencies bundled for consistency → <strong>containers</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q40\">Exam 5 · Q40</a>.</li>\n<li>Independent services, deployed separately → <strong>microservices</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q26\">Exam 2 · Q26</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q37\">Exam 2 · Q37</a>.</li>\n<li>Orchestrate many containers → <strong>GKE</strong>. Less cluster admin → <strong>GKE Autopilot</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q43\">Exam 5 · Q43</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q15\">Exam 1 · Q15</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q12\">Exam 3 · Q12</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q43\">Exam 2 · Q43</a>.</li>\n<li>One container, serverless, scale to zero → <strong>Cloud Run</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q47\">Exam 1 · Q47</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q12\">Exam 5 · Q12</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q35\">Exam 5 · Q35</a>.</li>\n<li>Small code triggered by events → <strong>Cloud Run functions</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q16\">Exam 1 · Q16</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q18\">Exam 2 · Q18</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q31\">Exam 6 · Q31</a>.</li>\n<li>Serverless = Google runs the servers. Key benefit = <strong>scalability</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q9\">Exam 2 · Q9</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q16\">Exam 2 · Q16</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>App Engine</strong>: web apps that autoscale, versions for <strong>different users</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q50\">Exam 2 · Q50</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q32\">Exam 2 · Q32</a>.</li>\n<li>No changes → <strong>rehost</strong>. Swap in Cloud SQL → <strong>replatform</strong>. Microservices → <strong>refactor</strong>. Keep in place → <strong>retain</strong>. Switch off → <strong>retire</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q5\">Exam 1 · Q5</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q13\">Exam 5 · Q13</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q34\">Exam 2 · Q34</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q37\">Exam 2 · Q37</a>.</li>\n<li>No existing app → <strong>greenfield</strong>. New app replacing old → <strong>brownfield</strong>. Also move selected legacy workloads <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q44\">Exam 3 · Q44</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q30\">Exam 3 · Q30</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q33\">Exam 5 · Q33</a>.</li>\n<li>VMware tools → <strong>VMware Engine</strong>. Licensing/low-level access → <strong>Bare Metal Solution</strong>. SQL Server to managed → <strong>DMS → Cloud SQL</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q47\">Exam 2 · Q47</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q42\">Exam 1 · Q42</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q38\">Exam 4 · Q38</a>.</li>\n<li>Kubernetes across clouds and on-prem → <strong>GKE Enterprise</strong> (fleets, central policy; <strong>Config Sync</strong> for consistent config) <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q24\">Exam 1 · Q24</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q20\">Exam 2 · Q20</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q31\">Exam 3 · Q31</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q25\">Exam 5 · Q25</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q38\">Exam 5 · Q38</a>.</li>\n<li>Data on-prem + Google services → <strong>hybrid</strong>. Several public clouds → <strong>multicloud</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q12\">Exam 6 · Q12</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q44\">Exam 6 · Q44</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q5\">Exam 4 · Q5</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q48\">Exam 6 · Q48</a>.</li>\n<li>Avoid lock-in → <strong>openness, open standards, open source</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q18\">Exam 5 · Q18</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q21\">Exam 6 · Q21</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q44\">Exam 5 · Q44</a>.</li>\n<li>Connect systems, unlock legacy, earn from data → <strong>API</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q42\">Exam 2 · Q42</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q18\">Exam 1 · Q18</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q8\">Exam 4 · Q8</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q21\">Exam 1 · Q21</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q11\">Exam 4 · Q11</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q9\">Exam 1 · Q9</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q3\">Exam 5 · Q3</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q2\">Exam 5 · Q2</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q38\">Exam 5 · Q38</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q42\">Exam 5 · Q42</a>.</li>\n<li>Secure, track, monetize APIs → <strong>Apigee</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q2\">Exam 2 · Q2</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q47\">Exam 5 · Q47</a>.</li>\n</ul>","rules":[{"lesson":"d4l1","title":"Why modernize: agility, faster releases and managed services","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"Takes weeks to release\", \"can't keep up with feature requests\": the motive is <strong>agility / faster release cycles</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q45\">Exam 5 · Q45</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q23\">Exam 1 · Q23</a>.</li>\n<li>\"IT team spends its week patching and setting up servers\": the answer is <strong>adopting managed services</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q24\">Exam 2 · Q24</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q31\">Exam 2 · Q31</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Why build cloud-native?\": <strong>agile feature releases</strong>, not \"the provider writes code for you\" <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q36\">Exam 4 · Q36</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d4l2","title":"Virtualization, virtual machines and Compute Engine","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"Run multiple operating systems on one physical server\": <strong>hypervisor</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q15\">Exam 4 · Q15</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Dedicated OS per application\" or \"cannot share an OS\": <strong>virtual machines</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q6\">Exam 6 · Q6</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Detailed control over VMs, OS and machine types\": <strong>Compute Engine</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q37\">Exam 3 · Q37</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Can be interrupted\", \"restarts from checkpoints\", \"cheapest\": <strong>Spot VMs</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q45\">Exam 2 · Q45</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q34\">Exam 4 · Q34</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d4l3","title":"Containers, microservices and Google Kubernetes Engine","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"Same code breaks in production because of different libraries\": <strong>containerization</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q40\">Exam 5 · Q40</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Split into independent services, deployed and scaled separately\": <strong>microservices</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q26\">Exam 2 · Q26</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Run and coordinate thousands of containers\", \"orchestrate containers\": <strong>GKE</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q43\">Exam 5 · Q43</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q15\">Exam 1 · Q15</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Kubernetes, but less cluster admin\": <strong>GKE Autopilot</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q43\">Exam 2 · Q43</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Why GKE?\": <strong>rapid deployment and testing / faster test-and-refine cycles</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q15\">Exam 1 · Q15</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q12\">Exam 3 · Q12</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d4l4","title":"Serverless: Cloud Run, Cloud Run functions and App Engine","html":"<ul>\n<li>Event-driven + small code + no servers: <strong>Cloud Run functions</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q16\">Exam 1 · Q16</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q18\">Exam 2 · Q18</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q31\">Exam 6 · Q31</a>.</li>\n<li>One container + serverless + autoscaling: <strong>Cloud Run</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q47\">Exam 1 · Q47</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q12\">Exam 5 · Q12</a>.</li>\n<li>Unpredictable launch traffic + don't pay when idle: <strong>scalability</strong> benefit of serverless <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q16\">Exam 2 · Q16</a>.</li>\n<li>A/B or canary of an app version for a subset of users: <strong>App Engine</strong> versions <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q32\">Exam 2 · Q32</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d4l5","title":"Migration paths: rehost, replatform, refactor, reimagine","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"No changes to code or architecture\" / \"same images and configs\": <strong>rehost</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q5\">Exam 1 · Q5</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q13\">Exam 5 · Q13</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Replace one self-managed layer with a managed service, logic unchanged\": <strong>replatform</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q34\">Exam 2 · Q34</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Benefit of refactoring into microservices\": <strong>more efficient and scalable</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q37\">Exam 2 · Q37</a>.</li>\n<li>\"No existing application\": <strong>greenfield</strong>. \"New app that will replace an existing one\": <strong>brownfield</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q44\">Exam 3 · Q44</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q30\">Exam 3 · Q30</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d4l6","title":"Specialized migration targets: VMware, bare metal and databases","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"VMware estate, keep VMware tools\": <strong>Google Cloud VMware Engine</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q47\">Exam 2 · Q47</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Licensing terms, low-level/hardware access, can't run on VMs\": <strong>Bare Metal Solution</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q42\">Exam 1 · Q42</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Move quickly to a managed service, keep SQL Server\": <strong>Database Migration Service → Cloud SQL</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q38\">Exam 4 · Q38</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d4l7","title":"Hybrid cloud, multicloud, GKE Enterprise and open standards","html":"<ul>\n<li>Kubernetes across <strong>Google Cloud + on-prem and/or another cloud</strong>, one management plane: <strong>GKE Enterprise</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q24\">Exam 1 · Q24</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q20\">Exam 2 · Q20</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q31\">Exam 3 · Q31</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q25\">Exam 5 · Q25</a>.</li>\n<li>Use Google Cloud services but <strong>keep data on-prem</strong>: <strong>hybrid</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q12\">Exam 6 · Q12</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q44\">Exam 6 · Q44</a>.</li>\n<li>Several <strong>public</strong> clouds: <strong>multicloud</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q5\">Exam 4 · Q5</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Worried about lock-in\": <strong>openness, interoperability, open standards</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q18\">Exam 5 · Q18</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q21\">Exam 6 · Q21</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Source code public, anyone can contribute\": <strong>open source</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q44\">Exam 5 · Q44</a>.</li>\n<li>Data spread across Google Cloud and other public cloud providers, brought together for analysis: <strong>multicloud</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q48\">Exam 6 · Q48</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d4l8","title":"APIs and Apigee: unlocking legacy systems and partner value","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"Systems need to exchange data\", \"partners\", \"legacy system + modern app\": <strong>API</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q9\">Exam 1 · Q9</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q18\">Exam 1 · Q18</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q38\">Exam 5 · Q38</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Manage, secure, analyze, monetize APIs\": <strong>Apigee</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q2\">Exam 2 · Q2</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q47\">Exam 5 · Q47</a>.</li>\n<li>When asked <em>how</em> an API creates value, pick the option that <strong>connects specific systems</strong> over vague \"discover opportunities\" or ML options <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q42\">Exam 5 · Q42</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q3\">Exam 5 · Q3</a>.</li>\n</ul>"}],"traps":[{"lesson":"d4l1","title":"Why modernize: agility, faster releases and managed services","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"Toil automation helps perform automatic updates\" and \"DevOps requires market trends to be measured\" sound like SRE/DevOps vocabulary but don't answer the question. <strong>Toil</strong> is repetitive manual operations work; automating it frees time but doesn't make app updates happen by themselves. <strong>DevOps</strong> is culture and practices for faster, more reliable delivery, not market tracking <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q23\">Exam 1 · Q23</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Personalized content from customer data\" is an AI/data benefit, not a modernization benefit <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q23\">Exam 1 · Q23</a>.</li>\n<li>Options that say code \"changes by itself\", the provider \"writes all source code\" or that you can \"speed up capital expenditure planning\" are always wrong <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q45\">Exam 5 · Q45</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q36\">Exam 4 · Q36</a>.</li>\n<li>Managed services do <strong>not</strong> take over <strong>user access management</strong>. Under shared responsibility, IAM decisions stay with the customer <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q31\">Exam 2 · Q31</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Edge computing</strong> runs workloads close to users or devices to cut latency. <strong>Bare metal provisioning</strong> leaves even more admin with you. Neither hands patching to Google <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q24\">Exam 2 · Q24</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d4l2","title":"Virtualization, virtual machines and Compute Engine","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Containers share the host OS kernel</strong>, so they don't give each app its own OS <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q6\">Exam 6 · Q6</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q15\">Exam 4 · Q15</a>.</li>\n<li>Cloud Build is a CI/CD build service, not somewhere to run an app. App Engine and Cloud Run hide the VM, so they fail a \"detailed VM control\" requirement <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q37\">Exam 3 · Q37</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Premium tier virtual machines\" isn't a real cost-saving option. In Google Cloud, <strong>Premium Tier</strong> is a <strong>Network Service Tier</strong> (traffic rides Google's global network). It's a networking choice, not a discounted VM <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q45\">Exam 2 · Q45</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Managed services\" is a general approach, not a compute option that trades interruptions for a lower price <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q45\">Exam 2 · Q45</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Hardware virtualization</strong> abstracts physical servers into VMs. It's an infrastructure layer, not a way to split an app into services <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q26\">Exam 2 · Q26</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d4l3","title":"Containers, microservices and Google Kubernetes Engine","html":"<ul>\n<li>GKE does <strong>not</strong> remove the need to package apps in containers, and it doesn't suggest code redesigns or copy source code between servers. Source-code collaboration belongs in version control <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q12\">Exam 3 · Q12</a>.</li>\n<li>Installing Kubernetes yourself on Compute Engine VMs works, but you then manage VMs, nodes, upgrades and scaling. That fails \"less cluster admin\" <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q43\">Exam 2 · Q43</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q25\">Exam 5 · Q25</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Apigee</strong> can sit in front of microservices as an API layer, but it doesn't schedule or scale containers <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q43\">Exam 5 · Q43</a>.</li>\n<li>GKE doesn't \"provide pre-written source code\", \"optimize VM performance\" or \"connect legacy systems\" (that's an API job) <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q15\">Exam 1 · Q15</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Cloud Run</strong> also runs containers and is less work, but it's not Kubernetes. When the stem says \"Kubernetes\" or \"thousands of containers to orchestrate\", stay with GKE <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q43\">Exam 2 · Q43</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q43\">Exam 5 · Q43</a>.</li>\n<li>Spot VMs and load balancing don't solve dependency drift <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q40\">Exam 5 · Q40</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d4l4","title":"Serverless: Cloud Run, Cloud Run functions and App Engine","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>App Engine is a valid distractor for Cloud Run</strong> questions. App Engine flexible can run containers, but when the stem says \"packaged as one container image\" and \"simplest serverless\", pick Cloud Run <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q47\">Exam 1 · Q47</a>.</li>\n<li>App Engine traffic splitting serves <strong>different versions to different users</strong>, not different versions to the same user <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q32\">Exam 2 · Q32</a>.</li>\n<li>Artifact Registry stores container images and language packages (it replaced Container Registry). It doesn't run them <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q9\">Exam 2 · Q9</a>.</li>\n<li>Managed Service for Apache Spark (formerly Dataproc) runs data processing jobs. <strong>Pub/Sub</strong> is asynchronous messaging that decouples services; an app on Cloud Run can use it, but it doesn't host apps <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q35\">Exam 5 · Q35</a>.</li>\n<li>AlloyDB Omni is a database. Bare Metal Solution is the opposite of serverless <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q12\">Exam 5 · Q12</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q16\">Exam 1 · Q16</a>.</li>\n<li>Disaster recovery and built-in security are real cloud benefits, but they don't answer \"unknown traffic, no idle cost\" <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q16\">Exam 2 · Q16</a>.</li>\n<li>Traffic splitting keeps each user on one version. Switching the <strong>same</strong> user between versions would spoil the test <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q32\">Exam 2 · Q32</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d4l5","title":"Migration paths: rehost, replatform, refactor, reimagine","html":"<ul>\n<li>Refactoring is <strong>not</strong> the quickest path and does <strong>not</strong> avoid code changes. No migration makes an app \"automatically PCI-DSS compliant\" <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q37\">Exam 2 · Q37</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Move then change\" and \"change then move\" describe rehost-then-modernize and modernize-then-rehost. They aren't the answer when the stem stresses building a <strong>new</strong> app <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q30\">Exam 3 · Q30</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q44\">Exam 3 · Q44</a>.</li>\n<li>Rehost vs replatform: if <strong>anything</strong> is swapped for a managed service, it's no longer pure lift and shift <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q34\">Exam 2 · Q34</a>.</li>\n<li>Retain and retire are distractors whenever the stem says the app is <strong>actively moving</strong> to Google Cloud <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q34\">Exam 2 · Q34</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d4l6","title":"Specialized migration targets: VMware, bare metal and databases","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>VMware vSphere</strong> is VMware's own product, not a Google Cloud service. Compute Engine would mean converting the VMs <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q47\">Exam 2 · Q47</a>.</li>\n<li>Compute Engine and serverless options fail the \"low-level server access + licensing\" requirement <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q42\">Exam 1 · Q42</a>.</li>\n<li>Moving SQL Server data to <strong>Firestore</strong> (redesign), <strong>BigQuery</strong> (analytics warehouse) or <strong>Cloud Storage</strong> exports doesn't keep the SQL Server approach <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q38\">Exam 4 · Q38</a>. Firestore is a <strong>NoSQL document</strong> database, so switching means rewriting queries, schemas and app logic. Cloud Storage is object storage, fine for backups and archives but not a running database <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q38\">Exam 4 · Q38</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>AlloyDB Omni</strong> is downloadable PostgreSQL-compatible database software you run yourself. It isn't a place to host apps <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q12\">Exam 5 · Q12</a>.</li>\n<li>Compute Engine now offers some bare metal machine types too, but \"strict licensing + run as-is + low-level access\" still points to <strong>Bare Metal Solution</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q42\">Exam 1 · Q42</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d4l7","title":"Hybrid cloud, multicloud, GKE Enterprise and open standards","html":"<ul>\n<li>A stem can mention <strong>GKE Enterprise</strong> and still ask for the deployment type. Three public clouds = <strong>multicloud</strong>, not hybrid <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q5\">Exam 4 · Q5</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Apigee hybrid</strong> manages APIs across environments, not Kubernetes workloads. It runs the API runtime in your own Kubernetes while Google manages the control plane <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q24\">Exam 1 · Q24</a>.</li>\n<li>Cloud Run, Cloud Run functions, App Engine and Compute Engine run only in Google Cloud, so none is a cross-environment management answer <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q31\">Exam 3 · Q31</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q25\">Exam 5 · Q25</a>.</li>\n<li>Proprietary databases, single private cloud or \"SaaS only\" increase dependence rather than reduce it <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q18\">Exam 5 · Q18</a>. SLAs and scalable infrastructure don't address portability. \"Database services\" alone don't either; a highly specialized managed database can <strong>increase</strong> lock-in <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q21\">Exam 6 · Q21</a>.</li>\n<li>PaaS and \"closed standard\" are distractors for open source. <strong>Proprietary software</strong> = the vendor controls the code. <strong>Closed standard</strong> = a spec you need permission or a license to implement. PaaS is a service model, not a licensing model <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q44\">Exam 5 · Q44</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Config Sync</strong> keeps cluster configuration consistent. It doesn't share content with partners <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q38\">Exam 5 · Q38</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d4l8","title":"APIs and Apigee: unlocking legacy systems and partner value","html":"<ul>\n<li>APIs expose <strong>selected</strong> capabilities under control. \"Reveal every backend record so partners can change it directly\" or \"add partner users directly to the database\" is wrong <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q21\">Exam 1 · Q21</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q2\">Exam 5 · Q2</a>. Direct database access needs credentials and schema knowledge, risks bad changes, and breaks partner apps whenever the schema changes <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q2\">Exam 5 · Q2</a>.</li>\n<li>Paid <strong>file downloads</strong> of exported data are stale and hard to integrate. Moving data to a different database type doesn't create a chargeable access model <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q2\">Exam 5 · Q2</a>.</li>\n<li>Compute Engine, GKE and GKE Enterprise all mean infrastructure or modernization work. They don't, by themselves, expose legacy data to a partner <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q18\">Exam 1 · Q18</a>.</li>\n<li>An API doesn't fix the legacy system's stability, retire it immediately, convert it to cloud-native or build the mobile app for you <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q8\">Exam 4 · Q8</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q11\">Exam 4 · Q11</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Manually connecting each source one at a time\" is the opposite of an API <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q11\">Exam 4 · Q11</a>.</li>\n<li>Personalized offers, charts and ML demand forecasts are AI/analytics answers <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q3\">Exam 5 · Q3</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q42\">Exam 5 · Q42</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q11\">Exam 4 · Q11</a>.</li>\n<li>Pay-as-you-go is a cloud benefit, not an API benefit <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q21\">Exam 1 · Q21</a>.</li>\n<li>AppSheet is no-code app building, and \"App Engine flexible\" is hosting. Neither manages APIs <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q2\">Exam 2 · Q2</a>. Config Sync and NoSQL databases don't share content with partners <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q38\">Exam 5 · Q38</a>.</li>\n<li>SRE, custom ML and multi-regional databases don't connect apps to stores <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q9\">Exam 1 · Q9</a>.</li>\n<li>What an API is <strong>not</strong>: an IDE plugin, a link between cloud networks (that's VPN or Interconnect), or container scheduling (that's Kubernetes/GKE) <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q42\">Exam 2 · Q42</a>.</li>\n<li>GKE can host the API backend, but Apigee does the management and monetization <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q47\">Exam 5 · Q47</a>.</li>\n</ul>"}],"questionable":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Item</th>\n<th>Issue</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q15\">Exam 1 · Q15</a></strong>, <strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q12\">Exam 3 · Q12</a></strong></td>\n<td>Correct but vague. \"Rapid deployment and testing\" and \"shortens the time to test and refine fixes\" are generic benefits of containers and CI/CD, not things GKE uniquely provides. They are keyed because every other option is clearly false.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q16\">Exam 2 · Q16</a></strong></td>\n<td>\"Scalability\" is keyed, but \"avoid paying when no users are active\" is really a cost (pay-per-use, scale-to-zero) benefit. Scalability is still the best of the four options.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q50\">Exam 2 · Q50</a></strong></td>\n<td>App Engine is right among the options, but for a new web app today Google usually steers toward <strong>Cloud Run</strong>. Option \"Anthos\" is the old name of GKE Enterprise.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"},"glossary":[{"term":"API (application programming interface)","definition":"A defined set of rules and methods that lets software applications communicate through requests and responses.","exam_context":"The answer whenever systems, partners or a legacy system and a modern app must exchange data. It avoids rewriting the legacy system.","lesson":"d4l8","domain":4},{"term":"Apigee","definition":"Google Cloud's API management platform. An API proxy in front of backend services enforces security, quotas and rate limits, collects analytics and supports monetization.","exam_context":"Pick it to expose and reuse backend services as APIs, track traffic, apply access rules (API keys, OAuth) and charge external developers.","lesson":"d4l8","domain":4},{"term":"Apigee hybrid","definition":"Apigee deployment in which the API runtime runs in your own Kubernetes environment and Google manages the control plane.","exam_context":"Still API management. Not the answer for running containers across clouds (that's GKE Enterprise).","lesson":"d4l7","domain":4},{"term":"App Engine","definition":"Google's original serverless PaaS for web applications, with automatic scaling, versions and traffic splitting. It has a standard environment (supported runtimes) and a flexible environment (containers on Compute Engine VMs).","exam_context":"Web app that autoscales while Google runs the infrastructure. Its versions let you serve different versions to different users. For one container image, prefer Cloud Run.","lesson":"d4l4","domain":4},{"term":"App Engine flexible environment","definition":"App Engine environment that runs your application in containers on Compute Engine VMs.","exam_context":"Hosting, not API management. For a single container with the simplest serverless experience, Cloud Run fits better.","lesson":"d4l4","domain":4},{"term":"Artifact Registry","definition":"Service that stores container images and language packages. It replaced Container Registry.","exam_context":"Distractor for \"where should the code run\". It stores images; it doesn't run them.","lesson":"d4l4","domain":4},{"term":"Bare Metal Solution","definition":"Dedicated physical servers in Google-managed facilities, connected to Google Cloud services over low-latency networking. A variant exists for SAP.","exam_context":"Workloads with strict third-party licensing, low-level server access or architectures that can't be virtualized, such as legacy Oracle databases, run as-is.","lesson":"d4l6","domain":4},{"term":"Brownfield","definition":"Building a new cloud-native application to replace an existing legacy system.","exam_context":"\"New cloud-native app that will eventually replace the on-premises system\" = invent in brownfield.","lesson":"d4l5","domain":4},{"term":"Cloud Run","definition":"Serverless platform that runs stateless container images (as services, jobs or functions), scales automatically and scales to zero.","exam_context":"One container, no clusters or servers to manage, scale to zero, any language. Prefer it over App Engine when the stem says container image.","lesson":"d4l4","domain":4},{"term":"Cloud Run functions","definition":"Event-driven serverless functions (formerly Cloud Functions) that run small, single-purpose code in response to HTTP requests, Pub/Sub messages or events delivered by Eventarc.","exam_context":"Code that runs whenever a file is uploaded, a form is submitted or a threshold is crossed. Not for long batch jobs.","lesson":"d4l4","domain":4},{"term":"Cloud-native application","definition":"An application designed for the cloud from the start, typically using microservices, containers and managed services.","exam_context":"Chosen because developers can release new features in an agile way.","lesson":"d4l1","domain":4},{"term":"Compute Engine","definition":"Google Cloud's infrastructure-as-a-service virtual machines, with managed instance groups for autoscaling and rolling updates.","exam_context":"Pick it when the workload needs detailed control over OS, networking and machine types. It's also the rehost target.","lesson":"d4l2","domain":4},{"term":"Config Sync","definition":"GKE Enterprise feature (successor to Anthos Config Management) that applies Kubernetes configuration and policies from a central source such as Git to registered clusters.","exam_context":"Keeps cluster configuration consistent. Distractor for sharing content with partners.","lesson":"d4l7","domain":4},{"term":"Containerization","definition":"Packaging application code together with its libraries and dependencies into a portable container.","exam_context":"Fixes code that works in development but breaks in production because of different package versions.","lesson":"d4l3","domain":4},{"term":"Database Migration Service","definition":"Managed service that migrates databases such as SQL Server, MySQL and PostgreSQL into Cloud SQL.","exam_context":"Move quickly to a managed database while keeping the same engine. Firestore and BigQuery are distractors.","lesson":"d4l6","domain":4},{"term":"Edge computing","definition":"Running workloads close to users, devices or data sources to reduce latency.","exam_context":"Distractor when the stem is about handing patching and server setup to Google (that's managed services).","lesson":"d4l1","domain":4},{"term":"Eventarc","definition":"Service that routes events from Google Cloud sources, such as Cloud Storage uploads, to targets like Cloud Run functions.","exam_context":"How a function gets triggered when a file lands in a Cloud Storage bucket.","lesson":"d4l4","domain":4},{"term":"GKE Autopilot","definition":"GKE mode in which Google manages nodes, scaling, security settings and cluster operations, and you pay per Pod resource request.","exam_context":"Kubernetes with less time spent configuring nodes and running the cluster.","lesson":"d4l3","domain":4},{"term":"GKE Enterprise","definition":"Google's platform (formerly Anthos) for managing Kubernetes clusters consistently across Google Cloud, on-premises and other clouds.","exam_context":"Centralized management and consistent security for containers across hybrid and multicloud environments.","lesson":"d4l7","domain":4},{"term":"Google Cloud VMware Engine","definition":"A managed VMware environment (vSphere, vCenter, vSAN, NSX, HCX) running natively on Google Cloud.","exam_context":"Move a VMware estate while admins keep familiar VMware tools and procedures.","lesson":"d4l6","domain":4},{"term":"Google Distributed Cloud","definition":"Google Cloud infrastructure and services extended to customer data centers and edge locations, including air-gapped options.","exam_context":"How GKE Enterprise reaches on-premises environments.","lesson":"d4l7","domain":4},{"term":"Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE)","definition":"Google's managed Kubernetes service for deploying, scaling and orchestrating containers.","exam_context":"Orchestrate many (even thousands of) containers. Exam phrasing: enables rapid deployment and testing.","lesson":"d4l3","domain":4},{"term":"Greenfield","definition":"Building a new application from scratch with no existing system to migrate.","exam_context":"\"No existing application to migrate or modify\" = invent in greenfield.","lesson":"d4l5","domain":4},{"term":"Hybrid cloud","definition":"A mix of on-premises or private infrastructure and public cloud.","exam_context":"Data must stay in your own data center while you use Google Cloud services such as BigQuery or ML.","lesson":"d4l7","domain":4},{"term":"Hypervisor","definition":"Software that creates and runs virtual machines, letting several operating systems share one physical machine.","exam_context":"\"Run Linux and Windows separately on the same physical server.\"","lesson":"d4l2","domain":4},{"term":"Kubernetes","definition":"Open-source container orchestration system, originally from Google, that deploys, scales and manages containers across clusters.","exam_context":"\"Orchestrate many containers\" = Kubernetes = GKE. Also the best-known example of open source.","lesson":"d4l3","domain":4},{"term":"Managed instance group (MIG)","definition":"A group of identical Compute Engine VMs managed as one, with autoscaling, autohealing and automatic rolling updates.","exam_context":"Explains how Compute Engine keeps full VM control and still autoscales and rolls out updates.","lesson":"d4l2","domain":4},{"term":"Managed services","definition":"Cloud services where the provider handles infrastructure administration such as patching, upgrades and server setup.","exam_context":"Frees IT staff from patching and database setup. Patching and upgrades are the task handled automatically.","lesson":"d4l1","domain":4},{"term":"Microservices","definition":"An architecture in which an application is split into small, independent services, each owning one business function.","exam_context":"Services built, deployed and scaled separately. Refactoring into microservices makes apps more efficient and scalable.","lesson":"d4l3","domain":4},{"term":"Monolithic architecture","definition":"An application built and deployed as one tightly coupled unit, with UI, business logic and data access together.","exam_context":"The thing teams move away from when they adopt microservices. Changing or scaling one part means handling the whole app.","lesson":"d4l3","domain":4},{"term":"Multicloud","definition":"Using more than one public cloud provider.","exam_context":"Kubernetes workloads across three public clouds = multicloud, even when GKE Enterprise manages them.","lesson":"d4l7","domain":4},{"term":"Network Service Tiers","definition":"Google Cloud's choice of network quality: Premium Tier uses Google's global network, Standard Tier uses the public internet for more of the path.","exam_context":"\"Premium tier virtual machines\" is a distractor. Premium Tier is a networking option, not a discounted VM.","lesson":"d4l2","domain":4},{"term":"Open source","definition":"Software whose source code is publicly available to study, change and contribute to.","exam_context":"Answer when the stem says code is open for public review and community contribution.","lesson":"d4l7","domain":4},{"term":"Open standards","definition":"Publicly available specifications, such as open APIs and data formats, that let technologies interoperate across vendors.","exam_context":"Reduces the fear that choosing one cloud provider makes future migration hard. SLAs, scalability and database services don't.","lesson":"d4l7","domain":4},{"term":"Proprietary software","definition":"Software whose source code the vendor owns and controls, usually not open for inspection or modification.","exam_context":"Opposite of open source. Distractor when the stem says developers can study, change and contribute code.","lesson":"d4l7","domain":4},{"term":"Pub/Sub","definition":"Asynchronous messaging service that lets independent services send and receive messages.","exam_context":"Decouples services and can trigger functions, but doesn't host applications.","lesson":"d4l4","domain":4},{"term":"Refactor","definition":"Migration that changes the application code, often splitting a monolith into microservices.","exam_context":"Benefit: more efficient and scalable. Not the fastest path, and code changes are required.","lesson":"d4l5","domain":4},{"term":"Rehost (lift and shift)","definition":"Moving an application to the cloud as-is, with no code or architecture changes.","exam_context":"Same OS images, settings and configs moved to Compute Engine. Fastest migration.","lesson":"d4l5","domain":4},{"term":"Reimagine","definition":"Migration approach that rethinks how the business process or application should work and rebuilds it with cloud-native capabilities.","exam_context":"The most change of all. Wrong whenever the stem says no code or architecture changes.","lesson":"d4l5","domain":4},{"term":"Replatform (move and improve)","definition":"Migration that keeps the app logic mostly unchanged but swaps components for managed services.","exam_context":"Replace a self-managed database with Cloud SQL while the application stays the same.","lesson":"d4l5","domain":4},{"term":"Retain","definition":"Keeping a workload in its current environment for now instead of migrating it.","exam_context":"Chosen when a workload isn't ready, has complex dependencies or must stay put. Wrong when the app is actively moving.","lesson":"d4l5","domain":4},{"term":"Retire","definition":"Decommissioning a workload that is no longer needed.","exam_context":"Distractor when the business still needs the application.","lesson":"d4l5","domain":4},{"term":"Serverless computing","definition":"A model in which the cloud provider manages servers, scaling and runtime, and you supply only code.","exam_context":"Main benefit is scalability, from few to many users and down to zero, so there's no idle cost.","lesson":"d4l4","domain":4},{"term":"Spot VMs","definition":"Deeply discounted Compute Engine VMs that use spare capacity and can be stopped when Google needs it back. Successor to Preemptible VMs.","exam_context":"Cheapest option for batch jobs that checkpoint, can restart and aren't urgent.","lesson":"d4l2","domain":4},{"term":"Vendor lock-in","definition":"Dependence on one provider's proprietary services, APIs or data formats that makes moving elsewhere hard.","exam_context":"Answer the worry with openness, interoperability, open standards and open source. Proprietary databases and SaaS-only strategies make it worse.","lesson":"d4l7","domain":4},{"term":"Virtual machine","definition":"An emulated computer with its own operating system, running on a hypervisor.","exam_context":"Each app needs its own dedicated OS = VMs, not containers.","lesson":"d4l2","domain":4}]},{"n":5,"title":"Trust and Security with Google Cloud","short":"Trust & Security","weight":"~17%","desc":"Shared responsibility, zero trust, IAM, encryption, threats, compliance, data sovereignty and residency.","lessons":[{"id":"d5l1","num":1,"title":"Core security concepts: CIA, posture, resilience","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The CIA triad</strong> is the base vocabulary of this domain:</li>\n<li><strong>Confidentiality</strong>: only approved users can access the data.</li>\n<li><strong>Integrity</strong>: data stays correct, complete and dependable.</li>\n<li><strong>Availability</strong>: data stays <strong>dependable and accessible when needed</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q27\">Exam 1 · Q27</a>.</li>\n<li>Typical controls: confidentiality comes from IAM, authentication, least privilege and encryption. Integrity means data isn't improperly changed, corrupted or deleted. Availability is supported by reducing downtime and planning for business continuity. Accurate data that nobody can reach is <strong>not</strong> available <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q27\">Exam 1 · Q27</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Compliance</strong> is a separate idea: meeting regulatory and industry standards. The exam uses it as a distractor for CIA questions <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q27\">Exam 1 · Q27</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Security posture</strong> is the <strong>complete view</strong> of an organization's cybersecurity readiness: network design, identity and access controls, monitoring coverage, and how consistently policies are applied <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q8\">Exam 3 · Q8</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Cyber resilience</strong> is the ability to <strong>reduce the chance</strong> of attacks, <strong>keep essential services running</strong> during an incident, <strong>limit business impact</strong> and <strong>recover quickly</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q7\">Exam 5 · Q7</a>. In short: anticipate, withstand, recover and adapt. It goes beyond prevention because it <strong>assumes some attacks will still succeed</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q7\">Exam 5 · Q7</a>.</li>\n<li>Security posture is something you can measure and manage. In Google Cloud, <strong>Security Command Center</strong> is where posture is defined, assessed and monitored <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q8\">Exam 3 · Q8</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Security is a continuous program, not a product.</strong> A new security platform doesn't stop leaks on its own. Long-term improvement comes from <strong>skilled people and an evolving security plan</strong> (<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q4\">Exam 5 · Q4</a>, see ⚠️). A good plan mixes <strong>people, process and technology</strong>: find the root cause, layer controls (<strong>defense in depth</strong>), monitor, train staff and keep improving <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q4\">Exam 5 · Q4</a>.</li>\n<li><em>(not in the question bank)</em> <strong>Threat</strong> = a potential danger. <strong>Vulnerability</strong> = a weakness that a threat can exploit. <strong>Risk</strong> = the likelihood and impact of a threat exploiting a vulnerability.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>CIA triad compared</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Term</th>\n<th>Plain meaning</th>\n<th>Typical exam wording</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Confidentiality</td>\n<td>Only approved people see it</td>\n<td>\"limiting data access only to approved users\"</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q27\">Exam 1 · Q27</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Integrity</td>\n<td>It stays correct</td>\n<td>\"correct, complete, and dependable\"</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q27\">Exam 1 · Q27</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Availability</td>\n<td>It's there when needed</td>\n<td>\"accessible when needed\"</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q27\">Exam 1 · Q27</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Compliance (not CIA)</td>\n<td>Meets rules and standards</td>\n<td>\"regulatory and industry standards\"</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q27\">Exam 1 · Q27</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Complete view of readiness\", \"overall state of security\" → <strong>security posture</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q8\">Exam 3 · Q8</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Prevent, keep running, limit impact, recover\" → <strong>cyber resilience</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q7\">Exam 5 · Q7</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Long-term\", \"improve the security approach\" → invest in <strong>expertise and process</strong>, not the most expensive tool <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q4\">Exam 5 · Q4</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Threat response</strong> is only one part of posture. It is reacting to incidents, not the whole picture <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q8\">Exam 3 · Q8</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Data masking</strong> hides values. It is not resilience <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q7\">Exam 5 · Q7</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Longer data retention</strong> is not a security fix. Keeping data longer means more data can leak. Retention should follow business, legal and privacy needs <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q4\">Exam 5 · Q4</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>IaaS</strong> and <strong>vendor lock-in</strong> are not resilience either. IaaS is a service model and lock-in is a portability concern <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q7\">Exam 5 · Q7</a>.</li>\n<li>Waiting for a \"newer platform\" or buying \"the most expensive platform\" is never the answer <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q4\">Exam 5 · Q4</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"The base vocabulary of cloud security: the CIA triad (confidentiality, integrity, availability), security posture and cyber resilience. These terms appear as answer options throughout the domain, so knowing each one precisely prevents mix-ups.","objectives":["Define confidentiality, integrity and availability and tell them apart from compliance","Explain security posture as the full view of an organization's security readiness","Describe cyber resilience as prevent, continue, limit impact and recover","Recognize that long-term security depends on expertise and process, not a single tool"],"takeaways":["Availability means data is dependable and accessible when needed.","Compliance (meeting standards) is not part of the CIA triad.","Security posture is the complete picture: network, identity, monitoring and consistent policies.","Cyber resilience covers prevention, continuity, limiting impact and fast recovery.","Buying the most expensive platform is never the long-term answer. Invest in people and an evolving plan.","Cyber resilience assumes some attacks will succeed, so it plans to withstand and recover, not only prevent.","Keeping data longer is not a security fix. More retained data means more can leak."],"questions":["e1q27","e3q8","e5q4","e5q7"]},{"id":"d5l2","num":2,"title":"Cybersecurity threats","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Phishing</strong>: a fake message that looks trusted (support team, salary portal) and pushes urgency (\"account disabled in 12 hours\") to steal <strong>credentials or bank details</strong>, often via a <strong>look-alike login page</strong>. It is a <strong>social engineering</strong> attack <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q31\">Exam 1 · Q31</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q28\">Exam 2 · Q28</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q41\">Exam 2 · Q41</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Malware</strong>: malicious software that <strong>secretly steals, alters or damages</strong> information without the user noticing <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q46\">Exam 2 · Q46</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Ransomware</strong>: a type of malware that <strong>encrypts your data and demands payment</strong>, usually in cryptocurrency, for the decryption key <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q17\">Exam 5 · Q17</a>. Some ransomware also <strong>threatens to publish stolen data</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q50\">Exam 5 · Q50</a>.</li>\n<li>Attacks chain together. <strong>Phishing</strong> is often the <strong>entry point</strong> that later leads to cryptomining or ransomware. The exam asks you to name what the scenario <strong>describes</strong>, not how the attacker first got in <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q25\">Exam 1 · Q25</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q17\">Exam 5 · Q17</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Cryptomining</strong> (cryptojacking): attackers use a compromised account to create <strong>unauthorized VMs</strong> that burn CPU on <strong>hashing to earn cryptocurrency</strong>. The first sign is often an <strong>unexpected bill</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q25\">Exam 1 · Q25</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Misconfiguration</strong>: a human error such as making a bucket <strong>public</strong>. No software flaw is exploited. It is one of the most common causes of cloud data exposure <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q26\">Exam 1 · Q26</a>. Reduce it with tight IAM permissions, Cloud Storage <strong>public access prevention</strong> and <strong>uniform bucket-level access</strong>, and regular posture checks in Security Command Center <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q26\">Exam 1 · Q26</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Advanced Persistent Threat (APT)</strong>: a skilled, well-funded attacker who gets in and <strong>stays hidden for a long time</strong> to spy or steal. <strong>Zero-day vulnerability</strong>: a software flaw that is <strong>unknown to the vendor</strong> and has no fix yet. Both are distractors when the stem says the cause was a simple mistake <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q26\">Exam 1 · Q26</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Unsecured third-party systems</strong>: attackers enter through a <strong>vendor's plugin or component</strong> connected to your delivery process (a supply chain attack). Your own controls can be strong and you are still exposed <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q20\">Exam 3 · Q20</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>LLM attack (prompt injection)</strong>: crafted prompts make an AI assistant <strong>ignore its instructions</strong>, leak restricted data or bypass response controls <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q50\">Exam 5 · Q50</a>. Other LLM risks include data leakage through model answers and unsafe tool use by AI agents <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q20\">Exam 3 · Q20</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>DDoS</strong>: a flood of traffic, often from a botnet, meant to <strong>exhaust resources</strong> so real users can't reach the service <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q10\">Exam 3 · Q10</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q2\">Exam 4 · Q2</a>. See Lesson 5 for the defense.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Threats compared</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Clue in the question</th>\n<th>Threat</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Fake email or portal asking for a password or bank details</td>\n<td>Phishing</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q31\">Exam 1 · Q31</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q28\">Exam 2 · Q28</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q41\">Exam 2 · Q41</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Software that secretly steals, alters or damages data</td>\n<td>Malware</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q46\">Exam 2 · Q46</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Files encrypted, payment demanded for the key</td>\n<td>Ransomware</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q17\">Exam 5 · Q17</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Unauthorized VMs, high CPU, rising bill, crypto rewards</td>\n<td>Cryptomining</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q25\">Exam 1 · Q25</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bucket accidentally public, no exploit used</td>\n<td>Misconfiguration</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q26\">Exam 1 · Q26</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Entry through a vendor plugin or component</td>\n<td>Unsecured third-party systems</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q20\">Exam 3 · Q20</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Prompts that override an AI assistant's instructions</td>\n<td>LLM attack (prompt injection)</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q50\">Exam 5 · Q50</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Huge volume of fake requests to take a site down</td>\n<td>DDoS</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q10\">Exam 3 · Q10</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q2\">Exam 4 · Q2</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Something <strong>asks the user</strong> for credentials or a click → <strong>phishing</strong>. Something <strong>demands payment</strong> to unlock data → <strong>ransomware</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Billing spike + unknown VMs</strong> → <strong>cryptomining</strong>, not DDoS <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q25\">Exam 1 · Q25</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Without exploiting any software flaw\" → <strong>misconfiguration</strong>, not zero-day or APT <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q26\">Exam 1 · Q26</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Spamming</strong> is unwanted bulk mail. It doesn't try to steal credentials <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q28\">Exam 2 · Q28</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Ransomware</strong> is a kind of malware, but it is loud (it announces itself). \"Without the user noticing\" points to generic <strong>malware</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q46\">Exam 2 · Q46</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Zero trust architecture</strong> is a defense model, not a threat vector <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q20\">Exam 3 · Q20</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>SQL injection</strong> is a code attack, not social engineering <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q41\">Exam 2 · Q41</a>. Social engineering exploits <strong>human trust, fear, urgency or curiosity</strong>, not a technical flaw <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q41\">Exam 2 · Q41</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Physical damage</strong> and <strong>hardware failure</strong> are not cyberattacks. Without a ransom note or an attacker, they are operational risks <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q20\">Exam 3 · Q20</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q17\">Exam 5 · Q17</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>SLA violations</strong> and <strong>incomplete data input</strong> are service and data quality issues, not threats <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q46\">Exam 2 · Q46</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"The exam describes a scenario and asks you to name the threat. This lesson covers phishing, malware, ransomware, cryptomining, misconfiguration, third-party (supply chain) risk, LLM prompt injection and DDoS, with the clue words that identify each one.","objectives":["Identify phishing, malware and ransomware from scenario clues","Recognize cryptomining from unauthorized VMs and unexpected bills","Distinguish misconfiguration and third-party risk from software exploits","Describe prompt injection as an attack on LLM-based applications","Tell misconfiguration apart from an APT or a zero-day vulnerability"],"takeaways":["Fake message asking for a password or bank details is phishing, a social engineering attack.","Encrypted files plus a payment demand is ransomware. Secret theft or damage is generic malware.","Unauthorized VMs and a rising bill point to cryptomining.","A public bucket opened by mistake is misconfiguration, not a zero-day.","Entry through a vendor plugin is an unsecured third-party system.","Prompts that make an AI assistant ignore its instructions are an LLM attack (prompt injection).","An APT hides in a network for a long time. A zero-day is an unknown, unpatched flaw. Neither fits a simple settings mistake.","Phishing is often the entry point for other attacks. Name the threat the scenario describes, not how the attacker got in."],"questions":["e1q25","e1q26","e1q31","e2q28","e2q41","e2q46","e3q10","e3q20","e5q17","e5q50"]},{"id":"d5l3","num":3,"title":"The shared responsibility model","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>In the cloud, <strong>security is shared</strong>. Google is responsible for <strong>security <em>of</em> the cloud</strong>. The customer is responsible for <strong>security <em>in</em> the cloud</strong>: what they put on it and how they configure it <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q20\">Exam 4 · Q20</a>.</li>\n<li>Google calls its version <strong>shared responsibility and shared fate</strong>: Google also gives customers secure defaults, tools and guidance to help them do their part <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q47\">Exam 4 · Q47</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Google always owns physical security</strong>: data center buildings, hardware and facilities. This holds for every service model, including IaaS <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q49\">Exam 1 · Q49</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q20\">Exam 4 · Q20</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q42\">Exam 6 · Q42</a>.</li>\n<li>In <strong>IaaS (Compute Engine)</strong> Google manages the physical infrastructure, hardware, network foundation and the <strong>virtualization layer</strong>. The customer keeps everything inside the VM: guest <strong>OS patching and hardening</strong>, installed software, application code, encryption of data inside the app, and application-level user access <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q49\">Exam 1 · Q49</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q20\">Exam 4 · Q20</a>.</li>\n<li>Customers <strong>can't install their own physical security hardware</strong> in Google's data centers. Their security work is configuration: IAM, encryption settings, firewall rules, logging <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q47\">Exam 4 · Q47</a>.</li>\n<li>Testing the <strong>availability of the customer's own applications</strong> is the customer's job, not Google's <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q18\">Exam 4 · Q18</a>.</li>\n<li>The <strong>customer defines data access policies</strong>: who can see which datasets and reports. That's the customer's IT team, not Google support and not end users <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q19\">Exam 5 · Q19</a>.</li>\n<li>The more managed the service (IaaS → PaaS → SaaS), the more Google takes on. <strong>Data and access</strong> always stay with the customer.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Who does what (IaaS)</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Responsibility</th>\n<th>Google</th>\n<th>Customer</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Physical data center security</td>\n<td>✅</td>\n<td>❌</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q49\">Exam 1 · Q49</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q20\">Exam 4 · Q20</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q42\">Exam 6 · Q42</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hardware and network infrastructure</td>\n<td>✅</td>\n<td>❌</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q42\">Exam 6 · Q42</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Guest OS patching and hardening</td>\n<td>❌</td>\n<td>✅</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q49\">Exam 1 · Q49</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q20\">Exam 4 · Q20</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Application code security</td>\n<td>❌</td>\n<td>✅</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q20\">Exam 4 · Q20</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Application-level user access / data access policies</td>\n<td>❌</td>\n<td>✅</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q49\">Exam 1 · Q49</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q19\">Exam 5 · Q19</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Encrypting sensitive data inside the app</td>\n<td>❌</td>\n<td>✅</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q49\">Exam 1 · Q49</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Physical, buildings, hardware, facilities → <strong>Google Cloud alone</strong>, not \"shared\" <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q42\">Exam 6 · Q42</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Who decides which employees access what\" → <strong>the customer's IT team</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q19\">Exam 5 · Q19</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"<strong>Customer and Google</strong>\" sounds balanced, but physical security is Google's alone <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q42\">Exam 6 · Q42</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Cloud Identity</strong> is a tool the customer uses. It manages users and groups and supplies the identities that IAM policies refer to. It doesn't decide policy <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q19\">Exam 5 · Q19</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Google Cloud Customer Care</strong> and <strong>external security providers</strong> don't decide your access rules or secure Google's data centers <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q19\">Exam 5 · Q19</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q42\">Exam 6 · Q42</a>.</li>\n<li>Assuming security is \"fully Google's responsibility\" is always wrong <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q25\">Exam 6 · Q25</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"Security in the cloud is shared. Google secures the cloud itself (physical infrastructure, hardware, network and the virtualization layer), while the customer secures what it runs in the cloud: operating systems (in IaaS), code, data and access policies. Google calls this shared responsibility and shared fate, and the split shifts toward Google as services become more managed.","objectives":["State which responsibilities Google always holds","List the customer's responsibilities when using IaaS such as Compute Engine","Identify who defines data access policies"],"takeaways":["Physical data center security belongs to Google alone, in every service model.","In IaaS the customer patches and hardens the guest OS and secures its code.","The customer's IT team defines who can access which data.","Assuming security is fully Google's job is always wrong.","Google handles security of the cloud. The customer handles security in the cloud.","Customers can't install their own physical hardware in Google's data centers. Their part is configuration."],"questions":["e1q49","e4q18","e4q20","e4q47","e5q19","e6q25","e6q42"]},{"id":"d5l4","num":4,"title":"Identity and access management","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>IAM (Identity and Access Management)</strong> defines <strong>who</strong> can do <strong>what</strong> on <strong>which resource</strong>. It's the answer when someone must view BigQuery datasets but not delete or create them <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q12\">Exam 1 · Q12</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Authentication</strong> = proving <strong>who you are</strong> (sign-in). <strong>Authorization</strong> = deciding <strong>what you may do</strong> after that. Authentication always comes <strong>first</strong>, then IAM checks permissions <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q24\">Exam 5 · Q24</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Auditing</strong> records who did what, when and from where, using <strong>audit logs</strong>. It supports investigations and compliance but doesn't verify identity <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q24\">Exam 5 · Q24</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>2-Step Verification (2SV) / multi-factor authentication (MFA)</strong>: a password <strong>plus</strong> a second factor such as a phone prompt, a code (SMS or authenticator app) or a <strong>hardware security key</strong>. A stolen password alone is no longer enough <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q47\">Exam 3 · Q47</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Least privilege</strong>: each person gets <strong>only the permissions their job needs</strong>, nothing more. If an account is compromised, the damage is limited to what it can do. In IAM you apply it with <strong>predefined or custom roles</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q22\">Exam 6 · Q22</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q12\">Exam 1 · Q12</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Map roles to job functions.</strong> When several departments share a project, define roles per function or team instead of per person. It's faster and easier to manage. In practice you put users in <strong>groups</strong> by department and grant roles to the groups, at the project, folder or resource level <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q7\">Exam 3 · Q7</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Service accounts</strong> are identities for <strong>applications and automated workloads</strong>, not shared logins for people <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q7\">Exam 3 · Q7</a>.</li>\n<li>Roles can be granted on a single resource. For example, a <strong>BigQuery viewer role</strong> lets an analyst read datasets without creating or deleting them <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q12\">Exam 1 · Q12</a>.</li>\n<li><em>(not in the question bank)</em> <strong>Cloud Identity</strong> manages users and groups and supports single sign-on (SSO). Best practice is to <strong>grant roles to groups</strong>, not to individuals.</li>\n<li>IAM role types: <strong>basic</strong> (Owner, Editor, Viewer: broad, across many services), <strong>predefined</strong> (per service, e.g. BigQuery Data Viewer) and <strong>custom</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q7\">Exam 3 · Q7</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q49\">Exam 6 · Q49</a>. <em>(not in the question bank)</em> Policies are <strong>inherited</strong> down the resource hierarchy (organization → folders → projects → resources).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Identity terms compared</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Term</th>\n<th>Question it answers</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Authentication</td>\n<td>\"Are you really who you claim to be?\"</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q24\">Exam 5 · Q24</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Authorization</td>\n<td>\"What are you allowed to do?\"</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q24\">Exam 5 · Q24</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Auditing</td>\n<td>\"What did you do?\" (logs)</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q24\">Exam 5 · Q24</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2SV / MFA</td>\n<td>\"Prove it a second way\"</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q47\">Exam 3 · Q47</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Least privilege</td>\n<td>\"Only what the job needs\"</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q22\">Exam 6 · Q22</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Who can view vs. delete/create\" → <strong>IAM</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q12\">Exam 1 · Q12</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Confirm the person is really the claimed user\" → <strong>authentication</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q24\">Exam 5 · Q24</a>.</li>\n<li>Password + phone/code/key → <strong>2SV/MFA</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q47\">Exam 3 · Q47</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Only the permissions needed for the role\" → <strong>least privilege</strong>, not zero trust <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q22\">Exam 6 · Q22</a>.</li>\n<li>Several teams, one project, fastest way → <strong>roles mapped to job functions</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q7\">Exam 3 · Q7</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Basic roles</strong> (Owner/Editor/Viewer) for every employee are too broad <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q7\">Exam 3 · Q7</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q49\">Exam 6 · Q49</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>One shared service account</strong> for all departments breaks accountability and least privilege <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q7\">Exam 3 · Q7</a>.</li>\n<li>Setting least-privilege roles <strong>separately for each employee</strong> is correct in spirit but slow. The exam wants the scalable option <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q7\">Exam 3 · Q7</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>VPC</strong> controls network traffic, not who can delete a dataset <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q12\">Exam 1 · Q12</a>. <strong>Cloud Storage</strong> has bucket-level access controls, but it isn't the service for BigQuery permissions <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q12\">Exam 1 · Q12</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Security by default</strong> means products ship with protections already on. It is not about how many permissions a user gets <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q22\">Exam 6 · Q22</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Privileged access</strong> is about elevated admin permissions, not about filtering network traffic <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q27\">Exam 3 · Q27</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Zero trust</strong> and <strong>least privilege</strong> are related. Zero trust is the overall model (\"verify every request\"). Least privilege is about how much access is granted <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q22\">Exam 6 · Q22</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"IAM controls who can do what on which resource. This lesson covers authentication versus authorization, 2-Step Verification, least privilege and mapping roles to job functions so access stays manageable.","objectives":["Use IAM to grant view-only access without delete or create rights","Distinguish authentication from authorization","Describe 2SV/MFA and the factors it uses","Apply least privilege and role-per-job-function design","Explain why service accounts are for workloads, not shared human logins"],"takeaways":["Who can view vs. delete a dataset is an IAM question.","Authentication proves who you are. Authorization decides what you can do.","Password plus phone prompt, code or security key is 2SV/MFA.","Least privilege means only the permissions the job needs.","Map roles to job functions (groups), not to each person, and avoid basic roles and shared service accounts.","Authentication happens first, then IAM authorizes. Auditing records what was done.","Grant roles to groups by job function. Service accounts are for applications, not people."],"questions":["e1q12","e3q7","e3q27","e3q47","e5q24","e6q22","e6q49"]},{"id":"d5l5","num":5,"title":"Network and application protection","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Firewall rules</strong> define which <strong>incoming (ingress) and outgoing (egress) traffic</strong> is allowed or denied to your resources in a VPC network. Rules match on things like direction, priority, protocol, ports, IP ranges, network tags and service accounts <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q27\">Exam 3 · Q27</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Google Cloud Armor</strong> protects internet-facing apps from <strong>DDoS</strong> and web attacks. It works at Google's edge with the external load balancer, so malicious traffic is filtered before it reaches the back ends <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q10\">Exam 3 · Q10</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q2\">Exam 4 · Q2</a>. It covers both <strong>volumetric floods</strong> and <strong>Layer 7 HTTP floods</strong>, and its <strong>web application firewall (WAF)</strong> rules block attacks such as <strong>SQL injection</strong> and <strong>cross-site scripting (XSS)</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q10\">Exam 3 · Q10</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q35\">Exam 2 · Q35</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Cloud VPN</strong> connects networks through <strong>encrypted IPsec tunnels</strong>. It protects data in transit between your office and Google Cloud. It doesn't block attack traffic to a public site <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q2\">Exam 4 · Q2</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Model Armor</strong> inspects <strong>AI prompts and responses</strong>. It reduces <strong>prompt injection and jailbreak</strong> risk, catches <strong>sensitive data exposure</strong> and blocks harmful output before it reaches users <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q33\">Exam 1 · Q33</a>. It is the defense against the LLM attacks in Lesson 2 <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q50\">Exam 5 · Q50</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Zero trust</strong> replaces \"trust the corporate network\" with <strong>verify every identity, device and request, from any location</strong>. Access decisions use identity, <strong>device posture</strong> and context, and are checked continuously <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q6\">Exam 5 · Q6</a>. Least privilege is usually part of a zero-trust strategy <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q22\">Exam 6 · Q22</a>. When an organization moves from project-by-project cloud use to broad transformation, the recommended shift is to a <strong>zero-trust model that verifies users, devices and applications</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q49\">Exam 6 · Q49</a>.</li>\n<li><em>(not in the question bank)</em> Google's own zero-trust implementation is <strong>BeyondCorp</strong>. Customers get it through <strong>Identity-Aware Proxy (IAP)</strong> and <strong>Chrome Enterprise Premium</strong> (formerly BeyondCorp Enterprise): access to apps based on identity and context, without a VPN.</li>\n<li><em>(not in the question bank)</em> <strong>VPC Service Controls</strong> draw a perimeter around managed services (BigQuery, Cloud Storage) to reduce data exfiltration.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Which protection?</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Need</th>\n<th>Use</th>\n<th>Not</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Allow/deny traffic in and out</td>\n<td>Firewall rules</td>\n<td>2SV, encryption keys</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q27\">Exam 3 · Q27</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Stop a DDoS / botnet flood on a website</td>\n<td>Google Cloud Armor</td>\n<td>SCC, IAP, KMS, Cloud VPN</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q10\">Exam 3 · Q10</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q2\">Exam 4 · Q2</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Filter AI prompts and responses</td>\n<td>Model Armor</td>\n<td>Cloud Run functions, Autoclass</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q33\">Exam 1 · Q33</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Verify every request regardless of location</td>\n<td>Zero trust architecture</td>\n<td>VPN, perimeter security</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q6\">Exam 5 · Q6</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q49\">Exam 6 · Q49</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>DDoS on a public site → <strong>Cloud Armor</strong>, every time <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q10\">Exam 3 · Q10</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q2\">Exam 4 · Q2</a>.</li>\n<li>Generative AI safety (prompt injection, jailbreak, harmful output) → <strong>Model Armor</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q33\">Exam 1 · Q33</a>.</li>\n<li>\"No longer trust the office network\" → <strong>zero trust</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q6\">Exam 5 · Q6</a>.</li>\n<li>SQL injection or cross-site scripting against a public web app → <strong>Cloud Armor WAF rules</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q10\">Exam 3 · Q10</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q35\">Exam 2 · Q35</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Security Command Center</strong> finds risks and misconfigurations. It doesn't absorb DDoS traffic <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q10\">Exam 3 · Q10</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q2\">Exam 4 · Q2</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>IAP</strong> controls who can reach an app based on identity. It's not a DDoS shield <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q10\">Exam 3 · Q10</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Cloud KMS</strong> manages encryption keys. It doesn't inspect or block traffic <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q10\">Exam 3 · Q10</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Cloud Run functions</strong> could call a security service, but they don't screen AI prompts themselves. Model Armor is the purpose-built control <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q33\">Exam 1 · Q33</a>.</li>\n<li>A <strong>VPN</strong> encrypts a network path but still trusts whoever is inside. It is the opposite of zero trust <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q6\">Exam 5 · Q6</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Strong perimeter and trust everything inside\" is the old castle-and-moat model <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q49\">Exam 6 · Q49</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"Protecting traffic and applications: firewall rules allow or deny traffic, Google Cloud Armor defends against DDoS, and Model Armor screens generative AI prompts and responses. Zero trust replaces trust in the network perimeter with verification of every request.","objectives":["Choose firewall rules to allow or deny network traffic","Choose Google Cloud Armor for DDoS protection on public apps","Choose Model Armor to reduce prompt injection and harmful AI output","Explain zero trust and how it differs from perimeter security and VPNs","Recognize Cloud Armor's WAF role against SQL injection and cross-site scripting"],"takeaways":["DDoS on a public website means Google Cloud Armor.","Security Command Center finds risks but doesn't stop DDoS traffic.","Model Armor inspects AI prompts and responses.","Zero trust verifies every identity, device and request from any location.","Perimeter security that trusts the internal network is the outdated model.","Cloud Armor handles volumetric and HTTP floods plus WAF attacks like SQL injection and XSS.","Cloud VPN encrypts traffic between networks. It doesn't stop DDoS."],"questions":["e1q33","e2q35","e3q10","e3q27","e5q6","e5q50","e6q22","e6q49"]},{"id":"d5l6","num":6,"title":"Encryption, backup and incident readiness","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Google Cloud <strong>encrypts customer data by default, at rest and in transit</strong>. No team has to switch it on per service <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q23\">Exam 5 · Q23</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q30\">Exam 5 · Q30</a>. Data at rest is encrypted <strong>before it is written to disk</strong>, using <strong>Google-managed keys</strong> by default <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q3\">Exam 4 · Q3</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q23\">Exam 5 · Q23</a>.</li>\n<li>Default encryption doesn't depend on <strong>tags or labels</strong>. Those help with organization, policy conditions and cost tracking <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q30\">Exam 5 · Q30</a>.</li>\n<li>Customers who want more control over keys can use <strong>Cloud KMS</strong> with <strong>customer-managed encryption keys (CMEK)</strong>, or <strong>customer-supplied encryption keys (CSEK)</strong> in supported services. They can control key location, rotation, permissions and lifecycle <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q18\">Exam 4 · Q18</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q23\">Exam 5 · Q23</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q30\">Exam 5 · Q30</a>.</li>\n<li>If someone physically got into a data center, the stored data is still protected because it's stored with <strong>strong encryption</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q3\">Exam 4 · Q3</a>. This is one layer of <strong>defense in depth</strong>, next to physical security, access controls and monitoring <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q3\">Exam 4 · Q3</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Data location</strong> is controlled by the customer's choice of <strong>regions, zones and multi-regions</strong>. Google doesn't hide data in random places <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q3\">Exam 4 · Q3</a>.</li>\n<li>To meet data protection expectations in many countries, <strong>encrypting personal data (PII)</strong> is a baseline control (<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q28\">Exam 3 · Q28</a>, see ⚠️).</li>\n<li>Moving to the cloud means you benefit from <strong>built-in security</strong> (default encryption, secured infrastructure) instead of building it all yourself (<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q19\">Exam 4 · Q19</a>, see ⚠️).</li>\n<li><strong>Regular backups</strong> let you restore to a <strong>known-good state</strong> after device failure, accidental deletion or ransomware. Good practice: scheduled backups, retention policies, protected backup storage and <strong>testing restores</strong>. Google Cloud offers <strong>Backup and DR Service</strong> for this <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q23\">Exam 6 · Q23</a>.</li>\n<li>An <strong>incident response plan</strong> prepares the company to reduce the impact of a breach. You still need one in the cloud <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q25\">Exam 6 · Q25</a>. It defines roles, escalation, communication, containment, investigation, recovery, <strong>notification duties</strong> and a post-incident review <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q25\">Exam 6 · Q25</a>.</li>\n<li><em>(not in the question bank)</em> Key management options, from least to most customer control:</li>\n<li><strong>Google default encryption</strong>: Google manages the keys.</li>\n<li><strong>CMEK (customer-managed encryption keys)</strong>: keys live in <strong>Cloud KMS</strong>, and the customer controls rotation and access.</li>\n<li><strong>Cloud HSM</strong>: keys in certified hardware security modules.</li>\n<li><strong>Cloud External Key Manager (EKM)</strong>: keys stay outside Google.</li>\n<li><strong>CSEK (customer-supplied encryption keys)</strong>: the customer supplies the key with each request.</li>\n<li><strong>Sensitive Data Protection</strong> (formerly Cloud DLP) discovers, classifies, masks, tokenizes and de-identifies sensitive data such as credit card numbers. It does not provide default encryption <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q30\">Exam 5 · Q30</a>. <strong>Data masking</strong> hides or replaces real values, for example in test environments <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q7\">Exam 5 · Q7</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q47\">Exam 3 · Q47</a>.</li>\n<li><em>(not in the question bank)</em> Google designs its own hardware, including the <strong>Titan</strong> security chip that verifies machines boot trusted code.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"When is data encrypted by default?\" → <strong>at rest and in transit</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q23\">Exam 5 · Q23</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Why is data protected without anyone enabling it?\" → <strong>encrypted by default</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q30\">Exam 5 · Q30</a>.</li>\n<li>Physical intrusion scenario → <strong>strong encryption</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q3\">Exam 4 · Q3</a>.</li>\n<li>Restore after corruption or ransomware → <strong>regular backups</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q23\">Exam 6 · Q23</a>. Prepare for a breach → <strong>incident response plan</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q25\">Exam 6 · Q25</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Can customers manage or rotate their own keys?\" → yes, with <strong>Cloud KMS / CMEK</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q18\">Exam 4 · Q18</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Security Command Center</strong> and <strong>Sensitive Data Protection</strong> don't do the default encryption <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q30\">Exam 5 · Q30</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Quantum computers\", \"unpredictable locations\" and \"instant deletion\" are fantasy options <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q3\">Exam 4 · Q3</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>SLOs, Cloud Monitoring and autoscaling</strong> don't restore lost data. Monitoring tells you something is wrong, and autoscaling adds instances that still depend on the damaged data <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q23\">Exam 6 · Q23</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Reducing MFA</strong> or <strong>improving the physical perimeter of your own data center</strong> doesn't prepare a cloud-only company for a breach <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q25\">Exam 6 · Q25</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Replace perimeter security with encryption keys\" is wrong. Encryption is one layer, not a replacement. You still need IAM, network security, logging, monitoring and vulnerability management <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q19\">Exam 4 · Q19</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Built-in security\" doesn't mean \"nothing left to do\". Plan your own part (identities, access, data permissions, app settings) from the start of the migration <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q19\">Exam 4 · Q19</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"Google Cloud encrypts all customer data at rest and in transit by default with Google-managed keys, which also protects data if someone gains physical access. Customers who want more key control can use Cloud KMS and CMEK. Customers still need regular, tested backups and an incident response plan to recover from failures, ransomware and breaches.","objectives":["State when Google Cloud encrypts data by default","Explain how encryption protects data from physical intrusion","Choose backups to restore a known-good state","Explain why an incident response plan is still needed in the cloud","Name the options for more control over encryption keys (Cloud KMS, CMEK, CSEK)"],"takeaways":["Data is encrypted by default at rest and in transit, with no setup.","Security Command Center and Sensitive Data Protection are not what provides default encryption.","Strong encryption protects stored data even if hardware is physically accessed.","Regular backups restore data after device failure or ransomware.","An incident response plan reduces the impact of a breach.","Customers can manage and rotate their own keys with Cloud KMS and CMEK.","Sensitive Data Protection discovers, classifies and masks sensitive data. It is not the default encryption layer."],"questions":["e3q28","e3q47","e4q3","e4q18","e4q19","e5q7","e5q23","e5q30","e6q23","e6q25"]},{"id":"d5l7","num":7,"title":"Security operations and threat intelligence","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Security Command Center (SCC)</strong> is the <strong>central place</strong> to see security posture across many projects, find <strong>misconfigurations and vulnerabilities</strong>, prioritize findings and act on them <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q30\">Exam 1 · Q30</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q35\">Exam 2 · Q35</a>. It also gives <strong>asset visibility</strong>, <strong>threat detection</strong> and attack surface views, and it flags compliance issues <strong>in your own resources</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q30\">Exam 1 · Q30</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q38\">Exam 6 · Q38</a>.</li>\n<li><em>(not in the question bank)</em> SCC can also <strong>detect cryptomining</strong> on your VMs, which links back to the threat in Lesson 2.</li>\n<li><strong>Google Security Operations</strong> (Google SecOps, formerly Chronicle) is a cloud-native <strong>SIEM/SOAR</strong> platform. It brings security data from apps, endpoints, networks and clouds into one place, uses AI and threat intelligence for detection, and speeds up <strong>investigation and response</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q40\">Exam 1 · Q40</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>SIEM</strong> (security information and event management) collects, normalizes and correlates security data to detect threats.</li>\n<li><strong>SOAR</strong> (security orchestration, automation and response) runs <strong>playbooks</strong> that automate response steps <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q40\">Exam 1 · Q40</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Threat intelligence</strong> is analyzed information about attackers: their <strong>goals, targets, malware campaigns and tactics, techniques and procedures (TTPs)</strong>, plus <strong>indicators of compromise (IoCs)</strong> such as malicious IPs, domains and file hashes. Teams use it to detect earlier and defend proactively <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q19\">Exam 3 · Q19</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Threat response</strong> is what you do after something is detected: investigate, contain, remove the threat, recover and improve. It uses threat intelligence but isn't the intelligence itself <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q19\">Exam 3 · Q19</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q8\">Exam 3 · Q8</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Mandiant</strong> is Google Cloud's incident response and consulting organization. It is known for <strong>frontline intelligence</strong> from hands-on response to major attacks and breach investigations <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q46\">Exam 1 · Q46</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>VirusTotal</strong> checks <strong>files, URLs, domains and IP addresses</strong> against <strong>many antivirus engines, URL scanners and community submissions</strong>. It is a crowdsourced malware intelligence source <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q17\">Exam 1 · Q17</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Google Threat Intelligence</strong> combines <strong>Mandiant</strong> frontline knowledge, <strong>VirusTotal</strong> crowdsourced data, Google's own visibility and <strong>AI (Gemini)</strong> analysis in one offering <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q10\">Exam 5 · Q10</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Security tools compared</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Product</th>\n<th>What it does</th>\n<th>Clue words</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Security Command Center</td>\n<td>Posture, misconfigurations, vulnerabilities across projects</td>\n<td>\"centralized\", \"posture\", \"risky configurations\", \"prioritize findings\"</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q30\">Exam 1 · Q30</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q35\">Exam 2 · Q35</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Google Security Operations</td>\n<td>SIEM/SOAR: collect, detect, investigate, respond</td>\n<td>\"many alerts\", \"one place\", \"respond faster\"</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q40\">Exam 1 · Q40</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mandiant</td>\n<td>Incident response experts, frontline intelligence</td>\n<td>\"hands-on incident response\"</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q46\">Exam 1 · Q46</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>VirusTotal</td>\n<td>Scan files/URLs against many engines</td>\n<td>\"suspicious file\", \"many antivirus engines\"</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q17\">Exam 1 · Q17</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Google Threat Intelligence</td>\n<td>Mandiant + VirusTotal + Google visibility + AI</td>\n<td>lists several sources together</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q10\">Exam 5 · Q10</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Find weaknesses / misconfigurations across projects → <strong>SCC</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q30\">Exam 1 · Q30</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q35\">Exam 2 · Q35</a>.</li>\n<li>Too many alerts, need detection and response workflow → <strong>Google SecOps</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q40\">Exam 1 · Q40</a>.</li>\n<li>Single suspicious file or URL → <strong>VirusTotal</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q17\">Exam 1 · Q17</a>.</li>\n<li>Question lists Mandiant <strong>and</strong> VirusTotal <strong>and</strong> AI → <strong>Google Threat Intelligence</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q10\">Exam 5 · Q10</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cloud Armor</strong> blocks attacks at the edge. It doesn't give you a posture view <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q35\">Exam 2 · Q35</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Threat response</strong> is acting on an incident. <strong>Threat intelligence</strong> is the knowledge that informs it <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q19\">Exam 3 · Q19</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>IAM</strong> and <strong>GKE</strong> show up as filler distractors in threat-intel questions <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q10\">Exam 5 · Q10</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Apigee</strong> manages and secures APIs. It is not a security operations platform <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q40\">Exam 1 · Q40</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Compliance auditing</strong> checks controls against standards. It isn't knowledge about attackers <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q19\">Exam 3 · Q19</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"Google Cloud's security operations and threat intelligence portfolio: Security Command Center for posture, Google Security Operations for detection and response, and Mandiant, VirusTotal and Google Threat Intelligence for knowledge about attackers.","objectives":["Choose Security Command Center for a central posture and vulnerability view","Choose Google Security Operations for alert ingestion, detection and response","Define threat intelligence and name its Google sources","Tell Mandiant, VirusTotal and Google Threat Intelligence apart"],"takeaways":["Central view of misconfigurations and vulnerabilities across projects is Security Command Center.","Too many alerts, need detection, investigation and response is Google Security Operations.","Threat intelligence is analyzed knowledge of attacker goals, targets and TTPs.","Mandiant = frontline incident response. VirusTotal = multi-engine file and URL scanning.","Google Threat Intelligence combines Mandiant, VirusTotal, Google visibility and AI.","SIEM collects and correlates security data. SOAR automates response with playbooks.","Threat response acts on an incident. Threat intelligence is the knowledge that informs it."],"questions":["e1q17","e1q30","e1q40","e1q46","e2q35","e3q8","e3q19","e5q10","e6q38"]},{"id":"d5l8","num":8,"title":"Privacy, data ownership, sovereignty and compliance","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Privacy</strong> in the cloud = <strong>restrictions on how personal data is accessed and shared</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q27\">Exam 6 · Q27</a>. Privacy and security overlap but differ: <strong>security</strong> protects systems and data from threats, <strong>privacy</strong> is about proper handling of personal data. A breach of personal data becomes a privacy incident <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q27\">Exam 6 · Q27</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Data sovereignty</strong>: data is governed by the <strong>laws of the country where it is stored</strong>. Those laws can cover protection, access, privacy, retention and auditability <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q10\">Exam 1 · Q10</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Data residency</strong> is the choice of <strong>where</strong> data is physically stored (region selection). Sovereignty is the legal consequence of that choice. Companies pick regions and use <strong>data residency controls</strong> to meet sovereignty duties <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q10\">Exam 1 · Q10</a>. <em>(not in the question bank)</em> <strong>Assured Workloads</strong> and sovereign cloud offerings help meet location and personnel controls for regulated industries.</li>\n<li><strong>Google Distributed Cloud</strong> extends Google Cloud to customer data centers and edge sites for strict <strong>locality, regulatory, latency or survivability</strong> needs. It is not a threat intelligence group <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q46\">Exam 1 · Q46</a>.</li>\n<li>Individual rights to <strong>access, correct or delete</strong> their personal data come from <strong>privacy</strong> laws, not sovereignty <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q10\">Exam 1 · Q10</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>The customer owns its data</strong>, not Google, not a third party, not shared ownership. Google <strong>processes</strong> it to provide the service, under the customer's instructions. The customer decides what to upload, who can access it, how long to keep it and when to delete it <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q4\">Exam 4 · Q4</a>.</li>\n<li>Customers <strong>control their data and can export or move it</strong> whenever they want, with each service's export tools, transfer services or APIs. No support ticket or special activation is needed. Normal network charges and the customer's own IAM and network settings still apply <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q6\">Exam 4 · Q6</a>.</li>\n<li>Google <strong>does not use customer data for advertising</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q18\">Exam 4 · Q18</a>.</li>\n<li>Google has its privacy practices <strong>independently audited against industry standards</strong> (for example ISO/IEC 27001, 27017, 27018, SOC reports) (<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q12\">Exam 4 · Q12</a>, see ⚠️).</li>\n<li><strong>Compliance Reports Manager</strong> gives customers <strong>certificates, audit reports and compliance documents</strong> such as ISO 27001 certificates and SOC reports, on demand <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q38\">Exam 6 · Q38</a>. SCC checks <strong>your</strong> resources. Compliance Reports Manager proves <strong>Google's</strong> certifications <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q38\">Exam 6 · Q38</a>.</li>\n<li><em>(not in the question bank)</em> The <strong>Compliance resource center</strong> lists certifications and regulations Google supports (GDPR, HIPAA, PCI DSS and more). <strong>Access Transparency</strong> logs Google staff access to your content, and <strong>Access Approval</strong> lets you approve that access first.</li>\n<li>Security spending under the <strong>OpEx</strong> model means <strong>no large upfront capital investment</strong> in security hardware. You still configure your own settings, and Google doesn't \"guarantee security for every workload\" <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q47\">Exam 4 · Q47</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Google's trust commitments</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Statement</th>\n<th>True?</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>The customer owns its data</td>\n<td>✅</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q4\">Exam 4 · Q4</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Customer can export/transfer data at any time</td>\n<td>✅</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q6\">Exam 4 · Q6</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Google doesn't use customer data for ads</td>\n<td>✅</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q18\">Exam 4 · Q18</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Privacy practices audited against industry standards</td>\n<td>✅</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q12\">Exam 4 · Q12</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Data can't leave Google Cloud / needs a ticket</td>\n<td>❌</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q6\">Exam 4 · Q6</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Google and the customer share ownership</td>\n<td>❌</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q4\">Exam 4 · Q4</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Google doesn't let customers change encryption keys</td>\n<td>❌</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q18\">Exam 4 · Q18</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>OpEx means you don't configure security</td>\n<td>❌</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q47\">Exam 4 · Q47</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Laws of the country where data is stored\" → <strong>data sovereignty</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q10\">Exam 1 · Q10</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Restrictions on access and sharing\" → <strong>privacy</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q27\">Exam 6 · Q27</a>.</li>\n<li>Need ISO 27001 certificate or audit report → <strong>Compliance Reports Manager</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q38\">Exam 6 · Q38</a>.</li>\n<li>Expanding to many countries → protect PII and follow <strong>each market's</strong> rules, not only the home country's <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q28\">Exam 3 · Q28</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Sovereignty is <strong>not</strong> \"a country can access all data in its borders\", <strong>not</strong> individual control of personal data (that's privacy), and <strong>not</strong> encryption <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q10\">Exam 1 · Q10</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Verifying a user's identity\" is <strong>authentication</strong>, not privacy. \"Meeting standards\" is <strong>compliance</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q27\">Exam 6 · Q27</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>SCC</strong>, <strong>Network Intelligence Center</strong> and <strong>Cloud Monitoring</strong> don't hold audit reports <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q38\">Exam 6 · Q38</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Aggregating customer data by industry</strong> or <strong>migrating data to an offline server when a threat appears</strong> are made-up policies <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q12\">Exam 4 · Q12</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q18\">Exam 4 · Q18</a>.</li>\n<li>A <strong>business license</strong> may be needed to operate in a market, but it doesn't protect customer data <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q28\">Exam 3 · Q28</a>.</li>\n<li>Following only the <strong>headquarters country's</strong> policies ignores where the data and users actually are <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q28\">Exam 3 · Q28</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"Google Cloud's trust commitments and compliance tools: customers own and can export their data, Google doesn't use it for advertising, and privacy practices are independently audited. It also covers data sovereignty, privacy, Compliance Reports Manager and how OpEx changes security spending.","objectives":["Define data sovereignty and privacy in the cloud","State who owns customer data and whether it can be exported","List Google's privacy commitments","Use Compliance Reports Manager to get certificates and audit reports","Distinguish data residency, data sovereignty and privacy rights"],"takeaways":["Data sovereignty means data is governed by the laws of the country where it is stored.","Privacy means restrictions on how data is accessed and shared.","The customer owns its data and can export it at any time.","Google does not use customer data for advertising and has its privacy practices audited.","ISO 27001 certificates and audit reports come from Compliance Reports Manager.","OpEx removes big upfront security investment but not the customer's configuration duties.","Residency is where data is stored. Sovereignty is whose laws then apply.","SCC checks your own resources. Compliance Reports Manager proves Google's certifications."],"questions":["e1q10","e1q46","e3q28","e4q4","e4q6","e4q12","e4q18","e4q47","e6q27","e6q38"]}],"ref":{"cheat":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Availability</strong> = accessible when needed. Confidentiality = approved users only. Integrity = correct and complete <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q27\">Exam 1 · Q27</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Security posture</strong> = full readiness picture. <strong>Cyber resilience</strong> = prevent, keep running, recover <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q8\">Exam 3 · Q8</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q7\">Exam 5 · Q7</a>.</li>\n<li>Long-term security = <strong>expertise and an evolving plan</strong>, not a pricier tool <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q4\">Exam 5 · Q4</a>.</li>\n<li>Fake message asking for credentials → <strong>phishing</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q31\">Exam 1 · Q31</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q28\">Exam 2 · Q28</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q41\">Exam 2 · Q41</a>.</li>\n<li>Secret theft or damage → <strong>malware</strong>. Encrypted files + payment demand → <strong>ransomware</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q46\">Exam 2 · Q46</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q17\">Exam 5 · Q17</a>.</li>\n<li>Rogue VMs + bill spike → <strong>cryptomining</strong>. Public bucket by mistake → <strong>misconfiguration</strong>, not APT or zero-day <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q25\">Exam 1 · Q25</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q26\">Exam 1 · Q26</a>.</li>\n<li>Vendor plugin breach → <strong>unsecured third-party systems</strong>. Prompt override → <strong>LLM attack</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q20\">Exam 3 · Q20</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q50\">Exam 5 · Q50</a>.</li>\n<li>Physical data center security → <strong>Google alone</strong>, in every model <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q49\">Exam 1 · Q49</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q20\">Exam 4 · Q20</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q42\">Exam 6 · Q42</a>.</li>\n<li>Data access policies → <strong>the customer's IT team</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q19\">Exam 5 · Q19</a>.</li>\n<li>Who can view vs delete → <strong>IAM</strong>. Prove identity → <strong>authentication</strong> (first), then <strong>authorization</strong>. Second factor → <strong>2SV/MFA</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q12\">Exam 1 · Q12</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q24\">Exam 5 · Q24</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q47\">Exam 3 · Q47</a>.</li>\n<li>Service accounts are for <strong>workloads</strong>, not shared human logins <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q7\">Exam 3 · Q7</a>.</li>\n<li>Only what the job needs → <strong>least privilege</strong>. Many teams → <strong>roles by job function</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q22\">Exam 6 · Q22</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q7\">Exam 3 · Q7</a>.</li>\n<li>Traffic allow/deny → <strong>firewall rules</strong>. DDoS → <strong>Cloud Armor</strong>. AI prompts → <strong>Model Armor</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q27\">Exam 3 · Q27</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q10\">Exam 3 · Q10</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q2\">Exam 4 · Q2</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q33\">Exam 1 · Q33</a>.</li>\n<li>Verify every request from anywhere → <strong>zero trust</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q6\">Exam 5 · Q6</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q49\">Exam 6 · Q49</a>.</li>\n<li>Default encryption → <strong>at rest and in transit</strong>, no setup needed. More key control → <strong>Cloud KMS / CMEK</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q23\">Exam 5 · Q23</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q30\">Exam 5 · Q30</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q3\">Exam 4 · Q3</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q18\">Exam 4 · Q18</a>.</li>\n<li>Encrypt PII, rely on <strong>built-in security</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q28\">Exam 3 · Q28</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q19\">Exam 4 · Q19</a>.</li>\n<li>Restore → <strong>backups</strong>. Prepare for breach → <strong>incident response plan</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q23\">Exam 6 · Q23</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q25\">Exam 6 · Q25</a>.</li>\n<li>Posture across projects → <strong>SCC</strong>. Alert overload → <strong>Google SecOps</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q30\">Exam 1 · Q30</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q35\">Exam 2 · Q35</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q40\">Exam 1 · Q40</a>.</li>\n<li>TTP knowledge → <strong>threat intelligence</strong>. Frontline IR → <strong>Mandiant</strong>. File scan → <strong>VirusTotal</strong>. All combined → <strong>Google Threat Intelligence</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q19\">Exam 3 · Q19</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q46\">Exam 1 · Q46</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q17\">Exam 1 · Q17</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q10\">Exam 5 · Q10</a>.</li>\n<li>Laws of the storage country → <strong>data sovereignty</strong>. Privacy → <strong>limits on access and sharing</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q10\">Exam 1 · Q10</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q27\">Exam 6 · Q27</a>.</li>\n<li>Customer <strong>owns</strong> and can <strong>export</strong> its data. <strong>No ads</strong> use. <strong>Audited</strong> privacy <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q4\">Exam 4 · Q4</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q6\">Exam 4 · Q6</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q18\">Exam 4 · Q18</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q12\">Exam 4 · Q12</a>.</li>\n<li>ISO 27001 certificates → <strong>Compliance Reports Manager</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q38\">Exam 6 · Q38</a>.</li>\n<li>OpEx security → <strong>no big upfront investment</strong>, but you still configure <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q47\">Exam 4 · Q47</a>.</li>\n</ul>","rules":[{"lesson":"d5l1","title":"Core security concepts: CIA, posture, resilience","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"Complete view of readiness\", \"overall state of security\" → <strong>security posture</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q8\">Exam 3 · Q8</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Prevent, keep running, limit impact, recover\" → <strong>cyber resilience</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q7\">Exam 5 · Q7</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Long-term\", \"improve the security approach\" → invest in <strong>expertise and process</strong>, not the most expensive tool <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q4\">Exam 5 · Q4</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d5l2","title":"Cybersecurity threats","html":"<ul>\n<li>Something <strong>asks the user</strong> for credentials or a click → <strong>phishing</strong>. Something <strong>demands payment</strong> to unlock data → <strong>ransomware</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Billing spike + unknown VMs</strong> → <strong>cryptomining</strong>, not DDoS <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q25\">Exam 1 · Q25</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Without exploiting any software flaw\" → <strong>misconfiguration</strong>, not zero-day or APT <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q26\">Exam 1 · Q26</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d5l3","title":"The shared responsibility model","html":"<ul>\n<li>Physical, buildings, hardware, facilities → <strong>Google Cloud alone</strong>, not \"shared\" <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q42\">Exam 6 · Q42</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Who decides which employees access what\" → <strong>the customer's IT team</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q19\">Exam 5 · Q19</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d5l4","title":"Identity and access management","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"Who can view vs. delete/create\" → <strong>IAM</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q12\">Exam 1 · Q12</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Confirm the person is really the claimed user\" → <strong>authentication</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q24\">Exam 5 · Q24</a>.</li>\n<li>Password + phone/code/key → <strong>2SV/MFA</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q47\">Exam 3 · Q47</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Only the permissions needed for the role\" → <strong>least privilege</strong>, not zero trust <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q22\">Exam 6 · Q22</a>.</li>\n<li>Several teams, one project, fastest way → <strong>roles mapped to job functions</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q7\">Exam 3 · Q7</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d5l5","title":"Network and application protection","html":"<ul>\n<li>DDoS on a public site → <strong>Cloud Armor</strong>, every time <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q10\">Exam 3 · Q10</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q2\">Exam 4 · Q2</a>.</li>\n<li>Generative AI safety (prompt injection, jailbreak, harmful output) → <strong>Model Armor</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q33\">Exam 1 · Q33</a>.</li>\n<li>\"No longer trust the office network\" → <strong>zero trust</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q6\">Exam 5 · Q6</a>.</li>\n<li>SQL injection or cross-site scripting against a public web app → <strong>Cloud Armor WAF rules</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q10\">Exam 3 · Q10</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q35\">Exam 2 · Q35</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d5l6","title":"Encryption, backup and incident readiness","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"When is data encrypted by default?\" → <strong>at rest and in transit</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q23\">Exam 5 · Q23</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Why is data protected without anyone enabling it?\" → <strong>encrypted by default</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q30\">Exam 5 · Q30</a>.</li>\n<li>Physical intrusion scenario → <strong>strong encryption</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q3\">Exam 4 · Q3</a>.</li>\n<li>Restore after corruption or ransomware → <strong>regular backups</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q23\">Exam 6 · Q23</a>. Prepare for a breach → <strong>incident response plan</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q25\">Exam 6 · Q25</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Can customers manage or rotate their own keys?\" → yes, with <strong>Cloud KMS / CMEK</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q18\">Exam 4 · Q18</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d5l7","title":"Security operations and threat intelligence","html":"<ul>\n<li>Find weaknesses / misconfigurations across projects → <strong>SCC</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q30\">Exam 1 · Q30</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q35\">Exam 2 · Q35</a>.</li>\n<li>Too many alerts, need detection and response workflow → <strong>Google SecOps</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q40\">Exam 1 · Q40</a>.</li>\n<li>Single suspicious file or URL → <strong>VirusTotal</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q17\">Exam 1 · Q17</a>.</li>\n<li>Question lists Mandiant <strong>and</strong> VirusTotal <strong>and</strong> AI → <strong>Google Threat Intelligence</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q10\">Exam 5 · Q10</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d5l8","title":"Privacy, data ownership, sovereignty and compliance","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"Laws of the country where data is stored\" → <strong>data sovereignty</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q10\">Exam 1 · Q10</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Restrictions on access and sharing\" → <strong>privacy</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q27\">Exam 6 · Q27</a>.</li>\n<li>Need ISO 27001 certificate or audit report → <strong>Compliance Reports Manager</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q38\">Exam 6 · Q38</a>.</li>\n<li>Expanding to many countries → protect PII and follow <strong>each market's</strong> rules, not only the home country's <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q28\">Exam 3 · Q28</a>.</li>\n</ul>"}],"traps":[{"lesson":"d5l1","title":"Core security concepts: CIA, posture, resilience","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Threat response</strong> is only one part of posture. It is reacting to incidents, not the whole picture <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q8\">Exam 3 · Q8</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Data masking</strong> hides values. It is not resilience <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q7\">Exam 5 · Q7</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Longer data retention</strong> is not a security fix. Keeping data longer means more data can leak. Retention should follow business, legal and privacy needs <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q4\">Exam 5 · Q4</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>IaaS</strong> and <strong>vendor lock-in</strong> are not resilience either. IaaS is a service model and lock-in is a portability concern <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q7\">Exam 5 · Q7</a>.</li>\n<li>Waiting for a \"newer platform\" or buying \"the most expensive platform\" is never the answer <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q4\">Exam 5 · Q4</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d5l2","title":"Cybersecurity threats","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Spamming</strong> is unwanted bulk mail. It doesn't try to steal credentials <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q28\">Exam 2 · Q28</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Ransomware</strong> is a kind of malware, but it is loud (it announces itself). \"Without the user noticing\" points to generic <strong>malware</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q46\">Exam 2 · Q46</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Zero trust architecture</strong> is a defense model, not a threat vector <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q20\">Exam 3 · Q20</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>SQL injection</strong> is a code attack, not social engineering <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q41\">Exam 2 · Q41</a>. Social engineering exploits <strong>human trust, fear, urgency or curiosity</strong>, not a technical flaw <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q41\">Exam 2 · Q41</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Physical damage</strong> and <strong>hardware failure</strong> are not cyberattacks. Without a ransom note or an attacker, they are operational risks <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q20\">Exam 3 · Q20</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q17\">Exam 5 · Q17</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>SLA violations</strong> and <strong>incomplete data input</strong> are service and data quality issues, not threats <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q46\">Exam 2 · Q46</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d5l3","title":"The shared responsibility model","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"<strong>Customer and Google</strong>\" sounds balanced, but physical security is Google's alone <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q42\">Exam 6 · Q42</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Cloud Identity</strong> is a tool the customer uses. It manages users and groups and supplies the identities that IAM policies refer to. It doesn't decide policy <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q19\">Exam 5 · Q19</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Google Cloud Customer Care</strong> and <strong>external security providers</strong> don't decide your access rules or secure Google's data centers <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q19\">Exam 5 · Q19</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q42\">Exam 6 · Q42</a>.</li>\n<li>Assuming security is \"fully Google's responsibility\" is always wrong <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q25\">Exam 6 · Q25</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d5l4","title":"Identity and access management","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Basic roles</strong> (Owner/Editor/Viewer) for every employee are too broad <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q7\">Exam 3 · Q7</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q49\">Exam 6 · Q49</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>One shared service account</strong> for all departments breaks accountability and least privilege <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q7\">Exam 3 · Q7</a>.</li>\n<li>Setting least-privilege roles <strong>separately for each employee</strong> is correct in spirit but slow. The exam wants the scalable option <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q7\">Exam 3 · Q7</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>VPC</strong> controls network traffic, not who can delete a dataset <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q12\">Exam 1 · Q12</a>. <strong>Cloud Storage</strong> has bucket-level access controls, but it isn't the service for BigQuery permissions <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q12\">Exam 1 · Q12</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Security by default</strong> means products ship with protections already on. It is not about how many permissions a user gets <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q22\">Exam 6 · Q22</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Privileged access</strong> is about elevated admin permissions, not about filtering network traffic <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q27\">Exam 3 · Q27</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Zero trust</strong> and <strong>least privilege</strong> are related. Zero trust is the overall model (\"verify every request\"). Least privilege is about how much access is granted <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q22\">Exam 6 · Q22</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d5l5","title":"Network and application protection","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Security Command Center</strong> finds risks and misconfigurations. It doesn't absorb DDoS traffic <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q10\">Exam 3 · Q10</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q2\">Exam 4 · Q2</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>IAP</strong> controls who can reach an app based on identity. It's not a DDoS shield <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q10\">Exam 3 · Q10</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Cloud KMS</strong> manages encryption keys. It doesn't inspect or block traffic <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q10\">Exam 3 · Q10</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Cloud Run functions</strong> could call a security service, but they don't screen AI prompts themselves. Model Armor is the purpose-built control <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q33\">Exam 1 · Q33</a>.</li>\n<li>A <strong>VPN</strong> encrypts a network path but still trusts whoever is inside. It is the opposite of zero trust <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q6\">Exam 5 · Q6</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Strong perimeter and trust everything inside\" is the old castle-and-moat model <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q49\">Exam 6 · Q49</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d5l6","title":"Encryption, backup and incident readiness","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Security Command Center</strong> and <strong>Sensitive Data Protection</strong> don't do the default encryption <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q30\">Exam 5 · Q30</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Quantum computers\", \"unpredictable locations\" and \"instant deletion\" are fantasy options <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q3\">Exam 4 · Q3</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>SLOs, Cloud Monitoring and autoscaling</strong> don't restore lost data. Monitoring tells you something is wrong, and autoscaling adds instances that still depend on the damaged data <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q23\">Exam 6 · Q23</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Reducing MFA</strong> or <strong>improving the physical perimeter of your own data center</strong> doesn't prepare a cloud-only company for a breach <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q25\">Exam 6 · Q25</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Replace perimeter security with encryption keys\" is wrong. Encryption is one layer, not a replacement. You still need IAM, network security, logging, monitoring and vulnerability management <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q19\">Exam 4 · Q19</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Built-in security\" doesn't mean \"nothing left to do\". Plan your own part (identities, access, data permissions, app settings) from the start of the migration <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q19\">Exam 4 · Q19</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d5l7","title":"Security operations and threat intelligence","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Cloud Armor</strong> blocks attacks at the edge. It doesn't give you a posture view <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q35\">Exam 2 · Q35</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Threat response</strong> is acting on an incident. <strong>Threat intelligence</strong> is the knowledge that informs it <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q19\">Exam 3 · Q19</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>IAM</strong> and <strong>GKE</strong> show up as filler distractors in threat-intel questions <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q10\">Exam 5 · Q10</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Apigee</strong> manages and secures APIs. It is not a security operations platform <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q40\">Exam 1 · Q40</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Compliance auditing</strong> checks controls against standards. It isn't knowledge about attackers <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q19\">Exam 3 · Q19</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d5l8","title":"Privacy, data ownership, sovereignty and compliance","html":"<ul>\n<li>Sovereignty is <strong>not</strong> \"a country can access all data in its borders\", <strong>not</strong> individual control of personal data (that's privacy), and <strong>not</strong> encryption <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q10\">Exam 1 · Q10</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Verifying a user's identity\" is <strong>authentication</strong>, not privacy. \"Meeting standards\" is <strong>compliance</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q27\">Exam 6 · Q27</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>SCC</strong>, <strong>Network Intelligence Center</strong> and <strong>Cloud Monitoring</strong> don't hold audit reports <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q38\">Exam 6 · Q38</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Aggregating customer data by industry</strong> or <strong>migrating data to an offline server when a threat appears</strong> are made-up policies <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q12\">Exam 4 · Q12</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q18\">Exam 4 · Q18</a>.</li>\n<li>A <strong>business license</strong> may be needed to operate in a market, but it doesn't protect customer data <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q28\">Exam 3 · Q28</a>.</li>\n<li>Following only the <strong>headquarters country's</strong> policies ignores where the data and users actually are <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q28\">Exam 3 · Q28</a>.</li>\n</ul>"}],"questionable":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Item</th>\n<th>Issue</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q28\">Exam 3 · Q28</a></strong></td>\n<td>Keyed \"automatically encrypt PII\". Encryption helps, but it doesn't by itself meet global compliance. Data residency, consent and local regulations matter too. Also, Google already encrypts all data at rest by default, so this isn't a special action. Pick it because the other options are clearly worse.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q4\">Exam 5 · Q4</a></strong></td>\n<td>Keyed \"hire cybersecurity experts\". Reasonable but vague. The real lesson is that security needs ongoing expertise and process, not a one-time tool purchase.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q12\">Exam 4 · Q12</a></strong></td>\n<td>Keyed \"auditing platform privacy practices against industry standards\". The idea is right (third-party audits such as ISO 27018/27701), but the phrasing is loose and option D (\"privacy reviews for critical customer applications\") sounds plausible to a newcomer.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q19\">Exam 4 · Q19</a></strong></td>\n<td>Keyed \"built-in security\". Very vague. It means Google's default protections (encryption, IAM, secure infrastructure, logging) plus planning your own share of the work from the start. The other options are plainly wrong (CapEx, more hardware, replacing perimeter security with keys).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q7\">Exam 3 · Q7</a></strong></td>\n<td>Keyed \"map IAM roles to job functions for each department\". Fine, but Google's stated best practice is to grant roles to <strong>groups</strong>. Treat \"map to job functions\" as the group-based answer.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"},"glossary":[{"term":"2-Step Verification (2SV)","definition":"Sign-in that requires a password plus a second factor such as a phone prompt, a code or a hardware security key. Also called multi-factor authentication (MFA).","exam_context":"Password plus phone prompt, code or security key is 2SV/MFA, not data masking or DDoS protection.","lesson":"d5l4","domain":5},{"term":"Advanced Persistent Threat (APT)","definition":"A skilled, well-resourced attacker who gains access and stays hidden in an environment for a long time to spy or steal data.","exam_context":"A distractor when the cause was a simple mistake such as a public bucket.","lesson":"d5l2","domain":5},{"term":"Auditing","definition":"Recording and reviewing who did what, when and from where, for example with audit logs.","exam_context":"Answers \"what did you do?\". It doesn't verify identity (authentication) or grant access (authorization).","lesson":"d5l4","domain":5},{"term":"Authentication","definition":"The process of proving a user is who they claim to be, for example by signing in.","exam_context":"\"Confirm the person signing in is really the claimed user\" is authentication, not authorization or auditing.","lesson":"d5l4","domain":5},{"term":"Authorization","definition":"Deciding what an authenticated identity is allowed to do. In Google Cloud this is done with IAM roles.","exam_context":"Comes after authentication. Don't confuse \"what can you do\" with \"who are you\".","lesson":"d5l4","domain":5},{"term":"Availability","definition":"The CIA principle that data and systems stay dependable and accessible when needed.","exam_context":"Pick \"accessible when needed\". \"Correct and complete\" is integrity, \"approved users only\" is confidentiality.","lesson":"d5l1","domain":5},{"term":"Backups","definition":"Point-in-time copies of data and configuration used to restore to a known-good state. Best practice includes scheduled backups, retention, protected storage and restore tests.","exam_context":"Restore after disk failure or ransomware is backups, not SLOs, monitoring or autoscaling.","lesson":"d5l6","domain":5},{"term":"Cloud Identity","definition":"Google service for managing users and groups, with single sign-on and identity federation. It supplies the identities used in IAM policies.","exam_context":"A tool, not a decision maker: the customer's IT team defines access policies.","lesson":"d5l4","domain":5},{"term":"Cloud Key Management Service (Cloud KMS)","definition":"Google Cloud service to create, manage, rotate and control access to encryption keys, including customer-managed encryption keys (CMEK).","exam_context":"Customers can change and rotate keys with Cloud KMS. It doesn't filter traffic.","lesson":"d5l6","domain":5},{"term":"Cloud VPN","definition":"Service that connects another network to a Google Cloud VPC through encrypted IPsec tunnels.","exam_context":"Protects data in transit between networks. It doesn't block DDoS, and it still trusts the network, unlike zero trust.","lesson":"d5l5","domain":5},{"term":"Compliance","definition":"Meeting legal, contractual and industry standards and being able to prove it. It is not part of the CIA triad.","exam_context":"\"Meeting regulatory and industry standards\" is compliance, used as a distractor for CIA and privacy questions.","lesson":"d5l1","domain":5},{"term":"Compliance Reports Manager","definition":"Google Cloud tool that gives customers certificates, audit reports and compliance documents such as ISO 27001 and SOC reports.","exam_context":"Answer for verifying the provider's certifications. SCC and Cloud Monitoring don't hold audit reports.","lesson":"d5l8","domain":5},{"term":"Confidentiality","definition":"The CIA principle that only approved users can access data. Supported by IAM, authentication, least privilege and encryption.","exam_context":"\"Limiting data access only to approved users\" is confidentiality, not availability.","lesson":"d5l1","domain":5},{"term":"Cryptomining","definition":"An attack where criminals use compromised accounts to run unauthorized compute that mines cryptocurrency at the victim's expense.","exam_context":"Clues: unknown VMs, high CPU on hashing, an unexpected bill.","lesson":"d5l2","domain":5},{"term":"Customer-managed encryption keys (CMEK)","definition":"Encryption keys held in Cloud KMS that the customer controls (location, rotation, permissions), instead of the default Google-managed keys.","exam_context":"Optional extra control. Default encryption already happens without it.","lesson":"d5l6","domain":5},{"term":"Cyber resilience","definition":"The ability to reduce the chance of attacks, keep essential services running during an incident, limit impact and recover quickly.","exam_context":"Matches \"prevent, continue, limit impact, restore\". Not data masking or IaaS.","lesson":"d5l1","domain":5},{"term":"Data masking","definition":"Hiding or replacing sensitive values (for example part of a card number) so users see altered data.","exam_context":"A distractor for resilience and MFA questions. It only hides values.","lesson":"d5l6","domain":5},{"term":"Data residency","definition":"The choice of where data is physically stored, for example by selecting regions.","exam_context":"Residency is where data lives. Sovereignty is whose laws then apply.","lesson":"d5l8","domain":5},{"term":"Data sovereignty","definition":"The principle that data is subject to the laws of the country where it is stored.","exam_context":"Not \"a country can access all data\" and not encryption. Personal control of data is privacy.","lesson":"d5l8","domain":5},{"term":"Default encryption","definition":"Google Cloud encrypts all customer data at rest (before it is written to disk, with Google-managed keys) and in transit automatically, with no customer setup.","exam_context":"Answer is \"at rest and in transit\" and \"encrypted by default\", not SCC or Sensitive Data Protection.","lesson":"d5l6","domain":5},{"term":"Defense in depth","definition":"Layering several security controls (people, process and technology) so one failure doesn't expose everything.","exam_context":"Encryption is one layer next to physical security and access controls, not a replacement for them.","lesson":"d5l1","domain":5},{"term":"Distributed Denial-of-Service (DDoS)","definition":"An attack that floods a service with traffic, often from a botnet, to exhaust resources and block real users.","exam_context":"The defense is Google Cloud Armor.","lesson":"d5l5","domain":5},{"term":"Firewall rules","definition":"Rules that allow or deny incoming and outgoing network traffic to resources.","exam_context":"Answer when the question is about which traffic is allowed or denied.","lesson":"d5l5","domain":5},{"term":"Google Cloud Armor","definition":"Google Cloud's edge security service that protects internet-facing applications from DDoS and web attacks.","exam_context":"DDoS or botnet flood on a public website means Cloud Armor, not SCC, IAP or KMS.","lesson":"d5l5","domain":5},{"term":"Google Distributed Cloud","definition":"Extends Google Cloud infrastructure and services to customer data centers and edge locations for strict locality, regulatory, latency or survivability needs.","exam_context":"A distractor in threat intelligence questions. It is infrastructure, not a security organization.","lesson":"d5l8","domain":5},{"term":"Google Security Operations","definition":"Cloud-native SIEM/SOAR platform (formerly Chronicle) that centralizes security data and uses AI and threat intelligence for detection, investigation and response.","exam_context":"Answer for alert overload and faster incident response.","lesson":"d5l7","domain":5},{"term":"Google Threat Intelligence","definition":"Offering that combines Mandiant frontline intelligence, VirusTotal crowdsourced data, Google's visibility and AI analysis.","exam_context":"Pick it when the question lists Mandiant and VirusTotal and AI together.","lesson":"d5l7","domain":5},{"term":"IAM basic roles","definition":"The broad Owner, Editor and Viewer roles that apply across many Google Cloud services.","exam_context":"Giving basic roles to every employee is too broad and breaks least privilege. Prefer predefined or custom roles.","lesson":"d5l4","domain":5},{"term":"Identity and Access Management (IAM)","definition":"Google Cloud service that controls who can do what on which resource by granting roles.","exam_context":"Answer for \"view but not delete or create\" access questions.","lesson":"d5l4","domain":5},{"term":"Identity-Aware Proxy (IAP)","definition":"Google Cloud service that controls access to applications based on user identity and context, without a VPN.","exam_context":"Controls who can reach an app. It is not a DDoS defense.","lesson":"d5l5","domain":5},{"term":"Incident response plan","definition":"A prepared, documented process for detecting, containing and recovering from security incidents such as data exposure.","exam_context":"Still needed in the cloud. Security is not fully Google's responsibility.","lesson":"d5l6","domain":5},{"term":"Indicators of compromise (IoCs)","definition":"Evidence that a system may be compromised, such as malicious IP addresses, domains or file hashes.","exam_context":"Part of threat intelligence, shared by sources such as VirusTotal and Mandiant.","lesson":"d5l7","domain":5},{"term":"Integrity","definition":"The CIA principle that data stays correct, complete and unaltered, without improper changes, corruption or deletion.","exam_context":"\"Correct, complete and dependable\" is integrity. \"Accessible when needed\" is availability.","lesson":"d5l1","domain":5},{"term":"Least privilege","definition":"Granting each identity only the permissions its job requires and nothing more.","exam_context":"\"Only the specific permissions needed for their role\" is least privilege, not zero trust.","lesson":"d5l4","domain":5},{"term":"LLM attack (prompt injection)","definition":"Crafted prompts that make an AI model ignore its instructions, leak restricted data or bypass controls.","exam_context":"Defended with Model Armor.","lesson":"d5l2","domain":5},{"term":"Malware","definition":"Malicious software that secretly steals, alters or damages information.","exam_context":"\"Without the user noticing\" points to malware. Ransomware is a loud subtype that demands payment.","lesson":"d5l2","domain":5},{"term":"Mandiant","definition":"Google Cloud's incident response and threat intelligence organization, known for frontline intelligence from real attacks.","exam_context":"Answer for \"hands-on incident response\" intelligence.","lesson":"d5l7","domain":5},{"term":"Misconfiguration","definition":"A security weakness caused by incorrect settings, such as making a storage bucket public.","exam_context":"\"No software flaw exploited\" means misconfiguration, not zero-day or APT.","lesson":"d5l2","domain":5},{"term":"Model Armor","definition":"Google Cloud capability that screens AI prompts and responses to reduce prompt injection and jailbreak risk and block harmful output.","exam_context":"Answer for securing public generative AI features.","lesson":"d5l5","domain":5},{"term":"Phishing","definition":"Social engineering where a fake trusted message tricks people into revealing credentials or clicking malicious links.","exam_context":"Fake portal, urgent account warning, request for password or bank details.","lesson":"d5l2","domain":5},{"term":"Privacy","definition":"In the cloud, restrictions on how personal data is accessed and shared.","exam_context":"Not identity verification (authentication) and not meeting standards (compliance).","lesson":"d5l8","domain":5},{"term":"Ransomware","definition":"Malware that encrypts the victim's data and demands payment for the decryption key.","exam_context":"Encrypted files plus a cryptocurrency demand. Defended with backups and an incident response plan.","lesson":"d5l2","domain":5},{"term":"Security by default","definition":"Designing products so protections are on from the start, without customers enabling each one.","exam_context":"Not the same as least privilege, which is about how many permissions a user gets.","lesson":"d5l4","domain":5},{"term":"Security Command Center (SCC)","definition":"Google Cloud's centralized security and risk management service that finds misconfigurations, vulnerabilities and threats across projects. It also gives asset visibility and is where security posture is managed.","exam_context":"Answer for a central posture view across many projects. It doesn't block DDoS or encrypt data.","lesson":"d5l7","domain":5},{"term":"Security posture","definition":"The overall state of an organization's security readiness across network, identity, monitoring and policies.","exam_context":"\"Complete view of cybersecurity readiness\" is security posture, not threat response.","lesson":"d5l1","domain":5},{"term":"Sensitive Data Protection","definition":"Google Cloud service (formerly Cloud DLP) that discovers, classifies, masks, tokenizes and de-identifies sensitive data.","exam_context":"It is not the reason stored data is encrypted. That is default encryption.","lesson":"d5l6","domain":5},{"term":"Service account","definition":"An identity for applications and automated workloads rather than for people.","exam_context":"One shared service account for all departments is wrong: it removes accountability and over-grants access.","lesson":"d5l4","domain":5},{"term":"Shared fate","definition":"Google's extension of shared responsibility: besides securing the cloud, Google helps customers secure their part with secure defaults, tools and guidance.","exam_context":"The customer still configures its own security. Shared fate doesn't mean Google guarantees every workload.","lesson":"d5l3","domain":5},{"term":"Shared responsibility model","definition":"The split of security duties between cloud provider and customer. Google secures the physical infrastructure. The customer secures its data, access and (in IaaS) operating systems and code.","exam_context":"Physical data center security is always Google's alone.","lesson":"d5l3","domain":5},{"term":"SIEM and SOAR","definition":"SIEM collects, normalizes and correlates security data to detect threats. SOAR automates response with playbooks. Google Security Operations provides both.","exam_context":"Alert overload and slow response point to a SIEM/SOAR platform: Google Security Operations.","lesson":"d5l7","domain":5},{"term":"Social engineering","definition":"Attacks that manipulate people through trust, fear, urgency or curiosity rather than exploiting a technical flaw. Phishing is the main example.","exam_context":"Tricking employees into revealing passwords is social engineering. SQL injection and DDoS are not.","lesson":"d5l2","domain":5},{"term":"Threat intelligence","definition":"Analyzed information about attackers' goals, targets, campaigns and tactics, techniques and procedures (TTPs).","exam_context":"Knowledge used to defend proactively. Threat response is acting on an incident.","lesson":"d5l7","domain":5},{"term":"Threat response","definition":"The actions taken after a threat is detected: investigate, contain, remove, recover and improve.","exam_context":"Not the same as threat intelligence (the knowledge) or security posture (the overall picture).","lesson":"d5l7","domain":5},{"term":"Unsecured third-party systems","definition":"Risk that enters through a vendor's software, plugin, library or integration connected to your environment (supply chain risk).","exam_context":"Breach through a vendor plugin despite strong internal controls is a third-party threat vector.","lesson":"d5l2","domain":5},{"term":"VirusTotal","definition":"Google-owned service that checks files, URLs, domains and IP addresses against many antivirus engines, scanners and community submissions.","exam_context":"Single suspicious file or URL checked against many engines is VirusTotal, not Mandiant.","lesson":"d5l7","domain":5},{"term":"Web application firewall (WAF)","definition":"Rules that inspect web requests and block application attacks such as SQL injection and cross-site scripting. Cloud Armor provides WAF rules.","exam_context":"SQL injection or XSS against a public app points to Cloud Armor.","lesson":"d5l5","domain":5},{"term":"Zero trust architecture","definition":"Security model that trusts no network location and verifies every identity, device and request before granting access.","exam_context":"Replaces perimeter trust and VPN-centric access. Google's version is BeyondCorp.","lesson":"d5l5","domain":5},{"term":"Zero-day vulnerability","definition":"A software flaw unknown to the vendor, which attackers can exploit before a fix exists.","exam_context":"\"Without exploiting any software flaw\" rules out a zero-day and points to misconfiguration.","lesson":"d5l2","domain":5}]},{"n":6,"title":"Scaling with Google Cloud Operations","short":"Operations & SRE","weight":"~17%","desc":"Cost management and billing, resource hierarchy, SRE and DevOps, SLAs/SLOs, observability, and sustainability.","lessons":[{"id":"d6l1","num":1,"title":"Cloud financial governance (FinOps)","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cloud spending is decentralized and variable.</strong> Many teams can create resources on demand, and the bill follows actual usage. On-premises spending was centralized (one procurement team) and fixed (hardware bought up front) <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q11\">Exam 2 · Q11</a>.</li>\n<li>That shift is why cloud needs <strong>financial governance</strong>: shared rules, visibility and accountability for who spends what. Google calls the practice <strong>FinOps</strong> (cloud financial management), where finance, technology and business teams work together on cost decisions.</li>\n<li><strong>Accountability comes from visibility.</strong> When teams overspend because they can't see how their usage hits the bill, <strong>share cost views with each department</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q25\">Exam 3 · Q25</a>.</li>\n<li>Cost visibility per team depends on structure: separate projects or folders per team, and <strong>labels</strong> on resources (for example <code>team=finance</code>) so the bill can be broken down. One identical tag on every resource gives no breakdown at all <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q25\">Exam 3 · Q25</a>.</li>\n<li>Labels only help when they are meaningful <strong>key-value pairs</strong> that differ by department, application, environment, cost center or owner. Billing reports, budgets and billing export can then group spend by those labels <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q25\">Exam 3 · Q25</a>.</li>\n<li>Accountability also needs <strong>clear ownership</strong>: someone who approves changes, deletes unused resources and answers for overruns. \"Everyone owns everything\" means nobody does <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q36\">Exam 3 · Q36</a>.</li>\n<li>Cloud costs are <strong>OpEx</strong>, not CapEx. Moving back to CapEx or buying physical servers is never the cloud cost answer <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q25\">Exam 3 · Q25</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q36\">Exam 3 · Q36</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Unpredictable demand + hard-to-control costs:</strong> apply <strong>intelligent recommendations</strong> (Active Assist / Recommender), such as rightsizing idle or oversized VMs and deleting unused resources. Cost recommendations are also gathered in the <strong>FinOps hub</strong> in Cloud Billing <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q36\">Exam 3 · Q36</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Teams don't see how their usage affects the bill\" → <strong>share cost views / reports per team</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q25\">Exam 3 · Q25</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Costs vary month to month, optimize resource use\" → <strong>intelligent recommendations</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q36\">Exam 3 · Q36</a>.</li>\n<li>\"What is cloud spending like?\" → <strong>decentralized and variable</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q11\">Exam 2 · Q11</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Centralized and fixed\" describes the old on-premises model <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q11\">Exam 2 · Q11</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Decentralize financial decisions\" or \"share project ownership across all teams\" sounds collaborative but removes accountability <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q36\">Exam 3 · Q36</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Increase capital expenditure\" or \"improve physical server purchasing\" are on-premises answers <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q25\">Exam 3 · Q25</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q36\">Exam 3 · Q36</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"Cloud spending is decentralized and variable, so organizations need financial governance (FinOps) to keep it under control. This lesson covers cost visibility, accountability and recommendations.","objectives":["Describe how cloud spending differs from on-premises spending","Explain how cost visibility per team creates accountability","Identify intelligent recommendations as the way to optimize resource use"],"takeaways":["Cloud spending is decentralized and variable. On-premises spending was centralized and fixed.","Share cost views with each department so teams see their impact on the bill.","Use labels, projects and folders to break costs down by team.","Labels must be meaningful key-value pairs (team, environment, cost center). One shared tag gives no breakdown.","Intelligent recommendations (Active Assist, also in the FinOps hub) rightsize and clean up resources when demand varies.","Moving to CapEx or buying physical servers is never the cloud cost answer."],"questions":["e2q11","e3q25","e3q36"]},{"id":"d6l2","num":2,"title":"Cost-control tools: budgets, estimates and discounts","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Budgets and budget alerts</strong> (Cloud Billing): set a budget amount, add <strong>threshold rules</strong> (for example 50%, 90%, 100% of the budget, actual or forecasted), and get notified by email or Pub/Sub. It warns before the invoice arrives <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q11\">Exam 1 · Q11</a>.</li>\n<li>A budget alert <strong>notifies</strong>. It does not stop spending by itself. Automatic action (like disabling billing) needs extra automation through Pub/Sub.</li>\n<li><strong>Google Cloud Pricing Calculator:</strong> the tool for a reliable estimate of monthly cost <strong>before</strong> you deploy <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q36\">Exam 2 · Q36</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Sustained use discounts (SUDs):</strong> applied <strong>automatically</strong> to eligible Compute Engine VMs once they run for <strong>more than 25% of the billing month</strong>. The discount grows the longer they run. No sign-up, no setting and no commitment <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q1\">Exam 6 · Q1</a>.</li>\n<li>Only some machine series are eligible (for example N1, N2, N2D, C2, M1, M2). E2 and most newer series get no SUDs.</li>\n<li>SUDs don't stack with CUDs: usage already covered by a commitment gets no SUD <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q1\">Exam 6 · Q1</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Committed use discounts (CUDs):</strong> you commit to a <strong>1-year or 3-year</strong> level of usage (a minimum amount of resources or of spend) in exchange for a bigger discount. That is a different product from SUDs <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q1\">Exam 6 · Q1</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Spot VMs:</strong> deep discount for interruptible capacity. Good for fault-tolerant batch work, not a way to get SUDs <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q1\">Exam 6 · Q1</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Quotas</strong> limit how much of a resource a project can use (CPUs, API calls). They protect against runaway usage, but they are not a spend alert, and costs can still climb inside the quota <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q11\">Exam 1 · Q11</a>.</li>\n<li>The Pricing Calculator accounts for region, machine size, storage class, network egress, discounts and usage hours, which is why manual math from pricing pages is slow and error-prone <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q36\">Exam 2 · Q36</a>.</li>\n<li><em>(not in the question bank)</em> <strong>Cloud Billing reports</strong> and <strong>billing export to BigQuery</strong> let you analyze spend by project, service and label. Billing accounts are linked to projects, and IAM billing roles control who can see or change them.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Cost tools compared</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Need</th>\n<th>Use</th>\n<th>Not</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Be told when spend crosses a limit</td>\n<td>✅ Budget + threshold alert</td>\n<td>❌ Quotas, past billing reports, stopping VMs</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q11\">Exam 1 · Q11</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Estimate cost before deploying</td>\n<td>✅ Pricing Calculator</td>\n<td>❌ Deploying a test workload, manual math from pricing pages, historical costs</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q36\">Exam 2 · Q36</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Discount for VMs that run most of the month</td>\n<td>✅ SUDs, automatic</td>\n<td>❌ 1/3-year commitment (that's CUDs), Spot VMs, smaller machines</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q1\">Exam 6 · Q1</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lower cost on predictable long-term usage</td>\n<td>✅ CUDs</td>\n<td></td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q1\">Exam 6 · Q1</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Optimize resources when demand varies</td>\n<td>✅ Intelligent recommendations</td>\n<td>❌ More CapEx</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q36\">Exam 3 · Q36</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Notify when costs cross X\" → <strong>budget threshold rule and alert</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q11\">Exam 1 · Q11</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Estimate before deploying\" → <strong>Pricing Calculator</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q36\">Exam 2 · Q36</a>.</li>\n<li>\"How do we get sustained use discounts?\" → <strong>do nothing, they are automatic</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q1\">Exam 6 · Q1</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Reviewing past bills looks backward. The question wants a proactive alert <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q11\">Exam 1 · Q11</a>.</li>\n<li>Stopping VMs outside peak hours is cost <strong>optimization</strong>. It does not notify anyone when spend crosses a limit <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q11\">Exam 1 · Q11</a>.</li>\n<li>Historical costs of a \"similar\" workload are only a rough guide. The new workload may use different services, regions or sizes <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q36\">Exam 2 · Q36</a>.</li>\n<li>Deploying a test environment \"to observe costs\" spends money to get an estimate the calculator gives for free <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q36\">Exam 2 · Q36</a>.</li>\n<li>A 1- or 3-year commitment is the <strong>CUD</strong> answer, not the SUD answer <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q1\">Exam 6 · Q1</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"Google Cloud gives specific tools for controlling cost: budgets with threshold alerts, the Pricing Calculator for estimates, and automatic or committed discounts for Compute Engine.","objectives":["Set up the right tool to be notified before spend exceeds a limit","Choose the Pricing Calculator to estimate cost before deploying","Tell sustained use discounts apart from committed use discounts and Spot VMs"],"takeaways":["Budget + threshold rule = notification when spend crosses a limit. It does not stop spending by itself.","The Pricing Calculator estimates monthly cost before deployment.","Sustained use discounts apply automatically once eligible VMs run more than 25% of the month. You do nothing.","Committed use discounts need a 1- or 3-year commitment.","SUDs only cover some machine series (not E2) and don't stack with CUDs.","Quotas cap resource usage. They are not a spend alert."],"questions":["e1q11","e2q36","e3q36","e6q1"]},{"id":"d6l3","num":3,"title":"Resource hierarchy and policy inheritance","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Hierarchy: <strong>Organization → Folders → Projects → Resources</strong>. Folders usually map to departments or teams.</li>\n<li><strong>Policies set on a folder are inherited</strong> by every project in the folder, including projects created later <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q19\">Exam 2 · Q19</a>.</li>\n<li>Inheritance goes all the way down: a folder-level access policy applies to the <strong>projects and to their resources</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q42\">Exam 3 · Q42</a>.</li>\n<li>Two kinds of policy flow down the hierarchy: <strong>IAM allow policies</strong> (who can do what) and <strong>organization policies</strong> (constraints on what can be configured, such as allowed regions). Both are inherited by child folders, projects and resources <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q19\">Exam 2 · Q19</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q42\">Exam 3 · Q42</a>.</li>\n<li>Folders can contain projects <strong>or other folders</strong>, so a department folder can hold environment sub-folders. Folders can group projects by department, environment, application or business unit <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q19\">Exam 2 · Q19</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q42\">Exam 3 · Q42</a>.</li>\n<li>Inherited access applies unless something blocks it, such as an <strong>IAM deny policy</strong> or an organization policy constraint <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q42\">Exam 3 · Q42</a>.</li>\n<li>Policies are not limited to one level. They can be set at organization, folder, project or (for many services) resource level. The effective policy is the union of the policy at that level and everything inherited from above.</li>\n<li>The hierarchy also supports cost governance: projects and folders give a natural way to group spend by team <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q25\">Exam 3 · Q25</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Apply rules once for a team and have them apply to every project\" → <strong>set the policy on the team's folder</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q19\">Exam 2 · Q19</a>.</li>\n<li>\"What happens to a folder-level policy?\" → <strong>inherited by the projects and their resources</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q42\">Exam 3 · Q42</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Policies can only be applied at the organization level\" or \"only to individual projects\" are both false <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q19\">Exam 2 · Q19</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Inherited by projects but not their resources\" stops inheritance one level too early <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q42\">Exam 3 · Q42</a>.</li>\n<li>A folder policy does not spread sideways to other folders in the organization <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q42\">Exam 3 · Q42</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"The resource hierarchy (organization, folders, projects, resources) lets administrators set IAM and organization policies once and have them inherited downward.","objectives":["Name the levels of the Google Cloud resource hierarchy","Explain how folder-level policies are inherited","Use folders to apply rules to a whole team's projects"],"takeaways":["Organization → Folders → Projects → Resources.","A folder policy is inherited by every project in it, and by those projects' resources.","IAM allow policies (who can do what) and organization policies (what can be configured) both inherit downward. Deny policies can block inherited access.","Policies can be set at any level, not only organization or project.","Inheritance flows down, never sideways to other folders."],"questions":["e2q19","e3q25","e3q42"]},{"id":"d6l4","num":4,"title":"Measuring reliability: availability, SLIs, SLOs and SLAs","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Availability</strong> is expressed as a <strong>percentage of uptime</strong> (or of successful requests) over a period, such as a monthly uptime percentage in an SLA <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q22\">Exam 4 · Q22</a>.</li>\n<li>A count of incidents is not a measure of availability: one long outage and several short ones can cost very different amounts of uptime <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q22\">Exam 4 · Q22</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>SLI (service-level indicator):</strong> the <strong>direct measurement</strong> of how the service is actually performing, such as the share of successful requests or request latency <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q10\">Exam 2 · Q10</a>.</li>\n<li>SLIs can measure availability, latency, error rate or throughput <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q10\">Exam 2 · Q10</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>SLO (service-level objective):</strong> the <strong>target</strong> value for an SLI that the team wants to meet, over a set period. It can be an availability target (\"99.9% of requests succeed over 30 days\") or a latency target (\"95% of requests complete within 300 ms\") <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q9\">Exam 4 · Q9</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q10\">Exam 4 · Q10</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>SLA (service-level agreement):</strong> a <strong>contract</strong> with customers that promises a level of service and states the consequence (usually service credits) when it is missed <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q48\">Exam 2 · Q48</a>.</li>\n<li>Order of strictness: <strong>SLO is stricter than the SLA</strong>. The internal target is set above the contractual promise so the team reacts before customers are owed anything <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q20\">Exam 5 · Q20</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Nines to downtime per year:</strong> 99.999% (\"five nines\") allows about <strong>5 minutes</strong> a year <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q50\">Exam 1 · Q50</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Allowed downtime</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Availability</th>\n<th>Per year</th>\n<th>Per month (approx.)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>99%</td>\n<td>~3.65 days</td>\n<td>~7.3 hours</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>99.9%</td>\n<td>~8.8 hours</td>\n<td>~44 minutes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>99.95%</td>\n<td>~4.4 hours</td>\n<td>~22 minutes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>99.99%</td>\n<td>~53 minutes</td>\n<td>~4.4 minutes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>99.999%</td>\n<td>~5.3 minutes <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q50\">Exam 1 · Q50</a></td>\n<td>~26 seconds</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>SLI vs SLO vs SLA</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Term</th>\n<th>What it is</th>\n<th>Keyword in the question</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>SLI</td>\n<td>A measurement</td>\n<td>\"direct measurement of how it is performing\"</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q10\">Exam 2 · Q10</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>SLO</td>\n<td>An internal target</td>\n<td>\"desired reliability and performance target\"</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q9\">Exam 4 · Q9</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q10\">Exam 4 · Q10</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>SLA</td>\n<td>A contract with penalties</td>\n<td>\"promised to customers\", \"service credit\"</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q48\">Exam 2 · Q48</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q7\">Exam 1 · Q7</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Measure\" → SLI. \"Target\" → SLO. \"Contract / promise / credit\" → SLA <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q10\">Exam 2 · Q10</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q9\">Exam 4 · Q9</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q48\">Exam 2 · Q48</a>.</li>\n<li>Availability questions → <strong>percentage of uptime</strong>, not number of regions or incidents <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q22\">Exam 4 · Q22</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Enhanced support\" and \"scalable infrastructure\" are filler distractors in the SLO questions <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q9\">Exam 4 · Q9</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q10\">Exam 4 · Q10</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Error reporting\" is a tool, not an SLI <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q10\">Exam 2 · Q10</a>.</li>\n<li>Response time is <strong>latency</strong>, one possible SLI. It is not how availability is expressed. A service can be available but slow <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q22\">Exam 4 · Q22</a>.</li>\n<li>More regions can <strong>improve</strong> availability, but the number of regions is not how availability is <strong>measured</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q22\">Exam 4 · Q22</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"Reliability is measured with availability percentages and the SLI, SLO and SLA trio. This lesson defines each term and converts nines into allowed downtime.","objectives":["Distinguish SLI, SLO and SLA","Express availability as a percentage of uptime","Convert an availability target into allowed downtime"],"takeaways":["SLI is a measurement, SLO is an internal target, SLA is a contract.","Availability is a percentage of uptime.","An SLO can target availability or latency, for example 95% of requests within 300 ms.","Five nines (99.999%) allows about 5 minutes of downtime a year.","Counting incidents or regions is not how availability is measured.","The SLO is set stricter than the SLA so teams react before customers are owed credits."],"questions":["e1q7","e1q50","e2q10","e2q48","e4q9","e4q22","e5q20"]},{"id":"d6l5","num":5,"title":"Error budgets and missed targets","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Error budget = 100% − SLO.</strong> With a 99.9% SLO, the service may be unavailable 0.1% of the time. That budget can be \"spent\" on risky releases and experiments.</li>\n<li><strong>Budget left → innovate.</strong> Ship features and take reasonable risks <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q25\">Exam 4 · Q25</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Below the SLO → focus on reliability.</strong> Slow down feature releases and fix stability first. Being below the SLO but above the SLA means customers are not owed credits yet, but the team must react <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q20\">Exam 5 · Q20</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Provider misses its SLA → the customer typically gets a service credit.</strong> Fees don't go up, the service isn't shut down, and the provider's SLA has nothing to do with your internal error budget <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q48\">Exam 2 · Q48</a>.</li>\n<li>Google Cloud SLAs call these <strong>Financial Credits</strong>. They are applied to future bills for the affected service, not paid as cash, and the customer must claim them under the SLA's terms <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q48\">Exam 2 · Q48</a>.</li>\n<li>Below the SLO but above the SLA is a <strong>warning sign</strong>: the team is burning its reliability margin and must act before it breaches the contract <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q20\">Exam 5 · Q20</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Business impact of a missed SLA:</strong> unplanned downtime can make <strong>customers leave</strong>. Credits rarely cover lost revenue or trust <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q7\">Exam 1 · Q7</a>.</li>\n<li>The sensible response to a provider's missed SLA is to review your own architecture, redundancy and support terms, not to weaken the SLA <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q7\">Exam 1 · Q7</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Error budget remaining → prioritize <strong>innovation / feature velocity</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q25\">Exam 4 · Q25</a>.</li>\n<li>Below SLO (budget exhausted) → prioritize <strong>reliability</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q20\">Exam 5 · Q20</a>.</li>\n<li>Provider breaks SLA → <strong>service credit</strong> for you, <strong>customer churn</strong> risk for your business <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q48\">Exam 2 · Q48</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q7\">Exam 1 · Q7</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Automatic expansion of the error budget\" mixes up a provider's contract with your internal target <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q48\">Exam 2 · Q48</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Renegotiate the SLA so uptime matters less\" and \"data automatically deleted\" are nonsense distractors <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q7\">Exam 1 · Q7</a>.</li>\n<li>Dropping below the SLO does not mean customers see outages or lower latency. It means the team should shift effort to reliability. If latency is the SLI, missing the SLO means latency went <strong>up</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q20\">Exam 5 · Q20</a>.</li>\n<li>A missed <strong>availability</strong> SLA does not mean data is deleted. Outages make data unreachable for a while. Data loss is a <strong>durability</strong> question <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q7\">Exam 1 · Q7</a>.</li>\n<li>Service credits usually have to be <strong>claimed</strong> under the SLA's terms. \"Automatic\" is not a safe assumption, but the exam still keys \"service credit\" <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q48\">Exam 2 · Q48</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"Error budgets turn the SLO into a decision tool: spend the budget on innovation, or switch to reliability work when the SLO is missed. It also covers what happens when a provider misses its SLA.","objectives":["Calculate an error budget from an SLO","Decide between innovation and reliability work based on the error budget","Describe the consequences of a missed SLA for the provider and the customer"],"takeaways":["Error budget = 100% − SLO.","Budget remaining → prioritize innovation and feature velocity.","Below the SLO → focus on reliability.","A missed provider SLA typically earns the customer a service credit.","Google Cloud calls them Financial Credits: claimed by the customer and applied to future bills.","The real business risk of downtime is customers leaving.","A missed availability SLA means downtime, not deleted data."],"questions":["e1q7","e2q48","e4q25","e5q20"]},{"id":"d6l6","num":6,"title":"DevOps and SRE culture","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>DevOps</strong> brings development and operations together with shared tools and automation. It fixes <strong>repetitive manual tasks that slow down workflows</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q49\">Exam 2 · Q49</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Breaking silos</strong> works through <strong>shared responsibility for business results</strong>, not blame or merging everyone into one identical job <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q43\">Exam 6 · Q43</a>.</li>\n<li>The automation behind DevOps is <strong>CI/CD pipelines</strong> and <strong>automated testing</strong>: build, test and deploy steps run the same way every time, with less human error <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q49\">Exam 2 · Q49</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q17\">Exam 3 · Q17</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Small, incremental changes</strong> reduce recovery time when bugs appear. Small releases are easier to test, review and roll back, and fewer changes means the cause is found faster <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q17\">Exam 3 · Q17</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Gradual rollout (canary):</strong> release a new feature to a <strong>small group first</strong>, watch it, then expand. This limits the <strong>blast radius</strong> (how many users a failure can hit) <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q49\">Exam 3 · Q49</a>.</li>\n<li>Security or privacy concerns about a new feature call for review, testing and a limited rollout, not refusing to launch <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q49\">Exam 3 · Q49</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Automate toil.</strong> Toil is manual, repetitive, automatable operational work. SRE prioritizes automating it <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q35\">Exam 3 · Q35</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure is normal.</strong> SRE accepts that some failure will happen, so the team learns from bugs and incidents instead of ignoring or punishing them <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q50\">Exam 6 · Q50</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Blameless postmortems:</strong> after an incident, document what happened, why, and how to prevent it. The goal is learning, not reprimand <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q50\">Exam 6 · Q50</a>.</li>\n<li>SRE is data-driven: use metrics, logs, traces, SLOs and incident data, and measure the impact of outages to decide what to fix first <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q35\">Exam 3 · Q35</a>.</li>\n<li>Shared responsibility does not mean identical jobs. Developers and operations keep their skills but share goals and context <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q43\">Exam 6 · Q43</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Reduce manual work\" → <strong>automate toil</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q35\">Exam 3 · Q35</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q49\">Exam 2 · Q49</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Reduce disruption when bugs appear after a release\" → <strong>small incremental changes</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q17\">Exam 3 · Q17</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Release a new system with less risk\" → <strong>roll out to a small group first</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q49\">Exam 3 · Q49</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Minor bug after launch\" → <strong>accept and learn from it</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q50\">Exam 6 · Q50</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Dev and ops work in silos\" → <strong>shared responsibility</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q43\">Exam 6 · Q43</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Pause production until all bugs are gone\" and \"eliminate all future bugs\" ignore that failure is normal <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q17\">Exam 3 · Q17</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q50\">Exam 6 · Q50</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Large changes together make rollback easier\" is the opposite of the truth <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q17\">Exam 3 · Q17</a>.</li>\n<li>Bug priority follows user and business impact, not bug size. Large bugs are not \"easier to review\" <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q17\">Exam 3 · Q17</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Reduce the effort spent understanding the impact of outages\" is wrong. Outage impact drives SLO and error budget decisions <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q35\">Exam 3 · Q35</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Accept and <strong>ignore</strong>\" is wrong. The SRE answer is accept and <strong>learn</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q50\">Exam 6 · Q50</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Postmortem to reprimand\" breaks the blameless rule <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q50\">Exam 6 · Q50</a>. \"Identify who caused each incident and apply penalties\" fails for the same reason <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q43\">Exam 6 · Q43</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Give developers sole responsibility for production\" and \"rely less on operational data\" are not SRE practices <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q35\">Exam 3 · Q35</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Roll out to everyone immediately for maximum data\" maximizes risk. Refusing to roll out at all is also wrong <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q49\">Exam 3 · Q49</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"DevOps and SRE change how teams build and run software: shared responsibility, automation of toil, small incremental changes, gradual rollouts and blameless learning from failure.","objectives":["Explain how DevOps reduces manual work and silos","Apply small incremental changes and gradual rollouts to reduce release risk","Describe SRE attitudes to toil, failure and postmortems"],"takeaways":["DevOps removes repetitive manual tasks through CI/CD pipelines, automated tests and other tooling.","Silos are fixed by shared responsibility for business results, not blame.","Small incremental changes shorten recovery when bugs appear.","Roll out new features to a small group first to limit the blast radius.","Automate toil.","Failure is normal: accept and learn from it in blameless postmortems."],"questions":["e2q49","e3q17","e3q35","e3q49","e6q43","e6q50"]},{"id":"d6l7","num":7,"title":"Observability, monitoring, logging and the Four Golden Signals","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Observability</strong> means having visibility into the health, performance and behavior of systems, using <strong>metrics, logs and traces</strong>. \"Out-of-the-box observability\" means <strong>monitoring IT infrastructure and applications</strong> with built-in tooling you don't have to build yourself <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q14\">Exam 1 · Q14</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Monitoring</strong> = <strong>collecting predefined and custom metrics</strong> (CPU, latency, request count, app-specific values) from applications and infrastructure. Predefined (system) metrics come from Google Cloud services. Custom (user-defined) metrics come from your own code <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q11\">Exam 3 · Q11</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Logging</strong> = <strong>recording application and operating system events as timestamped entries</strong> (plain text or structured), so you can review what happened later <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q19\">Exam 6 · Q19</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Four Golden Signals</strong> (from Google's SRE book):</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Signal</th>\n<th>Measures</th>\n<th>Exam wording</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Latency</strong></td>\n<td>Time to serve a request</td>\n<td>\"users wait a long time\", \"time between request and response\"</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q45\">Exam 1 · Q45</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q18\">Exam 3 · Q18</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Traffic</strong></td>\n<td>Demand on the system</td>\n<td>requests or transactions per second, active users</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q45\">Exam 1 · Q45</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q13\">Exam 3 · Q13</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Errors</strong></td>\n<td>Rate of failed requests</td>\n<td>HTTP 5xx, failed calls, timeouts, wrong responses</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q45\">Exam 1 · Q45</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q18\">Exam 3 · Q18</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Saturation</strong></td>\n<td>How full the system is</td>\n<td>\"how much capacity is used\", CPU, memory or I/O constrained</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q13\">Exam 3 · Q13</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Wait time\" / \"response time\" → <strong>latency</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q45\">Exam 1 · Q45</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q18\">Exam 3 · Q18</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Capacity used\" / \"resources constrained\" → <strong>saturation</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q13\">Exam 3 · Q13</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Collect metrics\" → monitoring <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q11\">Exam 3 · Q11</a>. \"Record events\" → logging <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q19\">Exam 6 · Q19</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Monitoring billing charges, release schedules or customer ratings is not what cloud observability means <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q14\">Exam 1 · Q14</a>.</li>\n<li>Watching spend in real time is a budget task, not \"monitoring\" in the ops sense <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q11\">Exam 3 · Q11</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Monitoring network and resource limitations\" describes monitoring/saturation, not logging <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q19\">Exam 6 · Q19</a>.</li>\n<li>Traffic is volume of demand, not how long one request takes. A service can have high traffic with low latency, or the reverse <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q45\">Exam 1 · Q45</a>.</li>\n<li>Errors means requests <strong>fail</strong>. A slow request that still returns a result is a latency problem <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q45\">Exam 1 · Q45</a>.</li>\n<li>Saturation can be high while the service still responds. It warns that the system is close to overload <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q13\">Exam 3 · Q13</a>.</li>\n<li>Recording user actions for compliance is <strong>audit logging</strong> (Cloud Audit Logs), not monitoring <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q11\">Exam 3 · Q11</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"Observability means visibility into infrastructure and applications through metrics and logs. The Four Golden Signals (latency, traffic, errors, saturation) are the core metrics SRE watches.","objectives":["Define observability, monitoring and logging","Name the Four Golden Signals","Map a symptom to the right golden signal"],"takeaways":["Out-of-the-box observability = monitoring IT infrastructure and applications.","Monitoring collects predefined (system) and custom (user-defined) metrics.","Logging records application and operating system events.","Latency = how long a request takes. Saturation = how full capacity is.","Traffic is demand, errors is the failure rate.","Recording user actions for compliance is audit logging, not monitoring."],"questions":["e1q14","e1q45","e3q11","e3q13","e3q18","e6q19"]},{"id":"d6l8","num":8,"title":"Google Cloud Observability tools","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cloud Monitoring:</strong> the managed place for <strong>metrics, dashboards, alerting and uptime checks</strong>. Also collects resource metadata for those views <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q43\">Exam 1 · Q43</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q15\">Exam 5 · Q15</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Cloud Logging:</strong> <strong>collect, store, search, analyze and route logs</strong> from GKE, VMs, managed services and custom app entries in one central place <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q43\">Exam 3 · Q43</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q7\">Exam 4 · Q7</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q27\">Exam 5 · Q27</a>. It speeds up troubleshooting by letting you analyze logs <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q10\">Exam 6 · Q10</a>.</li>\n<li>GKE sends container logs to Cloud Logging by default. VMs send logs through the <strong>Ops Agent</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q43\">Exam 3 · Q43</a>.</li>\n<li>You search and filter logs (by resource, container, severity, time, text) in <strong>Logs Explorer</strong>, which you open from the Console <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q7\">Exam 4 · Q7</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q10\">Exam 6 · Q10</a>.</li>\n<li>Monitoring shows <strong>that</strong> something is wrong. Logging shows <strong>what happened</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q7\">Exam 4 · Q7</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Cloud Audit Logs</strong> (inside Cloud Logging) record admin activity and data access, for \"who did what\" and compliance questions <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q11\">Exam 3 · Q11</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Cloud Trace:</strong> follows <strong>one request across microservices</strong> and shows the time spent at each step (distributed tracing, latency). A trace is made of <strong>spans</strong>, one per unit of work such as a service call or database query <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q14\">Exam 3 · Q14</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Error Reporting:</strong> automatically <strong>collects, groups and shows crashes and exceptions</strong>, with stack traces, counts and first/last-seen times, so developers don't dig through raw logs <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q48\">Exam 3 · Q48</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Cloud Profiler:</strong> continuously collects <strong>CPU and memory-allocation</strong> data from production code with low overhead, and shows <strong>which functions consume the most resources</strong> (often as flame graphs). It is the tool when one service is driving up cloud costs and you need the code responsible <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q50\">Exam 3 · Q50</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Cloud Debugger:</strong> let developers <strong>inspect the state of running code in real time without stopping it</strong> or causing user downtime <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q1\">Exam 3 · Q1</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q26\">Exam 5 · Q26</a>. Google shut Cloud Debugger down in 2023. Its open-source successor is Snapshot Debugger (see ⚠️).</li>\n<li>These tools are grouped under <strong>Google Cloud Observability</strong> (formerly the \"operations suite\", and before that Stackdriver).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Which tool?</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Question says</th>\n<th>Answer</th>\n<th>Common distractor</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Dashboards, alerts, uptime checks, metrics</td>\n<td>✅ Cloud Monitoring</td>\n<td>❌ Logging, Trace, Profiler</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q43\">Exam 1 · Q43</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q15\">Exam 5 · Q15</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Collect, store, search logs in one place</td>\n<td>✅ Cloud Logging</td>\n<td>❌ Monitoring, Cloud SDK, Data Catalog, Console</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q43\">Exam 3 · Q43</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q7\">Exam 4 · Q7</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q27\">Exam 5 · Q27</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q10\">Exam 6 · Q10</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>One request across many microservices</td>\n<td>✅ Cloud Trace</td>\n<td>❌ Profiler, Logging</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q14\">Exam 3 · Q14</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Group crashes and exceptions automatically</td>\n<td>✅ Error Reporting</td>\n<td>❌ Profiler, Trace, Monitoring</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q48\">Exam 3 · Q48</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Which code function burns CPU/memory</td>\n<td>✅ Cloud Profiler</td>\n<td>❌ Trace, Logging</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q50\">Exam 3 · Q50</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Inspect live code state without downtime</td>\n<td>✅ Cloud Debugger (retired)</td>\n<td>❌ Profiler, Trace</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q1\">Exam 3 · Q1</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q26\">Exam 5 · Q26</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Who did what, for compliance</td>\n<td>✅ Cloud Audit Logs</td>\n<td>❌ Monitoring</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q11\">Exam 3 · Q11</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Keyword → tool: <strong>metrics/dashboards/alerts</strong> = Monitoring, <strong>logs</strong> = Logging, <strong>request path/latency across services</strong> = Trace, <strong>exceptions</strong> = Error Reporting, <strong>CPU/memory by function</strong> = Profiler, <strong>live code state</strong> = Debugger.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Trace vs Profiler: Trace is about <strong>time across services</strong> for a request. Profiler is about <strong>resource use inside the code</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q14\">Exam 3 · Q14</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q50\">Exam 3 · Q50</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Search log entries across containers\" is Logging, even though it's GKE. GKE itself and the Console are not log tools <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q7\">Exam 4 · Q7</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q14\">Exam 3 · Q14</a>.</li>\n<li>Cloud Logging does not \"analyze live source code\", deploy infrastructure (that's Terraform or Infrastructure Manager) or store VM images (that's Compute Engine) <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q10\">Exam 6 · Q10</a>.</li>\n<li>Off-topic distractors for logging: <strong>Dialogflow</strong> builds chatbots, <strong>Cloud SDK</strong> is the <code>gcloud</code> command-line tooling, <strong>Data Catalog</strong> is metadata and data discovery <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q43\">Exam 3 · Q43</a>.</li>\n<li>Trying to follow one request by correlating logs from every service by hand is the slow way. That's what Trace is for <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q14\">Exam 3 · Q14</a>.</li>\n<li>Debugger never changed code in production or required taking the app offline <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q1\">Exam 3 · Q1</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"Google Cloud Observability provides one tool per job: Monitoring, Logging (with Logs Explorer and Cloud Audit Logs), Trace, Error Reporting, Profiler and the retired Debugger. The exam tests matching a need to the tool.","objectives":["Match an operations need to the right Google Cloud Observability tool","Tell Cloud Trace apart from Cloud Profiler","Recognize Cloud Logging as the central log service"],"takeaways":["Metrics, dashboards, alerts, uptime checks → Cloud Monitoring.","Collect, store and search logs in one place → Cloud Logging.","Search logs in Logs Explorer. VMs send logs through the Ops Agent, GKE sends them by default.","Who did what, for compliance → Cloud Audit Logs.","One request across microservices → Cloud Trace.","Crashes and exceptions grouped automatically → Error Reporting.","CPU and memory use per code function → Cloud Profiler.","Live code state without downtime → Cloud Debugger, now retired.","Dialogflow, Cloud SDK and Data Catalog are not logging tools."],"questions":["e1q43","e3q1","e3q11","e3q14","e3q43","e3q48","e3q50","e4q7","e5q26","e5q27","e6q10"]},{"id":"d6l9","num":9,"title":"Resilience, scaling and sustainability","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Redundancy</strong> = copies of important components across multiple resources so the system keeps running when one fails. The goal is <strong>no single point of failure</strong>: for example, several instances behind a load balancer, spread over more than one zone <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q40\">Exam 4 · Q40</a>.</li>\n<li>Redundancy vs <strong>backups</strong>: backups let you <strong>restore</strong> data after a loss. Redundancy keeps the service <strong>running</strong> during a failure <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q40\">Exam 4 · Q40</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Availability vs durability:</strong> availability is whether the service is reachable. Durability is whether stored data survives. An outage does not delete data. Backups, replication and disaster recovery protect against data loss <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q7\">Exam 1 · Q7</a>.</li>\n<li><em>(not in the question bank)</em> Resilience patterns: spread workloads across <strong>zones and regions</strong>, use managed instance groups with <strong>autoscaling</strong> and load balancing, replicate data, and plan <strong>disaster recovery</strong> with recovery time and recovery point objectives (RTO/RPO).</li>\n<li><strong>Scaling sustainably:</strong> move bursty demand to a <strong>public cloud provider with energy-efficient data centers</strong>. You get fast capacity and a lower carbon footprint at once <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q32\">Exam 6 · Q32</a>.</li>\n<li><em>(not in the question bank)</em> Google has been carbon neutral for its operations since 2007, matches its electricity use with renewable energy, and aims to run on 24/7 carbon-free energy by 2030. The <strong>Carbon Footprint</strong> tool reports emissions from your Google Cloud usage, and the region picker marks lower-carbon regions.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Keep operating if one component fails\" → <strong>redundancy</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q40\">Exam 4 · Q40</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Scale fast + sustainability goals\" → <strong>cloud provider with energy-efficient data centers</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q32\">Exam 6 · Q32</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Buying efficient servers or switching the on-premises energy supplier doesn't solve the \"scale quickly\" half. You're still limited by your own space, power, cooling and procurement time <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q32\">Exam 6 · Q32</a>.</li>\n<li>Rewriting code to use less energy is slow and doesn't add capacity <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q32\">Exam 6 · Q32</a>.</li>\n<li>Latency and security are not resilience design choices in this question <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q40\">Exam 4 · Q40</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"Resilient systems use redundancy so they keep running when a component fails. Moving to an energy-efficient public cloud lets organizations scale quickly while meeting sustainability goals.","objectives":["Define redundancy and distinguish it from backups and durability","Outline resilience patterns across zones and regions","Explain how public cloud supports sustainability goals"],"takeaways":["Redundancy = copies of components so there is no single point of failure.","Backups restore data, redundancy keeps the service running.","Availability is whether the service is reachable. Durability is whether data survives.","Spread workloads across zones and regions and use autoscaling.","Scale fast and sustainably with a cloud provider's energy-efficient data centers.","The Carbon Footprint tool reports emissions from your Google Cloud usage."],"questions":["e1q7","e4q40","e6q32"]},{"id":"d6l10","num":10,"title":"Google Cloud Customer Care","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Premium Support</strong> is the tier with the <strong>fastest response for critical (P1) issues</strong> and a <strong>dedicated Technical Account Manager (TAM)</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q21\">Exam 3 · Q21</a>.</li>\n<li><em>(not in the question bank)</em> Tiers in order:</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Tier</th>\n<th>Typical fit</th>\n<th>Notable features</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Basic</td>\n<td>Everyone, free</td>\n<td>Billing support, docs, community. No technical cases</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Standard</td>\n<td>Dev/test workloads</td>\n<td>Technical support during business hours, about 4-hour response for P2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Enhanced</td>\n<td>Production workloads</td>\n<td>24/7 for high-impact issues, about 1-hour response for P1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Premium</td>\n<td>Business-critical workloads</td>\n<td>About 15-minute P1 response, <strong>Technical Account Manager</strong>, proactive guidance <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q21\">Exam 3 · Q21</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<ul>\n<li><em>(not in the question bank)</em> Support cases use <strong>priority levels</strong> (P1 critical to P4 low). Set the priority by business impact. Cases can be escalated if the response doesn't match the impact.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Fastest response\" + \"dedicated TAM\" → <strong>Premium</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q21\">Exam 3 · Q21</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Enhanced is production-grade but has <strong>no TAM</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q21\">Exam 3 · Q21</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"Google Cloud Customer Care offers tiered support, from free Basic to Premium with the fastest response and a Technical Account Manager.","objectives":["List the Customer Care tiers in order","Pick the tier that matches a workload's criticality","Explain support case priorities"],"takeaways":["Basic → Standard → Enhanced → Premium.","Premium gives the fastest P1 response and a dedicated Technical Account Manager.","Enhanced is production-grade but has no TAM.","Set case priority by business impact."],"questions":["e3q21"]}],"ref":{"cheat":"<ul>\n<li>Cloud spending is <strong>decentralized and variable</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q11\">Exam 2 · Q11</a>.</li>\n<li>Teams overspend without knowing → <strong>share cost views per department</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q25\">Exam 3 · Q25</a>.</li>\n<li>Variable demand, optimize resources → <strong>intelligent recommendations</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q36\">Exam 3 · Q36</a>.</li>\n<li>Alert when spend crosses a limit → <strong>budget + threshold rule</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q11\">Exam 1 · Q11</a>.</li>\n<li>Estimate before deploying → <strong>Pricing Calculator</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q36\">Exam 2 · Q36</a>.</li>\n<li>SUDs are <strong>automatic</strong> after 25% of the month. 1/3-year commitments are CUDs <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q1\">Exam 6 · Q1</a>.</li>\n<li>Folder policy → inherited by <strong>projects and their resources</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q19\">Exam 2 · Q19</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q42\">Exam 3 · Q42</a>.</li>\n<li>Availability = <strong>% uptime</strong>. Five nines ≈ <strong>5 minutes/year</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q22\">Exam 4 · Q22</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q50\">Exam 1 · Q50</a>.</li>\n<li>SLI = measurement, SLO = target, SLA = contract <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q10\">Exam 2 · Q10</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q9\">Exam 4 · Q9</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q10\">Exam 4 · Q10</a>.</li>\n<li>Budget left → innovate. Below SLO → reliability <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q25\">Exam 4 · Q25</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q20\">Exam 5 · Q20</a>.</li>\n<li>Provider misses SLA → <strong>service credit</strong>, your risk is <strong>customer churn</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q48\">Exam 2 · Q48</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q7\">Exam 1 · Q7</a>.</li>\n<li>DevOps removes <strong>repetitive manual tasks</strong>. Silos fixed by <strong>shared responsibility</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q49\">Exam 2 · Q49</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q43\">Exam 6 · Q43</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Small incremental changes</strong>, <strong>roll out to a small group first</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q17\">Exam 3 · Q17</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q49\">Exam 3 · Q49</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Automate toil</strong>. Failure is normal: <strong>accept and learn</strong>, blameless postmortems <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q35\">Exam 3 · Q35</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q50\">Exam 6 · Q50</a>.</li>\n<li>Observability = monitoring <strong>infrastructure and applications</strong>. Monitoring = <strong>metrics</strong>. Logging = <strong>events recorded</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q14\">Exam 1 · Q14</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q11\">Exam 3 · Q11</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q19\">Exam 6 · Q19</a>.</li>\n<li>Wait time = <strong>latency</strong>, capacity used = <strong>saturation</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q45\">Exam 1 · Q45</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q18\">Exam 3 · Q18</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q13\">Exam 3 · Q13</a>.</li>\n<li>Dashboards/alerts/uptime checks → <strong>Cloud Monitoring</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q43\">Exam 1 · Q43</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q15\">Exam 5 · Q15</a>.</li>\n<li>Logs in one place → <strong>Cloud Logging</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q43\">Exam 3 · Q43</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q7\">Exam 4 · Q7</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q27\">Exam 5 · Q27</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q10\">Exam 6 · Q10</a>. Who did what → <strong>Cloud Audit Logs</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q11\">Exam 3 · Q11</a>.</li>\n<li>Request across microservices → <strong>Trace</strong>. Exceptions grouped → <strong>Error Reporting</strong>. CPU/memory per function → <strong>Profiler</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q14\">Exam 3 · Q14</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q48\">Exam 3 · Q48</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q50\">Exam 3 · Q50</a>.</li>\n<li>Live code state without downtime → <strong>Debugger</strong> (retired) <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q1\">Exam 3 · Q1</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q26\">Exam 5 · Q26</a>.</li>\n<li>Keep running if a component fails → <strong>redundancy</strong>, no single point of failure <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q40\">Exam 4 · Q40</a>. Outage ≠ data loss <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q7\">Exam 1 · Q7</a>.</li>\n<li>Scale fast + sustainability → <strong>cloud provider with energy-efficient data centers</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q32\">Exam 6 · Q32</a>.</li>\n<li>Fastest response + TAM → <strong>Premium</strong> support <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q21\">Exam 3 · Q21</a>.</li>\n</ul>","rules":[{"lesson":"d6l1","title":"Cloud financial governance (FinOps)","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"Teams don't see how their usage affects the bill\" → <strong>share cost views / reports per team</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q25\">Exam 3 · Q25</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Costs vary month to month, optimize resource use\" → <strong>intelligent recommendations</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q36\">Exam 3 · Q36</a>.</li>\n<li>\"What is cloud spending like?\" → <strong>decentralized and variable</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q11\">Exam 2 · Q11</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d6l2","title":"Cost-control tools: budgets, estimates and discounts","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"Notify when costs cross X\" → <strong>budget threshold rule and alert</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q11\">Exam 1 · Q11</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Estimate before deploying\" → <strong>Pricing Calculator</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q36\">Exam 2 · Q36</a>.</li>\n<li>\"How do we get sustained use discounts?\" → <strong>do nothing, they are automatic</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q1\">Exam 6 · Q1</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d6l3","title":"Resource hierarchy and policy inheritance","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"Apply rules once for a team and have them apply to every project\" → <strong>set the policy on the team's folder</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q19\">Exam 2 · Q19</a>.</li>\n<li>\"What happens to a folder-level policy?\" → <strong>inherited by the projects and their resources</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q42\">Exam 3 · Q42</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d6l4","title":"Measuring reliability: availability, SLIs, SLOs and SLAs","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"Measure\" → SLI. \"Target\" → SLO. \"Contract / promise / credit\" → SLA <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q10\">Exam 2 · Q10</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q9\">Exam 4 · Q9</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q48\">Exam 2 · Q48</a>.</li>\n<li>Availability questions → <strong>percentage of uptime</strong>, not number of regions or incidents <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q22\">Exam 4 · Q22</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d6l5","title":"Error budgets and missed targets","html":"<ul>\n<li>Error budget remaining → prioritize <strong>innovation / feature velocity</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q25\">Exam 4 · Q25</a>.</li>\n<li>Below SLO (budget exhausted) → prioritize <strong>reliability</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q20\">Exam 5 · Q20</a>.</li>\n<li>Provider breaks SLA → <strong>service credit</strong> for you, <strong>customer churn</strong> risk for your business <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q48\">Exam 2 · Q48</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q7\">Exam 1 · Q7</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d6l6","title":"DevOps and SRE culture","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"Reduce manual work\" → <strong>automate toil</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q35\">Exam 3 · Q35</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q49\">Exam 2 · Q49</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Reduce disruption when bugs appear after a release\" → <strong>small incremental changes</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q17\">Exam 3 · Q17</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Release a new system with less risk\" → <strong>roll out to a small group first</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q49\">Exam 3 · Q49</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Minor bug after launch\" → <strong>accept and learn from it</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q50\">Exam 6 · Q50</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Dev and ops work in silos\" → <strong>shared responsibility</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q43\">Exam 6 · Q43</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d6l7","title":"Observability, monitoring, logging and the Four Golden Signals","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"Wait time\" / \"response time\" → <strong>latency</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q45\">Exam 1 · Q45</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q18\">Exam 3 · Q18</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Capacity used\" / \"resources constrained\" → <strong>saturation</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q13\">Exam 3 · Q13</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Collect metrics\" → monitoring <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q11\">Exam 3 · Q11</a>. \"Record events\" → logging <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q19\">Exam 6 · Q19</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d6l8","title":"Google Cloud Observability tools","html":"<ul>\n<li>Keyword → tool: <strong>metrics/dashboards/alerts</strong> = Monitoring, <strong>logs</strong> = Logging, <strong>request path/latency across services</strong> = Trace, <strong>exceptions</strong> = Error Reporting, <strong>CPU/memory by function</strong> = Profiler, <strong>live code state</strong> = Debugger.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d6l9","title":"Resilience, scaling and sustainability","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"Keep operating if one component fails\" → <strong>redundancy</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q40\">Exam 4 · Q40</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Scale fast + sustainability goals\" → <strong>cloud provider with energy-efficient data centers</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q32\">Exam 6 · Q32</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d6l10","title":"Google Cloud Customer Care","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"Fastest response\" + \"dedicated TAM\" → <strong>Premium</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q21\">Exam 3 · Q21</a>.</li>\n</ul>"}],"traps":[{"lesson":"d6l1","title":"Cloud financial governance (FinOps)","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"Centralized and fixed\" describes the old on-premises model <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q11\">Exam 2 · Q11</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Decentralize financial decisions\" or \"share project ownership across all teams\" sounds collaborative but removes accountability <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q36\">Exam 3 · Q36</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Increase capital expenditure\" or \"improve physical server purchasing\" are on-premises answers <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q25\">Exam 3 · Q25</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q36\">Exam 3 · Q36</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d6l2","title":"Cost-control tools: budgets, estimates and discounts","html":"<ul>\n<li>Reviewing past bills looks backward. The question wants a proactive alert <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q11\">Exam 1 · Q11</a>.</li>\n<li>Stopping VMs outside peak hours is cost <strong>optimization</strong>. It does not notify anyone when spend crosses a limit <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q11\">Exam 1 · Q11</a>.</li>\n<li>Historical costs of a \"similar\" workload are only a rough guide. The new workload may use different services, regions or sizes <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q36\">Exam 2 · Q36</a>.</li>\n<li>Deploying a test environment \"to observe costs\" spends money to get an estimate the calculator gives for free <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q36\">Exam 2 · Q36</a>.</li>\n<li>A 1- or 3-year commitment is the <strong>CUD</strong> answer, not the SUD answer <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q1\">Exam 6 · Q1</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d6l3","title":"Resource hierarchy and policy inheritance","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"Policies can only be applied at the organization level\" or \"only to individual projects\" are both false <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q19\">Exam 2 · Q19</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Inherited by projects but not their resources\" stops inheritance one level too early <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q42\">Exam 3 · Q42</a>.</li>\n<li>A folder policy does not spread sideways to other folders in the organization <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q42\">Exam 3 · Q42</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d6l4","title":"Measuring reliability: availability, SLIs, SLOs and SLAs","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"Enhanced support\" and \"scalable infrastructure\" are filler distractors in the SLO questions <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q9\">Exam 4 · Q9</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q10\">Exam 4 · Q10</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Error reporting\" is a tool, not an SLI <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q10\">Exam 2 · Q10</a>.</li>\n<li>Response time is <strong>latency</strong>, one possible SLI. It is not how availability is expressed. A service can be available but slow <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q22\">Exam 4 · Q22</a>.</li>\n<li>More regions can <strong>improve</strong> availability, but the number of regions is not how availability is <strong>measured</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q22\">Exam 4 · Q22</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d6l5","title":"Error budgets and missed targets","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"Automatic expansion of the error budget\" mixes up a provider's contract with your internal target <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q48\">Exam 2 · Q48</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Renegotiate the SLA so uptime matters less\" and \"data automatically deleted\" are nonsense distractors <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q7\">Exam 1 · Q7</a>.</li>\n<li>Dropping below the SLO does not mean customers see outages or lower latency. It means the team should shift effort to reliability. If latency is the SLI, missing the SLO means latency went <strong>up</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q20\">Exam 5 · Q20</a>.</li>\n<li>A missed <strong>availability</strong> SLA does not mean data is deleted. Outages make data unreachable for a while. Data loss is a <strong>durability</strong> question <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q7\">Exam 1 · Q7</a>.</li>\n<li>Service credits usually have to be <strong>claimed</strong> under the SLA's terms. \"Automatic\" is not a safe assumption, but the exam still keys \"service credit\" <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q48\">Exam 2 · Q48</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d6l6","title":"DevOps and SRE culture","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"Pause production until all bugs are gone\" and \"eliminate all future bugs\" ignore that failure is normal <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q17\">Exam 3 · Q17</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q50\">Exam 6 · Q50</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Large changes together make rollback easier\" is the opposite of the truth <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q17\">Exam 3 · Q17</a>.</li>\n<li>Bug priority follows user and business impact, not bug size. Large bugs are not \"easier to review\" <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q17\">Exam 3 · Q17</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Reduce the effort spent understanding the impact of outages\" is wrong. Outage impact drives SLO and error budget decisions <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q35\">Exam 3 · Q35</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Accept and <strong>ignore</strong>\" is wrong. The SRE answer is accept and <strong>learn</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q50\">Exam 6 · Q50</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Postmortem to reprimand\" breaks the blameless rule <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q50\">Exam 6 · Q50</a>. \"Identify who caused each incident and apply penalties\" fails for the same reason <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q43\">Exam 6 · Q43</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Give developers sole responsibility for production\" and \"rely less on operational data\" are not SRE practices <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q35\">Exam 3 · Q35</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Roll out to everyone immediately for maximum data\" maximizes risk. Refusing to roll out at all is also wrong <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q49\">Exam 3 · Q49</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d6l7","title":"Observability, monitoring, logging and the Four Golden Signals","html":"<ul>\n<li>Monitoring billing charges, release schedules or customer ratings is not what cloud observability means <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q14\">Exam 1 · Q14</a>.</li>\n<li>Watching spend in real time is a budget task, not \"monitoring\" in the ops sense <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q11\">Exam 3 · Q11</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Monitoring network and resource limitations\" describes monitoring/saturation, not logging <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q19\">Exam 6 · Q19</a>.</li>\n<li>Traffic is volume of demand, not how long one request takes. A service can have high traffic with low latency, or the reverse <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q45\">Exam 1 · Q45</a>.</li>\n<li>Errors means requests <strong>fail</strong>. A slow request that still returns a result is a latency problem <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q45\">Exam 1 · Q45</a>.</li>\n<li>Saturation can be high while the service still responds. It warns that the system is close to overload <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q13\">Exam 3 · Q13</a>.</li>\n<li>Recording user actions for compliance is <strong>audit logging</strong> (Cloud Audit Logs), not monitoring <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q11\">Exam 3 · Q11</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d6l8","title":"Google Cloud Observability tools","html":"<ul>\n<li>Trace vs Profiler: Trace is about <strong>time across services</strong> for a request. Profiler is about <strong>resource use inside the code</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q14\">Exam 3 · Q14</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q50\">Exam 3 · Q50</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Search log entries across containers\" is Logging, even though it's GKE. GKE itself and the Console are not log tools <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q7\">Exam 4 · Q7</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q14\">Exam 3 · Q14</a>.</li>\n<li>Cloud Logging does not \"analyze live source code\", deploy infrastructure (that's Terraform or Infrastructure Manager) or store VM images (that's Compute Engine) <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q10\">Exam 6 · Q10</a>.</li>\n<li>Off-topic distractors for logging: <strong>Dialogflow</strong> builds chatbots, <strong>Cloud SDK</strong> is the <code>gcloud</code> command-line tooling, <strong>Data Catalog</strong> is metadata and data discovery <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q43\">Exam 3 · Q43</a>.</li>\n<li>Trying to follow one request by correlating logs from every service by hand is the slow way. That's what Trace is for <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q14\">Exam 3 · Q14</a>.</li>\n<li>Debugger never changed code in production or required taking the app offline <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q1\">Exam 3 · Q1</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d6l9","title":"Resilience, scaling and sustainability","html":"<ul>\n<li>Buying efficient servers or switching the on-premises energy supplier doesn't solve the \"scale quickly\" half. You're still limited by your own space, power, cooling and procurement time <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q32\">Exam 6 · Q32</a>.</li>\n<li>Rewriting code to use less energy is slow and doesn't add capacity <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e6q32\">Exam 6 · Q32</a>.</li>\n<li>Latency and security are not resilience design choices in this question <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e4q40\">Exam 4 · Q40</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d6l10","title":"Google Cloud Customer Care","html":"<ul>\n<li>Enhanced is production-grade but has <strong>no TAM</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q21\">Exam 3 · Q21</a>.</li>\n</ul>"}],"questionable":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Item</th>\n<th>Issue</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e3q1\">Exam 3 · Q1</a></strong>, <strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q26\">Exam 5 · Q26</a></strong></td>\n<td>Cloud Debugger was deprecated in 2022 and shut down in May 2023 (successor: open-source Snapshot Debugger). The answers describe what it did correctly, but the product no longer exists, and <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e5q26\">Exam 5 · Q26</a> asks which tool \"should\" be used today.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e2q48\">Exam 2 · Q48</a></strong></td>\n<td>Service credits under Google Cloud SLAs usually must be <strong>requested</strong> by the customer within a time window. \"Service credit\" is the right answer, but it is not automatic.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/gcdl/q/e1q7\">Exam 1 · Q7</a></strong></td>\n<td>Keys churn risk (C), which is right. Option A (\"use up its internal error budget\") is not absurd either, since provider downtime does count against your own SLO. C is the best \"business impact\" answer.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"},"glossary":[{"term":"Active Assist","definition":"Google Cloud's family of intelligent recommendations (Recommender), such as rightsizing VMs and deleting idle resources.","exam_context":"Variable demand and hard-to-control costs → apply intelligent recommendations.","lesson":"d6l1","domain":6},{"term":"Availability","definition":"The share of time a service is up and usable, expressed as a percentage of uptime.","exam_context":"Expressed as percentage of uptime. Five nines (99.999%) ≈ 5 minutes of downtime a year.","lesson":"d6l4","domain":6},{"term":"Blameless postmortem","definition":"A written review after an incident that records what happened, why, and how to prevent it, without blaming individuals.","exam_context":"SRE answer to a bug or incident: learn from it. Reprimanding the person responsible is a distractor.","lesson":"d6l6","domain":6},{"term":"Budget alert","definition":"A Cloud Billing budget with threshold rules that sends notifications when actual or forecasted spend crosses set percentages.","exam_context":"Answer for 'notify us when costs cross a limit'. Quotas and past billing reports are distractors.","lesson":"d6l2","domain":6},{"term":"Canary release","definition":"Releasing a change to a small group of users first, watching it, then expanding. Limits the blast radius.","exam_context":"SRE answer for releasing a new feature with less risk. Not everyone at once, and not refusing to launch.","lesson":"d6l6","domain":6},{"term":"CI/CD","definition":"Continuous integration and continuous delivery: automated pipelines that build, test and deploy code changes.","exam_context":"The DevOps automation that removes repetitive manual tasks and makes small, frequent releases possible.","lesson":"d6l6","domain":6},{"term":"Cloud Audit Logs","definition":"Logs in Cloud Logging that record admin activity and data access in Google Cloud.","exam_context":"Who did what, for compliance. Not the same as metric monitoring.","lesson":"d6l8","domain":6},{"term":"Cloud Debugger","definition":"A retired tool that let developers inspect the state of running code in real time without stopping the app.","exam_context":"Keyed for 'inspect live code state without downtime'. Shut down in 2023, successor is Snapshot Debugger.","lesson":"d6l8","domain":6},{"term":"Cloud Logging","definition":"Managed service that collects, stores, searches and analyzes logs from Google Cloud resources and applications.","exam_context":"Any 'collect, store, search logs in one place' or 'search log entries across containers' question.","lesson":"d6l8","domain":6},{"term":"Cloud Monitoring","definition":"Managed service for metrics, dashboards, alerting and uptime checks.","exam_context":"Answer for dashboards, alerts and uptime checks in one place. Not Trace, Logging or Profiler.","lesson":"d6l8","domain":6},{"term":"Cloud Profiler","definition":"Continuous, low-overhead profiler that shows CPU and memory use per code function in production.","exam_context":"Pick it when the question asks which functions consume the most resources.","lesson":"d6l8","domain":6},{"term":"Cloud Trace","definition":"Distributed tracing tool that follows a single request across services. Each trace is made of spans showing time spent at each step.","exam_context":"Pick it for slow requests across microservices. Not Profiler.","lesson":"d6l8","domain":6},{"term":"Committed use discount (CUD)","definition":"A discount in exchange for committing to a minimum level of resource usage or spend for 1 or 3 years.","exam_context":"The 1/3-year commitment option is a CUD, not the way to get sustained use discounts.","lesson":"d6l2","domain":6},{"term":"DevOps","definition":"A set of practices that unites development and operations through shared tools, automation and responsibility.","exam_context":"Solves repetitive manual tasks and team silos.","lesson":"d6l6","domain":6},{"term":"Durability","definition":"Whether stored data survives over time, protected by replication, backups and disaster recovery.","exam_context":"An availability outage makes data unreachable for a while. It doesn't delete it.","lesson":"d6l9","domain":6},{"term":"Error budget","definition":"The amount of unreliability an SLO allows: 100% minus the SLO.","exam_context":"Budget left → innovate. SLO missed → focus on reliability.","lesson":"d6l5","domain":6},{"term":"Error Reporting","definition":"Tool that automatically collects, groups and displays application crashes and exceptions.","exam_context":"Pick it when developers don't want to dig through raw logs for exceptions.","lesson":"d6l8","domain":6},{"term":"Errors","definition":"The rate of failed requests, such as HTTP 5xx responses, failed calls or timeouts.","exam_context":"Golden signal for failures. A slow request that still succeeds is latency, not errors.","lesson":"d6l7","domain":6},{"term":"FinOps","definition":"Cloud financial management practice where finance, technology and business teams share accountability for cloud spend.","exam_context":"Cloud spending is decentralized and variable, so share cost views to create accountability.","lesson":"d6l1","domain":6},{"term":"Four Golden Signals","definition":"Google SRE's core service metrics: latency, traffic, errors and saturation.","exam_context":"Map the symptom: wait time = latency, capacity used = saturation.","lesson":"d6l7","domain":6},{"term":"Google Cloud Observability","definition":"Google Cloud's suite for monitoring, logging, tracing, error reporting and profiling. Formerly the operations suite and Stackdriver.","exam_context":"Out-of-the-box observability = monitoring infrastructure and applications with these built-in tools.","lesson":"d6l8","domain":6},{"term":"IAM allow policy","definition":"A policy that grants roles (who can do what) on a resource. Set on a folder, it is inherited by its projects and their resources.","exam_context":"Folder-level access rules apply to every project and resource below, never sideways to other folders.","lesson":"d6l3","domain":6},{"term":"Intelligent recommendations","definition":"Automated suggestions (Active Assist / Recommender) such as rightsizing VMs or removing idle resources.","exam_context":"Answer for optimizing resource use when demand and costs vary.","lesson":"d6l1","domain":6},{"term":"Label","definition":"A key-value pair attached to a resource (for example team=finance) used to group and filter costs.","exam_context":"Labels must differ by team or cost center to break down the bill. One identical tag on everything gives no breakdown.","lesson":"d6l1","domain":6},{"term":"Latency","definition":"The time between a request and its response.","exam_context":"'Users wait a long time' or 'time between request and response'.","lesson":"d6l7","domain":6},{"term":"Logs Explorer","definition":"The Cloud Logging interface for searching, filtering and analyzing log entries.","exam_context":"Searching log entries across GKE containers is Cloud Logging (via Logs Explorer), not the Console itself.","lesson":"d6l8","domain":6},{"term":"Observability","definition":"Visibility into the health, performance and behavior of systems through metrics, logs and traces.","exam_context":"Out-of-the-box observability = monitoring IT infrastructure and applications.","lesson":"d6l7","domain":6},{"term":"Ops Agent","definition":"Google's agent installed on Compute Engine VMs to send logs and metrics to Cloud Logging and Cloud Monitoring.","exam_context":"How VM logs reach Cloud Logging. GKE sends logs by default.","lesson":"d6l8","domain":6},{"term":"Organization policy","definition":"A constraint set on the organization, a folder or a project that limits what can be configured (for example allowed regions). Inherited downward.","exam_context":"Like IAM policies, it flows down the hierarchy to projects and their resources.","lesson":"d6l3","domain":6},{"term":"Premium Support","definition":"Highest Google Cloud Customer Care tier, with the fastest critical response and a Technical Account Manager.","exam_context":"Fastest response + dedicated TAM → Premium, not Enhanced.","lesson":"d6l10","domain":6},{"term":"Pricing Calculator","definition":"Google Cloud tool for estimating the cost of a workload before deploying it.","exam_context":"Answer for 'reliable estimate before deploying'. Not a test deployment.","lesson":"d6l2","domain":6},{"term":"Quota","definition":"A limit on how much of a resource (CPUs, API calls) a project can use.","exam_context":"Quotas cap usage but don't alert on spend. Budget alerts do that.","lesson":"d6l2","domain":6},{"term":"Redundancy","definition":"Duplicating important components across resources so the system keeps running when one fails.","exam_context":"Answer for 'continue operating if one component fails'. Backups restore, they don't keep you running.","lesson":"d6l9","domain":6},{"term":"Resource hierarchy","definition":"Organization → folders → projects → resources structure used to organize and apply policies.","exam_context":"Folder policies are inherited by projects and their resources.","lesson":"d6l3","domain":6},{"term":"Saturation","definition":"How full a system is: the share of capacity (CPU, memory, I/O) being used.","exam_context":"'How much capacity is used' or 'resources becoming constrained'.","lesson":"d6l7","domain":6},{"term":"Service credit","definition":"Compensation a provider gives a customer when it misses its SLA. Google Cloud calls them Financial Credits: the customer claims them and they reduce future bills.","exam_context":"What a customer typically gets when the provider misses an SLA.","lesson":"d6l5","domain":6},{"term":"Service-level agreement (SLA)","definition":"A contract with customers promising a service level, with consequences such as credits if missed.","exam_context":"Contract/promise → SLA. Set looser than the SLO.","lesson":"d6l4","domain":6},{"term":"Service-level indicator (SLI)","definition":"A direct measurement of service performance, such as success rate or latency.","exam_context":"'Direct measurement of how the service is performing' → SLI.","lesson":"d6l4","domain":6},{"term":"Service-level objective (SLO)","definition":"An internal target value for an SLI.","exam_context":"'Desired reliability and performance target' → SLO.","lesson":"d6l4","domain":6},{"term":"Single point of failure","definition":"A component whose failure takes down the whole system.","exam_context":"Redundancy removes it, for example several instances behind a load balancer across zones.","lesson":"d6l9","domain":6},{"term":"Spot VM","definition":"A deeply discounted Compute Engine VM that Google can stop at any time. Fits fault-tolerant batch work.","exam_context":"Distractor in the sustained use discount question: Spot VMs are a separate pricing option.","lesson":"d6l2","domain":6},{"term":"Sustained use discount (SUD)","definition":"Automatic discount on eligible Compute Engine VMs (such as N1, N2, N2D, C2) that run for more than 25% of the billing month. It grows with usage.","exam_context":"Applied automatically, no action needed. Not a 1/3-year commitment and not Spot VMs. Doesn't stack with CUDs.","lesson":"d6l2","domain":6},{"term":"Technical Account Manager (TAM)","definition":"A named Google contact who gives proactive guidance, operational reviews and escalation help. Included in Premium Support.","exam_context":"Fastest response + dedicated TAM → Premium. Enhanced has no TAM.","lesson":"d6l10","domain":6},{"term":"Toil","definition":"Manual, repetitive, automatable operational work that grows with service size.","exam_context":"SRE best practice: prioritize automating it.","lesson":"d6l6","domain":6},{"term":"Traffic","definition":"The demand placed on a system, such as requests or transactions per second.","exam_context":"Golden signal for volume. It doesn't measure how long a request takes.","lesson":"d6l7","domain":6}]}],"questions":{"e1q1":{"id":"e1q1","exam":1,"n":1,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>CafePulse, a restaurant analytics website, receives daily sales records, customer feedback, and delivery logs from multiple branches. The team is converting this raw information into a cleaner, structured format so managers can later use it to discover business trends. Which step of the data value chain does this action represent?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Data analysis</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Data collection</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Data storage</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Data processing</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Data processing</strong></p>\n<p>Data processing turns raw data into a clean, consistent, structured form that is ready for analysis. The clue is \"converting this raw information into a cleaner, structured format so managers can <strong>later</strong> use it\". The data has already been collected, and the analysis comes afterwards.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Data analysis</strong>: analysis is the later step where managers look for trends in the prepared data.\n- <strong>B. Data collection</strong>: collection is gathering the sales records, feedback and logs, which has already happened.\n- <strong>C. Data storage</strong>: storage is keeping the data somewhere; it does not clean or restructure it.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Collect → process (clean, transform) → store → analyze. \"Cleaner, structured format\" = processing.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/dataflow/docs/overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/dataflow/docs/overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-data-analytics\">cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-data-analytics</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/certification/data-engineer\">cloud.google.com/learn/certification/data-engineer</a></p>\n<p><strong>⚠️ Note:</strong> The bank calls this sequence both the \"data value chain\" and the \"data supply chain\" (see Q35). Treat them as the same idea.</p>","lessons":["d2l1"]},"e1q2":{"id":"e1q2","exam":1,"n":2,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>BrightCart, a retail mobile app, wants to add features such as product image recognition, customer-support chat, and demand forecasting. The team includes both experienced data scientists and developers who have very limited machine learning experience. How does Google Cloud help BrightCart use artificial intelligence and machine learning effectively?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. By providing scalable on-premises tensor processing units</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. By providing APIs and tools for users with different levels of expertise</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. By providing affordable and scalable quantum computing for application development</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. By providing machine learning models that always require custom training</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. By providing APIs and tools for users with different levels of expertise</strong></p>\n<p>Google Cloud offers AI at several skill levels: pre-trained APIs (Vision, Natural Language and others) for developers with little ML knowledge, low-code options like AutoML, and full custom training on Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform (formerly Vertex AI) for data scientists. The clue is the mixed team: experienced data scientists and developers with limited ML experience.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. On-premises tensor processing units</strong>: TPUs are offered as cloud resources, and hardware alone does not help non-experts use ML.\n- <strong>C. Quantum computing</strong>: quantum computing is not how Google Cloud delivers AI/ML features to app teams.\n- <strong>D. Models that always require custom training</strong>: the opposite is true; pre-trained models and APIs work without any custom training.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Mixed skill levels = a range of AI tools, from pre-trained APIs to custom training.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/products/ai\">cloud.google.com/products/ai</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/docs/ai-ml\">docs.cloud.google.com/docs/ai-ml</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/machine-learning/beginner/beginners-guide\">docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/machine-learning/beginner/beginners-guide</a></p>","lessons":["d3l1","d3l3"]},"e1q3":{"id":"e1q3","exam":1,"n":3,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>SnackWave, a company that operates snack kiosks in office buildings, notices that the money collected from its machines often does not match the products dispensed. The company plans to replace cash-only purchases with a cloud-connected mobile payment system that records each purchase digitally. How could SnackWave benefit from this upgrade?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. They could relax data access permissions.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. They could reduce their error budget overspend.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. They could improve their perimeter security.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. They could view data history to see transactions.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. They could view data history to see transactions.</strong></p>\n<p>Recording each purchase digitally creates a reliable history of every transaction. SnackWave can then compare sales records with products dispensed and find where the mismatch happens. The clue is the problem itself: cash collected does not match what was sold, and the fix is a system that \"records each purchase digitally\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Relax data access permissions</strong>: digitizing payments is no reason to loosen access; payment data needs tight control.\n- <strong>B. Reduce error budget overspend</strong>: error budgets are an SRE reliability concept and have nothing to do with cash mismatches.\n- <strong>C. Improve perimeter security</strong>: the problem is missing records, not network security, and perimeter security is not what digital payments provide.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Digitizing a process creates data you can track, audit and reconcile.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/solutions/financial-services/payments\">cloud.google.com/solutions/financial-services/payments</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/introduction\">cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/introduction</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/iam/docs/overview\">cloud.google.com/iam/docs/overview</a></p>\n<p><strong>⚠️ Note:</strong> \"View data history\" is vague wording. The real benefit is a digital record of every purchase that can be reconciled against inventory. The other options are clearly wrong, so pick the keyed answer.</p>","lessons":["d2l1"]},"e1q4":{"id":"e1q4","exam":1,"n":4,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>PayPilot Finance is building a payments application that must record customer transactions with low latency while also allowing analysts to run fast reporting queries on the same PostgreSQL-compatible operational database. The team wants a Google Cloud database that includes a columnar engine to speed up analytical queries without moving the data to a separate analytics system. Which database is designed for this requirement?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Cloud SQL</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. AlloyDB for PostgreSQL</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Cloud Spanner</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Firestore</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. AlloyDB for PostgreSQL</strong></p>\n<p>AlloyDB is a fully managed, PostgreSQL-compatible database built for demanding transactional workloads. It also includes a built-in columnar engine that speeds up analytical queries on the same operational data. The clues are \"PostgreSQL-compatible\", \"low latency transactions\" and \"columnar engine ... without moving the data\" (mixed transactional and analytical work).</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Cloud SQL</strong>: Cloud SQL supports PostgreSQL but is a standard managed relational database without AlloyDB's columnar engine for fast analytics.\n- <strong>C. Cloud Spanner</strong>: Spanner is for global scale and strong consistency across regions, not the PostgreSQL-plus-columnar-analytics use case described here.\n- <strong>D. Firestore</strong>: Firestore is a NoSQL document database, not PostgreSQL-compatible.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> PostgreSQL + high performance + fast analytics on live data = AlloyDB.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/alloydb/docs/overview\">cloud.google.com/alloydb/docs/overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/alloydb/docs/columnar-engine/about\">cloud.google.com/alloydb/docs/columnar-engine/about</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/sql/docs/postgres/overview\">cloud.google.com/sql/docs/postgres/overview</a></p>","lessons":["d2l5","d2l6"]},"e1q5":{"id":"e1q5","exam":1,"n":5,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>BlueCart, a grocery delivery startup, is moving its existing order-processing application from its data center to Google Cloud. The team wants the application to run in the cloud with no changes to the application code and no redesign of the current architecture. Which migration approach matches this plan?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Rehosted</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Reimagined</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Replatformed</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Refactored</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Rehosted</strong></p>\n<p>Rehosting (\"lift and shift\") moves an application to the cloud as it is, with no code changes and no redesign. It is the fastest migration path. The clue is \"no changes to the application code and no redesign of the current architecture\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. Reimagined</strong>: reimagining means designing a new cloud-native application from scratch.\n- <strong>C. Replatformed</strong>: replatforming (\"move and improve\") makes small changes, such as swapping in a managed database.\n- <strong>D. Refactored</strong>: refactoring changes the code, often splitting a monolith into microservices.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> No code changes, no redesign = rehost (lift and shift).</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/cloud-migration\">cloud.google.com/learn/cloud-migration</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/migration-to-gcp-getting-started\">docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/migration-to-gcp-getting-started</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/migration-center/docs/migration-planning-overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/migration-center/docs/migration-planning-overview</a></p>","lessons":["d4l5"]},"e1q6":{"id":"e1q6","exam":1,"n":6,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>StreamNest, a video learning website, stores training clips and images that must be delivered to users in several countries. The company needs a Cloud Storage option that supports broad geographic access and a high level of availability. What Cloud Storage feature would StreamNest benefit from?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Multi-region bucket location</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Global metadata</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Data encryption</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Object versioning</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Multi-region bucket location</strong></p>\n<p>A multi-region bucket stores data redundantly across a large geographic area (for example, several regions in one continent). This gives broad access for users in many countries and higher availability than a single region. The clue is \"users in several countries\" plus \"a high level of availability\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. Global metadata</strong>: this is not a Cloud Storage location option and does not control where object data lives.\n- <strong>C. Data encryption</strong>: encryption protects data (and is on by default), but it does not improve geographic reach or availability.\n- <strong>D. Object versioning</strong>: versioning keeps old copies of objects to recover from deletes or overwrites; it is not about location or availability.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Users across many countries + high availability = multi-region bucket.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/locations\">cloud.google.com/storage/docs/locations</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/availability-durability\">cloud.google.com/storage/docs/availability-durability</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/storage-classes\">cloud.google.com/storage/docs/storage-classes</a></p>","lessons":["d2l7"]},"e1q7":{"id":"e1q7","exam":1,"n":7,"domain":6,"html":"<p><strong>PayWave Market, an online payments platform, depends on a public cloud provider for its customer-facing checkout service. The provider promised an SLA of 99.95% availability but did not meet that commitment during a busy shopping week. What is a possible impact on PayWave Market?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. PayWave Market could use up its internal error budget.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. PayWave Market might need to renegotiate the SLA so uptime matters less.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Unplanned downtime could cause customers to leave the platform.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. All information stored in PayWave Market’s database could be automatically deleted.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Unplanned downtime could cause customers to leave the platform.</strong></p>\n<p>When a provider misses its SLA, the customer-facing checkout service goes down. Customers who cannot pay may go to a competitor and not come back. The question asks for the business impact, and lost customers (churn) and lost revenue are the most direct one, especially during a busy shopping week.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Use up its internal error budget</strong>: provider downtime can count against PayWave's own SLO, but this is an internal reliability metric, not the main business impact asked for.\n- <strong>B. Renegotiate the SLA so uptime matters less</strong>: uptime still matters to customers; a weaker SLA does not fix anything.\n- <strong>D. All database information automatically deleted</strong>: an availability failure does not delete stored data.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Missed SLA → downtime → unhappy customers and lost revenue. The provider usually owes service credits.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/reliability/set-targets\">cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/reliability/set-targets</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/devops-sre/sre-fundamentals-slis-slas-and-slos\">cloud.google.com/blog/products/devops-sre/sre-fundamentals-slis-slas-and-slos</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/terms/sla\">cloud.google.com/terms/sla</a></p>\n<p><strong>⚠️ Note:</strong> Option A is not absurd, since provider downtime does count against your own SLO and error budget. C is keyed because it is the clearest business impact. Pick C on this exam.</p>","lessons":["d6l4","d6l5","d6l9"]},"e1q8":{"id":"e1q8","exam":1,"n":8,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>GlobeFleet Logistics, a transportation company with offices in several countries, wants to move from its aging data centers to public cloud services. What is one benefit GlobeFleet Logistics can gain from using the public cloud?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. It can schedule physical server repairs after office hours.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. It can receive guaranteed 100% availability for every cloud service in every region.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. It no longer needs security controls when moving data to the cloud.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. It can access data and computing resources without being limited to one physical location.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It can access data and computing resources without being limited to one physical location.</strong></p>\n<p>Public cloud resources are reachable over the internet from anywhere, and they run in Google's global network of regions. A company with offices in several countries can serve all of them without owning a data center near each office. The clue is \"offices in several countries\" moving off \"aging data centers\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Schedule physical server repairs after office hours</strong>: in the public cloud the provider handles physical hardware; the customer does not repair servers.\n- <strong>B. Guaranteed 100% availability</strong>: no cloud provider guarantees 100% availability; SLAs set targets such as 99.9% or 99.99%.\n- <strong>C. No longer needs security controls</strong>: under the shared responsibility model the customer still secures its data, access and configuration.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Public cloud = global reach, on-demand resources, no hardware to maintain. Never 100% uptime and never zero customer security work.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-public-cloud\">cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-public-cloud</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/advantages-of-cloud-computing\">cloud.google.com/learn/advantages-of-cloud-computing</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/discover/types-of-cloud-computing\">cloud.google.com/discover/types-of-cloud-computing</a></p>","lessons":["d1l2","d1l4","d1l5"]},"e1q9":{"id":"e1q9","exam":1,"n":9,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>MealBridge, a grocery pickup app, needs live product availability from several partner stores. The app must also send each customer’s confirmed order details back to the correct store immediately so staff can prepare the order. What should MealBridge use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Site reliability engineering (SRE)</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. An application programming interface (API)</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. A customized machine learning model</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. A multi-regional database</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. An application programming interface (API)</strong></p>\n<p>An API lets two software systems exchange data in a standard, controlled way. MealBridge can call each partner store's API to read live product availability and send confirmed orders back right away. The clue is two-way, real-time data exchange between separate systems.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Site reliability engineering (SRE)</strong>: SRE is a practice for running reliable services, not a way to connect systems.\n- <strong>C. A customized machine learning model</strong>: nothing here needs prediction; the app just needs to send and receive data.\n- <strong>D. A multi-regional database</strong>: a database stores data but does not connect the app to the partner stores' own systems.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Systems need to talk to each other = API.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/apis/docs/overview\">cloud.google.com/apis/docs/overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/apis/docs/client-libraries-explained\">cloud.google.com/apis/docs/client-libraries-explained</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/apis/design\">cloud.google.com/apis/design</a></p>","lessons":["d4l8"]},"e1q10":{"id":"e1q10","exam":1,"n":10,"domain":5,"html":"<p><strong>FinWell, a digital banking company, stores customer transaction records in cloud regions selected for regulatory reasons. The compliance team wants to understand how the legal idea of data sovereignty affects those records. What does data sovereignty mean for the stored data?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Data is governed by the laws and regulations of the country where it is stored.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. A country automatically has the right to access all data created inside its borders.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. A customer has the right to fully control their own personal information.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Data must be encrypted whenever it is stored or transmitted.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Data is governed by the laws and regulations of the country where it is stored.</strong></p>\n<p>Data sovereignty means data is subject to the laws of the country (or region) where it physically resides. That is why FinWell picks regions \"for regulatory reasons\". Where the data sits decides which rules apply to it.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. A country automatically has the right to access all data</strong>: sovereignty means local laws apply, not that a government can access everything automatically.\n- <strong>C. A customer has the right to fully control their own personal information</strong>: this describes data privacy and individual rights, not sovereignty.\n- <strong>D. Data must be encrypted</strong>: encryption is a security control; it is not the definition of sovereignty.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Sovereignty = whose laws apply. Residency = where the data is stored.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/sovereign-cloud\">cloud.google.com/sovereign-cloud</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/assured-workloads/docs/data-residency\">docs.cloud.google.com/assured-workloads/docs/data-residency</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/security/meet-regulatory-compliance-and-privacy-needs\">docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/security/meet-regulatory-compliance-and-privacy-needs</a></p>","lessons":["d5l8"]},"e1q11":{"id":"e1q11","exam":1,"n":11,"domain":6,"html":"<p><strong>RideMango is a food-delivery mobile app running several workloads on Google Cloud. The finance team does not want to wait until the monthly invoice arrives to discover that spending has gone beyond their planned cloud budget. They want to receive a notification when costs cross a defined limit, such as ₹75,000 for the month. What should RideMango do?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Stop Compute Engine virtual machines outside peak ordering hours.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Configure a budget threshold rule and alert.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Change project resource quota policies.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Review past billing reports to estimate future overspending.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Configure a budget threshold rule and alert.</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud Billing budgets let you set a planned amount and threshold rules (for example, 50%, 90% and 100% of the budget). When spending crosses a threshold, alerts go out to the chosen people. The clue is that finance wants a notification when costs cross a limit, not after the invoice arrives.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Stop VMs outside peak hours</strong>: this may lower costs, but it does not notify anyone when spending crosses the limit.\n- <strong>C. Change resource quota policies</strong>: quotas cap how many resources can be used; they are not a spending alert.\n- <strong>D. Review past billing reports</strong>: looking backward is exactly what finance wants to avoid; it is not proactive.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> \"Notify me when spend crosses X\" = budget + threshold alert. Budgets alert; they do not stop spending by themselves.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/billing/docs/how-to/budgets\">cloud.google.com/billing/docs/how-to/budgets</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/billing/docs/how-to/budget-api-overview\">cloud.google.com/billing/docs/how-to/budget-api-overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/docs/quotas/overview\">cloud.google.com/docs/quotas/overview</a></p>","lessons":["d6l2"]},"e1q12":{"id":"e1q12","exam":1,"n":12,"domain":5,"html":"<p><strong>SkillGrid Analytics, an online learning platform, has hired a new reporting analyst. The administrator wants the analyst to be able to view existing BigQuery datasets for dashboard work, but the analyst must not be allowed to delete datasets or create new datasets. Which Google Cloud service should the administrator use to define and assign the correct access roles?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Cloud Storage</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Identity and Access Management (IAM)</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Cloud Trace</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Identity and Access Management (IAM)</strong></p>\n<p>IAM controls who can do what on which resource. The administrator grants the analyst a role that allows viewing BigQuery data but not creating or deleting datasets. This follows the principle of least privilege. The clue is \"define and assign the correct access roles\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Cloud Storage</strong>: Cloud Storage is object storage, not an access control service.\n- <strong>C. Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)</strong>: VPC controls network connectivity, not per-user permissions on datasets.\n- <strong>D. Cloud Trace</strong>: Cloud Trace measures request latency in applications.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Who can do what on which resource = IAM. Give only the access needed (least privilege).</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/iam/docs/overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/iam/docs/overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/iam/docs/roles-overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/iam/docs/roles-overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/access-control\">docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/access-control</a></p>","lessons":["d5l4"]},"e1q13":{"id":"e1q13","exam":1,"n":13,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>ShopNexa is an online shopping mobile app that stores customer orders, inventory, and payment records in an existing PostgreSQL database. As the number of transactions increases, the database is becoming slower during peak sale events. The engineering team wants to move to a fully managed Google Cloud database service that is compatible with PostgreSQL, provides better performance, scales well, and supports high availability with minimal changes to the current application code. Which service should they choose?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Spanner</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. AlloyDB for PostgreSQL</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Bigtable</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Firestore</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. AlloyDB for PostgreSQL</strong></p>\n<p>AlloyDB is a fully managed, PostgreSQL-compatible database designed for higher performance, scalability and high availability. Because it speaks PostgreSQL, the existing application needs minimal code changes. The clues are \"existing PostgreSQL database\", \"better performance\" and \"minimal changes\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Spanner</strong>: Spanner offers a PostgreSQL interface, but it is built for global scale and usually needs more adaptation than AlloyDB.\n- <strong>C. Bigtable</strong>: Bigtable is a NoSQL wide-column database, not PostgreSQL-compatible.\n- <strong>D. Firestore</strong>: Firestore is a NoSQL document database; moving to it would mean rewriting the data layer.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Existing PostgreSQL that needs more performance with minimal changes = AlloyDB.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/products/alloydb\">cloud.google.com/products/alloydb</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/alloydb/docs/overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/alloydb/docs/overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/spanner/docs/postgresql-interface\">docs.cloud.google.com/spanner/docs/postgresql-interface</a></p>","lessons":["d2l6"]},"e1q14":{"id":"e1q14","exam":1,"n":14,"domain":6,"html":"<p><strong>TrailNest, a travel booking website, wants to understand what out-of-the-box observability means for its cloud operations team. The team needs a ready-to-use way to gain visibility into the health, performance, and behavior of the services running in its cloud environment. What does out-of-the-box observability mean in the context of cloud operations?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Monitoring software release schedules</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Monitoring IT infrastructure and applications</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Monitoring traveler ratings and survey responses</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Monitoring monthly cloud billing charges</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Monitoring IT infrastructure and applications</strong></p>\n<p>Out-of-the-box observability means you get visibility into the health and performance of your infrastructure and applications right away, with built-in metrics, logs and dashboards. On Google Cloud this comes from Google Cloud Observability (Cloud Monitoring, Cloud Logging and related tools). The clue is \"health, performance, and behavior of the services\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Monitoring software release schedules</strong>: release planning is a project management task, not observability.\n- <strong>C. Monitoring traveler ratings</strong>: customer feedback is business data, not service health.\n- <strong>D. Monitoring monthly cloud billing</strong>: cost tracking is billing management, not observability.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Observability = visibility into how your systems and apps are running.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/products/operations\">cloud.google.com/products/operations</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/monitoring/docs\">cloud.google.com/monitoring/docs</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/logging/docs\">cloud.google.com/logging/docs</a></p>","lessons":["d6l7"]},"e1q15":{"id":"e1q15","exam":1,"n":15,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>TaskFlow, a project management software company, is building several new containerized applications for different departments. The platform team wants a managed Google Cloud service that can help deploy, scale, and orchestrate those containers efficiently. Why should TaskFlow use Google Kubernetes Engine?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. It enables rapid application deployment and testing.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. It optimizes virtual machine performance.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. It connects legacy systems to an application.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. It provides pre-written source code for application development.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. It enables rapid application deployment and testing.</strong></p>\n<p>GKE is Google's managed Kubernetes service. It deploys, scales and orchestrates containers, so teams can release and test new versions quickly and consistently. The clue is \"deploy, scale, and orchestrate those containers efficiently\" for several new containerized apps.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. Optimizes VM performance</strong>: GKE manages containers, not the tuning of individual virtual machines.\n- <strong>C. Connects legacy systems to an application</strong>: connecting systems is the job of APIs (for example with Apigee), not GKE.\n- <strong>D. Provides pre-written source code</strong>: GKE runs your code; it does not write it for you.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Containers that need orchestration and scaling = GKE.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/kubernetes-engine-overview\">cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/kubernetes-engine-overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/deploy-app-cluster\">cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/deploy-app-cluster</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/get-started/deploy-workloads\">cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/get-started/deploy-workloads</a></p>\n<p><strong>⚠️ Note:</strong> \"Rapid deployment and testing\" is a generic benefit of containers and CI/CD, not something only GKE provides. It is keyed because the other options are clearly false. Pick A on this exam.</p>","lessons":["d4l3"]},"e1q16":{"id":"e1q16","exam":1,"n":16,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>MediBridge, a healthcare appointment platform, wants to run small pieces of backend code whenever users submit forms, upload documents, or trigger events from other cloud services. The team does not want to manage servers, and it cannot predict how much compute capacity will be needed as usage changes throughout the day. Which Google Cloud solution should MediBridge use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Compute Engine</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Google Kubernetes Engine</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Cloud Run functions</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Bare Metal Solution for SAP</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cloud Run functions</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud Run functions (formerly Cloud Functions) runs small pieces of code in response to events, such as a form submission, a file upload or a message from another service. It is serverless and scales automatically, including down to zero. The clues are \"small pieces of backend code\", \"trigger events\", \"does not want to manage servers\" and unpredictable demand.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Compute Engine</strong>: VMs must be sized and managed, which the team wants to avoid.\n- <strong>B. Google Kubernetes Engine</strong>: GKE runs containers but still involves cluster management; it is more than needed for small event-driven code.\n- <strong>D. Bare Metal Solution for SAP</strong>: this is dedicated physical hardware for special workloads, the opposite of serverless.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Small code + triggered by events + no servers = Cloud Run functions.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/functions\">cloud.google.com/functions</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/run/docs\">cloud.google.com/run/docs</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/kubernetes-engine-overview\">cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/kubernetes-engine-overview</a></p>","lessons":["d4l4","d4l6"],"same":["e2q18","e6q31"]},"e1q17":{"id":"e1q17","exam":1,"n":17,"domain":5,"html":"<p><strong>NovaInbox, a secure email collaboration platform, receives a file attachment that looks suspicious after being reported by an employee. The security research team wants to compare the file and related URLs against a large global threat intelligence dataset that aggregates results from many antivirus engines, URL scanners, and security community submissions. Which Google Cloud-affiliated tool should the team use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Cloud Translation API</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Cloud SQL</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. VirusTotal</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. BigQuery Omni</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. VirusTotal</strong></p>\n<p>VirusTotal, part of Google, checks files and URLs against many antivirus engines, URL scanners and community submissions. It holds a large shared threat intelligence dataset. The clue is the exact description: \"aggregates results from many antivirus engines, URL scanners, and security community submissions\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Cloud Translation API</strong>: it translates text between languages and has no security function.\n- <strong>B. Cloud SQL</strong>: it is a managed relational database, not a threat intelligence tool.\n- <strong>D. BigQuery Omni</strong>: it runs BigQuery analytics on data in other clouds; it is not a malware scanner.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Check a suspicious file or URL against many scanners = VirusTotal. Frontline incident-response intelligence = Mandiant.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/security/resources/google-threat-intelligence-virustotal\">cloud.google.com/security/resources/google-threat-intelligence-virustotal</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.virustotal.com/docs/api-overview\">docs.virustotal.com/docs/api-overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.virustotal.com/docs/contributors\">docs.virustotal.com/docs/contributors</a></p>","lessons":["d5l7"]},"e1q18":{"id":"e1q18","exam":1,"n":18,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>MapleBank still runs an older core banking system and does not have the budget to replace or modernize its infrastructure right now. However, MapleBank wants its business partner’s modern analytics platform to securely access selected data from the legacy system. What should MapleBank use to make its data accessible?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Compute Engine</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. GKE Enterprise</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. An application programming interface</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Google Kubernetes Engine</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. An application programming interface</strong></p>\n<p>An API exposes selected data and functions from a legacy system in a secure, controlled way, without replacing the system. MapleBank can let the partner's analytics platform request only the approved data. The clues are \"no budget to replace or modernize\" and \"securely access selected data\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Compute Engine</strong>: running VMs does not by itself make the legacy system's data accessible to a partner.\n- <strong>B. GKE Enterprise</strong>: it is a platform for running and modernizing container workloads, which the bank has no budget for.\n- <strong>D. Google Kubernetes Engine</strong>: GKE runs containers; it does not expose legacy data to partners.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Unlock legacy data without replacing the system = API.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-api-management\">cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-api-management</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/apigee/docs/api-platform/get-started/what-apigee\">cloud.google.com/apigee/docs/api-platform/get-started/what-apigee</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/instances/use-cases\">cloud.google.com/compute/docs/instances/use-cases</a></p>","lessons":["d4l8"]},"e1q19":{"id":"e1q19","exam":1,"n":19,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>StreamNest Media runs a video streaming website that creates application logs and service configuration files in JSON and XML formats. These files do not follow a fixed relational table structure, but they include nested fields, tags, attributes, and hierarchical elements that help organize the meaning of the data. How should this type of data be classified?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Structured data</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Semi-structured data</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Unstructured data</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Relational data</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Semi-structured data</strong></p>\n<p>Semi-structured data has no fixed table schema but carries its own organizing markers, such as tags, keys, attributes and nesting. JSON and XML are the classic examples. The clue is \"do not follow a fixed relational table structure, but they include nested fields, tags, attributes\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Structured data</strong>: structured data fits a fixed schema of rows and columns, which these files do not.\n- <strong>C. Unstructured data</strong>: unstructured data (images, audio, free text) has no organizing markers; JSON and XML do.\n- <strong>D. Relational data</strong>: relational data lives in tables with defined relationships, a type of structured data.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Rows and columns = structured. JSON/XML with tags = semi-structured. Images, audio, free text = unstructured.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/semi-structured-data\">cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/semi-structured-data</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/json-data\">cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/json-data</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/xml-functions\">cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/xml-functions</a></p>","lessons":["d2l3"]},"e1q20":{"id":"e1q20","exam":1,"n":20,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>BrightCart, an online grocery marketplace, wants a business intelligence solution that lets employees from sales, operations, and marketing explore trusted business data, build reports, and discover insights without depending on engineering teams for every analysis request. Which Google Cloud product or service should BrightCart use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Spanner</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. BigQuery</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Looker</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Dataflow</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Looker</strong></p>\n<p>Looker is Google Cloud's business intelligence platform. It gives business users governed, trusted data so they can explore, build reports and dashboards, and find insights on their own (self-service). The clue is employees from sales, operations and marketing working \"without depending on engineering teams\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Spanner</strong>: Spanner is a transactional database, not a BI tool.\n- <strong>B. BigQuery</strong>: BigQuery is the data warehouse that stores and queries data; Looker is the BI layer that business users work in.\n- <strong>D. Dataflow</strong>: Dataflow processes data in pipelines; it does not give business users reports.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Self-service reports and dashboards for business users = Looker. Warehouse = BigQuery.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/looker\">cloud.google.com/looker</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/introduction\">cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/introduction</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/dataflow/docs/overview\">cloud.google.com/dataflow/docs/overview</a></p>","lessons":["d2l8"]},"e1q21":{"id":"e1q21","exam":1,"n":21,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>ShopTrail, a retail analytics company, relies on older inventory and billing systems that are becoming difficult to adapt whenever new digital services are launched. The company wants a way to make existing system capabilities easier to reuse across mobile apps, partner portals, and cloud services without replacing everything at once. Why should ShopTrail use application programming interfaces APIs?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. To reveal every backend record so external partners can directly change it</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. To fully rebuild all legacy applications from the ground up</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. To gain long-term operational flexibility</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. To move IT spending to a pay-as-you-go model so infrastructure can scale</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. To gain long-term operational flexibility</strong></p>\n<p>APIs wrap existing system capabilities behind a stable interface. New mobile apps, partner portals and cloud services can reuse them without touching the legacy systems directly. Teams can then modernize the back end step by step. The clues are \"easier to reuse\" and \"without replacing everything at once\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Reveal every backend record so partners can directly change it</strong>: APIs expose only selected data and actions, with security controls.\n- <strong>B. Fully rebuild all legacy applications</strong>: APIs avoid a full rebuild; that is the point.\n- <strong>D. Move IT spending to pay-as-you-go</strong>: that describes cloud consumption pricing, not the purpose of APIs.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> APIs = reuse what you have, connect new services, modernize gradually.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/apigee\">cloud.google.com/apigee</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/solutions/unlocking-legacy-applications\">cloud.google.com/solutions/unlocking-legacy-applications</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/apigee/docs/api-platform/get-started/what-apigee\">docs.cloud.google.com/apigee/docs/api-platform/get-started/what-apigee</a></p>","lessons":["d4l8"]},"e1q22":{"id":"e1q22","exam":1,"n":22,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>ShopNest, an ecommerce website, wants to improve customer experience by analyzing different kinds of information collected from its platform. Which type of information is an example of unstructured data that ShopNest can use for business decisions?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Written feedback submitted by customers after delivery</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. GPS coordinates of partner store locations</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Monthly product revenue trends</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Stock quantity records in fulfillment centers</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Written feedback submitted by customers after delivery</strong></p>\n<p>Free-form written feedback has no fixed format or schema, so it is unstructured data. Machine learning (for example, sentiment analysis) can turn it into useful insight about customer experience. The clue is \"written feedback\", which is open text.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. GPS coordinates</strong>: coordinates are fixed numeric fields (latitude, longitude), so they are structured.\n- <strong>C. Monthly product revenue trends</strong>: revenue figures fit neatly into rows and columns.\n- <strong>D. Stock quantity records</strong>: inventory counts are classic structured table data.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Free text, images, audio, video = unstructured. Numbers in fixed fields = structured.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-big-data\">cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-big-data</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/storage\">cloud.google.com/storage</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/bigquery\">cloud.google.com/bigquery</a></p>","lessons":["d2l3"]},"e1q23":{"id":"e1q23","exam":1,"n":23,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>FitLoop, a fitness subscription app, is finding it difficult to keep up with users who expect frequent feature improvements and quick fixes. The company plans to modernize its older applications by moving them to Google Cloud. Why would this help FitLoop better meet user expectations?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Customer data can be used to provide personalized content</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Toil automation helps perform automatic updates</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Updates can be released more quickly to fix bugs</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. DevOps requires market trends to be measured and tracked</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Updates can be released more quickly to fix bugs</strong></p>\n<p>Modernizing applications on Google Cloud (managed services, containers, CI/CD) shortens the path from code change to production. FitLoop can ship features and fixes more often and more safely. The clue is users expecting \"frequent feature improvements and quick fixes\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Personalized content</strong>: personalization is a possible benefit of data, but it does not answer the need for faster fixes.\n- <strong>B. Toil automation helps perform automatic updates</strong>: reducing toil is an SRE idea; it does not mean apps update themselves.\n- <strong>D. DevOps requires market trends to be measured</strong>: DevOps is about faster, more reliable software delivery, not tracking market trends.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Modernize → release faster → meet user expectations sooner.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/solutions/application-modernization\">cloud.google.com/solutions/application-modernization</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/devops\">cloud.google.com/devops</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/architecture/devops\">cloud.google.com/architecture/devops</a></p>","lessons":["d4l1"]},"e1q24":{"id":"e1q24","exam":1,"n":24,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>OrbitCart, a retail marketplace, wants one platform that helps its engineering teams create new container-based services, modernize older applications, and operate workloads consistently across Google Cloud and another public cloud provider. Which Google Cloud solution should OrbitCart use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Apigee Hybrid</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. GKE Enterprise</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Cloud Run</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Compute Engine</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. GKE Enterprise</strong></p>\n<p>GKE Enterprise (formerly Anthos) is Google's platform to build, modernize and manage container workloads consistently across Google Cloud, on-premises and other public clouds. The clues are \"one platform\", \"modernize older applications\" and \"operate workloads consistently across Google Cloud and another public cloud provider\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Apigee Hybrid</strong>: Apigee manages APIs; it does not run and manage container workloads across clouds.\n- <strong>C. Cloud Run</strong>: Cloud Run is a serverless container service on Google Cloud, not a multicloud management platform.\n- <strong>D. Compute Engine</strong>: Compute Engine provides VMs on Google Cloud only.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Containers managed consistently across hybrid and multicloud = GKE Enterprise.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/enterprise/docs/deployment-options\">cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/enterprise/docs/deployment-options</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/enterprise/clusters/docs\">cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/enterprise/clusters/docs</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/run/docs/overview/what-is-cloud-run\">cloud.google.com/run/docs/overview/what-is-cloud-run</a></p>","lessons":["d4l7"]},"e1q25":{"id":"e1q25","exam":1,"n":25,"domain":5,"html":"<p><strong>BrightCart, an online grocery delivery app, runs several batch-processing workloads on Compute Engine. The finance team notices that the monthly Compute Engine bill has increased unexpectedly. After reviewing the project, the security team finds that a compromised account created unauthorized VM workloads that are consuming CPU resources to perform hashing calculations and earn cryptocurrency for the attacker. What type of attack is this?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Cryptomining</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Phishing</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. LLM attack</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Distributed Denial-of-Service (DDoS)</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cryptomining</strong></p>\n<p>Cryptomining (cryptojacking) is when attackers use someone else's computing resources to mine cryptocurrency. The victim pays the bill. The clues are unauthorized VMs, high CPU use for \"hashing calculations\" and a surprise rise in the compute bill.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. Phishing</strong>: phishing tricks people into giving away credentials; it may be how the account was compromised, but it is not what the VMs are doing.\n- <strong>C. LLM attack</strong>: this targets AI models, such as with prompt injection; no AI model is involved here.\n- <strong>D. DDoS</strong>: a DDoS floods a service with traffic to make it unavailable; it does not use the victim's VMs to earn money.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Surprise compute bill + unknown VMs + high CPU = cryptomining.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/security-command-center/cryptomining-protection-program\">cloud.google.com/security-command-center/cryptomining-protection-program</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/security-command-center/docs/cryptomining-detection-best-practices\">docs.cloud.google.com/security-command-center/docs/cryptomining-detection-best-practices</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/armor/docs/cloud-armor-overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/armor/docs/cloud-armor-overview</a></p>","lessons":["d5l2"]},"e1q26":{"id":"e1q26","exam":1,"n":26,"domain":5,"html":"<p><strong>MealTrail, a food delivery app, stores order history and customer contact details in a Cloud Storage bucket. During an internal review, the team discovers that a junior administrator accidentally allowed public internet access to the bucket. External users could view the files without exploiting any software flaw or using advanced attack methods. What is the root cause of this security incident?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Misconfiguration</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Advanced Persistent Threat (APT)</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Zero-day vulnerability</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Ransomware</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Misconfiguration</strong></p>\n<p>The bucket was exposed because an administrator set its access incorrectly. No software flaw or advanced attack was involved. Misconfiguration is one of the most common causes of cloud data exposure. The clue is \"accidentally allowed public internet access\" and \"without exploiting any software flaw\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. Advanced Persistent Threat (APT)</strong>: an APT is a skilled attacker staying hidden in a network for a long time, which is not what happened.\n- <strong>C. Zero-day vulnerability</strong>: a zero-day is an unknown software flaw; the stem says no flaw was exploited.\n- <strong>D. Ransomware</strong>: ransomware encrypts data and demands payment; here files were simply readable.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Human error in settings (like a public bucket) = misconfiguration.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/storage/docs/public-access-prevention\">docs.cloud.google.com/storage/docs/public-access-prevention</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/storage/docs/access-control\">docs.cloud.google.com/storage/docs/access-control</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/security-command-center/docs/security-command-center-overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/security-command-center/docs/security-command-center-overview</a></p>","lessons":["d5l2"]},"e1q27":{"id":"e1q27","exam":1,"n":27,"domain":5,"html":"<p><strong>HealthBridge, a telemedicine provider, stores patient appointment details and consultation records in Google Cloud. The security team is reviewing the core security principles that help protect data. They want to understand how availability strengthens the organization’s overall data security posture. What does availability mean in this context?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. By limiting data access only to approved users</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. By making sure data follows required regulatory and industry standards</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. By verifying that data remains correct, complete, and dependable</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. By making sure data remains dependable and accessible when needed</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. By making sure data remains dependable and accessible when needed</strong></p>\n<p>Availability is the \"A\" in the CIA triad (confidentiality, integrity, availability). It means authorized users can reach data and systems when they need them. For a telemedicine provider, doctors must be able to open patient records during consultations.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Limiting access to approved users</strong>: this is confidentiality.\n- <strong>B. Following regulatory and industry standards</strong>: this is compliance.\n- <strong>C. Data remains correct, complete, and dependable</strong>: this is integrity.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Confidentiality = only the right people. Integrity = correct and unaltered. Availability = there when needed.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-cloud-data-security\">cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-cloud-data-security</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/security\">docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/security</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/reliability\">docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/reliability</a></p>","lessons":["d5l1"]},"e1q28":{"id":"e1q28","exam":1,"n":28,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>PhotoNest Gallery stores millions of customer-uploaded images and backup files in Cloud Storage. The engineering team wants to reduce storage costs, but they do not want to create manual rules such as “move objects after 45 days” or “change storage class after a specific number of versions.” Instead, they want Cloud Storage to automatically move objects between storage classes based on how frequently each object is actually accessed. Which feature should they use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Object Lifecycle Management</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Cloud Storage Autoclass</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Storage Transfer Service</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Cloud CDN</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cloud Storage Autoclass</strong></p>\n<p>Autoclass automatically moves each object to the most cost-effective storage class based on how often that object is actually accessed. No manual rules are needed. The clue is \"do not want to create manual rules\" and \"based on how frequently each object is actually accessed\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Object Lifecycle Management</strong>: lifecycle management uses rules you write, such as age or number of versions, which the team wants to avoid.\n- <strong>C. Storage Transfer Service</strong>: it moves data into or between storage locations; it does not change storage classes based on access.\n- <strong>D. Cloud CDN</strong>: Cloud CDN caches content near users for faster delivery; it does not lower storage costs.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Access-based, automatic class changes = Autoclass. Rules you write (age, versions) = lifecycle management.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/autoclass\">cloud.google.com/storage/docs/autoclass</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/lifecycle\">cloud.google.com/storage/docs/lifecycle</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/storage-transfer/docs/overview\">cloud.google.com/storage-transfer/docs/overview</a></p>","lessons":["d2l7","d2l9"]},"e1q29":{"id":"e1q29","exam":1,"n":29,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>SunriseMart, a regional grocery chain, wants to move from running most applications in its own data center to using cloud services. The company wants to understand how this move can help its teams create more business value over time. How does switching from on-premises infrastructure to the cloud help SunriseMart gain value over time?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. They can expand their internal application hosting infrastructure</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. They can focus their efforts on solution development</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. They can increase development of data recovery systems</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. They can relax their on-premises data security protocols</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. They can focus their efforts on solution development</strong></p>\n<p>In the cloud, the provider runs the hardware, data centers and much of the platform. SunriseMart's teams spend less time on infrastructure and more time building solutions that create business value. The clue is \"create more business value over time\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Expand internal hosting infrastructure</strong>: moving to the cloud reduces the need for internal infrastructure.\n- <strong>C. Increase development of data recovery systems</strong>: the cloud offers managed backup and recovery, so teams build less of this themselves.\n- <strong>D. Relax data security protocols</strong>: security remains a shared responsibility; the company still protects its data and access.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Cloud = less undifferentiated infrastructure work, more focus on what makes the business unique.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-cloud-computing\">cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-cloud-computing</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/architecture/framework\">cloud.google.com/architecture/framework</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/security/shared-responsibility-data-model\">cloud.google.com/security/shared-responsibility-data-model</a></p>","lessons":["d1l1","d1l2","d1l5"]},"e1q30":{"id":"e1q30","exam":1,"n":30,"domain":5,"html":"<p><strong>BrightCart, a retail mobile app company, runs more than 240 Google Cloud projects for payment services, product search, inventory APIs, and analytics workloads. Its security team wants one centralized place to review the organization’s overall security posture, find risky configurations, and detect vulnerabilities across the Google Cloud environment. Which Google Cloud product should BrightCart use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Security Command Center</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Managed Service for Apache Spark</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Cloud Load Balancing</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Dataflow</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Security Command Center</strong></p>\n<p>Security Command Center is Google Cloud's central security and risk management service. It shows the security posture across the whole organization and finds misconfigurations, vulnerabilities and threats. The clues are \"240 projects\", \"one centralized place\", \"risky configurations\" and \"vulnerabilities\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. Managed Service for Apache Spark</strong>: it runs Spark and Hadoop data processing jobs, not security reviews.\n- <strong>C. Cloud Load Balancing</strong>: it spreads traffic across back ends; it does not assess security posture.\n- <strong>D. Dataflow</strong>: Dataflow runs data pipelines, not security scanning.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Central view of security posture, misconfigurations and vulnerabilities across Google Cloud = Security Command Center.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/security-command-center/docs\">docs.cloud.google.com/security-command-center/docs</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/security-command-center/docs/security-command-center-overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/security-command-center/docs/security-command-center-overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/security-command-center/docs/concepts-vulnerabilities-findings\">docs.cloud.google.com/security-command-center/docs/concepts-vulnerabilities-findings</a></p>","lessons":["d5l7"]},"e1q31":{"id":"e1q31","exam":1,"n":31,"domain":5,"html":"<p><strong>FinEdge Pay, a digital wallet company, trains employees to recognize common cybersecurity threats. One employee receives a message that looks like it came from the internal support team. The message says the employee’s account access will be disabled in 12 hours unless they click a link and sign in on a look-alike company portal. The portal is fake and is built to capture the employee’s username and password. What type of cybersecurity threat is this?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. DDoS attack</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Phishing</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Cryptomining</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. LLM attack</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Phishing</strong></p>\n<p>Phishing is a social engineering attack. A fake message pretends to come from a trusted source and tricks people into giving up credentials or clicking malicious links. The clues are the urgent threat (access disabled in 12 hours), a look-alike portal and a fake sign-in page built to capture usernames and passwords.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. DDoS attack</strong>: a DDoS floods a service with traffic; it does not trick employees into sharing passwords.\n- <strong>C. Cryptomining</strong>: cryptomining uses stolen computing power to mine cryptocurrency.\n- <strong>D. LLM attack</strong>: this targets AI models, such as with prompt injection; no AI model is involved.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Fake message + urgency + fake login page = phishing.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://support.google.com/mail/answer/8253\">support.google.com/mail/answer/8253</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cisa.gov/secure-our-world/recognize-and-report-phishing\">cisa.gov/secure-our-world/recognize-and-report-phishing</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cisa.gov/news-events/news/avoiding-social-engineering-and-phishing-attacks\">cisa.gov/news-events/news/avoiding-social-engineering-and-phishing-attacks</a></p>","lessons":["d5l2"]},"e1q32":{"id":"e1q32","exam":1,"n":32,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>PhotoTrail is a travel-photo sharing website that stores millions of uploaded images and videos in Cloud Storage. Some files are downloaded frequently for a few days, while others may not be accessed again for months, and the team cannot reliably predict these access patterns. The company wants Cloud Storage to automatically reduce storage costs by placing infrequently accessed objects into colder storage classes without the team manually creating rules or analyzing object usage. Which Cloud Storage feature should they enable?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Object Versioning</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Autoclass</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Lifecycle management rules</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Requester Pays</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Autoclass</strong></p>\n<p>Autoclass watches each object's access pattern and automatically moves it to colder or warmer storage classes. It fits when access is unpredictable and the team does not want to write rules. The clue is \"cannot reliably predict these access patterns\" and \"without the team manually creating rules\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Object Versioning</strong>: it keeps old versions of objects for recovery; it does not lower storage costs.\n- <strong>C. Lifecycle management rules</strong>: these need rules defined in advance (such as \"after 30 days\"), which the team wants to avoid.\n- <strong>D. Requester Pays</strong>: it makes the person downloading the data pay for access; it does not change storage classes.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Unpredictable access + no manual rules = Autoclass.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/autoclass\">cloud.google.com/storage/docs/autoclass</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/lifecycle\">cloud.google.com/storage/docs/lifecycle</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/requester-pays\">cloud.google.com/storage/docs/requester-pays</a></p>","lessons":["d2l7"]},"e1q33":{"id":"e1q33","exam":1,"n":33,"domain":5,"html":"<p><strong>FitGuide, a fitness coaching mobile app, is launching several public-facing generative AI chat features for workout planning and nutrition guidance. The security team wants a Google Cloud capability that can inspect AI prompts and responses, reduce prompt injection and jailbreak risks, and block harmful or inappropriate model output before it reaches users. Which Google Cloud capability should the team use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Cloud Storage Autoclass</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Model Armor</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Virtual Machine snapshots</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Cloud Run functions</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Model Armor</strong></p>\n<p>Model Armor is a Google Cloud service that screens prompts and responses for generative AI apps. It helps detect prompt injection and jailbreak attempts and filters harmful or inappropriate content before it reaches users. The clue is \"inspect AI prompts and responses\" and \"block harmful or inappropriate model output\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Cloud Storage Autoclass</strong>: it optimizes storage costs; it has nothing to do with AI safety.\n- <strong>C. Virtual Machine snapshots</strong>: snapshots back up VM disks; they do not inspect AI traffic.\n- <strong>D. Cloud Run functions</strong>: it runs event-driven code, not AI prompt and response screening.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Protect gen AI prompts and responses (prompt injection, jailbreaks, harmful output) = Model Armor.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/model-armor/overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/model-armor/overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/model-armor/sanitize-prompts-responses\">docs.cloud.google.com/model-armor/sanitize-prompts-responses</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/functions/docs\">docs.cloud.google.com/functions/docs</a></p>","lessons":["d5l5"]},"e1q34":{"id":"e1q34","exam":1,"n":34,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>TrailWise Trips is building a generative AI assistant to help customers plan vacations from past bookings, support chats, destination reviews, and loyalty data. The leadership team wants to launch quickly, but the analytics team says the company’s data is spread across different systems, has inconsistent quality, and is not governed well enough for reliable AI use. The company wants the data to be unified, cleaned, trusted, and governed before using it for AI workloads. What concept best describes this target state of the data?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. AI-ready data</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Coldline storage data</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Ephemeral data</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. On-premises data</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. AI-ready data</strong></p>\n<p>AI-ready data is data that is unified, clean, trusted and well governed, so AI models can use it reliably. Poor data quality leads to poor AI results. The clue is the target state: data that is \"unified, cleaned, trusted, and governed before using it for AI workloads\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. Coldline storage data</strong>: Coldline is a Cloud Storage class for rarely accessed data; it says nothing about quality or governance.\n- <strong>C. Ephemeral data</strong>: ephemeral data is short-lived or temporary, the opposite of a trusted foundation.\n- <strong>D. On-premises data</strong>: this describes where data lives, not whether it is ready for AI.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Good AI needs good data: unified, high quality, governed = AI-ready data.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-data-governance\">cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-data-governance</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-a-data-cloud\">cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-a-data-cloud</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/storage-classes\">cloud.google.com/storage/docs/storage-classes</a></p>","lessons":["d2l2"]},"e1q35":{"id":"e1q35","exam":1,"n":35,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>FinSightHub is a financial analytics platform that collects transaction events, customer activity logs, and operational records from many systems. The company wants to describe the complete journey of this data, starting from creation and ingestion, continuing through processing and storage, and ending when the data is used for reporting, analytics, and AI-driven business decisions. What term best describes this end-to-end data lifecycle process?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Data center modernization</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Data supply chain</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Open source integration</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Infrastructure as a Service (IaaS)</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Data supply chain</strong></p>\n<p>The data supply chain (also called the data value chain) describes data's full journey: creation and ingestion, processing, storage, and finally use in reporting, analytics and AI. The clue is \"complete journey ... starting from creation and ingestion ... ending when the data is used\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Data center modernization</strong>: this is about updating infrastructure, not the lifecycle of data.\n- <strong>C. Open source integration</strong>: this is about combining open source tools, not the journey of data.\n- <strong>D. Infrastructure as a Service (IaaS)</strong>: IaaS is a cloud service model for renting compute, storage and networking.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Data's end-to-end journey from creation to decisions = data supply chain (data value chain).</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/docs/data\">cloud.google.com/docs/data</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/blog/topics/developers-practitioners/what-data-pipeline-architecture-should-i-use\">cloud.google.com/blog/topics/developers-practitioners/what-data-pipeline-architecture-should-i-use</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-iaas\">cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-iaas</a></p>\n<p><strong>⚠️ Note:</strong> The bank uses \"data supply chain\" here and \"data value chain\" in Q1 for the same idea. Treat them as synonyms.</p>","lessons":["d2l1"]},"e1q36":{"id":"e1q36","exam":1,"n":36,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>Trackly, a logistics analytics company, wants to create custom machine learning models to predict delivery delays. The team needs a managed Google Cloud platform that helps them prepare data, train models, deploy them, and monitor model performance after deployment. Which service should they use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Document AI</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Natural Language API</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Google Kubernetes Engine</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform</strong></p>\n<p>Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform (formerly Vertex AI) is Google Cloud's unified, managed ML platform. It covers the full ML lifecycle: preparing data, training custom models, deploying them and monitoring them in production. The clues are \"custom machine learning models\" and the list of lifecycle steps.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Document AI</strong>: it extracts data from documents; it does not train general custom prediction models.\n- <strong>B. Natural Language API</strong>: it is a pre-trained API for text analysis, not a platform for building custom models.\n- <strong>C. Google Kubernetes Engine</strong>: GKE can run ML workloads, but it is a container platform, not a managed ML lifecycle service.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Build, train, deploy and monitor custom ML models = Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/machine-learning/training/overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/machine-learning/training/overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/machine-learning/model-monitoring/overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/machine-learning/model-monitoring/overview</a></p>","lessons":["d3l4","d3l6","d3l8"],"same":["e6q41"]},"e1q37":{"id":"e1q37","exam":1,"n":37,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>PulseHelp, a customer support platform, wants to use machine learning to gain insights from data that does not fit neatly into rows and columns. The company wants to analyze spoken customer conversations and identify whether callers are happy, neutral, or frustrated. Which scenario best shows machine learning unlocking business value from unstructured data?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Analyzing spreadsheet records of equipment failures to predict upcoming repair schedules.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Suggesting related items based on a customer’s earlier purchases.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Reviewing payment activity to detect possible fraud and financial risk.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Identifying customer sentiment from support center audio recordings.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Identifying customer sentiment from support center audio recordings.</strong></p>\n<p>Audio recordings are unstructured data. They do not fit into rows and columns. ML (speech-to-text plus sentiment analysis) can turn them into insight about how customers feel. The clue is \"data that does not fit neatly into rows and columns\" and \"spoken customer conversations\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Spreadsheet records of equipment failures</strong>: spreadsheets are structured data.\n- <strong>B. Suggesting items from earlier purchases</strong>: purchase history is structured transaction data.\n- <strong>C. Reviewing payment activity for fraud</strong>: payment records are structured data.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Audio, images, video and free text = unstructured. ML is what unlocks value from them.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/speech-to-text\">cloud.google.com/speech-to-text</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/natural-language/docs\">docs.cloud.google.com/natural-language/docs</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/natural-language/docs/basics\">docs.cloud.google.com/natural-language/docs/basics</a></p>","lessons":["d3l1","d3l4"]},"e1q38":{"id":"e1q38","exam":1,"n":38,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>TrendBasket is a fashion retail app that gathers customer information from its own website clicks, completed orders, abandoned carts, and loyalty membership forms. The marketing team wants to use this directly collected customer data to build personalized product recommendations and shopping offers. What type of data is this considered?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Second-party data</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Third-party data</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. First-party data</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Open standard data</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. First-party data</strong></p>\n<p>First-party data is collected directly by a company from its own customers through its own channels, such as website clicks, orders and loyalty sign-ups. It is usually the most reliable and relevant data for personalization. The clue is \"directly collected customer data\" from TrendBasket's own website and forms.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Second-party data</strong>: this is another company's first-party data shared with you through a partnership.\n- <strong>B. Third-party data</strong>: this is bought from outside providers who did not collect it from your customers.\n- <strong>D. Open standard data</strong>: this is not a data source category; open standards are about formats and interoperability.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Your own channels = first-party. A partner's data = second-party. Bought from aggregators = third-party.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://support.google.com/adspolicy/answer/6242605\">support.google.com/adspolicy/answer/6242605</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/solutions/customer-data-platform\">cloud.google.com/solutions/customer-data-platform</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/solutions/marketing-analytics\">cloud.google.com/solutions/marketing-analytics</a></p>","lessons":["d2l3"]},"e1q39":{"id":"e1q39","exam":1,"n":39,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>FreshCart Rewards is planning a new advertising campaign for its grocery delivery app. The marketing team buys anonymized and aggregated audience segments from an outside data provider. This provider collected the data from multiple sources and does not directly interact with FreshCart Rewards customers. Which data classification best describes this audience data?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. First-party data</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Second-party data</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Third-party data</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Unstructured data</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Third-party data</strong></p>\n<p>Third-party data is collected by an outside provider from many sources and sold to other companies. The provider has no direct relationship with the buyer's customers. The clues are \"buys ... from an outside data provider\", \"multiple sources\" and \"does not directly interact with FreshCart Rewards customers\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. First-party data</strong>: first-party data comes from FreshCart's own customers through its own channels.\n- <strong>B. Second-party data</strong>: second-party data is a partner's own first-party data shared directly, not aggregated from many sources.\n- <strong>D. Unstructured data</strong>: this describes format, not source; audience segments are typically structured.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Bought from an aggregator with no direct customer relationship = third-party data.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-big-data\">cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-big-data</a>\n- <a href=\"https://support.google.com/google-ads/answer/7542935\">support.google.com/google-ads/answer/7542935</a>\n- <a href=\"https://support.google.com/analytics/answer/11593727\">support.google.com/analytics/answer/11593727</a></p>","lessons":["d2l3"]},"e1q40":{"id":"e1q40","exam":1,"n":40,"domain":5,"html":"<p><strong>MedNova Health, a digital healthcare portal, receives a very large number of security alerts from applications, endpoints, network devices, and cloud services every day. Its security operations team wants a cloud-native platform that can bring security data into one place, use AI and threat intelligence to improve detection, support investigations, and help analysts respond to incidents faster. Which Google Cloud solution should MedNova Health use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Google Security Operations</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Apigee API Management</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Compute Engine</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Cloud Spanner</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Google Security Operations</strong></p>\n<p>Google Security Operations (formerly Chronicle) is a cloud-native security operations platform (SIEM and SOAR). It collects security data in one place, uses AI and threat intelligence to detect threats, and helps analysts investigate and respond faster. The clues are \"a very large number of security alerts\", \"bring security data into one place\" and \"respond to incidents faster\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. Apigee API Management</strong>: it manages and secures APIs; it is not a security operations platform.\n- <strong>C. Compute Engine</strong>: it provides virtual machines.\n- <strong>D. Cloud Spanner</strong>: it is a globally distributed relational database.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Centralize security data, detect, investigate and respond = Google Security Operations.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/security/products/security-operations\">cloud.google.com/security/products/security-operations</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/chronicle/docs/secops/secops-overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/chronicle/docs/secops/secops-overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/chronicle/docs/soar/overview-and-introduction/soar-overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/chronicle/docs/soar/overview-and-introduction/soar-overview</a></p>","lessons":["d5l7"]},"e1q41":{"id":"e1q41","exam":1,"n":41,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>PhotoTrail, a travel-sharing mobile app, wants to train a machine learning model that can classify uploaded photos into categories such as beaches, mountains, city landmarks, and forests. Which Google Cloud product or service should PhotoTrail use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Video Intelligence API</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. AutoML Vision</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. BigQuery ML</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Looker</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. AutoML Vision</strong></p>\n<p>AutoML Vision lets a team train a custom image classification model using its own labeled photos, without writing ML code. The categories here (beaches, mountains, landmarks, forests) are specific to PhotoTrail. That calls for a custom model, not a generic pre-trained one. The clue is \"train a machine learning model\" to \"classify uploaded photos into categories\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Video Intelligence API</strong>: it analyzes video, not still photos.\n- <strong>C. BigQuery ML</strong>: it builds models with SQL on structured data in BigQuery. It is not the direct way to train an image classifier from photos.\n- <strong>D. Looker</strong>: Looker is a BI and reporting tool, not an ML training service.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Custom image categories + labeled photos + little ML expertise = AutoML (Vision).</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/machine-learning/image-data/classification/train-model\">docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/machine-learning/image-data/classification/train-model</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/video-intelligence/docs\">docs.cloud.google.com/video-intelligence/docs</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/bqml-introduction\">docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/bqml-introduction</a></p>\n<p><strong>⚠️ Note:</strong> \"AutoML Vision\" is the old standalone product name. AutoML now lives inside Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform. The concept (custom image categories with labeled data and no code = AutoML) is still right, so pick B.</p>","lessons":["d3l4","d3l5"]},"e1q42":{"id":"e1q42","exam":1,"n":42,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>HarborLedger, a financial services company, wants to move a legacy database platform to Google Cloud. The platform depends on strict third-party licensing terms, low-level server access, and an architecture that cannot be easily redesigned for virtual machines or serverless services. What Google Cloud solution should HarborLedger use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Compute Engine</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Bare Metal Solution</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Cloud Run</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Cloud Run functions</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Bare Metal Solution</strong></p>\n<p>Bare Metal Solution provides dedicated physical servers in Google Cloud locations, close to Google Cloud services. It suits specialized workloads, such as some legacy databases, that need low-level hardware access or have licensing terms that do not allow virtualization. The clues are \"strict third-party licensing terms\", \"low-level server access\" and \"cannot be easily redesigned for virtual machines or serverless\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Compute Engine</strong>: Compute Engine provides virtual machines, which the workload cannot easily use.\n- <strong>C. Cloud Run</strong>: Cloud Run is serverless containers, which would need a redesign.\n- <strong>D. Cloud Run functions</strong>: event-driven serverless functions are far from a legacy database platform.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Licensing or hardware needs that rule out VMs = Bare Metal Solution.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/bare-metal/docs/bms-overview\">cloud.google.com/bare-metal/docs/bms-overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/overview\">cloud.google.com/compute/docs/overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/run/docs/overview/what-is-cloud-run\">cloud.google.com/run/docs/overview/what-is-cloud-run</a></p>","lessons":["d4l6"]},"e1q43":{"id":"e1q43","exam":1,"n":43,"domain":6,"html":"<p><strong>RideNest, a scooter rental mobile app, wants one fully managed place where its operations team can view the health of its Google Cloud infrastructure, create dashboards, configure alerts, and run uptime checks for customer-facing services. Which Google Cloud service should RideNest use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Cloud Trace</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Cloud Monitoring</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Cloud Logging</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Cloud Profiler</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cloud Monitoring</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud Monitoring collects metrics on the health and performance of infrastructure and services. It provides dashboards, alerting policies and uptime checks in one managed place. The clues are \"dashboards, configure alerts, and run uptime checks\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Cloud Trace</strong>: it tracks request latency across services; it is not the main dashboard and alerting tool.\n- <strong>C. Cloud Logging</strong>: it collects and searches logs; metrics, dashboards and uptime checks belong to Cloud Monitoring.\n- <strong>D. Cloud Profiler</strong>: it analyzes CPU and memory use in code to find inefficient functions.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Metrics, dashboards, alerts and uptime checks = Cloud Monitoring. Logs = Cloud Logging. Latency of requests = Cloud Trace.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/monitoring/docs/monitoring-overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/monitoring/docs/monitoring-overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/monitoring/uptime-checks\">docs.cloud.google.com/monitoring/uptime-checks</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/trace/docs/overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/trace/docs/overview</a></p>","lessons":["d6l8"],"same":["e5q15"]},"e1q44":{"id":"e1q44","exam":1,"n":44,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>StreamWagon is a media analytics company that processes clickstream and viewing-history data with Apache Spark. Its data engineers are spending too much time provisioning clusters, adjusting cluster sizes, and handling Spark infrastructure operations. The team wants a fully managed, serverless Spark environment on Google Cloud so they can submit Spark jobs and focus mainly on application code. Which service best meets this requirement?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Managed Service for Apache Spark managed clusters</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Managed Service for Apache Spark serverless</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. BigQuery</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Cloud Data Fusion</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Managed Service for Apache Spark serverless</strong></p>\n<p>The serverless option of Managed Service for Apache Spark (formerly Dataproc) lets teams submit Spark jobs without creating, sizing or managing clusters. Google handles the infrastructure and scaling. The clues are \"too much time provisioning clusters\", \"fully managed, serverless Spark\" and \"focus mainly on application code\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Managed clusters</strong>: this still requires creating and sizing clusters, the work the team wants to drop.\n- <strong>C. BigQuery</strong>: BigQuery is a SQL data warehouse, not a place to run existing Spark jobs as-is.\n- <strong>D. Cloud Data Fusion</strong>: it is a visual, no-code tool for building data pipelines, not a serverless Spark runtime.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Spark without managing clusters = Managed Service for Apache Spark serverless.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/products/managed-service-for-apache-spark\">cloud.google.com/products/managed-service-for-apache-spark</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/managed-spark/docs\">docs.cloud.google.com/managed-spark/docs</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/data-fusion/docs/concepts/overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/data-fusion/docs/concepts/overview</a></p>","lessons":["d2l9"],"same":["e3q34"]},"e1q45":{"id":"e1q45","exam":1,"n":45,"domain":6,"html":"<p><strong>MetroPay, a mobile wallet company, notices that one of its payment-status services often makes users wait a long time before showing the final result. According to Google’s Four Golden Signals, which signal should the operations team monitor to measure this part of the service’s performance?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Saturation</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Errors</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Latency</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Traffic</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Latency</strong></p>\n<p>Latency is the time it takes to serve a request. Users waiting a long time for the payment status to appear is a latency problem. The Four Golden Signals from Google's SRE practice are latency, traffic, errors and saturation.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Saturation</strong>: saturation measures how full the system is (CPU, memory, capacity).\n- <strong>B. Errors</strong>: errors measure the rate of failed requests, not slow ones.\n- <strong>D. Traffic</strong>: traffic measures demand, such as requests per second.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Slow = latency. Busy = traffic. Failing = errors. Full = saturation.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://sre.google/sre-book/monitoring-distributed-systems\">sre.google/sre-book/monitoring-distributed-systems</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/application-design-center/docs/monitor-applications\">docs.cloud.google.com/application-design-center/docs/monitor-applications</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/endpoints/docs/openapi/monitoring-your-api\">docs.cloud.google.com/endpoints/docs/openapi/monitoring-your-api</a></p>","lessons":["d6l7"]},"e1q46":{"id":"e1q46","exam":1,"n":46,"domain":5,"html":"<p><strong>BluePeak Insurance is reviewing Google Cloud’s advanced security capabilities after a series of industry-wide cyber incidents. The CISO wants to highlight the organization within Google Cloud that is known for intelligence gathered from hands-on incident response work during major cyberattacks across the world. Which organization provides this type of frontline threat intelligence?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. VirusTotal</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Mandiant</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Google Distributed Cloud</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Looker</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Mandiant</strong></p>\n<p>Mandiant, part of Google Cloud, is known for frontline threat intelligence. It gathers this from hands-on incident response to major cyberattacks around the world. The clue is \"intelligence gathered from hands-on incident response work\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. VirusTotal</strong>: VirusTotal provides crowdsourced scanning of files and URLs, not frontline incident-response intelligence.\n- <strong>C. Google Distributed Cloud</strong>: it extends Google Cloud infrastructure to edge and on-premises locations; it is not a threat intelligence group.\n- <strong>D. Looker</strong>: Looker is a business intelligence tool.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Frontline incident-response experts and intelligence = Mandiant. Crowdsourced file and URL scanning = VirusTotal.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/security/mandiant\">cloud.google.com/security/mandiant</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/security/consulting/mandiant-incident-response-services\">cloud.google.com/security/consulting/mandiant-incident-response-services</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/security/consulting/threat-intelligence-services\">cloud.google.com/security/consulting/threat-intelligence-services</a></p>","lessons":["d5l7","d5l8"]},"e1q47":{"id":"e1q47","exam":1,"n":47,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>FitTrackly, a fitness coaching website, has a small development team that built a web application packaged as one container image. The team wants to deploy it with minimal infrastructure management, automatic scaling, and a serverless hosting experience. Which Google Cloud service should FitTrackly use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Compute Engine</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. App Engine</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Google Kubernetes Engine</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Cloud Run</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cloud Run</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud Run is a serverless platform that runs container images. It scales automatically, including to zero, and the team does not manage servers or clusters. The clues are \"packaged as one container image\", \"minimal infrastructure management\" and \"serverless\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Compute Engine</strong>: VMs require managing the OS and scaling setup.\n- <strong>B. App Engine</strong>: App Engine is serverless, but it is code-centric; for an existing container image Cloud Run is the direct fit.\n- <strong>C. Google Kubernetes Engine</strong>: GKE runs containers but involves cluster management, more than a small team needs.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Container image + serverless + autoscaling = Cloud Run.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/run/docs\">docs.cloud.google.com/run/docs</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/run/docs/overview/what-is-cloud-run\">docs.cloud.google.com/run/docs/overview/what-is-cloud-run</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/kubernetes-engine-overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/kubernetes-engine-overview</a></p>","lessons":["d4l4"]},"e1q48":{"id":"e1q48","exam":1,"n":48,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>RiverPay, a digital payments company, collects transaction records from customers in several regions. The analytics team wants a serverless data warehouse that can store large datasets, run fast SQL-based analysis, and support intelligent analytics without managing servers. Which Google Cloud product or service should RiverPay use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Cloud Spanner</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Compute Engine</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. BigQuery</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Dataflow</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. BigQuery</strong></p>\n<p>BigQuery is Google Cloud's serverless, fully managed data warehouse. It stores very large datasets, runs fast SQL analytics and has built-in ML and AI features. The clues are \"serverless data warehouse\", \"fast SQL-based analysis\" and \"without managing servers\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Cloud Spanner</strong>: Spanner is a transactional (OLTP) database, not an analytics warehouse.\n- <strong>B. Compute Engine</strong>: it provides VMs that the team would need to manage.\n- <strong>D. Dataflow</strong>: Dataflow processes streaming and batch data; it is not a warehouse for storing and querying.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Serverless data warehouse + SQL analytics = BigQuery.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/introduction\">cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/introduction</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/spanner/docs/overview\">cloud.google.com/spanner/docs/overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/dataflow/docs/overview\">cloud.google.com/dataflow/docs/overview</a></p>","lessons":["d2l8"]},"e1q49":{"id":"e1q49","exam":1,"n":49,"domain":5,"html":"<p><strong>RiverBox Media, a video streaming company, is migrating several internal applications from its private data center to Google Cloud by using Compute Engine virtual machines. The security team is reviewing the Shared Responsibility Model to understand which security duties will move from RiverBox Media to Google Cloud when using Infrastructure as a Service. Which responsibility is inherently handled by Google Cloud?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Physical security of the data center facilities</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Managing application-level user access</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Encrypting sensitive data within the application</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Applying operating system patches to virtual machines</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Physical security of the data center facilities</strong></p>\n<p>Under the shared responsibility model, Google always secures the physical infrastructure: buildings, hardware and the underlying network. With IaaS (Compute Engine), the customer still manages the guest OS, applications, data and user access. The clue is \"inherently handled by Google Cloud\" with IaaS.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. Managing application-level user access</strong>: the customer controls who can use its applications.\n- <strong>C. Encrypting sensitive data within the application</strong>: Google encrypts data at rest by default, but application-level encryption is the customer's job.\n- <strong>D. Applying OS patches to VMs</strong>: with IaaS the customer manages the guest operating system, including patches.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> IaaS: Google secures the physical layer; you secure the OS, apps, data and access.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/security/shared-responsibility-shared-fate\">docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/security/shared-responsibility-shared-fate</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/docs/security/overview/whitepaper\">docs.cloud.google.com/docs/security/overview/whitepaper</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/regional-deployment-compute-engine\">docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/regional-deployment-compute-engine</a></p>","lessons":["d5l3"]},"e1q50":{"id":"e1q50","exam":1,"n":50,"domain":6,"html":"<p><strong>BrightRide, a ride-sharing mobile app, has set an availability service level objective (SLO) of 99.999% for its customer booking service. Based on this “five nines” availability target, approximately how much downtime can riders experience in one year?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. 5 minutes</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. 500 minutes</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. 5 hours</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. 5 days</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. 5 minutes</strong></p>\n<p>99.999% availability allows 0.001% downtime. A year has about 525,600 minutes, and 0.001% of that is about 5.26 minutes. So \"five nines\" means roughly 5 minutes of downtime per year.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. 500 minutes</strong>: about 500 minutes (roughly 8.3 hours) per year is closer to 99.9% availability.\n- <strong>C. 5 hours</strong>: this is far more than five nines allows; it is between 99.9% and 99.99%.\n- <strong>D. 5 days</strong>: this would be about 98.6% availability.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> 99.9% ≈ 8.8 hours/year, 99.99% ≈ 53 minutes/year, 99.999% ≈ 5 minutes/year.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://sre.google/sre-book/availability-table\">sre.google/sre-book/availability-table</a>\n- <a href=\"https://sre.google/sre-book/service-level-objectives\">sre.google/sre-book/service-level-objectives</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/monitoring/slo-monitoring\">cloud.google.com/monitoring/slo-monitoring</a></p>","lessons":["d6l4"]},"e2q1":{"id":"e2q1","exam":2,"n":1,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>TalentHive HR wants to use an online payroll and employee benefits application provided by a cloud vendor. The company does not want to manage servers, install platform software, or develop the application code itself. Which cloud service model does this represent?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Infrastructure as a service</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Platform as a service</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Software as a service</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Functions as a service</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Software as a service</strong></p>\n<p>TalentHive uses a finished application from a vendor. It does not manage servers, platform software, or code. In SaaS the provider runs everything, and the customer just uses the app and manages its own data and users. The clue is \"does not want to … develop the application code itself\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Infrastructure as a service</strong>: IaaS gives you virtual machines and networks, and you still manage the OS, platform, and app.\n- <strong>B. Platform as a service</strong>: PaaS removes server management, but you still write and deploy your own application code.\n- <strong>D. Functions as a service</strong>: FaaS runs code you write in response to events; TalentHive wants a ready-made app, not code.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Ready-made app you just log in to = SaaS; your code on managed platform = PaaS; your VMs = IaaS.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-saas\">cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-saas</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-paas\">cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-paas</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-iaas\">cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-iaas</a></p>","lessons":["d1l5"]},"e2q2":{"id":"e2q2","exam":2,"n":2,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>MetroTrail, a public transport route-planning app, wants its engineering teams to make existing backend services easier to expose, manage, secure, and reuse as APIs in upcoming web and mobile projects. Which Google product or solution should MetroTrail use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Application migration</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Apigee</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. The App Engine flexible environment</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. AppSheet</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Apigee</strong></p>\n<p>Apigee is Google Cloud's API management platform. It lets teams publish existing backend services as APIs and then secure, monitor, manage, and reuse them across web and mobile apps. The clue is \"expose, manage, secure, and reuse as APIs\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Application migration</strong>: Migration moves workloads to the cloud; it does not manage APIs.\n- <strong>C. The App Engine flexible environment</strong>: App Engine hosts application code; it is not an API management layer.\n- <strong>D. AppSheet</strong>: AppSheet is a no-code app builder for business users, not a tool for managing APIs.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Expose, secure, and manage APIs → Apigee.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/apigee/docs/api-platform/get-started/what-apigee\">docs.cloud.google.com/apigee/docs/api-platform/get-started/what-apigee</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/apigee\">cloud.google.com/apigee</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/appengine/docs/flexible/overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/appengine/docs/flexible/overview</a></p>","lessons":["d4l4","d4l8"]},"e2q3":{"id":"e2q3","exam":2,"n":3,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>RidePulse, a bike-sharing mobile app, is gaining users faster than expected. Its main application still runs on older company-owned infrastructure, and the operations team is finding it difficult and expensive to scale capacity for traffic spikes. What might be limiting RidePulse’s ability to keep up with application growth?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. The time it takes their serverless compute function to scale</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. The overreliance on platform as a service</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. The inaccessibility of their data due to perimeter security</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. The cost of provisioning hardware for peak usage</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. The cost of provisioning hardware for peak usage</strong></p>\n<p>With on-premises infrastructure, a company must buy and install enough hardware for its highest traffic peak. That is slow and expensive, and the extra capacity sits idle the rest of the time. The clue is \"older company-owned infrastructure\" that is \"difficult and expensive to scale\". Cloud solves this with on-demand, elastic capacity.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Serverless scaling time</strong>: RidePulse is not using serverless; its app runs on its own hardware.\n- <strong>B. Overreliance on platform as a service</strong>: PaaS is a cloud model, and RidePulse still runs on company-owned infrastructure.\n- <strong>C. Data inaccessible due to perimeter security</strong>: Security design does not explain why capacity is hard and expensive to scale.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> On-premises = buy for the peak in advance; cloud = scale on demand and pay for what you use.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-cloud-computing\">cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-cloud-computing</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/migration-to-google-cloud-best-practices\">docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/migration-to-google-cloud-best-practices</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/solutions/serverless\">cloud.google.com/solutions/serverless</a></p>","lessons":["d1l2"]},"e2q4":{"id":"e2q4","exam":2,"n":4,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>FreshBite Logistics has moved its inventory tracking system from its private data center to Google Cloud. The finance team wants to understand how this migration changes the way infrastructure spending is managed. How has cost management primarily been affected?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Costs will primarily shift from CapEx to OpEx.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Costs will primarily shift from OpEx to CapEx.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Cost management will stay the same, but the total cost of ownership (TCO) will be lower.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Cost management will stay the same, but the total cost of ownership (TCO) will be higher.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Costs will primarily shift from CapEx to OpEx.</strong></p>\n<p>In a private data center, the company buys servers and buildings up front, which is capital expenditure (CapEx). In the cloud, it pays for resources as it uses them, which is operating expenditure (OpEx). The question asks how the way spending is managed changes, and the main change is this shift to pay-as-you-go.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. OpEx to CapEx</strong>: This is the reverse; moving to the cloud reduces up-front capital purchases.\n- <strong>C. Same management, lower TCO</strong>: TCO may drop, but cost management does change: from buying assets to paying for usage.\n- <strong>D. Same management, higher TCO</strong>: Cost management changes, and the cloud is not assumed to raise TCO.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Moving to the cloud = CapEx (buy up front) becomes OpEx (pay as you go).</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/cloud-migration\">cloud.google.com/learn/cloud-migration</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/cost-optimization\">docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/cost-optimization</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/migration-center/docs/generate-tco-report\">docs.cloud.google.com/migration-center/docs/generate-tco-report</a></p>","lessons":["d1l3"]},"e2q5":{"id":"e2q5","exam":2,"n":5,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>HealthVista Insights is building a company-wide analytics portal for teams that use data platforms across Google Cloud, AWS, and Azure. The organization wants an enterprise business intelligence solution that can model, explore, visualize, and query data across these different cloud environments without being tied to only one provider. Which Google Cloud product should they choose?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Looker</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Compute Engine</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Cloud Spanner</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Spot VMs</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Looker</strong></p>\n<p>Looker is Google Cloud's enterprise business intelligence platform. It lets teams model, explore, visualize, and query data, and it connects to databases on many clouds, not just Google Cloud. The clues are \"enterprise business intelligence\" and \"across Google Cloud, AWS, and Azure\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. Compute Engine</strong>: Compute Engine provides virtual machines, not a BI tool.\n- <strong>C. Cloud Spanner</strong>: Spanner is a globally distributed operational database, not a BI and visualization platform.\n- <strong>D. Spot VMs</strong>: Spot VMs are low-cost, interruptible virtual machines and have nothing to do with BI.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Enterprise BI, dashboards, multicloud data modeling → Looker.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/looker/docs\">docs.cloud.google.com/looker/docs</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/looker\">docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/looker</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/instances/spot\">cloud.google.com/compute/docs/instances/spot</a></p>","lessons":["d2l8"]},"e2q6":{"id":"e2q6","exam":2,"n":6,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>BrightBee, an online tutoring website, wants to train a custom machine learning model that can sort short customer messages from its support form into categories such as billing, course feedback, technical issue, and tutor request. Which Google Cloud product or service should BrightBee use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Google Distributed Cloud</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Recommendations from Agent Search</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. AutoML Natural Language</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Pub/Sub</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. AutoML Natural Language</strong></p>\n<p>BrightBee wants a custom model that sorts short text messages into its own categories. AutoML Natural Language trains a custom text classification model from labeled examples without deep ML expertise. The clues are \"custom machine learning model\", \"short customer messages\" (text), and \"categories\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Google Distributed Cloud</strong>: This runs Google Cloud infrastructure in your own locations or at the edge; it is not an ML tool.\n- <strong>B. Recommendations from Agent Search</strong>: This recommends products or content to users; it does not classify support messages.\n- <strong>D. Pub/Sub</strong>: Pub/Sub is a messaging service for moving events, not for training models.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Custom categories + your own labeled text + little ML expertise → AutoML for text.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/natural-language/docs\">docs.cloud.google.com/natural-language/docs</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/machine-learning/training-overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/machine-learning/training-overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/pubsub/docs/overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/pubsub/docs/overview</a></p>\n<p><strong>⚠️ Note:</strong> \"AutoML Natural Language\" is an older standalone product name. Today AutoML is part of the AI platform (Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform, formerly Vertex AI), and Google also steers text classification toward tuning Gemini models. The idea is still right, so pick the keyed answer on this exam.</p>","lessons":["d3l5","d3l8"]},"e2q7":{"id":"e2q7","exam":2,"n":7,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>CityPetCare, a veterinary appointment website, decides to launch a mobile app so pet owners can check appointment details, vaccination reminders, and service updates while they are away from their computers. What business reason most likely led CityPetCare to create the mobile app?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Customers expect service information to be simple to access from any device.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Customers expect cloud-based information to be available only through mobile devices.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Developers can make customer data more secure only by using a mobile app.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Developers can test different versions of the service only through a mobile app.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Customers expect service information to be simple to access from any device.</strong></p>\n<p>Modern customers expect to reach services anytime, anywhere, on whatever device they have. A mobile app lets pet owners see appointments and reminders while away from their computers. The clue is \"while they are away from their computers\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. Only through mobile devices</strong>: Customers want access from any device, not only mobile; the website still exists.\n- <strong>C. Only a mobile app makes data secure</strong>: Security does not depend on using a mobile app.\n- <strong>D. Testing only through a mobile app</strong>: Developers can test versions on many platforms, and testing is not the business reason here.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Customer expectations drive transformation: access anywhere, on any device.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-digital-transformation\">cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-digital-transformation</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-cloud-computing\">cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-cloud-computing</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/security/security-principles\">docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/security/security-principles</a></p>","lessons":["d1l1"]},"e2q8":{"id":"e2q8","exam":2,"n":8,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>FreshCart, an online grocery delivery website, wants managers and analysts to explore sales trends, build dashboards, understand customer behavior, and make better business decisions from company data. How would FreshCart benefit from using Looker?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Optimal identity and access management</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Leading serverless warehousing technology</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Robust data roll-back accuracy</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Advanced business intelligence and analytics</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Advanced business intelligence and analytics</strong></p>\n<p>Looker is a business intelligence (BI) platform. It helps managers and analysts explore data, build dashboards, and make data-driven decisions. The clues are \"explore sales trends, build dashboards\" and \"make better business decisions\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Optimal identity and access management</strong>: Access management is the job of IAM, not Looker.\n- <strong>B. Leading serverless warehousing technology</strong>: The serverless data warehouse is BigQuery; Looker sits on top of it for BI.\n- <strong>C. Robust data roll-back accuracy</strong>: Rolling back data is a database feature, not what Looker provides.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Dashboards and BI for business users → Looker; storing and querying at scale → BigQuery.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/looker\">cloud.google.com/looker</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/looker/docs\">docs.cloud.google.com/looker/docs</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/looker-bi\">cloud.google.com/looker-bi</a></p>","lessons":["d2l8"]},"e2q9":{"id":"e2q9","exam":2,"n":9,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>BrightCart is adding a small order-notification feature to its shopping app. The development team wants to write the function code and let Google Cloud manage server provisioning, scaling, patching, and runtime infrastructure. Which infrastructure solution should BrightCart use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Virtual machines</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Bare Metal Solution</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Serverless computing</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Artifact Registry</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Serverless computing</strong></p>\n<p>In serverless computing, developers write the code and Google Cloud handles servers, scaling, patching, and the runtime. A small event-driven feature like an order notification is a classic fit. The clue is \"write the function code and let Google Cloud manage\" everything else.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Virtual machines</strong>: With VMs, the team still manages the operating system, patching, and scaling.\n- <strong>B. Bare Metal Solution</strong>: Bare Metal gives dedicated physical servers for special workloads, with the most management work.\n- <strong>D. Artifact Registry</strong>: Artifact Registry stores container images and packages; it does not run code.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> \"Just write the code, no servers to manage\" → serverless.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/serverless\">cloud.google.com/serverless</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/run/docs\">cloud.google.com/run/docs</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/artifact-registry/docs\">cloud.google.com/artifact-registry/docs</a></p>","lessons":["d4l4"]},"e2q10":{"id":"e2q10","exam":2,"n":10,"domain":6,"html":"<p><strong>PixovaPay, a digital payments website, is adopting Google’s Site Reliability Engineering practices to improve reliability for its transaction service. The operations team wants to know which concept is used as a direct measurement of how the service is actually performing. Which concept should they use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Service-level agreement</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Service-level indicator</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Error reporting</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Service-level objective</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Service-level indicator</strong></p>\n<p>A service-level indicator (SLI) is a direct, measured value of how the service is performing, such as the percentage of successful requests or request latency. The SLO is the target for that SLI, and the SLA is the contract with customers. The clue is \"direct measurement\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Service-level agreement</strong>: An SLA is a contract with customers that includes consequences if targets are missed; it is not a measurement.\n- <strong>C. Error reporting</strong>: Error reporting groups and shows application errors; it is a tool, not the SRE measurement concept.\n- <strong>D. Service-level objective</strong>: An SLO is the target value for an SLI, not the measurement itself.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> SLI = what you measure; SLO = the goal; SLA = the promise with penalties.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://sre.google/sre-book/service-level-objectives\">sre.google/sre-book/service-level-objectives</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/stackdriver/docs/solutions/slo-monitoring\">docs.cloud.google.com/stackdriver/docs/solutions/slo-monitoring</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/devops-sre/sre-fundamentals-sli-vs-slo-vs-sla\">cloud.google.com/blog/products/devops-sre/sre-fundamentals-sli-vs-slo-vs-sla</a></p>","lessons":["d6l4"]},"e2q11":{"id":"e2q11","exam":2,"n":11,"domain":6,"html":"<p><strong>QuickKart, an online grocery delivery platform, is moving several application teams to Google Cloud. The finance team notices that different engineering teams can create cloud resources as needed, and monthly costs change based on actual usage. What is the typical cloud spending behavior for most organizations?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Decentralized and variable</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Centralized and variable</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Decentralized and fixed</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Centralized and fixed</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Decentralized and variable</strong></p>\n<p>In the cloud, many teams can create resources themselves, so spending decisions are spread out (decentralized). Bills also change month to month with actual usage (variable). The stem describes both: teams create resources \"as needed\" and costs \"change based on actual usage\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. Centralized and variable</strong>: Spending is not controlled by one central team when many teams create resources.\n- <strong>C. Decentralized and fixed</strong>: Usage-based billing means costs are not fixed.\n- <strong>D. Centralized and fixed</strong>: This describes traditional on-premises budgeting, not the cloud.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Cloud spending = decentralized and variable; that is why FinOps and cost controls matter.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/cost-management\">cloud.google.com/cost-management</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/docs/costs-usage\">docs.cloud.google.com/docs/costs-usage</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/cost-optimization\">docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/cost-optimization</a></p>","lessons":["d6l1"]},"e2q12":{"id":"e2q12","exam":2,"n":12,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>BrightShelf, an online bookstore, keeps a large collection of product images, sales reports, and historical order exports. The team frequently reads only the latest files, while older files are accessed rarely. BrightShelf wants to lower storage costs by keeping less-used data in Nearline storage, Coldline storage, and Archive storage. Which Google Cloud product should BrightShelf use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Filestore</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Cloud Storage</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Knowledge Catalog</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Spanner</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cloud Storage</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud Storage is Google Cloud's object storage for files like images, reports, and exports. It offers storage classes (Standard, Nearline, Coldline, Archive) so rarely used data costs less. The clue is the direct mention of Nearline, Coldline, and Archive, which are Cloud Storage classes.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Filestore</strong>: Filestore is managed file (NFS) storage for apps that need a shared file system, without these storage classes.\n- <strong>C. Knowledge Catalog</strong>: This is a catalog for finding and governing data, not a place to store files.\n- <strong>D. Spanner</strong>: Spanner is a relational database for transactions, not low-cost file storage.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Nearline, Coldline, Archive → Cloud Storage classes for less frequently accessed data.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/storage/docs/storage-classes\">docs.cloud.google.com/storage/docs/storage-classes</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/storage/docs/lifecycle\">docs.cloud.google.com/storage/docs/lifecycle</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/filestore/docs/overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/filestore/docs/overview</a></p>","lessons":["d2l2","d2l7"]},"e2q13":{"id":"e2q13","exam":2,"n":13,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>CareNest, a healthcare appointment support website, receives thousands of patient questions through chat and phone every week. The support team wants to handle common requests more efficiently while still keeping conversations personalized and helpful for each patient. Which Google Cloud product or service should CareNest use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Recommendations AI</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Cloud Identity</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Contact Center AI Platform</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Cloud Text-to-Speech</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Contact Center AI Platform</strong></p>\n<p>Contact Center AI Platform is Google's customer-service solution. Virtual agents handle common requests automatically, and AI helps human agents resolve the rest, while keeping conversations personal. The clues are \"chat and phone\", \"common requests more efficiently\", and \"personalized\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Recommendations AI</strong>: This suggests products to shoppers; it does not handle support conversations.\n- <strong>B. Cloud Identity</strong>: Cloud Identity manages users and sign-in, not customer conversations.\n- <strong>D. Cloud Text-to-Speech</strong>: Text-to-Speech only turns text into spoken audio; it is one building block, not a full contact center solution.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> High-volume customer chats and calls → Contact Center AI.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/solutions/contact-center-ai-platform\">cloud.google.com/solutions/contact-center-ai-platform</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/contact-center/ccai-platform/docs\">docs.cloud.google.com/contact-center/ccai-platform/docs</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/text-to-speech/docs\">docs.cloud.google.com/text-to-speech/docs</a></p>","lessons":["d3l4","d3l8"],"same":["e6q5"]},"e2q14":{"id":"e2q14","exam":2,"n":14,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>TravelNest Analytics stores booking history in BigQuery on Google Cloud, while its partner clickstream files remain in object storage in another large public cloud. The analytics team wants to run SQL-based analysis across both locations without first copying all partner data into Google Cloud. Which multicloud product should they choose?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. AlloyDB Omni</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Cloud SQL</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. BigQuery Omni</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Cloud Run functions</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. BigQuery Omni</strong></p>\n<p>BigQuery Omni lets you run BigQuery SQL on data stored in other public clouds, such as AWS or Azure, without moving it into Google Cloud first. The clues are \"object storage in another large public cloud\" and \"without first copying\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. AlloyDB Omni</strong>: AlloyDB Omni is a PostgreSQL-compatible database you run anywhere; it is not for analyzing files in another cloud.\n- <strong>B. Cloud SQL</strong>: Cloud SQL is a managed relational database on Google Cloud, not a multicloud analytics tool.\n- <strong>D. Cloud Run functions</strong>: Cloud Run functions run event-driven code, not SQL analytics across clouds.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Query data in AWS/Azure with BigQuery, without moving it → BigQuery Omni.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/omni-introduction\">docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/omni-introduction</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/alloydb/omni/docs\">docs.cloud.google.com/alloydb/omni/docs</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/sql/docs/introduction\">docs.cloud.google.com/sql/docs/introduction</a></p>","lessons":["d2l6","d2l8"]},"e2q15":{"id":"e2q15","exam":2,"n":15,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>UrbanPedal, a bike-sharing company, operates smart docking stations across several cities. They store rider activity, station usage, and repair history in BigQuery. They want to maintain their docking stations more efficiently, reduce unnecessary part replacements, and support their sustainability goals. How can Google Cloud services help UrbanPedal?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Secure data access by using Identity and Access Management.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Provide access to third-party developers by creating an API using Apigee.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Create a model in BigQuery to predict future maintenance schedules.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Move data from BigQuery to Cloud Storage to reduce storage costs.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Create a model in BigQuery to predict future maintenance schedules.</strong></p>\n<p>UrbanPedal already keeps usage and repair history in BigQuery. With BigQuery ML it can build a predictive maintenance model right where the data lives. Servicing stations only when needed cuts waste and supports sustainability. The clues are \"maintain more efficiently\" and \"reduce unnecessary part replacements\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Secure data with IAM</strong>: IAM controls access, but it does not make maintenance more efficient.\n- <strong>B. Create an API with Apigee</strong>: Sharing data with outside developers does not predict maintenance needs.\n- <strong>D. Move data to Cloud Storage</strong>: Moving data may lower storage costs, but it does nothing for maintenance planning.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Historical data + \"predict\" + data already in BigQuery → build an ML model in BigQuery.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/bqml-introduction\">cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/bqml-introduction</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/reference/standard-sql/bigqueryml-syntax-create\">cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/reference/standard-sql/bigqueryml-syntax-create</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/apigee/docs/api-platform/get-started/what-apigee\">cloud.google.com/apigee/docs/api-platform/get-started/what-apigee</a></p>","lessons":["d3l5"]},"e2q16":{"id":"e2q16","exam":2,"n":16,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>BrightCart is preparing to release a container-based shopping assistant service. The team is unsure whether the service will receive 500 users or 50,000 users during launch week. They also want to avoid paying for compute capacity when no users are actively using the service. Which benefit of serverless computing best addresses this requirement?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Disaster recovery</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Reduced development costs</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Scalability</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Built-in security</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Scalability</strong></p>\n<p>Serverless platforms scale automatically with demand, from zero users to many thousands, without the team planning capacity. Because they can scale down to zero, BrightCart does not pay for idle capacity. The clue is the uncertain demand: \"500 users or 50,000 users\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Disaster recovery</strong>: Recovery from outages is not the issue; unpredictable traffic is.\n- <strong>B. Reduced development costs</strong>: Serverless can save effort, but the stem is about handling changing user load.\n- <strong>D. Built-in security</strong>: Security is a benefit of managed platforms, but it does not address variable demand.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Unknown or spiky traffic on serverless → automatic scaling (up and down to zero).</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/serverless\">cloud.google.com/serverless</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/run/docs/overview/what-is-cloud-run\">docs.cloud.google.com/run/docs/overview/what-is-cloud-run</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/run/pricing\">cloud.google.com/run/pricing</a></p>\n<p><strong>⚠️ Note:</strong> \"Avoid paying when no users are active\" is really a pay-per-use cost benefit (scale to zero). Scalability is still the best of the four options, so pick the keyed answer.</p>","lessons":["d4l4"]},"e2q17":{"id":"e2q17","exam":2,"n":17,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>FitRoute, a fitness class booking app, wants to create a machine learning model to predict member attendance trends for the next 18 months. The company has four years of attendance records from 220 studios, but each studio exported its data using different column names, date formats, and file structures. FitRoute must prepare the data before training the model. What should they do?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Use only the most recent 10 months of data so the model has fewer inconsistencies to process.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Transform the data into a consistent format.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Group the records by file format and train a separate model for each group.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Select a machine learning model type that can automatically process all different input formats.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Transform the data into a consistent format.</strong></p>\n<p>Machine learning needs clean, consistent data. When 220 studios use different column names, date formats, and file structures, the first step is to standardize the data into one format. Then the full four years of history can be used to train one good model. The clue is \"must prepare the data before training\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Use only the last 10 months</strong>: Throwing away most of the history weakens an 18-month forecast and does not fix the format problem.\n- <strong>C. Separate model per file format</strong>: This splits the data into weaker models and does not solve inconsistent columns or dates.\n- <strong>D. Model that handles any format</strong>: No model type automatically fixes messy, inconsistent input; data preparation is still needed.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Good ML starts with clean, consistent data: prepare and standardize first.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/preprocess-overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/preprocess-overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/dataflow/docs/machine-learning/ml-preprocess-data\">docs.cloud.google.com/dataflow/docs/machine-learning/ml-preprocess-data</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/ml-on-gcp-best-practices\">docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/ml-on-gcp-best-practices</a></p>","lessons":["d3l2"]},"e2q18":{"id":"e2q18","exam":2,"n":18,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>PhotoDrop, a media-sharing startup, wants to run small pieces of application logic whenever users upload image files to Cloud Storage. The developers want these pieces of code to be event-driven and serverless, and they do not want to manage virtual machines or runtime infrastructure. Which Google Cloud product should they use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Compute Engine</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Cloud Run functions</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Bare Metal Solution</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Spot VMs</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cloud Run functions</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud Run functions (formerly Cloud Functions) run small pieces of code in response to events, such as a file upload to Cloud Storage. They are serverless, so there are no VMs or runtime to manage. The clues are \"small pieces of application logic\", \"whenever users upload\", and \"event-driven and serverless\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Compute Engine</strong>: Compute Engine is virtual machines, which the team would have to manage.\n- <strong>C. Bare Metal Solution</strong>: Bare Metal is dedicated physical hardware, the opposite of serverless.\n- <strong>D. Spot VMs</strong>: Spot VMs are discounted virtual machines; they are still VMs to manage.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Small code triggered by an event, no servers → Cloud Run functions.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/functions/docs\">docs.cloud.google.com/functions/docs</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/run/docs/tutorials/trigger-functions-storage\">docs.cloud.google.com/run/docs/tutorials/trigger-functions-storage</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/compute/docs/instances/spot\">docs.cloud.google.com/compute/docs/instances/spot</a></p>","lessons":["d4l2","d4l4"],"same":["e1q16","e6q31"]},"e2q19":{"id":"e2q19","exam":2,"n":19,"domain":6,"html":"<p><strong>ShopEase Media is organizing its Google Cloud environment for separate teams such as marketing, analytics, and payments. The cloud administrator wants to apply access rules once for the analytics team and have those rules automatically apply to every project created under the analytics group. How does the Google Cloud resource hierarchy help with this requirement?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Policies can be applied at the folder level and are inherited by projects inside the folder.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Projects in the resource hierarchy are not affected by security policies.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Policies can only be applied at the organization level and affect all projects.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Policies can only be applied to individual projects.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Policies can be applied at the folder level and are inherited by projects inside the folder.</strong></p>\n<p>The resource hierarchy is organization, then folders, then projects, then resources. Policies set on a folder are inherited by every project inside it, including new ones. So the admin sets access once on the analytics folder. The clue is \"apply access rules once\" and \"automatically apply to every project\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. Projects not affected by policies</strong>: Projects inherit policies from their parents and can have their own.\n- <strong>C. Only at organization level</strong>: Policies can be set at the organization, folder, project, or resource level.\n- <strong>D. Only on individual projects</strong>: Setting policies project by project is possible, but it is not the only option and is what the admin wants to avoid.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Group projects in folders; policies flow down the hierarchy.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/resource-manager/docs/cloud-platform-resource-hierarchy\">docs.cloud.google.com/resource-manager/docs/cloud-platform-resource-hierarchy</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/iam/docs/resource-hierarchy-access-control\">docs.cloud.google.com/iam/docs/resource-hierarchy-access-control</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/iam/docs/allow-policies\">docs.cloud.google.com/iam/docs/allow-policies</a></p>","lessons":["d6l3"]},"e2q20":{"id":"e2q20","exam":2,"n":20,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>MarketNest, an online marketplace, runs containerized applications in Kubernetes clusters on Google Cloud, inside its private data center, and in another public cloud provider. The platform team wants one centralized way to manage these clusters, apply consistent security controls, and operate applications across all environments. Which Google Cloud service should they use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Compute Engine</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Cloud Run</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. GKE Enterprise</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. BigQuery Omni</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. GKE Enterprise</strong></p>\n<p>GKE Enterprise (formerly Anthos) gives one central way to manage Kubernetes clusters on Google Cloud, on-premises, and in other clouds. It applies consistent policies and security across all of them. The clues are \"Kubernetes clusters\" in three environments and \"one centralized way to manage\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Compute Engine</strong>: Compute Engine provides VMs on Google Cloud only; it does not manage clusters across environments.\n- <strong>B. Cloud Run</strong>: Cloud Run runs containers serverlessly; it is not a fleet manager for clusters in many places.\n- <strong>D. BigQuery Omni</strong>: BigQuery Omni is multicloud analytics, not container management.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Manage Kubernetes across hybrid and multicloud from one place → GKE Enterprise.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/enterprise/docs/deployment-options\">docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/enterprise/docs/deployment-options</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/fleets-overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/fleets-overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/enterprise/clusters/docs\">docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/enterprise/clusters/docs</a></p>","lessons":["d4l7"],"same":["e5q25"]},"e2q21":{"id":"e2q21","exam":2,"n":21,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>CarePulse, a preventive healthcare mobile app, wants to monitor patient wellness more efficiently. The company needs a way to automatically collect health measurements such as heart rate, activity level, sleep patterns, and other patient signals without requiring patients or staff to manually enter the data. What should CarePulse encourage patients to do?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Use at-home health screening devices and then upload their health data every evening</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Wear Internet of Things (IoT) devices that upload their health data in real time</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Self-assess their health data and then document and upload it in real time</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Visit a clinic assistant who will use Internet of Things (IoT) devices to collect and upload their health data</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Wear Internet of Things (IoT) devices that upload their health data in real time</strong></p>\n<p>Wearable IoT devices collect signals like heart rate, activity, and sleep automatically and send them continuously. Nobody has to type anything in. The clues are \"automatically collect\" and \"without requiring patients or staff to manually enter the data\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Home devices, upload every evening</strong>: Uploading once a day is a manual step and is not continuous.\n- <strong>C. Self-assess and upload</strong>: Self-reporting is manual and less accurate.\n- <strong>D. Visit a clinic assistant</strong>: This needs staff and in-person visits, which is the opposite of automatic.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Automatic, continuous data collection from the physical world → IoT devices.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/connected-devices\">docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/connected-devices</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/healthcare-api/docs/concepts/fhir\">docs.cloud.google.com/healthcare-api/docs/concepts/fhir</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/solutions/healthcare-life-sciences\">cloud.google.com/solutions/healthcare-life-sciences</a></p>","lessons":["d2l1"]},"e2q22":{"id":"e2q22","exam":2,"n":22,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>FitLoop, a fitness coaching startup, uses a cloud-based SaaS application to publish workout plans and nutrition tips for its subscribers. The SaaS provider manages the application platform and the infrastructure behind it. What is FitLoop exclusively responsible for?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Keeping the complete SaaS platform operational</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Maintaining customer-facing content</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Monitoring data center servers</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Monitoring underlying computer networks</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Maintaining customer-facing content</strong></p>\n<p>In SaaS, the provider runs the application, platform, servers, and networks. The customer is still responsible for its own data and content, such as the workout plans and tips FitLoop publishes. This is the shared responsibility model.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Keeping the SaaS platform operational</strong>: Running the platform is the SaaS provider's job.\n- <strong>C. Monitoring data center servers</strong>: The provider manages the physical servers.\n- <strong>D. Monitoring computer networks</strong>: Underlying networks are also managed by the provider.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> In SaaS, the customer always owns its data, content, and user access.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/paas-vs-iaas-vs-saas\">cloud.google.com/learn/paas-vs-iaas-vs-saas</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/security/shared-responsibility-shared-fate\">docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/security/shared-responsibility-shared-fate</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-cloud-security\">cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-cloud-security</a></p>","lessons":["d1l5"]},"e2q23":{"id":"e2q23","exam":2,"n":23,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>FreshCart Analytics wants to create a managed data pipeline that processes and transforms sales and inventory data before loading it into a data warehouse for reconciliation. The pipeline must scale automatically as data volume grows and should require minimal operational management. Which Google Cloud product or service should FreshCart Analytics choose?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Cloud Bigtable</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Cloud Storage</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Pub/Sub</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Dataflow</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Dataflow</strong></p>\n<p>Dataflow is Google Cloud's fully managed service for data processing pipelines. It transforms data in batch or streaming mode, scales automatically, and loads results into a warehouse like BigQuery. The clues are \"managed data pipeline\", \"processes and transforms\", and \"scale automatically\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Cloud Bigtable</strong>: Bigtable is a NoSQL database for large, fast reads and writes, not a transformation pipeline.\n- <strong>B. Cloud Storage</strong>: Cloud Storage stores files; it does not process or transform data.\n- <strong>C. Pub/Sub</strong>: Pub/Sub moves messages between systems, but it does not transform them.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Transform data in a managed, autoscaling pipeline (ETL, batch or streaming) → Dataflow.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/dataflow/docs\">cloud.google.com/dataflow/docs</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/dataflow/docs/concepts/overview\">cloud.google.com/dataflow/docs/concepts/overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/pubsub/docs/overview\">cloud.google.com/pubsub/docs/overview</a></p>","lessons":["d2l6","d2l9"]},"e2q24":{"id":"e2q24","exam":2,"n":24,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>EduCore Apps manages several internal learning platforms for schools. Its IT team spends most of each week installing operating system patches, upgrading application components, and setting up database servers. The leadership team wants developers to move faster by having Google Cloud take over more of these infrastructure administration tasks. Which cloud computing approach does this describe?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. On-premises hosting</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Adopting managed services</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Bare metal provisioning</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Edge computing</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Adopting managed services</strong></p>\n<p>With managed services, Google Cloud takes over routine admin work such as OS patching, upgrades, and database setup. Developers can then spend their time on building features. The clue is \"having Google Cloud take over more of these infrastructure administration tasks\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. On-premises hosting</strong>: On-premises keeps all of this work with the IT team.\n- <strong>C. Bare metal provisioning</strong>: Bare metal servers leave even more management to the customer.\n- <strong>D. Edge computing</strong>: Edge computing moves processing closer to users or devices; it does not reduce admin work.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Less patching and maintenance, more time for innovation → managed services.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/sql/docs/introduction\">docs.cloud.google.com/sql/docs/introduction</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/sql/docs\">docs.cloud.google.com/sql/docs</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/products\">cloud.google.com/products</a></p>","lessons":["d4l1"]},"e2q25":{"id":"e2q25","exam":2,"n":25,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>GlobePay, a digital payments company, runs its application for customers in North America, Europe, and Asia. The company needs a highly available operational database that can keep transactional data consistent and available across multiple geographic regions. How would GlobePay benefit from managing its data with Cloud Spanner?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Cloud Spanner is optimized for cold storage</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Cloud Spanner replicates data across regions in real time</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Cloud Spanner is optimized to ingest unstructured data</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Cloud Spanner visualizes and analyzes data in real time</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cloud Spanner replicates data across regions in real time</strong></p>\n<p>Spanner is a globally distributed relational database. It replicates data across regions synchronously and keeps transactions strongly consistent, with very high availability. The clues are \"consistent and available across multiple geographic regions\" and \"transactional data\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Optimized for cold storage</strong>: Cold storage is a Cloud Storage use case (Coldline, Archive), not Spanner.\n- <strong>C. Optimized for unstructured data</strong>: Spanner is relational and stores structured data.\n- <strong>D. Visualizes and analyzes data</strong>: Visualization is the job of BI tools like Looker; analytics is BigQuery.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Global, strongly consistent, highly available transactions → Spanner.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/spanner\">cloud.google.com/spanner</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/spanner/docs/instance-configurations\">docs.cloud.google.com/spanner/docs/instance-configurations</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/spanner/docs/replication\">docs.cloud.google.com/spanner/docs/replication</a></p>","lessons":["d2l6"]},"e2q26":{"id":"e2q26","exam":2,"n":26,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>RideLoop Mobility is replacing a large legacy ride-booking platform that is difficult to update and scale. The engineering team plans to divide the application into smaller independent services, where each service owns a specific business function and can be built, deployed, and scaled separately. Which modern architecture pattern are they adopting?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Monolithic architecture</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Microservices</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Spot VM architecture</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Hardware virtualization</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Microservices</strong></p>\n<p>Microservices architecture splits an application into small, independent services. Each one owns a business function and can be built, deployed, and scaled on its own. The stem describes exactly this.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Monolithic architecture</strong>: A monolith is one large application, which is what RideLoop is moving away from.\n- <strong>C. Spot VM architecture</strong>: Spot VMs are a low-cost compute option, not an architecture pattern.\n- <strong>D. Hardware virtualization</strong>: Virtualization runs many VMs on one machine; it does not describe how an app is designed.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Small, independent, separately deployable services → microservices.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-microservices-architecture\">cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-microservices-architecture</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/blog/topics/developers-practitioners/microservices-architecture-google-cloud\">cloud.google.com/blog/topics/developers-practitioners/microservices-architecture-google-cloud</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/compute/docs/instances/spot\">docs.cloud.google.com/compute/docs/instances/spot</a></p>","lessons":["d4l2","d4l3"]},"e2q27":{"id":"e2q27","exam":2,"n":27,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>HelpHive is a customer service platform that has added an AI support assistant to handle chats and voice calls. The assistant produces large volumes of text transcripts, call metadata, and speech-related files. Why should HelpHive use a cloud data warehouse such as BigQuery to understand this data?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. To automatically display both text and speech data in live dashboards without extra tools</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. To store and analyze large volumes of structured and unstructured data at scale</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. To encrypt network traffic between cloud resources and on-premises data centers</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. To convert organized database records into unstructured text and audio files</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. To store and analyze large volumes of structured and unstructured data at scale</strong></p>\n<p>BigQuery is a serverless data warehouse built for analyzing very large datasets with SQL. Besides tables of call metadata, it can work with text such as transcripts, and with files kept in Cloud Storage through features like object tables and built-in AI functions. That lets HelpHive find patterns across everything the assistant produces without managing servers.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Automatic live dashboards</strong>: BigQuery stores and queries data. Dashboards come from a BI tool such as Looker.\n- <strong>C. Encrypt network traffic</strong>: Encrypting links between sites is a networking and security task, not analytics.\n- <strong>D. Convert records into text and audio</strong>: This is the opposite of analysis and not what a warehouse does.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Analyze huge volumes of mixed data with SQL, serverless → BigQuery.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/introduction\">cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/introduction</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/object-tables\">cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/object-tables</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/write-api-streaming\">cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/write-api-streaming</a></p>","lessons":["d2l5","d2l8"]},"e2q28":{"id":"e2q28","exam":2,"n":28,"domain":5,"html":"<p><strong>A payroll assistant at MetroKart receives an email that appears to come from the company’s salary portal. The message says their account will be suspended unless they reply with their bank account number and login password. Which cybersecurity threat is this?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Ransomware</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Distributed Denial of Service</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Spamming</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Phishing</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Phishing</strong></p>\n<p>Phishing is a fake message that pretends to come from a trusted source to trick people into giving away sensitive data like passwords or bank details. The clues are an email that \"appears to come from the company’s salary portal\" and a request to reply with login and bank details.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Ransomware</strong>: Ransomware encrypts files and demands payment to unlock them.\n- <strong>B. Distributed Denial of Service</strong>: DDoS floods a service with traffic to knock it offline.\n- <strong>C. Spamming</strong>: Spam is unwanted bulk email; this message is a targeted attempt to steal credentials.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Fake trusted message asking for passwords or financial data → phishing.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cisa.gov/secure-our-world/recognize-and-report-phishing\">cisa.gov/secure-our-world/recognize-and-report-phishing</a>\n- <a href=\"https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/security/protect-yourself-from-phishing\">support.microsoft.com/en-us/security/protect-yourself-from-phishing</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/web-risk/docs/overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/web-risk/docs/overview</a></p>","lessons":["d5l2"]},"e2q29":{"id":"e2q29","exam":2,"n":29,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>RiverBank Plus, a digital banking company, notices that many customers stop using its savings and investment products each year. The company wants to use data and AI to predict which active customers are likely to leave, so the retention team can proactively offer better support, personalized discounts, or improved services. What should RiverBank Plus do?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Create a dashboard of customers who closed their accounts in the past, and manually look for visible patterns in the charts.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Create a ML model based on the demographics and activities of previous customers who closed their accounts.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Create a survey for all customers to identify their current level of satisfaction.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Create a report based on customer feedback collected during the previous year.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Create a ML model based on the demographics and activities of previous customers who closed their accounts.</strong></p>\n<p>Predicting which current customers will leave (churn) is a classic ML use case. A model learns patterns from past customers who left and then scores active customers by risk. The clues are \"predict which active customers are likely to leave\" and \"proactively\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Dashboard and manual pattern search</strong>: Dashboards look backward and manual review does not scale or predict individual risk.\n- <strong>C. Satisfaction survey</strong>: Surveys show current opinions but do not predict who will leave, and many customers won't answer.\n- <strong>D. Report on last year's feedback</strong>: A report describes the past; it does not predict future behavior.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> \"Predict\" + historical examples of the outcome → train an ML model.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-machine-learning\">cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-machine-learning</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/reference-patterns\">docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/reference-patterns</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/machine-learning/beginner/bqml\">docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/machine-learning/beginner/bqml</a></p>","lessons":["d3l1"]},"e2q30":{"id":"e2q30","exam":2,"n":30,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>LumaCart is an online grocery marketplace that stores customer profiles, orders, and inventory records in Cloud SQL. The leadership team wants to use this operational data to understand sales trends and customer behavior. How does Cloud SQL help LumaCart create business insights?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Integrates with business intelligence and analytics platforms</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Builds prediction outputs directly by running machine learning models</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Automatically creates live dashboards and intelligent charts</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Converts unstructured business files into relational tables</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Integrates with business intelligence and analytics platforms</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud SQL is a managed relational database for operational data. It does not create insights on its own, but it connects to analytics and BI tools such as BigQuery and Looker. Those tools turn the data into trends and reports.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. Runs ML models directly</strong>: Cloud SQL is a transactional database, not an ML platform.\n- <strong>C. Creates live dashboards automatically</strong>: Dashboards come from BI tools like Looker, not from Cloud SQL.\n- <strong>D. Converts unstructured files to tables</strong>: Cloud SQL stores structured data; it does not convert unstructured files.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Operational database (Cloud SQL) + BI/analytics tools = business insights.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/sql/docs/introduction\">cloud.google.com/sql/docs/introduction</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/looker\">cloud.google.com/looker</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/introduction\">cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/introduction</a></p>","lessons":["d2l6","d2l8"]},"e2q31":{"id":"e2q31","exam":2,"n":31,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>RideNest is moving several internal applications to Google Cloud and wants to use managed services wherever possible. The IT team wants to reduce the amount of routine infrastructure maintenance they perform themselves. Which task is typically handled automatically by a managed services platform?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Managing user access</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Patching and upgrades</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Data archiving</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Cost optimization</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Patching and upgrades</strong></p>\n<p>A managed service handles routine maintenance for you, such as applying security patches and software upgrades. This is the main way managed services cut infrastructure work. The clue is \"routine infrastructure maintenance\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Managing user access</strong>: Who can access what is the customer's responsibility, set with IAM.\n- <strong>C. Data archiving</strong>: Deciding what to archive and when is a customer data decision.\n- <strong>D. Cost optimization</strong>: Google offers tools and recommendations, but optimizing spending is the customer's job.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Managed service = provider does patching, upgrades, and maintenance; you own access and data.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/discover/what-is-managed-database-service\">cloud.google.com/discover/what-is-managed-database-service</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/compute/vm-manager/docs/patch\">docs.cloud.google.com/compute/vm-manager/docs/patch</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/security/shared-responsibility-model\">cloud.google.com/security/shared-responsibility-model</a></p>","lessons":["d4l1"]},"e2q32":{"id":"e2q32","exam":2,"n":32,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>StreamPilot, a video streaming app, is preparing to test a redesigned home page and a few recommendation features. The product team wants some users to access the new app version while other users continue using the existing version, so they can compare feedback before a full release. Why should StreamPilot use App Engine?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Because their app is containerized and enabled by microservices</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Because the updated app will only include new features</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. To run different versions of the app for different users</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. To run different versions of the app for the same user</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. To run different versions of the app for different users</strong></p>\n<p>App Engine can run several versions of an app at once and split traffic between them. Some users see the new version while others keep the current one. This supports A/B testing and gradual rollouts. The clue is \"some users … while other users\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Containerized and microservices</strong>: Being containerized is not why App Engine is chosen here; the need is traffic splitting.\n- <strong>B. Only new features</strong>: The update content is not the reason; the need is to compare two versions.\n- <strong>D. Different versions for the same user</strong>: Traffic splitting sends different users to different versions, not one user to several.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> App Engine traffic splitting → A/B tests and gradual rollouts across user groups.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/appengine/docs/standard/splitting-traffic\">cloud.google.com/appengine/docs/standard/splitting-traffic</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/appengine/docs/an-overview-of-app-engine\">cloud.google.com/appengine/docs/an-overview-of-app-engine</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/appengine/docs/standard/how-requests-are-routed\">cloud.google.com/appengine/docs/standard/how-requests-are-routed</a></p>","lessons":["d4l4"]},"e2q33":{"id":"e2q33","exam":2,"n":33,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>RideNest, a bike-sharing mobile app, collects trip records from its app, JSON logs from scooters, support chat transcripts, and maintenance images. The analytics team wants to keep all of this structured, semi-structured, and unstructured data together in its original format before deciding how to process it later. Which cloud data management solution should RideNest use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Data field</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Data lake</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Database</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Data warehouse</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Data lake</strong></p>\n<p>A data lake stores large amounts of raw data of any type (structured, semi-structured, unstructured) in its original format. The data can be processed later when needed. On Google Cloud, Cloud Storage is commonly used for this. The clues are \"all of this … data together\" and \"in its original format\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Data field</strong>: A data field is a single attribute in a record, not a storage solution.\n- <strong>C. Database</strong>: A database stores organized data for applications, usually with a defined schema.\n- <strong>D. Data warehouse</strong>: A warehouse holds cleaned, structured data ready for analysis, not raw files in any format.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Raw data, any format, decide later → data lake; cleaned data for analysis → data warehouse.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-a-data-lake\">cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-a-data-lake</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/discover/data-lake-vs-data-warehouse\">cloud.google.com/discover/data-lake-vs-data-warehouse</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/introduction\">cloud.google.com/storage/docs/introduction</a></p>","lessons":["d2l5"]},"e2q34":{"id":"e2q34","exam":2,"n":34,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>BrightCart Retail is moving its inventory application to Google Cloud. Instead of copying the existing virtual machines exactly as they are, the team chooses to replace the self-managed database layer with a managed Cloud SQL instance so database patching, backups, and maintenance require less manual work. The main application logic will remain mostly unchanged. Which migration path does this represent?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Rehost (Lift and shift)</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Replatform (Move and improve)</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Retain</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Retire</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Replatform (Move and improve)</strong></p>\n<p>Replatforming moves an app to the cloud and makes a few targeted improvements, like switching to a managed database, without rewriting the main code. Here BrightCart keeps its app logic but swaps a self-managed database for Cloud SQL. The clue is \"main application logic will remain mostly unchanged\" plus the database upgrade.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Rehost (Lift and shift)</strong>: Rehost copies VMs as they are, with no improvements.\n- <strong>C. Retain</strong>: Retain means keeping the app where it is for now.\n- <strong>D. Retire</strong>: Retire means shutting the app down.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Copy as is = rehost; small improvements like managed services = replatform; rewrite = refactor.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/migration-to-gcp-getting-started\">docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/migration-to-gcp-getting-started</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/migration-center/docs/migration-planning-overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/migration-center/docs/migration-planning-overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/sql/docs/introduction\">docs.cloud.google.com/sql/docs/introduction</a></p>","lessons":["d4l1","d4l5"]},"e2q35":{"id":"e2q35","exam":2,"n":35,"domain":5,"html":"<p><strong>FinSurely, a financial planning mobile app, runs workloads on Google Cloud and needs a central way to discover security weaknesses across its cloud resources and applications. The security team wants to identify risks, prioritize findings, and take remediation actions. Which Google Cloud service should FinSurely use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Security Command Center</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Google Cloud Armor</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Cloud Storage</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. VPC networks</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Security Command Center</strong></p>\n<p>Security Command Center is Google Cloud's central security and risk management service. It finds vulnerabilities and misconfigurations across your resources, helps prioritize findings, and supports remediation. The clues are \"central way to discover security weaknesses\" and \"prioritize findings\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. Google Cloud Armor</strong>: Cloud Armor protects web apps from DDoS and web attacks; it does not inventory risks across resources.\n- <strong>C. Cloud Storage</strong>: Cloud Storage stores objects; it is not a security tool.\n- <strong>D. VPC networks</strong>: VPCs are private networks for your resources, not a risk discovery tool.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> One central view of security risks and findings in Google Cloud → Security Command Center.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/security-command-center/docs/security-command-center-overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/security-command-center/docs/security-command-center-overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/security-command-center/docs/concepts-vulnerabilities-findings\">docs.cloud.google.com/security-command-center/docs/concepts-vulnerabilities-findings</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/armor/docs/cloud-armor-overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/armor/docs/cloud-armor-overview</a></p>","lessons":["d5l5","d5l7"]},"e2q36":{"id":"e2q36","exam":2,"n":36,"domain":6,"html":"<p><strong>FitCart, an online fitness equipment store, plans to launch a new analytics workload on Google Cloud. The team needs a reliable estimate of the expected monthly cost before they deploy the workload. How should FitCart create this estimate?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Use the Google Cloud Pricing Calculator.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Deploy the workload in a test environment to observe costs.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Use historical costs from an existing similar workload.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Review pricing pages and manually calculate an estimate.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Use the Google Cloud Pricing Calculator.</strong></p>\n<p>The Google Cloud Pricing Calculator estimates monthly costs for planned services before you deploy them. You enter the products and expected usage and get an estimate. The clues are \"estimate … before they deploy\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. Deploy in test environment</strong>: This costs money and time, and test usage may not match production.\n- <strong>C. Historical costs of a similar workload</strong>: Another workload may differ in size and services, so the estimate is less reliable.\n- <strong>D. Manually calculate from pricing pages</strong>: This is slow and easy to get wrong; the calculator does it for you.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Estimate cost before deploying → Pricing Calculator; track actual cost → billing reports and budgets.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/products/calculator\">cloud.google.com/products/calculator</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/billing/docs/how-to/estimate-costs\">docs.cloud.google.com/billing/docs/how-to/estimate-costs</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/pricing\">cloud.google.com/pricing</a></p>","lessons":["d6l2"]},"e2q37":{"id":"e2q37","exam":2,"n":37,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>PulseCart, an online grocery company, is moving its legacy shopping application to Google Cloud. The team plans to redesign the application into smaller, independently deployable services instead of keeping it as one large application. Which benefit would this refactoring approach provide?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. The redesigned application can become more efficient and scalable.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. No code changes will be required.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. This migration approach provides the quickest path to Google Cloud.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. The application will automatically become PCI-DSS compliant.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. The redesigned application can become more efficient and scalable.</strong></p>\n<p>Refactoring rewrites an application to be cloud native, for example as microservices. It takes the most effort, but the result is more efficient and scales better, because each service can scale on its own. The clue is \"redesign the application into smaller, independently deployable services\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. No code changes</strong>: Refactoring means significant code changes.\n- <strong>C. Quickest path to Google Cloud</strong>: Rehost (lift and shift) is the quickest; refactoring is the slowest.\n- <strong>D. Automatically PCI-DSS compliant</strong>: No architecture makes an app compliant automatically; compliance is a shared responsibility.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Refactor = most work, most cloud benefit (scalability, efficiency).</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/migration-to-gcp-getting-started\">docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/migration-to-gcp-getting-started</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-microservices-architecture\">cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-microservices-architecture</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/security/compliance/pci-dss\">cloud.google.com/security/compliance/pci-dss</a></p>","lessons":["d4l3","d4l5"]},"e2q38":{"id":"e2q38","exam":2,"n":38,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>BrightLens Analytics is building image recognition models on Google Cloud using TensorFlow. The data science team wants to train these advanced machine learning models more quickly by using hardware specifically designed for machine learning workloads. Which solution should BrightLens Analytics use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. TPUs</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. CPUs</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. High-memory VMs</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. GPUs</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. TPUs</strong></p>\n<p>Tensor Processing Units (TPUs) are Google's custom chips designed specifically for machine learning. They speed up training of large models such as image recognition networks, and they work well with TensorFlow. The clue is \"hardware specifically designed for machine learning workloads\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. CPUs</strong>: General-purpose processors. They can train models, but slowly compared with accelerators.\n- <strong>C. High-memory VMs</strong>: More RAM helps with big datasets in memory, but it doesn't accelerate the math of model training.\n- <strong>D. GPUs</strong>: GPUs also accelerate ML training and are a valid choice, but they were designed for graphics first. \"Designed specifically for ML\" points to TPUs.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Hardware built specifically for ML → TPUs.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/tpu\">cloud.google.com/tpu</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/tpu/docs/intro-to-tpu\">docs.cloud.google.com/tpu/docs/intro-to-tpu</a>\n- <a href=\"https://tensorflow.org/guide/tpu\">tensorflow.org/guide/tpu</a></p>","lessons":["d3l5","d3l9"]},"e2q39":{"id":"e2q39","exam":2,"n":39,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>GreenTrail, a regional travel booking company, is adopting cloud services, analytics, and automation to redesign internal workflows, improve collaboration, create better traveler experiences, and respond faster to changing customer demand. What is this approach called?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Digital transformation</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Data center migration</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Operations optimization</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. User personalization</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Digital transformation</strong></p>\n<p>Digital transformation means using modern digital technologies, like cloud, analytics, and automation, to change how a business works and delivers value to customers. GreenTrail is changing workflows, collaboration, and customer experience, not just one system. The breadth of change is the clue.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. Data center migration</strong>: Migration moves infrastructure; it does not by itself redesign workflows and customer experience.\n- <strong>C. Operations optimization</strong>: This is only one part of what GreenTrail is doing.\n- <strong>D. User personalization</strong>: Personalization is one possible outcome, not the overall approach.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Using technology to rethink processes, culture, and customer experience → digital transformation.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-digital-transformation\">cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-digital-transformation</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-cloud-migration\">cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-cloud-migration</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-operational-efficiency\">cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-operational-efficiency</a></p>","lessons":["d1l1"]},"e2q40":{"id":"e2q40","exam":2,"n":40,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>FinSecure Ledger is modernizing the database layer for its core payment-processing platform. The application needs a highly scalable PostgreSQL-compatible database, but compliance rules require the database to run inside FinSecure Ledger’s private on-premises data center while still using Google’s database technology. Which product should the team use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. AlloyDB Omni</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Cloud Storage</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. BigQuery</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Cloud Run</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. AlloyDB Omni</strong></p>\n<p>AlloyDB Omni is a downloadable version of AlloyDB, Google's PostgreSQL-compatible database. It can run anywhere, including a company's own data center. FinSecure gets Google's database technology while meeting the rule to stay on-premises. The clues are \"PostgreSQL-compatible\" and \"inside … private on-premises data center\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. Cloud Storage</strong>: Cloud Storage is object storage in Google Cloud, not a PostgreSQL database.\n- <strong>C. BigQuery</strong>: BigQuery is an analytics warehouse, not a transactional PostgreSQL database for payments.\n- <strong>D. Cloud Run</strong>: Cloud Run runs containers serverlessly; it is not a database.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> PostgreSQL-compatible, Google tech, run on-premises or in other clouds → AlloyDB Omni.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/alloydb/omni\">cloud.google.com/alloydb/omni</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/storage/docs/introduction\">docs.cloud.google.com/storage/docs/introduction</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/introduction\">docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/introduction</a></p>","lessons":["d2l6"]},"e2q41":{"id":"e2q41","exam":2,"n":41,"domain":5,"html":"<p><strong>SkyTrail Logistics uses Google Cloud to host its shipment tracking portal. The security team is reviewing common cyber threats and is especially worried about attackers tricking employees into revealing passwords or clicking malicious links. Which type of attack best represents this social engineering risk?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. SQL injection attacks</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Physical damage to hardware</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Phishing emails</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Distributed denial-of-service attacks</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Phishing emails</strong></p>\n<p>Phishing is a social engineering attack. Attackers send messages that look legitimate to trick people into revealing passwords or clicking malicious links. The clues are \"tricking employees\" and \"social engineering\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. SQL injection attacks</strong>: SQL injection targets application code by sending malicious database queries, not people.\n- <strong>B. Physical damage to hardware</strong>: This is a physical threat, not trickery of employees.\n- <strong>D. Distributed denial-of-service attacks</strong>: DDoS overwhelms services with traffic; it does not trick people.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Tricking people into giving secrets or clicking links → phishing (social engineering).</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://csrc.nist.gov/glossary/term/social_engineering\">csrc.nist.gov/glossary/term/social_engineering</a>\n- <a href=\"https://csrc.nist.gov/glossary/term/phishing\">csrc.nist.gov/glossary/term/phishing</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/web-risk/docs/advisory\">cloud.google.com/web-risk/docs/advisory</a></p>","lessons":["d5l2"]},"e2q42":{"id":"e2q42","exam":2,"n":42,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>BrightBite, a food delivery mobile app, wants its customer app to request menu details, send order information, and receive delivery updates from backend systems. The development team wants to use a standard way for different software components to exchange requests and responses. What is the purpose of an application programming interface (API)?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. To provide a defined set of rules and methods that allow software applications to communicate with each other</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. To provide cloud extensions for integrated development environments</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. To link private networks across different cloud vendors</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. To control and schedule multiple containerized applications</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. To provide a defined set of rules and methods that allow software applications to communicate with each other</strong></p>\n<p>An API (application programming interface) is a contract that defines how one piece of software can request data or actions from another. The mobile app uses APIs to get menus, send orders, and receive updates from the backend. The clue is \"a standard way for different software components to exchange requests and responses\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. Cloud extensions for IDEs</strong>: IDE plugins help developers write code; they are not what an API is.\n- <strong>C. Link private networks across clouds</strong>: Connecting networks is done with networking services like VPN or Interconnect.\n- <strong>D. Schedule containerized applications</strong>: Scheduling containers is the job of Kubernetes, such as GKE.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> API = agreed rules for apps to talk to each other.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/apis/docs/overview\">cloud.google.com/apis/docs/overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/api-gateway/docs\">cloud.google.com/api-gateway/docs</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/endpoints/docs/openapi/about-cloud-endpoints\">cloud.google.com/endpoints/docs/openapi/about-cloud-endpoints</a></p>","lessons":["d4l8"]},"e2q43":{"id":"e2q43","exam":2,"n":43,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>MediaNest, a video streaming startup, is building containerized applications with Kubernetes. The engineering team wants to reduce the time spent configuring nodes, scaling cluster infrastructure, and handling cluster operations so they can focus more on application development. Which Google Cloud service should MediaNest choose?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. GKE Autopilot</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Compute Engine</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Cloud Run</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. GKE Standard</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. GKE Autopilot</strong></p>\n<p>GKE Autopilot is a mode of Google Kubernetes Engine where Google manages the nodes, scaling, and cluster operations. The team still uses Kubernetes but spends its time on applications. The clues are \"Kubernetes\" and \"reduce the time spent configuring nodes … cluster operations\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. Compute Engine</strong>: Compute Engine gives VMs, so the team would have to build and run Kubernetes itself.\n- <strong>C. Cloud Run</strong>: Cloud Run is serverless containers without Kubernetes; the team is building with Kubernetes.\n- <strong>D. GKE Standard</strong>: In Standard mode, the customer manages and configures the nodes.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Kubernetes without managing nodes → GKE Autopilot; full node control → GKE Standard.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/autopilot-overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/autopilot-overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/choose-cluster-mode\">docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/choose-cluster-mode</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/run/docs/overview/what-is-cloud-run\">docs.cloud.google.com/run/docs/overview/what-is-cloud-run</a></p>","lessons":["d4l3"]},"e2q44":{"id":"e2q44","exam":2,"n":44,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>ShopTrail, a retail analytics website, keeps purchase history and customer behavior data in BigQuery. Its analysts know SQL and want to create machine learning models directly on that data without exporting it or learning Python or Java. Which Google Cloud tool should they use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Cloud Vision API</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. BigQuery ML</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Agent Studio</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Spanner</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. BigQuery ML</strong></p>\n<p>BigQuery ML lets analysts create and run ML models using standard SQL, directly on data already stored in BigQuery. No data export and no Python or Java are needed. The clues are \"analysts know SQL\" and \"without exporting it\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Cloud Vision API</strong>: Vision API is a pre-trained model for images, not for building models on purchase data.\n- <strong>C. Agent Studio</strong>: Agent Studio is a no-code tool for building AI agents, not for training models on BigQuery tables.\n- <strong>D. Spanner</strong>: Spanner is a transactional database, not an ML tool.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> SQL skills + data in BigQuery + ML → BigQuery ML.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/bqml-introduction\">docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/bqml-introduction</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/create-machine-learning-model\">docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/create-machine-learning-model</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/vision/docs\">docs.cloud.google.com/vision/docs</a></p>","lessons":["d3l3","d3l4","d3l5","d3l6"]},"e2q45":{"id":"e2q45","exam":2,"n":45,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>DataCrate Analytics runs a nightly data transformation job that processes millions of log records. The job can safely restart from checkpoints if compute resources are interrupted, and it does not need to finish immediately. The company wants to minimize compute costs and accepts that Google Cloud might stop the instances when capacity is needed elsewhere. Which compute option should the company choose?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Spot VMs</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Managed services</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Premium tier virtual machines</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Cloud Run functions</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Spot VMs</strong></p>\n<p>Spot VMs are spare Google Cloud capacity at a large discount. Google can stop them when it needs the capacity back. They suit fault-tolerant batch jobs that can restart and do not have tight deadlines. The clues are \"restart from checkpoints\", \"does not need to finish immediately\", and \"accepts that Google Cloud might stop the instances\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. Managed services</strong>: This is a general approach, not a low-cost interruptible compute option.\n- <strong>C. Premium tier virtual machines</strong>: There is no such VM type. In Google Cloud, Premium Tier is a Network Service Tier (traffic on Google's global network), which affects networking, not compute cost.\n- <strong>D. Cloud Run functions</strong>: Functions are for short event-driven code, not long nightly batch jobs over millions of records.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Fault-tolerant batch + lowest cost + OK to be interrupted → Spot VMs.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/compute/docs/instances/spot\">docs.cloud.google.com/compute/docs/instances/spot</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/compute/docs/instances/create-use-spot\">docs.cloud.google.com/compute/docs/instances/create-use-spot</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/compute/docs/instances/provisioning-models\">docs.cloud.google.com/compute/docs/instances/provisioning-models</a></p>","lessons":["d4l2"]},"e2q46":{"id":"e2q46","exam":2,"n":46,"domain":5,"html":"<p><strong>NovaCart, an online shopping platform, wants to improve its security awareness training for employees. The training team asks which type of cybersecurity threat can secretly steal, alter, or damage information on a device or system without the user noticing. Which cybersecurity threat does this describe?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. SLA policy violation</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Malware attack</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Ransomware attack</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Incomplete data input</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Malware attack</strong></p>\n<p>Malware is any malicious software, such as viruses, spyware, or trojans, that can secretly steal, change, or damage data. The broad description \"steal, alter, or damage information … without the user noticing\" fits malware in general.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. SLA policy violation</strong>: An SLA violation is a missed service promise, not a cyber threat.\n- <strong>C. Ransomware attack</strong>: Ransomware is one type of malware; it locks data and demands payment, which users notice right away.\n- <strong>D. Incomplete data input</strong>: This is a data quality issue, not an attack.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Malicious software that secretly harms data → malware; locks data for payment → ransomware.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://csrc.nist.gov/glossary/term/malware\">csrc.nist.gov/glossary/term/malware</a>\n- <a href=\"https://csrc.nist.gov/glossary/term/ransomware\">csrc.nist.gov/glossary/term/ransomware</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cisa.gov/topics/cyber-threats-and-advisories/malware-phishing-and-ransomware\">cisa.gov/topics/cyber-threats-and-advisories/malware-phishing-and-ransomware</a></p>","lessons":["d5l2"]},"e2q47":{"id":"e2q47","exam":2,"n":47,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>HarborStyle Retail runs a large on-premises VMware estate for its inventory, billing, and store operations systems. The company wants to move these workloads to Google Cloud, but it wants administrators to continue using familiar VMware tools and existing operating procedures with minimal change. Which Google Cloud service should HarborStyle Retail use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Compute Engine</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Google Cloud VMware Engine</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. VMware vSphere</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Bare Metal Solution</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Google Cloud VMware Engine</strong></p>\n<p>Google Cloud VMware Engine runs a full VMware environment as a managed service on Google Cloud. Admins keep using the VMware tools and procedures they already know. The clues are \"large on-premises VMware estate\" and \"familiar VMware tools … with minimal change\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Compute Engine</strong>: Moving to Compute Engine means converting VMs and leaving VMware tools behind.\n- <strong>C. VMware vSphere</strong>: vSphere is VMware's own software, not a Google Cloud service.\n- <strong>D. Bare Metal Solution</strong>: Bare Metal is for specialized workloads like Oracle databases, not for keeping VMware operations.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Move VMware workloads and keep VMware tools → Google Cloud VMware Engine.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/vmware-engine\">cloud.google.com/vmware-engine</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/vmware-engine/docs/overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/vmware-engine/docs/overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/vmware-engine/docs/workloads/howto-migrate-workloads\">docs.cloud.google.com/vmware-engine/docs/workloads/howto-migrate-workloads</a></p>","lessons":["d4l6"]},"e2q48":{"id":"e2q48","exam":2,"n":48,"domain":6,"html":"<p><strong>FreshCartGo, a grocery delivery mobile app, runs its backend on a cloud provider and has an SLA that promises a defined level of monthly service availability. During one month, the provider fails to meet the agreed availability target. What should FreshCartGo typically expect from the cloud provider?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Automatic increase in subscription fees</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Permanent shutdown of the affected cloud service</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Service credit for the service interruption</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Automatic expansion of the application’s error budget</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Service credit for the service interruption</strong></p>\n<p>An SLA is a contract that promises a level of service. If the provider misses it, the usual remedy is a service credit, which is a discount on future bills. The clue is \"fails to meet the agreed availability target\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Increase in subscription fees</strong>: A missed SLA leads to compensation, not higher prices.\n- <strong>B. Permanent shutdown of the service</strong>: Missing a target does not shut the service down.\n- <strong>D. Expansion of the error budget</strong>: The error budget is an internal SRE concept tied to the SLO; it does not grow because of an outage.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Missed SLA → service credits for the customer.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/terms/sla\">cloud.google.com/terms/sla</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/storage/sla\">cloud.google.com/storage/sla</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/stackdriver/docs/solutions/slo-monitoring\">docs.cloud.google.com/stackdriver/docs/solutions/slo-monitoring</a></p>\n<p><strong>⚠️ Note:</strong> Under Google Cloud SLAs, customers usually must request credits within a time window; they are not automatic. \"Service credit\" is still the right answer.</p>","lessons":["d6l4","d6l5"]},"e2q49":{"id":"e2q49","exam":2,"n":49,"domain":6,"html":"<p><strong>TaskNest is a project management app that is adopting a DevOps approach for its software delivery teams. The company wants to use tools and automation to make development, testing, deployment, and operations faster and more reliable. Which operational challenge will this help TaskNest resolve?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Repetitive manual tasks that slow down workflows</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Continuous supervision of creative design work</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Vendor distribution and product supply-chain delays</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Faulty physical hardware that blocks new ideas</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Repetitive manual tasks that slow down workflows</strong></p>\n<p>DevOps uses automation, like CI/CD pipelines and automated testing, to remove slow, error-prone manual steps. This makes building, testing, and releasing software faster and more reliable. The clue is \"tools and automation to make … faster and more reliable\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. Supervision of creative design work</strong>: DevOps is about software delivery and operations, not managing design work.\n- <strong>C. Supply-chain delays</strong>: Physical product supply chains are outside DevOps.\n- <strong>D. Faulty physical hardware</strong>: Hardware failures are an infrastructure issue, not what DevOps automation solves.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> DevOps = automate repetitive work to ship faster and more reliably.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/devops\">cloud.google.com/devops</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/devops\">docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/devops</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/operational-excellence/automate-and-manage-change\">docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/operational-excellence/automate-and-manage-change</a></p>","lessons":["d6l6"]},"e2q50":{"id":"e2q50","exam":2,"n":50,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>HomeFixNow, a home repair booking website, wants to build web applications that can automatically scale as customer traffic increases or decreases. The development team also wants Google Cloud to handle the underlying application infrastructure so they can focus on writing code. Which Google Cloud product should HomeFixNow use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Anthos</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Apigee</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. AutoML</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. App Engine</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. App Engine</strong></p>\n<p>App Engine is a fully managed platform (PaaS) for web applications. Developers deploy code, and Google handles the infrastructure and scales the app up and down with traffic. The clues are \"build web applications\", \"automatically scale\", and \"focus on writing code\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Anthos</strong>: Anthos (now GKE Enterprise) manages Kubernetes across hybrid and multicloud environments; it is not a simple app host.\n- <strong>B. Apigee</strong>: Apigee manages APIs; it does not host web apps.\n- <strong>C. AutoML</strong>: AutoML trains custom ML models, not web applications.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Deploy web app code, Google handles scaling and infrastructure → App Engine (or Cloud Run).</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/appengine\">cloud.google.com/appengine</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/appengine/docs\">docs.cloud.google.com/appengine/docs</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/appengine/docs/standard/how-instances-are-managed\">docs.cloud.google.com/appengine/docs/standard/how-instances-are-managed</a></p>\n<p><strong>⚠️ Note:</strong> App Engine is right among these options, but for new web apps Google now usually steers toward Cloud Run. Pick the keyed answer on this exam.</p>","lessons":["d4l4"]},"e3q1":{"id":"e3q1","exam":3,"n":1,"domain":6,"html":"<p><strong>MediCart, a healthcare shopping website, runs a production application on Google Cloud. Its developers want to understand what Cloud Debugger helped teams do when investigating application behavior. What was the main purpose of Cloud Debugger?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Apply code changes instantly in production without affecting the service level objective (SLO).</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Inspect running application source code and state in real time without causing user downtime.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Manage source code repositories and speed up application development.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Analyze live source code only after the application is taken offline for user downtime.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Inspect running application source code and state in real time without causing user downtime.</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud Debugger let developers take snapshots of a live application's variables and call stack, and add log points, while the app kept serving users. The stem asks about investigating behavior in production, which points to inspecting state without stopping the app.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Apply code changes instantly in production</strong>: A debugger observes code; it never deployed or changed code.\n- <strong>C. Manage source code repositories</strong>: That describes a source repository tool, not a debugger.\n- <strong>D. Analyze code only after taking the app offline</strong>: The whole point was to avoid downtime, so this is the opposite.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Debugger = look inside a running app without stopping it.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/stackdriver/docs/deprecations/debugger-deprecation\">docs.cloud.google.com/stackdriver/docs/deprecations/debugger-deprecation</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/gcp/diagnose-problems-in-your-production-apps-faster-with-google-cloud-debugger\">cloud.google.com/blog/products/gcp/diagnose-problems-in-your-production-apps-faster-with-google-cloud-debugger</a>\n- <a href=\"https://support.google.com/cloud/answer/6323288?hl=en\">support.google.com/cloud/answer/6323288?hl=en</a></p>\n<p><strong>⚠️ Note:</strong> Cloud Debugger was deprecated in 2022 and shut down in 2023; its open-source successor is Snapshot Debugger. The answer correctly describes what it did, so pick the keyed answer on this exam.</p>","lessons":["d6l8"]},"e3q2":{"id":"e3q2","exam":3,"n":2,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>NorthPeak Logistics is moving toward Google Cloud and wants its internal IT team to develop strong cloud skills as part of a long-term business transformation. Leadership wants employees to learn from trusted Google Cloud training content while also sharing knowledge across teams. What should NorthPeak Logistics do?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Focus on training existing employees instead of hiring new employees who already have cloud experience.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Give elevated access to third-party consultants and contractors so they can cover internal IT knowledge gaps.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Encourage self-directed, separate learning using official training materials.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Build a culture of continuous peer-to-peer learning using official training materials.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Build a culture of continuous peer-to-peer learning using official training materials.</strong></p>\n<p>The stem has two clues: learn from trusted Google Cloud content, and share knowledge across teams. Peer-to-peer learning built on official training covers both. Transformation is about people and culture, not only technology, so a lasting learning culture is the goal.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Train existing staff instead of hiring</strong>: Training staff is good, but refusing to hire cloud-experienced people is an early-stage habit; mature organizations upskill and hire. It also says nothing about sharing knowledge.\n- <strong>B. Give elevated access to consultants</strong>: It outsources knowledge instead of building internal skills and weakens least privilege.\n- <strong>C. Self-directed, separate learning</strong>: It uses official materials but misses the knowledge-sharing part.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Upskilling for transformation = official training + a culture of sharing.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/training\">cloud.google.com/learn/training</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn\">cloud.google.com/learn</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/certification\">cloud.google.com/learn/certification</a></p>","lessons":["d1l7"]},"e3q3":{"id":"e3q3","exam":3,"n":3,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>BrightDesk Assist is building a new support automation platform for its banking clients. Instead of only predicting which customers may complain, the company wants an AI system that can understand a customer’s goal, decide the next steps, call different internal tools, update records, and complete the support workflow with little or no human involvement. Which concept best describes this type of AI capability?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Descriptive analytics</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Generative AI</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Agentic AI</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Business Intelligence</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Agentic AI</strong></p>\n<p>Agentic AI systems pursue a goal on their own. They reason, plan next steps, call tools and APIs, and take actions to complete a workflow with little human help. The clues are \"decide the next steps\", \"call different internal tools\" and \"complete the workflow\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Descriptive analytics</strong>: It explains what happened in past data; it takes no actions.\n- <strong>B. Generative AI</strong>: It creates content, but on its own it does not plan and act across tools.\n- <strong>D. Business Intelligence</strong>: BI is reporting and dashboards for human decision makers.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Goal + plan + use tools + take action = agentic AI.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/discover/what-is-agentic-ai\">cloud.google.com/discover/what-is-agentic-ai</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/discover/what-are-ai-agents\">cloud.google.com/discover/what-are-ai-agents</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/overview</a></p>","lessons":["d3l1","d3l8"]},"e3q4":{"id":"e3q4","exam":3,"n":4,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>StreamHive, a media learning app, has accumulated a large archive of unstructured files such as recorded lessons, podcasts, images, and raw video clips. The company wants a Google Cloud storage solution that can hold large volumes of these files reliably and cost-effectively. Which storage solution should StreamHive use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Spanner</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Cloud Storage</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Bigtable</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Cloud SQL</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cloud Storage</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud Storage is Google Cloud's object storage. It holds any amount of unstructured files such as video, audio and images, with high durability and storage classes that lower cost for rarely used data. The clues are \"unstructured files\", \"large volumes\" and \"cost-effectively\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Spanner</strong>: A globally distributed relational database for transactions, not a file store.\n- <strong>C. Bigtable</strong>: A NoSQL wide-column database for high-throughput key-value data, not media files.\n- <strong>D. Cloud SQL</strong>: A managed relational database for structured, transactional data.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Files, images, video, backups, archives = Cloud Storage.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/storage/docs/introduction\">docs.cloud.google.com/storage/docs/introduction</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/storage/docs/storage-classes\">docs.cloud.google.com/storage/docs/storage-classes</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/bigtable/docs/overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/bigtable/docs/overview</a></p>","lessons":["d2l6","d2l7"]},"e3q5":{"id":"e3q5","exam":3,"n":5,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>MediRide, a health transportation booking app, forms a partnership with CareInn, a senior living network. CareInn securely shares its resident appointment and stay-preference data directly with MediRide so MediRide can offer better ride packages to shared customers. From MediRide’s perspective, what type of data is this?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. First-party data</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Second-party data</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Third-party data</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Public domain data</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Second-party data</strong></p>\n<p>Second-party data is another organization's first-party data that it shares directly with you as a partner. CareInn collected the data from its own residents and shares it straight with MediRide, so for MediRide it is second-party.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. First-party data</strong>: MediRide did not collect it from its own customers.\n- <strong>C. Third-party data</strong>: Third-party data is bought from a provider that aggregates data, not shared directly by a partner.\n- <strong>D. Public domain data</strong>: This data is private and shared securely, not openly available.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Own customers = first-party. Partner shares directly = second-party. Bought = third-party.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://support.google.com/adspolicy/answer/6242605\">support.google.com/adspolicy/answer/6242605</a>\n- <a href=\"https://business.google.com/us/privacy/strategy\">business.google.com/us/privacy/strategy</a>\n- <a href=\"https://business.adobe.com/blog/basics/dmp-cdp-crm-whats-the-difference-and-what-do-you-need\">business.adobe.com/blog/basics/dmp-cdp-crm-whats-the-difference-and-what-do-you-need</a></p>","lessons":["d2l3"]},"e3q6":{"id":"e3q6","exam":3,"n":6,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>BrightCast, a sports highlights streaming website, stores large collections of match commentary audio clips and game replay video files. These files do not follow a fixed table structure or predefined schema, and they are stored along with HTML pages and JSON metadata documents. How should the audio and video files be classified?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Structured data</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Semi-structured data</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Unstructured data</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Relational data</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Unstructured data</strong></p>\n<p>Audio and video files have no fixed schema or rows and columns. That makes them unstructured data. The stem even says they \"do not follow a fixed table structure or predefined schema\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Structured data</strong>: Structured data fits a fixed schema, like tables in a database.\n- <strong>B. Semi-structured data</strong>: This fits the JSON metadata and HTML, which have tags or keys, but not the audio and video files.\n- <strong>D. Relational data</strong>: Relational data is structured data in related tables.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Audio, video, images, free text = unstructured. JSON, HTML, XML = semi-structured.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/storage\">cloud.google.com/storage</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/object-table-introduction\">docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/object-table-introduction</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/ml-on-gcp-best-practices\">docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/ml-on-gcp-best-practices</a></p>","lessons":["d2l3"]},"e3q7":{"id":"e3q7","exam":3,"n":7,"domain":5,"html":"<p><strong>PixelHive Media is building a shared analytics environment on Google Cloud. Its marketing, finance, and engineering teams all need access to the same project, but each team needs different permissions based on the work they perform. What is the fastest and most efficient way to manage access?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Map IAM roles to job functions for each department.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Assign IAM basic roles to each employee.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Apply least-privilege roles separately for each employee.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Create one shared service account for all departments.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Map IAM roles to job functions for each department.</strong></p>\n<p>Each team needs different permissions, so define roles by what each job requires and grant them per department. This is far easier to manage than handling every person one by one. In practice you put each team in a group and grant the roles to the group.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. Assign basic roles to each employee</strong>: Basic roles (Owner, Editor, Viewer) are too broad and per-person grants don't scale.\n- <strong>C. Least-privilege roles separately for each employee</strong>: Least privilege is right, but per-employee management is slow and error-prone.\n- <strong>D. One shared service account for all departments</strong>: It removes accountability and gives everyone the same access.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Grant roles by job function to groups, not to individuals.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/iam/docs/overview\">cloud.google.com/iam/docs/overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/iam/docs/roles-overview\">cloud.google.com/iam/docs/roles-overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/iam/docs/choose-predefined-roles\">cloud.google.com/iam/docs/choose-predefined-roles</a></p>\n<p><strong>⚠️ Note:</strong> Google's stated best practice is to grant roles to groups. Read \"map roles to job functions for each department\" as the group-based answer. It is the best option here.</p>","lessons":["d5l4"]},"e3q8":{"id":"e3q8","exam":3,"n":8,"domain":5,"html":"<p><strong>BrightPath Finance is preparing for a Google Cloud compliance review. The audit team is evaluating the company’s network design, identity and access controls, monitoring coverage, and whether security policies are consistently applied across projects and resources. What term best describes this complete view of the company’s cybersecurity readiness?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Threat response</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Security posture</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Cryptomining</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Digital transformation</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Security posture</strong></p>\n<p>Security posture is the overall state of an organization's security: its network design, access controls, monitoring and how consistently policies are applied. The stem asks for a \"complete view of cybersecurity readiness\", which is the definition of posture.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Threat response</strong>: That is how you react to an incident, only one part of the picture.\n- <strong>C. Cryptomining</strong>: That is an attack type where stolen compute mines cryptocurrency.\n- <strong>D. Digital transformation</strong>: That is using technology to change how a business works, not a security term.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Overall security readiness across the environment = security posture.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/security-command-center/docs/security-posture-overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/security-command-center/docs/security-posture-overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/security-command-center/docs/security-command-center-overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/security-command-center/docs/security-command-center-overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/security\">docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/security</a></p>","lessons":["d5l1","d5l7"]},"e3q9":{"id":"e3q9","exam":3,"n":9,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>CareBridge Advisors, a healthcare consulting company, needs its employee portal to share and access different volumes of confidential staff documents across offices in several countries. Why would using Cloud Storage be beneficial for CareBridge Advisors?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. It stores geographically distributed copies of data to reduce the risk of loss.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. It provides a paid add-on that turns on data encryption.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. It randomly places data in different storage locations to protect against attacks.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. It has a small minimum storage quantity requirement.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. It stores geographically distributed copies of data to reduce the risk of loss.</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud Storage stores data redundantly, and multi-region or dual-region buckets keep copies in separate geographic locations. This protects against loss and suits a company with offices in several countries.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. Paid add-on for encryption</strong>: Google Cloud encrypts all data at rest by default at no extra cost.\n- <strong>C. Randomly places data to protect against attacks</strong>: Data placement is based on the location you choose, not random.\n- <strong>D. Small minimum storage quantity</strong>: Cloud Storage has no minimum storage quantity; this is not a real feature to rely on.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Cloud Storage = durable, redundant, optionally multi-region object storage.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/locations\">cloud.google.com/storage/docs/locations</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/availability-durability\">cloud.google.com/storage/docs/availability-durability</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/encryption/default-keys\">cloud.google.com/storage/docs/encryption/default-keys</a></p>\n<p><strong>⚠️ Note:</strong> Geographically separate copies are only true for multi-region and dual-region buckets. A regional bucket keeps redundant copies across zones in one region. The key is still the best option, so pick it on this exam.</p>","lessons":["d2l7"]},"e3q10":{"id":"e3q10","exam":3,"n":10,"domain":5,"html":"<p><strong>TrailCart Market runs an online shopping platform on Google Cloud. During a holiday sale, a malicious botnet starts sending a huge volume of fake HTTP requests to the application, trying to exhaust resources and prevent real shoppers from accessing the site. Which Google Cloud service should TrailCart Market use to help protect the application from Distributed Denial-of-Service attacks?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Security Command Center</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Google Cloud Armor</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Identity-Aware Proxy (IAP)</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Cloud Key Management Service (KMS)</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Google Cloud Armor</strong></p>\n<p>Google Cloud Armor protects applications behind Google's load balancers from DDoS attacks and web attacks. It can filter and rate-limit malicious traffic before it reaches the app. The clues are \"botnet\", \"huge volume of fake HTTP requests\" and \"DDoS\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Security Command Center</strong>: It gives visibility into security risks and findings; it doesn't block attack traffic.\n- <strong>C. Identity-Aware Proxy (IAP)</strong>: It controls who can reach an app based on identity, not volumetric attacks on a public site.\n- <strong>D. Cloud KMS</strong>: It manages encryption keys.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> DDoS or web attack protection = Cloud Armor.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/armor/docs/cloud-armor-overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/armor/docs/cloud-armor-overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/security-command-center/docs/security-command-center-overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/security-command-center/docs/security-command-center-overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/kms/docs/key-management-service\">docs.cloud.google.com/kms/docs/key-management-service</a></p>","lessons":["d5l2","d5l5"],"same":["e4q2"]},"e3q11":{"id":"e3q11","exam":3,"n":11,"domain":6,"html":"<p><strong>FreshTrail Delivery is running applications and infrastructure on Google Cloud. The operations team wants to understand the health and performance of its services by gathering measurements such as CPU usage, latency, request count, and application-specific values. What does monitoring mean in this cloud operations context?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Watching cloud spending in real time to make sure budgets are not exceeded.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Collecting predefined and custom metrics from applications and infrastructure.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Recording user actions to guarantee compliance with privacy regulations.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Tracking user locations to document regional access and usage.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Collecting predefined and custom metrics from applications and infrastructure.</strong></p>\n<p>In cloud operations, monitoring means collecting metrics, both built-in ones like CPU and latency and custom application values, to see service health and performance. Cloud Monitoring does this and supports dashboards and alerts.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Watching cloud spending</strong>: That is cost management or budgets, not operational monitoring.\n- <strong>C. Recording user actions for compliance</strong>: That is closer to audit logging.\n- <strong>D. Tracking user locations</strong>: That is not what operational monitoring means.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Monitoring = metrics. Logging = events. Tracing = request paths.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/monitoring/docs\">docs.cloud.google.com/monitoring/docs</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/monitoring/docs/collect-metrics-overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/monitoring/docs/collect-metrics-overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/stackdriver/docs\">docs.cloud.google.com/stackdriver/docs</a></p>","lessons":["d6l7","d6l8"]},"e3q12":{"id":"e3q12","exam":3,"n":12,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>FitFleet, a fitness tracking mobile app, runs its backend microservices on Google Kubernetes Engine. Developers frequently test fixes for application issues and want a platform that helps them deploy, validate, and adjust changes quickly. What makes Google Kubernetes Engine an effective solution for these developers?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. It shortens the time required to test and refine different fixes.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. It automatically provides intelligent recommendations to redesign application code.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. It eliminates the requirement to package applications into containers.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. It keeps application source code copied across multiple servers for cross-team collaboration.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. It shortens the time required to test and refine different fixes.</strong></p>\n<p>GKE runs containers and supports fast, repeatable deployments, rolling updates and easy rollbacks. That lets developers deploy a fix, check it, and adjust quickly. The clue is \"deploy, validate, and adjust changes quickly\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. Recommendations to redesign code</strong>: GKE runs workloads; it does not review or redesign code.\n- <strong>C. Eliminates packaging into containers</strong>: GKE runs containers, so packaging into containers is required.\n- <strong>D. Copies source code across servers</strong>: GKE runs container images, not shared copies of source code for collaboration.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> GKE = fast, consistent container deployments and rollbacks.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/kubernetes-engine-overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/kubernetes-engine-overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs\">docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/best-practices-continuous-integration-delivery-kubernetes\">docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/best-practices-continuous-integration-delivery-kubernetes</a></p>\n<p><strong>⚠️ Note:</strong> The keyed answer is vague. Faster testing is a general benefit of containers and CI/CD, not something only GKE provides. It is keyed because the other options are clearly false.</p>","lessons":["d4l3"]},"e3q13":{"id":"e3q13","exam":3,"n":13,"domain":6,"html":"<p><strong>FitTrackGo, a fitness tracking mobile app, runs several customer-facing services on Google Cloud. The operations team wants to measure the main SRE golden signals for the platform. They specifically need to identify how much of the available infrastructure capacity is being used and whether resources such as CPU, memory, or I/O are becoming constrained. Which metric should they focus on?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Latency</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Traffic</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Saturation</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Errors</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Saturation</strong></p>\n<p>Saturation measures how full the system is: how much CPU, memory or I/O capacity is used and whether it is becoming a constraint. The clues are \"how much of the available capacity is being used\" and \"constrained\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Latency</strong>: Latency is how long a request takes.\n- <strong>B. Traffic</strong>: Traffic is how much demand hits the system, such as requests per second.\n- <strong>D. Errors</strong>: Errors is the rate of failed requests.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Four golden signals: latency (time), traffic (demand), errors (failures), saturation (how full).</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://sre.google/sre-book/monitoring-distributed-systems/\">sre.google/sre-book/monitoring-distributed-systems/</a>\n- <a href=\"https://sre.google/sre-book/service-level-objectives/\">sre.google/sre-book/service-level-objectives/</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/perspectives/fsi/operational-excellence\">docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/perspectives/fsi/operational-excellence</a></p>","lessons":["d6l7"]},"e3q14":{"id":"e3q14","exam":3,"n":14,"domain":6,"html":"<p><strong>QuickFare, a ride-booking website, runs its checkout, driver-matching, pricing, and notification features as separate microservices on Google Cloud. The engineering team is investigating slow requests and wants to see how a single customer request moves through each service and how much time is spent at every step. Which Google Cloud tool should they use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Cloud Trace</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Cloud Profiler</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Cloud Logging</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE)</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cloud Trace</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud Trace is distributed tracing. It follows a single request as it moves through each microservice and shows how much time each step takes. The clues are \"how a single request moves through each service\" and \"time spent at every step\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. Cloud Profiler</strong>: It shows which code functions use the most CPU or memory, not the request path across services.\n- <strong>C. Cloud Logging</strong>: It stores log events but doesn't map a request's timing across services.\n- <strong>D. GKE</strong>: It runs containers; it is not an observability tool.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Slow request across microservices = Cloud Trace.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/trace/docs/overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/trace/docs/overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/profiler/docs/about-profiler\">docs.cloud.google.com/profiler/docs/about-profiler</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/logging/docs/overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/logging/docs/overview</a></p>","lessons":["d6l8"]},"e3q15":{"id":"e3q15","exam":3,"n":15,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>Trendora, a regional fashion marketplace, is building a new ecommerce application on Google Cloud. The application needs a cost-effective transactional database for customer orders, carts, and payments, and it will serve users only from one region. Which service should Trendora use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. BigQuery</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Bigtable</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Spanner</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Cloud SQL</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cloud SQL</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud SQL is a managed relational database for transactional (OLTP) workloads like orders and payments. It is cost-effective when the app serves one region. The clues are \"transactional\", \"cost-effective\" and \"only from one region\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. BigQuery</strong>: A data warehouse for analytics, not an app's transactional database.\n- <strong>B. Bigtable</strong>: A NoSQL database for huge, high-throughput key-value data, not relational transactions.\n- <strong>C. Spanner</strong>: Relational and transactional, but built for global scale; more than a single-region store needs.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Relational + regional + cost-effective = Cloud SQL. Relational + global scale = Spanner.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/sql/docs\">docs.cloud.google.com/sql/docs</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs\">docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/bigtable/docs\">docs.cloud.google.com/bigtable/docs</a></p>","lessons":["d2l5","d2l6"]},"e3q16":{"id":"e3q16","exam":3,"n":16,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>StyleNest, an online apparel store, is training a machine learning model to suggest relevant products to shoppers. The training dataset includes customers’ past purchases and browsing behavior, but it is missing profile attributes such as age group, gender, and location. The recommendation results are weak because an important part of the dataset was not included. Which data quality dimension is causing the poor model performance?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Validity</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Accuracy</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Timeliness</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Completeness</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Completeness</strong></p>\n<p>Completeness asks whether all the required data is present. Profile attributes like age group, gender and location are missing, so the dataset is incomplete. The clue is \"an important part of the dataset was not included\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Validity</strong>: Validity is whether values follow the expected format or rules.\n- <strong>B. Accuracy</strong>: Accuracy is whether values are correct, not whether they exist.\n- <strong>C. Timeliness</strong>: Timeliness is whether data is current and available when needed.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Missing data = completeness. Wrong values = accuracy. Wrong format = validity.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-data-governance\">cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-data-governance</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/dataplex/docs/use-auto-data-quality\">docs.cloud.google.com/dataplex/docs/use-auto-data-quality</a>\n- <a href=\"https://ibm.com/think/topics/data-quality-dimensions\">ibm.com/think/topics/data-quality-dimensions</a></p>","lessons":["d2l4"],"same":["e3q46"]},"e3q17":{"id":"e3q17","exam":3,"n":17,"domain":6,"html":"<p><strong>PulseCart, a mobile shopping app, wants to improve how it releases application updates. The engineering team wants to reduce user disruption when bugs appear after a release. What DevOps practice should PulseCart use while developing its application?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Pause production until all bugs have been eliminated</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Prioritize fixing large bugs during production because they are easier to review</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Implement small changes incrementally to reduce recovery time when bugs appear</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Implement large changes together to make rolling back easier when bugs appear</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Implement small changes incrementally to reduce recovery time when bugs appear</strong></p>\n<p>A core DevOps and SRE practice is to ship small, frequent changes. Small changes are easier to test, easier to find the cause of a bug in, and faster to roll back. That reduces disruption to users.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Pause production until all bugs are gone</strong>: Bug-free software is not realistic; this stops delivery.\n- <strong>B. Prioritize fixing large bugs in production</strong>: Bug priority should follow user and business impact, not size, and large bugs are harder to review, not easier.\n- <strong>D. Large changes together make rollback easier</strong>: Large batches make rollback harder and riskier.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Small, incremental releases = faster recovery, less risk.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/devops\">cloud.google.com/devops</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/architecture/devops\">cloud.google.com/architecture/devops</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/architecture/devops/devops-tech-continuous-delivery\">cloud.google.com/architecture/devops/devops-tech-continuous-delivery</a></p>","lessons":["d6l6"]},"e3q18":{"id":"e3q18","exam":3,"n":18,"domain":6,"html":"<p><strong>CityParkPass, a parking reservation mobile app, has recently launched on Google Cloud. The operations team is reviewing the service’s reliability signals and is specifically tracking how much time passes between a user sending a request and the application returning a response. Which performance metric are they measuring?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Traffic</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Saturation</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Latency</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Error rate</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Latency</strong></p>\n<p>Latency is the time between a request and its response. The stem describes exactly that: \"how much time passes between a user sending a request and the application returning a response\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Traffic</strong>: Traffic measures demand, such as requests per second.\n- <strong>B. Saturation</strong>: Saturation measures how full the system's resources are.\n- <strong>D. Error rate</strong>: Error rate measures how many requests fail.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Wait time = latency.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://sre.google/sre-book/monitoring-distributed-systems/\">sre.google/sre-book/monitoring-distributed-systems/</a>\n- <a href=\"https://sre.google/sre-book/service-level-objectives/\">sre.google/sre-book/service-level-objectives/</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/operational-excellence/measure-and-achieve-reliability-goals\">docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/operational-excellence/measure-and-achieve-reliability-goals</a></p>","lessons":["d6l7"]},"e3q19":{"id":"e3q19","exam":3,"n":19,"domain":5,"html":"<p><strong>LogiNest Retail uses Google Security Operations to strengthen its Cloud SecOps program. The security team depends on analyzed information about attacker goals, common targets, malware campaigns, tactics, techniques, and procedures so they can detect risks earlier and defend the environment proactively. What is this type of cybersecurity information called?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Threat intelligence</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Threat response</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Compliance auditing</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Identity management</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Threat intelligence</strong></p>\n<p>Threat intelligence is analyzed information about attackers: their goals, targets, malware campaigns and tactics, techniques and procedures (TTPs). Security teams use it to detect and stop threats earlier. Google Security Operations includes threat intelligence from Google and Mandiant.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. Threat response</strong>: That is acting on an incident, not the information used to anticipate it.\n- <strong>C. Compliance auditing</strong>: That checks controls against regulations or standards.\n- <strong>D. Identity management</strong>: That controls user identities and access.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Knowledge about attackers and their methods = threat intelligence.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/chronicle/docs/detection\">docs.cloud.google.com/chronicle/docs/detection</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/security/products/threat-intelligence\">cloud.google.com/security/products/threat-intelligence</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/chronicle/docs/overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/chronicle/docs/overview</a></p>","lessons":["d5l7"]},"e3q20":{"id":"e3q20","exam":3,"n":20,"domain":5,"html":"<p><strong>MedNest Health has strong internal security controls for its Google Cloud environment, but it recently experienced a data exposure incident. The investigation found that attackers entered through a small appointment-booking plugin from an outside vendor that was connected to MedNest Health’s software delivery process. Which modern cybersecurity threat vector does this situation demonstrate?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Unsecured third-party systems</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Physical damage</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. LLM attacks</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Zero trust architecture</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Unsecured third-party systems</strong></p>\n<p>The attackers came in through an outside vendor's plugin connected to the software delivery process. That is a supply chain risk from an unsecured third-party system. Your security is only as strong as the vendors and components you connect to.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. Physical damage</strong>: Nothing physical happened; this was a software entry point.\n- <strong>C. LLM attacks</strong>: No AI model or prompt was involved.\n- <strong>D. Zero trust architecture</strong>: Zero trust is a security model, not a threat vector.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Breach through a vendor's plugin or library = third-party / supply chain risk.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/software-supply-chain-security/docs/overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/software-supply-chain-security/docs/overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/software-supply-chain-security/docs/dependencies\">docs.cloud.google.com/software-supply-chain-security/docs/dependencies</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/solutions/ciso-third-party-risk-management\">cloud.google.com/solutions/ciso-third-party-risk-management</a></p>","lessons":["d5l2"]},"e3q21":{"id":"e3q21","exam":3,"n":21,"domain":6,"html":"<p><strong>QuickCartPay is a payment-processing website that runs business-critical services on Google Cloud in production. The operations team needs the fastest support response for urgent incidents and also wants guidance from a dedicated Technical Account Manager. Which Google Cloud Customer Care service level should QuickCartPay choose?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Enhanced</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Basic</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Premium</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Standard</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Premium</strong></p>\n<p>Premium Support is the Customer Care tier for business-critical workloads. It has the fastest response for critical issues and includes a dedicated Technical Account Manager (TAM). The clues are \"fastest support response\" and \"dedicated Technical Account Manager\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Enhanced</strong>: Faster than Standard, but it has no dedicated TAM and isn't the fastest.\n- <strong>B. Basic</strong>: Basic covers billing support and documentation, not technical incident response.\n- <strong>D. Standard</strong>: Meant for smaller workloads with slower response times and no TAM.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Fastest response + TAM = Premium.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/support\">cloud.google.com/support</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/support/docs/overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/support/docs/overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/support/docs/premium\">docs.cloud.google.com/support/docs/premium</a></p>","lessons":["d6l10"]},"e3q22":{"id":"e3q22","exam":3,"n":22,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>Northstar Clinics runs some sensitive patient-management systems in its own private data center, but it also wants to use Google Cloud services for analytics, application scaling, and newer digital services. The company wants an architecture that lets it use both environments together. What kind of architecture does a hybrid cloud offer Northstar Clinics?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. A combination of different virtualized servers</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. A combination of serverless computing and public cloud services</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. A combination of private cloud infrastructure and public cloud services</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. A combination of multiple public cloud provider services</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. A combination of private cloud infrastructure and public cloud services</strong></p>\n<p>Hybrid cloud combines on-premises or private cloud infrastructure with public cloud services, working together. Northstar keeps sensitive systems in its own data center and uses Google Cloud for analytics and new services, which is hybrid.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Different virtualized servers</strong>: Virtualization alone doesn't make an architecture hybrid.\n- <strong>B. Serverless plus public cloud</strong>: Serverless runs in the public cloud, so this is just public cloud.\n- <strong>D. Multiple public cloud providers</strong>: That is multicloud, not hybrid.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Hybrid = on-prem/private + public. Multicloud = more than one public cloud.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-hybrid-cloud\">cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-hybrid-cloud</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-cloud-computing\">cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-cloud-computing</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/hybrid-multicloud-patterns-and-practices\">docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/hybrid-multicloud-patterns-and-practices</a></p>","lessons":["d1l4"]},"e3q23":{"id":"e3q23","exam":3,"n":23,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>ClearNest Realty runs an internal portal where field agents upload apartment photos after site visits. The compliance team currently reviews every image manually because some photos may accidentally include sensitive documents, such as national ID cards or payment cards. ClearNest wants its developers to add automated image analysis to the portal as quickly as possible, without building and training a custom model. Which solution should ClearNest use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Pre-trained APIs</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. AutoML</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Custom training</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. BigQuery ML</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Pre-trained APIs</strong></p>\n<p>Pre-trained APIs such as the Cloud Vision API are ready-to-use models that developers call directly. They can detect text and objects in images with no training. The clues are \"as quickly as possible\" and \"without building and training a custom model\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. AutoML</strong>: AutoML still needs labeled data and training a custom model.\n- <strong>C. Custom training</strong>: This takes the most time and ML expertise.\n- <strong>D. BigQuery ML</strong>: It trains models with SQL on data in BigQuery, not a ready-made image analysis service.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Common task + fastest + no training = pre-trained API.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/vision\">cloud.google.com/vision</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/vision/docs\">docs.cloud.google.com/vision/docs</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/sensitive-data-protection/docs/classification-redaction\">docs.cloud.google.com/sensitive-data-protection/docs/classification-redaction</a></p>","lessons":["d3l3","d3l4","d3l5"]},"e3q24":{"id":"e3q24","exam":3,"n":24,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>StorySpark is a content creation app that wants to explain a basic AI concept to its new product team. The team needs to understand what type of AI can produce new outputs such as text, images, audio, code, or summaries based on user prompts. What is generative AI?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. A machine learning model used only for language translation</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. A specific field of AI that can be used to create content</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. A general term that includes every type of machine learning algorithm</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. An AI model used only for labeling images</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. A specific field of AI that can be used to create content</strong></p>\n<p>Generative AI is a subset of AI that creates new content, such as text, images, audio, code and summaries, from prompts. The stem's clue is \"produce new outputs\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Only for language translation</strong>: Translation is one possible use, not the definition.\n- <strong>C. Includes every type of ML algorithm</strong>: Generative AI is a subset of ML, not the umbrella term.\n- <strong>D. Only for labeling images</strong>: Labeling images is classification, a different task.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Generative AI = creates new content.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/use-cases/generative-ai\">cloud.google.com/use-cases/generative-ai</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/discover/what-is-generative-ai\">cloud.google.com/discover/what-is-generative-ai</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/ai/generative-ai\">cloud.google.com/ai/generative-ai</a></p>","lessons":["d3l1","d3l7","d3l8"]},"e3q25":{"id":"e3q25","exam":3,"n":25,"domain":6,"html":"<p><strong>MetroNest Apps runs several workloads on Google Cloud for its product, finance, and customer support teams. Recently, leadership noticed that some teams are spending more than expected, but the teams do not clearly see how their usage affects the monthly bill. What should MetroNest Apps do to help control cloud costs?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Improve the physical server purchasing process to reduce costs.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Share cost views with each department to create more accountability.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Change the cost model from operational expenditure to capital expenditure.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Apply one common tag to every cloud resource.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Share cost views with each department to create more accountability.</strong></p>\n<p>Teams can't control costs they can't see. Giving each department visibility into its own spending creates accountability and drives better decisions. This is a core FinOps idea: shared responsibility for cloud costs.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Improve physical server purchasing</strong>: Buying physical servers isn't part of cloud cost control.\n- <strong>C. Change from OpEx to CapEx</strong>: Cloud is OpEx by nature; switching models doesn't fix visibility.\n- <strong>D. One common tag on every resource</strong>: One tag for everything can't separate costs by team. Labels must differ per team to help.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Visibility per team = accountability = cost control.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/cost-management\">cloud.google.com/cost-management</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/billing/docs/reports\">docs.cloud.google.com/billing/docs/reports</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/resource-manager/docs/labels-overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/resource-manager/docs/labels-overview</a></p>","lessons":["d6l1","d6l3"]},"e3q26":{"id":"e3q26","exam":3,"n":26,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>TrailPulse is a fitness tracking app that receives live activity events from thousands of users, such as step counts, heart-rate readings, and GPS updates. The analytics team wants to analyze these incoming events as they arrive so the app can show near real-time insights. What is a defining function of streaming analytics?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Processing data records continuously</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Processing data records in batches</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Processing a single historical data reload</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Accessing data with high latency</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Processing data records continuously</strong></p>\n<p>Streaming analytics processes events continuously as they arrive, so insights are available in near real time. The clue is \"analyze these incoming events as they arrive\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. Processing in batches</strong>: Batch processing collects data and handles it later in groups.\n- <strong>C. A single historical data reload</strong>: That is a one-time batch load, not streaming.\n- <strong>D. Accessing data with high latency</strong>: Streaming aims for low latency.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Streaming = continuous, as it arrives. Batch = scheduled groups.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-streaming-analytics\">cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-streaming-analytics</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/solutions/stream-analytics\">cloud.google.com/solutions/stream-analytics</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/dataflow/docs/concepts/streaming-pipelines\">docs.cloud.google.com/dataflow/docs/concepts/streaming-pipelines</a></p>","lessons":["d2l9"]},"e3q27":{"id":"e3q27","exam":3,"n":27,"domain":5,"html":"<p><strong>ParcelPilot, a delivery tracking website, is hosting its application on Google Cloud. The security team wants to define which incoming and outgoing network traffic should be allowed or denied before users and systems can reach the application. Which security measure should ParcelPilot use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. 2-Step Verification</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Privileged access</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Encryption keys</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Firewall rules</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Firewall rules</strong></p>\n<p>Firewall rules define which incoming and outgoing network traffic is allowed or denied for your resources. The clue is \"incoming and outgoing network traffic should be allowed or denied\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. 2-Step Verification</strong>: It confirms a user's identity at sign-in; it doesn't filter network traffic.\n- <strong>B. Privileged access</strong>: That is about elevated permissions for users.\n- <strong>C. Encryption keys</strong>: They protect data confidentiality, not traffic flow.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Allow or deny network traffic = firewall rules.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/firewall/docs/firewalls\">docs.cloud.google.com/firewall/docs/firewalls</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/firewall/docs/using-firewalls\">docs.cloud.google.com/firewall/docs/using-firewalls</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/firewall/docs/firewall-policies-rule-details\">docs.cloud.google.com/firewall/docs/firewall-policies-rule-details</a></p>","lessons":["d5l4","d5l5"]},"e3q28":{"id":"e3q28","exam":3,"n":28,"domain":5,"html":"<p><strong>StreamWave, a video streaming platform, plans to expand its service to viewers in multiple countries. The company wants to use cloud capabilities to help meet global compliance and data protection expectations for customer information. What should StreamWave do?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Automatically encrypt personally identifiable information.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Obtain a business license before launching in each new market.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Allow users to turn off two-factor authentication.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Follow only the data policies of the country where the company headquarters is registered.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Automatically encrypt personally identifiable information.</strong></p>\n<p>Protecting personal data is a key part of data protection rules in many countries. Encrypting PII protects it if it is accessed without permission. It is the only option that uses cloud capabilities to protect customer data.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. Obtain a business license</strong>: A legal step, but it doesn't protect customer data.\n- <strong>C. Let users turn off two-factor authentication</strong>: This weakens security.\n- <strong>D. Follow only headquarters' data policies</strong>: Companies must respect the laws of every country where they serve users.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Global data protection starts with protecting PII; ignoring local laws or weakening auth is always wrong.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/security/compliance/offerings\">cloud.google.com/security/compliance/offerings</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/docs/security/encryption/default-encryption\">docs.cloud.google.com/docs/security/encryption/default-encryption</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/trust-center\">cloud.google.com/trust-center</a></p>\n<p><strong>⚠️ Note:</strong> Encryption alone doesn't meet global compliance. Data residency, consent and local rules matter too, and Google Cloud already encrypts all data at rest by default. Pick the keyed answer because the other options are clearly worse.</p>","lessons":["d5l6","d5l8"]},"e3q29":{"id":"e3q29","exam":3,"n":29,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>BrightBasket, an online grocery marketplace, uses traditional technology to host its ecommerce website. During holiday sales, the company often buys too much capacity or does not have enough capacity for sudden traffic spikes. BrightBasket has decided to move its website to cloud technology. What aspect of cloud technology will benefit BrightBasket’s ecommerce business?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Agile infrastructure means that they only pay for what they need, when they need it.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Operational expenditure means that their total cost of ownership is more predictable.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Unlimited storage means that their website will never experience downtime.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Shared responsibility means that the cloud provider gives increased visibility during traffic spikes.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Agile infrastructure means that they only pay for what they need, when they need it.</strong></p>\n<p>The problem is buying too much capacity or too little for traffic spikes. Cloud resources scale up and down with demand, and you pay only for what you use. That removes the need to guess capacity in advance.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. OpEx makes TCO more predictable</strong>: Pay-per-use costs can vary more, and OpEx doesn't solve the capacity problem.\n- <strong>C. Unlimited storage means no downtime</strong>: Storage has nothing to do with downtime, and no system has zero downtime.\n- <strong>D. Shared responsibility gives visibility during spikes</strong>: Shared responsibility splits security duties; it isn't about visibility or traffic.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Over- or under-provisioning problem = elastic scaling + pay for what you use.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/advantages-of-cloud-computing\">cloud.google.com/learn/advantages-of-cloud-computing</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/pricing\">cloud.google.com/pricing</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/security/shared-responsibility-data-model\">cloud.google.com/security/shared-responsibility-data-model</a></p>","lessons":["d1l2","d1l3","d1l5"]},"e3q30":{"id":"e3q30","exam":3,"n":30,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>CedarPeak Insurance currently runs a claims-processing system in its own data center. The company wants to build a new cloud-native application on Google Cloud that will eventually replace the existing on-premises system. Which application modernization approach should CedarPeak Insurance use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Move the application to Google Cloud, and then change it.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Change the application first, and then move it to Google Cloud.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Invent in greenfield.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Invent in brownfield.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Invent in brownfield.</strong></p>\n<p>In Google's framing, \"invent in brownfield\" means building a new cloud-native application that will replace an existing legacy system. The clue is a new app on Google Cloud that \"will eventually replace the existing on-premises system\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Move, then change</strong>: This migrates the existing app first (lift and shift, then modernize); the stem builds a new app instead.\n- <strong>B. Change, then move</strong>: This modernizes the existing app before migrating it; again, not building new.\n- <strong>C. Invent in greenfield</strong>: Greenfield means no existing system at all; here one exists and will be replaced.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> New app, nothing existing = greenfield. New app that replaces a legacy one = brownfield.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/solutions/application-modernization\">cloud.google.com/solutions/application-modernization</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/migration-to-gcp-getting-started\">docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/migration-to-gcp-getting-started</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/solutions/infrastructure-modernization\">cloud.google.com/solutions/infrastructure-modernization</a></p>","lessons":["d4l5"]},"e3q31":{"id":"e3q31","exam":3,"n":31,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>BlueTrail Logistics runs several warehouse applications in its private data center, but the company also wants to build and run newer applications on Google Cloud. It needs a hybrid-cloud management solution that can help teams manage applications consistently across on-premises and cloud environments. What solution should BlueTrail Logistics use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. GKE Enterprise</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Cloud Run functions</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. App Engine</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Compute Engine</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. GKE Enterprise</strong></p>\n<p>GKE Enterprise (formerly Anthos) is Google Cloud's platform for managing applications and Kubernetes clusters consistently across on-premises, Google Cloud and other clouds. The clue is \"hybrid-cloud management\" across \"on-premises and cloud environments\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. Cloud Run functions</strong>: Serverless functions that run in Google Cloud, not a hybrid management platform.\n- <strong>C. App Engine</strong>: A serverless app platform that runs only in Google Cloud.\n- <strong>D. Compute Engine</strong>: Virtual machines in Google Cloud; it doesn't manage on-premises environments.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Consistent management across on-prem and clouds = GKE Enterprise.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/enterprise\">cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/enterprise</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/enterprise/docs/deployment-options\">docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/enterprise/docs/deployment-options</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/functions/docs/concepts/overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/functions/docs/concepts/overview</a></p>","lessons":["d4l7"]},"e3q32":{"id":"e3q32","exam":3,"n":32,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>QuickServeNow runs a food delivery mobile app from its own data center. The company is moving the app to Google Cloud so it can reduce hardware management and scale more easily. After the migration, which change will QuickServeNow need to adapt to?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Paying compensation for Google Cloud service outages</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Managing Google Cloud’s underlying network infrastructure</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Adjusting to a consumption-based pay-as-you-go spending model</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Optimizing the physical storage systems that run Google Cloud</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Adjusting to a consumption-based pay-as-you-go spending model</strong></p>\n<p>On premises, QuickServeNow bought hardware up front. On Google Cloud it pays for what it uses each month, so costs now go up and down with usage. Finance and engineering teams have to adapt: track spending continuously, set budgets and alerts, and design for cost as well as performance.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Paying compensation for outages</strong>: The provider gives service credits under its SLA. The customer never pays for the provider's outages.\n- <strong>B. Managing Google's network</strong>: Google runs its own network infrastructure.\n- <strong>D. Optimizing Google's physical storage</strong>: The physical hardware is Google's responsibility.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Moving to cloud = CapEx up front becomes OpEx pay-as-you-go, and the business must learn to manage variable spending.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/pricing\">cloud.google.com/pricing</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/docs/security/overview/whitepaper\">cloud.google.com/docs/security/overview/whitepaper</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/security/shared-responsibility-shared-fate\">cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/security/shared-responsibility-shared-fate</a></p>","lessons":["d1l3","d1l5"]},"e3q33":{"id":"e3q33","exam":3,"n":33,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>FitCart, a grocery delivery mobile app, is documenting how customer order data is first collected from app events, then cleaned and standardized in analytics pipelines, and finally used by a recommendation model to suggest products. What is this end-to-end view of how data is collected, transformed, and used called?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Cloud resource hierarchy</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Compute modernization</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. The data supply chain</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Multi-cloud networking</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. The data supply chain</strong></p>\n<p>The data supply chain (also called the data value chain) is the end-to-end journey of data: collected, processed and transformed, stored, then used for analytics or AI. The stem walks through exactly that path from app events to a recommendation model.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Cloud resource hierarchy</strong>: That organizes Google Cloud resources into organization, folders and projects.\n- <strong>B. Compute modernization</strong>: That is about updating how applications run, not data flow.\n- <strong>D. Multi-cloud networking</strong>: That connects networks across cloud providers.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Collect, transform, use: the end-to-end data journey = data supply chain.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/discover/what-is-data-architecture\">cloud.google.com/discover/what-is-data-architecture</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/docs/data\">docs.cloud.google.com/docs/data</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/resource-manager/docs/cloud-platform-resource-hierarchy\">cloud.google.com/resource-manager/docs/cloud-platform-resource-hierarchy</a></p>","lessons":["d2l1"]},"e3q34":{"id":"e3q34","exam":3,"n":34,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>StreamHive, a music analytics startup, wants to execute Apache Spark batch jobs on Google Cloud for playlist trend analysis. The engineering team does not want to create, operate, scale, or tune Spark clusters, and prefers a fully serverless option where they only submit the workload. Which service should they choose?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Managed Service for Apache Spark on clusters</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Managed Service for Apache Spark serverless deployment</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Cloud SQL</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Compute Engine</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Managed Service for Apache Spark serverless deployment</strong></p>\n<p>The serverless option of Managed Service for Apache Spark (formerly Dataproc) runs Spark batch jobs without any clusters to create or manage. You submit the workload and Google handles the infrastructure and scaling. The clues are \"does not want to create, operate, scale, or tune Spark clusters\" and \"fully serverless\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Managed Service for Apache Spark on clusters</strong>: It still requires creating and sizing clusters.\n- <strong>C. Cloud SQL</strong>: A relational database; it doesn't run Spark jobs.\n- <strong>D. Compute Engine</strong>: You'd have to build and run your own Spark cluster on VMs, which is the most work.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Spark with no clusters to manage = the serverless option.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/managed-spark/docs/serverless-overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/managed-spark/docs/serverless-overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/products/managed-service-for-apache-spark\">cloud.google.com/products/managed-service-for-apache-spark</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/sql/docs/introduction\">docs.cloud.google.com/sql/docs/introduction</a></p>","lessons":["d2l9"],"same":["e1q44"]},"e3q35":{"id":"e3q35","exam":3,"n":35,"domain":6,"html":"<p><strong>MetroCart, a grocery delivery mobile app, wants to reduce the amount of manual operational work its engineering team performs every week. The team wants to follow a site reliability engineering practice that improves operational efficiency. Which SRE best practice should MetroCart adopt?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Rely less on operational data when deciding what to improve.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Give developers sole responsibility for production systems.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Reduce the effort spent understanding the impact of outages.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Prioritize automating repetitive toil.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Prioritize automating repetitive toil.</strong></p>\n<p>In SRE, toil is manual, repetitive operational work that adds no lasting value. SRE teams aim to automate it so engineers can spend time on improvements. The clue is \"reduce the amount of manual operational work\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Rely less on operational data</strong>: SRE is data-driven; decisions rely on metrics and SLOs.\n- <strong>B. Give developers sole responsibility</strong>: SRE is about shared ownership between developers and operations.\n- <strong>C. Reduce effort understanding outages</strong>: SRE uses blameless postmortems to learn from outages, not ignore them.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Manual, repetitive work = toil. SRE answer = automate it.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://sre.google/sre-book/eliminating-toil/\">sre.google/sre-book/eliminating-toil/</a>\n- <a href=\"https://sre.google/workbook/eliminating-toil/\">sre.google/workbook/eliminating-toil/</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/operational-excellence\">docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/operational-excellence</a></p>","lessons":["d6l6"]},"e3q36":{"id":"e3q36","exam":3,"n":36,"domain":6,"html":"<p><strong>CraftlyPay, a creator marketplace, earns most of its revenue from commissions on monthly sales. Because sales volume changes a lot from month to month, the company is having trouble predicting and controlling its Google Cloud costs. How can CraftlyPay optimize the use of its cloud resources?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. By applying intelligent recommendations</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. By decentralizing financial decision making</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. By sharing project ownership across all teams</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. By increasing their capital expenditure</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. By applying intelligent recommendations</strong></p>\n<p>Google Cloud's intelligent recommendations (Active Assist and Recommender) analyze usage and suggest actions, such as resizing oversized VMs or removing idle resources. That helps match resources to changing demand and control costs.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. Decentralizing financial decisions</strong>: This tends to make costs less controlled, not more.\n- <strong>C. Sharing project ownership across all teams</strong>: Broad ownership blurs accountability and doesn't optimize resources.\n- <strong>D. Increasing capital expenditure</strong>: Buying up front is the opposite of matching cloud use to variable demand.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Variable demand + optimize resources = intelligent recommendations.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/cost-management\">cloud.google.com/cost-management</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/recommender/docs/whatis-activeassist\">docs.cloud.google.com/recommender/docs/whatis-activeassist</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/billing/docs/how-to/finops-hub\">docs.cloud.google.com/billing/docs/how-to/finops-hub</a></p>","lessons":["d6l1","d6l2"]},"e3q37":{"id":"e3q37","exam":3,"n":37,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>FleetNest, a logistics tracking company, runs a custom in-house routing application that needs detailed control over the virtual machines, operating system configuration, networking, and machine types. The team also wants the infrastructure to support autoscaling and automatic rolling updates. Which Google Cloud product or solution should FleetNest use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Cloud Build</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Cloud Run</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Compute Engine</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. App Engine</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Compute Engine</strong></p>\n<p>Compute Engine gives full control over virtual machines, including the operating system, machine types and networking. With managed instance groups it also supports autoscaling and automatic rolling updates. The clue is \"detailed control over the virtual machines\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Cloud Build</strong>: A CI/CD service that builds and tests code; it doesn't host the application.\n- <strong>B. Cloud Run</strong>: Serverless containers; you don't control the VMs or OS.\n- <strong>D. App Engine</strong>: A serverless app platform that hides the underlying VMs.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Need control of VMs and OS = Compute Engine.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/compute/docs/instance-groups\">docs.cloud.google.com/compute/docs/instance-groups</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/compute/docs/autoscaler\">docs.cloud.google.com/compute/docs/autoscaler</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/compute/docs/instance-groups/rolling-out-updates-to-managed-instance-groups\">docs.cloud.google.com/compute/docs/instance-groups/rolling-out-updates-to-managed-instance-groups</a></p>","lessons":["d4l2","d4l4"]},"e3q38":{"id":"e3q38","exam":3,"n":38,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>TrustLedger Bank, a digital lending platform, is preparing to launch AI agents that can help employees analyze customer risk and automate routine financial workflows. Before deployment, the bank is unifying fragmented datasets, applying strong governance controls, improving data quality, and making trusted data easier for teams to discover and use. What is TrustLedger Bank creating?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. A legacy data center</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. An AI-ready data foundation</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. A hardware-defined network</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. A perimeter security model</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. An AI-ready data foundation</strong></p>\n<p>An AI-ready data foundation is data that is unified, clean, governed and easy to discover, so AI and agents can use it reliably. The bank is unifying datasets, applying governance and improving quality before launching AI agents, which is exactly that.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. A legacy data center</strong>: The bank is modernizing, not building old on-premises infrastructure.\n- <strong>C. A hardware-defined network</strong>: The work is about data, not networking.\n- <strong>D. A perimeter security model</strong>: That is an older security approach, unrelated to preparing data for AI.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Unified, clean, governed data before AI = AI-ready data foundation.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-data-governance\">cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-data-governance</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/transform/how-to-build-strong-data-foundations-gen-ai\">cloud.google.com/transform/how-to-build-strong-data-foundations-gen-ai</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/choose-agentic-ai-architecture-components\">docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/choose-agentic-ai-architecture-components</a></p>","lessons":["d2l2"]},"e3q39":{"id":"e3q39","exam":3,"n":39,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>FinoraCart is modernizing a legacy enterprise transaction database for its retail payment platform on Google Cloud. The team needs very high performance and availability, but they also want to stay aligned with an open-source database engine so they can reduce the risk of restrictive vendor lock-in. Which Google Cloud service should they choose?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Bigtable</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Spanner</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. AlloyDB for PostgreSQL</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Firestore in Datastore mode</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. AlloyDB for PostgreSQL</strong></p>\n<p>AlloyDB for PostgreSQL is a fully managed, PostgreSQL-compatible database built for high performance and availability on enterprise transactional workloads. Because it is compatible with open-source PostgreSQL, apps and skills can move more easily, which reduces lock-in. The clues are \"very high performance and availability\" and \"open-source database engine\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Bigtable</strong>: A NoSQL wide-column database; it isn't relational or based on an open-source SQL engine.\n- <strong>B. Spanner</strong>: High performance and availability, but its own Google-built engine; less closely tied to open-source PostgreSQL.\n- <strong>D. Firestore in Datastore mode</strong>: A NoSQL document database, not for enterprise relational transactions.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Enterprise performance + PostgreSQL compatibility = AlloyDB.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/alloydb/docs\">docs.cloud.google.com/alloydb/docs</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/spanner/docs\">docs.cloud.google.com/spanner/docs</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/bigtable/docs\">docs.cloud.google.com/bigtable/docs</a></p>\n<p><strong>⚠️ Note:</strong> AlloyDB itself is not open source. It is compatible with open-source PostgreSQL, which is what \"aligned with an open-source engine\" means. Spanner also offers a PostgreSQL interface, but AlloyDB is the closer fit, so pick it on this exam.</p>","lessons":["d2l6"]},"e3q40":{"id":"e3q40","exam":3,"n":40,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>GlowTrail Media is adopting AI for its creative and engineering teams. The company wants a system that can produce new campaign emails, generate product banner images, and create sample application code from user prompts. Which branch of artificial intelligence is focused on creating new content like this?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Generative AI (gen AI)</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Predictive analytics</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Data warehousing</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Relational databases</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Generative AI (gen AI)</strong></p>\n<p>Generative AI creates new content such as text, images and code from prompts. Emails, banner images and sample code are all newly generated content.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. Predictive analytics</strong>: It forecasts outcomes from data; it doesn't create content.\n- <strong>C. Data warehousing</strong>: It stores and analyzes data; it is not a branch of AI.\n- <strong>D. Relational databases</strong>: They store structured data; they are not AI.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Create new content = generative AI.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/models/beginners-guide\">docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/models/beginners-guide</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/solutions/generative-ai\">cloud.google.com/solutions/generative-ai</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-artificial-intelligence\">cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-artificial-intelligence</a></p>","lessons":["d3l1","d3l7","d3l8"]},"e3q41":{"id":"e3q41","exam":3,"n":41,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>PetNest Analytics, a mobile app company, has accumulated several petabytes of historical user activity and transaction data. The analytics team needs a fast, scalable, fully managed solution to run advanced queries and analysis across this large dataset. Which Google Cloud product or service should PetNest Analytics use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. BigQuery</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Cloud SQL</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Firestore</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Cloud Storage</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. BigQuery</strong></p>\n<p>BigQuery is Google Cloud's serverless, fully managed data warehouse. It runs fast SQL analytics over terabytes to petabytes of data. The clues are \"petabytes\", \"advanced queries and analysis\" and \"fully managed\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. Cloud SQL</strong>: A transactional database, not built for petabyte-scale analytics.\n- <strong>C. Firestore</strong>: A NoSQL document database for apps, not large-scale analysis.\n- <strong>D. Cloud Storage</strong>: It stores files cheaply but isn't a query and analysis engine by itself.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Large-scale analytics with SQL = BigQuery.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/introduction\">cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/introduction</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/sql/docs/introduction\">cloud.google.com/sql/docs/introduction</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/introduction\">cloud.google.com/storage/docs/introduction</a></p>","lessons":["d2l5","d2l8"]},"e3q42":{"id":"e3q42","exam":3,"n":42,"domain":6,"html":"<p><strong>BrightCart, an online shopping platform, is organizing its Google Cloud resource hierarchy. The company has one folder named retail-apps that contains multiple projects for checkout, inventory, and analytics workloads. The cloud administrator plans to apply user access policies at the retail-apps folder level. What will happen to those access policies?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. The policy will be inherited by the projects in the folder but will not affect their resources.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. The policy will be inherited by the projects and their resources within the folder.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. The policy will be applied to all folders within the organization.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. The policy applies to the folder only, and will not be inherited by any projects.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. The policy will be inherited by the projects and their resources within the folder.</strong></p>\n<p>In the Google Cloud resource hierarchy (organization, folders, projects, resources), policies flow down. A policy set on a folder is inherited by every project in it and by all resources in those projects.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Inherited by projects but not their resources</strong>: Inheritance continues down to resources.\n- <strong>C. Applied to all folders in the organization</strong>: Policies flow down, not up or sideways to other folders.\n- <strong>D. Folder only, not inherited</strong>: Inheritance is how the hierarchy works.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Policies flow down the hierarchy: organization, folder, project, resource.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/resource-manager/docs/cloud-platform-resource-hierarchy\">docs.cloud.google.com/resource-manager/docs/cloud-platform-resource-hierarchy</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/iam/docs/resource-hierarchy-access-control\">docs.cloud.google.com/iam/docs/resource-hierarchy-access-control</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/iam/docs/allow-policies\">docs.cloud.google.com/iam/docs/allow-policies</a></p>","lessons":["d6l3"]},"e3q43":{"id":"e3q43","exam":3,"n":43,"domain":6,"html":"<p><strong>SkyTrack Logistics runs containerized workloads on GKE, backend services on virtual machines, and several managed Google Cloud services. The operations team wants a single Google Cloud tool to collect, store, search, and analyze custom log entries from these environments. Which Google Cloud tool should SkyTrack Logistics use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Dialogflow</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Cloud Logging</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Cloud SDK</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Data Catalog</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cloud Logging</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud Logging is the managed service to collect, store, search and analyze logs from GKE, VMs and Google Cloud services, including custom log entries. The clues are \"collect, store, search, and analyze\" and \"log entries\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Dialogflow</strong>: A tool for building conversational agents and chatbots.\n- <strong>C. Cloud SDK</strong>: Command-line tools and libraries for working with Google Cloud.\n- <strong>D. Data Catalog</strong>: A metadata service for discovering data assets, not logs.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Logs from anywhere in Google Cloud = Cloud Logging.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/logging/docs/overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/logging/docs/overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/logging/docs/integrate/gke\">docs.cloud.google.com/logging/docs/integrate/gke</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/stackdriver/docs/solutions/agents/ops-agent\">docs.cloud.google.com/stackdriver/docs/solutions/agents/ops-agent</a></p>","lessons":["d6l8"]},"e3q44":{"id":"e3q44","exam":3,"n":44,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>BrightCart, a small retail startup, plans to launch its first cloud-based shopping platform and design all supporting infrastructure directly on Google Cloud. The team does not have an existing application to migrate or modify. Which application modernization approach should BrightCart use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Move the application to Google Cloud first, and then modernize it.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Modernize the application first, and then move it to Google Cloud.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Invent in greenfield.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Invent in brownfield.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Invent in greenfield.</strong></p>\n<p>Greenfield means starting fresh with no existing application. BrightCart is building its first platform directly on Google Cloud. The clue is \"does not have an existing application to migrate or modify\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Move first, then modernize</strong>: There is nothing to move.\n- <strong>B. Modernize first, then move</strong>: There is nothing to modernize.\n- <strong>D. Invent in brownfield</strong>: Brownfield means building a new app to replace an existing system.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Nothing existing = greenfield.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/solutions/application-modernization\">cloud.google.com/solutions/application-modernization</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/migration-to-gcp-getting-started\">docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/migration-to-gcp-getting-started</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/solutions/camp\">cloud.google.com/solutions/camp</a></p>","lessons":["d4l5"]},"e3q45":{"id":"e3q45","exam":3,"n":45,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>HelioWorks Analytics wants its operations analysts to create and test internal AI agents that can answer policy questions, summarize business data, and automate simple approval workflows. The analysts do not have advanced coding or machine learning skills, so the company needs a Google Cloud interface where they can visually design, prototype, and publish agents with a no-code or low-code experience. Which option should they use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Agent Studio</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Spanner</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Security Command Center</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Cloud Run</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Agent Studio</strong></p>\n<p>Agent Studio is the no-code and low-code visual interface for designing, prototyping and publishing AI agents. It suits analysts without coding or ML skills. The clues are \"visually design\" and \"no-code or low-code\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. Spanner</strong>: A globally distributed relational database.\n- <strong>C. Security Command Center</strong>: A security and risk management service.\n- <strong>D. Cloud Run</strong>: A serverless platform for running containers; it needs developers.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> No-code or low-code agent building = Agent Studio.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/products/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform\">cloud.google.com/products/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/run/docs\">docs.cloud.google.com/run/docs</a></p>","lessons":["d3l6","d3l8"]},"e3q46":{"id":"e3q46","exam":3,"n":46,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>MediBridge Health is preparing clinical records for a machine learning model that will help predict patient follow-up needs. During data profiling, the team notices that many records do not contain required attributes such as date of birth, heart rate, and diagnosis code. Which data quality dimension is affected by this issue?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Timeliness</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Completeness</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Uniqueness</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Validity</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Completeness</strong></p>\n<p>Completeness measures whether all required data is present. Records missing date of birth, heart rate and diagnosis code are incomplete. The clue is \"do not contain required attributes\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Timeliness</strong>: That is about whether data is current, not missing.\n- <strong>C. Uniqueness</strong>: That is about duplicate records.\n- <strong>D. Validity</strong>: That is about values following the expected format or rules.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Missing required fields = completeness.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-data-governance\">cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-data-governance</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/dataplex/docs/auto-data-quality-overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/dataplex/docs/auto-data-quality-overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/dataplex/docs/use-auto-data-quality\">docs.cloud.google.com/dataplex/docs/use-auto-data-quality</a></p>","lessons":["d2l4"],"same":["e3q16"]},"e3q47":{"id":"e3q47","exam":3,"n":47,"domain":5,"html":"<p><strong>QuickCart Labs is rolling out stricter access controls for employees who sign in to Google Cloud. The security team wants every user to enter their password and then prove their identity again by using a phone prompt, verification code, or hardware security key. Which security practice is being implemented?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Distributed Denial-of-Service protection</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Data masking</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Two-step verification (2SV) / Multi-factor authentication</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Threat intelligence sharing</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Two-step verification (2SV) / Multi-factor authentication</strong></p>\n<p>Two-step verification, a form of multi-factor authentication, requires a password plus a second proof such as a phone prompt, code or security key. This blocks attackers who only have a stolen password.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. DDoS protection</strong>: Protects against floods of traffic, not user sign-in.\n- <strong>B. Data masking</strong>: Hides sensitive values in data.\n- <strong>D. Threat intelligence sharing</strong>: Shares information about attackers; it doesn't verify identity.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Password + second proof = 2SV / MFA.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/docs/authentication/mfa-requirement\">docs.cloud.google.com/docs/authentication/mfa-requirement</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/docs/authentication/use-cases\">docs.cloud.google.com/docs/authentication/use-cases</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/armor/docs/cloud-armor-overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/armor/docs/cloud-armor-overview</a></p>","lessons":["d5l4","d5l6"]},"e3q48":{"id":"e3q48","exam":3,"n":48,"domain":6,"html":"<p><strong>BrightCart, a grocery delivery mobile app, runs multiple backend services on Google Cloud. The developers want a tool that can automatically collect, group, and show application crashes and exceptions as they happen, so they do not have to inspect large volumes of raw logs manually. Which Google Cloud Observability tool should they use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Cloud Profiler</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Error Reporting</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Cloud Trace</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Cloud Monitoring</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Error Reporting</strong></p>\n<p>Error Reporting automatically collects application crashes and exceptions, groups similar ones together, and shows them as they happen. The clue is \"collect, group, and show application crashes and exceptions\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Cloud Profiler</strong>: It shows CPU and memory use by code function.\n- <strong>C. Cloud Trace</strong>: It shows request latency across services.\n- <strong>D. Cloud Monitoring</strong>: It tracks metrics and alerts, but doesn't group exceptions.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Crashes and exceptions grouped for you = Error Reporting.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/error-reporting/docs/grouping-errors\">docs.cloud.google.com/error-reporting/docs/grouping-errors</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/profiler/docs/about-profiler\">docs.cloud.google.com/profiler/docs/about-profiler</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/trace/docs/overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/trace/docs/overview</a></p>","lessons":["d6l8"]},"e3q49":{"id":"e3q49","exam":3,"n":49,"domain":6,"html":"<p><strong>LumaCart, an online marketplace, wants to introduce a camera-based image recognition login feature for its internal support portal. The engineering team wants to release the feature while following SRE principles and reducing risk. What should LumaCart do?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Roll out the new system to a small group of support employees to test it first.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Roll out the new system to all support employees immediately to collect the maximum amount of usage data.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Avoid rolling out the new system because it may have authentication weaknesses.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Avoid rolling out the new system because it may conflict with privacy requirements.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Roll out the new system to a small group of support employees to test it first.</strong></p>\n<p>SRE favors gradual rollouts, such as a canary release to a small group first. If something goes wrong, only a few users are affected and the change is easy to roll back. The clue is \"reducing risk\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. Roll out to everyone immediately</strong>: This maximizes the impact if something breaks.\n- <strong>C. Avoid rollout over authentication weaknesses</strong>: SRE manages risk; it doesn't block innovation out of fear.\n- <strong>D. Avoid rollout over privacy conflicts</strong>: Same: test and manage the risk rather than refusing to launch.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> New feature + reduce risk = roll out to a small group first.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://sre.google/sre-book/release-engineering\">sre.google/sre-book/release-engineering</a>\n- <a href=\"https://sre.google/workbook/canarying-releases\">sre.google/workbook/canarying-releases</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/deploy/docs/deployment-strategies/canary\">cloud.google.com/deploy/docs/deployment-strategies/canary</a></p>","lessons":["d6l6"]},"e3q50":{"id":"e3q50","exam":3,"n":50,"domain":6,"html":"<p><strong>MealSprint, a food delivery web app, notices that one backend service is causing unusually high cloud costs. The development team wants to inspect the running production application and continuously collect CPU usage and memory-allocation data so they can find which specific code functions are consuming the most resources. Which Google Cloud tool should they use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Cloud Logging</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Cloud Trace</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Error Reporting</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Cloud Profiler</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cloud Profiler</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud Profiler continuously collects CPU and memory-allocation data from running production apps with low overhead. It shows which functions use the most resources. The clues are \"CPU usage and memory-allocation\" and \"which specific code functions\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Cloud Logging</strong>: It stores log events, not resource use by function.\n- <strong>B. Cloud Trace</strong>: It shows request latency across services, not code-level resource use.\n- <strong>C. Error Reporting</strong>: It groups crashes and exceptions.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Which code uses the most CPU or memory = Cloud Profiler.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/profiler/docs/about-profiler\">docs.cloud.google.com/profiler/docs/about-profiler</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/trace/docs/overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/trace/docs/overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/logging/docs/overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/logging/docs/overview</a></p>","lessons":["d6l8"]},"e4q1":{"id":"e4q1","exam":4,"n":1,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>FitLens Market is building an AI application for a small group of professional sports coaches who need very specific performance predictions from proprietary athlete training data. Instead of using a ready-made pre-trained API, the company chooses to develop and train its own custom ML model. What is the main tradeoff of this approach?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. It requires less technical expertise but offers faster implementation speed.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. It requires more effort and expertise but provides higher business differentiation.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. It shifts your cloud expenditure from OpEx to CapEx.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. It guarantees that the model will be 100% accurate.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It requires more effort and expertise but provides higher business differentiation.</strong></p>\n<p>Building a custom model takes more data work, ML skills, time and money than calling a pre-trained API. In return, the model is tuned to the company's own proprietary data, which competitors can't copy. The clues are \"very specific predictions\" and \"proprietary athlete training data\": that is exactly where custom models pay off.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Less expertise, faster</strong>: It is the opposite: custom models need more expertise and take longer than pre-trained APIs.\n- <strong>C. Shifts OpEx to CapEx</strong>: Training a model on Google Cloud is still pay-as-you-go spending, so it stays OpEx.\n- <strong>D. 100% accurate</strong>: No ML model is ever guaranteed to be 100% accurate.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Pre-trained API = fast and easy, low differentiation. Custom model = more effort, more differentiation.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/machine-learning/training-overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/machine-learning/training-overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/ml-on-gcp-best-practices\">docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/ml-on-gcp-best-practices</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-machine-learning\">cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-machine-learning</a></p>","lessons":["d3l3"]},"e4q2":{"id":"e4q2","exam":4,"n":2,"domain":5,"html":"<p><strong>HealthWave Portal hosts a public appointment-booking website on Google Cloud. The security team wants to protect the website from distributed denial-of-service attacks that could overwhelm the application with malicious traffic. Which Google Cloud service should HealthWave Portal use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Google Cloud Armor</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Cloud Build</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Cloud VPN</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Security Command Center</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Google Cloud Armor</strong></p>\n<p>Google Cloud Armor protects internet-facing apps from DDoS attacks and common web attacks. It sits at the edge of Google's network in front of the load balancer and filters malicious traffic before it reaches the app. The clue is \"distributed denial-of-service attacks\" against a public website.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. Cloud Build</strong>: It is a CI/CD service that builds and tests code, not a traffic filter.\n- <strong>C. Cloud VPN</strong>: It creates an encrypted tunnel between networks, it doesn't block attack traffic to a public site.\n- <strong>D. Security Command Center</strong>: It gives a central view of security risks and findings, but it doesn't filter incoming DDoS traffic.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> DDoS or web attacks on a public site → Cloud Armor.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/armor/docs/cloud-armor-overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/armor/docs/cloud-armor-overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/armor/docs/advanced-network-ddos\">docs.cloud.google.com/armor/docs/advanced-network-ddos</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/security-command-center/docs/security-command-center-overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/security-command-center/docs/security-command-center-overview</a></p>","lessons":["d5l2","d5l5"],"same":["e3q10"]},"e4q3":{"id":"e4q3","exam":4,"n":3,"domain":5,"html":"<p><strong>StreamSure Media, a video streaming company, stores customer profiles, watch history, and billing-related records in Google Cloud. The security team is concerned about a rare situation where someone with malicious intent might physically access Google Cloud infrastructure. How does Google Cloud help protect stored customer data in this situation?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Data is instantly deleted whenever physical intrusion is detected.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Data is stored on quantum computers using encryption that cannot be broken.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Data is stored using strong encryption mechanisms.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Data is stored in unpredictable global locations so attackers cannot locate it.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Data is stored using strong encryption mechanisms.</strong></p>\n<p>Google Cloud encrypts all customer data at rest by default. If someone physically stole a disk, they would only get encrypted data that is useless without the keys, which are managed separately. This is one layer of Google's defense-in-depth model, alongside strict physical data center security.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Instantly deleted on intrusion</strong>: Google doesn't wipe customer data when an intrusion is detected; that would destroy customers' data.\n- <strong>B. Quantum computers</strong>: Customer data isn't stored on quantum computers, and no encryption is \"unbreakable\" by marketing claim.\n- <strong>D. Unpredictable global locations</strong>: Data stays in the locations the customer chooses; hiding it is not the protection model.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Physical theft of a disk → encryption at rest makes the data unreadable.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/docs/security/encryption/default-encryption\">cloud.google.com/docs/security/encryption/default-encryption</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/security/encryption\">cloud.google.com/security/encryption</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/docs/security/encryption-in-transit\">cloud.google.com/docs/security/encryption-in-transit</a></p>","lessons":["d5l6"]},"e4q4":{"id":"e4q4","exam":4,"n":4,"domain":5,"html":"<p><strong>MediVault, a healthcare records platform, is moving encrypted patient files and application data into Google Cloud. The compliance team wants to confirm who retains ownership of the customer data after it is uploaded to Google Cloud. Who owns the data?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. A third party</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. The customer and Google share ownership</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Google</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. The customer</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. The customer</strong></p>\n<p>Under Google Cloud's terms, customers own their data. Google only processes it to provide the services the customer asked for, under the customer's instructions. Moving data into the cloud does not transfer ownership.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. A third party</strong>: No third party gains ownership of data just because it is stored in Google Cloud.\n- <strong>B. Shared ownership</strong>: Responsibility for security is shared, but ownership of the data is not.\n- <strong>C. Google</strong>: Google is the processor of the data, not its owner.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Your data stays yours. Google processes it, never owns it.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/privacy\">cloud.google.com/privacy</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/transparency\">cloud.google.com/transparency</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/docs/security/deletion\">docs.cloud.google.com/docs/security/deletion</a></p>","lessons":["d5l8"]},"e4q5":{"id":"e4q5","exam":4,"n":5,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>TrailPilot Maps, a travel planning website, wants to run its Kubernetes applications across three different public cloud providers so it can use more deployment locations around the world. The engineering team uses GKE Enterprise to manage and deploy these Kubernetes workloads across the different cloud environments. What type of deployment is this?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. On-premises</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Multi-cloud</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Hybrid-cloud</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Private cloud</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Multi-cloud</strong></p>\n<p>Multi-cloud means using more than one public cloud provider. Here TrailPilot runs Kubernetes workloads on three different public clouds, and GKE Enterprise (formerly Anthos) gives one way to manage them all. No on-premises data center is mentioned, so it isn't hybrid.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. On-premises</strong>: Everything here runs on public clouds, not in the company's own data center.\n- <strong>C. Hybrid-cloud</strong>: Hybrid means combining on-premises (or private) infrastructure with public cloud, which the stem doesn't describe.\n- <strong>D. Private cloud</strong>: A private cloud is dedicated infrastructure for one organization, not several public providers.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Several public clouds → multi-cloud. On-premises + public cloud → hybrid.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-multicloud\">cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-multicloud</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/multi-cloud/docs\">cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/multi-cloud/docs</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/enterprise/clusters/docs\">cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/enterprise/clusters/docs</a></p>","lessons":["d4l7"]},"e4q6":{"id":"e4q6","exam":4,"n":6,"domain":5,"html":"<p><strong>TrailBite, a meal-planning mobile app, stores recipe photos and analytics files in Google Cloud. The company plans to move a portion of this data to another cloud provider for a new reporting workflow. Which statement is true?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Customer data cannot be transferred out of Google Cloud.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Customers control their own data and can export or transfer it when needed.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Outgoing data transfer must first be activated in the Google Cloud console.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. The customer must open a technical support ticket with the appropriate Google Cloud team.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Customers control their own data and can export or transfer it when needed.</strong></p>\n<p>Google Cloud's privacy commitments say the customer owns and controls its data. That includes the right to take it out at any time, for example to another provider. Google supports open standards and data portability to avoid vendor lock-in.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Cannot be transferred out</strong>: Customers can always export their data; lock-in is something Google explicitly says it avoids.\n- <strong>C. Must activate outgoing transfer</strong>: There's no special switch to \"enable\" exports; customers can move data whenever they want, paying normal network charges.\n- <strong>D. Open a support ticket</strong>: Customers don't need Google's permission or a ticket to move their own data.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Customer owns the data → customer can move it out anytime.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/privacy\">cloud.google.com/privacy</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/privacy/common-privacy-principles\">cloud.google.com/privacy/common-privacy-principles</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/transparency\">cloud.google.com/transparency</a></p>","lessons":["d5l8"]},"e4q7":{"id":"e4q7","exam":4,"n":7,"domain":6,"html":"<p><strong>PixelKart, an online shopping mobile app, runs its checkout service across several containers on Google Kubernetes Engine. The DevOps team suspects that one container is generating errors and wants to search the application’s runtime log entries across all containers to find the issue. Which Google Cloud product should the team use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Google Cloud Console</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Cloud Trace</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Cloud Monitoring</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Cloud Logging</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cloud Logging</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud Logging collects, stores and lets you search log entries from applications and Google Cloud services, including GKE containers. The clue is \"search the application's runtime log entries across all containers\". Logs show the actual error messages each container wrote.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Google Cloud Console</strong>: The console is the web interface for managing resources; the log search itself is done with Cloud Logging.\n- <strong>B. Cloud Trace</strong>: Trace shows how long requests take as they pass through services (latency), not raw log messages.\n- <strong>C. Cloud Monitoring</strong>: Monitoring tracks metrics, dashboards and alerts, not the text of individual log entries.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Search log entries / error messages → Cloud Logging. Metrics and alerts → Monitoring. Latency → Trace.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/logging/docs/view/logs-explorer-interface\">docs.cloud.google.com/logging/docs/view/logs-explorer-interface</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/how-to/view-logs\">docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/how-to/view-logs</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/trace/docs/overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/trace/docs/overview</a></p>","lessons":["d6l8"]},"e4q8":{"id":"e4q8","exam":4,"n":8,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>TrailNest, a campsite booking company, wants to update its older reservation platform so travelers can search available campsites and book stays from a mobile app. What is the main benefit of using an application programming interface (API) for this modernization?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. They do not have to build the customer-facing mobile app</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. They can immediately retire their legacy reservation platform</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. They can automatically convert their systems into cloud-native applications</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. They do not have to rewrite the legacy reservation platform</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. They do not have to rewrite the legacy reservation platform</strong></p>\n<p>An API exposes the existing system's data and functions to new apps in a standard way. The mobile app can call the API to search and book campsites while the old reservation platform keeps running behind it. This lets companies modernize the customer experience without a risky full rewrite.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Don't have to build the mobile app</strong>: The company still has to build the app; the API only connects it to the backend.\n- <strong>B. Immediately retire the legacy platform</strong>: The API relies on the legacy platform still running, so it isn't retired.\n- <strong>C. Automatically convert to cloud-native</strong>: An API doesn't turn an old system into a cloud-native one by itself.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> API on top of a legacy system = new experiences without rewriting the old system.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/solutions/unlocking-legacy-applications\">cloud.google.com/solutions/unlocking-legacy-applications</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/apigee\">cloud.google.com/apigee</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/apigee/docs/api-platform/get-started/what-apigee\">docs.cloud.google.com/apigee/docs/api-platform/get-started/what-apigee</a></p>","lessons":["d4l8"]},"e4q9":{"id":"e4q9","exam":4,"n":9,"domain":6,"html":"<p><strong>PulsePay, a payment processing app, wants its engineering team to define the target reliability and performance level that its customer-facing service should meet. In Site Reliability Engineering, what is the term for this desired reliability and performance target?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Service-level objective</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Enhanced support</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Scalable infrastructure</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Service-level indicator</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Service-level objective</strong></p>\n<p>A service-level objective (SLO) is the target level of reliability or performance a team commits to, such as 99.9% of checkout requests succeeding. The clue is \"target\". It is set against a measured indicator (the SLI).</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. Enhanced support</strong>: This is a Google Cloud Customer Care support tier, not a reliability target.\n- <strong>C. Scalable infrastructure</strong>: This is an infrastructure property, not an SRE term for a reliability goal.\n- <strong>D. Service-level indicator</strong>: An SLI is the measurement itself (for example the success rate), not the target.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> SLI = what you measure. SLO = the target. SLA = the contract with penalties.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://sre.google/sre-book/service-level-objectives\">sre.google/sre-book/service-level-objectives</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/devops-sre/sre-fundamentals-sli-vs-slo-vs-sla\">cloud.google.com/blog/products/devops-sre/sre-fundamentals-sli-vs-slo-vs-sla</a>\n- <a href=\"https://sre.google/resources/practices-and-processes/art-of-slos/\">sre.google/resources/practices-and-processes/art-of-slos/</a></p>","lessons":["d6l4"],"same":["e4q10"]},"e4q10":{"id":"e4q10","exam":4,"n":10,"domain":6,"html":"<p><strong>StreamCart, an online grocery delivery platform, wants its operations team to define the expected target for how reliable and responsive its checkout service should be for customers. In Site Reliability Engineering, which term describes this desired reliability and performance target?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Service-level objective</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Enhanced support</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Scalable infrastructure</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Service-level indicator</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Service-level objective</strong></p>\n<p>A service-level objective (SLO) is the target value for reliability and responsiveness, for example 99.9% of checkouts completing in under a set time. The words \"expected target\" point straight to the SLO. It is the same concept as the previous question with a different company.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. Enhanced support</strong>: This is a paid Customer Care support tier, not a reliability target.\n- <strong>C. Scalable infrastructure</strong>: This describes infrastructure that can grow, not an SRE target.\n- <strong>D. Service-level indicator</strong>: The SLI is the measured value (like actual latency); the SLO is the goal for it.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> \"Target\" → SLO. \"Measurement\" → SLI.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://sre.google/sre-book/service-level-objectives\">sre.google/sre-book/service-level-objectives</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/devops-sre/sre-fundamentals-sli-vs-slo-vs-sla\">cloud.google.com/blog/products/devops-sre/sre-fundamentals-sli-vs-slo-vs-sla</a>\n- <a href=\"https://sre.google/resources/practices-and-processes/art-of-slos/\">sre.google/resources/practices-and-processes/art-of-slos/</a></p>","lessons":["d6l4"],"same":["e4q9"]},"e4q11":{"id":"e4q11","exam":4,"n":11,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>StyleHub, a clothing retailer, keeps its store inventory data in an older warehouse management system. Customers often see jackets, shoes, and accessories listed as available on StyleHub’s website, but when they visit a nearby store, some items are already sold out. How could StyleHub benefit from using an application programming interface (API)?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. To generate personalized product suggestions for shoppers</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. To improve the stability of the on-premises legacy system itself</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. By manually connecting each inventory source to the website one at a time</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. By programmatically connecting the inventory system to their website</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. By programmatically connecting the inventory system to their website</strong></p>\n<p>The problem is that the website shows stale stock because it isn't connected to the real inventory. An API lets the website ask the warehouse system for current stock automatically, in code. Customers then see accurate availability without replacing the old system.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Personalized product suggestions</strong>: That is a recommendations use case, not a fix for wrong stock levels.\n- <strong>B. Improve stability of the legacy system</strong>: An API exposes the system to others; it doesn't make the old system itself more stable.\n- <strong>C. Manually connecting each source</strong>: APIs are about automated, programmatic connections, not manual work.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> APIs connect systems programmatically so data stays in sync.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/apigee\">cloud.google.com/apigee</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/apigee/docs/api-platform/get-started/what-apigee\">docs.cloud.google.com/apigee/docs/api-platform/get-started/what-apigee</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/solutions/unlocking-legacy-applications\">cloud.google.com/solutions/unlocking-legacy-applications</a></p>","lessons":["d4l8"]},"e4q12":{"id":"e4q12","exam":4,"n":12,"domain":5,"html":"<p><strong>PixelHarbor, a photo-sharing startup, stores user albums and profile information in Google Cloud. Its security team wants assurance that customer information stored in the platform is protected and handled according to recognized privacy expectations. How does Google Cloud help ensure customer data remains secure and private when stored?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. By aggregating training data for customers within each industry</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. By automatically locking files containing suspicious code</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. By auditing platform privacy practices against industry standards</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. By providing privacy reviews for critical customer applications</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. By auditing platform privacy practices against industry standards</strong></p>\n<p>Google Cloud's privacy controls are checked by independent third-party audits against recognized standards and certifications, such as ISO/IEC 27018 for protecting personal data in the cloud. The audit reports give customers outside evidence that their data is handled as promised. The clue is \"recognized privacy expectations\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Aggregating training data</strong>: Google does not pool customer data by industry; that would break its privacy commitments.\n- <strong>B. Locking suspicious files</strong>: This describes malware handling, not how privacy is assured against standards.\n- <strong>D. Privacy reviews for customer applications</strong>: Google doesn't review the privacy of each customer's own applications; that is the customer's job.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Proof of privacy and security → independent audits and certifications (ISO, SOC).</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/transparency\">cloud.google.com/transparency</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/docs/security/encryption/default-encryption\">cloud.google.com/docs/security/encryption/default-encryption</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/security/compliance/offerings\">cloud.google.com/security/compliance/offerings</a></p>\n<p><strong>⚠️ Note:</strong> The idea is right (third-party audits against standards such as ISO 27018), but the wording is loose and D can sound plausible. C is still the best option; pick it on this exam.</p>","lessons":["d5l8"]},"e4q13":{"id":"e4q13","exam":4,"n":13,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>HealthBridge Analytics stores database export files in Cloud Storage for disaster recovery. The files are rarely needed and are expected to be read only once every 12 months during recovery testing. Which Cloud Storage class should HealthBridge Analytics choose to minimize storage cost?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Coldline class</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Standard class</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Nearline class</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Archive class</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Archive class</strong></p>\n<p>Archive is the cheapest Cloud Storage class for data accessed less than once a year. Disaster-recovery exports read only during a yearly test fit it exactly. Archive has the lowest storage price, with higher retrieval costs and a longer minimum storage period, which is fine for rarely read data.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Coldline class</strong>: Coldline suits data read about once a quarter; it costs more to store than Archive.\n- <strong>B. Standard class</strong>: Standard is for frequently accessed data and has the highest storage price.\n- <strong>C. Nearline class</strong>: Nearline suits data read about once a month, too frequent a pattern for this case.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Monthly → Nearline. Quarterly → Coldline. Yearly or less → Archive.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/storage/docs/storage-classes\">docs.cloud.google.com/storage/docs/storage-classes</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/storage/pricing\">cloud.google.com/storage/pricing</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/dr-scenarios-planning-guide\">docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/dr-scenarios-planning-guide</a></p>","lessons":["d2l7"]},"e4q14":{"id":"e4q14","exam":4,"n":14,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>CallFlow Analytics, a contact center platform, wants to convert a large archive of recorded customer service conversations into written text. After the calls are transcribed, the text will be used for sentiment analysis and reporting. Which pre-trained Google Cloud service should CallFlow Analytics use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Vision API</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Speech-to-Text API</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Cloud Translation API</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Cloud SQL</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Speech-to-Text API</strong></p>\n<p>Speech-to-Text is a pre-trained API that turns audio into written text. Recorded calls are audio, and the goal is transcripts, so it fits directly. The text can then go to the Natural Language API or other tools for sentiment analysis.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Vision API</strong>: Vision analyzes images, not audio.\n- <strong>C. Cloud Translation API</strong>: Translation converts text from one language to another; it can't transcribe audio.\n- <strong>D. Cloud SQL</strong>: Cloud SQL is a managed relational database, not an AI service.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Audio → text = Speech-to-Text. Text → audio = Text-to-Speech.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/speech-to-text/docs\">cloud.google.com/speech-to-text/docs</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/vision/docs\">cloud.google.com/vision/docs</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/translate/docs\">cloud.google.com/translate/docs</a></p>","lessons":["d3l4"]},"e4q15":{"id":"e4q15","exam":4,"n":15,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>SkyPeak Labs wants to consolidate several test environments so Linux and Windows workloads can run separately on the same physical server. Which technology makes it possible to run multiple computer operating systems on one physical hardware machine?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Hypervisor</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Containers</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Serverless computing</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Open source</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Hypervisor</strong></p>\n<p>A hypervisor is the software layer that creates and runs virtual machines. It splits one physical server into several VMs, and each VM can run its own operating system, such as Linux and Windows side by side. This is the basis of virtualization and of Compute Engine.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. Containers</strong>: Containers share the host's operating system kernel, so they don't run different full operating systems.\n- <strong>C. Serverless computing</strong>: Serverless hides the servers entirely; it isn't the technology that runs multiple OSes on one machine.\n- <strong>D. Open source</strong>: Open source is a licensing model, not a virtualization technology.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Multiple operating systems on one physical machine → hypervisor (VMs).</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-a-virtual-machine\">cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-a-virtual-machine</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/discover/containers-vs-vms\">cloud.google.com/discover/containers-vs-vms</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/compute/docs/overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/compute/docs/overview</a></p>","lessons":["d4l2"]},"e4q16":{"id":"e4q16","exam":4,"n":16,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>CareNest, a healthcare support app, has received customer feedback that patients need help outside regular business hours. The company decides to extend its phone support availability into evenings and weekends. Why is CareNest making this change?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Users expect professional expertise</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Users require personalization</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Users expect always-on services</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Users require regional access</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Users expect always-on services</strong></p>\n<p>Modern customers expect help and services to be available whenever they need them, not only in office hours. Extending support into evenings and weekends responds directly to that. The clue is \"outside regular business hours\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Professional expertise</strong>: The feedback is about when support is available, not the skill of the staff.\n- <strong>B. Personalization</strong>: Personalization means tailoring to each user, which the change doesn't address.\n- <strong>D. Regional access</strong>: The issue is time of day, not geographic location.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> \"Evenings and weekends\", \"24/7\" → always-on expectations.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-digital-transformation\">cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-digital-transformation</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/ai-machine-learning/introducing-customer-engagement-suite-with-google-ai\">cloud.google.com/blog/products/ai-machine-learning/introducing-customer-engagement-suite-with-google-ai</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/contact-center/insights/docs\">docs.cloud.google.com/contact-center/insights/docs</a></p>","lessons":["d1l1"]},"e4q17":{"id":"e4q17","exam":4,"n":17,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>FinBridge Analytics is building internal AI assistants for finance, compliance, and customer support teams. The company wants a managed Google Cloud platform where developers can explore foundation models, adapt them using company data, and deploy production-ready AI agents without managing the full infrastructure themselves. Which Google Cloud solution should FinBridge Analytics choose?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Compute Engine</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Cloud Load Balancing</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Google Kubernetes Engine</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform</strong></p>\n<p>Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform (formerly Vertex AI) is Google Cloud's managed AI platform. Developers can explore foundation models, tune or ground them with company data, and build and deploy AI agents without running the infrastructure. The clues \"foundation models\", \"adapt with company data\" and \"deploy agents\" all point to it.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. Compute Engine</strong>: Compute Engine gives raw VMs, so the team would manage everything themselves.\n- <strong>C. Cloud Load Balancing</strong>: Load balancing spreads traffic across servers; it isn't an AI development platform.\n- <strong>D. Google Kubernetes Engine</strong>: GKE runs containers but would require the team to build and manage the AI stack.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Foundation models + company data + deploy agents → Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/model-garden\">cloud.google.com/model-garden</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/build/runtime\">docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/build/runtime</a></p>","lessons":["d3l6","d3l8"]},"e4q18":{"id":"e4q18","exam":4,"n":18,"domain":5,"html":"<p><strong>TicketNest, an online event booking platform, is moving sensitive customer information such as names, email addresses, booking history, and payment-related records to Google Cloud. The security team wants to know which Google Cloud privacy policy helps protect customer data from being used outside the purpose of providing cloud services. Which policy supports this requirement?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Google tests the service availability of customer applications.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Google does not use customer data for advertising purposes.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Google migrates customer data to an offline server when a threat is detected.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Google does not allow customers to change encryption keys.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Google does not use customer data for advertising purposes.</strong></p>\n<p>Google Cloud commits that it doesn't use customer data for ads. Customer data is processed only to provide the cloud services the customer contracted for. This is the policy that keeps data from being used \"outside the purpose of providing cloud services\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Tests availability of customer applications</strong>: Testing uptime is unrelated to how customer data is used.\n- <strong>C. Migrates data to an offline server</strong>: This is not a real Google Cloud policy and has nothing to do with data use.\n- <strong>D. Doesn't allow changing encryption keys</strong>: False: customers can manage and rotate their own keys with Cloud KMS.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Google Cloud privacy commitment: your data is not used for ads.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/transparency\">cloud.google.com/transparency</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/privacy/data-protection-impact-assessment\">cloud.google.com/privacy/data-protection-impact-assessment</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/docs/security/encryption/default-encryption\">docs.cloud.google.com/docs/security/encryption/default-encryption</a></p>","lessons":["d5l3","d5l6","d5l8"]},"e4q19":{"id":"e4q19","exam":4,"n":19,"domain":5,"html":"<p><strong>BrightWave Journal, a digital publishing company, is shutting down its private data center. The company wants its editors, designers, and partners to create, access, and share content through cloud-based services instead of local infrastructure. What should BrightWave Journal consider during this move?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Built-in security when moving their data to the cloud</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Replacing their perimeter security with data encryption keys</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Optimizing cost management with a capital expenditure model</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Increasing physical hardware capacity when moving their data to the cloud</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Built-in security when moving their data to the cloud</strong></p>\n<p>Moving to Google Cloud means relying on security that is built into the platform: a secure global infrastructure, encryption by default, and identity-based access. When the company leaves its own data center, these default protections become part of how its content stays safe. The company still configures access and protects its own data.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. Replace perimeter security with keys</strong>: Encryption keys are one control, not a replacement for a full security approach.\n- <strong>C. Cost management with CapEx</strong>: Cloud moves spending toward OpEx, not CapEx.\n- <strong>D. Increase physical hardware capacity</strong>: In the cloud the provider manages hardware; the company doesn't buy more.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Moving to Google Cloud → you gain built-in, default security layers.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/docs/security/overview/whitepaper\">cloud.google.com/docs/security/overview/whitepaper</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/security/shared-responsibility-shared-fate\">cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/security/shared-responsibility-shared-fate</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/cloud-migration\">cloud.google.com/learn/cloud-migration</a></p>\n<p><strong>⚠️ Note:</strong> The keyed wording is vague. It means Google's default protections (encryption, secure infrastructure). The other options are plainly wrong, so pick A on this exam.</p>","lessons":["d5l6"]},"e4q20":{"id":"e4q20","exam":4,"n":20,"domain":5,"html":"<p><strong>PixelloApps is moving several web servers for its image-sharing platform to Google Cloud by using an infrastructure as a service model. The team wants to understand the shared responsibility model before deploying its virtual machines. In this model, which security responsibility belongs to the cloud provider?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Defining user permissions for application data</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Hardening and patching the guest operating system</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Securing the custom application code</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Protecting the physical data center facilities</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Protecting the physical data center facilities</strong></p>\n<p>In the shared responsibility model, Google always secures the physical layer: buildings, hardware and the global network. With IaaS (Compute Engine VMs), the customer keeps more duties than with PaaS or SaaS. The clue is \"infrastructure as a service\", which leaves only the lowest layers to Google.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Defining user permissions</strong>: Access to application data is always the customer's job.\n- <strong>B. Patching the guest OS</strong>: In IaaS the customer manages and patches the operating system on its VMs.\n- <strong>C. Securing custom application code</strong>: The customer owns and secures its own code.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Google always owns physical security. In IaaS, the customer owns the OS and everything above it.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/security/shared-responsibility-shared-fate\">docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/security/shared-responsibility-shared-fate</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/docs/security/overview/whitepaper\">docs.cloud.google.com/docs/security/overview/whitepaper</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/security/infrastructure\">cloud.google.com/security/infrastructure</a></p>","lessons":["d5l3"]},"e4q21":{"id":"e4q21","exam":4,"n":21,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>PageVerse, a digital library startup, wants to scan and publish thousands of archived newspapers, letters, and photographs so researchers can access them online. Why is a public cloud a better choice than hosting everything in the company’s own data center?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. It requires the company to manage more in-house hardware</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. It provides a dedicated physical encryption key by default</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. It is cost-effective when storing and serving data at large scale</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. It focuses mainly on increasing capital expenditure</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It is cost-effective when storing and serving data at large scale</strong></p>\n<p>Public cloud gives near-unlimited storage and global delivery on a pay-as-you-go basis. PageVerse can store and serve large volumes of scans without buying and running its own hardware. Costs grow with use instead of requiring big upfront purchases.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. More in-house hardware</strong>: Public cloud reduces the hardware a company must manage.\n- <strong>B. Dedicated physical encryption key</strong>: Data is encrypted by default, but not with a dedicated physical key per customer.\n- <strong>D. Increasing capital expenditure</strong>: Cloud shifts spending away from CapEx toward OpEx.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Large-scale storage and delivery without buying hardware → public cloud.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/advantages-of-cloud-computing\">cloud.google.com/learn/advantages-of-cloud-computing</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/pricing\">cloud.google.com/pricing</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/storage/docs/introduction\">docs.cloud.google.com/storage/docs/introduction</a></p>","lessons":["d1l2","d1l3","d1l4"]},"e4q22":{"id":"e4q22","exam":4,"n":22,"domain":6,"html":"<p><strong>SkyCart Logistics uses Google Cloud to run a package-tracking portal for its customers. The operations team is reviewing reliability targets and wants to understand how cloud service availability is commonly expressed. How is service availability measured in cloud technology?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Number of available regions</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Percentage of uptime</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Speed of response time</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Number of downtime incidents</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Percentage of uptime</strong></p>\n<p>Availability is the share of time a service is up and working, expressed as a percentage, such as 99.9% or 99.99%. Each extra nine means less allowed downtime. For example, 99.99% allows roughly 52 minutes of downtime a year.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Number of regions</strong>: More regions can help availability, but they are not how it is measured.\n- <strong>C. Speed of response</strong>: Response time is latency, a performance metric, not availability.\n- <strong>D. Number of downtime incidents</strong>: The count of incidents ignores how long each one lasted, so it isn't the standard measure.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Availability = % uptime (the \"nines\").</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/compute/sla\">cloud.google.com/compute/sla</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/storage/sla\">cloud.google.com/storage/sla</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/reliability/use-slos-and-slis\">cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/reliability/use-slos-and-slis</a></p>","lessons":["d6l4"]},"e4q23":{"id":"e4q23","exam":4,"n":23,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>MealTrail, a small food delivery app, needs a budget-friendly database to store customer profiles, restaurant menus, orders, and payment records. The app requires SQL queries and a relational structure, but the team does not want to manage database servers manually. Which Google Cloud service should MealTrail use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Cloud Storage</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. BigQuery</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Cloud SQL</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Dataflow</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cloud SQL</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud SQL is Google Cloud's fully managed relational database for MySQL, PostgreSQL and SQL Server. It supports SQL and tables, and Google handles patching, backups and server maintenance. For a small app it is a cost-effective choice.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Cloud Storage</strong>: Cloud Storage holds objects (files), not relational tables with SQL queries.\n- <strong>B. BigQuery</strong>: BigQuery is a data warehouse for analytics, not an operational database for an app's orders and payments.\n- <strong>D. Dataflow</strong>: Dataflow processes data in pipelines; it doesn't store it as a database.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Small or medium relational app database, managed → Cloud SQL.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/sql/docs/introduction\">docs.cloud.google.com/sql/docs/introduction</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/introduction\">docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/introduction</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/dataflow/docs/overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/dataflow/docs/overview</a></p>","lessons":["d2l6"]},"e4q24":{"id":"e4q24","exam":4,"n":24,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>PixelTrail Media is creating a web platform that can analyze customer support content from many formats, including written messages, source code snippets, voice recordings, screenshots, and short videos. The team wants to use Google Cloud’s multimodal foundation model that can understand and combine information across these different input types. Which model should PixelTrail Media use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. BigQuery</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Gemini</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Cloud Storage</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Cloud SQL</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Gemini</strong></p>\n<p>Gemini is Google's multimodal foundation model family. It can understand and reason across text, code, audio, images and video in one model. The clue is \"multimodal foundation model\" handling many input types.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. BigQuery</strong>: BigQuery is a data warehouse, not a foundation model.\n- <strong>C. Cloud Storage</strong>: Cloud Storage stores files; it doesn't understand their content.\n- <strong>D. Cloud SQL</strong>: Cloud SQL is a relational database, not an AI model.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Multimodal foundation model from Google → Gemini.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/use-cases/multimodal-ai\">cloud.google.com/use-cases/multimodal-ai</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/models/google-models\">docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/models/google-models</a>\n- <a href=\"https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs/models\">ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs/models</a></p>","lessons":["d3l7"]},"e4q25":{"id":"e4q25","exam":4,"n":25,"domain":6,"html":"<p><strong>PixelQuest Studio runs a cloud-native mobile game called RuneDash on Google Cloud. The team tracks service reliability using service level objectives and error budgets. During the current review period, RuneDash is meeting its service level objective and the team still has plenty of error budget remaining. What should PixelQuest Studio prioritize?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Innovation to improve user experience</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Hardware reliability to improve availability</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Stability to avoid prolonged user downtime</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Speed to release new features</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Innovation to improve user experience</strong></p>\n<p>An error budget is the amount of unreliability the SLO allows (100% − SLO). RuneDash is meeting its SLO and has plenty of budget left, so the service is reliable enough. SRE practice says to spend the remaining budget on innovation: ship improvements and take reasonable risks, because users are already getting the reliability they were promised.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. Hardware reliability</strong>: Reliability work is the priority when the SLO is missed or the budget is used up, not when budget remains.\n- <strong>C. Stability to avoid downtime</strong>: Same reason. The service is already within its target.\n- <strong>D. Speed to release new features</strong>: Close, but release speed alone isn't the goal. The budget is spent on innovation that improves the user experience, and raw speed can burn the budget carelessly.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Budget left → innovate. SLO missed / budget gone → reliability.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/sre\">cloud.google.com/sre</a>\n- <a href=\"https://sre.google/workbook/error-budget-policy\">sre.google/workbook/error-budget-policy</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/service-mesh/docs/observability/design-slo\">docs.cloud.google.com/service-mesh/docs/observability/design-slo</a></p>","lessons":["d6l5"]},"e4q26":{"id":"e4q26","exam":4,"n":26,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>NovaVerse AI is building a mobile app that generates personalized study videos from text prompts. The team needs to train very large foundation models as quickly as possible and wants Google Cloud hardware that is purpose-built for high-performance machine learning workloads. Which Google Cloud hardware components should NovaVerse AI use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Standard hard disk drives (HDDs)</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. GPUs and TPUs</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Archival storage nodes</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Virtual Private Cloud routers</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. GPUs and TPUs</strong></p>\n<p>GPUs and TPUs are accelerators built for the heavy math in machine learning. TPUs are Google's custom chips designed specifically for ML, and GPUs are widely used for training large models. The clue is \"purpose-built for high-performance machine learning workloads\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Standard HDDs</strong>: Hard disks are slow storage, not compute for training.\n- <strong>C. Archival storage nodes</strong>: Archival storage is for rarely accessed data, not fast training.\n- <strong>D. VPC routers</strong>: Routers move network traffic; they don't run ML computations.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Train large models fast → GPUs and TPUs (TPUs are Google's ML-specific chips).</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/compute/docs/gpus/overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/compute/docs/gpus/overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/tpu/docs/intro-to-tpu\">docs.cloud.google.com/tpu/docs/intro-to-tpu</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/ai-hypercomputer/docs/overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/ai-hypercomputer/docs/overview</a></p>","lessons":["d3l9"]},"e4q27":{"id":"e4q27","exam":4,"n":27,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>BrightCart, an online grocery company, is moving its internal order processing and inventory applications from its own data center to the cloud. How could this move affect the company’s operations and personnel costs?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Reduced on-premises infrastructure management costs</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Increased on-premises hardware maintenance costs</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Reduced cloud software licensing costs</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Increased cloud hardware management costs</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Reduced on-premises infrastructure management costs</strong></p>\n<p>When applications move to the cloud, Google runs the physical hardware, power, cooling and much of the maintenance. The company spends less time and money managing its own servers. Staff can then shift from upkeep to work that adds business value.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. Increased on-premises maintenance</strong>: Moving out of the data center reduces on-premises maintenance.\n- <strong>C. Reduced cloud software licensing</strong>: Licensing costs are not the main effect, and they don't necessarily go down.\n- <strong>D. Increased cloud hardware management</strong>: The customer doesn't manage cloud hardware; Google does.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Moving to cloud → less infrastructure management for your own team.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-cloud-migration\">cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-cloud-migration</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/advantages-of-cloud-computing\">cloud.google.com/learn/advantages-of-cloud-computing</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/cost-optimization\">cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/cost-optimization</a></p>","lessons":["d1l3"]},"e4q28":{"id":"e4q28","exam":4,"n":28,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>StyleSnapper, a mobile shopping app, wants to build a custom machine learning model that can sort its private catalog photos into categories such as shoes, bags, watches, and jackets. The team already has accurate labeled image examples, but they do not have enough experience to write advanced machine learning training code. Which Google Cloud solution should they use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. AutoML on Agent Platform</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Cloud Storage Coldline</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Dataproc</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Cloud Interconnect</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. AutoML on Agent Platform</strong></p>\n<p>AutoML trains a custom model on your own labeled data without you writing training code. StyleSnapper has labeled images and custom categories but lacks ML coding experience, which is exactly AutoML's use case. It runs inside Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform (formerly Vertex AI).</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. Cloud Storage Coldline</strong>: Coldline is a storage class for rarely accessed data, not an ML tool.\n- <strong>C. Dataproc</strong>: Dataproc runs managed Spark and Hadoop clusters for data processing, not no-code model training.\n- <strong>D. Cloud Interconnect</strong>: Interconnect is a private network link to Google Cloud, unrelated to ML.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Custom categories + labeled data + no ML coding → AutoML.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/machine-learning/image-data/classification/train-model\">docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/machine-learning/image-data/classification/train-model</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/machine-learning/training/automl-training-overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/machine-learning/training/automl-training-overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/storage-classes\">cloud.google.com/storage/docs/storage-classes</a></p>","lessons":["d3l1","d3l3","d3l4","d3l5","d3l6"]},"e4q29":{"id":"e4q29","exam":4,"n":29,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>FreshCart Lane, a regional grocery delivery website, is replacing its owned data center equipment with Google Cloud services. The finance team wants to understand how this move usually changes the way the company pays for IT. How does the company’s IT spending generally shift when FreshCart Lane adopts cloud technology?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Away from cost management toward capital expenditure</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Away from operational expenditure toward cost management</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Away from capital expenditure toward operational expenditure</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Away from operational expenditure toward capital expenditure</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Away from capital expenditure toward operational expenditure</strong></p>\n<p>On premises, a company buys servers and data center equipment up front. That is capital expenditure (CapEx). In the cloud it rents capacity and pays monthly for what it uses, which is operational expenditure (OpEx). So moving to Google Cloud shifts IT spending from CapEx toward OpEx.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Cost management → CapEx</strong>: Cost management isn't a spending model, and cloud moves away from CapEx, not toward it.\n- <strong>B. OpEx → cost management</strong>: Cloud increases OpEx. It doesn't move away from it.\n- <strong>D. OpEx → CapEx</strong>: This is exactly backwards.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Cloud migration = CapEx → OpEx.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/cloud-migration\">cloud.google.com/learn/cloud-migration</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/cost-optimization\">docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/cost-optimization</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-iaas\">cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-iaas</a></p>","lessons":["d1l3"]},"e4q30":{"id":"e4q30","exam":4,"n":30,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>ShopSphere, an online shopping platform, is evaluating which data should be stored directly in a relational database. Which type of raw data is best suited to be stored in a relational database without additional processing?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Product catalog stock levels</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Product banner images</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Product demonstration videos</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Buyer support call recordings</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Product catalog stock levels</strong></p>\n<p>Relational databases hold structured data that fits neatly into rows and columns. Stock levels are numbers tied to product IDs, so they can be stored directly in a table. The other options are unstructured media files.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. Banner images</strong>: Images are unstructured data; they belong in object storage such as Cloud Storage.\n- <strong>C. Demonstration videos</strong>: Videos are unstructured and are stored as objects, not table rows.\n- <strong>D. Call recordings</strong>: Audio is unstructured and would need processing (such as transcription) before it fits a table.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Rows and columns → relational database. Images, video, audio → Cloud Storage.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/sql/docs\">cloud.google.com/sql/docs</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/introduction\">cloud.google.com/storage/docs/introduction</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/architecture/storage-advisor\">cloud.google.com/architecture/storage-advisor</a></p>","lessons":["d2l3","d2l6"]},"e4q31":{"id":"e4q31","exam":4,"n":31,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>GreenNest Retail maintains its inventory and analytics applications on servers in its own office data center. Its development team is unhappy because provisioning environments is slow, scaling is difficult, and experimentation is limited by fixed hardware capacity. How would moving to Google Cloud help the developers?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. They can expect 100% service availability.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. They can avoid the limitations of serverless computing.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. They can have new tools to innovate and optimize resource usage.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. They can optimize maintenance for their on-premises infrastructure.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. They can have new tools to innovate and optimize resource usage.</strong></p>\n<p>The cloud lets developers create environments in minutes, scale on demand and use managed services and new tools. They pay only for what they use, so experiments are cheap and resources aren't wasted. This removes the slow provisioning and fixed capacity limits in the stem.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. 100% availability</strong>: No cloud provider guarantees 100% availability.\n- <strong>B. Avoid serverless limitations</strong>: Serverless is one of the cloud tools developers gain, not something they escape.\n- <strong>D. Optimize on-premises maintenance</strong>: The move is away from on-premises, so this doesn't help developers.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Slow provisioning and fixed capacity → cloud gives on-demand tools and elastic resources.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/docs/overview\">cloud.google.com/docs/overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/run/docs/overview/what-is-cloud-run\">cloud.google.com/run/docs/overview/what-is-cloud-run</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/cost-optimization\">cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/cost-optimization</a></p>","lessons":["d1l1","d1l2"]},"e4q32":{"id":"e4q32","exam":4,"n":32,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>TravelNest, an online hotel booking platform, runs its main web application from a Google Cloud region in Western Europe. Users in Singapore and Japan say pages take longer to load, even though their internet speed tests show high download and upload speeds. What is the most likely cause?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Too few application servers</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Insufficient network bandwidth</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Network latency</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Incorrectly configured application servers</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Network latency</strong></p>\n<p>Latency is the delay for data to travel between the user and the server. Users in Asia reaching a region in Western Europe face long physical distance, so each request takes longer. Their high speed-test results show bandwidth is fine; distance is the problem.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Too few servers</strong>: Users near Europe would also suffer if capacity were short; the issue here is location.\n- <strong>B. Insufficient bandwidth</strong>: Speed tests show high bandwidth, which rules this out.\n- <strong>D. Misconfigured servers</strong>: A configuration problem would affect all users, not just distant ones.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Fast connection but slow pages for distant users → latency (distance).</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/load-balancing/docs/load-balancing-overview\">cloud.google.com/load-balancing/docs/load-balancing-overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/cdn/docs/overview\">cloud.google.com/cdn/docs/overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/docs/geography-and-regions\">cloud.google.com/docs/geography-and-regions</a></p>","lessons":["d1l6"]},"e4q33":{"id":"e4q33","exam":4,"n":33,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>FitTrackly, a fitness coaching website, is moving from its on-premises systems to Google Cloud. The leadership team wants to understand what this move means beyond simply changing where applications run. What does adopting cloud computing mainly represent for the company’s transformation?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. A change that benefits only very large organizations</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. A chance to redesign current business processes and services</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. A change that matters only to the IT team</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. A way to keep the same business model while only reducing costs</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. A chance to redesign current business processes and services</strong></p>\n<p>Digital transformation is more than moving servers. The cloud gives companies the chance to rethink how they work, serve customers and create new products. Leadership is asking about \"beyond simply changing where applications run\", which is this idea.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Only large organizations</strong>: Cloud benefits organizations of every size, including small startups.\n- <strong>C. Only the IT team</strong>: Transformation affects the whole business, not just IT.\n- <strong>D. Same model, only lower costs</strong>: Cost savings alone miss the main point of transformation.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Cloud transformation = rethinking the business, not just relocating IT.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-cloud-computing\">cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-cloud-computing</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/architecture/framework\">cloud.google.com/architecture/framework</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/transform/digital-transformation\">cloud.google.com/transform/digital-transformation</a></p>","lessons":["d1l1","d1l7"]},"e4q34":{"id":"e4q34","exam":4,"n":34,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>BrightCart Analytics runs nightly sales-report calculations on a Compute Engine virtual machine. The job saves progress regularly, can be restarted without data loss, and does not need to finish immediately. The company wants the cheapest suitable compute option for this workload. Which option should BrightCart Analytics choose?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. A Spot VM on Compute Engine</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. A custom VM in a pay-as-you-go model on Compute Engine</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. A standard VM in a pay-as-you-go model on Compute Engine</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. A Cloud Run function with a small memory limit</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. A Spot VM on Compute Engine</strong></p>\n<p>Spot VMs run on Google's spare capacity at a large discount. Google can stop them when it needs the capacity back. That is acceptable here because the job saves progress, can restart without data loss and doesn't need to finish right away. These are the classic signs of a Spot VM workload.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. Custom VM, pay-as-you-go</strong>: Custom sizing can save a little, but it is still full price compared with Spot.\n- <strong>C. Standard VM, pay-as-you-go</strong>: Works, but it is the most expensive of the VM choices for an interruptible job.\n- <strong>D. Cloud Run function with small memory</strong>: Functions are for short, event-driven code, not long nightly batch calculations.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Interruptible + restartable + cheapest → Spot VMs (the successor to Preemptible VMs).</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/compute/docs/instances/spot\">docs.cloud.google.com/compute/docs/instances/spot</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/spot-vms/pricing\">cloud.google.com/spot-vms/pricing</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/functions/docs\">docs.cloud.google.com/functions/docs</a></p>","lessons":["d4l2"]},"e4q35":{"id":"e4q35","exam":4,"n":35,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>StormMap Analytics, a climate technology company, is building a machine learning model to predict severe storms, floods, and heat waves across several regions. How should StormMap Analytics collect training data to improve prediction accuracy?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Collect all weather data evenly across all regions</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Collect all weather data primarily from high-risk regions</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Collect only severe weather data evenly across all regions</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Collect only severe weather data primarily from high-risk regions</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Collect all weather data evenly across all regions</strong></p>\n<p>A model learns patterns from its training data, so the data must represent the conditions it will predict for. StormMap predicts across several regions, so it needs data from all of them. It also needs normal weather as well as severe events, so the model can learn what comes before a storm and tell severe from normal.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. All data, mainly from high-risk regions</strong>: The model would be skewed toward those regions and predict poorly elsewhere.\n- <strong>C. Only severe data, evenly</strong>: With no normal weather, the model has nothing to compare severe events against.\n- <strong>D. Only severe data, high-risk regions</strong>: Both problems at once: biased regions and no normal cases.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Training data must be representative: every region, normal and extreme cases.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course/overfitting/imbalanced-datasets\">developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course/overfitting/imbalanced-datasets</a>\n- <a href=\"https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course/classification\">developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course/classification</a>\n- <a href=\"https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course/fairness/types-of-bias\">developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course/fairness/types-of-bias</a></p>","lessons":["d3l2"]},"e4q36":{"id":"e4q36","exam":4,"n":36,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>FitGlow, a wellness mobile app company, wants to build an application that can adjust workout plans, meal suggestions, and notifications based on each user’s changing preferences across different seasons. Why should FitGlow build a cloud-native application instead of only modernizing its existing on-premises application?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Developers can depend on the cloud provider to write all source code</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Developers can release new features in an agile way</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. IT managers can move the existing application architecture without making updates</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. IT managers can speed up capital expenditure planning</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Developers can release new features in an agile way</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud-native apps are built from microservices, containers and managed services. Teams can update one part at a time and ship changes often and safely. That fits an app that must keep adapting to users' changing preferences.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Provider writes the source code</strong>: The company's developers still write their own code.\n- <strong>C. Move architecture without updates</strong>: That describes lift-and-shift, not building cloud-native.\n- <strong>D. Speed up CapEx planning</strong>: Cloud-native apps use pay-as-you-go OpEx, not more capital planning.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Cloud-native → frequent, agile releases.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-cloud-native\">cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-cloud-native</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/resources/rearchitecting-to-cloud-native\">cloud.google.com/resources/rearchitecting-to-cloud-native</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/solutions/application-modernization\">cloud.google.com/solutions/application-modernization</a></p>","lessons":["d4l1"]},"e4q37":{"id":"e4q37","exam":4,"n":37,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>PuzzleRift Games is modernizing its multiplayer arcade platform so the game backend can run on Google Cloud instead of company-owned servers in its office data center. What should PuzzleRift Games generally expect when moving from traditional on-premises technology to cloud technology?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. No change to existing responsibilities</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. A shift toward OpEx</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. A shift toward using structured data</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Increased hardware maintenance</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. A shift toward OpEx</strong></p>\n<p>On-premises technology needs upfront capital spending on servers and facilities. In the cloud, the company pays for resources as it uses them, which is operational expenditure. This is the main financial change when moving to the cloud.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. No change to responsibilities</strong>: Responsibilities change: Google takes over the physical infrastructure.\n- <strong>C. Shift toward structured data</strong>: Moving to the cloud doesn't change the type of data a business has.\n- <strong>D. Increased hardware maintenance</strong>: Hardware maintenance goes down because Google handles it.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> On-premises → cloud = CapEx → OpEx.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/cloud-migration\">cloud.google.com/learn/cloud-migration</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/cost-optimization\">docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/cost-optimization</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/advantages-of-cloud-computing\">cloud.google.com/learn/advantages-of-cloud-computing</a></p>","lessons":["d1l3"]},"e4q38":{"id":"e4q38","exam":4,"n":38,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>MetroFleet Logistics runs its vehicle booking platform on an on-premises SQL Server database. The database team is spending too much time handling capacity planning, patching, and routine administration. The company wants to move quickly to a Google Cloud managed service while keeping the SQL Server database approach. What should MetroFleet Logistics do?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Redesign the applications to use a cloud-first database such as Firestore.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Use Database Migration Service to migrate the database to Cloud SQL.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Export historical SQL Server tables to Cloud Storage.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Move all SQL Server data into BigQuery.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Use Database Migration Service to migrate the database to Cloud SQL.</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud SQL supports SQL Server as a managed service, so Google handles patching, backups and capacity. Database Migration Service helps move the database with little downtime. The clue is \"move quickly\" while \"keeping the SQL Server database approach\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Redesign for Firestore</strong>: Rewriting for a NoSQL database is slow and drops the SQL Server approach.\n- <strong>C. Export tables to Cloud Storage</strong>: Files in Cloud Storage aren't a running database.\n- <strong>D. Move data into BigQuery</strong>: BigQuery is for analytics, not the operational database for a booking platform.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Keep the same relational engine, stop managing it → Cloud SQL (via Database Migration Service).</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/sql/docs/sqlserver\">docs.cloud.google.com/sql/docs/sqlserver</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/database-migration/docs/sqlserver/guide\">docs.cloud.google.com/database-migration/docs/sqlserver/guide</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/sql\">cloud.google.com/sql</a></p>","lessons":["d4l6"]},"e4q39":{"id":"e4q39","exam":4,"n":39,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>FreshCartGo, a grocery delivery mobile app, wants to build a data pipeline that reads customer order events, transforms them, and writes the results to BigQuery. The team wants to avoid managing servers or manually resizing processing clusters when order volume increases during weekends. What is the main benefit of using a serverless data processing pipeline service such as Dataflow?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. It gives the team complete control over the underlying compute resources.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. It removes the need to analyze the processed data.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. It manages and scales the pipeline infrastructure automatically.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. It guarantees that the processed data contains no errors.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It manages and scales the pipeline infrastructure automatically.</strong></p>\n<p>Dataflow is a serverless service for batch and streaming pipelines. Google provisions the workers and scales them up and down with the data volume. The team writes pipeline logic and doesn't resize clusters for weekend peaks.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Complete control of compute</strong>: Serverless gives less control over the underlying machines, not more.\n- <strong>B. Removes need to analyze data</strong>: Dataflow prepares data; analysis still happens, for example in BigQuery.\n- <strong>D. Guarantees no errors</strong>: No service guarantees error-free data; data quality still needs checks.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Serverless pipeline (Dataflow) = no servers to manage, automatic scaling.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/products/dataflow\">cloud.google.com/products/dataflow</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/dataflow/docs/overview\">cloud.google.com/dataflow/docs/overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/dataflow/docs/horizontal-autoscaling\">cloud.google.com/dataflow/docs/horizontal-autoscaling</a></p>","lessons":["d2l9"]},"e4q40":{"id":"e4q40","exam":4,"n":40,"domain":6,"html":"<p><strong>BrightCart runs an online grocery website on Google Cloud. The engineering team wants to copy important application components across multiple resources so the website can continue operating if one component fails. Which design consideration should BrightCart use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Redundancy</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Backups</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Latency</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Security</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Redundancy</strong></p>\n<p>Redundancy means running duplicate copies of important components, such as instances across several zones. If one fails, another keeps serving traffic. The clue is \"copy components across multiple resources\" so the site \"continues operating\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. Backups</strong>: Backups let you restore data later; they don't keep the site running during a failure.\n- <strong>C. Latency</strong>: Latency is response delay, not a resilience design.\n- <strong>D. Security</strong>: Security protects against threats, not against component failure.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Keep running when something fails → redundancy. Recover lost data → backups.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/reliability/build-highly-available-systems\">docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/reliability/build-highly-available-systems</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/reliability\">docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/reliability</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/infra-reliability-guide/design\">docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/infra-reliability-guide/design</a></p>","lessons":["d6l9"]},"e4q41":{"id":"e4q41","exam":4,"n":41,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>StreamNest Media hosts its video preview application in one Google Cloud region in Germany. Many users in the United States are experiencing high latency when opening the application. The company wants to improve the user experience for those U.S. customers. What should StreamNest Media do?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Create a separate billing account for users in the United States.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Deploy the application across more zones in the same German region.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Deploy the application across more regions in Europe.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Deploy a replica of the application in a region in the United States.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Deploy a replica of the application in a region in the United States.</strong></p>\n<p>Latency comes from physical distance between users and the servers. Running a copy of the app in a U.S. region puts it close to U.S. users, which cuts the delay. Google Cloud's global regions make this possible.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Separate billing account</strong>: Billing has no effect on network performance.\n- <strong>B. More zones in Germany</strong>: Zones help availability, but the app is still far from U.S. users.\n- <strong>C. More regions in Europe</strong>: Europe is still far from the United States.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Distant users with high latency → deploy closer to them, in their region.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/docs/get-started/consider-geographic-distribution\">docs.cloud.google.com/docs/get-started/consider-geographic-distribution</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/performance-optimization/plan-resource-allocation\">docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/performance-optimization/plan-resource-allocation</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/load-balancing/docs/https/http-load-balancing-best-practices\">docs.cloud.google.com/load-balancing/docs/https/http-load-balancing-best-practices</a></p>","lessons":["d1l6"]},"e4q42":{"id":"e4q42","exam":4,"n":42,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>TrendCart, an online clothing marketplace, builds a machine learning model to recommend fast-changing seasonal styles to shoppers. The model gives weak predictions because it was trained only on customer behavior and product catalog data from three years ago, even though fashion preferences change frequently. Which data quality dimension is most affected?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Uniqueness</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Validity</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Timeliness</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Consistency</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Timeliness</strong></p>\n<p>Timeliness means data is current enough for its purpose. Fashion trends change quickly, so three-year-old behavior data no longer reflects what shoppers want. The clues are \"three years ago\" and \"change frequently\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Uniqueness</strong>: Uniqueness is about duplicate records, which isn't the issue.\n- <strong>B. Validity</strong>: Validity is about values following the right format or rules.\n- <strong>D. Consistency</strong>: Consistency is about data agreeing across systems or records.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Stale data → timeliness. Duplicates → uniqueness. Wrong format → validity.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-data-governance\">cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-data-governance</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/dataplex/docs/auto-data-quality-overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/dataplex/docs/auto-data-quality-overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://ibm.com/think/topics/data-quality-dimensions\">ibm.com/think/topics/data-quality-dimensions</a></p>","lessons":["d2l4"]},"e4q43":{"id":"e4q43","exam":4,"n":43,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>HealthHive, a telemedicine app, is creating AI features that help users understand symptoms and book care. To keep users confident in the platform, HealthHive defines strict principles so its AI systems are transparent, fair, safe, privacy-preserving, and designed to reduce harmful bias. What practice is HealthHive applying?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Responsible AI</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Data virtualization</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Edge computing</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Legacy modernization</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Responsible AI</strong></p>\n<p>Responsible AI means building AI that is fair, transparent, safe, privacy-preserving and accountable. Google publishes its own AI Principles on the same lines. The stem lists exactly those qualities.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. Data virtualization</strong>: This means querying data where it lives without copying it; it isn't about ethics.\n- <strong>C. Edge computing</strong>: Edge computing processes data close to where it is created, unrelated to AI principles.\n- <strong>D. Legacy modernization</strong>: This means updating old systems, not governing AI behavior.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Fair, transparent, safe, private, low bias → Responsible AI.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/responsible-ai\">cloud.google.com/responsible-ai</a>\n- <a href=\"https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/guides/intro-responsible-ai\">developers.google.com/machine-learning/guides/intro-responsible-ai</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/security/use-ai-securely-and-responsibly\">docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/security/use-ai-securely-and-responsibly</a></p>","lessons":["d3l10"]},"e4q44":{"id":"e4q44","exam":4,"n":44,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>BrightCart, an ecommerce website, is comparing Google Cloud’s infrastructure approach with traditional off-the-shelf server hardware. The infrastructure team learns that Google uses purpose-built servers in its data centers. What is one main benefit of these purpose-built servers compared with standard servers?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. They are optimized for specific tasks, making them more efficient.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. They are backward compatible with legacy disk drives.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. They are cheaper to build than standard servers.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. They run software that cannot be deployed on standard servers.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. They are optimized for specific tasks, making them more efficient.</strong></p>\n<p>Google designs its own servers and data centers for its workloads, leaving out parts it doesn't need. This makes them more efficient in performance and energy use, and it reduces the attack surface. The clue is \"purpose-built\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. Backward compatible with legacy disks</strong>: Compatibility with old hardware isn't the goal of custom servers.\n- <strong>C. Cheaper to build</strong>: The main benefit stated is efficiency, not a lower build cost per server.\n- <strong>D. Software that can't run elsewhere</strong>: Google Cloud runs standard software such as Linux and containers.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Purpose-built = optimized for the task = more efficient.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/infrastructure\">cloud.google.com/infrastructure</a>\n- <a href=\"https://datacenters.google/efficiency\">datacenters.google/efficiency</a></p>","lessons":["d1l6"]},"e4q45":{"id":"e4q45","exam":4,"n":45,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>LoopLily, a social journaling mobile app, needs a database to store user profiles, post metadata, comments, and app settings. The development team wants a flexible and scalable NoSQL database that works well for web and mobile applications. Which Google Cloud product or service should the team use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Spanner</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Cloud Storage</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. BigQuery</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Firestore</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Firestore</strong></p>\n<p>Firestore is a serverless NoSQL document database built for web and mobile apps. It stores flexible documents such as profiles, posts and settings, and scales automatically. It also offers real-time sync and offline support for mobile clients.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Spanner</strong>: Spanner is a globally distributed relational database, not a flexible NoSQL store for a simple app.\n- <strong>B. Cloud Storage</strong>: Cloud Storage stores files, not app records you query.\n- <strong>C. BigQuery</strong>: BigQuery is an analytics data warehouse, not an app database.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> NoSQL for web and mobile apps → Firestore.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/firestore/native/docs/overview\">cloud.google.com/firestore/native/docs/overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/spanner/docs\">cloud.google.com/spanner/docs</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/introduction\">cloud.google.com/storage/docs/introduction</a></p>","lessons":["d2l6"]},"e4q46":{"id":"e4q46","exam":4,"n":46,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>FreshMart Loop is building a backend system for its grocery delivery website. The system must store daily order details, customer profiles, payment records, and purchase history. The information has a predictable structure, uses related tables, and needs to support cross-references between records. Which type of service should FreshMart Loop use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Non-relational database</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Data warehouse</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Relational database</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Data lake</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Relational database</strong></p>\n<p>Relational databases store structured data in related tables and use keys to link records. Orders, customers and payments with predictable structure and cross-references fit this model. It also supports reliable transactions for payment records.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Non-relational database</strong>: NoSQL suits flexible or changing structures, not fixed related tables.\n- <strong>B. Data warehouse</strong>: A warehouse is for large-scale analytics, not the day-to-day backend.\n- <strong>D. Data lake</strong>: A data lake stores raw data in any format, not structured transactional records.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Predictable structure + related tables + transactions → relational database.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/sql/docs/introduction\">docs.cloud.google.com/sql/docs/introduction</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/introduction\">docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/introduction</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-a-data-lake\">cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-a-data-lake</a></p>","lessons":["d2l5"]},"e4q47":{"id":"e4q47","exam":4,"n":47,"domain":5,"html":"<p><strong>FinPeak Wallet is moving its payment platform security controls to Google Cloud. The finance team wants to understand how the OpEx model affects cloud security spending. What is one benefit of this model?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. The company does not need to make large upfront capital investments in cloud security.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. The company can install and operate custom physical security hardware in Google Cloud data centers.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. The company does not need to configure security settings for its cloud resources.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Google Cloud fully guarantees security for every workload.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. The company does not need to make large upfront capital investments in cloud security.</strong></p>\n<p>With OpEx, security is consumed as a service and paid for as it's used. The company doesn't buy security hardware or build its own facilities upfront. It benefits from Google's investment in secure infrastructure.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. Install custom physical hardware</strong>: Customers can't place their own security hardware in Google's data centers.\n- <strong>C. No need to configure security</strong>: Under shared responsibility, customers still configure access and settings.\n- <strong>D. Google guarantees all security</strong>: Security is shared; Google doesn't guarantee every workload is secure.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> OpEx security = pay as you use, no big upfront investment.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/cost-optimization\">docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/cost-optimization</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/security\">docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/security</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/security/shared-responsibility-shared-fate\">docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/security/shared-responsibility-shared-fate</a></p>","lessons":["d5l3","d5l8"]},"e4q48":{"id":"e4q48","exam":4,"n":48,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>NovaForge Labs, a software company building generative AI tools, plans to run very large model training and inference workloads. The team needs a Google Cloud architecture that combines AI-optimized hardware, open software, popular ML frameworks, and flexible ways to consume compute capacity. What is this Google Cloud differentiator called?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Multi-cloud architecture</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. AI Hypercomputer</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Serverless data pipeline</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Distributed denial-of-service protection</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. AI Hypercomputer</strong></p>\n<p>AI Hypercomputer is Google Cloud's integrated architecture for AI. It combines AI-optimized hardware (TPUs, GPUs), open software, popular ML frameworks and flexible consumption models for training and serving at scale. The stem lists exactly these parts.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Multi-cloud architecture</strong>: Multi-cloud means using several cloud providers, not an AI architecture.\n- <strong>C. Serverless data pipeline</strong>: That describes services like Dataflow for data processing.\n- <strong>D. DDoS protection</strong>: This is a security capability, such as Cloud Armor.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Hardware + open software + ML frameworks + flexible consumption → AI Hypercomputer.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/ai-hypercomputer/docs/overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/ai-hypercomputer/docs/overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/ai-hypercomputer/docs/consumption-models\">docs.cloud.google.com/ai-hypercomputer/docs/consumption-models</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/docs/ai-ml\">docs.cloud.google.com/docs/ai-ml</a></p>","lessons":["d3l9"],"same":["e5q34"]},"e4q49":{"id":"e4q49","exam":4,"n":49,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>BrightTrail Foods runs an order-tracking website from its own data center and is planning to migrate the application to Google Cloud. The leadership team wants to compare the long-term financial impact of continuing on-premises operations with the expected cost and business value of using cloud services. Why should the team review total cost of ownership before migration?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. To evaluate error budget</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. To understand service level availability</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. To evaluate return on investment</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. To calculate required compute power</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. To evaluate return on investment</strong></p>\n<p>Total cost of ownership (TCO) counts all costs over time, including hardware, staff, facilities and licenses. Comparing on-premises TCO with cloud costs and expected business value shows the return on investment of migrating. The clue is \"long-term financial impact\" and \"business value\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Evaluate error budget</strong>: Error budget is an SRE reliability concept, not a financial comparison.\n- <strong>B. Service level availability</strong>: Availability is a reliability measure, not a cost analysis.\n- <strong>D. Required compute power</strong>: Sizing compute is a technical planning task, not the purpose of TCO.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> TCO comparison → informs ROI and the business case for migration.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/migration-center/docs/generate-tco-report\">cloud.google.com/migration-center/docs/generate-tco-report</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/migration-center/docs/migration-center-overview\">cloud.google.com/migration-center/docs/migration-center-overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/cost-optimization\">cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/cost-optimization</a></p>","lessons":["d1l3"]},"e4q50":{"id":"e4q50","exam":4,"n":50,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>Lendora Bank uses a machine learning model to evaluate mortgage applications and decide whether each request should be accepted or rejected. Because the company operates in a heavily regulated industry, its risk and compliance teams must show auditors why the model made a specific decision for each applicant. Which AI concept helps meet this requirement?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Generative AI</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Explainable AI</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Unstructured data processing</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Multi-cloud networking</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Explainable AI</strong></p>\n<p>Explainable AI shows which factors drove a model's prediction for each case. In regulated industries like lending, companies must justify decisions to auditors and customers. The clue is \"show auditors why the model made a specific decision\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Generative AI</strong>: Generative AI creates new content; it doesn't explain decisions.\n- <strong>C. Unstructured data processing</strong>: This is about handling text, images or audio, not explaining predictions.\n- <strong>D. Multi-cloud networking</strong>: This connects multiple clouds and has nothing to do with model decisions.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> \"Why did the model decide this?\" → Explainable AI.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/responsible-ai\">cloud.google.com/responsible-ai</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/xai-overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/xai-overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://ibm.com/think/topics/explainable-ai\">ibm.com/think/topics/explainable-ai</a></p>","lessons":["d3l10"]},"e5q1":{"id":"e5q1","exam":5,"n":1,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>PhotoNest, an online image storage app, has thousands of uploaded pictures that need to be automatically categorized by objects, places, and general labels. The team wants to use a Google Cloud service with pre-trained machine learning instead of building and training its own model. Which Google Cloud product or service should PhotoNest use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Vision API</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. BigQuery ML</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. AutoML Vision</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Looker</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Vision API</strong></p>\n<p>Vision API is a pre-trained model that labels images out of the box. It detects objects, places, landmarks and general labels with a simple API call. The clue is \"pre-trained machine learning instead of building and training its own model\", which rules out anything that needs your own training data.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. BigQuery ML</strong>: it trains models with SQL on tabular data in BigQuery, not ready-made image labeling.\n- <strong>C. AutoML Vision</strong>: it trains a custom model on your own labeled images, which the team wants to avoid.\n- <strong>D. Looker</strong>: it is a BI and dashboard tool, not an image analysis service.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Common labels, no training = pre-trained API (Vision API). Your own custom categories = AutoML.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/vision/docs\">docs.cloud.google.com/vision/docs</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/vision/docs/detect-labels-image-api\">docs.cloud.google.com/vision/docs/detect-labels-image-api</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/bqml-introduction\">docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/bqml-introduction</a></p>","lessons":["d3l4","d3l5"]},"e5q2":{"id":"e5q2","exam":5,"n":2,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>AgriPulse Insights stores valuable crop-yield, soil-quality, and weather-pattern data in a relational database. The company wants to create a new income source by allowing partner companies to use this data inside their own mobile and web applications. Which cloud-first approach should AgriPulse Insights choose?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Add partner users directly to the relational database.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Sell paid downloads of old exported data files.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Provide access to the data through a paid API.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Move the data into a non-relational database.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Provide access to the data through a paid API.</strong></p>\n<p>An API lets partners pull current data straight into their own apps in a controlled, secure way. Charging for API access turns the data into a new revenue stream. The clues are \"new income source\" and \"inside their own mobile and web applications\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Add partner users directly to the database</strong>: this is a security risk and tightly couples partners to your internal systems.\n- <strong>B. Sell paid downloads of old exported files</strong>: the data is stale and can't be used live inside apps.\n- <strong>D. Move to a non-relational database</strong>: changing the database type does not give partners access or create revenue.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Monetize data for partners' apps = a paid, managed API.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/apigee\">cloud.google.com/apigee</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/apigee/docs/api-platform/monetization/overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/apigee/docs/api-platform/monetization/overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/api-gateway/docs/about-api-gateway\">docs.cloud.google.com/api-gateway/docs/about-api-gateway</a></p>","lessons":["d4l8"]},"e5q3":{"id":"e5q3","exam":5,"n":3,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>FitPerks, a fitness membership app, gives reward points to its customers. The company recently partnered with nearby gyms, sports shops, and health cafes so members can earn and redeem points across all partner locations. Why should FitPerks use application programming interfaces?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. To copy all partner information into its own system for disaster recovery</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. To create charts showing reward program performance</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. To generate personalized offers for members</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. To connect partner systems so loyalty point details stay current</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. To connect partner systems so loyalty point details stay current</strong></p>\n<p>APIs let separate systems talk to each other in real time. FitPerks needs gyms, shops and cafes to read and update the same points balance, so every partner sees the current number when a member earns or redeems. The clue is \"earn and redeem points across all partner locations\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Copy partner data for disaster recovery</strong>: that is backup, not integration, and you don't copy partners' systems.\n- <strong>B. Create performance charts</strong>: that is a reporting/BI task, not why you integrate partners.\n- <strong>C. Generate personalized offers</strong>: that is a machine learning use case, not the core reason for APIs here.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Several businesses sharing live data = connect them with APIs.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/apis/docs/overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/apis/docs/overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-api-management\">cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-api-management</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/apigee/docs/api-platform/get-started/what-apigee\">docs.cloud.google.com/apigee/docs/api-platform/get-started/what-apigee</a></p>","lessons":["d4l8"]},"e5q4":{"id":"e5q4","exam":5,"n":4,"domain":5,"html":"<p><strong>GreenNest Finance, a small digital banking company, experiences a serious customer data leak only eight months after installing a newer security platform. The leadership team wants to reduce the chance of similar incidents over time and improve its overall security approach. What should GreenNest Finance do to support long-term security?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Increase data retention periods to at least eight years.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Spend more money to buy the most expensive security platform available.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Hire cybersecurity experts to improve and expand the data security plan.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Wait until a newer and more advanced security platform becomes available.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Hire cybersecurity experts to improve and expand the data security plan.</strong></p>\n<p>A leak soon after buying a new platform shows that tools alone don't create security. Long-term protection needs skilled people who keep improving policies, processes and controls as threats change. The clue is \"over time\" and \"improve its overall security approach\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Increase data retention</strong>: keeping data longer increases what can leak, it doesn't protect it.\n- <strong>B. Buy the most expensive platform</strong>: price doesn't fix gaps in process and expertise, which just failed.\n- <strong>D. Wait for a newer platform</strong>: waiting leaves the company exposed and again relies only on tools.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Security is ongoing expertise and process, not a one-time product purchase.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/security\">docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/security</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/blueprints/security-foundations\">docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/blueprints/security-foundations</a>\n- <a href=\"https://nist.gov/cyberframework\">nist.gov/cyberframework</a></p>\n<p><strong>⚠️ Note:</strong> \"Hire cybersecurity experts\" is vague. The real lesson is that security needs ongoing expertise and process. It is still clearly the best of the four options, so pick it.</p>","lessons":["d5l1"]},"e5q5":{"id":"e5q5","exam":5,"n":5,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>MedSnap Care, a healthcare mobile app, lets users upload photos of visible symptoms so the app can identify possible condition categories and route the case to the right care team. Which technology is most closely related to this capability?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Cloud Logging</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Cloud Profiler</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. App Engine</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Machine learning</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Machine learning</strong></p>\n<p>Recognizing condition categories in symptom photos is image classification. A machine learning model learns from many labeled example images and then classifies new ones, which lets the app route each case to the right team. On Google Cloud this could be the Vision API, AutoML or a custom model, but the technology behind all of them is ML.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Cloud Logging</strong>: Stores and searches log entries. It doesn't analyze images.\n- <strong>B. Cloud Profiler</strong>: Measures CPU and memory use in application code.\n- <strong>C. App Engine</strong>: Hosts web applications. It can run the app, but it doesn't classify images.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Recognize, classify or predict from images, text or data → machine learning.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-machine-learning\">cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-machine-learning</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/logging/docs/overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/logging/docs/overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/appengine/docs\">docs.cloud.google.com/appengine/docs</a></p>","lessons":["d3l1"]},"e5q6":{"id":"e5q6","exam":5,"n":6,"domain":5,"html":"<p><strong>ClearGate Apps, a multinational SaaS company, is redesigning how employees and partners access its internal systems. The company no longer wants to assume that traffic from an office location or corporate network is automatically trusted. Instead, every identity, endpoint, and application request must be verified, authenticated, and authorized before access is granted, whether the request comes from headquarters, a branch office, or a remote location. Which security model is being described?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Zero trust architecture</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Virtual Private Network (VPN)</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Shared Responsibility Model</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Capital Expenditure (CapEx) model</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Zero trust architecture</strong></p>\n<p>Zero trust means no request is trusted because of where it comes from. Every user, device and request is verified, authenticated and authorized each time, whether it is from the office or from home. The clue is \"no longer wants to assume that traffic from... corporate network is automatically trusted\". Google's version of this is BeyondCorp.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. VPN</strong>: a VPN extends the trusted network, which is the perimeter model zero trust replaces.\n- <strong>C. Shared Responsibility Model</strong>: it splits security duties between provider and customer, not an access model.\n- <strong>D. CapEx model</strong>: it is a spending model, not a security model.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> \"Never trust, always verify\", regardless of location = zero trust.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/beyondcorp\">cloud.google.com/beyondcorp</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/docs/security/zero-trust\">cloud.google.com/docs/security/zero-trust</a>\n- <a href=\"https://nist.gov/publications/zero-trust-architecture\">nist.gov/publications/zero-trust-architecture</a></p>","lessons":["d5l5"]},"e5q7":{"id":"e5q7","exam":5,"n":7,"domain":5,"html":"<p><strong>MetroLedger, a digital banking platform, recently suffered a security incident that temporarily disrupted a few customer-facing services. The leadership team now wants the company to improve its ability to reduce the chance of future attacks, continue running essential services during an incident, limit business impact, and restore normal operations quickly after disruption. Which concept best describes this capability?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Data masking</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Cyber resilience</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Infrastructure as a Service</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Vendor lock-in</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cyber resilience</strong></p>\n<p>Cyber resilience is the ability to prevent attacks where possible, keep essential services running during an incident, limit the damage and recover quickly. The stem lists exactly these parts: reduce the chance, continue running, limit impact, restore operations.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Data masking</strong>: it hides sensitive values in data, a single control, not overall resilience.\n- <strong>C. Infrastructure as a Service</strong>: it is a cloud service model, not a security capability.\n- <strong>D. Vendor lock-in</strong>: it is a risk of depending on one provider, unrelated to recovering from attacks.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Prevent, withstand, recover = cyber resilience.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/security/resources/cyber-resilience\">cloud.google.com/security/resources/cyber-resilience</a>\n- <a href=\"https://csrc.nist.gov/glossary/term/cyber_resiliency\">csrc.nist.gov/glossary/term/cyber_resiliency</a>\n- <a href=\"https://nist.gov/cyberframework\">nist.gov/cyberframework</a></p>","lessons":["d5l1","d5l6"]},"e5q8":{"id":"e5q8","exam":5,"n":8,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>FinSight Ledger, a financial analytics company, has moved several large customer transaction and reporting databases to Google Cloud. The company wants to get more business value from its data, manage data-related costs more effectively, and improve compliance with financial regulations. What should FinSight Ledger do?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Delete data that has been stored for more than 18 months.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Move relational data into modern NoSQL databases.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Generate quarterly reports about data access and usage.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Establish an effective data governance program.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Establish an effective data governance program.</strong></p>\n<p>Data governance sets the rules for how data is owned, classified, accessed, kept and deleted. That one program covers all three goals: more value (trusted, findable data), cost control (lifecycle and retention rules) and compliance (access policies and auditing). The clue is the mix of value, cost and regulations.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Delete data older than 18 months</strong>: a blanket rule may break financial retention laws and destroy useful data.\n- <strong>B. Move to NoSQL</strong>: changing the database type doesn't address value, cost or compliance.\n- <strong>C. Quarterly access reports</strong>: reporting is one small piece of governance, not the whole answer.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Value + cost + compliance for data = data governance.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-data-governance\">cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-data-governance</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/data-governance\">docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/data-governance</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/compliance\">cloud.google.com/compliance</a></p>","lessons":["d2l2"]},"e5q9":{"id":"e5q9","exam":5,"n":9,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>ParkNest, a mobile parking app, stores driver profiles, payment records, parking sensor events, and customer support logs in separate systems. The company wants to move to a unified cloud data solution on Google Cloud. What is the main benefit of this approach?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Data will always be cheaper to store.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Data can be automatically protected from all external threats.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Data will always be backed up and cannot be lost or deleted.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Data can support innovation because it is no longer isolated in silos.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Data can support innovation because it is no longer isolated in silos.</strong></p>\n<p>When data sits in separate systems, teams can't combine it to find insights. A unified data platform brings profiles, payments, sensor events and support logs together, so the company can analyze across them and build new features. The clue is \"separate systems\" moving to a \"unified\" solution.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Always cheaper</strong>: cost depends on usage and design; \"always\" is an overclaim.\n- <strong>B. Protected from all threats</strong>: no platform guarantees protection from every threat.\n- <strong>C. Cannot be lost or deleted</strong>: backups help, but data can still be deleted or lost through mistakes.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Breaking down data silos = new insights and innovation. Watch for \"always\" and \"all\" as red flags.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/data-cloud\">cloud.google.com/data-cloud</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/solutions/data-analytics-and-ai\">cloud.google.com/solutions/data-analytics-and-ai</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/bigquery\">cloud.google.com/bigquery</a></p>","lessons":["d2l1"]},"e5q10":{"id":"e5q10","exam":5,"n":10,"domain":5,"html":"<p><strong>AsterBay Retail, an international ecommerce company, wants its Security Operations team to improve how it detects and investigates advanced attackers. The team needs a Google Cloud security solution that brings together Mandiant frontline incident response knowledge, VirusTotal crowdsourced malware intelligence, Google’s broad visibility, and AI-assisted analysis so analysts can better understand threats and act before attacks cause damage. Which Google Cloud security offering best fits this requirement?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Google Threat Intelligence</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE)</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Cloud Load Balancing</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Identity and Access Management (IAM)</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Google Threat Intelligence</strong></p>\n<p>Google Threat Intelligence combines Mandiant's frontline incident response knowledge, VirusTotal's crowdsourced malware data and Google's own visibility, with Gemini-powered analysis. It helps security teams understand who is attacking and act early. The stem names these exact sources, so the match is direct.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. GKE</strong>: it runs containers, it is not a threat intelligence service.\n- <strong>C. Cloud Load Balancing</strong>: it distributes traffic across backends.\n- <strong>D. IAM</strong>: it controls who can access which resources, not threat research.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Mandiant + VirusTotal + Google visibility = Google Threat Intelligence.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/security/products/threat-intelligence\">cloud.google.com/security/products/threat-intelligence</a>\n- <a href=\"https://gtidocs.virustotal.com/docs/use-cases\">gtidocs.virustotal.com/docs/use-cases</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/chronicle/docs/detection\">docs.cloud.google.com/chronicle/docs/detection</a></p>","lessons":["d5l7"]},"e5q11":{"id":"e5q11","exam":5,"n":11,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>PixelPeak Media, a digital content company, is comparing cloud providers for its next innovation program. The team wants access to ready-to-use AI agents, advanced Google first-party models, and a unified AI development platform for building and managing AI solutions. Under which category of Google Cloud’s top differentiators do these capabilities belong?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Global network infrastructure</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. World-leading AI</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. CapEx reduction</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Open standards</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. World-leading AI</strong></p>\n<p>Ready-to-use AI agents, Google's first-party models (such as Gemini) and a unified AI development platform all describe Google Cloud's AI leadership. These are AI capabilities, so they fall under the AI differentiator.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Global network infrastructure</strong>: it is about Google's private fiber network and global reach, not AI models.\n- <strong>C. CapEx reduction</strong>: it is a financial benefit of cloud in general, not a Google differentiator category.\n- <strong>D. Open standards</strong>: it is about avoiding lock-in and interoperability, not AI products.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Agents, Gemini models, AI platform = the AI differentiator.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise\">cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/products/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform\">cloud.google.com/products/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/overview</a></p>","lessons":["d3l8","d3l9"]},"e5q12":{"id":"e5q12","exam":5,"n":12,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>FitSprint, a fitness tracking web app, has packaged its stateless frontend service into a container. The team wants to deploy it on Google Cloud so it can scale down to zero when there is no traffic and quickly scale up during busy periods, without requiring the team to create or manage Kubernetes clusters or servers. Which service should FitSprint use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Compute Engine</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Cloud Run</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Bare Metal Solution</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. AlloyDB Omni</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cloud Run</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud Run runs stateless containers without any servers or clusters to manage. It scales automatically with traffic, including down to zero when idle, so you pay nothing while there are no requests. The clues are \"container\", \"scale down to zero\" and \"without... Kubernetes clusters or servers\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Compute Engine</strong>: these are VMs you manage yourself, and they don't scale to zero.\n- <strong>C. Bare Metal Solution</strong>: dedicated physical servers for special workloads such as Oracle.\n- <strong>D. AlloyDB Omni</strong>: a downloadable PostgreSQL-compatible database, not a container hosting service.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Container + serverless + scale to zero = Cloud Run.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/run/docs/overview/what-is-cloud-run\">docs.cloud.google.com/run/docs/overview/what-is-cloud-run</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/run/docs/about-instance-autoscaling\">docs.cloud.google.com/run/docs/about-instance-autoscaling</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/compute/docs/instances\">docs.cloud.google.com/compute/docs/instances</a></p>","lessons":["d4l4","d4l6"]},"e5q13":{"id":"e5q13","exam":5,"n":13,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>GreenCart Finance, a small payment processing company, must leave its private server room because the facility contract ends soon. The IT team wants to move its existing virtual machines to Compute Engine with the same operating system images, application settings, and configurations, making only the minimum changes needed to run in Google Cloud. Which migration strategy is this?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Rehost (Lift and shift)</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Refactor</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Reimagine</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Replatform</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Rehost (Lift and shift)</strong></p>\n<p>Rehosting moves existing VMs to the cloud as they are, with the same OS, settings and configuration. It is the fastest path, which fits a hard deadline like an expiring facility contract. The clue is \"minimum changes needed\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. Refactor</strong>: it changes the application code to use cloud features, which takes more time.\n- <strong>C. Reimagine</strong>: it rethinks or rebuilds the application from scratch, the most effort.\n- <strong>D. Replatform</strong>: it makes some optimizations, such as moving to a managed service, so more than minimal changes.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Same VMs, no changes, quick exit = lift and shift.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/migration-to-gcp-getting-started\">docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/migration-to-gcp-getting-started</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/migration-center/docs/migration-planning-overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/migration-center/docs/migration-planning-overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/application-development/application-rationalization-what-why-and-how\">cloud.google.com/blog/products/application-development/application-rationalization-what-why-and-how</a></p>","lessons":["d4l5"]},"e5q14":{"id":"e5q14","exam":5,"n":14,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>ShopSprint has migrated customer orders, payment records, and stock-level details to a cloud-based relational database. What capability does a relational database provide?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. It keeps massive volumes of raw data in the same format in which it was collected</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. It stores transactional data that applications can access electronically</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. It performs fast analysis of very large and multi-dimensional datasets</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. It processes unstructured data and makes it available across several geographic locations</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. It stores transactional data that applications can access electronically</strong></p>\n<p>A relational database stores structured data in tables with defined relationships. It is built for transactions such as orders, payments and stock updates, which applications read and write constantly. The clue is the list of operational records: orders, payments, stock levels.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Keeps raw data in its original format</strong>: that describes a data lake.\n- <strong>C. Fast analysis of huge datasets</strong>: that describes a data warehouse such as BigQuery.\n- <strong>D. Processes unstructured data across locations</strong>: relational databases are for structured data; this fits object storage better.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Database = transactions. Data warehouse = analysis. Data lake = raw data.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/products/databases\">cloud.google.com/products/databases</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/sql/docs/introduction\">cloud.google.com/sql/docs/introduction</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/what-are-transactional-databases\">cloud.google.com/learn/what-are-transactional-databases</a></p>","lessons":["d2l5"]},"e5q15":{"id":"e5q15","exam":5,"n":15,"domain":6,"html":"<p><strong>NimbusRide runs several customer-facing applications on Google Cloud. The operations team wants to collect application metrics and resource metadata, then view that information in dashboards to understand service performance. Which Google Cloud tool should they use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Cloud Monitoring</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Cloud Trace</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Cloud Logging</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Cloud Debugger</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cloud Monitoring</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud Monitoring collects metrics, events and metadata from applications and Google Cloud resources. It shows them in dashboards and can send alerts. The clue is \"metrics\" and \"dashboards\" to understand performance.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. Cloud Trace</strong>: it follows single requests across services to find latency, not general metrics dashboards.\n- <strong>C. Cloud Logging</strong>: it collects and stores log entries, not metrics.\n- <strong>D. Cloud Debugger</strong>: it inspected running code state to find bugs (and has been retired).</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Metrics + dashboards + alerts = Cloud Monitoring. Logs = Cloud Logging. Latency of a request = Cloud Trace.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/monitoring/docs\">docs.cloud.google.com/monitoring/docs</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/monitoring/dashboards\">docs.cloud.google.com/monitoring/dashboards</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/trace/docs\">docs.cloud.google.com/trace/docs</a></p>","lessons":["d6l8"],"same":["e1q43"]},"e5q16":{"id":"e5q16","exam":5,"n":16,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>TrailCart, a small grocery delivery app, wants its developers and analysts to quickly explore reusable machine learning resources on Google Cloud without building everything from scratch. How does Google Cloud AI Hub help Google Cloud customers get started with machine learning?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. It serves artificial intelligence models for real-time predictions</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. It automatically writes complete machine learning model code for users</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. It automatically collects, cleans, and prepares users’ machine learning datasets</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. It provides a repository of ready-to-use artificial intelligence and machine learning resources</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. It provides a repository of ready-to-use artificial intelligence and machine learning resources</strong></p>\n<p>AI Hub was a catalog of reusable AI and ML assets, such as models, notebooks and pipelines. Teams could find something close to what they needed and start from it. The clue is \"explore reusable machine learning resources... without building everything from scratch\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Serves models for real-time predictions</strong>: that is model serving on the AI platform, not a catalog.\n- <strong>B. Writes complete model code</strong>: no catalog writes your whole model for you.\n- <strong>C. Collects, cleans and prepares datasets</strong>: data preparation is a separate step and tool set.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Hub = repository of reusable resources.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/ai-machine-learning/introducing-ai-hub-and-kubeflow-pipelines-making-ai-simpler-faster-and-more-useful-for-businesses\">cloud.google.com/blog/products/ai-machine-learning/introducing-ai-hub-and-kubeflow-pipelines-making-ai-simpler-faster-and-more-useful-for-businesses</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/model-garden\">cloud.google.com/model-garden</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/products/ai\">cloud.google.com/products/ai</a></p>\n<p><strong>⚠️ Note:</strong> AI Hub is an older product. Its role is now mostly filled by Model Garden in the AI platform. The definition is still correct, so pick the key.</p>","lessons":["d3l6"]},"e5q17":{"id":"e5q17","exam":5,"n":17,"domain":5,"html":"<p><strong>CarePulse Clinics, a healthcare services company, finds that an attacker has locked access to its appointment records and patient history database by encrypting the files. The attacker sends a message demanding payment in cryptocurrency before providing the decryption key needed to restore the data. What type of cybersecurity threat does this describe?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Phishing</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Ransomware</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Cryptomining</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Hardware failure</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Ransomware</strong></p>\n<p>Ransomware encrypts a victim's data and demands payment, often in cryptocurrency, for the decryption key. The clues are \"encrypting the files\" and \"demanding payment\" to restore access.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Phishing</strong>: it tricks people into giving away credentials or clicking malicious links. It may deliver ransomware, but it isn't the lockout itself.\n- <strong>C. Cryptomining</strong>: attackers secretly use your computing power to mine cryptocurrency; they don't lock your data.\n- <strong>D. Hardware failure</strong>: it is an accident, not an attack with a ransom demand.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Data locked + payment demanded = ransomware. Mention of crypto doesn't mean cryptomining.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cisa.gov/stopransomware/ransomware-101\">cisa.gov/stopransomware/ransomware-101</a>\n- <a href=\"https://nist.gov/itl/smallbusinesscyber/guidance-topic/ransomware\">nist.gov/itl/smallbusinesscyber/guidance-topic/ransomware</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/security/mitigating-ransomware-attacks\">docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/security/mitigating-ransomware-attacks</a></p>","lessons":["d5l2"]},"e5q18":{"id":"e5q18","exam":5,"n":18,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>BrightCart Market, an online retail company, is moving its inventory, payments, and customer engagement applications to the cloud. The leadership team is worried about becoming dependent on a single provider and wants future flexibility to run applications across public cloud, private cloud, and on-premises environments when needed. Which principle should best guide the company’s cloud modernization strategy?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Adopting proprietary, vendor-specific databases</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Keeping all mission-critical workloads in a single private cloud</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Committing to openness and interoperability</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Relying exclusively on Software as a Service (SaaS) products</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Committing to openness and interoperability</strong></p>\n<p>Open standards, open source and interoperable tools let workloads move between public cloud, private cloud and on-premises. That avoids depending on one provider. The clues are \"dependent on a single provider\" and \"flexibility to run applications across\" environments.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Proprietary, vendor-specific databases</strong>: these increase lock-in, the opposite of the goal.\n- <strong>B. Everything in a single private cloud</strong>: this removes flexibility instead of adding it.\n- <strong>D. Only SaaS products</strong>: SaaS can tie you to each vendor and doesn't give control over where apps run.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Fear of lock-in + hybrid/multicloud flexibility = openness and interoperability.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-multicloud\">cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-multicloud</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/hybrid-multicloud-patterns/drivers\">docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/hybrid-multicloud-patterns/drivers</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/blog/topics/inside-google-cloud/empowering-customers-and-the-ecosystem-with-an-open-cloud\">cloud.google.com/blog/topics/inside-google-cloud/empowering-customers-and-the-ecosystem-with-an-open-cloud</a></p>","lessons":["d4l7"]},"e5q19":{"id":"e5q19","exam":5,"n":19,"domain":5,"html":"<p><strong>BrightCart, a retail mobile app company, stores customer purchase analytics in Google Cloud. The operations manager wants to understand who should decide which employees can access different company datasets and reports. Who is responsible for defining data access policies?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Cloud Identity</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Google Cloud Customer Care team</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. BrightCart’s IT team</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. BrightCart’s app users</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. BrightCart’s IT team</strong></p>\n<p>Under the shared responsibility model, the customer owns its data and decides who can access it. Google secures the underlying infrastructure, but the customer's IT team defines access policies and grants roles through IAM. The clue is \"who should decide which employees can access\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Cloud Identity</strong>: it is a tool for managing users and groups, not the party who sets policy.\n- <strong>B. Google Cloud Customer Care</strong>: support helps with issues but doesn't decide your access rules.\n- <strong>D. App users</strong>: end customers don't control internal employee access.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Google secures the cloud; the customer secures data and access in the cloud.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/iam/docs/overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/iam/docs/overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/identity/docs/overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/identity/docs/overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/resource-manager/docs/cloud-platform-resource-hierarchy\">docs.cloud.google.com/resource-manager/docs/cloud-platform-resource-hierarchy</a></p>","lessons":["d5l3"]},"e5q20":{"id":"e5q20","exam":5,"n":20,"domain":6,"html":"<p><strong>CityPulse Hosting provides a public cloud service used by several small businesses. Its service performance has dropped below the internal service level objective (SLO), but it is still above the contractual service level agreement (SLA). What is the likely result of this service performance change?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. CityPulse Hosting is encouraged to release new feature updates more quickly</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. CityPulse Hosting is encouraged to focus more on service reliability</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Customers experience unexpected or extended service outages</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Customers experience lower service latency</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. CityPulse Hosting is encouraged to focus more on service reliability</strong></p>\n<p>The SLO is the internal reliability target. Falling below it usually means the error budget (100% − SLO) is used up. The SRE response is to slow down risky changes and spend effort on reliability until performance is back on target. Being above the SLA means no contract penalty yet.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Release features faster</strong>: that is what you do when error budget remains, not when the SLO is missed.\n- <strong>C. Unexpected or extended outages</strong>: performance is still above the SLA, so customers aren't necessarily seeing major outages.\n- <strong>D. Lower latency</strong>: performance dropped, so latency would not improve.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> SLO missed = focus on reliability. Budget left = innovate.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://sre.google/sre-book/service-level-objectives\">sre.google/sre-book/service-level-objectives</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/devops-sre/sre-fundamentals-sli-vs-slo-vs-sla\">cloud.google.com/blog/products/devops-sre/sre-fundamentals-sli-vs-slo-vs-sla</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/reliability\">cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/reliability</a></p>","lessons":["d6l4","d6l5"]},"e5q21":{"id":"e5q21","exam":5,"n":21,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>BrightWave Media wants to analyze campaign clicks, app installs, website visits, and social media engagement data together so it can predict future customer acquisition. How should the company use cloud technology to gain new insights from this data?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Import the datasets into a custom data warehouse, and then archive older data</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Import and selectively archive the datasets in a custom data lake</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Keep the datasets separate and make predictions using machine learning</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Combine the datasets and make predictions using machine learning</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Combine the datasets and make predictions using machine learning</strong></p>\n<p>Predictions get better when the model can see how clicks, installs, visits and social engagement relate to each other. Combining the data in one place, then training ML on it, reveals these patterns. The clues are \"analyze... together\" and \"predict\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Custom warehouse, then archive</strong>: archiving older data removes history the model needs, and there is no prediction step.\n- <strong>B. Selectively archive in a data lake</strong>: storing and archiving is not analysis or prediction.\n- <strong>C. Keep the datasets separate</strong>: silos hide the cross-channel patterns the company is looking for.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> New insights = combine the data, then apply ML.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/bigqueryml-intro\">cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/bigqueryml-intro</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-a-data-warehouse\">cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-a-data-warehouse</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-a-data-lake\">cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-a-data-lake</a></p>","lessons":["d3l1","d3l2"]},"e5q22":{"id":"e5q22","exam":5,"n":22,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>FitPulse, a new wellness mobile app, collects a massive volume of user data such as workout duration, step patterns, heart-rate activity, and estimated calories burned. The company wants to keep this data for future exploration and analytics, even before deciding exactly how it will be processed. Which data management solution should FitPulse use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Data lake</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Data warehouse</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Data field</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Database</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Data lake</strong></p>\n<p>A data lake stores large volumes of raw data in its original format, structured or not, at low cost. You decide later how to process and analyze it. The clue is \"keep this data... even before deciding exactly how it will be processed\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. Data warehouse</strong>: it stores cleaned, structured data in a defined schema for analysis, so you must decide the structure first.\n- <strong>C. Data field</strong>: a single attribute in a record, not a storage solution.\n- <strong>D. Database</strong>: built for day-to-day transactions, not for storing huge raw datasets for later exploration.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Raw now, decide later = data lake. Structured, ready for analysis = data warehouse.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/discover/what-is-a-data-lake\">cloud.google.com/discover/what-is-a-data-lake</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-a-data-warehouse\">cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-a-data-warehouse</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/introduction\">cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/introduction</a></p>","lessons":["d2l5"]},"e5q23":{"id":"e5q23","exam":5,"n":23,"domain":5,"html":"<p><strong>PixelNest Media stores application files and customer activity records in Google Cloud and also sends data between services for processing. The security team wants to know when Google Cloud automatically encrypts customer data by default. When is data automatically encrypted in Google Cloud?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. When it is in transit only.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. When it is at rest and in transit.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Data is not automatically encrypted by default.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. When it is at rest only.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. When it is at rest and in transit.</strong></p>\n<p>Google Cloud encrypts all customer data at rest by default, with no action needed. Data moving between Google's facilities and services is also encrypted in transit. The stem mentions both stored files and data \"sent between services\", which points to both states.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. In transit only</strong>: stored data is also encrypted by default.\n- <strong>C. Not encrypted by default</strong>: default encryption is a core Google Cloud security feature.\n- <strong>D. At rest only</strong>: data in transit is encrypted as well.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Google Cloud default = encrypted at rest and in transit.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/security/encryption\">cloud.google.com/security/encryption</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/docs/security/encryption/default-encryption\">cloud.google.com/docs/security/encryption/default-encryption</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/docs/security/encryption-in-transit\">cloud.google.com/docs/security/encryption-in-transit</a></p>","lessons":["d5l6"]},"e5q24":{"id":"e5q24","exam":5,"n":24,"domain":5,"html":"<p><strong>TrailBee Travel uses Google Cloud to host its booking platform. Before a user can open the internal support dashboard, the platform must confirm that the person signing in is really the claimed user. Which aspect of cloud identity management does this action describe?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Authorization</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Authentication</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Auditing</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Encrypting</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Authentication</strong></p>\n<p>Authentication confirms that users are who they claim to be, for example with a password plus 2-step verification. The clue is \"confirm that the person signing in is really the claimed user\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Authorization</strong>: it decides what an authenticated user is allowed to do, which comes after identity is confirmed.\n- <strong>C. Auditing</strong>: it records who did what, for later review.\n- <strong>D. Encrypting</strong>: it protects data from being read, it doesn't verify identity.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Authentication = who are you? Authorization = what can you do?</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/docs/authentication\">cloud.google.com/docs/authentication</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/iam/docs/overview\">cloud.google.com/iam/docs/overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/identity/docs/overview\">cloud.google.com/identity/docs/overview</a></p>","lessons":["d5l4"]},"e5q25":{"id":"e5q25","exam":5,"n":25,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>NimbusPay Systems runs containerized payment applications in Kubernetes clusters across Google Cloud, another public cloud, and an on-premises environment. The platform team wants one consistent and centralized way to manage these Kubernetes environments. Which Google Cloud service should NimbusPay Systems choose?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Cloud Run functions</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. GKE Enterprise</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Cloud Run</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Compute Engine</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. GKE Enterprise</strong></p>\n<p>GKE Enterprise (formerly Anthos) gives one consistent way to manage Kubernetes clusters across Google Cloud, other public clouds and on-premises. It centralizes policy, configuration and operations for all of them. The clues are \"Kubernetes clusters across... another public cloud, and an on-premises environment\" and \"centralized\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Cloud Run functions</strong>: it runs single event-driven functions, not multi-environment cluster management.\n- <strong>C. Cloud Run</strong>: it runs serverless containers on Google Cloud, not fleets of clusters across environments.\n- <strong>D. Compute Engine</strong>: these are VMs, not a Kubernetes management layer.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Kubernetes everywhere (hybrid/multicloud), managed from one place = GKE Enterprise.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/fleets-overview\">cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/fleets-overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/multi-cloud/docs\">cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/multi-cloud/docs</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/enterprise/clusters/docs\">cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/enterprise/clusters/docs</a></p>","lessons":["d4l3","d4l7"],"same":["e2q20"]},"e5q26":{"id":"e5q26","exam":5,"n":26,"domain":6,"html":"<p><strong>CodeNest Retail, an ecommerce company, runs a production application on Google Cloud. The development team wants to inspect how the application code behaves while it is running and analyze the application’s state to find difficult programming bugs. Which Google Cloud tool should CodeNest Retail use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Debugger</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Profiler</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Cloud Monitoring</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Trace</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Debugger</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud Debugger let developers inspect the state of running code, such as variable values at a line, without stopping the application. That helps find hard bugs in production. The clue is \"inspect how the application code behaves while it is running and analyze the application's state\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. Profiler</strong>: it shows which code uses the most CPU and memory, for performance, not bug state.\n- <strong>C. Cloud Monitoring</strong>: it tracks metrics and dashboards, not code state.\n- <strong>D. Trace</strong>: it measures request latency across services.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Inspect running code state = Debugger. Resource use by code = Profiler. Latency = Trace.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://support.google.com/cloud/answer/6323288\">support.google.com/cloud/answer/6323288</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/profiler/docs/about-profiler\">docs.cloud.google.com/profiler/docs/about-profiler</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/trace/docs/overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/trace/docs/overview</a></p>\n<p><strong>⚠️ Note:</strong> Cloud Debugger was shut down in 2023; its open-source successor is Snapshot Debugger. The key describes what it did correctly and the other tools don't fit, so pick it on this exam.</p>","lessons":["d6l8"]},"e5q27":{"id":"e5q27","exam":5,"n":27,"domain":6,"html":"<p><strong>FitTrack Labs runs several wellness applications on Google Cloud. The operations team wants to collect, view, and store all logs created by these applications and Google Cloud resources in one centralized service. Which Google Cloud service should FitTrack Labs use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Cloud Profiler</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Cloud Monitoring</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Cloud Trace</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Cloud Logging</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Cloud Logging</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud Logging collects, stores, searches and analyzes logs from applications and Google Cloud resources in one central place. The clue is \"collect, view, and store all logs\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Cloud Profiler</strong>: it analyzes CPU and memory use by code.\n- <strong>B. Cloud Monitoring</strong>: it handles metrics, dashboards and alerts, not log storage.\n- <strong>C. Cloud Trace</strong>: it measures request latency across services.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Logs = Cloud Logging. Metrics = Cloud Monitoring.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/logging/docs\">cloud.google.com/logging/docs</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/monitoring/docs\">cloud.google.com/monitoring/docs</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/trace/docs\">cloud.google.com/trace/docs</a></p>","lessons":["d6l8"]},"e5q28":{"id":"e5q28","exam":5,"n":28,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>ShopNest has already migrated a few small applications to Google Cloud to reduce short-term costs. The leadership team now wants cloud adoption to become a company-wide transformation that supports innovation and faster delivery. How should the organization adapt its leadership approach?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Increase visible top-down sponsorship and foster a culture of blamelessness</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Move cloud funding from an operational expenditure model to capital expenditure</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Make cloud adoption primarily driven by individual contributors</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Invest in on-premises infrastructure to redefine relationships between IT and employees</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Increase visible top-down sponsorship and foster a culture of blamelessness</strong></p>\n<p>Moving from a few cost-saving migrations to a real transformation needs strong executive support and a culture where people can experiment. A blameless culture lets teams learn from failures instead of hiding them, which drives innovation and faster delivery. The clue is \"company-wide transformation\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. Move funding from OpEx to CapEx</strong>: cloud moves spending the other way, toward OpEx.\n- <strong>C. Driven mainly by individual contributors</strong>: this is early, tactical adoption in Google's Cloud Adoption Framework; bottom-up effort alone can't change a whole company.\n- <strong>D. Invest in on-premises infrastructure</strong>: this goes against cloud adoption.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Transformation = executive sponsorship + blameless, learning culture.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/adoption-framework\">cloud.google.com/adoption-framework</a>\n- <a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/google_cloud_adoption_framework_whitepaper.pdf\">services.google.com/fh/files/misc/google_cloud_adoption_framework_whitepaper.pdf</a>\n- <a href=\"https://sre.google/sre-book/postmortem-culture/\">sre.google/sre-book/postmortem-culture/</a></p>","lessons":["d1l3","d1l7"]},"e5q29":{"id":"e5q29","exam":5,"n":29,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>ShopTrail, an online fashion marketplace, wants to use customer browsing history, previous purchases, and product ratings to improve the shopping experience. Which scenario is a good use case for machine learning?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Grouping data when there are no useful past examples or patterns to learn from</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Completing tasks that mainly depend on human judgment, life experience, and intuition</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Creating personalized product recommendations for customers</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Making final decisions about complex ethical dilemmas</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Creating personalized product recommendations for customers</strong></p>\n<p>ML learns patterns from lots of past data, such as browsing, purchases and ratings, and uses them to predict what each customer will like. Recommendations are a classic ML use case. The clue is the rich historical customer data.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. No useful past examples or patterns</strong>: ML needs data with patterns to learn from.\n- <strong>B. Tasks based on human judgment and intuition</strong>: these depend on experience ML doesn't have.\n- <strong>D. Final decisions on ethical dilemmas</strong>: these need human accountability, not a model.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Lots of data + patterns + prediction at scale = good ML fit.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-machine-learning\">cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-machine-learning</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/use-cases/recommendations\">cloud.google.com/use-cases/recommendations</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/recommendation-overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/recommendation-overview</a></p>","lessons":["d3l1","d3l10"]},"e5q30":{"id":"e5q30","exam":5,"n":30,"domain":5,"html":"<p><strong>ClearPath Analytics stores customer reports and transaction files in Google Cloud. The security team asks why stored data in Google Cloud is protected without every application team manually enabling encryption for each service. What is the main reason?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Data is encrypted by Security Command Center</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Data is encrypted by Sensitive Data Protection</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Data is encrypted by default</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Data is encrypted when an appropriate tag is applied</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Data is encrypted by default</strong></p>\n<p>Google Cloud automatically encrypts all customer data at rest, with no setup by application teams. That is why stored data is protected without anyone turning encryption on per service. The clue is \"without every application team manually enabling encryption\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Security Command Center</strong>: it finds misconfigurations and threats, it doesn't encrypt data.\n- <strong>B. Sensitive Data Protection</strong>: it discovers, classifies and masks sensitive data, it isn't the default encryption layer.\n- <strong>D. When a tag is applied</strong>: encryption doesn't depend on tags; it is always on.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Google Cloud encryption at rest = automatic, on by default.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/docs/security/encryption/default-encryption\">docs.cloud.google.com/docs/security/encryption/default-encryption</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/security-command-center/docs/security-command-center-overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/security-command-center/docs/security-command-center-overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/sensitive-data-protection/docs/sensitive-data-protection-overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/sensitive-data-protection/docs/sensitive-data-protection-overview</a></p>","lessons":["d5l6"]},"e5q31":{"id":"e5q31","exam":5,"n":31,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>MediScan Analytics is building image-recognition models to help hospitals detect patterns in medical scan images. The team wants to reduce the time required to train these machine learning models on Google Cloud. Which domain-specific hardware is designed for this purpose?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Bare Metal Solution</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Spot VMs</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Cloud TPUs</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Containers</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cloud TPUs</strong></p>\n<p>Tensor Processing Units are chips Google designed specifically to speed up machine learning training and inference. The clues are \"reduce the time required to train\" and \"domain-specific hardware\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Bare Metal Solution</strong>: dedicated physical servers for special workloads, not ML-specific chips.\n- <strong>B. Spot VMs</strong>: lower-cost VMs that can be stopped at any time. They are a pricing option, not special hardware.\n- <strong>D. Containers</strong>: a way to package software, not hardware.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Hardware built for ML = TPU.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/tpu/docs/intro-to-tpu\">docs.cloud.google.com/tpu/docs/intro-to-tpu</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/tpu\">cloud.google.com/tpu</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/compute/docs/instances/spot\">docs.cloud.google.com/compute/docs/instances/spot</a></p>","lessons":["d3l9"]},"e5q32":{"id":"e5q32","exam":5,"n":32,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>HelpNest, a customer service web platform, wants to introduce a system that can read support tickets, decide the next best action, trigger approved workflows such as refunds or account updates, and close routine issues without a human agent. Which technology best describes this capability?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Infrastructure as a Service (IaaS)</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Legacy on-premises computing</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Agentic AI</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Open source software</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Agentic AI</strong></p>\n<p>Agentic AI systems don't just answer questions. They understand a goal, decide on next steps and take actions through tools and workflows. Here the system reads tickets, picks the next action, triggers refunds or updates, and closes issues on its own. The clue is \"decide the next best action\" and \"trigger approved workflows\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. IaaS</strong>: it is a cloud service model for renting compute, storage and networking.\n- <strong>B. Legacy on-premises computing</strong>: it describes where systems run, not an AI capability.\n- <strong>D. Open source software</strong>: it describes how software is licensed, not what it does.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> AI that plans and takes actions = agentic AI.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/discover/what-is-agentic-ai\">cloud.google.com/discover/what-is-agentic-ai</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/discover/what-are-ai-agents\">cloud.google.com/discover/what-are-ai-agents</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-iaas\">cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-iaas</a></p>","lessons":["d3l8"]},"e5q33":{"id":"e5q33","exam":5,"n":33,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>UrbanTrail Logistics, a delivery management company, has begun building cloud-native applications to replace older warehouse and routing systems. What additional step should UrbanTrail Logistics take as part of adopting the cloud?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Give third-party partners privileged access to the cloud network.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Move selected legacy infrastructure workloads to the cloud.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Continue performing maintenance on old on-premises hardware.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Increase capital spending on existing on-premises projects.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Move selected legacy infrastructure workloads to the cloud.</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud adoption usually has two tracks: build new cloud-native apps and migrate existing workloads. Moving suitable legacy workloads lets the company retire old hardware and get cloud benefits for those systems too. The clue is \"additional step\" beyond building new apps.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Give partners privileged access</strong>: this weakens security and isn't an adoption step.\n- <strong>C. Keep maintaining old hardware</strong>: this keeps the cost and burden that cloud adoption aims to cut.\n- <strong>D. Increase on-premises capital spending</strong>: this moves away from the cloud and its OpEx model.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Cloud adoption = build new cloud-native apps and migrate existing workloads.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/cloud-migration\">cloud.google.com/learn/cloud-migration</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/migration-to-gcp-getting-started\">docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/migration-to-gcp-getting-started</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/adoption-framework\">cloud.google.com/adoption-framework</a></p>","lessons":["d4l5"]},"e5q34":{"id":"e5q34","exam":5,"n":34,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>ModelForge Health, a medical research analytics company, wants to train very large machine learning models for drug discovery. The team needs Google Cloud infrastructure that is purpose-built for AI workloads and combines high-performance hardware, open software, ML frameworks, and flexible ways to consume compute capacity. Which Google Cloud differentiator should the company use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Domain Name Service (DNS)</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. AI Hypercomputer</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Software as a Service (SaaS)</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Edge locations</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. AI Hypercomputer</strong></p>\n<p>AI Hypercomputer is Google Cloud's integrated supercomputing architecture for AI. It combines performance-optimized hardware (TPUs and GPUs), open software and ML frameworks, and flexible consumption models. The stem describes exactly these parts for training very large models.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. DNS</strong>: it translates domain names into IP addresses.\n- <strong>C. SaaS</strong>: it delivers finished applications, not AI training infrastructure.\n- <strong>D. Edge locations</strong>: they cache and deliver content close to users, not train large models.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Hardware + open software + flexible consumption for AI = AI Hypercomputer.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/ai-hypercomputer/docs/overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/ai-hypercomputer/docs/overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/ai-infrastructure\">cloud.google.com/ai-infrastructure</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/ai-hypercomputer/docs/consumption-models\">docs.cloud.google.com/ai-hypercomputer/docs/consumption-models</a></p>","lessons":["d3l9"],"same":["e4q48"]},"e5q35":{"id":"e5q35","exam":5,"n":35,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>GreenCart Labs is building several customer-facing applications for order tracking, product search, and user notifications. Its developers want to use their preferred programming languages and tools, but the company also wants to reduce the amount of time spent managing servers and infrastructure. Which Google Cloud solution should GreenCart Labs use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Cloud Run</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Managed Service for Apache Spark</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Pub/Sub</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Bare Metal Solution</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Cloud Run</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud Run runs containers in a fully managed, serverless environment. Because anything that can go in a container works, developers can use any language, library or tool they prefer. Google handles servers and scaling. The clues are \"preferred programming languages and tools\" and \"reduce... managing servers\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. Managed Service for Apache Spark</strong>: (Dataproc) it runs Spark and Hadoop data processing jobs, not customer-facing apps.\n- <strong>C. Pub/Sub</strong>: it is a messaging service between systems, not an app hosting platform.\n- <strong>D. Bare Metal Solution</strong>: dedicated physical servers that you manage, the opposite of the goal.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Any language + no servers to manage = Cloud Run.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/run/docs/overview/what-is-cloud-run\">cloud.google.com/run/docs/overview/what-is-cloud-run</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/products/managed-service-for-apache-spark\">cloud.google.com/products/managed-service-for-apache-spark</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/pubsub/docs/overview\">cloud.google.com/pubsub/docs/overview</a></p>","lessons":["d4l4"]},"e5q36":{"id":"e5q36","exam":5,"n":36,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>BrightCart, an online retail platform, already uses cloud infrastructure to scale its website during seasonal traffic spikes. The leadership team now wants a technology that can independently plan, reason, and carry out multi-step business tasks such as researching customer segments, preparing sales outreach, and coordinating follow-up actions with minimal human direction. What is this concept called?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Agentic AI</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Virtual Machines</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Hybrid cloud</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Domain Name Service (DNS)</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Agentic AI</strong></p>\n<p>Agentic AI can plan, reason and complete multi-step tasks toward a goal with little human direction. Researching segments, drafting outreach and coordinating follow-ups is a chain of actions an AI agent can carry out. The clue is \"independently plan, reason, and carry out multi-step business tasks\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. Virtual Machines</strong>: they are compute resources, not decision-making systems.\n- <strong>C. Hybrid cloud</strong>: it combines on-premises and public cloud, an infrastructure model.\n- <strong>D. DNS</strong>: it resolves domain names to IP addresses.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Plan + reason + act on multi-step tasks = agentic AI.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/discover/what-is-agentic-ai\">cloud.google.com/discover/what-is-agentic-ai</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/discover/what-are-ai-agents\">cloud.google.com/discover/what-are-ai-agents</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/products/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform\">cloud.google.com/products/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform</a></p>","lessons":["d3l8"]},"e5q37":{"id":"e5q37","exam":5,"n":37,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>BlueWave Analytics, a multinational reporting platform, uses Google Cloud along with other cloud providers to manage data across different environments. The company wants its data platforms to work together smoothly, exchange data easily, and avoid vendor lock-in or restrictive technology boundaries. Which Google Cloud differentiator best supports this requirement?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. AI Hypercomputer</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Deep commitment to openness and interoperability</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Shift from OpEx to CapEx</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Bare Metal Solution</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Deep commitment to openness and interoperability</strong></p>\n<p>Google Cloud supports open source, open standards and multicloud tools so data platforms can work across providers and data can move easily. That reduces vendor lock-in. The clues are \"other cloud providers\", \"exchange data easily\" and \"avoid vendor lock-in\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. AI Hypercomputer</strong>: it is AI training and serving infrastructure, not about cross-cloud data exchange.\n- <strong>C. Shift from OpEx to CapEx</strong>: cloud moves spending toward OpEx, and this isn't about interoperability.\n- <strong>D. Bare Metal Solution</strong>: dedicated servers for special workloads, unrelated to openness.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Avoid lock-in, work across clouds = openness and interoperability.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/data-cloud\">cloud.google.com/data-cloud</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/security/compliance/swipo-codes\">cloud.google.com/security/compliance/swipo-codes</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/bare-metal/docs/bms-overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/bare-metal/docs/bms-overview</a></p>","lessons":["d2l2"]},"e5q38":{"id":"e5q38","exam":5,"n":38,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>GlobeTrek Media, a travel content website, wants hotel chains and tour partners to display its latest destination posts, images, and campaign updates inside their own apps and websites. What should GlobeTrek Media use to share this content smoothly with partner systems?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. A NoSQL database</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Config Sync</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. The App Engine standard environment</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. An application programming interface</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. An application programming interface</strong></p>\n<p>An API lets partners' apps and websites request GlobeTrek's latest posts, images and updates automatically. The content stays current without manual copying. The clue is \"display... inside their own apps and websites\" and \"partner systems\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. A NoSQL database</strong>: it stores data, but doesn't by itself give partners a safe way to access it.\n- <strong>B. Config Sync</strong>: it keeps Kubernetes cluster configurations consistent, unrelated to sharing content.\n- <strong>C. App Engine standard environment</strong>: it hosts web apps, but isn't the mechanism for partner integration.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Share data or content with other companies' systems = API.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/api-gateway/docs/glossary\">docs.cloud.google.com/api-gateway/docs/glossary</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/api-gateway/docs/about-api-gateway\">docs.cloud.google.com/api-gateway/docs/about-api-gateway</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/appengine/docs/standard/overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/appengine/docs/standard/overview</a></p>","lessons":["d4l7","d4l8"]},"e5q39":{"id":"e5q39","exam":5,"n":39,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>StreamForge, a video creation app, is launching a generative AI feature that will train and serve large models at scale. Its engineering team needs an integrated Google Cloud architecture that combines purpose-built accelerators such as GPUs and TPUs with open software, ML frameworks, and flexible consumption options. Which Google Cloud offering best fits this requirement?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. AI Hypercomputer</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Cloud Storage Coldline</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Google Workspace</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. AI Hypercomputer</strong></p>\n<p>AI Hypercomputer brings together GPUs and TPUs, open software, ML frameworks and flexible consumption options in one architecture for training and serving large AI models. The stem lists these exact components.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. Cloud Storage Coldline</strong>: a low-cost storage class for rarely accessed data.\n- <strong>C. VPC</strong>: it provides private networking for cloud resources, not AI compute.\n- <strong>D. Google Workspace</strong>: productivity apps such as Gmail and Docs.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> GPUs/TPUs + open software + flexible consumption = AI Hypercomputer.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/ai-hypercomputer/docs/overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/ai-hypercomputer/docs/overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/ai-infrastructure\">cloud.google.com/ai-infrastructure</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/compute/docs/gpus\">docs.cloud.google.com/compute/docs/gpus</a></p>","lessons":["d3l9"]},"e5q40":{"id":"e5q40","exam":5,"n":40,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>PixelTrail, a mobile photo-sharing startup, has developers who build new features on their laptops, but the same code sometimes breaks after release because the production environment has different runtime libraries and package versions. Which technology should PixelTrail use to bundle the application code with its required dependencies so the application behaves consistently across development, testing, and production?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Containerization</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Serverless computing</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Spot VMs</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Load balancing</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Containerization</strong></p>\n<p>A container packages the application code with its libraries and dependencies. The same container runs the same way on a laptop, in testing and in production. The clue is \"bundle the application code with its required dependencies\" so it \"behaves consistently\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. Serverless computing</strong>: it removes server management, but doesn't by itself solve dependency mismatch.\n- <strong>C. Spot VMs</strong>: a cheaper VM pricing option, unrelated to packaging.\n- <strong>D. Load balancing</strong>: it spreads traffic across instances, not about consistent environments.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> \"Works on my machine\" problems = containers.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/what-are-containers\">cloud.google.com/learn/what-are-containers</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/discover/what-is-containerization\">cloud.google.com/discover/what-is-containerization</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/load-balancing/docs/load-balancing-overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/load-balancing/docs/load-balancing-overview</a></p>","lessons":["d4l3"]},"e5q41":{"id":"e5q41","exam":5,"n":41,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>BrightCart, an online grocery app, has hired a small group of data scientists and software engineers. The team is building its own advanced machine learning model by writing custom training code in Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform notebooks. Which service should BrightCart use to train this model?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Custom training</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. AutoML</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Compute Engine</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Prebuilt APIs</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Custom training</strong></p>\n<p>Custom training lets data scientists write their own training code and run it on the AI platform (formerly Vertex AI). It gives full control over the model. The clues are \"data scientists and software engineers\", \"advanced\" model and \"writing custom training code\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. AutoML</strong>: it trains models with little or no code, for teams without deep ML skills.\n- <strong>C. Compute Engine</strong>: you could run code on VMs, but it isn't the managed ML training service and adds work.\n- <strong>D. Prebuilt APIs</strong>: these are pre-trained models; you don't train them yourself.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> ML experts + own code = custom training. No code = AutoML. No training = prebuilt APIs.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/machine-learning/start/training-guide\">docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/machine-learning/start/training-guide</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/machine-learning/training/overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/machine-learning/training/overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/machine-learning\">docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/machine-learning</a></p>","lessons":["d3l3","d3l5","d3l6"]},"e5q42":{"id":"e5q42","exam":5,"n":42,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>EventEase, a local event venue platform, lets customers reserve halls, meeting rooms, and party spaces online. EventEase has recently partnered with FreshFork Catering so customers can request meals while making a venue reservation. How can EventEase use application programming interfaces (APIs) to create new business value?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Use an API to redesign the reservation website for a specific customer segment</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Use an API to discover possible future partnership opportunities</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Use an API to move all customer records into a machine learning model to estimate meal demand</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Use an API to connect the venue reservation system with FreshFork Catering’s order request form</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Use an API to connect the venue reservation system with FreshFork Catering’s order request form</strong></p>\n<p>APIs let two companies' systems work together. Connecting the reservation system to the caterer's ordering lets customers book a venue and order meals in one flow. That creates new value for both partners. The clue is the new partnership and \"request meals while making a venue reservation\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Redesign the website for a segment</strong>: that is a design task, not what APIs do.\n- <strong>B. Discover future partnerships</strong>: APIs connect systems, they don't find business partners.\n- <strong>C. Move all records into an ML model</strong>: that is forecasting, not the partner integration described.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> New partner + combined customer experience = connect systems with APIs.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/apis/docs/overview\">cloud.google.com/apis/docs/overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/api-gateway/docs/about-api-gateway\">cloud.google.com/api-gateway/docs/about-api-gateway</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/solutions/new-channels-using-apis\">cloud.google.com/solutions/new-channels-using-apis</a></p>","lessons":["d4l8"]},"e5q43":{"id":"e5q43","exam":5,"n":43,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>QuickCart, an online grocery delivery platform, is moving its new microservices into containers. The engineering team expects to run and coordinate several thousand containers and wants a managed Google Cloud service that can automate container deployment, scaling, and operations. Which service should QuickCart choose?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Cloud Run functions</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE)</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Apigee</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. BigQuery Omni</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE)</strong></p>\n<p>GKE is Google's managed Kubernetes service. Kubernetes orchestrates containers: it deploys, scales, heals and manages many containers across a cluster. The clues are \"several thousand containers\" and \"automate container deployment, scaling, and operations\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Cloud Run functions</strong>: it runs small event-driven functions, not large container orchestration.\n- <strong>C. Apigee</strong>: it manages APIs, not containers.\n- <strong>D. BigQuery Omni</strong>: it runs BigQuery analytics on data in other clouds.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Orchestrate many containers = Kubernetes = GKE.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs\">docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/functions/docs\">docs.cloud.google.com/functions/docs</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/apigee/docs/api-platform/get-started/what-apigee\">docs.cloud.google.com/apigee/docs/api-platform/get-started/what-apigee</a></p>","lessons":["d4l3"]},"e5q44":{"id":"e5q44","exam":5,"n":44,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>NimbusLedger, a small fintech website, is modernizing its development process. Its engineering team wants to adopt software whose source code is available for public review, so developers can study it, change it, improve it, and contribute updates back to the community. Which term best describes this type of technology?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Proprietary software</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Open source</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Platform as a Service (PaaS)</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Closed standard</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Open source</strong></p>\n<p>Open source software has public source code that anyone can view, modify and improve, and contribute changes back to the community. The stem describes this exactly.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Proprietary software</strong>: the vendor owns and controls the code, which you usually can't see or change.\n- <strong>C. PaaS</strong>: it is a cloud service model for running apps, not a licensing model.\n- <strong>D. Closed standard</strong>: a specification controlled by one party, the opposite of open.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Public code, anyone can change and contribute = open source.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://opensource.org/osd\">opensource.org/osd</a>\n- <a href=\"https://opensource.google/\">opensource.google/</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-paas\">cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-paas</a></p>","lessons":["d4l7"]},"e5q45":{"id":"e5q45","exam":5,"n":45,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>TrailBite, a food delivery website, is losing customers because its legacy data center applications cannot respond quickly to new menu, payment, and delivery feature requests. The company decides to modernize these applications by moving toward cloud-based application architecture. What may have led TrailBite to make this business decision?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Its data center applications already scale automatically whenever demand changes.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. It wants to move from a pay-as-you-go model to a capital expenditure model.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Its application code changes by itself without any developer making updates.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Its data center applications take several weeks to update and release.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Its data center applications take several weeks to update and release.</strong></p>\n<p>Slow release cycles stop a company from responding to customer needs, which is exactly why TrailBite is losing customers. Cloud-based architecture, such as microservices, containers and CI/CD, speeds up changes. The clue is \"cannot respond quickly to new... feature requests\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Already scale automatically</strong>: if they did, that would be a reason not to change.\n- <strong>B. Move from pay-as-you-go to CapEx</strong>: cloud moves the other way, toward OpEx.\n- <strong>C. Code changes by itself</strong>: this doesn't happen and isn't a business problem.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Slow releases + losing customers = modernize applications.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/solutions/application-modernization\">cloud.google.com/solutions/application-modernization</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/cloud-migration\">cloud.google.com/learn/cloud-migration</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-cloud-native\">cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-cloud-native</a></p>","lessons":["d4l1"]},"e5q46":{"id":"e5q46","exam":5,"n":46,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>BrightCart, a small retail mobile app, wants to add image labeling and text sentiment detection using Google Cloud AI services. The team wants these capabilities without building machine learning models from scratch or hiring a dedicated machine learning team. How do ready-to-use Google Cloud AI and machine learning APIs make AI and ML easier for customers to adopt?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. They don't require data science expertise.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. They don't require data input and validation.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. They require fewer security permissions.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. They all require custom training models.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. They don't require data science expertise.</strong></p>\n<p>Pre-trained APIs, such as the Vision API and Natural Language API, use models Google has already built and trained. A developer sends an image or text to the API and gets labels or sentiment back. Nobody has to collect training data, design a model or tune it, so no data scientists or ML engineers are needed. The clue is \"without building machine learning models from scratch or hiring a dedicated machine learning team\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. Don't require data input and validation</strong>: You still send the images and text to analyze, and you should validate them.\n- <strong>C. Fewer security permissions</strong>: Access is still controlled with IAM. That isn't the adoption benefit.\n- <strong>D. All require custom training</strong>: The opposite. They are pre-trained.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Pre-trained API = no model building, no data science team. A developer just calls it.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/ai/apis\">cloud.google.com/ai/apis</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/vision/docs\">cloud.google.com/vision/docs</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/natural-language\">cloud.google.com/natural-language</a></p>","lessons":["d3l3","d3l4"]},"e5q47":{"id":"e5q47","exam":5,"n":47,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>PayBridge, a digital banking company, has created a controlled API that lets approved personal finance apps retrieve customer transaction information. PayBridge wants to track API traffic, apply access control and security rules, and create pricing plans so external developers can be charged for using the API. Which Google Cloud solution should PayBridge use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Apigee API Management</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Google Kubernetes Engine</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Spot VMs</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Cloud Run functions</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Apigee API Management</strong></p>\n<p>Apigee is Google Cloud's platform for managing APIs. It tracks traffic and analytics, enforces security and access control, and supports monetization with pricing plans for developers. The stem lists all three: track traffic, access rules, charge developers.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. GKE</strong>: it runs containers, but doesn't manage or monetize APIs.\n- <strong>C. Spot VMs</strong>: a low-cost compute pricing option.\n- <strong>D. Cloud Run functions</strong>: it runs event-driven code, not API management.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Secure, monitor and monetize APIs = Apigee.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/apigee/docs/api-platform/get-started/what-apigee\">docs.cloud.google.com/apigee/docs/api-platform/get-started/what-apigee</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/apigee/docs/api-platform/monetization/overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/apigee/docs/api-platform/monetization/overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/apigee/docs/api-platform/develop/policy-attachment-and-enforcement\">docs.cloud.google.com/apigee/docs/api-platform/develop/policy-attachment-and-enforcement</a></p>","lessons":["d4l4","d4l8"]},"e5q48":{"id":"e5q48","exam":5,"n":48,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>TrendNest, a retail analytics mobile app, wants to bring together its own customer activity data, partner campaign data, and external market data into one unified cloud environment. The goal is to prepare that combined data so data scientists can efficiently build and train new machine learning models. Which Google Cloud differentiator addresses this requirement?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. AI-ready data cloud</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Global edge network</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Infrastructure as a Service (IaaS)</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Two-step verification (2SV)</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. AI-ready data cloud</strong></p>\n<p>Google Cloud's data platform brings internal, partner and external data together in one place and prepares it for AI. Data scientists can then build and train models on unified, well-managed data. The clues are \"bring together... into one unified cloud environment\" and \"train new machine learning models\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. Global edge network</strong>: it delivers content fast to users, not data preparation for ML.\n- <strong>C. IaaS</strong>: it is a service model for raw compute and storage, not a data differentiator.\n- <strong>D. 2SV</strong>: it is a sign-in security feature.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Unified data prepared for ML = AI-ready data cloud.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/bigquery\">cloud.google.com/bigquery</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-iaas\">cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-iaas</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/identity-platform/docs/web/mfa\">cloud.google.com/identity-platform/docs/web/mfa</a></p>","lessons":["d2l2"]},"e5q49":{"id":"e5q49","exam":5,"n":49,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>NovaStyle Apparel, a fashion e-commerce company, wants to use advanced generative AI to improve product recommendations, summarize customer feedback, and automate merchandising insights. However, its sales, inventory, customer, and marketing data is fragmented across many systems and stored in different formats. What foundational step should the company take to successfully support this AI transformation?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Build an AI-ready data platform</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Deploy a multi-cloud network architecture</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Shift operational expenditures to capital expenditures</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Restrict all data access to on-premises environments</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Build an AI-ready data platform</strong></p>\n<p>Generative AI is only as good as the data it can use. When data is spread across systems in different formats, the first step is to unify, clean and govern it on a platform AI can draw from. The clue is \"fragmented across many systems\" and \"foundational step\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. Multi-cloud network architecture</strong>: networking doesn't fix fragmented, inconsistent data.\n- <strong>C. Shift OpEx to CapEx</strong>: a spending change, unrelated, and the opposite of the cloud trend.\n- <strong>D. Restrict data to on-premises</strong>: this blocks the cloud AI services the company wants to use.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Good AI starts with unified, AI-ready data.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/data-cloud\">cloud.google.com/data-cloud</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/bigquery\">cloud.google.com/bigquery</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/introduction\">docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/introduction</a></p>","lessons":["d2l2"]},"e5q50":{"id":"e5q50","exam":5,"n":50,"domain":5,"html":"<p><strong>FinBridgeIQ, a fintech website, uses an internal LLM-based assistant to help employees search payment reports and customer billing summaries. Security teams discover that an attacker could submit cleverly written prompts that cause the assistant to ignore its original instructions, reveal restricted financial records, or bypass built-in response controls. Which modern cybersecurity threat best describes this risk?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Cryptomining</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Distributed Denial-of-Service (DDoS)</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. LLM attack (prompt injection)</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Ransomware</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. LLM attack (prompt injection)</strong></p>\n<p>Prompt injection is when an attacker writes inputs that trick a large language model into ignoring its instructions, revealing restricted data or bypassing its safety controls. The stem describes exactly that: \"cleverly written prompts\" that make the assistant \"ignore its original instructions\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Cryptomining</strong>: attackers secretly use your compute to mine cryptocurrency.\n- <strong>B. DDoS</strong>: it floods a service with traffic to make it unavailable.\n- <strong>D. Ransomware</strong>: it encrypts data and demands payment.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Malicious prompts that hijack an AI model = prompt injection.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://genai.owasp.org/llmrisk/llm01-prompt-injection/\">genai.owasp.org/llmrisk/llm01-prompt-injection/</a>\n- <a href=\"https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/PromptInjection\">owasp.org/www-community/attacks/PromptInjection</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/security/products/model-armor\">cloud.google.com/security/products/model-armor</a></p>","lessons":["d5l2","d5l5"]},"e6q1":{"id":"e6q1","exam":6,"n":1,"domain":6,"html":"<p><strong>PulseCart, an online grocery app, runs several Compute Engine VM instances for its order-processing API. The VMs usually stay active for most of each billing month, and the finance team wants to receive sustained use discounts. What should PulseCart do?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Choose Spot VMs when creating virtual machines.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Choose smaller machine types with lower CPU and memory.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Nothing. Sustained use discounts are automatically applied.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Commit to virtual machine usage for a one-year or three-year period.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Nothing. Sustained use discounts are automatically applied.</strong></p>\n<p>Sustained use discounts (SUDs) are applied automatically to eligible Compute Engine VMs once they run for more than 25% of the billing month, and the discount grows the longer they run. There is no sign-up and no contract. The clue is \"stay active for most of each billing month\": that usage pattern earns the discount on its own.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Choose Spot VMs</strong>: Spot VMs are a separate, deeply discounted option for interruptible workloads, not a way to get SUDs, and they can be stopped at any time.\n- <strong>B. Choose smaller machine types</strong>: This may lower the bill, but it has nothing to do with qualifying for sustained use discounts.\n- <strong>D. Commit for one or three years</strong>: That describes committed use discounts (CUDs), a different program that requires a commitment.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> SUD = automatic, for running most of the month. CUD = you sign a 1- or 3-year commitment.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/compute/docs/sustained-use-discounts\">docs.cloud.google.com/compute/docs/sustained-use-discounts</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/compute/docs/instances/committed-use-discounts-overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/compute/docs/instances/committed-use-discounts-overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/compute/docs/instances/spot\">docs.cloud.google.com/compute/docs/instances/spot</a></p>","lessons":["d6l2"]},"e6q2":{"id":"e6q2","exam":6,"n":2,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>RideGrid Logistics wants to quickly add an AI assistant to help warehouse employees check shipment status, summarize delay reasons, and answer common operations questions. Instead of building and training custom AI models from the beginning, the company plans to use pre-built Google Cloud AI agents and templates. What is the main business tradeoff of this approach?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. It provides faster implementation speed but may offer less specialized business differentiation.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. It provides maximum business differentiation but takes years to implement.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. It requires a massive team of expert data scientists to maintain.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. It requires shifting all cloud costs to a capital expenditure (CapEx) model.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. It provides faster implementation speed but may offer less specialized business differentiation.</strong></p>\n<p>Pre-built agents and templates let a company deliver value quickly, because the hard work of building and training models is already done. The tradeoff is that competitors can use the same building blocks, so the result is less unique to the business. The stem's clue is \"quickly\" and \"instead of building and training custom AI models.\"</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. Maximum differentiation, takes years</strong>: That describes the opposite approach, building custom models from scratch.\n- <strong>C. Massive team of data scientists</strong>: Pre-built solutions exist precisely to reduce the need for deep ML expertise.\n- <strong>D. Shift to CapEx</strong>: Cloud AI services are usually paid as operating expense (OpEx), not upfront capital spending.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Pre-built = fast, less differentiation. Custom = more effort, more differentiation.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/products/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform\">cloud.google.com/products/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/build/agent-garden\">docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/build/agent-garden</a></p>","lessons":["d3l3","d3l8"]},"e6q3":{"id":"e6q3","exam":6,"n":3,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>StreamlyPay is building a customer-support chatbot for its mobile wallet app. The team wants to compare and use different foundation models without being restricted to a single provider. They want access to Google models, open models, and partner models from other companies. Does Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform meet this requirement?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. No, it only permits customers to use Google-owned proprietary models.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Yes, it provides access to a broad selection of foundation models from Google, open-model communities, and third-party partners.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. No, it is designed to support only third-party foundation models.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Yes, but it is limited to open models only.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Yes, it provides access to a broad selection of foundation models from Google, open-model communities, and third-party partners.</strong></p>\n<p>Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform (formerly Vertex AI) offers a catalog of models (Model Garden) that includes Google models such as Gemini, open models, and partner models from other companies. This lets teams compare models and pick the best fit without being locked into one provider. The stem asks for exactly that mix: Google, open, and partner models.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Only Google-owned models</strong>: The platform deliberately includes non-Google models.\n- <strong>C. Only third-party models</strong>: Google's own models, such as Gemini, are a core part of the catalog.\n- <strong>D. Limited to open models only</strong>: Open models are one part of the choice, not the only one.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Model choice on Google Cloud = Google + open + partner models in one platform.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/products/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform\">cloud.google.com/products/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/model-garden\">cloud.google.com/model-garden</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/models/partner-models/use-partner-models\">docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/models/partner-models/use-partner-models</a></p>","lessons":["d3l6","d3l7"]},"e6q4":{"id":"e6q4","exam":6,"n":4,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>FreshBasket Analytics, a grocery delivery website, currently processes sales transactions once every quarter to identify buying patterns and create business reports. The leadership team wants more accurate insights and wants managers to react to customer demand as it happens. What should FreshBasket Analytics do?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Change from a relational database to a NoSQL database.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Filter the data so reports are generated faster.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Switch from batch processing to stream processing.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Process batch reports every two weeks instead of every quarter.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Switch from batch processing to stream processing.</strong></p>\n<p>Batch processing analyzes data in large chunks on a schedule, here once a quarter, so insights are always out of date. Stream processing analyzes data continuously as it arrives, so managers can react to demand in near real time. The clue is \"react to customer demand as it happens.\"</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Change to NoSQL</strong>: The database type does not decide how fast data is analyzed. The processing model does.\n- <strong>B. Filter the data</strong>: Less data may make reports faster, but they are still quarterly and possibly less accurate.\n- <strong>D. Batch every two weeks</strong>: This is an improvement, but it is still batch and still not \"as it happens.\"</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> React as it happens → streaming. Periodic reports → batch.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/dataflow/docs/overview\">cloud.google.com/dataflow/docs/overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/dataflow/docs/concepts/streaming-pipelines\">cloud.google.com/dataflow/docs/concepts/streaming-pipelines</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/pubsub/docs/overview\">cloud.google.com/pubsub/docs/overview</a></p>","lessons":["d2l9"]},"e6q5":{"id":"e6q5","exam":6,"n":5,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>ShopEaseNow, an online shopping app, wants to improve its customer support operations by using a purpose-built Google Cloud AI solution. The company wants to help support agents resolve issues faster, automate common customer interactions, and provide more personalized support experiences. Which Google Cloud AI solution should ShopEaseNow use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Text-to-Speech API</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Cloud Talent Solution</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Document AI</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Contact Center AI</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Contact Center AI</strong></p>\n<p>Contact Center AI is Google Cloud's purpose-built solution for customer service. It uses virtual agents to automate common interactions, assists human agents with suggestions in real time, and gives insights from conversations. The stem's clues are \"customer support operations,\" \"help support agents resolve issues faster,\" and \"automate common customer interactions.\"</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Text-to-Speech API</strong>: It turns text into spoken audio. It is a building block, not a full support solution.\n- <strong>B. Cloud Talent Solution</strong>: It improves job search and recruiting, not customer support.\n- <strong>C. Document AI</strong>: It extracts data from documents such as forms and invoices.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Customer support at scale → Contact Center AI.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/solutions/contact-center-ai-platform\">cloud.google.com/solutions/contact-center-ai-platform</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/contact-center/ccai-platform/docs/agent-assist\">docs.cloud.google.com/contact-center/ccai-platform/docs/agent-assist</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/text-to-speech/docs\">docs.cloud.google.com/text-to-speech/docs</a></p>","lessons":["d3l4","d3l8"],"same":["e2q13"]},"e6q6":{"id":"e6q6","exam":6,"n":6,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>MedLedger, a healthcare records company, wants to move several older applications from its private data center to Google Cloud. Each application was designed to run with its own dedicated operating system and cannot share the same OS environment with other applications. Which infrastructure solution should the company choose?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Virtual machines</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Open source software</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Serverless computing</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Containers</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Virtual machines</strong></p>\n<p>Each virtual machine runs its own full guest operating system on top of a hypervisor. That gives every legacy application a dedicated OS, isolated from the others, which is exactly what the stem requires: apps that \"cannot share the same OS environment.\" On Google Cloud, this means Compute Engine.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. Open source software</strong>: This is a licensing model for software, not an infrastructure choice.\n- <strong>C. Serverless computing</strong>: You don't control the OS at all, and older apps often need changes to run serverless.\n- <strong>D. Containers</strong>: Containers share the host's operating system kernel, which conflicts with the dedicated-OS requirement.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Dedicated OS per app → virtual machines. Shared OS kernel, lightweight → containers.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/compute/docs/overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/compute/docs/overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/compute/docs/instances\">docs.cloud.google.com/compute/docs/instances</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/discover/containers-vs-vms\">cloud.google.com/discover/containers-vs-vms</a></p>","lessons":["d4l2"]},"e6q7":{"id":"e6q7","exam":6,"n":7,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>FitTrail, a fitness tracking mobile app, needs to store user workout logs, device readings, and profile preferences. The development team expects the structure of this data to change over time as new wearable devices and health metrics are added. What is a defining feature of a non-relational database?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Reporting across many separate data sources</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. A schema that must be strictly enforced before data is stored</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. A flexible data model</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Queries that join data from several tables</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. A flexible data model</strong></p>\n<p>Non-relational (NoSQL) databases do not require a fixed schema. Records can have different fields, and new fields can be added without redesigning tables. That fits FitTrail, whose data structure \"will change over time as new wearable devices and health metrics are added.\" Firestore and Bigtable are Google Cloud examples.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Reporting across many data sources</strong>: That describes a data warehouse such as BigQuery, not a NoSQL feature.\n- <strong>B. Strictly enforced schema</strong>: That is a trait of relational databases, the opposite of NoSQL.\n- <strong>D. Joins across several tables</strong>: Joins between related tables are a classic relational database strength.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Changing structure → NoSQL, flexible model. Related tables and transactions → relational.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/discover/what-is-nosql\">cloud.google.com/discover/what-is-nosql</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/firestore\">cloud.google.com/firestore</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/bigtable/docs\">docs.cloud.google.com/bigtable/docs</a></p>","lessons":["d2l5"]},"e6q8":{"id":"e6q8","exam":6,"n":8,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>ShopKart, an online marketplace, is organizing different types of business information before moving it into Google Cloud for analytics and storage. The team wants to identify which type of information is unstructured data. Which option is an example of unstructured data?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Monthly warehouse stock records</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Product star ratings</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Customer purchase orders</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Customer support call transcripts</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Customer support call transcripts</strong></p>\n<p>Unstructured data has no predefined format or fixed fields. Free text, audio, images, and video are typical examples. Call transcripts are free-form conversation text, so they are unstructured, even though they can be analyzed later with tools like the Natural Language API.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Monthly warehouse stock records</strong>: These fit neatly into rows and columns (item, quantity, date), so they are structured.\n- <strong>B. Product star ratings</strong>: A rating is a single numeric value in a fixed field, so it is structured.\n- <strong>C. Customer purchase orders</strong>: Orders have defined fields like customer, item, price, and date, so they are structured.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Text, audio, video, transcripts → unstructured. Table fields → structured. JSON/XML → semi-structured.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/discover/what-is-unstructured-data\">cloud.google.com/discover/what-is-unstructured-data</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/discover/what-is-structured-data\">cloud.google.com/discover/what-is-structured-data</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/natural-language/docs\">cloud.google.com/natural-language/docs</a></p>","lessons":["d2l3"]},"e6q9":{"id":"e6q9","exam":6,"n":9,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>GardenLens, a plant care mobile app, wants its data science team to build and train custom machine learning models that can identify plant diseases from images. The team wants to use an open source machine learning library that provides a flexible ecosystem of tools, libraries, and community resources. Which product or solution should GardenLens use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Cloud Run functions</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Apache Beam</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Dataflow</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. TensorFlow</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. TensorFlow</strong></p>\n<p>TensorFlow is an open source machine learning library, originally created by Google, with a large ecosystem of tools, libraries, and community resources. Data scientists use it to build and train custom models, such as image classifiers for plant diseases. The clues are \"open source machine learning library\" and \"build and train custom models.\"</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Cloud Run functions</strong>: A serverless service for running small pieces of code in response to events, not an ML library.\n- <strong>B. Apache Beam</strong>: An open source model for data processing pipelines, not for training ML models.\n- <strong>C. Dataflow</strong>: Google's managed service that runs Apache Beam pipelines for batch and streaming data, not an ML library.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Open source ML library → TensorFlow. Data pipelines → Apache Beam / Dataflow.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://tensorflow.org\">tensorflow.org</a>\n- <a href=\"https://tensorflow.org/learn\">tensorflow.org/learn</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/dataflow/docs/concepts/beam-programming-model\">docs.cloud.google.com/dataflow/docs/concepts/beam-programming-model</a></p>","lessons":["d3l5"]},"e6q10":{"id":"e6q10","exam":6,"n":10,"domain":6,"html":"<p><strong>TrailNest Tours, a travel-booking website, runs several applications on Google Cloud. The operations team wants a service that helps them inspect application events, review error messages, and find the cause of production issues faster. What does Cloud Logging help TrailNest Tours do?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Analyze live source code and record code update history.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Deploy infrastructure as code.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Analyze logs and accelerate application troubleshooting.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Manage the storage of custom VM images.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Analyze logs and accelerate application troubleshooting.</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud Logging collects, stores, searches, and analyzes log data from applications and Google Cloud services in one place. Teams use it to review events and error messages and find the root cause of problems faster. The stem's clues are \"inspect application events,\" \"review error messages,\" and \"find the cause of production issues.\"</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Analyze source code and record update history</strong>: That is the job of source control and code repositories, not logging.\n- <strong>B. Deploy infrastructure as code</strong>: That is done with tools like Terraform, not Cloud Logging.\n- <strong>D. Manage custom VM images</strong>: Custom images are managed in Compute Engine, not in Cloud Logging.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Logs in one place, faster troubleshooting → Cloud Logging. Metrics and alerts → Cloud Monitoring.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/logging/docs/overview\">cloud.google.com/logging/docs/overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/logging/docs/view/logs-explorer-interface\">cloud.google.com/logging/docs/view/logs-explorer-interface</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/logging/docs/log-analytics\">cloud.google.com/logging/docs/log-analytics</a></p>","lessons":["d6l8"]},"e6q11":{"id":"e6q11","exam":6,"n":11,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>PhotoNest, a photo editing web app, wants to rent virtual machines, storage, and networking from a cloud provider so its engineers can build and configure customized servers. The company prefers paying only for the resources it uses instead of buying physical hardware upfront. Which cloud service model should PhotoNest use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Hybrid cloud</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Software as a service</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Infrastructure as a service</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Platform as a service</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Infrastructure as a service</strong></p>\n<p>IaaS provides the basic building blocks: virtual machines, storage, and networking, rented on a pay-as-you-go basis. The customer still configures and manages the operating systems and software on top. The clues are \"rent virtual machines, storage, and networking\" and \"build and configure customized servers.\" Compute Engine is the typical Google Cloud example.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Hybrid cloud</strong>: This is a deployment model (on-premises plus cloud), not a service model.\n- <strong>B. Software as a service</strong>: SaaS delivers a finished application, with no servers to configure.\n- <strong>D. Platform as a service</strong>: PaaS hides the servers so developers just deploy code, which is the opposite of building custom servers.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Rent VMs, storage, network → IaaS. Deploy code, no servers → PaaS. Ready-made app → SaaS.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-iaas\">cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-iaas</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-saas\">cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-saas</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-paas\">cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-paas</a></p>","lessons":["d1l4","d1l5"]},"e6q12":{"id":"e6q12","exam":6,"n":12,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>ShopPulse, an ecommerce website, wants to analyze page load times, visitor behavior, clickstream events, and checkout performance by using BigQuery. However, the company does not want to move all of its data to Google Cloud and wants to keep part of its data in its own data center. Which environment should ShopPulse use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Private cloud</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. On-premises</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Multi-cloud</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Hybrid cloud</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Hybrid cloud</strong></p>\n<p>A hybrid cloud combines on-premises infrastructure with public cloud services. ShopPulse wants to use BigQuery (a public cloud service) while keeping part of its data in its own data center. That mix of on-premises and cloud is the definition of hybrid.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Private cloud</strong>: A private cloud is dedicated to one organization and would not, by itself, give access to BigQuery.\n- <strong>B. On-premises</strong>: Staying fully on-premises means not using BigQuery in Google Cloud at all.\n- <strong>C. Multi-cloud</strong>: Multi-cloud means using more than one public cloud provider. The stem mentions only Google Cloud and a private data center.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> On-premises + one public cloud → hybrid. Several public clouds → multicloud.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-hybrid-cloud\">cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-hybrid-cloud</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/hybrid-cloud\">cloud.google.com/hybrid-cloud</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/introduction\">cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/introduction</a></p>","lessons":["d4l7"]},"e6q13":{"id":"e6q13","exam":6,"n":13,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>MetroHire, a job-matching web application, wants to deploy its custom application in Google Cloud. The development team needs the application to scale as user traffic changes, but they do not want to manage servers, operating systems, or underlying infrastructure. Which service model should MetroHire use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Infrastructure as a service</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Platform as a service</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Software as a service</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Network as a service</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Platform as a service</strong></p>\n<p>With PaaS, developers deploy their own application code, and the provider manages the servers, operating systems, and scaling. The clues are \"deploy its custom application\" and \"do not want to manage servers, operating systems, or underlying infrastructure.\" Google Cloud examples include App Engine and Cloud Run.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Infrastructure as a service</strong>: IaaS still requires the customer to manage VMs and operating systems.\n- <strong>C. Software as a service</strong>: SaaS delivers a ready-made application. MetroHire wants to run its own custom app.\n- <strong>D. Network as a service</strong>: This is about renting network capabilities, not hosting an application.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Your own code + no server management → PaaS.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/paas-vs-iaas-vs-saas\">cloud.google.com/learn/paas-vs-iaas-vs-saas</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/appengine\">cloud.google.com/appengine</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/appengine/docs\">docs.cloud.google.com/appengine/docs</a></p>","lessons":["d1l5"]},"e6q14":{"id":"e6q14","exam":6,"n":14,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>CareKart, a healthcare analytics company, is helping its operations managers understand appointment bookings, cancellations, and patient satisfaction scores. The managers want to compare past performance using dashboards, while the data science team wants systems that can learn from data and automatically predict future no-show risk. Which statement best describes the fundamental difference between machine learning and traditional business intelligence?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Business intelligence predicts future trends using algorithms, while machine learning only visualizes past historical data.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Business intelligence relies on unstructured data like images, while machine learning only works with structured relational tables.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Business intelligence focuses on analyzing historical data to create reports and dashboards, while machine learning learns from data to make automated predictions or decisions.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Business intelligence requires TPUs to operate, while machine learning only runs on standard CPUs.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Business intelligence focuses on analyzing historical data to create reports and dashboards, while machine learning learns from data to make automated predictions or decisions.</strong></p>\n<p>BI looks backward. It summarizes what already happened in reports and dashboards, like the managers comparing past performance. ML learns patterns from data and applies them to new cases, like predicting no-show risk. The stem describes both uses side by side.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. BI predicts, ML visualizes the past</strong>: This swaps the two roles.\n- <strong>B. BI uses unstructured data, ML only structured</strong>: BI usually works on structured data, and ML can handle images, text, and audio.\n- <strong>D. BI needs TPUs, ML only CPUs</strong>: TPUs are hardware for accelerating ML, not BI, and ML is not limited to CPUs.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> BI = dashboards about the past. ML = learns from data to predict and decide.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-business-intelligence\">cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-business-intelligence</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-machine-learning\">cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-machine-learning</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/bqml-introduction\">docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/bqml-introduction</a></p>","lessons":["d3l1","d3l8"]},"e6q15":{"id":"e6q15","exam":6,"n":15,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>PixelCart Market, an online shopping website, runs its main application from servers in its own data center. During holiday sales, many customers visit the website at the same time, and the application often becomes unavailable. What is the most likely reason for this problem?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. A serverless compute function is unable to scale properly.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. The application contains inconsistent customer data.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. The company does not have enough servers to handle the traffic.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. The application can only be configured in an on-premises environment.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. The company does not have enough servers to handle the traffic.</strong></p>\n<p>An on-premises data center has a fixed amount of hardware. When holiday traffic exceeds that capacity, the application slows down or becomes unavailable. Adding servers takes time and money, so the company cannot react to short spikes. The clue is \"many customers visit at the same time\" with servers \"in its own data center.\"</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. A serverless function cannot scale</strong>: The app runs on the company's own servers, not on serverless, and serverless scales automatically.\n- <strong>B. Inconsistent customer data</strong>: Data quality problems cause wrong results, not outages during traffic peaks.\n- <strong>D. Can only be configured on-premises</strong>: This says nothing about why the app fails under load.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> On-premises scaling pain = not enough servers for the peak, and adding more is slow and costly.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/performance-optimization/scalability\">cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/performance-optimization/scalability</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/autoscaler\">cloud.google.com/compute/docs/autoscaler</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/run/docs/about-functions\">cloud.google.com/run/docs/about-functions</a></p>","lessons":["d1l2"]},"e6q16":{"id":"e6q16","exam":6,"n":16,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>MealPilot Analytics is preparing a training dataset for a food delivery recommendation model. The team wants to make sure that each customer’s stored order total correctly matches the amount shown on the original payment invoice. Which data quality dimension does this represent?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Completeness</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Accuracy</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Timeliness</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Uniqueness</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Accuracy</strong></p>\n<p>Accuracy measures whether data correctly reflects the real-world value or its trusted source. Checking that the stored order total matches the original payment invoice is a direct accuracy check. The clue is \"correctly matches the amount shown on the original.\"</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Completeness</strong>: This is about missing values, not whether existing values are correct.\n- <strong>C. Timeliness</strong>: This is about whether data is up to date and available when needed.\n- <strong>D. Uniqueness</strong>: This is about duplicate records.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Missing → completeness. Matches source → accuracy. Right format → validity. Duplicates → uniqueness. Stale → timeliness.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-data-governance\">cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-data-governance</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/dataplex/docs/auto-data-quality-overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/dataplex/docs/auto-data-quality-overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://ibm.com/think/topics/data-quality-dimensions\">ibm.com/think/topics/data-quality-dimensions</a></p>","lessons":["d2l4"]},"e6q17":{"id":"e6q17","exam":6,"n":17,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>MediLedger Clinics has several hundred scanned patient intake forms and needs to find important details such as appointment dates, patient names, and specific medical keywords. The team wants to use Google Cloud APIs to process these files instead of manually reading every scan. Why should MediLedger Clinics use application programming interfaces APIs?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. To replace the scanned forms with a customer feedback questionnaire</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. To stream the document data continuously and encrypt words that do not match</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. To create searchable digital versions of the scanned forms and find important information</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. To convert the documents into unstructured data</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. To create searchable digital versions of the scanned forms and find important information</strong></p>\n<p>Google Cloud APIs such as the Vision API (text detection) and Document AI can read scanned documents, turn the images into machine-readable text, and extract key fields like names and dates. This removes the need to read every scan by hand. The clues are \"scanned patient intake forms\" and \"find important details.\"</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Replace forms with a questionnaire</strong>: This changes the business process. It does not process the existing scans.\n- <strong>B. Stream data and encrypt non-matching words</strong>: This is not what document-processing APIs do and does not help find information.\n- <strong>D. Convert to unstructured data</strong>: Scans are already unstructured. The goal is to turn them into searchable, structured information.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Scanned forms → APIs make them searchable and extract key fields.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/document-ai/docs/overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/document-ai/docs/overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/vision/docs/ocr\">docs.cloud.google.com/vision/docs/ocr</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/document-ai/docs/extracting-overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/document-ai/docs/extracting-overview</a></p>","lessons":["d3l4"]},"e6q18":{"id":"e6q18","exam":6,"n":18,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>FitStyle, a clothing recommendation website, wants to use its full history of browsing behavior, purchases, ratings, and search activity to show personalized product suggestions. The company wants the suggestions to become more accurate as new user data is collected. Which method should FitStyle use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Data automation</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Trends analysis</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Machine learning</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Multiple regression</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Machine learning</strong></p>\n<p>Machine learning finds patterns in historical data and keeps improving as new data arrives. A recommendation system that uses browsing, purchases, ratings, and searches, and gets more accurate over time, is a classic ML use case. The key clue is \"become more accurate as new user data is collected.\"</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Data automation</strong>: Automating data tasks moves or processes data but does not learn to predict preferences.\n- <strong>B. Trends analysis</strong>: This describes past patterns and does not personalize suggestions for each user.\n- <strong>D. Multiple regression</strong>: A single statistical technique for predicting a number. It is not the broad, self-improving approach the stem describes.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Learns and improves as data grows → machine learning.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-machine-learning\">cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-machine-learning</a>\n- <a href=\"https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course\">developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/discover/what-is-predictive-analytics\">cloud.google.com/discover/what-is-predictive-analytics</a></p>\n<p><strong>⚠️ Note:</strong> Multiple regression is itself a statistical/ML technique, so D is not strictly wrong. \"Machine learning\" is the broader and best answer because the system must keep improving with new data. Pick the keyed answer on this exam.</p>","lessons":["d3l1"]},"e6q19":{"id":"e6q19","exam":6,"n":19,"domain":6,"html":"<p><strong>TrailSnap, a travel booking website, runs several services in Google Cloud. The operations team wants to keep records of application errors, operating system messages, service activity, and other events so they can review what happened during troubleshooting. What is logging within the context of cloud technology?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Recording application and operating system events as timestamped entries</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Monitoring network and resource limitations</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Tracking source code across an organization</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Recording infrastructure and hardware expenditure</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Recording application and operating system events as timestamped entries</strong></p>\n<p>Logging means recording what happened, when it happened: errors, OS messages, requests and other events. Each entry has a timestamp and can be plain text or structured data. The team can then search and review the history while troubleshooting. On Google Cloud this is Cloud Logging.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. Monitoring network and resource limits</strong>: That's monitoring with metrics, such as saturation, not logging.\n- <strong>C. Tracking source code</strong>: That's version control, for example Git repositories.\n- <strong>D. Recording hardware expenditure</strong>: That's billing and cost reporting.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Logs = what happened (events). Metrics = how much (numbers over time).</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/logging/docs/overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/logging/docs/overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/logging/docs\">docs.cloud.google.com/logging/docs</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/logging/docs/view/logs-explorer-interface\">docs.cloud.google.com/logging/docs/view/logs-explorer-interface</a></p>","lessons":["d6l7"]},"e6q20":{"id":"e6q20","exam":6,"n":20,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>FreshCart Metrics, an online grocery delivery company, wants to analyze sales, inventory, customer behavior, and delivery performance data in a data warehouse. Business analysts need to run flexible queries, create custom reports, and perform ad-hoc analysis across structured and semi-structured datasets. How should FreshCart Metrics proceed?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Import data into a semi-structured time-series database.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Choose a system to store structured and semi-structured data that supports ad-hoc analysis and custom reporting.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Copy unstructured data into a single large object store.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Ensure data is stored in structured tables and rows to support transactional queries and relationships.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Choose a system to store structured and semi-structured data that supports ad-hoc analysis and custom reporting.</strong></p>\n<p>A data warehouse, such as BigQuery, is built for analytics. It stores structured and semi-structured data and lets analysts run flexible, ad-hoc queries and build reports. The stem's clues are \"data warehouse,\" \"flexible queries,\" \"custom reports,\" and \"structured and semi-structured datasets.\" Option B restates those needs.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Semi-structured time-series database</strong>: This is specialized for time-stamped data, not broad business analysis.\n- <strong>C. Unstructured data in an object store</strong>: Raw object storage does not by itself support easy ad-hoc SQL reporting.\n- <strong>D. Structured tables for transactional queries</strong>: That describes a transactional (OLTP) relational database, not an analytics warehouse, and it ignores semi-structured data.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Analyst reporting and ad-hoc queries → data warehouse. Day-to-day transactions → relational database.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-a-data-warehouse\">cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-a-data-warehouse</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/introduction\">docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/introduction</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/query-overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/query-overview</a></p>","lessons":["d2l5"]},"e6q21":{"id":"e6q21","exam":6,"n":21,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>TrailCart, a growing grocery delivery website, wants to move its customer ordering platform to the cloud. The technical leadership team is worried that choosing one cloud provider could make future migration difficult if the business later wants to move workloads elsewhere. Which approach would best reduce this concern?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Open standards</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Database services</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Service level agreements</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Scalable infrastructure</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Open standards</strong></p>\n<p>Vendor lock-in happens when an application depends on one provider's proprietary technology, making it hard to move. Open standards and open source tools, such as Kubernetes and containers, work across providers, so workloads stay portable. The clue is the worry that one provider \"could make future migration difficult.\"</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. Database services</strong>: Managed databases are useful, but proprietary ones can increase lock-in rather than reduce it.\n- <strong>C. Service level agreements</strong>: SLAs define reliability commitments, not portability between providers.\n- <strong>D. Scalable infrastructure</strong>: Scalability helps handle growth but does not make it easier to leave a provider.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Avoid lock-in → openness: open standards, open source, portable workloads.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-multicloud\">cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-multicloud</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/hybrid-multicloud-patterns/drivers\">docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/hybrid-multicloud-patterns/drivers</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/architecture/framework\">cloud.google.com/architecture/framework</a></p>","lessons":["d4l7"]},"e6q22":{"id":"e6q22","exam":6,"n":22,"domain":5,"html":"<p><strong>ShieldWorks Labs, a healthcare software company, is configuring access to its Google Cloud resources. The security team wants each employee to receive only the specific permissions needed for their role, such as viewing logs, managing billing, or deploying applications, and nothing beyond those job responsibilities. Which security principle describes this approach?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Cyber resilience</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Zero-trust</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Least privilege</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Security by default</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Least privilege</strong></p>\n<p>The principle of least privilege means giving each user only the permissions they need to do their job, and nothing more. This limits damage from mistakes or compromised accounts. The clue is \"only the specific permissions needed for their role... and nothing beyond.\" In Google Cloud, this is applied with IAM roles.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Cyber resilience</strong>: This is the ability to withstand and recover from attacks, not a rule for assigning permissions.\n- <strong>B. Zero-trust</strong>: Zero trust means verifying every request and trusting no network by default. It is related but broader than limiting permissions.\n- <strong>D. Security by default</strong>: This means systems ship with secure settings already turned on.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Only what the job needs → least privilege.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/iam/docs/using-iam-securely\">docs.cloud.google.com/iam/docs/using-iam-securely</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/iam/docs/resource-hierarchy-access-control\">docs.cloud.google.com/iam/docs/resource-hierarchy-access-control</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/iam/docs/roles-overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/iam/docs/roles-overview</a></p>","lessons":["d5l4","d5l5"]},"e6q23":{"id":"e6q23","exam":6,"n":23,"domain":5,"html":"<p><strong>GlowCart, an ecommerce mobile app, is worried that its order database and application files could be lost or corrupted because of a storage device failure or a ransomware attack. The operations team wants a way to return the environment to an earlier known-good state after such an incident. What should GlowCart do?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Define service level objectives (SLOs).</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Use Cloud Monitoring.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Back up data regularly.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Enable autoscaling.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Back up data regularly.</strong></p>\n<p>Backups are copies of data saved at points in time. If storage fails or ransomware encrypts or corrupts files, the team can restore from a backup to an earlier known-good state. The clue is \"return the environment to an earlier known-good state.\"</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Define SLOs</strong>: Service level objectives set reliability targets but do not protect or restore data.\n- <strong>B. Use Cloud Monitoring</strong>: Monitoring can alert you to a problem but cannot restore lost or corrupted data.\n- <strong>D. Enable autoscaling</strong>: Autoscaling adjusts capacity to traffic. It does nothing for data loss.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Need to restore after loss or ransomware → backups.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/backup-disaster-recovery\">cloud.google.com/backup-disaster-recovery</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/architecture/dr-scenarios-planning-guide\">cloud.google.com/architecture/dr-scenarios-planning-guide</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/monitoring/docs\">cloud.google.com/monitoring/docs</a></p>","lessons":["d5l6"]},"e6q24":{"id":"e6q24","exam":6,"n":24,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>TravelVista, a photo-sharing app, receives 18,000 travel photos every week from its users. The team wants to quickly detect famous places, common items, and readable text in those images without training its own machine learning model. Which Google Cloud service should TravelVista use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Vision API</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Cloud Translation API</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. BigQuery ML</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Vision API</strong></p>\n<p>The Vision API is a pre-trained Google Cloud service that analyzes images. It can detect landmarks, label common objects, and read text (OCR) without the customer training any model. The clues are \"famous places, common items, and readable text in those images\" and \"without training its own machine learning model.\"</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform</strong>: This is the full platform for building, training, and deploying models and agents. It is more than needed for a ready-made image task.\n- <strong>C. Cloud Translation API</strong>: It translates text between languages. It does not analyze images.\n- <strong>D. BigQuery ML</strong>: It builds models with SQL on data in BigQuery, not image recognition out of the box.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Generic photo labels, landmarks, text in images → Vision API.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/vision/docs\">cloud.google.com/vision/docs</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/vision/docs/ocr\">cloud.google.com/vision/docs/ocr</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/bqml-introduction\">cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/bqml-introduction</a></p>","lessons":["d3l4"]},"e6q25":{"id":"e6q25","exam":6,"n":25,"domain":5,"html":"<p><strong>ClearFunds, a digital payments company, runs all of its applications and databases on Google Cloud. The security team wants to be ready in case sensitive customer data is exposed or accessed without authorization. What should the company do to prepare for this type of event?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Reduce the use of multi-factor authentication</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Assume data security is fully Google’s responsibility, so very little preparation is needed</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Create an incident response plan to reduce the impact</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Improve physical perimeter security around its own data center</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Create an incident response plan to reduce the impact</strong></p>\n<p>An incident response plan defines in advance who does what when a security event happens: how to detect, contain, investigate, communicate, and recover. Having it ready reduces damage and recovery time. The clue is \"be ready in case sensitive customer data is exposed.\"</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Reduce MFA use</strong>: Multi-factor authentication protects accounts. Reducing it increases risk.\n- <strong>B. Assume security is fully Google's responsibility</strong>: Under the shared responsibility model, the customer still owns its data, access controls, and response.\n- <strong>D. Improve physical security of its own data center</strong>: ClearFunds runs everything on Google Cloud, and Google secures the physical data centers.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Restore data → backups. Prepare for a breach → incident response plan.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/docs/security/overview/whitepaper\">docs.cloud.google.com/docs/security/overview/whitepaper</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/security/shared-responsibility-shared-fate\">docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/security/shared-responsibility-shared-fate</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/discover/incident-response-plan-template\">cloud.google.com/discover/incident-response-plan-template</a></p>","lessons":["d5l3","d5l6"]},"e6q26":{"id":"e6q26","exam":6,"n":26,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>FarmSight AI is training a machine learning model to predict crop disease risk from field images and sensor readings. The team wants to improve how accurately the model makes predictions on new farm data. What could help improve the prediction accuracy of the model?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. More storage capacity</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Higher network bandwidth</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. More training data</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Faster CPU processors</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. More training data</strong></p>\n<p>A machine learning model learns patterns from its training data. More relevant, good-quality, representative data usually helps the model generalize better and make more accurate predictions on new data. The clue is \"improve how accurately the model makes predictions.\"</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. More storage capacity</strong>: Storage holds data but does not by itself make the model smarter.\n- <strong>B. Higher network bandwidth</strong>: Faster data transfer does not change what the model learns.\n- <strong>D. Faster CPU processors</strong>: Faster hardware can speed up training, but it does not improve accuracy on its own.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Better accuracy ← more (and better) training data, not more hardware.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-machine-learning\">cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-machine-learning</a>\n- <a href=\"https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course/overfitting/data-characteristics\">developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course/overfitting/data-characteristics</a>\n- <a href=\"https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/guides/text-classification/step-1\">developers.google.com/machine-learning/guides/text-classification/step-1</a></p>","lessons":["d3l2"]},"e6q27":{"id":"e6q27","exam":6,"n":27,"domain":5,"html":"<p><strong>CareLoop Wellness, a healthcare appointment mobile app, is moving patient scheduling and profile data to Google Cloud. The team wants to understand what privacy means when users’ personal information is stored, accessed, and shared through cloud services. How is privacy defined in the context of cloud technology?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Restrictions on data access and sharing</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Processes used to verify a user’s identity</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Exposure to data breaches and cyber attacks</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Meeting required regulatory and industry standards</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Restrictions on data access and sharing</strong></p>\n<p>In cloud technology, privacy is about controlling who can access personal data and how it is used and shared. It gives individuals and organizations control over their information. The clue is \"personal information is stored, accessed, and shared.\"</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. Processes to verify identity</strong>: That is authentication, one security control, not the definition of privacy.\n- <strong>C. Exposure to breaches and attacks</strong>: This describes security risk or threats, not privacy itself.\n- <strong>D. Meeting regulatory and industry standards</strong>: That is compliance. Compliance can support privacy, but it is a different concept.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Privacy = limits on access and sharing. Authentication = verifying identity. Compliance = meeting standards.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/privacy\">cloud.google.com/privacy</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/transparency\">cloud.google.com/transparency</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/docs/security/overview/whitepaper\">cloud.google.com/docs/security/overview/whitepaper</a></p>","lessons":["d5l8"]},"e6q28":{"id":"e6q28","exam":6,"n":28,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>ShopSphere, an online retail platform, is auditing its customer profile table before loading it into an analytics system. The team finds that many customer email values look like “ravi.example.com” or “support-shop.com” because they do not contain the “@” character and fail the expected email pattern. Which data quality dimension is most directly affected?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Timeliness</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Completeness</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Validity</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Uniqueness</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Validity</strong></p>\n<p>Validity checks whether data follows the expected format, type, or rules. An email address without an \"@\" character fails the email format rule, so it is invalid. The clue is \"fail the expected email pattern.\"</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Timeliness</strong>: This is about whether data is current. The values are wrong in format, not out of date.\n- <strong>B. Completeness</strong>: The email fields are filled in, so nothing is missing.\n- <strong>D. Uniqueness</strong>: This is about duplicate records, which the stem does not mention.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Wrong format or broken rule → validity. Doesn't match the real value → accuracy.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/dataplex/docs/auto-data-quality-overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/dataplex/docs/auto-data-quality-overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/dataplex/docs/use-auto-data-quality\">docs.cloud.google.com/dataplex/docs/use-auto-data-quality</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-data-governance\">cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-data-governance</a></p>","lessons":["d2l4"]},"e6q29":{"id":"e6q29","exam":6,"n":29,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>FreshCartGo uses scooters to deliver groceries across several city zones. Its operations team finds that the same scooter is saved more than once in the fleet database with slightly different asset codes, so the maintenance prediction model counts the scooter’s travel distance multiple times. Which data quality dimension should be corrected?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Timeliness</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Validity</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Uniqueness</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Explainability</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Uniqueness</strong></p>\n<p>Uniqueness means each real-world thing is recorded only once. The same scooter saved several times with slightly different asset codes is a duplicate, which makes the model count its travel distance more than once. The clue is \"saved more than once.\"</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Timeliness</strong>: This is about data being current, not duplicated.\n- <strong>B. Validity</strong>: The asset codes may each be in a valid format. The problem is that they refer to the same scooter.\n- <strong>D. Explainability</strong>: This is about understanding how an ML model reaches its outputs. It is not a data quality dimension.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Duplicates → uniqueness.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-data-governance\">cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-data-governance</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/dataplex/docs/auto-data-quality-overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/dataplex/docs/auto-data-quality-overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://ibm.com/think/topics/data-quality-dimensions\">ibm.com/think/topics/data-quality-dimensions</a></p>","lessons":["d2l4"]},"e6q30":{"id":"e6q30","exam":6,"n":30,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>FitBuddy, a wellness app company, has finished building and testing a custom AI agent that can answer member questions about workout plans and nutrition programs. The team now wants to call the agent from its existing Android, iOS, and web applications using code. Which interface should the team use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Agent Platform API</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Billing Export API</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Cloud Storage API</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Identity and Access Management (IAM)</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Agent Platform API</strong></p>\n<p>Once an agent is built and tested on Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform, applications call it programmatically through the platform's API. Android, iOS, and web apps send requests to the API and receive the agent's responses. The clues are \"call the agent from its existing... applications using code.\"</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. Billing Export API</strong>: Billing export sends cost data to BigQuery for analysis. It has nothing to do with calling an agent.\n- <strong>C. Cloud Storage API</strong>: This stores and retrieves objects such as files. It does not run AI agents.\n- <strong>D. IAM</strong>: IAM controls who can access resources. It secures the call but is not the interface for calling the agent.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Build agents without code → Agent Studio. Call agents from your apps → Agent Platform API.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/reference/rest\">docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/reference/rest</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/apis\">docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/apis</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/iam/docs/overview\">cloud.google.com/iam/docs/overview</a></p>","lessons":["d3l6","d3l8"]},"e6q31":{"id":"e6q31","exam":6,"n":31,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>ShopSwift runs a flash-sale app that needs to execute lightweight backend code whenever a product price drops below a chosen threshold. The team wants a serverless compute option that can automatically respond to events without managing servers or clusters. Which Google Cloud compute solution should ShopSwift use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Google Kubernetes Engine</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Cloud Run functions</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Bare Metal Solution</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Compute Engine</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Cloud Run functions</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud Run functions (formerly Cloud Functions) runs small pieces of code in response to events, such as a price change or a new file. It is serverless: there are no servers or clusters to manage, and you pay only when the code runs. The clues are \"lightweight backend code,\" \"automatically respond to events,\" and \"without managing servers or clusters.\"</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Google Kubernetes Engine</strong>: GKE runs containers on clusters, which the stem explicitly wants to avoid managing.\n- <strong>C. Bare Metal Solution</strong>: Dedicated physical servers for specialized workloads like Oracle databases. Not serverless.\n- <strong>D. Compute Engine</strong>: Virtual machines that you must manage yourself.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Small code triggered by events, no servers → Cloud Run functions.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/functions\">cloud.google.com/functions</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/run/docs/write-functions\">docs.cloud.google.com/run/docs/write-functions</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/kubernetes-engine-overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/kubernetes-engine-overview</a></p>","lessons":["d4l4"],"same":["e1q16","e2q18"]},"e6q32":{"id":"e6q32","exam":6,"n":32,"domain":6,"html":"<p><strong>GreenStay Hotels, a hospitality platform, expects a large increase in booking traffic during its summer travel campaign. The company wants to scale computing capacity quickly while also supporting its environmental sustainability goals. What should GreenStay Hotels do?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Rewrite the application code so that it consumes less energy.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Use a public cloud provider that operates energy-efficient data centers.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Switch the existing on-premises data center to a carbon-neutral energy supplier.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Buy energy-efficient physical servers for the existing on-premises data center.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Use a public cloud provider that operates energy-efficient data centers.</strong></p>\n<p>A public cloud gives near-instant extra capacity for traffic peaks, without buying hardware. At the same time, large providers like Google run highly energy-efficient data centers and invest heavily in carbon-free energy. One choice meets both goals in the stem: \"scale computing capacity quickly\" and \"sustainability goals.\"</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Rewrite the code to use less energy</strong>: This may help sustainability a little, but it is slow and does not add capacity.\n- <strong>C. Carbon-neutral supplier for on-premises</strong>: This helps sustainability but does not solve fast scaling.\n- <strong>D. Buy efficient on-premises servers</strong>: Buying hardware is slow and costly, and capacity sits idle after the peak.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Scale fast + sustainability → cloud provider with energy-efficient data centers.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/sustainability\">cloud.google.com/sustainability</a>\n- <a href=\"https://datacenters.google/operating-sustainably\">datacenters.google/operating-sustainably</a>\n- <a href=\"https://sustainability.google/operations/\">sustainability.google/operations/</a></p>","lessons":["d6l9"]},"e6q33":{"id":"e6q33","exam":6,"n":33,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>BrightAxle Components makes small metal brackets for electric scooters. The team has thousands of camera images from the inspection station, and each image is already labeled as acceptable or faulty. They want to create a simple model that can identify faulty brackets on the assembly line, but they do not have employees with machine learning or data science experience. Which Google Cloud solution should they use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Pre-trained APIs</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Document AI</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. AutoML</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Discovery AI for Retail</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. AutoML</strong></p>\n<p>AutoML lets teams train custom models on their own labeled data without writing ML code or needing data science skills. BrightAxle has thousands of images already labeled \"acceptable\" or \"faulty,\" and its own specific product, so it needs a custom model built the easy way. The clues are \"labeled,\" \"simple model,\" and \"no ML or data science experience.\"</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Pre-trained APIs</strong>: These recognize general objects. They do not know what a faulty bracket from this factory looks like.\n- <strong>B. Document AI</strong>: This extracts data from documents like forms and invoices, not product images.\n- <strong>D. Discovery AI for Retail</strong>: This improves product search and recommendations for retailers, not defect detection.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Labeled data + your own categories + no ML coders → AutoML.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/machine-learning/image-data/classification/train-model\">docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/machine-learning/image-data/classification/train-model</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/machine-learning/tutorials/image-classification-automl/training\">docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/machine-learning/tutorials/image-classification-automl/training</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/document-ai/docs/overview\">cloud.google.com/document-ai/docs/overview</a></p>","lessons":["d3l3","d3l4","d3l5","d3l8"]},"e6q34":{"id":"e6q34","exam":6,"n":34,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>MedRoute Health, a mobile appointment-booking app, is training its product team on cloud technology concepts. The team wants a simple definition of artificial intelligence before deciding whether to add features such as image understanding, language understanding, and automated recommendations. Which definition best describes artificial intelligence?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Any system that receives data continuously in real time</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Any system that automatically organizes data into a structured format</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Any system that uses advanced analytics only to create predictions</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Any system that can perform tasks that normally require human cognition</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Any system that can perform tasks that normally require human cognition</strong></p>\n<p>Artificial intelligence is the broad field of making computers do things that normally need human thinking, such as understanding images or language, reasoning, and making recommendations. Machine learning is one way to build AI. The stem's examples (image understanding, language understanding, recommendations) are all cognitive tasks.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Receives data continuously in real time</strong>: This describes streaming data, not AI.\n- <strong>B. Organizes data into a structured format</strong>: This describes data processing or transformation, not intelligence.\n- <strong>C. Uses advanced analytics only to create predictions</strong>: Prediction is one AI use, but AI is broader than predictive analytics.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> AI = tasks that need human cognition. ML = learning from data to predict and decide.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-artificial-intelligence\">cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-artificial-intelligence</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/artificial-intelligence-vs-machine-learning\">cloud.google.com/learn/artificial-intelligence-vs-machine-learning</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/discover/deep-learning-vs-machine-learning\">cloud.google.com/discover/deep-learning-vs-machine-learning</a></p>","lessons":["d3l1"]},"e6q35":{"id":"e6q35","exam":6,"n":35,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>DataBridge Finance, a payment analytics company, runs several customer-facing applications in its own data center. As customer traffic grows, the company needs more compute capacity, but adding new servers requires purchasing hardware, setting it up, configuring it, and maintaining it. Why does DataBridge Finance often struggle to scale this on-premises application infrastructure?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Scaling compute instances could breach compliance and/or regulation</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Increasing compute capacity is time-consuming and costly</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Their serverless compute functions struggle to meet the demand</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Their multi-cloud architecture is complex and expensive</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Increasing compute capacity is time-consuming and costly</strong></p>\n<p>On-premises, every new server must be bought, delivered, installed, configured, and maintained. That takes weeks or months and large upfront spending. The stem lists exactly these steps: \"purchasing hardware, setting it up, configuring it, and maintaining it.\" The cloud removes this delay by offering capacity on demand.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Scaling could breach compliance</strong>: Adding servers is not normally a compliance problem, and the stem says nothing about regulation.\n- <strong>C. Serverless functions struggle</strong>: The company runs its own data center servers, not serverless functions.\n- <strong>D. Multi-cloud complexity</strong>: The company is fully on-premises, not using multiple clouds.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> On-premises scaling = slow and costly to add capacity.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-cloud-computing\">cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-cloud-computing</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/benefits-of-cloud-computing\">cloud.google.com/learn/benefits-of-cloud-computing</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/discover/what-is-serverless-architecture\">cloud.google.com/discover/what-is-serverless-architecture</a></p>","lessons":["d1l2"]},"e6q36":{"id":"e6q36","exam":6,"n":36,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>StayNest, a short-term rental website, stores booking history and property details in BigQuery. The company wants to predict which listings will receive the highest demand during the next holiday season. The database administrators are comfortable with SQL, but the company does not have data scientists or machine learning engineers. What should StayNest do?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Use custom training in Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform with TensorFlow.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Add pre-trained APIs to the rental application.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Use BigQuery ML to build models with SQL.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Create a model with AutoML by using labeled training data.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Use BigQuery ML to build models with SQL.</strong></p>\n<p>BigQuery ML lets people who know SQL create and run machine learning models directly where the data already lives, in BigQuery. No data movement and no ML coding are needed. The clues are \"stores... in BigQuery,\" \"comfortable with SQL,\" and \"no data scientists.\"</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Custom training with TensorFlow</strong>: This needs ML engineers who write code, which StayNest does not have.\n- <strong>B. Pre-trained APIs</strong>: These handle generic tasks such as vision or translation. They cannot forecast demand from StayNest's own booking data.\n- <strong>D. AutoML with labeled data</strong>: AutoML is also low-code, but BigQuery ML is the more direct fit when the data is in BigQuery and the team speaks SQL.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> SQL analysts + data in BigQuery → BigQuery ML.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/bqml-introduction\">docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/bqml-introduction</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/create-machine-learning-model\">docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/create-machine-learning-model</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/machine-learning/tabular-data/overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/machine-learning/tabular-data/overview</a></p>","lessons":["d3l3","d3l4","d3l5"]},"e6q37":{"id":"e6q37","exam":6,"n":37,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>StyleNest Market is preparing to launch its online fashion catalog in many regions. The company needs to automatically translate English product descriptions and help pages into 18 different languages for its international storefronts. Which Google Cloud service provides this pre-trained translation capability?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Vision API</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Speech-to-Text API</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Cloud Translation API</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. BigQuery ML</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Cloud Translation API</strong></p>\n<p>The Cloud Translation API is a pre-trained service that translates text between many languages. StyleNest can send English product descriptions and help pages to the API and get versions in each target language automatically. The clue is \"translate English product descriptions... into 18 different languages.\"</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Vision API</strong>: It analyzes images, such as objects, landmarks, and text in photos. It does not translate.\n- <strong>B. Speech-to-Text API</strong>: It converts spoken audio into text. The content here is already written text.\n- <strong>D. BigQuery ML</strong>: It builds custom ML models with SQL. It is not a ready-made translation service.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Audio → Speech-to-Text. Languages → Translation. Text topics and sentiment → Natural Language.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/translate\">cloud.google.com/translate</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/translate/docs\">docs.cloud.google.com/translate/docs</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/vision/docs\">docs.cloud.google.com/vision/docs</a></p>","lessons":["d3l4"]},"e6q38":{"id":"e6q38","exam":6,"n":38,"domain":5,"html":"<p><strong>MedLedger, a healthcare records platform, needs to verify that its cloud service provider has ISO 27001 certification before storing sensitive customer data. The compliance team also needs access to audit reports, certificates, and supporting compliance documents. Which Google Cloud tool should MedLedger use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Compliance Reports Manager</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Security Command Center</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Network Intelligence Center</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Cloud Monitoring</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Compliance Reports Manager</strong></p>\n<p>Compliance Reports Manager gives customers self-service access to Google Cloud's compliance documents, such as ISO 27001 certificates, SOC reports, and audit reports. Compliance teams use it to verify the provider's certifications. The clues are \"ISO 27001 certification\" and \"audit reports, certificates, and supporting compliance documents.\"</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. Security Command Center</strong>: It finds security risks and misconfigurations in your own cloud resources. It does not provide Google's certificates.\n- <strong>C. Network Intelligence Center</strong>: It monitors and troubleshoots network performance and connectivity.\n- <strong>D. Cloud Monitoring</strong>: It tracks metrics, uptime, and alerts for applications and infrastructure.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Need Google's certificates and audit reports → Compliance Reports Manager.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/security/compliance/compliance-reports-manager\">cloud.google.com/security/compliance/compliance-reports-manager</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/security/compliance/iso-27001\">cloud.google.com/security/compliance/iso-27001</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/security/products/security-command-center\">cloud.google.com/security/products/security-command-center</a></p>","lessons":["d5l7","d5l8"]},"e6q39":{"id":"e6q39","exam":6,"n":39,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>SwiftStay, a regional travel booking app, stores room availability and customer reservation records for thousands of hotels. During holiday seasons, the app must handle sudden spikes in bookings while keeping reservation transactions accurate. How does storing this reservation data in the cloud benefit the company?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. It provides on-premises hardware access to transaction data</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. It performs real-time data transformation at scale inside an on-premises database</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. It supports accurate business transactions at scale</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. It provides physical server access during peak booking demand</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. It supports accurate business transactions at scale</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud databases built for transactions, such as Cloud SQL and Spanner, keep each reservation consistent and correct while scaling to handle many more users. This lets SwiftStay absorb holiday spikes without double-bookings or lost records. The clues are \"sudden spikes in bookings\" and \"keeping reservation transactions accurate.\"</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. On-premises hardware access</strong>: Moving data to the cloud removes the need to manage on-premises hardware.\n- <strong>B. Real-time transformation inside an on-premises database</strong>: This contradicts the move to the cloud and is about transformation, not transactions.\n- <strong>D. Physical server access during peaks</strong>: Cloud customers do not get physical server access. The provider manages the hardware.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Transactional data in the cloud → accurate transactions at scale.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/advantages-of-cloud-computing\">cloud.google.com/learn/advantages-of-cloud-computing</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/discover/what-is-cloud-scalability\">cloud.google.com/discover/what-is-cloud-scalability</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/scalable-and-resilient-apps\">docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/scalable-and-resilient-apps</a></p>","lessons":["d2l1"]},"e6q40":{"id":"e6q40","exam":6,"n":40,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>BrightCare Hospital stores many kinds of patient information in its clinical system. Some of the information is stored in fixed fields that can be easily searched, filtered, and analyzed in tables. Which item is an example of structured data that BrightCare Hospital stores in its system?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. CT scan images</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Operation room video recordings</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Patient blood pressure history</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Doctor’s handwritten notes</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Patient blood pressure history</strong></p>\n<p>Structured data fits into fixed fields, like rows and columns, so it is easy to search, filter, and analyze. Blood pressure readings are numbers with dates and patient IDs, which fit neatly into a table. The clue is \"stored in fixed fields that can be easily searched, filtered, and analyzed in tables.\"</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. CT scan images</strong>: Images have no fixed fields, so they are unstructured.\n- <strong>B. Operating room video recordings</strong>: Video is unstructured data.\n- <strong>D. Doctor's handwritten notes</strong>: Free-form handwriting is unstructured until it is processed and extracted.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Table fields and numeric readings → structured. Images, video, free text → unstructured.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-a-relational-database\">cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-a-relational-database</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/discover/data-lake-vs-data-warehouse\">cloud.google.com/discover/data-lake-vs-data-warehouse</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-data-cloud\">cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-data-cloud</a></p>","lessons":["d2l3"]},"e6q41":{"id":"e6q41","exam":6,"n":41,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>FitPulse Analytics is building an AI feature that can predict user workout progress from historical activity data. The data science team wants a managed Google Cloud platform that supports the complete machine learning lifecycle, including preparing data, training custom models, evaluating them, deploying them, and managing them. Which Google Cloud product or service should FitPulse Analytics use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Dataproc</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Compute Engine</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Recommendations AI</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform</strong></p>\n<p>Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform (formerly Vertex AI) is Google Cloud's unified, managed platform for the whole ML lifecycle: preparing data, training custom models, evaluating, deploying, and managing them. It also hosts foundation models and agents. The clue is \"supports the complete machine learning lifecycle.\"</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Dataproc</strong>: A managed service for Apache Spark and Hadoop data processing, not a full ML lifecycle platform.\n- <strong>B. Compute Engine</strong>: Raw virtual machines. The team would have to build and manage every ML tool itself.\n- <strong>C. Recommendations AI</strong>: A specialized product for retail recommendations, not a general platform for custom models.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Full managed ML lifecycle, foundation models, agents → Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/products/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform\">cloud.google.com/products/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/overview</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/machine-learning/training-overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/machine-learning/training-overview</a></p>","lessons":["d3l6","d3l8"],"same":["e1q36"]},"e6q42":{"id":"e6q42","exam":6,"n":42,"domain":5,"html":"<p><strong>FitCart, a retail mobile app company, has moved its order processing, analytics, and customer-facing services from its own server room to Google Cloud. During a quarterly security review, the security lead wants to confirm who is responsible for protecting the physical buildings, hardware, and facilities where the cloud resources run. Who is responsible for securing the physical infrastructure of the data centers?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. FitCart and Google Cloud</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. External managed security service providers</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Google Cloud</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. FitCart</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: C. Google Cloud</strong></p>\n<p>Under the shared responsibility model, the cloud provider always secures the physical layer: data center buildings, hardware, power, and facilities. This is true for IaaS, PaaS, and SaaS alike. The customer is responsible for things like its data, access settings, and how it configures services. The clue is \"physical buildings, hardware, and facilities.\"</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. FitCart and Google Cloud</strong>: Many layers are shared, but physical data center security is not. FitCart has no access to Google's buildings.\n- <strong>B. External managed security providers</strong>: Third parties may help with the customer's side, but they do not secure Google's data centers.\n- <strong>D. FitCart</strong>: FitCart left its own server room. It no longer owns the physical infrastructure.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Physical data center security → Google alone, in every service model.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/security/shared-responsibility-shared-fate\">docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/security/shared-responsibility-shared-fate</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/docs/security/infrastructure/design\">docs.cloud.google.com/docs/security/infrastructure/design</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/docs/security/overview/whitepaper\">docs.cloud.google.com/docs/security/overview/whitepaper</a></p>","lessons":["d5l3"]},"e6q43":{"id":"e6q43","exam":6,"n":43,"domain":6,"html":"<p><strong>FreshBite, a food delivery website, has separate software development and operations teams that use different tools and processes. The company wants to reduce delays, misunderstandings, and production issues caused by these teams working in isolation. How can improving communication between the teams help reduce these silo-related problems?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. By identifying who caused each incident and applying penalties</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. By merging all development and operations duties into one identical job role</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. By adding stronger encryption controls to improve team workflows</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. By promoting shared responsibility for business results</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. By promoting shared responsibility for business results</strong></p>\n<p>DevOps culture breaks down silos by making development and operations jointly responsible for delivering and running reliable software. When both teams share the same goals, they communicate more, hand off less, and fix issues together. The clue is \"teams working in isolation\" causing \"delays, misunderstandings, and production issues.\"</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Identify who caused incidents and apply penalties</strong>: Blame makes people hide problems. DevOps and SRE favor blameless culture.\n- <strong>B. Merge all duties into one identical role</strong>: DevOps aligns teams. It does not require everyone to do the exact same job.\n- <strong>C. Stronger encryption controls</strong>: Encryption is a security measure and does nothing for team communication.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Silos are fixed by shared responsibility for outcomes, not by blame.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/devops\">cloud.google.com/devops</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/devops\">docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/devops</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/devops-sre/how-communication-contributes-to-software-delivery-success\">cloud.google.com/blog/products/devops-sre/how-communication-contributes-to-software-delivery-success</a></p>","lessons":["d6l6"]},"e6q44":{"id":"e6q44","exam":6,"n":44,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>SecureVision Labs, a fraud detection company, wants to use advanced machine learning capabilities from Google Cloud to improve risk analysis. However, regulatory rules require customer transaction data to remain in the company’s own on-premises data center. Which approach should SecureVision Labs use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. A hybrid-cloud approach</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. A multi-cloud approach</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. A public-cloud approach</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. A private-cloud approach</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. A hybrid-cloud approach</strong></p>\n<p>A hybrid cloud connects on-premises infrastructure with a public cloud. SecureVision can keep regulated transaction data in its own data center while still using Google Cloud's ML capabilities. The clues are \"data to remain in the company's own on-premises data center\" plus \"advanced machine learning capabilities from Google Cloud.\"</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. Multi-cloud</strong>: This means using several public cloud providers. The stem involves one cloud plus on-premises.\n- <strong>C. Public-cloud</strong>: Moving fully to the public cloud would break the rule to keep data on-premises.\n- <strong>D. Private-cloud</strong>: A private cloud alone would not give access to Google Cloud's ML services.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Data stays on-premises + Google Cloud services → hybrid.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-hybrid-cloud\">cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-hybrid-cloud</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/deployment-archetypes/hybrid\">docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/deployment-archetypes/hybrid</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/distributed-cloud\">cloud.google.com/distributed-cloud</a></p>","lessons":["d4l7"]},"e6q45":{"id":"e6q45","exam":6,"n":45,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>PolicyPilot, an insurance technology company, wants to launch several enterprise AI features, including claim summarization, support response drafting, and document classification. The team wants to avoid building a large model from scratch and instead start from a broadly trained model that can be adapted to its business needs. What is a primary strategic advantage of using foundation models for these AI initiatives?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. They provide a powerful, pre-trained starting point that can be fine-tuned, significantly reducing development time.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. They remove the need for organizations to apply governance or security controls to their own data.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. They make sure the organization never needs to pay for compute resources.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. They guarantee that every generated response will be factually correct without human review.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. They provide a powerful, pre-trained starting point that can be fine-tuned, significantly reducing development time.</strong></p>\n<p>Foundation models are large models trained on broad data. Companies can adapt them to their own needs through prompting, tuning, or grounding, instead of building a model from scratch. That saves time and cost across many use cases, such as summarizing, drafting, and classifying. The clue is \"start from a broadly trained model that can be adapted.\"</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. Remove the need for governance or security</strong>: Organizations must still govern and protect their data when using any AI model.\n- <strong>C. Never pay for compute</strong>: Using and tuning models still consumes paid compute.\n- <strong>D. Guarantee factually correct responses</strong>: Foundation models can hallucinate. Human review and grounding are still needed.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Foundation models = pre-trained start, fine-tune, save time, still need review.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/discover/what-are-foundation-models\">cloud.google.com/discover/what-are-foundation-models</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/models\">docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/models</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/models/tuning\">docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/models/tuning</a></p>","lessons":["d3l7","d3l10"]},"e6q46":{"id":"e6q46","exam":6,"n":46,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>BrightCart, an online grocery app, collects customer orders, delivery logs, product reviews, and support messages from several systems. The company wants to process this cloud-based data and make it easy for analysts to query it for reporting, dashboards, and business insights. Which cloud data storage system should BrightCart use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Relational database</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Private data center</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Data field</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Data warehouse</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Data warehouse</strong></p>\n<p>A data warehouse, such as BigQuery, gathers data from many systems into one place, organized for analysis. Analysts can query it for reports, dashboards, and business insights. The clues are \"from several systems\" and \"easy for analysts to query it for reporting, dashboards, and business insights.\"</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Relational database</strong>: It is designed for day-to-day transactions in one application, not large-scale analytics across many sources.\n- <strong>B. Private data center</strong>: This is a location for infrastructure, not a data storage system, and the data is already cloud-based.\n- <strong>C. Data field</strong>: A field is a single column or attribute in a record, not a storage system.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Raw data in its original format → data lake. Analyst reporting and dashboards → data warehouse.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-a-data-warehouse\">cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-a-data-warehouse</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/introduction\">docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/introduction</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/loading-data\">docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/loading-data</a></p>","lessons":["d2l5"]},"e6q47":{"id":"e6q47","exam":6,"n":47,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>ChatPath Support, a customer service analytics company, stores thousands of written chat transcripts between shoppers and support agents. The business team wants an automated way to analyze the text and discover the main subjects customers mention most often, such as delivery delays, refunds, product quality, or account issues. Which Google Cloud service should they use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Vision API</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Speech-to-Text API</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Cloud Translation API</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Natural Language API</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D. Natural Language API</strong></p>\n<p>The Natural Language API is a pre-trained service that analyzes written text. It can identify entities, classify content into topics, and detect sentiment. That lets ChatPath find the main subjects customers mention, such as refunds or delivery delays. The clues are \"written chat transcripts\" and \"discover the main subjects.\"</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Vision API</strong>: It analyzes images, not written conversations.\n- <strong>B. Speech-to-Text API</strong>: It turns audio into text. The transcripts are already written.\n- <strong>C. Cloud Translation API</strong>: It translates between languages but does not find topics.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Text topics, entities, sentiment → Natural Language API.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/natural-language/docs\">cloud.google.com/natural-language/docs</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/speech-to-text/docs\">cloud.google.com/speech-to-text/docs</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/translate/docs\">cloud.google.com/translate/docs</a></p>","lessons":["d3l4"]},"e6q48":{"id":"e6q48","exam":6,"n":48,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>BrightPulse Rewards, a mobile loyalty app, receives several petabytes of customer activity records, partner campaign logs, and purchase events from systems running on Google Cloud and on two other public cloud providers. The leadership team wants to bring these data sources together across providers so analysts can generate insights for long-term planning and business decisions. What type of environment should BrightPulse Rewards use?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Multi cloud environment</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Virtual machine environment</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Hybrid cloud environment</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Container environment</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Multi cloud environment</strong></p>\n<p>BrightPulse runs on Google Cloud, but some of its data lives with two other public cloud providers. Using services from more than one public cloud provider is a multicloud environment. Google supports this with tools that work across clouds, such as BigQuery Omni for analyzing data where it sits and Looker for BI across sources.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. Virtual machine environment</strong>: VMs are a compute choice. They say nothing about combining data across providers.\n- <strong>C. Hybrid cloud environment</strong>: Hybrid means on-premises or private infrastructure plus public cloud. Here every source is a public cloud.\n- <strong>D. Container environment</strong>: Containers package applications. They don't define where data from different providers lives.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Several public clouds → multicloud. On-premises + public cloud → hybrid.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/system-design/compute\">cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/system-design/compute</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/discover/what-is-multicloud\">cloud.google.com/discover/what-is-multicloud</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/introduction\">cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/introduction</a></p>","lessons":["d4l7"]},"e6q49":{"id":"e6q49","exam":6,"n":49,"domain":5,"html":"<p><strong>NovaRetail Bank is expanding its cloud adoption from a small project-by-project approach to a broader business transformation approach. The security team wants to modernize access control and protect users, applications, and data across different environments. How should NovaRetail Bank change its cloud security?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Provide employee identities using only Google Cloud authentication.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Adopt a zero-trust model that verifies users, devices and applications.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Focus on strong perimeter security and trust resources inside the private network.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Focus mainly on three Identity and Access Management IAM basic roles: owner, editor, and viewer.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: B. Adopt a zero-trust model that verifies users, devices and applications.</strong></p>\n<p>Zero trust means no user or device is trusted just because it is inside the corporate network. Every request is checked based on identity, device and context, wherever it comes from. This fits a bank that wants to protect users, apps and data \"across different environments\" as it moves beyond a project-by-project approach. The clue is \"modernize access control\".</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>A. Only Google Cloud authentication</strong>: Limiting identity to one method doesn't modernize the security model and may not cover every environment.\n- <strong>C. Strong perimeter, trust the inside</strong>: This is the old castle-and-moat model that zero trust replaces.\n- <strong>D. Mainly the three basic roles</strong>: Owner, editor and viewer are too broad and go against least privilege.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Verify every request from anywhere → zero trust.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-zero-trust\">cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-zero-trust</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/beyondcorp\">cloud.google.com/beyondcorp</a>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/iam/docs/roles-overview\">docs.cloud.google.com/iam/docs/roles-overview</a></p>","lessons":["d5l4","d5l5"]},"e6q50":{"id":"e6q50","exam":6,"n":50,"domain":6,"html":"<p><strong>GameNest, a browser-based multiplayer game platform, released a new version of its matchmaking feature. After launch, the engineering team discovered a small issue that affects a limited number of players but does not cause a major outage. The team wants to handle the issue according to SRE principles. How should GameNest approach this bug?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Accept and learn from the bug because failure is normal</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Accept and ignore the bug because it is only minor</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Hold a postmortem to reprimand the employee responsible for the bug</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Document bug correction to eliminate all future bugs</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: A. Accept and learn from the bug because failure is normal</strong></p>\n<p>Site Reliability Engineering (SRE) accepts that no system is 100% reliable. Failures will happen, so teams fix the problem and learn from it, often through a blameless postmortem, to make the system more reliable. The clue is \"handle the issue according to SRE principles\" for a small, non-critical bug.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the others are wrong:</strong>\n- <strong>B. Accept and ignore the bug</strong>: SRE accepts failure but does not ignore it. Every issue is a chance to learn and improve.\n- <strong>C. Postmortem to reprimand the employee</strong>: SRE postmortems are blameless. They focus on systems and processes, not punishing people.\n- <strong>D. Eliminate all future bugs</strong>: Zero bugs is unrealistic. SRE manages risk with error budgets instead of aiming for perfection.</p>\n<p><strong>Remember:</strong> Failure is normal: accept it, learn from it, run blameless postmortems.</p>\n<p><strong>References:</strong>\n- <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/u/0/d/1h2TCYk4HqRcrashLlr_Kb8A6esNssGrmxHzdyYH1ydQ/edit\">docs.google.com/document/u/0/d/1h2TCYk4HqRcrashLlr_Kb8A6esNssGrmxHzdyYH1ydQ/edit</a>\n- <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/management-tools/meeting-reliability-challenges-with-sre-principles\">cloud.google.com/blog/products/management-tools/meeting-reliability-challenges-with-sre-principles</a></p>","lessons":["d6l6"]}}};
